ng","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Thinking levels: what `agent` bakes, and what the baked aliases change"},"1618":{"id":"/litellm-routing-38","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Routing inside the `agent` group, the four unpinned lanes (`agent-swarm`,\n`agent-explore`, `agent-review`, `agent-swarm-fast`), and the `agent` to `coder`\nfallback"},"1619":{"id":"/litellm-routing-39","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"The two fast-compaction lanes, and the proxy callback that moves one summary call of\na session onto a cheaper tier"},"1620":{"id":"/litellm-routing-40","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Reading the routing back, and editing an alias safely"},"1621":{"id":"/litellm-routing-41","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"**What this enables**"},"1622":{"id":"/litellm-routing-42","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Moving a workload between the Arc iGPU and a Spark box without touching a workspace"},"1623":{"id":"/litellm-routing-43","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Diagnosing a wrong-backend answer from the routing rather than from the client"},"1624":{"id":"/litellm-routing-44","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Deep reference for the model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request\npicks a backend, what a pinned conversation does, and what happens when a Spark box goes\ndown."},"1625":{"id":"/litellm-routing-45","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Short version lives in README.md (\"LiteLLM model aliases\"); this file is\nthe long version behind it. Symptom-first checks live in\ntroubleshooting.md."},"1626":{"id":"/litellm-routing-46","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Related: coder-templates.md (the templates that render\n`~/.grok/config.toml`), grok-build-and-agents.md (the CLI and\nthe control-plane chat), project-conventions.md."},"1627":{"id":"/litellm-routing-47","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Workspaces and desktops always talk to one base URL,\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`, and pick a model alias. That is the\nhost-gateway door."},"1628":{"id":"/litellm-routing-48","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"The control-plane chat agent dials the same proxy at `http://litellm:4000/v1`, over the\ncompose network, from inside the `coder` container. That is the server-side door."},"1629":{"id":"/litellm-routing-49","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Both doors authenticate with the same `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` value."},"1630":{"id":"/litellm-routing-50","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Inside a workspace the key travels as the `LITELLM_API_KEY` environment variable, which\n`~/.grok/config.toml` references by `env_key`, so no key byte lands in a file."},"1631":{"id":"/litellm-routing-51","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"An alias is a `model_name:` entry in `litellm/config.yaml`, not a model."},"1632":{"id":"/litellm-routing-52","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"Every alias is a stable name. A workspace setting never has to change when a backend\nmoves behind one."},"1633":{"id":"/litellm-routing-53","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"A rename or deletion is the edit that can reach workspaces: both\n`templates/*/grok-config.toml.tftpl` twins render the alias list into\n`~/.grok/config.toml`, a running workspace keeps the copy it rendered at start, and\nthe next start re-renders it."},"1634":{"id":"/litellm-routing-54","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`model_list:` is the alias list; `router_settings:` is the routing."},"1635":{"id":"/litellm-routing-55","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`routing_strategy: least-busy` is the picker;\n`optional_pre_call_checks: [\"session_affinity\"]` is the pinning layer above it."},"1636":{"id":"/litellm-routing-56","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`fallbacks:` carries the degrade path `agent: [\"coder\"]`, repeated for each thinking\nalias, because those groups go down with exactly the same boxes. The four unpinned\nlanes and the two fast-compaction lanes hop one step further, through the lane that\nbakes their own level, so a lane that asked for `low` is not served an `xhigh` answer\nby its fallback."},"1637":{"id":"/litellm-routing-57","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`allowed_fails: 40` and `cooldown_time: 5` decide when a box stops being a candidate."},"1638":{"id":"/litellm-routing-58","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`background_health_checks: true`, with `health_check_interval: 300` and\n`health_check_concurrency: 2`, re-probe a box on their own, so recovery does not\nwait for user traffic to rediscover it. Those two keys have to sit in\n`general_settings:`; under `router_settings:` the proxy raises `ValueError` at boot.\nThe interval is LiteLLM's own default and the concurrency is deliberately small:\na probe is a real generation that carries the lane's baked `reasoning_effort`, and\non a box whose prompt cache sits full it evicts a warm conversation to fit itself.\nSee the TensorFold log section of troubleshooting.md."},"1639":{"id":"/litellm-routing-59","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`num_retries: 2` covers a transient blip; moving off a busy box is routing's job, not\nthe retry chain's. `request_timeout: 1200` is now the tightest deadline on the chain -\nthe CLI's streaming idle allowance (`inference_idle_timeout_secs`) sits at 1800 s - so\nthe proxy is the end that gives up first, at a full twenty minutes per attempt."},"1640":{"id":"/litellm-routing-60","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`model_group_retry_policy:` gives the nine agent lanes their own `RetryPolicy`. The\nreason it exists is narrow enough to be worth its own section below."},"1641":{"id":"/litellm-routing-61","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"TensorFold on the Spark boxes refuses an over-admitted stream with the Zig error value\n`NoRoom`, delivered as an HTTP 500 body. The OpenAI SDK hands that to LiteLLM with no\nint `status_code`, which at the pinned `v1.102.1` puts it on the status-less branch of\n`exception_mapping_utils`, so the router sees `APIConnectionError`."},"1642":{"id":"/litellm-routing-62","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"`RetryPolicy` in that tag has no `APIConnectionErrorRetries` field. `DefaultRetries` is\nthe only field that can reach the error, which is why each agent lane declares one:"},"1643":{"id":"/litellm-routing-63","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"Read these three facts before changing the block:"},"1644":{"id":"/litellm-routing-64","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"The nine Spark groups are independent and none inherits `agent`, so each needs its own\nsix keys. The zeros pin the exceptions `DefaultRetries` would otherwise reach; at this\ntag `NotFoundError` has no field of its own, so without a policy a typo'd model id is\nretried on `DefaultRetries`."},"1645":{"id":"/litellm-routing-65","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"The policy is scoped to the Spark lanes. `coder` (Arc), `chat` and `compaction-fast`\n(the Arc group a summary hops to) keep the plain `num_retries` path."},"1646":{"id":"/litellm-routing-66","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"These retries are instant and stay on the same box. A status-less exception means\n`_deployment_ids_to_skip_on_retry` excludes nothing and `_time_to_sleep_before_retry`\nreturns 0 while any deployment is healthy, and on the five pinned groups the\nworkspace-wide `x-litellm-session-id` pin sends every subagent of a workspace to one\nbox anyway. The block makes a refusal less likely to surface. Relocation comes from the\nbox and the fan-out. The four unpinned lanes - `agent-swarm`, `agent-explore`,\n`agent-review` and `agent-swarm-fast` - are the exception: with no pin to name a box,\neach of their retries is re-placed by `least-busy` and can land on another one."},"1647":{"id":"/litellm-routing-67","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"The two levers that actually move load off a shedding box:"},"1648":{"id":"/litellm-routing-68","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"The box-side reclaim switch. The recipe measured the same refusal going from\n\"2 of 5 streams refused\" to \"5 of 5 admitted\" once patch 0029 is live with\n`TF_FLASHNEXT_ADMIT_RECLAIM=1`. `//scripts:spark-configure --apply` owns the per-host\noverride file those settings belong in, and `//scripts:spark-verify` already fails a\nbox whose `streams.max` drifts from the fleet's `PARALLEL`. Asserting the\nadmit-reclaim setting itself is still open work: neither script names that key today."},"1649":{"id":"/litellm-routing-69","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"Keep what one workspace puts on one box at or under that box's `PARALLEL` (5). A\npinned lane sends the whole fan-out to the box its session key resolves to, so the\nwidth worth asking for there is 4: one box's streams, minus the parent's own request.\nThe unpinned `agent-swarm` lane is what the writers ride by default, and the one that\ncan afford a wider wave - 15 subagents spread by `least-busy` over three boxes fill each\nbox's `PARALLEL` exactly, which is the\ndifference between buying wall-clock and buying queue depth. That default asks every box\nfor all of its streams, and it reserves nothing for the parent's own request the way\nthe one-box width of 4 does. That is a deliberate trade rather than an\noversight: the parent's conversation, the reader waves and the review pass all arrive\non the same three boxes and share those `PARALLEL` streams, so at 15 a single busy\nworkspace can have the fleet fully subscribed and the last arrivals wait. What makes\nthat survivable is `limit_behavior = \"queue\"` holding the overflow instead of failing\nthe spawn, so the cost of overshoot is a subagent's latency. That 15 is\n`grok_swarm_width` (3 boxes x `PARALLEL` 5), the default on every lane whatever the\noperator picks there; drop it when several workspaces share the fleet, or set\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent` to hold a width outright. The\ntwo reader lanes\nspread the same way, and each group counts its own in-flight requests, but the box's\n`PARALLEL` streams are one pool that every alias arriving on it shares.\nPast 15 across the fleet the boxes have no\nstreams left to give, whatever the lane.\nThe same 15 is the budget of a whole tree, because `max_depth` (rendered from\n`grok_subagent_max_depth`, default 2, validated 1..3) lets a child spawn. `reviewer`,\n`security-auditor` and `tester` name `spawn_subagent` in their `tools`, and every mapper\nsuch a child raises holds a stream the same way its parent does, so a parent sums the\nin-flight `1 + spawns` over every task and keeps that total at or below the fleet's\nstream count (`fleet:` in the plan, 15 by default,\n`templates/common/ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py:42`). That checker refuses a wave whose\ncost passes the fleet, and the sum is a separate figure from the session cap\n`[subagents] max_concurrent` sets, because a grandchild opens its own session. The box\nrefuses an over-admitted stream the same way it refuses any other, with `NoRoom`."},"1650":{"id":"/litellm-routing-70","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"Two knobs that look right and do nothing here: `retry_after` is only a `min_timeout` and\nis never consulted while the group has healthy deployments, and `fallbacks_by_model`\ndoes not exist in this tag (the proxy warns and ignores an unknown `router_settings`\nkey, so a config that looks correct silently does nothing, which is why\n`//tests:bats_litellm_config_test` rejects it by name)."},"1651":{"id":"/litellm-routing-71","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias   | Model                | Backend                                                                                | Structured tool calls |\n| ------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `agent` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | `SPARK1/2/3_OPENAI_URL` (TensorFold on :8888 - one deployment per Spark box, optional) | yes (measured)        |","table":"table-0"},"1652":{"id":"/litellm-routing-72","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias       | Model                | Backend                                                                          | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ----------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-low` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `low` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1653":{"id":"/litellm-routing-73","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias          | Model                | Backend                                                                             | Structured tool calls                 |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-medium` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `medium` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1654":{"id":"/litellm-routing-74","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias         | Model                | Backend                                                                            | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-xhigh` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `xhigh` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1655":{"id":"/litellm-routing-75","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias         | Model                | Backend                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-swarm` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `xhigh`, and the first group with no session pin, so each request is placed on the least-busy healthy box; the default `grok_subagents_lane`, so the writer types fan out here | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1656":{"id":"/litellm-routing-76","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias           | Model                | Backend                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | Structured tool calls                 |\n| --------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-explore` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `low`, and no pin here either, so a wave of one-shot read-only children is placed across the fleet rather than queueing behind one box; the default `grok_explore_lane` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1657":{"id":"/litellm-routing-77","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias          | Model                | Backend                                                                                                                                                                                                                | Structured tool calls                 |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-review` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `xhigh` like `agent`, and the same pin lift as `agent-explore`, so a wave of reviewers spreads the same way; the default `grok_review_lane` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1658":{"id":"/litellm-routing-78","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias              | Model                | Backend                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ------------------ | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-xhigh-fast` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent-xhigh`, as its own group; the same deployments, the same baked `xhigh`, the same session pin - this lane differs from its parent in one call of the conversation, which the proxy callback moves to `compaction-fast` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1659":{"id":"/litellm-routing-79","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias              | Model                | Backend                                                                                                                                                         | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ------------------ | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-swarm-fast` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent-swarm`, as its own group; baked `xhigh`, the same pin lift as `agent-swarm`, and the same summary hop to `compaction-fast` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1660":{"id":"/litellm-routing-80","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias   | Model               | Backend | Structured tool calls |\n| ------- | ------------------- | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `coder` | `qwen2.5-coder:32b` |         | no (measured)         |","table":"table-0"},"1661":{"id":"/litellm-routing-81","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias        | Model               | Backend | Structured tool calls |\n| ------------ | ------------------- | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `coder-fast` | `qwen2.5-coder:14b` |         | no (measured)         |","table":"table-0"},"1662":{"id":"/litellm-routing-82","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias             | Model               | Backend                                                       | Structured tool calls                              |\n| ----------------- | ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `compaction-fast` | `qwen2.5-coder:14b` | `[model.*]` block and no `local.model_choices` entry names it | no (measured on these weights, as on `coder-fast`) |","table":"table-0"},"1663":{"id":"/litellm-routing-83","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias  | Model        | Backend | Structured tool calls |\n| ------ | ------------ | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `chat` | `qwen2.5:7b` |         | yes (measured)        |","table":"table-0"},"1664":{"id":"/litellm-routing-84","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The `agent` row is ONE model group with three deployments, one per Spark box,\nall equal."},"1665":{"id":"/litellm-routing-85","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The eight `agent-*` rows are that same lane with a thinking level baked into\n`litellm_params.reasoning_effort`. Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and\n`xhigh` and nothing else - there is no `high` - so the level is a name to pick\nrather than a knob to turn, and `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` are\nhow the level reaches Coder Agents: its model dropdown chooses an alias, not a\nrequest parameter. The `agent-explore` and `agent-review` rows are the same idea aimed\nat a fleet role rather than at a person in that dropdown - see\nthe fan-out lanes."},"1666":{"id":"/litellm-routing-86","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"`agent` is not the unthinking default. It bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`, so a\nCoder Agents chat on the default alias thinks hard, and `agent-xhigh` asks the\nbox for the same level by name. A `reasoning_effort` the client sends in the\nrequest still wins over the baked one."},"1667":{"id":"/litellm-routing-87","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"Those eight groups reuse the same `SPARK1/2/3_OPENAI_URL` / `SPARK_n_API_KEY`\npairs as `agent`. They are separate model groups on the same hardware, not eight\nmore boxes: routing state is per group, so least-busy's counters and the session\npin are computed inside a group and two chats on different aliases can land on the\nsame box at once. What does not change is the box - 5 full 262144-token streams in\ntotal, whichever aliases arrive on it. This is not the \"two deployments on one\nbox\" mistake below, which is about a single group counting two URLs on one machine\nas two machines."},"1668":{"id":"/litellm-routing-88","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The three rows after `agent-xhigh` differ from the four above them in routing state\nonly: same model, same three boxes, the thinking level each row names, and no session\npin. See\nthe swarm lane and\nthe fan-out lanes."},"1669":{"id":"/litellm-routing-89","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The two rows after those are not new lanes. `agent-xhigh-fast` is `agent-xhigh` and\n`agent-swarm-fast` is `agent-swarm`, deployment for deployment, and what differs is\nwhere one call of the conversation is answered - the CLI's summary request, which a\nproxy callback re-serves from `compaction-fast` on the Arc when the summary fits that\nwindow, and from a Spark group at thinking `low` when it does not. See\nthe fast-compaction lanes."},"1670":{"id":"/litellm-routing-90","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"`compaction-fast` is that hop's small-summary target, and a row no template offers a client: no\n`reasoning_effort` line, no tool-calling flags, and `max_input_tokens: 32768` rather\nthan the Spark rows' 262144, because a summary is prose and 32768 is the honest\nwindow of those weights."},"1671":{"id":"/litellm-routing-91","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The Spark boxes serve TensorFold's OpenAI-compatible `/v1` and nothing else: Ollama\nruns on the Arc, so a Spark box never sees the Ollama protocol."},"1672":{"id":"/litellm-routing-92","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"`model:` is sent to the backend verbatim, so `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` has to match the\nserved id byte-for-byte, case and hyphens included."},"1673":{"id":"/litellm-routing-93","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The `agent` lane deliberately carries the `openai/` provider prefix. `ollama_chat/`\nwould route those boxes through the Ollama-specific code path, and `api_base` has to\ninclude `/v1`."},"1674":{"id":"/litellm-routing-94","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"The Qwen2.5-Coder weights do not emit structured tool calls on this runtime."},"1675":{"id":"/litellm-routing-95","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"Measured against this box's IPEX-LLM Ollama, whose bundled `ollama` is 0.9.3."},"1676":{"id":"/litellm-routing-96","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"A request carrying `tools` for `qwen2.5-coder:32b` or `:14b` comes back as plain text,\n`finish_reason: \"stop\"`, and no `tool_calls` field at all."},"1677":{"id":"/litellm-routing-97","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"It behaves that way on both `/api/chat` and `/v1/chat/completions`."},"1678":{"id":"/litellm-routing-98","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"The model writes the JSON call into the message body, which no agent driver can consume."},"1679":{"id":"/litellm-routing-99","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"`qwen2.5:7b` on the same runtime does return a real `tool_calls` array."},"1680":{"id":"/litellm-routing-100","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"So the limitation tracks the model, not the runtime."},"1681":{"id":"/litellm-routing-101","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-agent-mode-needs","content":"Agent mode therefore needs either the Spark `agent` lane or the small `chat` model."},"1682":{"id":"/litellm-routing-102","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-agent-mode-needs","content":"Grok Build, Cline, Roo and Kilo all default to `agent`."},"1683":{"id":"/litellm-routing-103","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-agent-mode-needs","content":"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (the default, with `chat` standing in when the Spark lane is dark),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, `agent-swarm`, and `chat` as Coder Agents chat\nmodel configs, for the same reason: an agent turn without callable tools cannot browse or\nedit, so registering `coder` or `coder-fast` would only add a visibly broken model to\nthe pickers. Nine Spark aliases drive tools, so nine could be registered; only the\nfive above are - the thinking aliases are there to make the level selectable from that\npicker, while `agent-explore` and `agent-review` answer a parent's dispatch rather than\na person at the dropdown, so registering them would add two names nobody types. The two\nfast-compaction lanes are absent for a mechanical reason of the same kind: the hop keys\non the summary instruction the Grok CLI appends, and the aibridge behind this dropdown\nnever sends one, so a chat on `agent-xhigh-fast` is a chat on `agent-xhigh` wearing a\nlonger name."},"1684":{"id":"/litellm-routing-104","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#one-group-three-deployments","content":"Three deployments, one per Spark box, and no `order:` tier. A tier would make the\nlower boxes unreachable while any tier-1 box stayed healthy."},"1685":{"id":"/litellm-routing-105","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#one-group-three-deployments","content":"Spreading across every reachable box is the point of running three of them, five full\n262144-token streams each. Pinning only narrows the candidate list first."},"1686":{"id":"/litellm-routing-106","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#one-group-three-deployments","content":"One endpoint per box, one box per endpoint. Never point two deployments, or two\naliases, at the same physical machine: the proxy counts deployments, not machines."},"1687":{"id":"/litellm-routing-107","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"`least-busy` picks the healthy deployment carrying the fewest in-flight requests."},"1688":{"id":"/litellm-routing-108","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"Its counters are per deployment and expire after a hardcoded 3600 s; there is no\n`routing_strategy_args` block to set, and `ttl` there would be a paste from newer docs."},"1689":{"id":"/litellm-routing-109","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"It decides only for unpinned traffic: the affinity pin below resolves first, and\n`least-busy` runs when nothing is pinned, when the pinned deployment is unhealthy or in\ncooldown, and for any request with no session id. Every `agent-swarm`,\n`agent-explore`, `agent-review` and `agent-swarm-fast` request is unpinned traffic,\nwhich is the whole of those four lanes."},"1690":{"id":"/litellm-routing-110","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"Health checks, cooldowns and those counters all attribute per deployment through\n`model_info.id`, which is why each box carries a unique id: `agent-spark-1`,\n`agent-spark-2`, `agent-spark-3`. Each thinking group, the swarm group, the two\nfan-out groups and the two fast groups carry their own triple -\n`agent-low-spark-1/2/3`,\n`agent-medium-spark-1/2/3`,\n`agent-xhigh-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-swarm-spark-1/2/3`,\n`agent-explore-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-review-spark-1/2/3`,\n`agent-xhigh-fast-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-swarm-fast-spark-1/2/3` - so the same physical\nbox is a separate deployment, with its own health state and its own in-flight counter,\ninside every group it belongs to. `compaction-fast` needs no triple: the Arc is one box\nand its single deployment is `compaction-fast-arc`."},"1691":{"id":"/litellm-routing-111","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"A cooled box therefore stays visibly out of `healthy_deployments` instead of blending\ninto the group's aggregate stats."},"1692":{"id":"/litellm-routing-112","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"`session_affinity` sits above the picker, in `router_settings.optional_pre_call_checks`,\nand it arms the router's `DeploymentAffinityCheck`. Checked on LiteLLM 1.102.1."},"1693":{"id":"/litellm-routing-113","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"One conversation from one client sticks to the box that answered its first turn, for as\nlong as that box stays healthy."},"1694":{"id":"/litellm-routing-114","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"The pin idles out after 1 h (3600 s) without a request, and every request refreshes it.\nIt bounds idle time between requests, not conversation length."},"1695":{"id":"/litellm-routing-115","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"A conversation that goes quiet for longer than the TTL may move."},"1696":{"id":"/litellm-routing-116","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"A busy pinned box sheds its own overflow in its own scheduler instead of queueing\ninside the proxy."},"1697":{"id":"/litellm-routing-117","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"The trade is deliberate: stickiness buys prefix-cache locality and one predictable box\nfor the whole conversation, so one workspace's agent loop can sit on a busy box."},"1698":{"id":"/litellm-routing-118","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-swarm-lane-agent-swarm","content":"The fifth Spark group, and the first of the four that carry no pin. Same\n`openai/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` on the same three boxes, same baked `xhigh`, same tool-calling\nflags and 262144 window - the\ndifference is routing state only, so no pin resolves and every request goes to the global\n`least-busy` picker, landing on the healthy box with the fewest requests in flight."},"1699":{"id":"/litellm-routing-119","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-swarm-lane-agent-swarm","content":"One key lifts the pin; apart from that key and the group's own deployment, retry-policy\nand fallback entries, nothing in the config distinguishes it from the five pinned lanes:"},"1700":{"id":"/litellm-routing-120","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-swarm-lane-agent-swarm","content":"At the pinned `v1.102.1` tag that key is a real `Router.__init__` argument and is not in\n`ROUTER_SETTINGS_MANAGED_OUTSIDE_CONFIG`, so `config.yaml` reaches it, and its valid flags\nare exactly `deployment_affinity`, `responses_api_deployment_check`, `session_affinity`\nand `encrypted_content_affinity`. An entry REPLACES the global flag list for the group it\nnames rather than adding to it, so the empty list means no affinity check of any kind runs\nfor `agent-swarm`. Every group absent from the map keeps falling back to the global\n`[\"session_affinity\"]`, which is why the five lanes a conversation rides still pin exactly\nas they did. Setting the key also self-arms the affinity callback, so those groups keep\npinning."},"1701":{"id":"/litellm-routing-121","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-swarm-lane-agent-swarm","content":"This key is the opt-out; a client header is not. An empty or short `x-litellm-session-id`\ndoes not skip the pin at this tag - that header is read verbatim at any length, and only\nthe generic `x-<vendor>-session-id` family is shape-checked - so a caller that tried to\nspread by sending an empty value would collapse every swarm request onto one shared pin."},"1702":{"id":"/litellm-routing-122","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-swarm-lane-agent-swarm","content":"Two facts not to get wrong:"},"1703":{"id":"/litellm-routing-123","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-swarm-lane-agent-swarm","content":"It has no pin, and that is what makes it the wrong home for a long single-thread\nconversation. Pinning buys prefix-cache locality; without it the next turn may be\nprefilled on a different box and pay prefill again. A chat, an orchestrator, or\nanything whose turns share a warm prefix belongs on `agent`. The lane is for\nindependent parallel work - subagents, batch edits, one-shot jobs, which is also why\nthe two reader lanes below lift the pin the same way."},"1704":{"id":"/litellm-routing-124","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-swarm-lane-agent-swarm","content":"The spread is bought by removing a pin, not by adding a proxy queue or a cap. There is\nstill no `rpm`, `tpm` or `max_parallel_requests` anywhere in the config, and\n`queue_class`, `queue_class_config` and `enable_queue` do not exist at this tag. A\nsaturated box keeps shedding load in its own scheduler; the proxy parks nothing behind\nan in-memory semaphore."},"1705":{"id":"/litellm-routing-125","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-swarm-lane-agent-swarm","content":"That is the arithmetic the lane exists for: 3 boxes x `PARALLEL` 5 full-length streams is\n15 concurrent streams, and a pinned workspace only ever reaches the 5 of the one box its\nkey resolves to."},"1706":{"id":"/litellm-routing-126","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fan-out-lanes-agent-explore-and-agent-review","content":"The sixth and seventh Spark groups, `agent-explore` and `agent-review`, lift the same pin\nthe swarm lane lifts, and for the same reason taken one step further: both serve a child\nthat answers once. The `explore` type and the seeded `explorer` agent dig for evidence and\nreturn a digest; `reviewer` and `security-auditor` read what landed and return findings.\nNeither carries a long warm prefix into a next turn, so the prefix-cache warmth the pin\nprotects is worth nothing here, while its cost is paid in full - the parent loop and every\nchild it spawns carry that workspace's one `x-litellm-session-id`, so a pinned wave of six\nresolves to a single deployment and waits on that box's `PARALLEL` admission slots while\nthe other two boxes sit reachable and under-offered."},"1707":{"id":"/litellm-routing-127","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fan-out-lanes-agent-explore-and-agent-review","content":"That check is a panel of three skeptics dispatched in one turn - the requirements angle,\nthe proof angle that re-runs the producers' own `done:` commands, and the adversary angle -\neach riding `agent-review`, each owning no file, and each allowed up to three read-only\nmappers of its own under `max_depth` 2. Three spawners with three mappers each is twelve of\nthe fifteen streams the fleet owns, which fits beside a parent still holding its own\nconversation, and the panel is cheap precisely because the writers have all landed by the\ntime it runs: the fleet is then idle, and both the skeptics and the mappers they spawn are\nunpinned traffic that `least-busy` can place on all three boxes at once."},"1708":{"id":"/litellm-routing-128","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fan-out-lanes-agent-explore-and-agent-review","content":"The baked level is the only thing that separates them. An explore child is a lookup, so it\ntakes `low` and answers fast; a review child is the check that a landed claim is actually\ntrue, so it takes the `xhigh` `agent` itself carries. Each names itself with an empty flag\nlist beside the swarm lane's (`litellm/config.yaml:721-729`) and declares its own six-key\n`model_group_retry_policy` block, because a group inherits nothing from its siblings."},"1709":{"id":"/litellm-routing-129","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fan-out-lanes-agent-explore-and-agent-review","content":"What the lift costs is what it costs the swarm lane, said plainly: a second turn of the\nsame conversation is not sticky, so it may be prefilled on a different box and pay prefill\nagain. For a child that answers once there is no second turn to pay for; point a\nconversation at these aliases and there is. A long single-thread chat, an orchestrator, or\nanything whose turns share a warm prefix belongs on `agent`."},"1710":{"id":"/litellm-routing-130","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fan-out-lanes-agent-explore-and-agent-review","content":"Each reader lane is the default for its own role and is set by its own template\nvariable, so either rolls back alone without moving the other or the writers'\n`agent-swarm` default:"},"1711":{"id":"/litellm-routing-131","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fan-out-lanes-agent-explore-and-agent-review","content":"| Lane            | Role it carries                                       | Default         | `--var` that puts it back on a pinned lane                                                                 |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-explore` | the built-in `explore` type and the seeded `explorer` | `agent-explore` | `--var grok_explore_lane=agent-low` keeps the level and the pin; `=agent` sends mapping work at full depth |","table":"table-1"},"1712":{"id":"/litellm-routing-132","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fan-out-lanes-agent-explore-and-agent-review","content":"| Lane           | Role it carries                                                                                         | Default        | `--var` that puts it back on a pinned lane                                                            |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-review` | the seeded `reviewer` and `security-auditor`, which carry the independent review of a multi-file change | `agent-review` | `--var grok_review_lane=agent-xhigh` keeps the level and the pin; `=agent` matches the parent exactly |","table":"table-1"},"1713":{"id":"/litellm-routing-133","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fan-out-lanes-agent-explore-and-agent-review","content":"The variables are declared in `templates/workspace/coder.tf:24-52` and its devcontainer\ntwin, and they are what fills `[subagents.models]` in\n`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl`. The fan-out width rides a different variable\nand does not follow any of these three: `grok_swarm_width` (default 15) is the width on\nevery lane, and `main.tf` renders it as exactly\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent = var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent != null ? var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent : var.grok_swarm_width`.\nThe lane decides placement, not width - what a pinned lane changes is where those 15 land,\nand one box only ever admits `PARALLEL` (5) of them. The workspace side of that seam is\ngrok-build-and-agents.md. A contributor home\nnames the same three lanes with `GROK_EXPLORE_LANE`, `GROK_PLAN_LANE` and\n`GROK_REVIEW_LANE` for `//scripts:seed-dev-env`. Neither lane is registered as a Coder\nAgents chat model, so a pin is the only way onto either one."},"1714":{"id":"/litellm-routing-134","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"The eighth and ninth Spark groups, and the only two that ask the proxy to answer one of\ntheir calls somewhere else. Everything else about each is its parent: `agent-xhigh-fast` is\n`agent-xhigh` - three equal `openai/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` deployments, `xhigh` baked, the\nsession pin kept - and `agent-swarm-fast` is `agent-swarm`, the same three boxes with the\npin lifted for it alone among the new lanes. The nine Spark lane groups are now\n`agent agent-low agent-medium agent-xhigh agent-swarm agent-explore agent-review\nagent-xhigh-fast agent-swarm-fast`, plus the Arc group `compaction-fast`."},"1715":{"id":"/litellm-routing-135","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"What the hop is: on those two aliases only, the proxy recognises the CLI's\nconversation-summary call and re-serves it from somewhere cheaper than the Spark box it\narrived on - `compaction-fast`, the small Ollama-served coding weight on the Arc, when the\nsummary fits that window, and a Spark group thinking at `low` when it does not. That is\nwhere the speed comes from - the Spark lane answers at xhigh thinking and a summary does\nnot need it. The CLI appends its summary instruction as the last turn of the body - the\nauto-compaction ask, or the `/compact` one wrapped in a `<summary_request>` tag - and the\ncallback matches a stable clause inside whichever one it sees,\n`produce a faithful, concise summary of the conversation so far` and\n`summarize the conversation so far`, in a turn short enough to be the instruction itself.\nIt reads that ask nowhere else in the body: the system prompt rides every turn of the\nsession, so matching there would move a whole conversation to the summary target the moment\nan operator pasted one of those sentences into a workspace rulebook. It reads it from\nwhichever key the protocol carries the turns under - `messages` for Chat Completions and\nAnthropic Messages, `input` for a model whose `api_backend` is `responses` - so the hop does\nnot quietly stop working on a protocol choice. A recognized ask is\nrewritten to the target model, with `tools`, `tool_choice` and `reasoning_effort` dropped\nfrom it: a summary is prose, the schemas are dead weight to it, and a small model handed a\ntool schema may answer with a call instead of a summary. Every other call of the same\nconversation stays on its Spark box, and a session on any other alias compacts exactly as\nit always has. The hop is a property of the alias, not of the proxy: picking one of these\ntwo aliases is the opt-in, and `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION=0` is the kill switch beside it. One\nresidue is worth naming: a user turn whose entire content is a quoted ask looks like an ask,\nand gets the summary target's answer."},"1716":{"id":"/litellm-routing-136","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"What it is not: a client setting. The Grok Build CLI has no compaction-model key, so there\nis nothing in `~/.grok/config.toml` that points that call anywhere, and\n`[models] session_summary` is not the compaction lane either - it serves titles and the\nsession recap. The whole remedy therefore lives server-side, and the measurement behind it\nis the `compact failed: exceeded wall-clock budget 300s` section of\ntroubleshooting.md."},"1717":{"id":"/litellm-routing-137","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"Why the extra config is worth it: compactions that did succeed on the Spark lane took 107 s\nto 1915 s, and 18 of the 36 sampled successes ran past the CLI's 300 s wall-clock budget -\nthey are the summaries that budget was discarding. What the hop changes without a stopwatch\nis who answers: a 14B Arc weight with no thinking level and no schemas in the prompt, rather\nthan a Spark box thinking at xhigh over the same request with its tool schemas attached. No\ntiming has been measured on the hop yet, so no speed-up figure is quoted here for either\ntier; `docs/troubleshooting.md` names the one log line that shows it firing, and that line\nis where a measurement starts."},"1718":{"id":"/litellm-routing-138","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"Which tier answers: the guard is `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` (default 24000), and\nit picks a tier rather than ending the hop. Below it the summary goes to `compaction-fast`,\nwhich is the right place for it, because those weights answer inside a 32768-token window\nand Ollama clamps an over-long input instead of refusing it - a summary pushed in over that\ncomes back quietly truncated, and a truncated summary is worse than the slower one it would\nreplace. That window is also the ceiling the guard cannot raise past: the fork happens at the\nsmaller of `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` and the `max_input_tokens` `config.yaml`\ndeclares for the Arc target group, so `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT=100000` forks at\n32768 and logs one `compaction router: EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT=100000 is above\nthe 32768 tokens config.yaml declares for 'compaction-fast'; forking at the declared window\ninstead` line. The declared window is a fact about the weights and the pinned LiteLLM has no\ncontext-window check between the hook and Ollama, so a knob cannot reach past it; a target\ngroup that declares no window leaves the guard whole, and prints one\n`compaction router: 'compaction-fast' declares no max_input_tokens in config.yaml; forking at\nEVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT alone` line rather than failing quietly.\nAbove it the summary goes to `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` (default\n`agent-low`): the same three Spark boxes, the 262144-token window a real summary needs, and\nthe thinking baked to `low` instead of `xhigh`. Blank that knob, or name the lane the call\narrived on, and an oversized summary stays where it is, which is the pre-hop behaviour. The\nestimate is counted over everything the backend prefills - each turn's text, the payloads\nthe text flattener cannot see, and every request-level schema key - and it charges three\nclasses of character separately: ASCII prose at four characters to a token, a base64 payload\nat two, and anything outside ASCII at one. Reading those denser classes at the prose rate was\nhow a 90,000-character screenshot read as 22,500 tokens and went to a 32768-token model.\nEvery one of them errs toward the larger number, and the larger number means the Spark tier."},"1719":{"id":"/litellm-routing-139","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"Where the guard sits against the client: the shipped `[session]` block sets\n`auto_compact_threshold_percent = 70` and both fast aliases declare a `context_window` of\n262144 (`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:387`, and `:284` and `:301` for the two\naliases), so a session on either one reaches its auto-compaction at about 183,500 tokens -\nover seven times the guard. A summary that size cannot be read faithfully by a 32768-token\nmodel, so the Arc tier was never going to serve it and the guard is what stops it trying: on\nthat estimate the call goes to `agent-low`, which keeps the window and drops the baked\n`xhigh` thinking. Pointing `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_TARGET` at `agent-low` is the lever that\ncovers a full-size summary even with the limit left alone, and it is a routing choice with\nits own trade, not a free win: the summary is still a Spark call, holding a stream on a box\nthe writers want, at a thinking level turned down from the one the lane exists to bake."},"1720":{"id":"/litellm-routing-140","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"The known limit on that tier is its fallback, and it is the stack's standing one: every\nSpark group's last resort is the Arc, so `agent-low: [\"coder\"]`\n(`litellm/config.yaml:862`, and the same shape under `agent-xhigh-fast: [\"agent-xhigh\",\n\"coder\"]` and `agent-swarm-fast: [\"agent-swarm\", \"coder\"]`) means that when all three Sparks\nare cooled an oversized summary lands on `coder` - a 32768-token group - and is answered\nfrom a truncated reading, which is the outcome the fork exists to prevent. Nothing here\nmakes that worse: the pair's own chains end at `coder` too, and so does\n`compaction-fast: [\"agent-low\", \"coder\"]`. The fork changes who tries first. What an operator\nholds is the choice of who tries second: blank `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` to leave\nthe summary on the lane it arrived on, point it at another Spark group, or edit the fallback\nlist beside it in the same routing file. When the Sparks are unreachable the answer is the\none every agent lane already has - the working degraded mode is `chat`."},"1721":{"id":"/litellm-routing-141","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"What has been measured is the old path, not the new one. The 107 s to 1915 s spread and the\n18 abandoned successes above are both from the Spark lane before the hop existed, swept out\nof the session logs in troubleshooting.md. Neither tier has a timing\nof its own yet: the hop has not served a summary on a running stack, so no speed-up figure\nis claimed for either one. `tests/python/test_compaction_router.py` proves which group each\nrequest shape lands on; the operator's own log line is what will prove what it costs."},"1722":{"id":"/litellm-routing-142","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"Everything a caller can tune is one of five environment variables, read in\n`litellm/compaction_router.py` and nowhere else, which makes the litellm service's\n`environment:` block in `docker-compose.yml` the whole policy:"},"1723":{"id":"/litellm-routing-143","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"| Knob                     | Default                            | What flipping it does                                                           |\n| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION` | on; `0`, `false`, `off` disable it | the kill switch - off, every call of every lane stays where its alias routes it |","table":"table-2"},"1724":{"id":"/litellm-routing-144","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"| Knob                           | Default                             | What flipping it does                                  |\n| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_LANES` | `agent-xhigh-fast,agent-swarm-fast` | which groups may hop; name one to narrow the hop to it |","table":"table-2"},"1725":{"id":"/litellm-routing-145","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"| Knob                            | Default           | What flipping it does                                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_TARGET` | `compaction-fast` | the model group a summary under the guard is moved to; a name that is no `model_name:` in `config.yaml` is refused and the call stays put |","table":"table-2"},"1726":{"id":"/litellm-routing-146","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"| Knob                                 | Default | What flipping it does                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` | `24000` | the token estimate that decides which of the two tiers takes the call; a ceiling this module may lower and never raise, because the Arc target group's own `max_input_tokens` caps it and logs one line when it does |","table":"table-2"},"1727":{"id":"/litellm-routing-147","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"| Knob                                  | Default     | What flipping it does                                                                                                                          |\n| ------------------------------------- | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` | `agent-low` | where a summary above the guard goes, checked against `config.yaml` the same way; blank it, or name the lane it arrived on, and it stays there |","table":"table-2"},"1728":{"id":"/litellm-routing-148","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"Three ways to land this feature and get nothing. The first is caught for you at the pinned\nLiteLLM and the other two are silent, which is why the silent two are what the gates check:"},"1729":{"id":"/litellm-routing-149","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"`litellm_settings.callbacks` must name the *instance*,\n`compaction_router.proxy_handler_instance`, never the class. `ProxyLogging` dispatches\n`async_pre_call_hook` only to a callback that IS a `CustomLogger` and overrides that hook\nin its own class dict. At the pinned `v1.102.1` a class entry fails loudly:\n`litellm/proxy/common_utils/callback_utils.py` maps it to a `ValueError` that names\n`proxy_handler_instance` and the config does not load. That check is recent, so the older\nwording you may find in a comment - a class boots clean, the lanes keep working, the hop\nnever fires - describes what it used to do."},"1730":{"id":"/litellm-routing-150","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"The module is mounted as a single file,\n`./litellm/compaction_router.py:/app/compaction_router.py:ro`. A directory mount over\n`/app` hides `config.yaml` beside it, and it has to sit in the same directory as the\nconfig file because that is the directory the proxy resolves a callback's dotted name\nagainst - the bare module name in\n`callbacks` resolves no other way."},"1731":{"id":"/litellm-routing-151","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-fast-compaction-lanes-agent-xhigh-fast-and-agent-swarm-fast","content":"A new group inherits neither the retry policy nor the affinity lift. Both fast lanes\ndeclare their own six-key `model_group_retry_policy` block, and only `agent-swarm-fast`\njoins `model_group_affinity_config` with `[]`; `agent-xhigh-fast` is absent from that map\non purpose, so it keeps falling back to the global `[\"session_affinity\"]` and stays\npinned like its parent. That one key is the whole difference between the pinned twin and\nthe spread one."},"1732":{"id":"/litellm-routing-152","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"A value has to look like an id to count: alphanumerics, hyphens or underscores, 8\ncharacters or more. The sources, in order, are `x-litellm-trace-id`, then\n`x-litellm-session-id`, then any `x-<vendor>-session-id`, then\n`session-id`/`session_id`/`thread-id`/`conversation_id` (Codex style), then W3C\ntraceparent and baggage, plus `metadata.session_id` in the body."},"1733":{"id":"/litellm-routing-153","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"The Grok CLI forwards `extra_headers` verbatim."},"1734":{"id":"/litellm-routing-154","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"Both workspace templates render `x-litellm-session-id = \"ws-<workspace-uuid>\"` under\n`[models]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`, so one key covers every conversation of a\nworkspace, subagent calls included."},"1735":{"id":"/litellm-routing-155","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"In `shared` profile mode that is one file per workspace, re-rendered on that workspace's own\nstart, so two workspaces of one owner can carry different renders of that key."},"1736":{"id":"/litellm-routing-156","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-floats-freely","content":"Cline and Roo editor traffic carries no session id: the extension settings have no\ncustom-header field."},"1737":{"id":"/litellm-routing-157","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-floats-freely","content":"Coder Agents chats carry none either. Coder's aibridge forwards model calls with the\nprovider key only and adds no per-conversation session id."},"1738":{"id":"/litellm-routing-158","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-floats-freely","content":"Both float across the healthy boxes by load."},"1739":{"id":"/litellm-routing-159","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-pinned-box-cools-or-goes-unhealthy","content":"That request routes elsewhere, to whichever box `least-busy` picks."},"1740":{"id":"/litellm-routing-160","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-pinned-box-cools-or-goes-unhealthy","content":"The pin itself is kept."},"1741":{"id":"/litellm-routing-161","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-pinned-box-cools-or-goes-unhealthy","content":"The conversation goes back to the box once it is healthy again."},"1742":{"id":"/litellm-routing-162","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-the-spark-lanes-to-coder","content":"When every Spark box is down, `router_settings.fallbacks` degrades `agent` straight to\n`coder`, which is text-only."},"1743":{"id":"/litellm-routing-163","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-the-spark-lanes-to-coder","content":"Chat stays usable while agent mode quietly loses its tools. That is the point of the\nhop, not an accident."},"1744":{"id":"/litellm-routing-164","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-the-spark-lanes-to-coder","content":"Each thinking alias carries the same hop of its own - `agent-low: [\"coder\"]`, and the\nsame for `agent-medium` and `agent-xhigh`. Groups are independent, so none of them\ninherits `agent`'s entry, and the five pinned lanes go down with the same boxes."},"1745":{"id":"/litellm-routing-165","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-the-spark-lanes-to-coder","content":"`agent-swarm` degrades one step further: `agent-swarm: [\"agent\", \"coder\"]`. Health\nstate, cooldowns and in-flight counters are per group, so its own three deployments can\nall be cooled while the same boxes are still healthy under `agent`; `agent` is tried\nfirst, where `least-busy` may still have a box to pick, and Arc `coder` stays the last\nresort it is for every other Spark lane. The chain is acyclic: agent-swarm -> agent ->\ncoder, and `coder` declares no hop of its own."},"1746":{"id":"/litellm-routing-166","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-the-spark-lanes-to-coder","content":"The two fan-out lanes degrade the way the swarm lane does, one step further than the\npinned lanes, and each hops through the lane that bakes its own level:\n`agent-explore: [\"agent-low\", \"coder\"]` and `agent-review: [\"agent-xhigh\", \"coder\"]`\n(`litellm/config.yaml:882-883`). The extra step is worth taking for the same reason -\nthese groups' own three deployments can all be cooling while the same boxes are healthy\nunder the pinned alias, since health state and cooldowns are kept per group - and the\nhop is chosen so a parent that dispatched an explore child at `low` gets a `low` answer\nfrom the fallback rather than an `xhigh` it did not ask for. Both chains are acyclic and\nend at `coder`: agent-explore -> agent-low -> coder, agent-review -> agent-xhigh ->\ncoder, and neither fallback lane names a lane that hops back here."},"1747":{"id":"/litellm-routing-167","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-the-spark-lanes-to-coder","content":"Each fast lane hops to the lane it is the twin of:\n`- agent-xhigh-fast: [\"agent-xhigh\", \"coder\"]` and\n`- agent-swarm-fast: [\"agent-swarm\", \"coder\"]`\n(`litellm/config.yaml:887-888`). Same boxes, same baked level, same pin behaviour, so a\nsession that lost its own group keeps everything except that group's routing state. Both\nchains are acyclic and end at `coder` like every other here: agent-xhigh-fast ->\nagent-xhigh -> coder, agent-swarm-fast -> agent-swarm -> coder."},"1748":{"id":"/litellm-routing-168","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-the-spark-lanes-to-coder","content":"`compaction-fast` degrades to a Spark rather than to another Arc alias -\n`- compaction-fast: [\"agent-low\", \"coder\"]`\n(`litellm/config.yaml:893`) - and that direction is the point. `coder`, `coder-fast` and\n`compaction-fast` all answer from the one Ollama container on the Arc, so a hop between\nthem is a hop to whatever is already down. `agent-low` is the quickest answer the fleet\nhas, which is what a summary was after on this lane in the first place. Acyclic:\ncompaction-fast -> agent-low -> coder. It is the one chain here that a user never picks:\nit belongs to the group the callback moves traffic to, not to an alias anyone types."},"1749":{"id":"/litellm-routing-169","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-the-spark-lanes-to-coder","content":"It used to be a chain, `agent -> agent-fast -> coder`. `agent-fast` was a second alias\nfor the same model on a second box with a lower output ceiling; one group of three equal\ndeployments already covers every reachable box, so the chain collapsed to one acyclic\nhop."},"1750":{"id":"/litellm-routing-170","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-the-spark-lanes-to-coder","content":"If a concurrency cap is ever reintroduced, its pairing is `fallbacks` plus\n`context_window_fallbacks`. Both, not one."},"1751":{"id":"/litellm-routing-171","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-the-spark-lanes-to-coder","content":"There are no rpm, tpm or `max_parallel_requests` caps anywhere, on purpose. A cap parks\nrequests behind an in-memory semaphore inside the proxy; without one, a fully offered\nbox sheds load in its own scheduler."},"1752":{"id":"/litellm-routing-172","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-agent-backend-card","content":"The dashboard's **Agent backend** card exists to surface exactly that failure."},"1753":{"id":"/litellm-routing-173","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-agent-backend-card","content":"Its per-box widgets go to error state while `agent` still answers, because `agent`\nanswers from the Arc while every Spark is down."},"1754":{"id":"/litellm-routing-174","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-agent-backend-card","content":"The card and its per-box widgets read `AGENT_MODEL_URL` and `SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` from\n`.env`, and compose builds both from the box's `SPARKn_HOST` plus `SPARK_HTTP_PORT`,\nso a card follows the hostname you set once and they answer without a key."},"1755":{"id":"/litellm-routing-175","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"The `x-litellm-model-id` response header names the deployment that answered."},"1756":{"id":"/litellm-routing-176","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"With a pin, it names the pinned box; without one, whichever box `least-busy` picked."},"1757":{"id":"/litellm-routing-177","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"The proxy's own `/health` lists every deployment, so trust it over the absence of\nboot errors."},"1758":{"id":"/litellm-routing-178","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"A wrong model id does not crash the proxy: 1.102.1 builds the Router with\n`ignore_invalid_deployments` forced to True, so a bad id drops that deployment at boot\nwith one log line and later surfaces as \"No deployments available\"."},"1759":{"id":"/litellm-routing-179","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"Measured on a harness boot of this config against the live Spark boxes, before\n`session_affinity` existed."},"1760":{"id":"/litellm-routing-180","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"That 30-request burst was multi-session, fan-out-style traffic."},"1761":{"id":"/litellm-routing-181","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"It spread 12/10/8 across the three deployments and returned 30x 200."},"1762":{"id":"/litellm-routing-182","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"With no concurrency cap configured, the proxy neither queued nor shed inside itself,\nand a fully offered box absorbed the overflow in its own scheduler."},"1763":{"id":"/litellm-routing-183","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"A single conversation pinned to one box now keeps its streams on that box while it\nstays healthy - that is the behaviour the burst predates."},"1764":{"id":"/litellm-routing-184","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Edit `litellm/config.yaml`."},"1765":{"id":"/litellm-routing-185","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Restart the proxy on the stack you changed, so it re-reads that file."},"1766":{"id":"/litellm-routing-186","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Check that `SPARK_1_API_KEY`, `SPARK_2_API_KEY` and `SPARK_3_API_KEY` are still\nnon-empty. An empty key fails every request inside the proxy, before it ever reaches\nthe box."},"1767":{"id":"/litellm-routing-187","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Order matters when an alias is new rather than changed: the proxy reads\n`litellm/config.yaml` at boot and a workspace renders its catalog from that file when it\nstarts, so restart the proxy first and boot the workspace after it. A workspace older than\nthe proxy asks for an alias the config does not have, and the router answers \"No\ndeployments available\" rather than falling back. The rollback is one variable per lane, and\nit does not need the proxy at all: `--var grok_subagents_lane=agent` re-pins the writers to\none box, and `--var grok_explore_lane=agent-low` and `--var grok_review_lane=agent` put each\nreader role back on a pinned alias that every config version already carries."},"1768":{"id":"/litellm-routing-188","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Neither stack runner has a `restart` subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`\nare the whole set), so the proxy restart stays a direct compose call. Stable stack:"},"1769":{"id":"/litellm-routing-189","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"The dev sandbox takes the same restart with its own project name and env file:"},"1770":{"id":"/litellm-routing-190","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (the default, with `chat` standing in when the Spark lane is dark),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, `agent-swarm`, and `chat` as the Coder Agents\nchat model configs, through `CODER_AGENTS_PROVIDER_URL`\n(default `http://litellm:4000/v1`). `agent-swarm` is registerable but is never picked\nfor an auxiliary lane pin: the control-plane chat agent is one conversation, and one\nconversation wants the pin."},"1771":{"id":"/litellm-routing-191","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"The two fan-out lanes are absent from that list on purpose. `agent-explore` and\n`agent-review` exist to be pinned by `[subagents.models]`, not chosen from a picker, so\nediting them moves a fleet role's traffic and leaves the Agents dropdowns alone."},"1772":{"id":"/litellm-routing-192","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"The two fast-compaction lanes are absent for a mechanical reason of the same kind: the\nhop keys on the summary instruction the Grok CLI appends, and the aibridge behind those\ndropdowns never sends one, so an Agents chat on `agent-xhigh-fast` is a chat on\n`agent-xhigh` under a longer name. They are a CLI and a workspace-form choice instead."},"1773":{"id":"/litellm-routing-193","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"A new alias is a client catalog entry as well as a proxy entry. Both `templates/*/main.tf`\ntwins carry one `{value, name, description}` object per alias in `local.model_choices`,\nwhich is what the workspace create form offers for `grok_default_model`, and both\n`templates/*/grok-config.toml.tftpl` twins carry its `[model.\"<alias>\"]` block. An alias\nthe config has and the twins do not is an alias no workspace ever lists."},"1774":{"id":"/litellm-routing-194","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"The editor roster is the third copy of the name. `__evoT1StackModels` in both\n`templates/*/settings.json.tftpl` twins is the alias list Cline and Roo/Kilo are offered,\nand that seed is copied onto the per-owner `vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume only while\nthe file is absent - a roster that gains an id never reaches an owner whose\n`settings.json` already exists, so re-seeding means deleting that volume's file."},"1775":{"id":"/litellm-routing-195","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"A routing hook adds two artifacts beyond the alias itself. `litellm/compaction_router.py`\nis a payload file beside the config, bind-mounted single-file as\n`./litellm/compaction_router.py:/app/compaction_router.py:ro` - never a directory mount\nover `/app`, which would hide `config.yaml` - and it is live only while\n`litellm_settings.callbacks` names `compaction_router.proxy_handler_instance`, the\ninstance rather than the class."},"1776":{"id":"/litellm-routing-196","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"Both of those live in `docker-compose.yml` and `litellm/config.yaml` respectively, so\nthey restart differently: `restart litellm` re-reads the config file, while a new mount\nand the five `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION*` keys need the container recreated with the same\ncompose invocation and `up -d litellm`."},"1777":{"id":"/litellm-routing-197","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"So an alias edited or deleted in `litellm/config.yaml` moves in the Agents model\ndropdowns exactly as it does in the CLI."},"1778":{"id":"/litellm-routing-198","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"The IPEX-LLM runtime is Intel's XPU path (oneAPI / Level Zero) for the Arc stack."},"1779":{"id":"/litellm-routing-199","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"`OLLAMA_NUM_GPU=999` offloads every layer to the 140T, and the iGPU pulls its weights\nstraight out of the 96 GB DDR5."},"1780":{"id":"/litellm-routing-200","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"Vanilla Ollama on an Intel iGPU is effectively CPU-bound, and several times slower on a\n32B coder model."},"1781":{"id":"/litellm-routing-201","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in the ollama logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."},"1782":{"id":"/litellm-routing-202","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#moe-over-dense","content":"`qwen2.5-coder:32b` is treated here as a Mixture-of-Experts model: 32B-class quality\nwith far fewer active parameters per token."},"1783":{"id":"/litellm-routing-203","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#moe-over-dense","content":"On a memory-rich but bandwidth- and latency-constrained iGPU that is the sweet spot,\nbecause an equivalent dense model would move every parameter per token and run much\nslower at the same footprint."},"1784":{"id":"/litellm-routing-204","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-one-front-door-rule","content":"Workspaces see a stable OpenAI-compatible URL and stable alias names."},"1785":{"id":"/litellm-routing-205","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-one-front-door-rule","content":"So you can flip `coder` from the local Arc to a Spark box, or add a new alias, and no\nworkspace setting has to change."},"1786":{"id":"/luks-unlock","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"page","content":"Unlock an encrypted host remotely","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock"},"1787":{"id":"/luks-unlock-0","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock","content":"Configure the stack host so a reboot with an encrypted root can be answered from a laptop, and prove the boot image can do it before you reboot."},"1788":{"id":"/luks-unlock-1","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"Unlock an encrypted host remotely"},"1789":{"id":"/luks-unlock-2","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#the-command-that-matters","content":"The command that matters"},"1790":{"id":"/luks-unlock-3","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"What install writes"},"1791":{"id":"/luks-unlock-4","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"Unlocking from a laptop"},"1792":{"id":"/luks-unlock-5","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"What the check catches"},"1793":{"id":"/luks-unlock-6","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#static-networks","content":"Static networks"},"1794":{"id":"/luks-unlock-7","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#two-keys-two-purposes","content":"Two keys, two purposes"},"1795":{"id":"/luks-unlock-8","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#what-this-deliberately-does-not-do","content":"What this deliberately does not do"},"1796":{"id":"/luks-unlock-9","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#where-to-look-next","content":"Where to look next"},"1797":{"id":"/luks-unlock-10","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1798":{"id":"/luks-unlock-11","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"Why a reboot is the dangerous moment on an encrypted headless box"},"1799":{"id":"/luks-unlock-12","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"The one command that answers \"can I unlock this from a laptop\""},"1800":{"id":"/luks-unlock-13","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"What `install` writes, and which file each line belongs to"},"1801":{"id":"/luks-unlock-14","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"The unlock itself, step by step, from the operator's own machine"},"1802":{"id":"/luks-unlock-15","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"What the check catches, and what it deliberately leaves alone"},"1803":{"id":"/luks-unlock-16","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"**What this enables**"},"1804":{"id":"/luks-unlock-17","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"Rebooting a server nobody is standing next to"},"1805":{"id":"/luks-unlock-18","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"Catching a config that looks right and boots dead, before the reboot"},"1806":{"id":"/luks-unlock-19","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"Reading the exact ssh command and host key fingerprint to expect"},"1807":{"id":"/luks-unlock-20","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"An Ubuntu server with a LUKS root stops in the initramfs on every reboot and\nwaits for a passphrase. With no keyboard attached, that wait is the outage: the\nmachine is up, the disks are unreadable, and the only fix is a person at the\nconsole. The distro answer is to move an SSH server and cryptsetup's unlock\nprompt into the initramfs, so the passphrase is typed over SSH from anywhere."},"1808":{"id":"/luks-unlock-21","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"`//scripts:luks-unlock` writes that config and then proves the generated\ninitramfs really contains the unlock path. The proof is the point: every one of\nthe six settings involved fails by staying silent until the boot that needs it."},"1809":{"id":"/luks-unlock-22","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#the-command-that-matters","content":"That is `check`, the default verb, and it writes nothing. It answers one\nquestion - if this box rebooted right now, could you decrypt it from a laptop -\nand closes with either `ok: this box can be unlocked from a laptop after a\nreboot` or `error: do not reboot yet - N blocker(s)`."},"1810":{"id":"/luks-unlock-23","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#the-command-that-matters","content":"Run it before every reboot of an encrypted box, and after any kernel or\n`initramfs-tools` update. It exits `1` when something blocks the unlock, so it\nfits in front of a maintenance script as a gate."},"1811":{"id":"/luks-unlock-24","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"`install` installs the two packages, writes the config, regenerates the\ninitramfs, and then runs the same check over what it produced. Add `--dry-run`\nfirst and every write and command is printed as `would: ...` instead of\nexecuted. Nothing reboots."},"1812":{"id":"/luks-unlock-25","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"| File                                  | Line it writes | Why it matters                                                                                              |\n| ------------------------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/etc/cryptsetup-initramfs/conf-hook` | `CRYPTSETUP=y` | Without it the cryptroot scripts never enter the boot image, so there is nothing to type a passphrase into. |","table":"table-0"},"1813":{"id":"/luks-unlock-26","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"| File                                         | Line it writes                                                          | Why it matters                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/etc/initramfs-tools/conf.d/zz-luks-unlock` | `DROPBEAR=y`, `IP=`, `UMASK=0077`, and `HOOKS=` when a list is in force | One file this CLI owns, named to sort last so it wins over every other drop-in. `IP=` is what gives the boot image an address; `UMASK=0077` keeps the packed key out of unprivileged hands. |","table":"table-0"},"1814":{"id":"/luks-unlock-27","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"| File                                      | Line it writes               | Why it matters                                                                                               |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `/etc/dropbear/initramfs/authorized_keys` | the operator's public key(s) | Password logins are off in the initramfs. No key there means the SSH server refuses everyone, including you. |","table":"table-0"},"1815":{"id":"/luks-unlock-28","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"| File                                    | Line it writes                              | Why it matters                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/etc/dropbear/initramfs/dropbear.conf` | `DROPBEAR_OPTIONS=\"-p 2222 -s -I 60 -j -k\"` | `-s` keeps password logins off, `-I` bounds a session someone walked away from, and the port stays off the installed sshd's so the different host keys do not look like an attack. An existing `DROPBEAR_OPTIONS` is left alone. |","table":"table-0"},"1816":{"id":"/luks-unlock-29","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"| File                                          | Line it writes                               | Why it matters                                                                                 |\n| --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/etc/dropbear/initramfs/dropbear_*_host_key` | generated with `dropbearkey` when none exist | dropbear refuses to start with no host key, and the package only creates them at install time. |","table":"table-0"},"1817":{"id":"/luks-unlock-30","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"The order is deliberate: the hook packs the authorized key at generation time,\nso a key written after `update-initramfs` is not in the image. That is why\n`install` ends by reading the image back."},"1818":{"id":"/luks-unlock-31","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"Pin the identity while the box is still up, so a stranger's key cannot be\nmistaken for it later:"},"1819":{"id":"/luks-unlock-32","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"Reboot the box."},"1820":{"id":"/luks-unlock-33","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"Wait a minute, then connect on the initramfs port. The check prints the\ncommand; it keeps its own `known_hosts` on purpose:"},"1821":{"id":"/luks-unlock-34","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"Type the passphrase:"},"1822":{"id":"/luks-unlock-35","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"The boot continues on its own once the mapping appears - no second reboot. If\nthe SSH connection is refused, the box is still in the initramfs or before it:\nthe boot does not proceed past an unmapped root, so waiting is safe and the\nconsole (out-of-band console, IPMI, or the provider's serial console) still\nshows where it stopped."},"1823":{"id":"/luks-unlock-36","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"Every row below is a `check` line you can get instead of an `ok`, and each one\nhas booted dead in the field."},"1824":{"id":"/luks-unlock-37","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                             | Cause                  | Fix                                                                                               |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `no initramfs address (IP is unset, none, or off)` | nothing ever set `IP=` | `install --ip dhcp`, or `--iface ens3` to pin one NIC, or a raw netboot spec for a static network |","table":"table-1"},"1825":{"id":"/luks-unlock-38","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                          | Cause                                                   | Fix                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HOOKS leaves out net dropbear` | a hand-written `HOOKS=` list that predates the packages | `install` merges the four names it needs into whatever list is in force |","table":"table-1"},"1826":{"id":"/luks-unlock-39","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                 | Cause                                                       | Fix                                                            |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `the boot image holds no bin/dropbear` | the package was installed after the last `update-initramfs` | re-run `install` so the image is regenerated after the install |","table":"table-1"},"1827":{"id":"/luks-unlock-40","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                             | Cause                                                                              | Fix                                                                                        |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `no authorized key was packed into the boot image` | the key was written after the image, or in a path the installed hook does not read | re-run `install`; the CLI asks the installed hook which path it reads rather than assuming |","table":"table-1"},"1828":{"id":"/luks-unlock-41","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                              | Cause                         | Fix                                          |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `the key body holds characters that are not base64` | a pasted key wrapped mid-body | re-paste on one line; `check` names the line |","table":"table-1"},"1829":{"id":"/luks-unlock-42","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                                          | Cause                                                          | Fix                                                                         |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `key file /etc/keys/root.key is not covered by KEYFILE_PATTERN` | the crypttab key lives on disk and never reaches the initramfs | add a `KEYFILE_PATTERN` to `conf-hook`, or move that volume to a passphrase |","table":"table-1"},"1830":{"id":"/luks-unlock-43","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                            | Cause                                                              | Fix                                                                             |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `MODULES=dep packs only drivers loaded right now` | the NIC driver is not loaded today, so it will be missing tomorrow | name the driver in `/etc/initramfs-tools/modules`, or go back to `MODULES=most` |","table":"table-1"},"1831":{"id":"/luks-unlock-44","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                                                 | Cause                                                 | Fix                                                                     |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `the initramfs listens on 2222, the same port the installed sshd uses` | the two daemons share a port with different host keys | pick another `--port`, and keep using the separate `UserKnownHostsFile` |","table":"table-1"},"1832":{"id":"/luks-unlock-45","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                             | Cause                                  | Fix                                              |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `an encrypted /boot needs GRUB to read the initrd` | `/boot` is inside the encrypted volume | verify GRUB can read it before trusting a reboot |","table":"table-1"},"1833":{"id":"/luks-unlock-46","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"The last one is worth dwelling on: `check` can read every config file and still\nbe looking at an image GRUB cannot load, so an encrypted `/boot` is reported as\na warning to verify by hand rather than cleared."},"1834":{"id":"/luks-unlock-47","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#static-networks","content":"A box with no DHCP gets the raw initramfs form, which is passed through\nuntouched because `initramfs-tools` parses it, not this CLI:"},"1835":{"id":"/luks-unlock-48","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#static-networks","content":"`--iface ens3` with `--ip dhcp` produces the same pinned-device shape with\nDHCP kept. A name the kernel never assigned is a blocker, not a typo you\ndiscover after the reboot: `check` compares it against `/sys/class/net`."},"1836":{"id":"/luks-unlock-49","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#two-keys-two-purposes","content":"The unlock key authorizes a root shell on a machine whose disks are still\nsealed, and the initramfs image holding it sits on an unencrypted `/boot`.\nAnyone who can read `/boot` can read that public key, so treat it as a\nseparate credential with its own purpose: a dedicated key whose private half\nlives on the laptop you bring to a reboot, not the key you push to every\nrepository. `UMASK=0077` keeps the image itself out of unprivileged hands, and\nthe initramfs host keys stay separate from `/etc/ssh` on purpose - they are\ngenerated per boot image, and sharing them would leak which key the sealed\nmachine answers to."},"1837":{"id":"/luks-unlock-50","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-this-deliberately-does-not-do","content":"**Reboot anything.** The reboot is the operator's decision, made after a\ngreen `check`."},"1838":{"id":"/luks-unlock-51","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-this-deliberately-does-not-do","content":"**Edit `/etc/crypttab` or the LUKS headers.** The volumes are read to decide\nwhether the boot can open them. Key slots, passphrases and headers are never\nwritten, so this CLI cannot be the thing that destroys a key."},"1839":{"id":"/luks-unlock-52","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-this-deliberately-does-not-do","content":"**Enroll a TPM.** `systemd-cryptenroll` is a different design with a\ndifferent failure mode - a firmware update that changes the PCR policy seals\nthe box with nobody in the loop - and it belongs in its own change."},"1840":{"id":"/luks-unlock-53","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-this-deliberately-does-not-do","content":"**Add an auto-reboot watchdog.** A box sitting in the initramfs with dropbear\nlistening is the recoverable state; rebooting on a timer destroys the\nrecovery window and can turn one bad boot into a loop. `-I` bounds the\nsession, and the listener stays."},"1841":{"id":"/luks-unlock-54","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#where-to-look-next","content":"troubleshooting.md for the symptom-first list once the\nbox is back up."},"1842":{"id":"/luks-unlock-55","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#where-to-look-next","content":"public-access.md for what the box exposes on the network\nafter it boots - the initramfs listener is gone by then."},"1843":{"id":"/luks-unlock-56","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#where-to-look-next","content":"operate/index.md for the rest of the day-to-day runbooks."},"1844":{"id":"/new-repo","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"page","content":"New projects from the template","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo"},"1845":{"id":"/new-repo-0","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo","content":"How scripts/new-repo.sh fetches the ezrepo scaffold from GitHub, renders it into a new repository, and what the Coder side adds."},"1846":{"id":"/new-repo-1","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"New projects from the template"},"1847":{"id":"/new-repo-2","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#two-kinds-of-template","content":"Two kinds of template"},"1848":{"id":"/new-repo-3","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"What the scaffold ships"},"1849":{"id":"/new-repo-4","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"Lanes"},"1850":{"id":"/new-repo-5","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"Rendering a repository"},"1851":{"id":"/new-repo-6","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"What each GitHub step needs"},"1852":{"id":"/new-repo-7","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"Coder side"},"1853":{"id":"/new-repo-8","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#where-this-is-enforced","content":"Where this is enforced"},"1854":{"id":"/new-repo-9","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1855":{"id":"/new-repo-10","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Two different things called \"template\", and which one this page is about"},"1856":{"id":"/new-repo-11","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"What the scaffold repository ships, and the account-wide gaps it closes"},"1857":{"id":"/new-repo-12","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Where the scaffold is fetched from, and how to pin or replace it"},"1858":{"id":"/new-repo-13","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Rendering a repository with `new-repo`, its flags, and its lanes"},"1859":{"id":"/new-repo-14","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Which GitHub steps need which App permission, and what the CLI does when one is refused"},"1860":{"id":"/new-repo-15","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"The `New project` workspace preset and the optional Coder Task"},"1861":{"id":"/new-repo-16","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Where all of this is enforced"},"1862":{"id":"/new-repo-17","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"**What this enables**"},"1863":{"id":"/new-repo-18","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Starting a new repository with the quality gate already in place, in one command"},"1864":{"id":"/new-repo-19","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Getting the same scaffold on a laptop, in CI, and from a workspace"},"1865":{"id":"/new-repo-20","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Pinning a scaffold revision, or rendering a local tree with no network at all"},"1866":{"id":"/new-repo-21","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Telling a refused GitHub permission apart from a broken render"},"1867":{"id":"/new-repo-22","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#two-kinds-of-template","content":"This repository already uses the word *template* for a **Coder template**: the Terraform tree\nunder `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/` that `push-template.sh` registers with\nthe Coder server."},"1868":{"id":"/new-repo-23","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#two-kinds-of-template","content":"The other *template* is a **project scaffold**: a tree of files that `scripts/new-repo.sh` copies,\nsubstitutes, and hands to `git init` as the first commit of a brand new repository. It is not\nvendored here. It lives in its own repository -- by default `https://github.com/toxicoder/ezrepo`\n-- and this repo's whole job is to fetch that tree and render it. The scaffold is a product with\nits own CI and its own release rhythm, and a vendored copy drifts from the day it is committed, so\nnothing here is kept in step with it."},"1869":{"id":"/new-repo-24","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#two-kinds-of-template","content":"The coupling runs one way: this repo may name the scaffold repository; the scaffold repository\nnever names this one. And the scaffold's quality gate is its own -- the `.self-check/` suite and\nthe CI that runs it in that repository -- so a scaffold change is tested where it is made, and a\nlane or a file the scaffold adds needs no change here."},"1870":{"id":"/new-repo-25","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"These files belong to the scaffold repository. This repo does not ship them, lint them, or keep a\ncopy: it fetches them at run time and renders them. The scaffold is language-neutral at the core,\nwith optional per-language lanes. Everything below was chosen from what is measurably missing\nacross the account, not from taste: across a sample of eleven ordinary projects, `SECURITY.md`,\n`CONTRIBUTING.md`, issue templates, a pull-request template, `dependabot.yml`, `CODEOWNERS` and\n`.editorconfig` were each present in none of them, and no repository in the surveyed set had a\nsingle release or tag."},"1871":{"id":"/new-repo-26","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area      | What ships                                                                                                                      |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Community | `SECURITY.md` with a supported-versions table and private-report route, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, `CHANGELOG.md` |","table":"table-0"},"1872":{"id":"/new-repo-27","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area   | What ships                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| GitHub | `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `release.yml`, `security.yml`, `dependabot.yml`, `CODEOWNERS`, issue templates with blank issues disabled, a PR template with a Safety/ops-impact section |","table":"table-0"},"1873":{"id":"/new-repo-28","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area    | What ships                                                                                                                                    |\n| ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Hygiene | `.editorconfig`, `.gitattributes`, `.gitignore`, `env.example` (the render writes it out as `.env.example`), `.yamllint.yml`, `.shellcheckrc` |","table":"table-0"},"1874":{"id":"/new-repo-29","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area  | What ships                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ----- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Gates | `.pre-commit-config.yaml` whose local hooks **fail** when a tool is missing, plus `tests/run.sh`, a dispatcher that errors rather than skipping when a runner is absent |","table":"table-0"},"1875":{"id":"/new-repo-30","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area | What ships                                                                                       |\n| ---- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Pins | `tool-versions.env`, read by the dev container, `make doctor`, and CI, so the three cannot drift |","table":"table-0"},"1876":{"id":"/new-repo-31","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area           | What ships                                                                              |\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Agent contract | `AGENTS.md` with the cost-tiered \"which check first\" table and the path-to-test mapping |","table":"table-0"},"1877":{"id":"/new-repo-32","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area | What ships                                                                                                 |\n| ---- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Docs | `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/project-conventions.md`, and `docs/adr/` with a template plus one worked ADR |","table":"table-0"},"1878":{"id":"/new-repo-33","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area      | What ships                                                           |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Packaging | a multi-stage non-root `Dockerfile` and a separate `Dockerfile.test` |","table":"table-0"},"1879":{"id":"/new-repo-34","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"The rule that ties it together: **a check that silently skips is a bug**. Two existing\nrepositories print `tool not found ... Skipping` and then exit zero, and a pre-commit config in\nanother wraps every hook in `command -v ... || exit 0`. Nothing in the scaffold may do that, and\nthe scaffold's own `.self-check/` suite is what holds the line."},"1880":{"id":"/new-repo-35","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"Lane ids are not declared here. `scripts/new-repo.sh` discovers them from the fetched tree's\n`.template-lanes/` directory at run time, so a lane the scaffold repository adds needs no change\nin this repo. The four below are what it ships today:"},"1881":{"id":"/new-repo-36","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"| Lane              | Adds                                                                                |\n| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `shell` (default) | `src/main.sh`, a pure helper under `src/lib/`, Bats suites, `scripts/lint-shell.sh` |","table":"table-1"},"1882":{"id":"/new-repo-37","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"| Lane     | Adds                                                                                                    |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `python` | `pyproject.toml` with Ruff and `fail_under = 100`, `mypy.ini` at strict, a package under `src/`, pytest |","table":"table-1"},"1883":{"id":"/new-repo-38","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"| Lane         | Adds                                                                        |\n| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `typescript` | ESLint 9 flat config, Prettier, Vitest with 100% thresholds, `tsc --noEmit` |","table":"table-1"},"1884":{"id":"/new-repo-39","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"| Lane | Adds                                                                               |\n| ---- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `go` | `go.mod`, `.golangci.yml`, table-driven tests, `go vet`, `gosec`, a coverage floor |","table":"table-1"},"1885":{"id":"/new-repo-40","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"Each lane lives at `<scaffold>/.template-lanes/<lane>/`. A file there is copied over the rendered\ntree at the same path, so a lane may add files or replace a core one. `LANE.md` and\n`gitignore.fragment` are lane metadata: the renderer appends the fragment to `.gitignore` and drops\nboth from the output. Ask for a lane the fetched tree does not have and the run dies naming the\nlanes it does offer."},"1886":{"id":"/new-repo-41","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"The scaffold is fetched before anything is rendered: a shallow (`--depth 1`), single-branch clone\nof `--template-ref` into a scratch directory an EXIT trap removes. `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` and an\nempty credential helper keep a scaffold that turned private from hanging on a prompt nobody can\nanswer inside a workspace. A fetched tree with no `template.yml` at its root is a hard failure --\nthat manifest belongs to the scaffold repository, so pick a ref that has it. The new repository's\nfirst commit records what it was built from: `- Scaffold: <url>@<sha>.`, or\n`- Scaffold: local tree <dir>.` for a `--template-dir` render."},"1887":{"id":"/new-repo-42","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag        | Effect                                   |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `--dry-run` | render, report, create nothing on GitHub |","table":"table-2"},"1888":{"id":"/new-repo-43","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag          | Effect                                                                        |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--out <dir>` | parent directory for the rendered repository (default: the current directory) |","table":"table-2"},"1889":{"id":"/new-repo-44","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag        | Effect                         |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `--no-push` | render and commit locally only |","table":"table-2"},"1890":{"id":"/new-repo-45","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag          | Effect                 |\n| ------------- | ---------------------- |\n| `--no-remote` | skip every GitHub step |","table":"table-2"},"1891":{"id":"/new-repo-46","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag        | Effect                        |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `--private` | create the repository private |","table":"table-2"},"1892":{"id":"/new-repo-47","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag            | Effect                           |\n| --------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| `--as-template` | mark it as a template repository |","table":"table-2"},"1893":{"id":"/new-repo-48","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag                                             | Effect                                                        |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--description`, `--owner`, `--holder`, `--year` | token values; the owner defaults to the authenticated account |","table":"table-2"},"1894":{"id":"/new-repo-49","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag          | Effect                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--lane <id>` | add a lane; repeatable, default `shell`. Ids are discovered from the fetched scaffold, so a lane the scaffold adds needs no change here. `--lane none` clears the lanes named so far and renders the bare core, whose `make test` asks for a lane |","table":"table-2"},"1895":{"id":"/new-repo-50","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag          | Effect                  |\n| ------------- | ----------------------- |\n| `--topic <t>` | extra topic; repeatable |","table":"table-2"},"1896":{"id":"/new-repo-51","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag                   | Effect                                                                                                |\n| ---------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--template-url <url>` | scaffold repository to clone (default: `$EZREPO_URL`, else `https://github.com/toxicoder/ezrepo.git`) |","table":"table-2"},"1897":{"id":"/new-repo-52","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag                   | Effect                                                                                            |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--template-ref <ref>` | branch or tag to fetch (default: `$EZREPO_REF`, else `main`). Pin a tag for a reproducible render |","table":"table-2"},"1898":{"id":"/new-repo-53","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag                   | Effect                                                                                                  |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--template-dir <dir>` | render this local tree and fetch nothing (default: `$EZREPO_DIR`); `--template-ref` has no effect on it |","table":"table-2"},"1899":{"id":"/new-repo-54","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag                            | Effect |\n| ------------------------------- | ------ |\n| `--task`, `--task-template <n>` |        |","table":"table-2"},"1900":{"id":"/new-repo-55","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag              | Effect                            |\n| ----------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `--branch <name>` | default branch, `main` by default |","table":"table-2"},"1901":{"id":"/new-repo-56","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"The three scaffold flags exist so the source can be pointed elsewhere without editing anything:\nthe env pair covers a mirror or a fork for a whole deployment, and `--template-dir` renders a tree\nyou already hold, which is the offline route and the one the hermetic tests use."},"1902":{"id":"/new-repo-57","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"The run exits 0 when the render succeeded -- a refused GitHub step is a `warning:`, not a failure --\nand 1 on a render failure, a bad argument, or a scaffold that cannot be fetched."},"1903":{"id":"/new-repo-58","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"Placeholders are substituted in file contents *and* in file and directory names:\n`__PROJECT_NAME__`, `__PROJECT_SLUG__`, `__PROJECT_MODULE__`, `__PROJECT_DESCRIPTION__`,\n`__PROJECT_OWNER__`, `__PROJECT_LICENSE_HOLDER__`, `__PROJECT_YEAR__`. Those seven are named once,\nin `NEW_REPO_TOKENS`, and they are the whole contract between this script and the scaffold -- a\nceiling rather than an equality, so the template may use a subset of them. A token that survives is\na hard failure, not a warning -- a repository that ships with its placeholders showing is worse\nthan no template at all."},"1904":{"id":"/new-repo-59","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"Markdown carries a token in a second spelling. Prettier reads a bare `__PROJECT_NAME__` as strong\nemphasis and rewrites it to `**PROJECT_NAME**`, so a markdown formatter run over the template turns\nevery prose placeholder into that form; the renderer substitutes both spellings. Which of the seven\nthe scaffold actually uses, in either spelling, is the scaffold's own invariant and its own\n`.self-check/` suite checks it."},"1905":{"id":"/new-repo-60","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"Two kinds of path are removed on the way out: a lane's reserved metadata (`LANE.md`,\n`gitignore.fragment`, whose contents are consumed rather than copied) and the generator-owned paths\nin `NEW_REPO_TEMPLATE_META`, stripped from every render: `.template-lanes/` (the overlay source this\nscript reads), `.self-check/` (the scaffold's conformance suite, which goes stale the moment a real\nproject diverges from the template it was checked against), and `.git` (which would hand the new\nrepository the template's history and origin remote)."},"1906":{"id":"/new-repo-61","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"The credential here is the Coder External Auth GitHub App **user-to-server token**; this stack\nnever introduces a static token or PAT. An App can be granted one endpoint and refused another, so\nthe CLI runs the publish side as independent steps, each reporting `ok:` or `warning:` on its own.\nNo step is fatal, because aborting on the first refusal would abandon the steps that were allowed."},"1907":{"id":"/new-repo-62","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"| Step   | Endpoint           | App permission                                            |\n| ------ | ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Create | `POST /user/repos` | `Administration: write` (or `Repository creation: write`) |","table":"table-3"},"1908":{"id":"/new-repo-63","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"| Step     | Endpoint               | App permission          |\n| -------- | ---------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| Settings | `PATCH /repos/{o}/{r}` | `Administration: write` |","table":"table-3"},"1909":{"id":"/new-repo-64","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"| Step   | Endpoint                    | App permission          |\n| ------ | --------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| Topics | `PUT /repos/{o}/{r}/topics` | `Administration: write` |","table":"table-3"},"1910":{"id":"/new-repo-65","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"| Step   | Endpoint                                  | App permission          |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| Alerts | `PUT /repos/{o}/{r}/vulnerability-alerts` | `Administration: write` |","table":"table-3"},"1911":{"id":"/new-repo-66","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"| Step | Endpoint       | App permission       |\n| ---- | -------------- | -------------------- |\n| Push | git over HTTPS | the credential above |","table":"table-3"},"1912":{"id":"/new-repo-67","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"Dependabot **secrets** are a different permission that this App does not currently hold, which is\nwhy the CLI does not attempt them. When a step is refused the CLI prints the endpoint, GitHub's\nmessage, and the permission to ask for, then re-mints nothing and continues."},"1913":{"id":"/new-repo-68","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"Because git and `gh` consult `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` before the stored login, an expired token\npair from an old refresh can shadow a working credential. Every call tries the environment first\nand, on refusal, retries once with the pair cleared and says so in a `warning:`. Re-mint with\n`coder external-auth access-token github`."},"1914":{"id":"/new-repo-69","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"Two things, and neither replaces the CLI."},"1915":{"id":"/new-repo-70","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"The **`New project (scaffold ready)` preset** in both templates is a form shortcut. It pins the\ngolden image, turns on the DinD sidecar in the workspace template, and passes this stack as\n`additional_repos`, which is what puts `scripts/new-repo.sh` inside a fresh workspace beside the\nproject being started. The scaffold does not ride along: the CLI fetches it from GitHub when it\nruns. It is a preset, so it only sets parameter values."},"1916":{"id":"/new-repo-71","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"**`--task`** calls `coder task create` once the repository exists. The Coder server here is v2.36.6\nand does ship `coder task`, but the Terraform provider is pinned to `~> 2.18`, so a task cannot be\ndeclared inside a template. The CLI therefore creates it at run time and, when it cannot reach the\nCoder API -- the usual case outside a logged-in host shell -- prints the exact command and continues\nwith exit 0. That follows `new-workspace.sh`, which treats \"cannot create right now\" as a not-yet\nrather than an error."},"1917":{"id":"/new-repo-72","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"The CLI never creates the workspace itself. It prints\n`next: bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <url>` and stops, because `new-workspace.sh` owns the\ndev-container probe and the template choice, and a second copy of that decision would eventually\ndisagree with the first."},"1918":{"id":"/new-repo-73","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"That preset/CLI pair is the whole hand-off. It is where this repo hands a new project over, and the\nonly coupling between the two repositories: this repo names the scaffold it clones, the scaffold\nnever names this one."},"1919":{"id":"/new-repo-74","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#where-this-is-enforced","content":"The split follows the ownership: the scaffold's own invariants are checked in the scaffold\nrepository, and this repo checks only the renderer."},"1920":{"id":"/new-repo-75","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#where-this-is-enforced","content":"The scaffold's `.self-check/` suite, in that repository and run by its own CI, owns the template's\ninternal invariants: every token the template uses is one this script declares, every lane ships\nits `LANE.md` plus `gitignore.fragment` pair, every tool is pinned rather than `@latest`, the\nworkflows declare `permissions`, the docs links resolve, and the pins cover the lanes present."},"1921":{"id":"/new-repo-76","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#where-this-is-enforced","content":"`//tests:bats_new_repo_test` owns the renderer: token substitution in file contents and in file\nand directory names, the markdown `**NAME**` alias, the lane overlay and its `gitignore`\nfragments, the generator-owned paths stripped from every render, the argv of the scaffold clone,\nthe credential ladder that retries once with `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` cleared, the GitHub ladder\nstaying advisory (a refused step warns and never fails the run), and the Coder Task being a\nconvenience that cannot fail a render."},"1922":{"id":"/new-repo-77","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#where-this-is-enforced","content":"`//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test` keeps `_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD[\"new-repo\"]` honest about what the CLI\nactually reads."},"1923":{"id":"/observability","page_id":"/observability","type":"page","content":"Observability bundle","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability"},"1924":{"id":"/observability-0","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability","content":"The ten profile-gated monitoring services: what each one does, how metrics, logs and traces reach Grafana, how an alert reaches Alertmanager, where each config file lives, what the data costs on disk, and what is deliberately left out."},"1925":{"id":"/observability-1","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"Observability bundle"},"1926":{"id":"/observability-2","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"The ten services"},"1927":{"id":"/observability-3","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#how-the-data-moves","content":"How the data moves"},"1928":{"id":"/observability-4","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"Alerts and rules"},"1929":{"id":"/observability-5","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#the-flag","content":"The flag"},"1930":{"id":"/observability-6","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"Where each config lives"},"1931":{"id":"/observability-7","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#the-dashboards","content":"The dashboards"},"1932":{"id":"/observability-8","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"Which page answers which question"},"1933":{"id":"/observability-9","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#how-the-sparks-pages-reach-one-machine","content":"How the Sparks pages reach one machine"},"1934":{"id":"/observability-10","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#a-dashboard-is-a-committed-file","content":"A dashboard is a committed file"},"1935":{"id":"/observability-11","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#the-gate","content":"The gate"},"1936":{"id":"/observability-12","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"Retention and disk"},"1937":{"id":"/observability-13","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#reaching-the-uis","content":"Reaching the UIs"},"1938":{"id":"/observability-14","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Operator checks"},"1939":{"id":"/observability-15","page_id":"/observability","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"url":"/observability#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"What is deliberately not here"},"1940":{"id":"/observability-16","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1941":{"id":"/observability-17","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"What each of the ten services does, and why this stack ships it at all"},"1942":{"id":"/observability-18","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"The path a metric, a log line and a span take before Grafana renders them"},"1943":{"id":"/observability-19","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"The one flag that decides whether the bundle exists, and how to turn it off"},"1944":{"id":"/observability-20","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"Where each component's config lives in the checkout, and what it costs to change"},"1945":{"id":"/observability-21","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"Which of the thirteen provisioned dashboards answers which question, and how a panel actually gets changed"},"1946":{"id":"/observability-22","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"Retention caps, the named volumes the data lands on, and how to reach each UI"},"1947":{"id":"/observability-23","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"What is not here - no pager destination until you set one, no object storage, no trace client"},"1948":{"id":"/observability-24","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"**What this enables**"},"1949":{"id":"/observability-25","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"Reading the box's history from Grafana instead of from `docker compose logs`"},"1950":{"id":"/observability-26","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"Telling a scrape fault from a write fault from a read fault in three commands"},"1951":{"id":"/observability-27","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"Deciding what to add next without guessing what is already wired"},"1952":{"id":"/observability-28","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#observability-bundle","content":"Ten services sit behind one Compose profile. Six of them store and read - `prometheus`,\n`mimir`, `loki`, `tempo`, `grafana` and `alloy` - and four watch the box and speak up -\n`node_exporter`, `cadvisor`, `blackbox_exporter` and `alertmanager`. They answer one\nquestion the rest of the stack cannot - what this box was doing ten minutes ago - and they\nare the only part of the stack that keeps data after a container is gone. Everything else on this page is about the seams between them,\nbecause that is where a monitoring stack silently stops working: a service can be\n`running`, a dashboard can be green, and a panel can still be empty."},"1953":{"id":"/observability-29","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"| Service      | Image                     | Job on this box                                                                                                |\n| ------------ | ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `prometheus` | `prom/prometheus:v3.16.0` | Scrape. Holds the last `PROMETHEUS_RETENTION_TIME` of series on its own TSDB and copies every sample to Mimir. |","table":"table-0"},"1954":{"id":"/observability-30","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"| Service | Image                 | Job on this box                                                                                      |\n| ------- | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `mimir` | `grafana/mimir:3.2.5` | Long-term metric store. Prometheus `remote_write`s into it; Grafana queries the long view out of it. |","table":"table-0"},"1955":{"id":"/observability-31","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"| Service | Image                 | Job on this box                                                                  |\n| ------- | --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `loki`  | `grafana/loki:3.7.10` | Log store. Takes the lines Alloy ships and answers LogQL from Grafana's Explore. |","table":"table-0"},"1956":{"id":"/observability-32","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"| Service | Image                 | Job on this box                                                              |\n| ------- | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `tempo` | `grafana/tempo:3.1.1` | Trace store. Listens for OTLP on `4317` and answers trace queries on `3200`. |","table":"table-0"},"1957":{"id":"/observability-33","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"| Service   | Image                    | Job on this box                                                                                                            |\n| --------- | ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grafana` | `grafana/grafana:13.3.1` | The only read path and the only published UI. Metrics, logs and traces all render here, from four provisioned datasources. |","table":"table-0"},"1958":{"id":"/observability-34","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"| Service | Image                   | Job on this box                                                                |\n| ------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `alloy` | `grafana/alloy:v1.22.0` | The collector. Tails the host's Docker json-file logs and pushes them to Loki. |","table":"table-0"},"1959":{"id":"/observability-35","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"| Service         | Image                                      | Job on this box                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `node_exporter` | `quay.io/prometheus/node-exporter:v1.12.1` | The stack host as a machine. Reads `/proc` and `/sys` read-only under `--path.rootfs=/host` with `pid: host`, and adds `--collector.processes` so the process series exist at all. |","table":"table-0"},"1960":{"id":"/observability-36","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"| Service    | Image                             | Job on this box                                                                                                        |\n| ---------- | --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `cadvisor` | `ghcr.io/google/cadvisor:v0.61.0` | Per-container metrics. The bundle's one privileged container, `--docker_only` with `image,name` whitelisted as labels. |","table":"table-0"},"1961":{"id":"/observability-37","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"| Service             | Image                            | Job on this box                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | -------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `blackbox_exporter` | `prom/blackbox-exporter:v0.29.1` | The prober for anything with no `/metrics` to scrape: Prometheus hands it each URL as `__param_target`, so one container probes every Spark. |","table":"table-0"},"1962":{"id":"/observability-38","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"| Service        | Image                       | Job on this box                                                                                  |\n| -------------- | --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `alertmanager` | `prom/alertmanager:v0.35.0` | Delivery. Groups, inhibits and silences what the rule files fire, and holds the repeat interval. |","table":"table-0"},"1963":{"id":"/observability-39","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-ten-services","content":"The split is upstream's own: Prometheus is a fast local scratch store, Mimir is the same\nPromQL over longer retention, and Alloy is the single agent that touches the host\nfilesystem. This box needs all three because a container that dies takes its own log\nfile out of `docker compose logs` reach only if you are too late - but a workspace that\nwas slow at 03:00 is unrecoverable without retention."},"1964":{"id":"/observability-40","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#how-the-data-moves","content":"Four things are worth naming precisely, because each is a place a panel goes empty while\nevery container stays up:"},"1965":{"id":"/observability-41","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#how-the-data-moves","content":"**Prometheus is the only scraper.** Its scrape config targets the apps that publish\n`/metrics` - `coder:2112` and `litellm:4000` - plus the six components that publish\ntheir own (`prometheus:9090`, `mimir:8080`, `loki:3100`, `tempo:3200`,\n`grafana:3000`, `alloy:12345`), the stack host through `node_exporter:9101` and\n`cadvisor:8080`, Alertmanager itself at `alertmanager:9093`, and the Spark jobs that\nreach `blackbox_exporter:9115` as their prober. Fifteen jobs in all. The LiteLLM\nscrape carries a bearer token, so the metrics stay behind `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` the way\nevery other LiteLLM endpoint is. Measured on the pinned v3.16.0: `$VAR` and `${VAR}`\nunder `global.external_labels` come back filled in at `/api/v1/status/config`, where\n`$(VAR)` stays literal, and a scrape target address and `authorization.credentials`\nexpand nothing at all - `$(VAR)`, `${VAR}` and `$VAR` in the latter each reached a\nlistener that logs its request headers verbatim - so the key cannot travel that way,\nand it arrives as a file instead. Compose renders `configs.litellm_metrics_token` into\n`/etc/prometheus-secrets/litellm_token` in the container (`docker-compose.yml:839-841`,\n`:1452`), and the `litellm` job presents that file through\n`authorization.credentials_file` (`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:126-129`).\nThe config's `content:` interpolates the very same\n`${LITELLM_MASTER_KEY:-change-me-litellm}` the proxy boots with\n(`docker-compose.yml:1459`), so with no dotenv both ends land on the shipped\nplaceholder and the scrape works, and the fault an operator actually meets is the two\nends disagreeing: rotate the key without recreating `prometheus` and the file still\nholds the old one."},"1966":{"id":"/observability-42","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#how-the-data-moves","content":"**The apps with no `/metrics` are observed by logs alone, on purpose.** `ollama`,\n`searxng`, `kasm`, `homepage`, the two nginx hops, `hermes`, and the three Infisical\ncontainers (`infisical`, `infisical-db`, `infisical-redis`) publish no Prometheus\nendpoint, so scraping them would leave a target answering 404 forever and a tally no\none trusts. Ollama is the one operators ask about: upstream has had the request for a\n`/metrics` endpoint open since 2024 (ollama/ollama#3144), and the IPEX-LLM image runs a\nstock `ollama serve`, so there is nothing to scrape. Its traffic is measured anyway,\nbecause every request reaches it through LiteLLM, which does publish. What every one of\nthose containers does contribute is its log stream, which is where a SearXNG rate limit\nor a Kasm start failure lands."},"1967":{"id":"/observability-43","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#how-the-data-moves","content":"**Mimir is written to, not scraped from.** Prometheus `remote_write`s to\n`http://mimir:8080/api/v1/push`. A query against the `prometheus` datasource answers\nfrom the local TSDB; the `mimir` datasource answers through\n`http://mimir:8080/prometheus`. Those two disagreeing is the diagnostic, not a bug."},"1968":{"id":"/observability-44","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#how-the-data-moves","content":"**Alloy, not Loki, reads the disk.** Loki has no Docker credential and no host mount.\nThe only container that sees the host's container log directory is Alloy, and it sees\nit read-only. That directory is `${STACK_DOCKER_LOG_DIR:-/var/lib/docker/containers}`,\nmounted at the same path inside the collector, so a host whose daemon keeps its logs\nsomewhere else (rootless Docker does) needs that key set or the collector tails\nnothing."},"1969":{"id":"/observability-45","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#how-the-data-moves","content":"**Traces arrive over OTLP only, and one client already speaks it.** Anything that wants\ntraces in this stack sends OTLP gRPC to `tempo:4317`. Grafana is the one shipped\ncaller: `GF_TRACING_ENABLED` points its own exporter at `tempo:4317`, so the trace\nviewer has the product's own spans before any workspace emits one. Tempo's\nmetrics_generator then computes span-metrics and service-graph series and\n`remote_write`s them back into Prometheus, which is why Prometheus carries\n`--web.enable-remote-write-receiver`: without that flag the write is refused with a 404\nand the traces dashboard has nothing to plot."},"1970":{"id":"/observability-46","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#how-the-data-moves","content":"Grafana's four datasources carry fixed UIDs - `prometheus` (the default), `mimir`,\n`loki`, `tempo` - and every shipped dashboard references those UIDs, so renaming one in\nthe UI breaks the dashboards that were provisioned against it."},"1971":{"id":"/observability-47","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"Rules are evaluated by Prometheus and delivered by Alertmanager: the `alerting:` block at\n`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:52` names `alertmanager:9093`, so grouping,\ninhibition, silences and the repeat interval all belong to that one service."},"1972":{"id":"/observability-48","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"| Rule file                                        | Recording-rule prefix                                      | Alerts               | Critical                                                                                                        |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `observability/prometheus/rules/stack.rules.yml` | `host:` (one `container:` for the cAdvisor throttle ratio) | 9, all `tier=\"host\"` | `HostCpuPegged`, `HostMemoryNearExhaustion`, `HostFilesystemNearFull`, `HostNodeExporterGap`, `HostCadvisorGap` |","table":"table-1"},"1973":{"id":"/observability-49","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"| Rule file                                        | Recording-rule prefix | Alerts                                                    | Critical                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `observability/prometheus/rules/spark.rules.yml` | `spark:`              | 7, all `tier=\"spark\"` and each stamped `spark=<hostname>` | `SparkDown`, `SparkGpuRowRemapFailure`, `SparkGpuEccUncorrectable`, `SparkGpuSeriesSilent` |","table":"table-1"},"1974":{"id":"/observability-50","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"| Rule file                                            | Recording-rule prefix | Alerts                     | Critical                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `observability/prometheus/rules/inference.rules.yml` | `litellm:`            | 10, all `tier=\"inference\"` | `LiteLLMModelErrorRatioSustained`, `LiteLLMDeploymentUnhealthy`, `LiteLLMDeploymentCoolingDownRepeatedly`, `LiteLLMBackendSeriesGone`, `LiteLLMMetricsAbsent` |","table":"table-1"},"1975":{"id":"/observability-51","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"Four things about that wiring are worth knowing before you edit it:"},"1976":{"id":"/observability-52","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"**The gap alerts select on the job name.** `HostNodeExporterGap` and `HostCadvisorGap`\nfire when `job=\"node_exporter\"` or `job=\"cadvisor\"` carries no series, which is why the\njob name is the Compose service name (`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:142` and\n`:151`). Renaming either service without renaming its job makes two alerts permanently\nquiet, and no test will tell you."},"1977":{"id":"/observability-53","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"**The Spark tier is quiet until the boxes are enrolled.** Those two jobs read their\ntargets from `observability/spark-targets/sparks.json` through `file_sd_configs`, a file\nthe stack host generates and `.gitignore:76` keeps out of git. No file means no target\ngroups, which reads as zero Sparks rather than as a broken probe. The boxes' own\nnode_exporter and DCGM exporter are installed on the boxes by `scripts/spark-monitor.sh`,\nnot by this Compose file."},"1978":{"id":"/observability-54","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"**Grafana lists the rules because the datasource says to.** The Prometheus datasource is\nregistered `alerting: true` (`observability/grafana/provisioning/datasources/stack.yaml:26`);\nwithout that flag the rules answer only on Prometheus' own `/alerts` page and never\nappear under Alerting -> Rules. Delivery still belongs to Alertmanager."},"1979":{"id":"/observability-55","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"**An alert a person builds in Grafana goes to the same Alertmanager.** The provisioned\ncontact point is `type: alertmanager` at `http://alertmanager:9093`\n(`observability/grafana/provisioning/alerting/stack.yaml:28`), not a webhook, so it\nspeaks `/api/v2/alerts` and Alertmanager's grouping, inhibition and silences apply to\nGrafana-originated alerts exactly as they do to Prometheus'. One place holds the\nsilences, whichever engine fired."},"1980":{"id":"/observability-56","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#alerts-and-rules","content":"Nothing leaves the box until the operator says who to tell. Alertmanager's own config\nparser cannot expand an environment variable inside a webhook URL, so\n`observability/alertmanager/alertmanager.yml` carries no URL at all: all four webhook\nreceivers read `url_file: /alertmanager/webhook_url`. That file is written at container\nstart by the image's entrypoint, `observability/alertmanager/boot-webhook-url.sh`, from\nthe `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL` key (`.env.example:635`, empty as shipped), and the script\nthen `exec`s the binary. An empty key is a working configuration: the container starts,\nthe rules fire, the receivers are no-ops, and the entrypoint regenerates the file every\nstart so a removed key cannot survive as a stale destination on disk. The Alertmanager UI\nis loopback-bound for the same reason it has no auth - it can silence alerts - so read it\nfrom a shell on the stack host, or use Grafana's Alerting pages, which need no extra port."},"1981":{"id":"/observability-57","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-flag","content":"One key decides whether the bundle exists: `OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED`, default `true`, in\n`.env.example` and `.env.sample` beside it. All ten services carry\n`profiles: [\"observability\"]`, and `compose_observability_args()` in\n`scripts/lib/common.sh` is the single place that reads the key - it prints\n`--profile observability` when the value is truthy and nothing for `false`, `0`, `no` or\n`off`. Every stack runner goes through it: `dev-stack.sh` and `prod-stack.sh` in their\n`compose_prefix()` and `run_compose()`, and `update-stack.sh` in `stack_compose()`."},"1982":{"id":"/observability-58","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-flag","content":"To turn the bundle off, set the key in the env file that belongs to that project -\n`.env` for the stable stack, `dev-stack.env` for the dev one - and bring the stack up:"},"1983":{"id":"/observability-59","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-flag","content":"A profile decides which services a command *selects*; it does not delete what is already\nrunning. The ten containers stay up until you stop them, so after flipping the key off\neither take the project down (`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- down`) or delete the\nten by name through compose. Their named volumes survive either way, and `reset` is the\none that destroys them."},"1984":{"id":"/observability-60","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-flag","content":"Both flag paths are gated: `bazelisk run //scripts:validate` runs\n`docker compose config -q` twice, once plain and once with `--profile observability`, so\na compose file that only parses with the bundle on (or only with it off) fails the build\nrather than the deploy."},"1985":{"id":"/observability-61","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"The bundle's payloads are one directory of their own, bind-mounted read-only, so\n`git diff` is the record of every monitoring change and nothing is edited in the running\ncontainer:"},"1986":{"id":"/observability-62","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                                      | Read by                                                                                                                                   |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml` | Prometheus: the fourteen scrape jobs, the `alerting:` block that names Alertmanager, the three `rule_files:` and the Mimir `remote_write` |","table":"table-2"},"1987":{"id":"/observability-63","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                          | Read by |\n| ----------------------------- | ------- |\n| `observability/loki/loki.yml` | Loki    |","table":"table-2"},"1988":{"id":"/observability-64","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                            | Read by |\n| ------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `observability/tempo/tempo.yml` | Tempo   |","table":"table-2"},"1989":{"id":"/observability-65","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                            | Read by                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `observability/mimir/mimir.yml` | Mimir, including `multitenancy_enabled: false` - see the query path below |","table":"table-2"},"1990":{"id":"/observability-66","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                                                        | Read by                                   |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `observability/grafana/provisioning/datasources/stack.yaml` | The four datasources and their fixed UIDs |","table":"table-2"},"1991":{"id":"/observability-67","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                                                       | Read by                                                                                                   |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `observability/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/stack.yaml` | The provider that loads the JSON below: folder `EVO-T1`, `allowUiUpdates: false`, `disableDeletion: true` |","table":"table-2"},"1992":{"id":"/observability-68","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                                | Read by                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `observability/grafana/dashboards/` | The thirteen provisioned dashboard JSONs - the section below names each one and what it answers - mounted again at `/var/lib/grafana/dashboards` (`docker-compose.yml:1049`), which is the path the provider reads |","table":"table-2"},"1993":{"id":"/observability-69","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                                                  | Read by                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `observability/grafana/unified-storage/overrides.yml` | Grafana's unified-storage quota overrides, mounted read-only at `/etc/grafana/stack-overrides.yml` and named there by `GF_UNIFIED_STORAGE_OVERRIDES_PATH` (`docker-compose.yml:1035`, `:1055`) - the note below says what its presence buys |","table":"table-2"},"1994":{"id":"/observability-70","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                               | Read by                                                     |\n| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `observability/alloy/config.alloy` | The log pipeline: which files to tail, what to ship to Loki |","table":"table-2"},"1995":{"id":"/observability-71","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                              | Read by                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `observability/prometheus/rules/` | Prometheus, through that `rule_files:` block: `stack.rules.yml`, `spark.rules.yml`, `inference.rules.yml`. They arrive with the directory this file is mounted from, so there is no second place to register a rule |","table":"table-2"},"1996":{"id":"/observability-72","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                                          | Read by                                                                                                                  |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `observability/alertmanager/alertmanager.yml` | Alertmanager: the `tier` routes, three inhibit rules, and five receivers (four webhook, one blackhole for the heartbeat) |","table":"table-2"},"1997":{"id":"/observability-73","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                                             | Read by                                                                                                                  |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `observability/alertmanager/boot-webhook-url.sh` | The container's `entrypoint:`: writes `/alertmanager/webhook_url` from `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL`, then execs the binary |","table":"table-2"},"1998":{"id":"/observability-74","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                                  | Read by                                                                                                      |\n| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `observability/blackbox/blackbox.yml` | blackbox_exporter: the two HTTP probe modules the Spark jobs pass by name, `spark_health` and `spark_models` |","table":"table-2"},"1999":{"id":"/observability-75","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"| Path                                                     | Read by                                                                      |\n| -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `observability/grafana/provisioning/alerting/stack.yaml` | The `stack-alertmanager` contact point and Grafana's default alerting policy |","table":"table-2"},"2000":{"id":"/observability-76","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"Two consequences of the `:ro` mounts, both the same rule the other payload directories\nfollow. A bind follows the *path*, so an edit made in the checkout is already inside the\nrunning container, and the only question left is when the process reads it again:\nand these are directory binds, so measured against the pinned image an in-place rewrite\nof a mounted file and a rename of a new one over it both reached the container with no\nrestart and no recreate, which is why the dashboards section below can promise a minute\nrather than a redeploy. The one\ncredential that behaves differently is the LiteLLM bearer token, and the reason is that\nit is not a bind - Compose writes a `configs` entry when it creates the container, so\nrotating `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` takes `up -d --force-recreate prometheus`, where a plain\n`up -d` leaves the old bytes in place, and so does a `restart`. And treat the provisioned\ndashboards and datasources as read-only in the UI: `allowUiUpdates: false` and\n`disableDeletion: true` stop a save from overwriting the JSON, so anything you build by\nhand lands in Grafana's own database in `grafana-data` instead - it works, and it drifts\nfrom git invisibly."},"2001":{"id":"/observability-77","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"The `grafana` service carries two settings that live in its `environment:` block\n(`docker-compose.yml:1034-1035`) rather than in any file above, each one there to quiet a\nGrafana 13.3.1 defect this box pays for as log noise.\n`GF_LOG_FILTERS: search-manifest-watcher:error` drops that one logger to `error` and\nleaves every other logger at its default. The watcher warns twice each poll, and the\npolls in the log this branch was cut from were 41 to 59 s apart -\n`source listed zero manifests`, then `zero manifests, keeping previous set` - whenever no\napp publishes a search manifest, which is every OSS install with nothing provisioned into\nthe app platform; upstream calls that a logging defect (grafana/grafana#137058) and\nconfirms search keeps working, because retrying an empty source is intended and only the\nlevel is wrong. The filter is a workaround for that defect, not a preference: it comes\nout when the pin moves to a release carrying the fix, and the pin today is\n`grafana/grafana:13.3.1` (`docker-compose.yml:976`).\n`GF_UNIFIED_STORAGE_OVERRIDES_PATH: /etc/grafana/stack-overrides.yml`\npoints the unified-storage resource server at the file the table names: without it the\nquota lookup the browse view fires on every dashboard page answers 501, since that\nserver's quota service is nil with nothing pointed at it (grafana/grafana#136271), and the\nbox prints one error line per page load while the browser console fills with 501s; with it\nthe same call answers 200. Both halves were measured on `grafana/grafana:13.3.1` as two\ncontainers differing only in that variable: the one without the file answered 501 for both\n`dashboard.grafana.app/dashboards` and `folder.grafana.app/folders` and the one with it\nanswered 200 for both, with `/api/health` 200 either way. On the filter side, over one\n300-second window the unfiltered container wrote 16 `search-manifest-watcher` lines, all\n`level=warn`, and the filtered one wrote none from that logger - the only line in it that\nstill names it is Grafana's own `level=info` record that the setting came from the\nenvironment. The overrides file itself says `overrides: {}`, which is\nGrafana's own defaults - it is the file's presence that clears the 501, not any limit set\nin it. What the quota call then reports is that default, `{\"usage\":0,\"limit\":1000}` for\ndashboards and for folders on 13.3.1; four plausible cap shapes left the number where it\nwas, and a scalar or an unparseable document under `overrides:` stops the container at\nboot, so the file's header asks for a limit to be proven on the pinned image before it is\nwritten down."},"2002":{"id":"/observability-78","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#where-each-config-lives","content":"The thirteen dashboards are provisioned, so they appear on a fresh Grafana in the\n`EVO-T1` folder with no clicking - the section below names each one and the question it\nanswers. The LiteLLM board is the one that depends on a credential: its metrics sit behind\n`LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` like every other LiteLLM route, and the bearer Prometheus presents is\nthe file Compose rendered from that key, so anything that puts the two ends out of step -\na key rotated in the env file with `prometheus` never recreated, a token file that is\nmissing or wrong - leaves every panel on that board reading empty while the others stay\nfull."},"2003":{"id":"/observability-79","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-dashboards","content":"Thirteen JSON files in `observability/grafana/dashboards/` are the only dashboards this\nstack ships. `observability/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/stack.yaml:11` loads them all\ninto one `EVO-T1` folder (`:14`) with `disableDeletion: true` (`:16`),\n`updateIntervalSeconds: 60` (`:17`) and `allowUiUpdates: false` (`:18`). Read those three\nvalues before you try to fix a panel. Then find your question in the tables below: every\npage name and every panel name quoted here is copied out of the JSON, so you can search\nthe UI for it. Panel counts include the panels a collapsed row holds."},"2004":{"id":"/observability-80","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"The stack host - the box the bundle itself runs on:"},"2005":{"id":"/observability-81","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Page                    | UID                    | Panels |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------- | ---------------------- | ------ |\n| What is the box doing as a machine: per-core CPU, load, the PSI pressure stats, memory and swap, IOPS and throughput per device, `Space and inode headroom per mount`, dropped packets, conntrack, file descriptors, `Top containers by CPU` | Stack host task manager | `evo-host-taskmanager` | 36     |","table":"table-3"},"2006":{"id":"/observability-82","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                                                                     | Page       | UID              | Panels |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- | ---------------- | ------ |\n| Which container is at fault: `CPU usage against CFS throttling`, `Memory working set against its limit`, `OOM kills`, `Uptime since last start`, `Reported containers and how stale each is` | Containers | `evo-containers` | 12     |","table":"table-3"},"2007":{"id":"/observability-83","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"The DGX Sparks - the off-stack boxes Prometheus probes through blackbox:"},"2008":{"id":"/observability-84","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                            | Page                | UID                   | Panels |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- | --------------------- | ------ |\n| Which box needs looking at: one repeated stat strip per enrolled box, then `Every Spark, one row per box` and `Serving request rate, by deployment` | DGX Sparks overview | `evo-sparks-overview` | 11     |","table":"table-4"},"2009":{"id":"/observability-85","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Page          | UID              | Panels |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ---------------- | ------ |\n| Everything about the one box you have picked: the host rows, the GPU rows (`GPU utilisation`, `Framebuffer used and free`, `GPU and memory temperature against the slowdown point`, `ECC row remaps in the last hour`), the blackbox probe rows, and that box's own deployments | One DGX Spark | `evo-spark-host` | 41     |","table":"table-4"},"2010":{"id":"/observability-86","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Page            | UID                | Panels |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------- | ------------------ | ------ |\n| The fleet as one number: `Total GPU utilisation`, `Total GPU power draw`, `Hottest GPU core`, `Fullest framebuffer`, `Uncorrectable ECC rows, last hour`, the per-box comparison tables, and the fleet admission queue | DGX Spark fleet | `evo-sparks-fleet` | 15     |","table":"table-4"},"2011":{"id":"/observability-87","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"The inference backends - the software that serves the models, not the machines it runs\non, which are two rows above:"},"2012":{"id":"/observability-88","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Page   | UID                    | Panels |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ | ---------------------- | ------ |\n| What callers see, per model: `Request rate by model`, `Error ratio by model, 4xx vs 5xx`, the three latency panels, `Time to first token by model (p50/p95/p99)`, `Cache hit ratio`, `Served models, one row per deployment` | Models | `evo-inference-models` | 24     |","table":"table-5"},"2013":{"id":"/observability-89","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | Page               | UID                      | Panels |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------------------------ | ------ |\n| What the router is doing, per deployment: `Deployment inventory and state`, `Backends off the healthy state`, `Cooldown transitions per deployment`, `Admission rejections by reason`, `Balance per deployment`, `Aliases served per deployment`, `Upstream p95 against total p95, per deployment` | Inference backends | `evo-inference-backends` | 24     |","table":"table-5"},"2014":{"id":"/observability-90","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"The bundle itself:"},"2015":{"id":"/observability-91","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                                               | Page         | UID        | Panels |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------ | ---------- | ------ |\n| Start here: the `EVO-T1 dashboards` list and one link panel per board, then a stat per bundle service beside `Targets up`, `Targets configured` and `Scrape freshness` | EVO-T1 stack | `evo-home` | 33     |","table":"table-6"},"2016":{"id":"/observability-92","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Page           | UID                  | Panels |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- | -------------------- | ------ |\n| Is the monitoring still working: `Scrape targets up`, `Prometheus TSDB on disk`, `Remote write to Mimir`, and one reporting-stat per exporter and per bundle process, each reading `0` while that process answers its scrape and `1` when it has no series at all | Stack overview | `evo-stack-overview` | 28     |","table":"table-6"},"2017":{"id":"/observability-93","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                                                      | Page   | UID          | Panels |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ | ------------ | ------ |\n| What is firing and did it get out: `Firing alerts`, the three severity counts, `Firing series by alert`, `Alertmanager intake`, `Notifications dropped`, `Slowest rule group` | Alerts | `evo-alerts` | 16     |","table":"table-6"},"2018":{"id":"/observability-94","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                   | Page           | UID              | Panels |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- | ---------------- | ------ |\n| What did a container say: `Log volume`, `stdout and stderr`, `Errors only` | Container logs | `evo-stack-logs` | 3      |","table":"table-6"},"2019":{"id":"/observability-95","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                  | Page                   | UID                | Panels |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------ | ------ |\n| Where did a request spend its time: `Span rate by service`, `Span latency, p95 by service`, `Span errors` | Traces and service map | `evo-stack-traces` | 4      |","table":"table-6"},"2020":{"id":"/observability-96","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"| Question                                                                                                                                                                          | Page | UID           | Panels |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---- | ------------- | ------ |\n| What did the proxy cost and do: `Spend in the selected range`, `Spend per model, selected range`, `Requests by status code`, `Deployment state`, `Cooldown events per deployment` |      | `evo-litellm` | 17     |","table":"table-6"},"2021":{"id":"/observability-97","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#which-page-answers-which-question","content":"Four of those carry a `How to read this page` text panel - `evo-alerts`,\n`evo-inference-models`, `evo-litellm` and `evo-stack-traces` - and it is worth reading\nbefore you chase a flat line on one of them."},"2022":{"id":"/observability-98","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#how-the-sparks-pages-reach-one-machine","content":"`evo-sparks-overview` and `evo-spark-host` each declare a `host` templating variable, both\nof them a `query` variable whose query is `label_values(spark:box_up, hostname)`, so the\ndropdown lists the boxes Prometheus actually sees and nothing else. On `evo-spark-host` the\nvariable is labelled `Spark` and every panel on the page filters to whatever it holds."},"2023":{"id":"/observability-99","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#how-the-sparks-pages-reach-one-machine","content":"The overview does not ask you to choose first. Seven stat panels - `Spark $host`,\n`GPU util`, `Framebuffer fill`, `Temp headroom`, `Power`, `CPU`, `Memory` - each carry\n`repeat` on that same variable with `repeatDirection: h`, so the strip renders one column\nper enrolled box. The link panel `Open this Spark` repeats alongside them and points at\n`/d/evo-spark-host/one-dgx-spark?var-host=${var:queryparam}`: the tile under the box you\nare reading opens that box's page with `host` already set. That is the path from a red tile\non the overview to the GPU rows for that machine, and it is the only link between the two\nboards. `evo-sparks-fleet` declares no variable at all, because it aggregates across every\nbox by design."},"2024":{"id":"/observability-100","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#a-dashboard-is-a-committed-file","content":"The provider owns the `EVO-T1` folder, so nothing you do in the browser to a shipped panel\nsurvives: `allowUiUpdates: false` refuses the save, `disableDeletion: true` refuses the\ndelete, and the 60-second poll quietly undoes whatever the UI let you stage. A board you\nbuild from scratch is not thrown away - it lands in Grafana's own database on `grafana-data`"},"2025":{"id":"/observability-101","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#a-dashboard-is-a-committed-file","content":"but it is invisible to git and to the gate below, which is the same drift the `:ro` mounts\nabove exist to prevent."},"2026":{"id":"/observability-102","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#a-dashboard-is-a-committed-file","content":"So the way to change a panel is to edit its JSON and let the interval apply it. The folder\nis bind-mounted read-only onto the path the provider scans (`docker-compose.yml:1049`), so\nan edit to a file that is already there is visible to the running container and shows up\nwithin a minute - no restart, and no recreate. A new file needs no registration either, for\nthe same reason. Recreate only when the mount line or the provisioning tree itself changed\n(`docker-compose.yml:1047-1048`), and then with the `up -d` rule from the section above:"},"2027":{"id":"/observability-103","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-gate","content":"The folder is source, so it is tested as source:"},"2028":{"id":"/observability-104","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-gate","content":"`tests/bats/observability-dashboards.bats` asserts seventeen properties over every file in\nit: the folder is non-empty and each file parses with the repo's own parser; each is pure\nASCII, LF-only, one trailing newline; the pinned top-level keys and house values are\npresent; UIDs and titles are unique across the folder; panel ids are unique integers;\nevery panel carries a full `gridPos` and no two visible panels overlap; every panel stays\ninside the house key set; every target opens with `refId` and carries its own datasource;\nevery datasource is one of the four provisioned UIDs; every querying panel carries a\n`description`; every stat states its own empty reading rather than leaving it to Grafana;\nevery referenced variable is declared; every templating variable is well\nformed; every link names a dashboard that exists in the folder; every UID besides\n`evo-home` is named by a link panel or a `links:` entry there; and no file carries an\nimport block, a host name, or a dead metric shape."},"2029":{"id":"/observability-105","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-gate","content":"The one to read before you write an `expr`: every metric name in every `expr` has to sit on\na pinned allow-list, and that list is assembled at test time from the recording rules this\nrepo ships under `observability/prometheus/rules/` as well as the pinned names\n(`tests/bats/observability-dashboards.bats:688`). A new recording rule therefore needs no\ntest edit, and a metric nothing here records fails the build instead of rendering an empty\ntile. `bazelisk run //scripts:validate` covers the coarser layer: it parses every dashboard\nJSON through `scripts/lib/parse_json.py` (`scripts/validate.sh:104`), the gate that already\ncovers the three rule files."},"2030":{"id":"/observability-106","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#the-gate","content":"Nothing in this section has been rendered in a live Grafana from this checkout: the pages\nare proven by that structure and by metric names a source in this repo backs, not by\npixels."},"2031":{"id":"/observability-107","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"Everything lands on named volumes under Docker's volume root on the host disk. Nothing\nhere reaches an object store, so the caps are what protect that disk:"},"2032":{"id":"/observability-108","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"| Component  | Cap (env key, default)                                                                       | Volume -> container path           |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| Prometheus | `PROMETHEUS_RETENTION_TIME` (15d), `PROMETHEUS_RETENTION_SIZE` (10GB) - whichever hits first | `prometheus-data` -> `/prometheus` |","table":"table-7"},"2033":{"id":"/observability-109","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"| Component | Cap (env key, default)                    | Volume -> container path      |\n| --------- | ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| Mimir     | `MIMIR_RETENTION_PERIOD` (2160h, 90 days) | `mimir-data` -> `/data/mimir` |","table":"table-7"},"2034":{"id":"/observability-110","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"| Component | Cap (env key, default)                 | Volume -> container path |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n| Loki      | `LOKI_RETENTION_PERIOD` (168h, 7 days) | `loki-data` -> `/loki`   |","table":"table-7"},"2035":{"id":"/observability-111","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"| Component | Cap (env key, default)                                                                  | Volume -> container path     |\n| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Tempo     | `TEMPO_BLOCK_RETENTION_TIME` (168h), applied by the backend-scheduler's compaction work | `tempo-data` -> `/var/tempo` |","table":"table-7"},"2036":{"id":"/observability-112","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"| Component | Cap (env key, default)       | Volume -> container path             |\n| --------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| Grafana   | n/a (dashboards, not series) | `grafana-data` -> `/var/lib/grafana` |","table":"table-7"},"2037":{"id":"/observability-113","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"| Component | Cap (env key, default)                                      | Volume -> container path              |\n| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| Alloy     | none shipped; it keeps its own state under `--storage.path` | `alloy-data` -> `/var/lib/alloy/data` |","table":"table-7"},"2038":{"id":"/observability-114","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"| Component    | Cap (env key, default)                                     | Volume -> container path               |\n| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| Alertmanager | none; it keeps silences and notification state, not series | `alertmanager-data` -> `/alertmanager` |","table":"table-7"},"2039":{"id":"/observability-115","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"The four exporters keep nothing between runs: what they hold is a scrape interval deep, so\nrecreating one costs no history and needs no volume of its own."},"2040":{"id":"/observability-116","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#retention-and-disk","content":"Change a cap in the env file and recreate. The Prometheus size cap is the one that\nactually bounds the box: it is the only figure that does not depend on how fast the\nseries arrive. Tempo's cap is the odd one, because Tempo 3.x moved it out of the config\nfile - it reaches the container as `-backend-scheduler.provider.work.compaction.block-retention`\non the command line in docker-compose.yml, so editing tempo.yml for it changes nothing.\n`bazelisk run //scripts:storage-clean` is the interactive janitor for the persistent\nlayer if a volume outgrows what you meant it to hold."},"2041":{"id":"/observability-117","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#reaching-the-uis","content":"| What    | Where                                                                                                  | Notes                                                                                                                                                          |\n| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Grafana | `http://<lan>:3005` (`GRAFANA_PORT`), and `grafana-<suffix>.<base>` when `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` names it | The only published name in the bundle. Login is `GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER` / `GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD` from `.env`; anonymous access and analytics reporting are off. |","table":"table-8"},"2042":{"id":"/observability-118","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#reaching-the-uis","content":"| What       | Where                                       | Notes                                                                        |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Prometheus | `http://127.0.0.1:9090` (`PROMETHEUS_PORT`) | `:2112` metrics port. Reading a graph there needs a shell on the stack host. |","table":"table-8"},"2043":{"id":"/observability-119","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#reaching-the-uis","content":"| What  | Where                                                   | Notes                                          |\n| ----- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| Mimir | nothing published - `mimir:8080` on the compose network | Reach it through Grafana's `mimir` datasource. |","table":"table-8"},"2044":{"id":"/observability-120","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#reaching-the-uis","content":"| What | Where                           | Notes                             |\n| ---- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Loki | nothing published - `loki:3100` | Reach it through Grafana Explore. |","table":"table-8"},"2045":{"id":"/observability-121","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#reaching-the-uis","content":"| What  | Where                                                     | Notes                             |\n| ----- | --------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Tempo | nothing published - `tempo:3200` query, `tempo:4317` OTLP | Reach it through Grafana Explore. |","table":"table-8"},"2046":{"id":"/observability-122","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#reaching-the-uis","content":"| What  | Where                             | Notes                                                                              |\n| ----- | --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Alloy | nothing published - `alloy:12345` | The component graph UI stays inside the network; read the container's log instead. |","table":"table-8"},"2047":{"id":"/observability-123","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#reaching-the-uis","content":"| What         | Where                                             | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Alertmanager | `http://127.0.0.1:9093` (`OBS_ALERTMANAGER_PORT`) | Loopback on purpose. Its UI silences alerts and the service ships no auth, so an Alertmanager reachable by the whole LAN is not something to hand a network. Read silences here from a shell on the stack host, or through Grafana's Alerting pages. |","table":"table-8"},"2048":{"id":"/observability-124","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#reaching-the-uis","content":"| What                | Where                                                                                                                                                                               | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| The three exporters | `node_exporter` on `127.0.0.1:9101` (`OBS_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT`), `cadvisor` on `127.0.0.1:9290` (`OBS_CADVISOR_PORT`), `blackbox_exporter` on `127.0.0.1:9115` (`OBS_BLACKBOX_PORT`) | Prometheus reaches all three by service name on the compose network; the loopback mapping exists so you can read a metric off the box itself. None reaches the LAN, which matters most for blackbox: it is a general-purpose outbound HTTP prober. |","table":"table-8"},"2049":{"id":"/observability-125","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#reaching-the-uis","content":"Public mode follows the same path as the other names: `STACK_GRAFANA_NAME` (default label\n`grafana`) is composed by `scripts/lib/domain.sh` with the rest, `bootstrap` writes\n`STACK_GRAFANA_URL`, and the router sends that Host to `grafana:3000`. Only Grafana gets\na name - the other nine have no reason to answer the LAN, let alone the WAN, and\nAlertmanager least of all. Full\ndetail on the naming parts and the Traefik side is in\npublic-access.md."},"2050":{"id":"/observability-126","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Start with the one command that asks all of these at once, then drop to the individual\nprobes when it names a section:"},"2051":{"id":"/observability-127","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"It walks the bundle outward from the dotenv - the operator keys, the host's log\ndirectory, the profile render, each container's state, each endpoint, whether data is\nactually moving - and prints the stack's usual `summary: N ok, W warnings, F failed`. It\nis the check that catches the two failures a `running` state hides: an Alloy pointed at\nan empty log root, and a Prometheus that scraped everything for a day and never wrote a\nsample to Mimir. `--no-docker` skips the four unpublished backends when you are not on a\nshell that can address the daemon."},"2052":{"id":"/observability-128","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Its service list is still the original six (`OBS_SERVICES`, `scripts/observability-check.sh:165`).\nThe four exporters and Alertmanager declare no `healthcheck` and publish no readiness path\nof their own, so their state comes from the `status` rows and the container logs below,\nnot from this check."},"2053":{"id":"/observability-129","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Then, from the checkout on the stack host, in this order. Each one splits one symptom from\nthe next."},"2054":{"id":"/observability-130","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Is the bundle selected at all:"},"2055":{"id":"/observability-131","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Ten rows when `OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED` is on, none when it is off. Expect `running`, not\n`healthy`, on the eight that declare no `healthcheck`: `mimir`, `loki`, `tempo` and\n`alloy` (those four images are distroless, so they carry no shell and no `wget`/`curl`\nfor a healthcheck to run), plus `node_exporter`, `cadvisor`, `blackbox_exporter` and\n`alertmanager`. Only `prometheus` and `grafana` declare one. A missing row means the\nprofile was not selected - see the\ntroubleshooting entry on which env file the dev project actually reads."},"2056":{"id":"/observability-132","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Did Grafana come up and can it read its own database:"},"2057":{"id":"/observability-133","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Are the scrapes up:"},"2058":{"id":"/observability-134","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Every target should read `\"health\":\"up\"`. A target missing entirely is the scrape config;\na target present and `down` is the app not serving `/metrics`, or - for `litellm` alone -\nthe bearer file not holding the key the proxy answers with. That failure describes itself\ntwice over: a file that is not there leaves one red target whose `lastError` names\n`/etc/prometheus-secrets/litellm_token` while Prometheus loads the rest of the config\nnormally, and a file holding the wrong key brings LiteLLM's 401 body `No connected db.`,\nwhich at v1.102.1 is the wrong-token branch rather than a database complaint - its auth\npath compares the bearer against `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` and returns PROXY_ADMIN before it\nlooks for a DB. Either way the fix is the file and a recreate of `prometheus` so Compose\nrenders it again; the metrics stay authenticated. What that 401 offers instead is turning\nthe endpoint's auth off (`litellm_settings.require_auth_for_metrics_endpoint`), and this\nstack refuses it: `:4000` is published to the LAN, and these metrics name model aliases\nand traffic volumes."},"2059":{"id":"/observability-135","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Is the write to Mimir landing:"},"2060":{"id":"/observability-136","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"A refused or failing `remote_write` shows up in the Prometheus log. The clean version of\nthe same question is asked in the UI: the `prometheus` datasource has the series and the\n`mimir` one does not, which is a write fault, not a query fault."},"2061":{"id":"/observability-137","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Are the three unpublished backends alive: their health endpoints are\n`http://loki:3100/ready`, `http://tempo:3200/ready` and `http://mimir:8080/ready`, all\ncompose-DNS only. Those exact URLs are the `siteMonitor` of the **Observability** group's\ncards on the dashboard, so the card badge is the probe - and because those four\ncontainers have no healthcheck, it is the only automatic one. For the logs behind a red\nbadge, use the container log rather than an exec, since a distroless image has no shell\nto exec into:"},"2062":{"id":"/observability-138","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"Is the whole thing still well-formed on both flag paths:"},"2063":{"id":"/observability-139","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"That gate is what makes the monitoring payloads ordinary source: it parses the three rule\nfiles, `alertmanager.yml`, `blackbox.yml` and the Grafana alerting policy, and parses every\ndashboard JSON through `scripts/lib/parse_json.py` (`scripts/validate.sh:104`). A panel\nthat is not well-formed JSON, a dashboard whose `title` is blank, and the generated Spark\nfile-SD document are gate failures now rather than an empty tile someone notices in the\nbrowser."},"2064":{"id":"/observability-140","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#operator-checks","content":"And the shipped payload checks, which cover the dashboard group and its card URLs:"},"2065":{"id":"/observability-141","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No destination is configured, so nothing pages anyone as shipped.** The rules, the\nroutes and the receivers all exist; only the URL is missing. `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL`\nis empty in `.env.example`, so on a box nobody edited every alert is visible - on\nPrometheus' own alerts page and under Grafana's Alerting pages - and is delivered\nnowhere. Who gets woken is an operator decision, made in the env file."},"2066":{"id":"/observability-142","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No healthcheck or dashboard card for the four new services.** None of\n`node_exporter`, `cadvisor`, `blackbox_exporter` or `alertmanager` declares a\n`healthcheck`, and the dashboard's **Observability** group still carries five cards\n(dashboard.md), so their liveness is the `status` row plus the container\nlog."},"2067":{"id":"/observability-143","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No object storage.** Loki, Mimir and Tempo keep blocks on named volumes on this\nbox's disk. The follow-up is a bucket and the storage config in each of those three\nfiles; nothing about the read path changes when it lands."},"2068":{"id":"/observability-144","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No trace client.** Tempo has an OTLP door and a datasource, and no caller. Pointing\nthe first service at `tempo:4317` is a change in that service, not in this bundle."},"2069":{"id":"/observability-145","page_id":"/observability","tags":["observability","prometheus","grafana","loki","tempo","mimir","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/observability#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"When a symptom shows up as an empty panel, start at\ntroubleshooting.md, Observability section: the failures an operator\nactually hits are all on one side of a seam - the scrape, the write, the tenant path, or\nthe log root Alloy was given."},"2070":{"id":"/operate","page_id":"/operate","type":"page","content":"Operate the box","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Operate the box"],"tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate"},"2071":{"id":"/operate-0","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate","content":"Day-to-day operation — the dashboard and desktops, what to refresh after a git pull, the public shape, and the symptom-first fix list."},"2072":{"id":"/operate-1","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"Operate the box"},"2073":{"id":"/operate-2","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#the-two-commands","content":"The two commands"},"2074":{"id":"/operate-3","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"Where to look"},"2075":{"id":"/operate-4","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#before-you-reach-for-a-hammer","content":"Before you reach for a hammer"},"2076":{"id":"/operate-5","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"**What's on this page**"},"2077":{"id":"/operate-6","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"Which page answers which operational question"},"2078":{"id":"/operate-7","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"The two commands an operator runs most"},"2079":{"id":"/operate-8","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"**What this enables**"},"2080":{"id":"/operate-9","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"Getting to the right runbook in one click instead of searching the README"},"2081":{"id":"/operate-10","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"Refreshing a stale checkout before diagnosing what it looks like"},"2082":{"id":"/operate-11","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#the-two-commands","content":"Everything else is a target those two feed:"},"2083":{"id":"/operate-12","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#the-two-commands","content":"`prod-stack` addresses the stable project by name from any directory; `dev-stack` does the\nsame for the dev one. `update-configs` is what a `git pull` cannot do for you, because the\nfiles that go stale are not tracked — see\nconfig-updates.md."},"2084":{"id":"/operate-13","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                               | Page         |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------ |\n| A card is red, or the dashboard will not load | dashboard.md |","table":"table-0"},"2085":{"id":"/operate-14","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                               | Page               |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| Something fails, and you have an error string | troubleshooting.md |","table":"table-0"},"2086":{"id":"/operate-15","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                         | Page              |\n| --------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| A `git pull` landed and the ports moved | config-updates.md |","table":"table-0"},"2087":{"id":"/operate-16","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                         | Page               |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| A model answered from the wrong backend | litellm-routing.md |","table":"table-0"},"2088":{"id":"/operate-17","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                                    | Page               |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| A workspace is stuck, or its editor will not start | coder-templates.md |","table":"table-0"},"2089":{"id":"/operate-18","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                                   | Page             |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ---------------- |\n| A public name does not resolve or redirects wrong | public-access.md |","table":"table-0"},"2090":{"id":"/operate-19","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                                              | Page           |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------- |\n| The box will not finish booting and nobody is at the console | luks-unlock.md |","table":"table-0"},"2091":{"id":"/operate-20","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                | Page      |\n| ------------------------------ | --------- |\n| The assistant will not sign in | hermes.md |","table":"table-0"},"2092":{"id":"/operate-21","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                                          | Page               |\n| -------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| A credential needs rotating, or the vault is unreachable | operate/secrets.md |","table":"table-0"},"2093":{"id":"/operate-22","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                                                  | Page                  |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| Something is firing, or you want to know what the bundle watches | operate/monitoring.md |","table":"table-0"},"2094":{"id":"/operate-23","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                         | Page                |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| A Spark is not showing up in Prometheus | spark-monitoring.md |","table":"table-0"},"2095":{"id":"/operate-24","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#before-you-reach-for-a-hammer","content":"`storage-clean` is the janitor for the persistent layer and asks before it deletes; the\ndev-stack `reset` and a Kasm reset both destroy state that has no backup. The cost of each\nis spelled out on the entry in troubleshooting.md — read that line\nfirst."},"2096":{"id":"/operate/monitoring","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","type":"page","content":"Monitoring and alerts","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Operate the box"],"tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"url":"/operate/monitoring"},"2097":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-0","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring","content":"Running the observability bundle day to day: where the 30 alerts live, what Alertmanager pages on and off, why its UI stays on loopback, where a silence belongs, and how the three DGX Sparks become Prometheus targets."},"2098":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-1","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"url":"/operate/monitoring#monitoring-and-alerts","content":"Monitoring and alerts"},"2099":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-2","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"url":"/operate/monitoring#alerts","content":"Alerts"},"2100":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-3","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"url":"/operate/monitoring#the-spark-exporters","content":"The Spark exporters"},"2101":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-4","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"url":"/operate/monitoring#where-to-look-next","content":"Where to look next"},"2102":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-5","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#monitoring-and-alerts","content":"**What's on this page**"},"2103":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-6","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#monitoring-and-alerts","content":"The alerts the bundle ships, and the two pages that show them"},"2104":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-7","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#monitoring-and-alerts","content":"What an empty `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL` means for who gets paged"},"2105":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-8","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#monitoring-and-alerts","content":"Why Alertmanager's UI is loopback-only, and where a silence belongs"},"2106":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-9","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#monitoring-and-alerts","content":"Making the three DGX Sparks Prometheus targets with `//scripts:spark-monitor`"},"2107":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-10","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#monitoring-and-alerts","content":"**What this enables**"},"2108":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-11","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#monitoring-and-alerts","content":"Telling a firing alert from a missing exporter before you restart anything"},"2109":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-12","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#monitoring-and-alerts","content":"Enrolling the Sparks without hand-editing a Prometheus config"},"2110":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-13","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#monitoring-and-alerts","content":"Every command here is a Bazel target run from a **host** shell. Inside a Coder workspace\nthe `docker` CLI only reaches that workspace's own DinD sidecar, so a check aimed at the\nstack's own containers has to be run where the stack lives."},"2111":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-14","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#alerts","content":"Three rule files arrive with Prometheus and need no second registration:\n`observability/prometheus/rules/stack.rules.yml` carries the twelve host alerts,\n`spark.rules.yml` the eight Spark alerts, and `inference.rules.yml` the ten inference\nalerts - 30 in all. Prometheus evaluates them. The bundle now also runs Alertmanager\n(`prom/alertmanager:v0.35.0`) to group them, hold them, and decide who hears about them."},"2112":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-15","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#alerts","content":"Three of the host alerts exist because the obvious design has a blind spot. The two\nsingle-exporter gap alerts each wait for the *other* exporter to vouch for the box, so\nboth exporters dying at once - a powered-off host, a restarted Docker - was silent;\n`HostExportersSilent` asks no second opinion and fires on the double absence. A dead\nblackbox prober produces no probe series at all, so nothing it probes can report it;\n`BlackboxProbeGap` reads `up{job=\"blackbox\"}`, which is why Prometheus scrapes the prober\nas a service of its own. And the same argument one layer up: every rule pages *through*\nAlertmanager, and an Alertmanager that is gone loses no series that any other rule reads,\nso the bundle keeps evaluating and firing into nothing. `AlertmanagerGap` is critical\nwhere those two are warning, and asks both shapes of the fault - `absent()` for the\nservice being gone and `up == 0` for a container that runs without answering."},"2113":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-16","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#alerts","content":"One Spark alert closes the same shape on the other side. `SparkDown` needs the probe to\nfail as well as the scrape, so a box that is genuinely up and simply not reporting host\nmetrics pages nothing, and `SparkGpuSeriesSilent` asks only the DCGM family.\n`SparkHostMetricsSilent` is the one that reads a box whose health probe succeeds while\n`spark:box_up` has never been 1 - the usual cause being a hand-edited file where the\nstamped `exporter` label lost its exact spelling, which drops that box's group out of\nevery job's keep filter."},"2114":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-17","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#alerts","content":"What that means day to day:"},"2115":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-18","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#alerts","content":"**An empty `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL` is the shipped state, and it pages nobody.**\nThe rules still fire and stay readable on Prometheus's `/alerts` page and on Grafana's\nAlerting page; only the delivery step is silent. Set the key to a webhook URL when you\nwant a person told, and never put a URL that carries a credential in `.env`."},"2116":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-19","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#alerts","content":"**Alertmanager's UI is published on loopback only** (`OBS_ALERTMANAGER_PORT`, 9093),\nfor the same reason Coder's `:2112` metrics port is: that UI can silence a firing\nalert, so an unauthenticated silencer on the LAN is a way to make a real fault\ninvisible. Read it from a shell on the stack host."},"2117":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-20","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#alerts","content":"**Make silences in Alertmanager, not in Grafana.** Alertmanager owns grouping,\nsilences and the repeat interval, so a silence created anywhere else does not stop the\nroute - and the two views then disagree about why nobody was paged."},"2118":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-21","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#alerts","content":"The three exporters feeding those rules sit beside the bundle on the same\n`observability` profile, all published to `127.0.0.1` only: node_exporter\n(`quay.io/prometheus/node-exporter:v1.12.1`) on `OBS_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (9101), cAdvisor\n(`ghcr.io/google/cadvisor:v0.61.0`) on `OBS_CADVISOR_PORT` (9290), and blackbox_exporter\n(`prom/blackbox-exporter:v0.29.1`) on `OBS_BLACKBOX_PORT` (9115). The data path behind\nthem, and what each config file costs to change, is\nobservability.md."},"2119":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-22","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#the-spark-exporters","content":"The three DGX Sparks are not scrape targets until you make them one. Each box needs two\ncontainers - node_exporter on `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (9100) and the NVIDIA DCGM\nexporter on `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` (9400) - and neither exists on a box until\n`--apply` creates it:"},"2120":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-23","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#the-spark-exporters","content":"Plan is the default and opens no ssh session and no docker call, so the first two are\nsafe to run before anything is decided. `SPARK_MONITOR_ENABLED` (false as shipped) is the\nopt-in that decides whether `--targets` prints the three boxes or an empty array, and the\nDCGM image comes from `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE`. That one tag -\n`nvcr.io/nvidia/k8s/dcgm-exporter:4.4.2-4.7.0-ubuntu22.04` - is the pin this stack could\nnot verify upstream, so `--apply` is what proves it: it surfaces whatever the registry\nanswers when the pull fails, and `--status` prints the tag a box actually runs."},"2121":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-24","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#the-spark-exporters","content":"`--targets` prints the `sparks.json` that Prometheus reads through `file_sd_configs`; the\nredirect that writes it on the host, the refresh interval, and what an absent file mean\nare in `observability/spark-targets/README.md`. The box-by-box walkthrough, the counter\nfile the DCGM container is handed, and the failure modes are\nspark-monitoring.md."},"2122":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-25","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#where-to-look-next","content":"Observability bundle - the ten profile-gated services, the path\na metric takes, the retention, and what the bundle costs on disk"},"2123":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-26","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#where-to-look-next","content":"Spark monitoring - enrolling the three DGX Sparks: the two\nexporter containers, the `SPARK_MONITOR_ENABLED` opt-in, and what `--apply` does to a\nbox."},"2124":{"id":"/operate/monitoring-27","page_id":"/operate/monitoring","tags":["operate","observability","prometheus","alertmanager","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/monitoring#where-to-look-next","content":"Rotate a credential - what to re-check after a key the bundle reads moves"},"2125":{"id":"/operate/secrets","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"page","content":"Secrets with Infisical","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Operate the box"],"tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets"},"2126":{"id":"/operate/secrets-0","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets","content":"The opt-in secrets vault: the twelve managed keys, the operator CLI verbs and their exit codes, the adoption sequence, what the workspace boot publishes and what it refuses, what happens when the vault is unreachable, and how a rotated credential reaches its service."},"2127":{"id":"/operate/secrets-1","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"Secrets with Infisical"},"2128":{"id":"/operate/secrets-2","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#the-flag","content":"The flag"},"2129":{"id":"/operate/secrets-3","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"The workspace boot fetch"},"2130":{"id":"/operate/secrets-4","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"The three services"},"2131":{"id":"/operate/secrets-5","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"The operator CLI"},"2132":{"id":"/operate/secrets-6","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Adopting the vault"},"2133":{"id":"/operate/secrets-7","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"The twelve managed keys"},"2134":{"id":"/operate/secrets-8","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"Keys the vault does not hold"},"2135":{"id":"/operate/secrets-9","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"When Infisical is unreachable"},"2136":{"id":"/operate/secrets-10","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-coder_pg_password","content":"Rotating CODER_PG_PASSWORD"},"2137":{"id":"/operate/secrets-11","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"The credential boundary"},"2138":{"id":"/operate/secrets-12","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"What is deliberately not here"},"2139":{"id":"/operate/secrets-13","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"**What's on this page**"},"2140":{"id":"/operate/secrets-14","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"What the integration is, and the one property it deliberately does not change"},"2141":{"id":"/operate/secrets-15","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The flag that decides whether the bundle exists, and the second gate on the workspace side"},"2142":{"id":"/operate/secrets-16","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"What the workspace boot publishes into `~/.infisical/agent.env`, what it refuses, and the residual it cannot refuse"},"2143":{"id":"/operate/secrets-17","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The three `secrets`-profile containers, and the CLI verb that reads the flag"},"2144":{"id":"/operate/secrets-18","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The adoption sequence, one command per step, with the container each step touches"},"2145":{"id":"/operate/secrets-19","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The twelve managed keys, who consumes each one, and what breaks when it is wrong"},"2146":{"id":"/operate/secrets-20","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The three runners that refresh the env file from the vault before compose renders"},"2147":{"id":"/operate/secrets-21","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"What a vault outage does and does not do to `up`, to `pull`, and to a workspace boot"},"2148":{"id":"/operate/secrets-22","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The `CODER_PG_PASSWORD` rotation path, the volume-init trap under it, and the probe defect that blocks the verb today"},"2149":{"id":"/operate/secrets-23","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"**What this enables**"},"2150":{"id":"/operate/secrets-24","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"Holding the stack's credentials in one place instead of in twelve lines of a dotenv file"},"2151":{"id":"/operate/secrets-25","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"Rotating a key without opening an editor, and knowing when the running service picks it up"},"2152":{"id":"/operate/secrets-26","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"Deciding whether adopting the vault is worth it, from what is actually wired"},"2153":{"id":"/operate/secrets-27","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"Self-hosted Infisical is the source of record for twelve of this stack's credentials,\nbehind a flag that defaults off. Three Compose services under profile `secrets` hold the\nvault, its Postgres and its Redis; one operator CLI moves keys between the vault and the\nhost's dotenv file; and every service keeps reading credentials the way it always did."},"2154":{"id":"/operate/secrets-28","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"That last sentence is the design, so it is worth stating exactly. Compose interpolates\n`${VAR:-default}` against the `.env` beside `docker-compose.yml`, and nothing in the\ncompose layer ever opens a connection to a secrets service. An unreachable vault must not\nbe able to stop `up`, so the integration does not replace the dotenv chain - it feeds it.\n`pull` writes the managed keys into `.env` and compose reads that file exactly as it\nalways has. The vault is an editor for `.env`, not a dependency of the project. Read that\nas the whole delivery path: a credential change reaches a service through the env file and\na recreate, never through a live call to the vault. The one place the deploy path dials the\nvault at all is the runners' own refresh before compose interpolates, and that call is\nbest-effort - one `warning:` and the bring-up continues on whatever the file already held."},"2155":{"id":"/operate/secrets-29","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"This is the **free self-hosted tier**. No dynamic secrets, no cross-project sharing, and\nno `LICENSE_KEY` anywhere in the checkout: both of those features are paid, and the\nbundle ships neither. What is here is static shared secrets in one project, exported as\ndotenv."},"2156":{"id":"/operate/secrets-30","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-flag","content":"One key decides whether the bundle exists: `INFISICAL_ENABLED`, default `false`, in\n`.env.example` beside its own comment block. Owner: `platform`. Expiry: `2026-12-31`."},"2157":{"id":"/operate/secrets-31","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-flag","content":"Unlike `OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED`, which ships on, **an unset key means off**. The\nacceptance test is `truthy()` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` - `on`, `1`, `true`, `yes` - and\nanything else, including the key being absent entirely, is off. Off means the three\ncontainers are never selected (`profiles: [\"secrets\"]`), the runner CLIs pass no profile\nargv (`compose_infisical_args` in `scripts/lib/infisical.sh` prints nothing, mirroring\n`compose_observability_args`), and every mutating verb of the CLI refuses with a `note:`\nthat names the key to set. That is deliberate: a subsystem nobody opted into must not be\nable to hold the stack's credentials hostage."},"2158":{"id":"/operate/secrets-32","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-flag","content":"To adopt it, set the key in the env file that belongs to the project you run - `.env` for\nthe stable stack, `dev-stack.env` for the dev one - and bring the stack up as usual:"},"2159":{"id":"/operate/secrets-33","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-flag","content":"The runner appends the profile argv, so the three services come up with the rest of the\nproject. A profile selects services; it does not delete what is already running, so\nflipping the key back off needs a `down` (or deleting the three by name) before the\ncontainers actually go away. Their named volumes survive that, and `reset` is the verb\nthat destroys them."},"2160":{"id":"/operate/secrets-34","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-flag","content":"There is a **second gate** under the first: `INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START`, default `false`.\nIt decides only whether a workspace's boot fetch dials the vault at all. It is off by\ndefault so a workspace never waits on the vault, and it does nothing while\n`INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off. Both gates are read inside the boot body itself, so the rendered\nscript is one text for every workspace and the flag pair arrives on the agent environment."},"2161":{"id":"/operate/secrets-35","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-flag","content":"Both gates reach the templates through `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`: the CLI forwards\n`--var infisical_enabled=true` and `--var infisical_pull_on_start=true` only for a truthy value in\n`.env`, so the shipped state pushes neither word and each template's own `default = false` stands.\nWith the flag off the `coder_script` resource is absent outright - no script, no boot log line,\nno `INFISICAL_*` key on the agent environment - and the `.bashrc` hook is not written either,\nbecause that line is gated on `infisical_hook`, which both twins set from `var.infisical_enabled`."},"2162":{"id":"/operate/secrets-36","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl` is the body both twins render into\n`coder_script \"infisical_secrets\"` (`run_on_start`, 120s timeout). One fact about its output\ndecides everything else in this section: `~/.infisical/agent.env` is sourced by every shell the\nworkspace starts, under `set -a`, from the line the startup script appends to `~/.bashrc`. It is\nnot a config file one process reads; it is an `export` line per key in the environment of every\nterminal, tmux pane and command from then on, and it outlives the start that wrote it."},"2163":{"id":"/operate/secrets-37","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"Two kinds of volume meet in that path and they are not the same thing. What rides per owner is\nstate only: `~/.grok/notes`, `~/.grok/memory` and `~/.grok/sessions` on `grok-notes-<owner-id>`,\n`grok-memory-<owner-id>` and `grok-sessions-<owner-id>`, plus the `vscode-settings-<owner-id>`\noverlay at `~/.shared/vscode`. Everything else under `~/.grok` is a real file on that workspace's\nown home volume, `config.toml` above all - it carries this workspace's own model lanes, its\nsession-affinity key and its Grok theme. `~/.infisical` rides no per-owner mount at all, so\n`agent.env` sits on this workspace's own home and only this workspace reads it. That asymmetry is\nwhy the body filters rather than trusting a share boundary: a file a workspace can read is a file\nanything running inside it can read."},"2164":{"id":"/operate/secrets-38","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"The body gates itself twice before it dials anything. First the **six configured names**:\n`INFISICAL_DOMAIN`, `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID`, `INFISICAL_MACHINE_IDENTITY_ID`,\n`INFISICAL_ENVIRONMENT`, `INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_ID` and\n`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET`. Any one of them empty prints which names are missing and\nwhere they come from, and exits 0. Then `INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START`, whose off path prints that the\nboot \"keeps the credentials it already has\" and also exits 0. Every other failure path - no\n`infisical` binary in the image, no `HOME`, a refused Universal Auth login, an empty export, a\nrefused publish - exits 0 as well. The golden image bakes the pinned CLI (`images/dev/Dockerfile`,\n`INFISICAL_CLI_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env`), so the missing-binary path is what an\nimage older than that pin hits. A vault outage has never once stopped a workspace."},"2165":{"id":"/operate/secrets-39","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"What gets published is whatever the workspace's own machine identity can read in the project,\nwhich is what the vault scopes - the twelve-key inventory is a host-side list\n(`infisical/managed-secrets.txt`) and is deliberately not repeated in the body. So the workspace is\nnot limited to those twelve names, and two filters run on the key name before a line is written:"},"2166":{"id":"/operate/secrets-40","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"Every key beginning `INFISICAL_` is dropped, so the vault's address and its Universal Auth pair -\nboth plausible keys in the very project being exported - are never republished as workspace\nenvironment. Those names reach the workspace only on the agent environment, which Coder renders\nper start and never writes to disk."},"2167":{"id":"/operate/secrets-41","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"Every name on the body's deny list is dropped, compared on the key upper-cased so `BASH_ENV`,\n`bash_env` and `Bash_Env` all fall in one arm. The list is what a shell, the dynamic loader, an\ninterpreter or git treats as code or as loader configuration rather than as data: `BASH_ENV` and\n`ENV` (executed by every non-interactive bash), `PROMPT_COMMAND` (by every interactive one),\n`PS4` and `IFS` (how the sourcing shell parses itself), `PATH` and `HOME` (which binary and\nwhich config the next command reads), the `LD_*` group (`LD_PRELOAD`, `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`,\n`LD_AUDIT`, `LD_DEBUG`), the interpreter-option group (`NODE_OPTIONS`, `PYTHONPATH`,\n`PYTHONSTARTUP`, `PERL5OPT`, `PERL5LIB`, `RUBYOPT`), the `GIT_*` group (`GIT_SSH_COMMAND`,\n`GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`, `GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF`, `GIT_DIR`), and the program-choice group (`PAGER`,\n`LESS`, `EDITOR`, `VISUAL`, `SSH_ASKPASS`, `SUDO_ASKPASS`)."},"2168":{"id":"/operate/secrets-42","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"After the loop the staged file is read once more against one alternation built from that same\nvariable, and a match makes the body **refuse to publish**: it removes the staged file, prints\n`infisical: refused to publish <home>/.infisical/agent.env; the staged file names a\nshell-control variable. Nothing was written and no secret value is shown.` and exits 0. The\n`agent.env` a previous boot wrote then stays exactly as it was, which is the point of refusing\nrather than correcting. The obvious alternative - appending `unset PATH HOME IFS` lines to the\nfile - is the wrong shape: those names would be gone from every shell that sourced the file and\nfrom everything those shells then launch. A refusal costs one refresh; that would cost the\nworkspace."},"2169":{"id":"/operate/secrets-43","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"Every value is rewritten rather than copied, and that is a security step, not a stylistic one -\nsee the last bullet of The credential boundary for the upstream\nquoting bug that makes it necessary. Two shapes still do not land, and the log says a different\nthing for each:"},"2170":{"id":"/operate/secrets-44","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"**A value whose opening single quote does not close on its own line** - a PEM or a multi-line\ncertificate is the shape - is dropped whole. Its continuation lines are swallowed until one\ncarries a quote, because publishing them would invent keys out of secret bytes. One note per\nboot, naming neither key nor value: `infisical: skipped a secret whose value spans more than\none line; only single-line secrets are published.` The body carries no other shape, so a\ncredential of that shape has to reach a workspace by some path other than this fetch."},"2171":{"id":"/operate/secrets-45","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"**A key the shell could not use as an identifier** - empty, starting with a digit, or holding a\ncharacter outside `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, which is also where blank and comment lines fall out - is\nskipped silently. Nothing is printed for it; the only line that says how many keys landed is the\nsuccess line, `ok: infisical: loaded N secret keys into <home>/.infisical/agent.env`, and it\ncounts what the publish loop actually wrote."},"2172":{"id":"/operate/secrets-46","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"**The security boundary**, stated so no operator has to guess at it: because `agent.env` is\nsourced by every shell a workspace starts, a vault key that the shell, the dynamic loader, an\ninterpreter or git would treat as code is not a secret, it is a command line chosen by whoever can\nwrite a secret in the project. The two defences are the deny list above, matched on the\nnormalised case, and the pre-publish pass that refuses to write the file at all and leaves the\nprevious `agent.env` in place."},"2173":{"id":"/operate/secrets-47","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"**The residual, honestly.** That list names what a shell or a loader *executes*. It is not a list\nof every environment variable that changes what a program does, and it cannot be one. Anyone with\nwrite access to the Infisical project still chooses which ordinary variables a workspace boots\nwith, and because the fetch's line is appended to `~/.bashrc` and runs under `set -a`, one of\nthose names can overwrite whatever that file set earlier in it. Treat write access\nto the vault project as the trust boundary it is: it is shell access to every workspace that boots\nwith the fetch on. Read access is a much smaller grant than write, and the machine identity the\nworkspaces authenticate as should hold only the former."},"2174":{"id":"/operate/secrets-48","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"The file itself is written mode 600 inside a mode 700 `~/.infisical`, staged beside its\ndestination and moved into place in one step rather than appended, so a reader never sees a\nhalf-written file. `docs/coder-templates.md` carries the template side of all of this: the eight\n`infisical_*` variables, why the credential pair rides the agent environment rather than the\nrendered script body, and the order the boot body runs in."},"2175":{"id":"/operate/secrets-49","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"| Service     | Image                           | Job                                                                                                                         | Published                                                                                                                                 | Volume |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ |\n| `infisical` | `infisical/infisical:v0.167.10` | The vault. Serves the UI and the API on container port `8080`, runs its own cron and queue loop against Postgres and Redis. | `\"${INFISICAL_PORT:-8182}:8080\"` - published plainly, the way `:4000` and `:8181` are, so a workspace reaches it through the host-gateway | -      |","table":"table-0"},"2176":{"id":"/operate/secrets-50","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"| Service        | Image                | Job                                                                         | Published                  | Volume                                            |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical-db` | `postgres:16-alpine` | Infisical's store. Infisical is PostgreSQL-only; the Mongo path is retired. | nothing - compose DNS only | `infisical-db-data` -> `/var/lib/postgresql/data` |","table":"table-0"},"2177":{"id":"/operate/secrets-51","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"| Service           | Image            | Job                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Published                  | Volume                                                                                                                                    |\n| ----------------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical-redis` | `redis:7-alpine` | Job queue and cache. Runs `--save '' --appendonly no` under the `noeviction` policy: a `volatile-*` policy evicts queued jobs under memory pressure, and a dropped job is a sync that silently never runs. | nothing - compose DNS only | `infisical-redis-data` -> `/data` (empty while persistence is off; declared so a future change lands on a volume, not the writable layer) |","table":"table-0"},"2178":{"id":"/operate/secrets-52","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"All three carry `restart: unless-stopped`, the `secrets` profile, and the same three-line\n`json-file` logging block the rest of the stack uses, with `tag:` equal to the service\nname so Alloy ships each container to Loki under `container=<name>`. None of the three is\na Prometheus target - Infisical publishes no `/metrics` endpoint the stack can rely on and\nneither alpine image ships one - so they are recorded as log-only in\nobservability.md."},"2179":{"id":"/operate/secrets-53","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"The `infisical` container takes `ENCRYPTION_KEY`, `AUTH_SECRET`, `SITE_URL`,\n`DB_CONNECTION_URI`, `REDIS_URL`, `HOST=0.0.0.0` and `PORT=8080` from the environment.\n`DB_CONNECTION_URI` and the db service's `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` both interpolate\n`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD`, which is the only thing keeping the role and its pointer in step\nacross a rotation. The public name, when you turn one on, is `STACK_INFISICAL_NAME`\n(default label `infisical`) composed by `scripts/lib/domain.sh` like every other name;\nthe router sends that Host to `http://infisical:8080`. See\npublic-access.md."},"2180":{"id":"/operate/secrets-54","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"Everything an operator does goes through one CLI, from any cwd:"},"2181":{"id":"/operate/secrets-55","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"| Verb     | What it does                                                                                                                                                           | Writes  |\n| -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `status` | What this box is configured to do: the flag, the profile argv, the port, the vault URL, the inventory size, and which required keys are still empty. Contacts nothing. | nothing |","table":"table-1"},"2182":{"id":"/operate/secrets-56","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"| Verb    | What it does                                                                                                                                                              | Writes  |\n| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `check` | `status` plus whether the CLI binary is installed, whether the required keys are all set, and whether the machine identity can actually exchange a token. Run this first. | nothing |","table":"table-1"},"2183":{"id":"/operate/secrets-57","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"| Verb          | What it does                                                                                                    | Writes     |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| `install-cli` | Fetch the pinned release (`INFISICAL_CLI_VERSION`) and install the binary. Never pipes a download into a shell. | the binary |","table":"table-1"},"2184":{"id":"/operate/secrets-58","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"| Verb        | What it does                                                                                                                          | Writes                    |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `bootstrap` | Create the super admin, the organization and the machine identity on a fresh instance, then record the identifiers into the env file. | the instance, then `.env` |","table":"table-1"},"2185":{"id":"/operate/secrets-59","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"| Verb   | What it does                                                                                                            | Writes    |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| `push` | Seed or migrate the managed keys from the env file into the vault, then read the vault back and compare a hash per key. | the vault |","table":"table-1"},"2186":{"id":"/operate/secrets-60","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"| Verb   | What it does                                                 | Writes |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------ |\n| `pull` | Reconcile the managed keys from the vault into the env file. | `.env` |","table":"table-1"},"2187":{"id":"/operate/secrets-61","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"| Verb         | What it does                                                          | Writes                 |\n| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| `rotate KEY` | Mint a fresh value for one managed key, vault first, env file second. | the vault, then `.env` |","table":"table-1"},"2188":{"id":"/operate/secrets-62","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"| Verb         | What it does                                                  | Writes  |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `run -- CMD` | Exec a command with the project's secrets in its environment. | nothing |","table":"table-1"},"2189":{"id":"/operate/secrets-63","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"`--env-file PATH` points the verb at a dotenv file other than the `.env` compose itself\nreads. `--help` prints the usage block above."},"2190":{"id":"/operate/secrets-64","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"Exit codes, which are how a script tells \"the vault said no\" from \"the vault is switched\noff\":"},"2191":{"id":"/operate/secrets-65","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"**0** - the verb did its job. Always for `status` and `check`: a check that fails on a\nstack that never adopted Infisical is a check nobody runs. `run` exits 0 on the\nflag-off refusal too, having printed the `note:`; every other mutating verb exits 5."},"2192":{"id":"/operate/secrets-66","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"**1** - a real failure. The CLI errored, a read-back hash disagreed, a `rotate` target\nis not a managed key. A `rotate` or `run` that names no key and no command lands here as\nwell, on an `error:` line."},"2193":{"id":"/operate/secrets-67","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"**2** - an unknown subcommand, always raised before any work starts."},"2194":{"id":"/operate/secrets-68","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"**5** - refused, because `INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off or a prerequisite is empty. Printed\nas a `note:` that names the key to set."},"2195":{"id":"/operate/secrets-69","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"There is deliberately no `--json` mode: a JSON answer about a credential is an answer that\nleaks it in a different font."},"2196":{"id":"/operate/secrets-70","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"Nothing else in the repo shells out to this CLI. The three runners that address the compose\nproject - `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack`, `//scripts:prod-stack` and `//scripts:update-stack` -\nsource `scripts/lib/infisical.sh` and call `infisical_pull` themselves, immediately before compose\ninterpolates the env file, so a key rotated in the vault lands on the bring-up in front of you\ninstead of waiting for a human to run the verb. Each prints one `-> vault: refreshing the env file\nfrom Infisical` phase line when the flag is on, and each treats a failed pull as one `warning:`\nnaming the retry - `the env file was not refreshed from Infisical; <file> keeps the credentials it\nalready has - retry with bazelisk run //scripts:infisical -- pull` - and then continues. The\nbranch never runs while the flag is off, so a stack that never adopted the vault reads and prints\nexactly what it did before. `bazelisk run //scripts:update-stack -- --dry-run` plans that fetch\n(`note: dry run: would refresh <file> from Infisical`) and writes nothing; the two stack runners\nhave no dry-run mode, so their refresh happens only on a real `up`."},"2197":{"id":"/operate/secrets-71","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"The sequence, in this order. Each step names what it changes."},"2198":{"id":"/operate/secrets-72","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Give the vault its own bootstrap credentials and its admin address in `.env`.\n`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD`, `INFISICAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY` and `INFISICAL_AUTH_SECRET` ship\nas `change-me-*` placeholders in `.env.example`, and `INFISICAL_ADMIN_EMAIL` ships\nempty while `INFISICAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD` ships as a placeholder - `bootstrap` refuses\neither if it is still empty or still starts with `change-me`. These four cannot come\nfrom the vault, because the vault needs them to start."},"2199":{"id":"/operate/secrets-73","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Turn the flag on: `INFISICAL_ENABLED=true` in the project's env file."},"2200":{"id":"/operate/secrets-74","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Install the CLI binary on the host."},"2201":{"id":"/operate/secrets-75","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"This writes only the binary, at the path `infisical_cli_install_path` prints, and nothing\nelse changes. The release it fetches is the pin: `INFISICAL_CLI_VERSION` in `.env.example`\n(0.45.8 as shipped), from the goreleaser asset name the seam builds. Nothing here pulls a\nversion out of the network."},"2202":{"id":"/operate/secrets-76","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Start the three services. `up` selects the profile now that the flag is on:"},"2203":{"id":"/operate/secrets-77","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"That creates `infisical`, `infisical-db` and `infisical-redis`, on the image tags the\n`image:` lines in `docker-compose.yml` pin (`infisical/infisical:v0.167.10` as shipped). The\nvault answers on `http://127.0.0.1:8182` (`INFISICAL_PORT`) on the stack host, and on the\npublished LAN face. Expect the two alpine containers to read `running` rather than `healthy`:\nneither declares a healthcheck, because the only consumer waits on `service_started`. With\nthe flag already on, this first `up` also tries the env-file refresh and gets nothing: there\nis no `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID` yet, so the seam prints `note: the pull was refused before it\nran; <file> keeps the values it has` and the runner follows with its `warning:` line. That\npair is noise until step 5 writes the identifiers - it is not a failed bring-up."},"2204":{"id":"/operate/secrets-78","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Bootstrap the instance:"},"2205":{"id":"/operate/secrets-79","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"This creates the super admin, the organization and the machine identity **inside the\n`infisical` container**, and writes the returned identifiers into `.env`. It is\nidempotent in the safe direction: if `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID` or\n`INFISICAL_MACHINE_IDENTITY_ID` is already set it prints a `note:` and refuses to run\nagain. It needs `python3` on PATH to parse the response, and the CLI from step 3. The\nmachine identity's own token is checked for presence and deliberately not stored: it stays\ninside Infisical and every later run exchanges the pair below for a fresh one."},"2206":{"id":"/operate/secrets-80","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Paste the machine identity's Universal Auth pair into `.env`. `bootstrap` prints the `note:`\nthat says so once it has written the identifiers, and nothing downstream guesses:\n`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_ID` and `INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET` are two of\nthe six keys `check` reports as empty, and a `push` or `pull` with either blank is refused\nbefore it authenticates. Create the credentials for that identity in the Infisical UI and put\nboth values in the env file; neither belongs in a commit."},"2207":{"id":"/operate/secrets-81","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Adopt what is already in `.env`:"},"2208":{"id":"/operate/secrets-82","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"This seeds the twelve managed keys into the vault, skipping any key the env file does\nnot hold or holds empty - with a `warning:`, never an empty write. It then exports the\nproject again and compares a hash per key, so a set the server accepted and dropped is\nreported as a failure rather than a clean seed."},"2209":{"id":"/operate/secrets-83","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Prove the round trip:"},"2210":{"id":"/operate/secrets-84","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"With the vault and `.env` agreeing this writes nothing new. It is the read path, so it\nis the one to run before you trust `push`."},"2211":{"id":"/operate/secrets-85","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Recreate the consumers so they pick up what the vault holds:"},"2212":{"id":"/operate/secrets-86","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Compose reads `.env` when it creates a container, and this `up` refreshes that file from\nthe vault first (see The operator CLI for the runners' own pull).\nNothing is recreated until\nyou ask for it, so the `pull` in step 8 alone changes no running service - this step does."},"2213":{"id":"/operate/secrets-87","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"Then, any time after, `check` is the single command that asks every precondition at once:"},"2214":{"id":"/operate/secrets-88","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"The inventory is data, not code: `infisical/managed-secrets.txt`, read through\n`infisical_managed_keys()`. Never re-hardcode the list. The count is asserted - `check`\nwarns when the inventory is not exactly twelve, because a silently-short list would leave\na credential outside the vault while still reporting a clean sync. Every key must also\nexist in `.env.example`, since compose keeps interpolating that file."},"2215":{"id":"/operate/secrets-89","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                 | Consumed by                          | What breaks if it is wrong |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------ | -------------------------- |\n| `CODER_PG_PASSWORD` | `db` container's `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` |                            |","table":"table-2"},"2216":{"id":"/operate/secrets-90","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                  | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                                                             |\n| -------------------- | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` |             | `/metrics` carries this key as its bearer token too, so that target goes down with it. |","table":"table-2"},"2217":{"id":"/operate/secrets-91","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                    | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong            |\n| ---------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_SECRET` |             | The dashboard's own login is refused. |","table":"table-2"},"2218":{"id":"/operate/secrets-92","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                      | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                        |\n| ------------------------ | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` |             | The dashboard asks for a credential nobody holds. |","table":"table-2"},"2219":{"id":"/operate/secrets-93","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                         | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                                                         |\n| --------------------------- | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD` |             | The dashboard on `:9119` and its public name both refuse the login, on every door. |","table":"table-2"},"2220":{"id":"/operate/secrets-94","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                       | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                                                 |\n| ------------------------- | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HERMES_DASHBOARD_SECRET` |             | The dashboard session does not hold; the Chat tab will not stay signed in. |","table":"table-2"},"2221":{"id":"/operate/secrets-95","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                     | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                                                                 |\n| ----------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `HERMES_API_SERVER_KEY` |             | The programmatic door the agents call with answers unauthorised; the Chat tab still works. |","table":"table-2"},"2222":{"id":"/operate/secrets-96","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                      | Consumed by             | What breaks if it is wrong                                                                                                                           |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD` | Grafana (observability) | The observability admin login fails. `GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER` is not managed, so an operator who changed only the password is locked out by a half-sync. |","table":"table-2"},"2223":{"id":"/operate/secrets-97","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                       | Consumed by      | What breaks if it is wrong                             |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `STACK_SEARCH_SECRET_KEY` | `SEARXNG_SECRET` | `search` lane answers empty rather than with an error. |","table":"table-2"},"2224":{"id":"/operate/secrets-98","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key               | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                                                                                                                                              |\n| ----------------- | ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SPARK_1_API_KEY` |             | That box's deployments fail upstream auth and every alias riding them errors - while the other two boxes keep serving, which is what makes it read as a partial outage. |","table":"table-2"},"2225":{"id":"/operate/secrets-99","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key               | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong |\n| ----------------- | ----------- | -------------------------- |\n| `SPARK_2_API_KEY` |             | As above, for that box.    |","table":"table-2"},"2226":{"id":"/operate/secrets-100","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key               | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong |\n| ----------------- | ----------- | -------------------------- |\n| `SPARK_3_API_KEY` |             | As above, for that box.    |","table":"table-2"},"2227":{"id":"/operate/secrets-101","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"The first eight are the list `scripts/bootstrap.sh` used to carry twice; that duplication\nis what this inventory replaced."},"2228":{"id":"/operate/secrets-102","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"Two lists sit outside the inventory, and they are outside for different reasons."},"2229":{"id":"/operate/secrets-103","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"Four secret-shaped keys compose interpolates are among them, and each one is\noutside for a reason worth knowing before you add it:"},"2230":{"id":"/operate/secrets-104","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD`, `INFISICAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, `INFISICAL_AUTH_SECRET` - the\nvault's own bootstrap credentials. By definition they cannot come from the vault: it\nneeds them to start. They live in `.env`, and you set them."},"2231":{"id":"/operate/secrets-105","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"`CODER_DEFAULT_TOKEN_LIFETIME` - a duration, not a secret."},"2232":{"id":"/operate/secrets-106","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"Three more are credentials that never appear in a compose interpolation, so they\ntravel to their consumer through a CLI rather than through `${VAR}`:"},"2233":{"id":"/operate/secrets-107","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"`INFISICAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD` - the login `bootstrap` uses to create the project and the\nmachine identity (`scripts/lib/infisical.sh:1216`), minted by\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh:119`. It is spent before the project exists, so there is no\nvault to read it from yet."},"2234":{"id":"/operate/secrets-108","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET` - the machine identity's Universal Auth\nsecret, which is the credential every fetch authenticates with\n(`scripts/lib/infisical.sh:803`). Storing it in the project it unlocks would make the\nvault a precondition of reading the vault."},"2235":{"id":"/operate/secrets-109","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` - the optional Context7 MCP lane's upstream key, forwarded as\n`context7_api_key` by `scripts/push-template.sh:573-577` and seeded into\n`~/.grok/config.toml` by `//scripts:seed-dev-env`. Most operators never set it, and\n`pull` prints one `warning:` per inventory key the vault does not carry, so shipping\nit in the inventory would make the default state a warning on every fetch."},"2236":{"id":"/operate/secrets-110","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"Anything compose interpolates that looks like a credential and appears nowhere on this\npage is either a bug in the inventory or a bug in the compose file, and `check` is the\ncommand that catches the first."},"2237":{"id":"/operate/secrets-111","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"The outage is bounded, because the only read the deploy path makes is a best-effort refresh\nwhose failure is one `warning:` line."},"2238":{"id":"/operate/secrets-112","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"**`up` cannot be stopped by it.** Compose never contacts Infisical: it interpolates `.env`,\nwhich is a file on disk, and whether the three containers are part of the project is a\nprofile decision made from one key, not a network result. The runner's own pull runs before\nthat interpolation and a failed one costs a line, not the bring-up - `warning: the env file\nwas not refreshed from Infisical; <file> keeps the credentials it already has - retry with\nbazelisk run //scripts:infisical -- pull` - and compose recreates against what the file\nalready held. `//scripts:prod-stack` and `//scripts:update-stack` behave the same way; the\nlatter plans the fetch instead of running it under `--dry-run`."},"2239":{"id":"/operate/secrets-113","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"**`pull` leaves `.env` alone and says so.** A failed export is an `error:` line naming\nthe file as untouched, and the verb exits 1. An export that comes back empty is a\n`warning:` and the same untouched file. A key the vault does not carry, or carries\nempty, is a `warning:` per key and the existing value in `.env` stays exactly as it was"},"2240":{"id":"/operate/secrets-114","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"a half-seeded project must not lock every service out of its own credential."},"2241":{"id":"/operate/secrets-115","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"**A workspace still starts.** The boot fetch is best-effort: with the flag off, with\n`INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START` off, with no CLI in the image (the golden image bakes the\npinned release, so that is an image older than the pin), with a refused Universal Auth\nlogin, with an empty export, or with a refused publish, it exits 0 and the workspace\ncontinues its startup. `agent.env` keeps whatever the last start wrote, since the body\nstages beside the destination and moves in one step rather than truncating first. A vault\noutage has never once stopped a workspace."},"2242":{"id":"/operate/secrets-116","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"**`rotate` stops at the first half.** If the vault refuses the write, `.env` is left\nuntouched and the verb exits 1 - the stack keeps running on the old value. The reverse\ncase prints the recovery in the error line: if the vault took the new value and the env\nwrite failed, re-run `pull` to converge."},"2243":{"id":"/operate/secrets-117","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"So neither side can fail to *start* over a vault that is down. The workspace is the one that\nnever can: every path in the boot body, refusals included, exits 0 and the agent goes on with\nthe environment it already had. The stack is the one with state to corrupt, and the way it\nbreaks is the opposite of an outage - the refresh succeeds by writing, so a vault that answers\nbut holds a value nothing authenticates with is what brings `coder`, `litellm`, `homepage`,\n`hermes`, `grafana` or `searxng` up unable to log in. That is a converge-and-recreate problem,\nnot a start problem: fix the value in the vault, run `up` again, and read the `db` and `coder`\nlogs if Coder takes longer than a recreate to come back."},"2244":{"id":"/operate/secrets-118","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-coder_pg_password","content":"The verb mints a fresh value, writes the **vault first**, and only touches `.env` once the\nvault accepted it - so there is no moment where `.env` holds a password the vault does not\nknow about. `rotate` names one managed key; naming anything outside the inventory is an\n`error:` and exit 1."},"2245":{"id":"/operate/secrets-119","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-coder_pg_password","content":"Whether a name counts as managed is decided by `infisical_is_managed_key` in\n`scripts/lib/infisical.sh`, which reads the inventory and compares the names inside the\nshell. The first version piped the reader into `grep -qx` instead, and under the CLI's\n`set -euo pipefail` that lost: `grep -q` exits on its first match, the reader then died of\nSIGPIPE, the pipeline reported 141, and the verb called a genuinely managed key unmanaged\non 195 of 200 attempts. Two suites now hold the line - one drives all twelve names through\nthe predicate inside a `pipefail` shell, the other runs the verb against each of them - so\nthe shape cannot come back unnoticed. A name outside the inventory is still an `error:` and\nexit 1, and the inventory itself stays the only list."},"2246":{"id":"/operate/secrets-120","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-coder_pg_password","content":"Then the note the CLI prints is the real edge, and it is worth repeating here: compose\nreads `.env` only when a container is recreated, so the rotated password reaches Coder on\nthe next `up`. Nothing hot-reloads it."},"2247":{"id":"/operate/secrets-121","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-coder_pg_password","content":"Under that sits one trap. The Postgres image honours `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` **only at first\nvolume init**, so changing it in `.env` and recreating `db` would leave the stored\npassword unchanged and lock Coder out of its own database. Two things cover it:"},"2248":{"id":"/operate/secrets-122","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-coder_pg_password","content":"`scripts/lib/pg-apply-password.sh` reconciles the password at the `db` entrypoint, so\nthe stored credential is brought in line with `CODER_PG_PASSWORD` on every start of that\ncontainer."},"2249":{"id":"/operate/secrets-123","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-coder_pg_password","content":"`restart_coder_if_pg_auth_failed`, sourced by both stack runners, bounces `coder` when\nits logs show `password authentication failed` - which is the failure mode a stale\npassword produces when the two halves land in the wrong order."},"2250":{"id":"/operate/secrets-124","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-coder_pg_password","content":"Both run as part of `up`, so the operator's job is one `up` after the rotate. Read the\n`db` and `coder` logs if Coder takes longer than a recreate to come back:"},"2251":{"id":"/operate/secrets-125","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-coder_pg_password","content":"`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD` has the same first-init behaviour and no reconciler beside it - the\ncompose block says so at its own comment - so rotating it needs the `infisical-db-data`\nvolume reset, not a recreate."},"2252":{"id":"/operate/secrets-126","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"The rules that shape every write in this subsystem, inherited from\n`scripts/dashboard-password.sh`:"},"2253":{"id":"/operate/secrets-127","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"A value does not travel in argv except where upstream offers no other channel. The seam's\nheader names the exceptions: `--client-secret=` on the Universal Auth login and `--password=`\non `bootstrap`, because that CLI takes neither on stdin; and the `KEY=@<file>` form for a\nsingle write, which names a file rather than the value, as `--file=<tmp600>` does for a bulk\nset. Everything else that carries a managed value crosses the seam by redirect into a\nmode-600 file, never through `$( )`, a pipeline, or a variable the CLI fills."},"2254":{"id":"/operate/secrets-128","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"A value never appears in a log line, including a failure line. Every ok, warning and\nerror names the **key**, never the value, and counts rather than listing. The one function\nallowed to put a credential on a stream is `infisical_login`, whose whole job is handing the\naccess token to its caller on stdout while its commentary goes to stderr; the callers that do\nnot need it send that stream to `/dev/null`. The boot body scrubs the token and the client\nsecret out of the CLI's own error text before it prints what the export said."},"2255":{"id":"/operate/secrets-129","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"A file that holds one is mode 600 and is removed on the failure path too, by the `EXIT`\ntrap the seam installs - export, push payload, verify read-back and rotate value all go\nthrough it. bash keeps one `EXIT` handler, so the seam captures whatever the caller had\ninstalled and runs it after its own temps are gone; what an entry script must never do is\ntake that slot back after sourcing the seam, which would strand a file of secrets on the\nfailure path."},"2256":{"id":"/operate/secrets-130","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"A write is verified by hashing the value read back before anything claims `ok:`."},"2257":{"id":"/operate/secrets-131","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"A key the vault does not carry is a warning, never a blanked line."},"2258":{"id":"/operate/secrets-132","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"**A vault value is never sourced in the shape the CLI wrote it.** Measured against\n`infisical/infisical:v0.167.10` with CLI v0.45.8: `secrets export --format=dotenv` wraps\neach value in single quotes and leaves an embedded quote raw, so a secret carrying an\napostrophe closes its quote early and the rest of the line is shell - sourcing it either\naborts or runs the tail as a command. `--format=dotenv-export` uses the same writer and\nfails the same way. The boot body therefore takes every value apart and re-emits it under\nquoting that has no escape hatch, which is what makes `~/.bashrc`'s `set -a; . agent.env`\nsafe to keep. Anything else that reads that file inherits the guarantee; nothing should\nfeed the CLI's own dotenv output to a shell."},"2259":{"id":"/operate/secrets-133","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"There is no reason to open `.env` by hand while this subsystem is adopted, and no command\nhere prints a credential. If you are cat-ing the env file to debug a sync, you are reading\nthe wrong file: `status` and `check` answer the same question."},"2260":{"id":"/operate/secrets-134","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No dynamic secrets, no cross-project sharing.** Both are paid features of Infisical\nand neither is used: no `LICENSE_KEY` is read, generated, or referenced. The model is\nstatic shared secrets exported as dotenv."},"2261":{"id":"/operate/secrets-135","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No vault dependency in the deploy path.** Compose reads `.env`, and the one call the\nrunners make before it is best-effort: warn, continue, bring the stack up on the values the\nfile already held. The follow-up that would change that is a compose-secrets rewrite of every\nservice block, and it would make every bring-up wait on a server nothing here waits on today."},"2262":{"id":"/operate/secrets-136","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No multi-line value reaches a workspace.** The boot fetch publishes single-line values only\nand drops the rest with one note; see\nThe workspace boot fetch."},"2263":{"id":"/operate/secrets-137","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No scrape, no alert.** The three containers are log-only, like `ollama`, `kasm` and\nthe rest. `bazelisk run //scripts:observability-check` does not walk them; their state\nis `dev-stack -- status` and their logs are in Loki under their `container=` tag."},"2264":{"id":"/operate/secrets-138","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No second inventory.** Twelve keys, one file. A key added to compose without joining\n`infisical/managed-secrets.txt` stays outside the vault silently, which is why `check`\nasserts the count rather than trusting the file."},"2265":{"id":"/operate/secrets-139","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"Related: observability.md (the `GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD` login and\nthe log stream these containers ship), public-access.md (the\n`infisical-*` public name), troubleshooting.md."},"2266":{"id":"/project-conventions","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"page","content":"Project conventions","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions"},"2267":{"id":"/project-conventions-0","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"The shell, function, script, template and naming rules this repo holds itself to, and the gates that enforce them."},"2268":{"id":"/project-conventions-1","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Project conventions"},"2269":{"id":"/project-conventions-2","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#file-header","content":"File header"},"2270":{"id":"/project-conventions-3","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"Functions"},"2271":{"id":"/project-conventions-4","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#tests","content":"Tests"},"2272":{"id":"/project-conventions-5","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"Ingress"},"2273":{"id":"/project-conventions-6","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"Scripts"},"2274":{"id":"/project-conventions-7","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#templates","content":"Templates"},"2275":{"id":"/project-conventions-8","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"Names and flags"},"2276":{"id":"/project-conventions-9","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"**What's on this page**"},"2277":{"id":"/project-conventions-10","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"The file header and the function docstring block both gates require"},"2278":{"id":"/project-conventions-11","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Ingress rules: what runs from a shell and what is a Bazel target"},"2279":{"id":"/project-conventions-12","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"One language per file, the shared log contract, and the payload copies the templates share"},"2280":{"id":"/project-conventions-13","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Names, flags, and the style guide shell follows"},"2281":{"id":"/project-conventions-14","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"**What this enables**"},"2282":{"id":"/project-conventions-15","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Writing a script that passes `//:lint` and `//tests:doc_coverage` first try"},"2283":{"id":"/project-conventions-16","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Matching the house style instead of inventing a local one"},"2284":{"id":"/project-conventions-17","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Shell follows the Google Shell Style Guide.\nBranch rules live in branching.md; the Bazel harness lives in\ncontribute/building-with-bazel.md and\ncontribute/test-with-bazel.md."},"2285":{"id":"/project-conventions-18","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#file-header","content":"Every `scripts/**/*.sh` file starts with a `# ## ` header that names the file and says whether it is an entry point or a sourced library."},"2286":{"id":"/project-conventions-19","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"Every function carries this block immediately above the definition:"},"2287":{"id":"/project-conventions-20","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"`tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails the build when a header or a block is missing."},"2288":{"id":"/project-conventions-21","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#tests","content":"Every function under `scripts/` is named by an asserting test under `tests/`: one suite per\nmodule, plus one test per entry-level function that drives the whole script (`tests/bats/\nentry-fns.bats` is where those live). `tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a name is\nmissing, and `tests/doc_coverage.sh` when a `# @function` block is missing or incomplete.\nTests are hermetic: docker, ssh, curl, git and the other external tools resolve through\n`tests/bats/tool_stubs`."},"2289":{"id":"/project-conventions-22","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"Bazelisk is the only ingress, with one exception: `scripts/setup-host.sh`. That script is\nthe **stack host's** bootstrap — it installs Bazelisk, the shell/Starlark lint toolchain\n(shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier) and Terraform — so it has to run before Bazel exists, and\nstays the single documented `bash scripts/setup-host.sh`. On a laptop you do not run it:\nthe contributor container or `bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image` brings the same pinned\ntoolchain in from `images/dev/` instead\n(contribute/devcontainer.md). `--repair` is the other half of\nthat exemption: leftover Docker bind-mounts in Bazel's runfiles tree make\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` die creating `prod-stack.runfiles` (`Permission\ndenied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`) before any stack script can run.\nOn Linux it also writes a systemd-resolved drop-in (`1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8`) and\nruns `tailscale set --accept-dns=false` so host `git pull` still resolves\n`github.com` when MagicDNS is the only nameserver."},"2290":{"id":"/project-conventions-23","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"Everything else is a Bazel target:"},"2291":{"id":"/project-conventions-24","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"`make` only delegates. An operator CLI runs as `bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`\n(`<stem>` = the file name under `scripts/`); `make help` lists the shims."},"2292":{"id":"/project-conventions-25","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"No `eval`. Prefer `\"${var}\"`, `[[ ]]`, and `$(...)`."},"2293":{"id":"/project-conventions-26","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"One language per file where reasonable. A bash, python, or awk program that lives\ninside another file is its own tested file, imported with `source`, `cat` + `sh -c`\n/ `ssh`, `python3 file.py`, `awk -f`, or Terraform `file()` / `templatefile()`. Keep\n1–3 line awk, usage heredocs, Dockerfiles, YAML data, docs fences, and\n`python3 -m webbrowser`. Remote probes are POSIX and self-contained under\n`scripts/lib/probes/`; the host `cat`s them and tests run the file with `sh`."},"2294":{"id":"/project-conventions-27","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"Operator CLIs source `scripts/lib/common.sh` and do not redefine `log` / `die` /\n`skip`. Grouping: `section` prints `== name ==`, `phase` prints `-> text`, `ok`\nprints `ok: text`, `summary` prints `summary: N ok, W warnings, F failed` plus\n`  failed: …` and optional `next: cmd`. `JSON_MODE` set sends prose to stderr.\nCLIs without `--json` unset `JSON_MODE` after source. Keep prefixes `warning:` /\n`note:` / `error:`."},"2295":{"id":"/project-conventions-28","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"A script never runs another repo script by relative path, and never assumes the cwd is the\ncheckout: it resolves the repo root with `resolve_checkout` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` and then\n`cd`s there (see the harness doc), because a Bazel sandbox may only hold a copy. That helper tries\n`${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` / `${PWD}` /\n`${SELF_DIR}/..` in order, and a candidate counts only when it holds the marker file\n(`docker-compose.yml` unless the caller names another) and its `pwd -P` is not under a\n`bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or `*.runfiles` tree — an explicit `REPO_ROOT` is\nthe one candidate trusted as given, which is how the Bats runner names the copy under test.\nNothing proving out is one\n`error: unresolvable repo root: ...` line on stderr and exit `5`, never a guessed cwd."},"2296":{"id":"/project-conventions-29","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"External tools are probed with `command -v` and reported as \"not on PATH\", not assumed.\n`require_tool` skips locally and fails under `CI=true`."},"2297":{"id":"/project-conventions-30","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"A probe-style CLI takes its target as a flag (`--url`) rather than a hard-coded host, and reports\n\"nothing answered\" as a result rather than a crash, so one run works from the host and from inside\na workspace and still answers the question the operator asked. Its suite stubs the transport."},"2298":{"id":"/project-conventions-31","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#templates","content":"`templates/workspace/` (the plain dev container) and `templates/devcontainer/` (the\nDinD-backed one) keep independent `.tftpl` payload copies (`startup.sh.tftpl`,\n`settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). The twins must change together — today\nonly `settings.json.tftpl` is byte-identical across the two directories, so edit both\ncopies of that payload. `templates/common/` exists once above the twins instead and\nholds the single-copy payloads both templates consume: `GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`,\n`vscode-alt.svg`, `notes-setup.sh`, `tmux.conf`, `grok-build-terminal.sh`,\n`vscode-terminal-tmux.sh`, `grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md`, `ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md`,\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md`, `stack-skills.sh.tftpl`,\n`ez-bazel-first.SKILL.md`, `ez-bazel-first.owned_files_check.py`,\n`ez-bazel-first.glob_budget.py`, `ez-strong-types.SKILL.md`,\n`ez-strong-types.type_baseline.py`, `ez-trunk-based-repo.SKILL.md`,\n`ez-micro-plan.SKILL.md`, `ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py`,\n`stack-repo-skills.sh.tftpl`,\n`coder-agents.md`, `mcp-cache-proxy.json`, and `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`. Markdown\nand JSON are read with `file()` (chomp the markdown); shell helpers that Terraform must\nnot re-interpolate are also `file()` (so `$folder` stays `$folder`); `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`\nis `templatefile()` because it interpolates `${github_auth_id}`. `coder_script`\nbodies are sibling `.tftpl` files next to each `main.tf` (`code-server.sh.tftpl`,\nand on `devcontainer` also `dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`) rendered with `templatefile()`;\n`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`, `stack-skills.sh.tftpl` (the body that seeds the\n`ez-learn` / `ez-skillify` / `ez-create-skill` trio) and `stack-repo-skills.sh.tftpl` (the\nbody that seeds the four-command repository family `ez-bazel-first` / `ez-strong-types` /\n`ez-trunk-based-repo` / `ez-micro-plan`, each python helper into a `scripts/` subdir\nbeside its `SKILL.md`)\nare the three that live in\n`templates/common/` instead, so both twins share one copy of them.\nThe SVG stays small enough that the resulting data URL fits Coder v2.36's 256-character\n`workspace_apps.icon` column. The push\n(`scripts/push-template.sh` through `template_push_one` in `scripts/lib/template-push.sh`)\nstages every common payload beside each template's staged `main.tf` copy, which is the only place the\nserver-side Terraform can read them from, so editing the common copy changes both\ntemplates; a staged copy that lacks any one of them fails the push early by design."},"2299":{"id":"/project-conventions-32","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#templates","content":"Dollar escaping differs across those two layers. A `templatefile()` `.tftpl` treats `$$`\nas an escaped `$` everywhere (`$name` or `$${name}`), and leaves `$((` arithmetic\nalone. An HCL heredoc in `main.tf` (`<<-EOT`) only escapes `$${name}` to `${name}`;\na lone `$` is already literal, so shell arithmetic is `$((...))` with one dollar.\n`$$((` is two dollars, and Coder runs `coder_script` bodies as `bash -c`, which\nexpands `$$` to the PID. Do not wrap `templatefile()` in `chomp()`."},"2300":{"id":"/project-conventions-33","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`devcontainer` — the dev-container template (directory `templates/devcontainer`, Coder\ntemplate name `devcontainer`), always backed by its own DinD sidecar. Its sibling is\n`workspace`: directory `templates/workspace`, template name `workspace`, the plain\ndev container with the optional `dind` parameter."},"2301":{"id":"/project-conventions-34","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`devcontainer.json` — a file the *application repository* carries, and the one thing the\n`devcontainer` template builds. `coder_devcontainer.repo` in\n`templates/devcontainer/main.tf` sets no `config_path`, so `@devcontainers/cli` on the\nDinD sidecar searches the clone for `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` (or a\n`devcontainer.json` anywhere inside it). This repo ships a `.devcontainer/` of its own —\nthe **contributor** one for a laptop and CI, built from `images/dev/Dockerfile` — and it\nis not that nested kind: `.devcontainer/contributor-env` alongside it makes\n`probe_repo` answer a miss, so a Coder workspace of this repo stays on the `workspace`\ntemplate instead of building its own container inside its own DinD sidecar. Other\nrepositories keep carrying their own nested dev-container files, and those are still a\nprobe hit. See contribute/devcontainer.md."},"2302":{"id":"/project-conventions-35","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`additional_repos` — the `--additional-repos` flag on the new-workspace CLI, and the\n`coder_parameter` of the same name that receives its value in both templates\n(`mutable = true`, default empty): extra git URLs for the workspace's clone folders,\ngiven as `url1,url2` or one URL per line. The flag exists on\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` only - the stack runners (`dev-stack`,\n`prod-stack`) have no `--additional-repos` (their flags are `-p`, `-f`, `-e`,\n`--tail`, `--follow`, `--json`, `-y`, `-h`)."},"2303":{"id":"/project-conventions-36","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`--project-name` - every stack-runner subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`),\non both `dev-stack` and `prod-stack`. `dev-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-dev`, the dev sandbox;\n`prod-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-prod`, the stable stack it brings up, and it addresses a\nstable stack brought up by hand with `--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` (the checkout\nfolder's name). The env file follows the runner: the dev project takes `dev-stack.env`\nbeside the compose file when one exists, and any other project gets none, so a run aimed at\nthe stable stack never picks up the dev host ports; `prod-stack` names no env file unless\n`-e` does, and compose then reads the `.env` beside the compose file itself."},"2304":{"id":"/project-conventions-37","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`--check` — the read-only mode `scripts/setup-host.sh` and `scripts/bootstrap.sh` both\ntake. `setup-host --check` names the tools that are missing, leftover runfiles that\nwill make `bazelisk run` fail with Permission denied, and whether `github.com`\nresolves, and installs nothing; `setup-host --repair` is the recovery for those\nleftovers and, on Linux, for MagicDNS-only host DNS (uses sudo when chmod or\n`/etc` is not enough). `bootstrap --check` prints the same\nprobes the full run makes (tools, compose plugin, placeholder secrets by name, hosts,\ncerts, GPU, images) and writes nothing. An unrecognised argument in either script is\nan error, never a silent fall-through to the mutating run."},"2305":{"id":"/project-conventions-38","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"GitHub auth is Coder External Auth, id `github` by default (Coder's injected\nGitHub App when coder.env is absent). A custom OAuth app in coder.env keeps\nits `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` (commonly `primary-github`); push-template.sh\nforwards that id. There is no GitHub-token template variable and no PAT in\nany agent env map."},"2306":{"id":"/project-conventions-39","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"Runtime stays `docker compose`: Bazel runs the CLIs that drive Compose and never talks to a\ndaemon, a Spark box, or a model registry by itself. No Kubernetes layer."},"2307":{"id":"/public-access","page_id":"/public-access","type":"page","content":"Public subdomains","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access"},"2308":{"id":"/public-access-0","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"Serving the same seven services on public names over real TLS, additively, with the LAN shape left intact."},"2309":{"id":"/public-access-1","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Public subdomains"},"2310":{"id":"/public-access-2","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"The path a public request takes"},"2311":{"id":"/public-access-3","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"The composed names"},"2312":{"id":"/public-access-4","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"Getting the naming right"},"2313":{"id":"/public-access-5","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"What `bootstrap` changes when the names are set"},"2314":{"id":"/public-access-6","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"Traefik configuration"},"2315":{"id":"/public-access-7","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"Checking the other machine"},"2316":{"id":"/public-access-8","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Authelia"},"2317":{"id":"/public-access-9","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Two follow-ups, both by hand"},"2318":{"id":"/public-access-10","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"**What's on this page**"},"2319":{"id":"/public-access-11","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The path a public request takes, from the browser to the container"},"2320":{"id":"/public-access-12","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The naming rule, and what `auto` composes from the parts you set"},"2321":{"id":"/public-access-13","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"What `bootstrap.sh` changes when the names are set"},"2322":{"id":"/public-access-14","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The Traefik file-provider config the other box needs, pasted as-is"},"2323":{"id":"/public-access-15","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Checking the other machine (including the Hermes WebSocket), Authelia, and the two follow-ups left by hand"},"2324":{"id":"/public-access-16","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"**What this enables**"},"2325":{"id":"/public-access-17","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Exposing the stack without breaking the LAN routes anyone at home uses"},"2326":{"id":"/public-access-18","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Telling a router problem apart from an Authelia problem when a name fails"},"2327":{"id":"/public-access-19","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Putting the seven routers on the Traefik box without guessing its timeouts"},"2328":{"id":"/public-access-20","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The default shape is LAN-only. `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` additionally serves the same\nseven services on public names over real TLS, each name composed from the parts you set\nonce. It is purely additive: every LAN route keeps working. Grafana and Infisical are the\ntwo new services on this path: the observability backends behind Grafana (Prometheus,\nLoki, Tempo, Mimir) stay unexposed, because\nSECURITY.md keeps\nmetrics and logs off the LAN, let alone the WAN."},"2329":{"id":"/public-access-21","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"TLS terminates at Traefik. The `router` sidecar holds no certificate."},"2330":{"id":"/public-access-22","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Cloudflare proxies the name (orange cloud) in front of Traefik. WebSockets are on\nthere by default, which is what the Hermes Chat tab needs."},"2331":{"id":"/public-access-23","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"The router's whole job is picking an upstream from the Host header, and an unmatched\nHost answers `421` rather than a guess, so a mistyped or rewritten Host fails loudly\ninstead of landing in the wrong service. A rewritten Host matches no server block\nhere, so it also arrives as `421`."},"2332":{"id":"/public-access-24","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Kasm is the only hop that speaks TLS to its origin. `proxy_ssl_verify` is off there\nbecause the certificate is the one Kasm's installer generates and reissues. Hermes,\nlike Homepage, Coder and LiteLLM, is plain HTTP at the origin (`http://hermes:9119`)."},"2333":{"id":"/public-access-25","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Nothing in this path replaces the LAN routes: `https://<lan-ip>/` still serves the\ndashboard on the self-signed cert, `https://<lan-ip>:3001` still serves Coder's\nown TLS listener, and `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` still serves Hermes."},"2334":{"id":"/public-access-26","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"A workspace **app** link (the **VS Code Web** button is the only app this stack still\nproxies) rides the coder row of that table. `coder_app.code_server` sets\n`subdomain = false`, so its URL keeps the `/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/<slug>` path\nform and goes to `coder:3000` on its way to the editor on `localhost:13337` inside the\nworkspace. A 502 on that link is an app-lane failure and `litellm-gmktecbeast.` took\nno part in it."},"2335":{"id":"/public-access-27","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Every `location` adds `X-Evo-Proxy` with the upstream name, and `always` is what\nmakes it survive a 502. Read it beside `X-Coder-Request-Id`, which Coder's own proxy\nsets on an answered request: neither header means the request stopped at Traefik or\nCloudflare, and `421` means the Host header matched no server block here."},"2336":{"id":"/public-access-28","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"These are what the shipped defaults compose. The operator sets the parts, not these\nstrings: the grammar is `<service>-<environment>-<suffix>.<base>`, always ONE label\nunder the zone."},"2337":{"id":"/public-access-29","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                      | Router upstream |\n| ------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `homepage:3000` |","table":"table-0"},"2338":{"id":"/public-access-30","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                            | Router upstream               |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `coder:3000` - plain listener |","table":"table-0"},"2339":{"id":"/public-access-31","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                                | Router upstream                         |\n| ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `coder-dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `coder:3000` - the dev stack's own name |","table":"table-0"},"2340":{"id":"/public-access-32","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                           | Router upstream                              |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `https://kasm:4443` (`proxy_ssl_verify off`) |","table":"table-0"},"2341":{"id":"/public-access-33","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                              | Router upstream |\n| --------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `litellm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `litellm:4000`  |","table":"table-0"},"2342":{"id":"/public-access-34","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                             | Router upstream |\n| -------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `hermes:9119`   |","table":"table-0"},"2343":{"id":"/public-access-35","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                              | Router upstream                       |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `grafana:3000` - the observability UI |","table":"table-0"},"2344":{"id":"/public-access-36","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                                | Router upstream                                                |\n| ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `infisical:8080` - the vault, up only with `INFISICAL_ENABLED` |","table":"table-0"},"2345":{"id":"/public-access-37","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"The dev row is what `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack -- up` composes for the\n`evo-t1-dev` project: that command applies `STACK_ENV_LABEL=dev` when neither the shell\nnor the env file already sets a label, and only for that project, so\n`--project-name evo-t1-review` gets none. A label goes on every name, so that stack's\ndashboard is `dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` too, and all seven need their own Traefik\nrouters (see below). `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` never invents a label."},"2346":{"id":"/public-access-38","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"Hermes is the same additive hop as the other four. Traefik forwards\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with the original Host, and the router sends that\nHost to `http://hermes:9119`. The LAN URL `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` stays.\nHTTP basic auth on the dashboard stays too; Authelia is the extra public gate.\nSee hermes.md."},"2347":{"id":"/public-access-39","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`scripts/lib/domain.sh` owns the grammar and every part is normalized before it is\njoined, so a value copied out of a browser bar cannot leak into an origin or a\n`server_name`:"},"2348":{"id":"/public-access-40","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`STACK_DOMAIN_BASE` (default `overeazy.io`) is the zone the edge terminates TLS for;\n`STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX` (default `gmktecbeast`) is this box's label, shared by every\nname."},"2349":{"id":"/public-access-41","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`STACK_CODER_NAME` / `STACK_KASM_NAME` / `STACK_LITELLM_NAME` / `STACK_HERMES_NAME` /\n`STACK_GRAFANA_NAME` / `STACK_INFISICAL_NAME` (defaults `coder` / `kasm` / `litellm` /\n`hermes` / `grafana` / `infisical`) are the service prefixes. `STACK_GRAFANA_NAME`\nnames the observability UI only: the metrics backends it fronts get no key of their\nown, and stay unexposed.\n`STACK_DASHBOARD_NAME` is empty by default, which puts the dashboard on the zone apex."},"2350":{"id":"/public-access-42","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`STACK_INFISICAL_NAME` (default `infisical`) is the seventh prefix and one of the two\nbehind a compose profile - `STACK_GRAFANA_NAME` is the other, whose `grafana` service\nsits under `profiles: [\"observability\"]`. The vault's three containers start only under\n`profiles: [\"secrets\"]`, which `INFISICAL_ENABLED` (off as shipped) selects, so a\nstack with the flag off has no vault behind that name and answers `502`. `domain.sh`\nreads no flag for either of them, so both names still compose and still reach\n`HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS`, which is the safer half of that trade. The router sends it\nto `http://infisical:8080` - the container port, not the published one - and the\nmapping is `${INFISICAL_PORT:-8182}:8080`, published plainly rather than\nloopback-bound for the same host-gateway reason the search lane gives for `:8181`."},"2351":{"id":"/public-access-43","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`STACK_ENV_LABEL` is empty for the stable stack and sits **after** the service prefix.\nTwo reasons: the one-label rule below rules the dotted form out anyway, and\n`classify_public_entry` in `scripts/lib/bootstrap.sh` routes an entry by its service\nprefix, so `coder-dev-box.example.com` is Coder while `dev-coder-box.example.com`\nfalls through to the dashboard."},"2352":{"id":"/public-access-44","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"The shape is load-bearing: each name is **one label under the zone**, and each service\nprefix joins it with a **hyphen**. A wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so\nthe dotted two-label form (`coder.gmktecbeast.`) has no certificate at the public edge:\nthe browser aborts with `ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` and Traefik never sees the\nrequest. Measured on this box - `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` completes the handshake\nwith the zone's `*.overeazy.io` cert, while the dotted form fails with a TLS alert 40\nand presents no certificate at all."},"2353":{"id":"/public-access-45","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` has three states, and only the middle one composes:"},"2354":{"id":"/public-access-46","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"empty is LAN-only;"},"2355":{"id":"/public-access-47","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`auto` composes the seven names from the parts above;"},"2356":{"id":"/public-access-48","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"a comma-separated list is used verbatim, which is how you pin a name by hand (leave\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE` empty, or that key keeps re-deriving)."},"2357":{"id":"/public-access-49","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"A part that cannot be a DNS label composes nothing: that service is left out of the list\nrather than sent as an empty entry, and a missing zone keeps the whole stack LAN-only."},"2358":{"id":"/public-access-50","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` derives:"},"2359":{"id":"/public-access-51","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_DASHBOARD_URL`, which compose feeds to `HOMEPAGE_EXTERNAL_URL` so NextAuth\nbuilds its redirects and Secure cookie from the public origin;"},"2360":{"id":"/public-access-52","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL` /\n`STACK_GRAFANA_URL`, the card `href`s. A `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is what fills\n`STACK_HERMES_URL`. Compose copies that value into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`, the\nHost and Origin the Chat tab checks. The same prefix rule fills `STACK_GRAFANA_URL`\nfrom a `grafana-*` or `grafana.*` entry, and compose copies that into\n`HOMEPAGE_VAR_GRAFANA_URL`, the observability card's `href`;"},"2361":{"id":"/public-access-53","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_INFISICAL_URL`, from a `infisical-*` or `infisical.*` entry, the vault card's\n`href`. Its badge reads down while `INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off, which is the flag\nsaying so rather than a broken link;"},"2362":{"id":"/public-access-54","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"an upgraded `CODER_ACCESS_URL` (the public coder name);"},"2363":{"id":"/public-access-55","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"an extended `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` - homepage validates the incoming Host against\nthat exact-match list, so an unlisted name fails its host check;"},"2364":{"id":"/public-access-56","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"a Coder certificate whose DNS SANs now carry the public names alongside the existing\nones. The LAN cert keeps working; in public mode the browser never sees it, since\nTraefik presents real ones;"},"2365":{"id":"/public-access-57","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE=auto`, which records that the names are derived. Under `auto`\nthe parts are the source of truth and `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is output only, so a list\nedited by hand there is ignored and bootstrap prints a `note:` naming the key to\nclear. It clears itself on the LAN-only and explicit-list paths;"},"2366":{"id":"/public-access-58","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"the router's vhost table, whose token set is exactly the seven vhosts it answers\n(`__EVO_HOST_INFISICAL__` among them: a name that will not compose refuses the render\nrather than writing half a table): `proxy/router.conf.template` is rendered into\n`proxy/router.gen.conf` (gitignored) and `STACK_ROUTER_CONF=router.gen.conf` is\nwritten, because compose mounts `./proxy/${STACK_ROUTER_CONF:-router.conf}`. The\n`warn_missing_router_hosts` check runs against that rendered file, so a name the\ntable does not answer is reported before the recreate rather than as a `421` after\nit."},"2367":{"id":"/public-access-59","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"Set the naming parts in `.env` and put `auto` in `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` (`.env.sample`\ndocuments every part and shows the explicit-list shape beside it), and in\n`dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs beside the stable one. Then re-run `bootstrap`\nand recreate the stack you switched. The commands are in the README."},"2368":{"id":"/public-access-60","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"Nothing in this repo configures Traefik. This is the file the other box needs: drop it\nin the directory that box's `providers.file.directory` watches and Traefik picks it up\nwithout a restart. The shape below is the one Traefik v2 and v3 both read; the\nentryPoint name, the certificate and the middleware name have to match what that box\nalready defines, so treat those three as the values to align, not as new requirements."},"2369":{"id":"/public-access-61","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"`passHostHeader: true` is the line that makes this work at all. Without it Traefik\nsends the upstream URL's host, no `server_name` matches, and this box answers `421`."},"2370":{"id":"/public-access-62","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"**A published dev stack needs its own routers here.** The `dev-stack.env` behind\n`bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack` moves every published port by 10000 (the recipe is\nin the\nREADME),\nso that stack's `router` answers on `18080` (`STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` default 8080) and\nits names carry the environment label\n(`coder-dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`). A dev name is a different `Host` rule and a\ndifferent backend port: add a router per dev name to the file above aimed at\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:18080`, and the DNS records for those names have to exist\ntoo, or the browser fails before this box is ever asked."},"2371":{"id":"/public-access-63","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"`authelia@file` is a placeholder for the Authelia middleware that box already\ndeclares. Name it exactly as that box names it - a middleware created by labels is\n`authelia@docker`, and a name Traefik cannot resolve fails the router rather than\nserving it unauthenticated. The six interactive names (dashboard, Coder, Kasm, Hermes,\nGrafana, Infisical) carry it; LiteLLM must not."},"2372":{"id":"/public-access-64","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"`tls: {}` means \"serve whatever certificate matches the SNI\", which is the zone's\n`*.overeazy.io` pair. Add `tls: { certResolver: <name> }` if that box issues through\nan ACME resolver instead. SNI has to be the public name itself - the wildcard covers\nexactly one label, which is why the hyphenated one-label form is the only one that\nhandshakes."},"2373":{"id":"/public-access-65","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"**WebSockets need nothing here.** Traefik forwards `Upgrade` and `Connection`\nas-is; there is no `protocol: websocket` to set and no middleware to add. The Hermes\nChat tab's `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` open on that alone. They fail only when\nsomething in between drops the upgrade, and Cloudflare has WebSockets on by\ndefault, so the usual suspect is an Authelia middleware configured to buffer."},"2374":{"id":"/public-access-66","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"**Timeouts.** `proxy/router.conf` on this box already holds per-service read\ntimeouts - dashboard 60s, coder, hermes, grafana and infisical 300s, LiteLLM 600s,\nKasm 3600s - so Traefik must not be the shorter link. One transport for all seven at\n`3600s` is the simple choice because Kasm needs that floor anyway. To cap each tier\nseparately, declare one `loadBalancer` service per tier, each pointing at the same\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with `passHostHeader: true` and its own transport\n(`evo-t1-coder` / `evo-t1-hermes` / `evo-t1-grafana` / `evo-t1-infisical` at `300s`,\n`evo-t1-litellm` at\n`600s`, `evo-t1-kasm` at `3600s`), and point each router at its own service. If a\nsession dies at about a minute with the page still loaded, check the client-facing side\ntoo: an entryPoint's `transport.respondingTimeouts.readTimeout` (default 60s) and\n`idleTimeout` (default 180s) apply to the browser-to-Traefik hop."},"2375":{"id":"/public-access-67","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"SearXNG is deliberately absent. It stays LAN-only on `:8181`, which is where the\nworkspaces reach it through `host.docker.internal`; putting it behind Traefik would\nexpose a search box that has no authentication of its own."},"2376":{"id":"/public-access-68","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"This repo cannot verify any of it; check it on the Traefik box."},"2377":{"id":"/public-access-69","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"A router per name pointing at `http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080`, including\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` and `grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`. That backend\nis `:8080` with the original Host, the same as the other names - not `:9119` and not\n`:3005`. A router aimed at a container port skips this\nbox's vhost. The file in Traefik configuration is that\nconfig; diff what the box runs against it."},"2378":{"id":"/public-access-70","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"The original Host header preserved - a rewritten one comes back as `421`."},"2379":{"id":"/public-access-71","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"Any authentication as the Traefik router's middleware. Authelia already fronts\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` (the name 302s), the same interactive policy as\nthe homepage and Coder names, and `grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` belongs behind\nthat same policy: Grafana is an interactive login, not an API endpoint, and the name\nfronts the metrics bundle. The sidecar forwards what it receives and\nauthenticates nothing, so a name whose router skips the auth middleware reaches\nits container the same way the LAN does. The app path is behind that policy\nlike the rest of its name, so an expired session surfaces there too."},"2380":{"id":"/public-access-72","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"DNS records for all seven names, each one label deep and Cloudflare-proxied (orange\ncloud). WebSockets are on there by default. A missing record shows up as a DNS\nerror, a rewritten Host as `421`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm and LiteLLM resolve\nthat way and complete the TLS handshake, checked from this box; the Hermes\nname is the same zone and the same proxy. The Grafana and Infisical records are new\nwith their names and have not been measured from this box, so check them there."},"2381":{"id":"/public-access-73","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"WebSocket `Upgrade` forwarded by Traefik and Authelia on the Hermes router. The\nChat tab uses `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`, and Hermes sends a keepalive every 20\nseconds. A proxy that drops `Upgrade` loads the page and then fails the tab."},"2382":{"id":"/public-access-74","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"`https://gmktecbeast.overeazy.io/`, `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`,\n`kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`, `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`,\n`grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` and `infisical-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` 302 to\n`https://authelia.overeazy.io/`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm, Hermes, Grafana and Infisical\nare interactive sessions, and the Hermes policy is already in place. Covering a newly named host\nis an Authelia-side config choice; nothing here adds or requires a policy, so the\nGrafana and Infisical policies are the two this box cannot assume exist. Hermes\nis not an API bypass: it stays behind that interactive policy, and after Authelia\nit asks for its own HTTP basic auth. Both prompts on the public URL are expected.\nGrafana and Infisical are the same shape: Authelia first, then each one's own login."},"2383":{"id":"/public-access-75","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Put LiteLLM behind it and every client breaks: LiteLLM authenticates from the\n`Authorization` header, and header-carried API traffic cannot pass an interactive\nsign-in. Either leave `litellm-gmktecbeast` outside any policy or add an explicit\nbypass - and then `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` is the only credential in front of an\nendpoint that went from LAN-only to reachable WAN-wide (see\nSECURITY.md)."},"2384":{"id":"/public-access-76","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Workspace agents never reach Authelia either way. The template rewrites the agent URL\nto `http://host.docker.internal:3002`, the plain listener published through\n`CODER_AGENT_TUNNEL_PORT`, so agent traffic stays on the box."},"2385":{"id":"/public-access-77","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Workspaces created before public mode existed need a template re-push plus a\nrestart, to pick up the template's `coder_agent_url` variable (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:3002`). That variable is what keeps agents dialing the\nlocal plain listener whatever the access URL says."},"2386":{"id":"/public-access-78","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Kasm stores its connection endpoints in its own setup database (first-boot wizard or\nadmin UI), so the endpoint has to change there too or streams keep dialing the LAN\naddress."},"2387":{"id":"/spark-monitoring","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"page","content":"Spark monitoring","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring"},"2388":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-0","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring","content":"Enrolling the three DGX Sparks into the Prometheus stack: the two exporter containers a box needs, the one opt-in key, the plan/prove/install commands, how the boxes reach Prometheus through file_sd, and what this change leaves unproven."},"2389":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-1","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"Spark monitoring"},"2390":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-2","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"What lands on a Spark"},"2391":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-3","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"The flag"},"2392":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-4","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#the-commands","content":"The commands"},"2393":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-5","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#plan-the-default-and-the-one-that-is-safe-anywhere","content":"Plan (the default, and the one that is safe anywhere)"},"2394":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-6","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"Prove (`--status`)"},"2395":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-7","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"Install (`--apply`)"},"2396":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-8","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#the-rest","content":"The rest"},"2397":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-9","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"How the boxes get into Prometheus"},"2398":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-10","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"What this change does not prove"},"2399":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-11","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#failure-modes","content":"Failure modes"},"2400":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-12","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"**What's on this page**"},"2401":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-13","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"The two containers that make a Spark a Prometheus target, and the fact that neither\nexists until you run `--apply`"},"2402":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-14","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"The one key that decides whether the fleet is monitored at all, and what reads it"},"2403":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-15","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"The three commands - plan, prove, install - plus one box, JSON, and the target document"},"2404":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-16","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"How the boxes get into Prometheus, and why an unenrolled fleet reads as zero Sparks"},"2405":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-17","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"What this change does not prove: the DCGM tag, the profiling counters, NVLink"},"2406":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-18","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"The three failures worth recognising from the CLI's own words"},"2407":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-19","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"**What this enables**"},"2408":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-20","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"Reading GPU utilisation, temperature, power and framebuffer pressure per Spark in\nGrafana instead of ssh-ing into a box and running `nvidia-smi`"},"2409":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-21","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"Catching a box left on an old exporter tag before someone asks why its panels differ\nfrom its neighbours'"},"2410":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-22","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"Enrolling or un-enrolling the fleet with one key, without editing a compose file"},"2411":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-23","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"Run every command here in a **shell on the stack host**. The probe reaches each box over\nssh, and a Coder workspace container is not a shell that resolves the LAN names the\n`SPARKn_HOST` keys hold - the workspace-side run in the examples below shows exactly what\nthat costs. The whole bundle this depends on sits behind the Compose profile\n`profiles: [\"observability\"]`, and observability.md is the page for\nthe six services that do the scraping."},"2412":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-24","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"Two containers per box, both created by this CLI and by nothing else:"},"2413":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-25","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"| Container             | Image                                      | Port (.env key, default)          | What it answers                                                                          |\n| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `spark-node-exporter` | `quay.io/prometheus/node-exporter:v1.12.1` | `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (9100) | CPU, memory, disk, filesystems, network, conntrack, file descriptors, `node_processes_*` |","table":"table-0"},"2414":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-26","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"| Container             | Image                                                      | Port (.env key, default)          | What it answers                                                                                                                            |\n| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `spark-dcgm-exporter` | `nvcr.io/nvidia/k8s/dcgm-exporter:4.4.2-4.7.0-ubuntu22.04` | `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` (9400) | The `DCGM_FI_*` GPU series - utilisation, memory copy, framebuffer used and free, temperatures, clocks, power, PCIe replays, remapped rows |","table":"table-0"},"2415":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-27","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"**Neither of them exists until you run `--apply`.** A box that has never been enrolled\nruns no exporter, so Prometheus scrapes nothing and the Spark panels stay empty. This\nchange installs nothing on its own: no deploy, no `up`, no `refresh-configs` creates a\ncontainer on a Spark. `--apply` is the only flag that touches a box, and it says so in\nits own header line before the first one\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:327`)."},"2416":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-28","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"Both containers are idempotent by name (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:79-80`), so a second\n`--apply` starts what is already there rather than creating a duplicate on a port the\nfirst one holds. The DCGM exporter is not run on the image's own field list: the CLI\nferries `observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv` to `/opt/evo-t1/dcgm/custom-counters.csv`\non the box and bind-mounts it read-only\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:97-98`, `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:239`), so the `DCGM_FI_*`\nseries Prometheus sees are the ones this repo reviews. Editing that CSV and re-running\n`--apply` reports `updated`, and the CLI then tells you to `--restart` so the running\nexporter rereads it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:696-697`)."},"2417":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-29","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`.env.example:629`. It is read by **this CLI**, at `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:212` - not\nby Compose. Nothing in `docker-compose.yml` consults it, because the exporters do not run\nin the stack's project at all; they run on the boxes. With the key off:"},"2418":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-30","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`--apply` skips every box instead of installing a container nothing scrapes\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:541`),"},"2419":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-31","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`--targets` prints the empty document `[]` (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:302`),"},"2420":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-32","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`--status` still probes, because knowing what a box runs never needs the flag."},"2421":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-33","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"The other keys, all in `.env.example`: `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (line 607),\n`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` (line 612), `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE` (line 621, the pin\nabove, and the knob you correct if the registry refuses that tag). The host-side exporters\nthis bundle adds live beside them - `OBS_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (583), `OBS_CADVISOR_PORT`\n(590), `OBS_BLACKBOX_PORT` (596), `OBS_ALERTMANAGER_PORT` (601) - and\n`ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL` (635) is empty by default; nothing requires it. Those four\nhost exporters publish on the **host's loopback only**, so a probe of them runs in a host\nshell, the same as Prometheus' own UI on `PROMETHEUS_PORT`."},"2422":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-34","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"The fleet itself comes from `SPARK_HOSTS` or the `SPARKn_HOST` keys and is resolved by\n`derive_spark_hosts` (`scripts/lib/spark.sh:67`); this CLI derives no hostname, and every\nbox line it prints names the host, because three Sparks of which one is quiet is otherwise\nindistinguishable from three Sparks of which one is down."},"2423":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-35","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#plan-the-default-and-the-one-that-is-safe-anywhere","content":"No flag, or `--dry-run`, is a plan: it opens no ssh session, starts no container, and\nprints what is configured plus the exact `docker run` lines `--apply` would ferry. This\nis the run to make before pointing Prometheus at three boxes you have not looked at.\nCaptured output, with the CLI's placeholder host names replaced by `<host>`:"},"2424":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-36","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#plan-the-default-and-the-one-that-is-safe-anywhere","content":"That run was made from a Coder workspace, which is why the fleet fell back to the\nplaceholder trio. From the host shell, with the `SPARKn_HOST` keys in the checkout's\n`.env`, the same command prints three real boxes and no host warning, and adds the\n`docker run` lines the plan promises (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:412-425`)."},"2425":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-37","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"This is the only mode that looks at a box. It ferries one read-only probe\n(`scripts/lib/probes/spark-monitor.sh`) base64-encoded through `probe_transport`\n(`scripts/lib/spark.sh:173`), which prints twelve `KEY=value` lines per box: docker\navailability, both exporters' state, port and image, the DCGM counter count, and the GPU\ncount and models. The probe never pulls, never writes, never installs, and exits 0 even\nwhen the box has nothing on it - the CLI decides what is a warning. Captured output from\nthis workspace, host names elided:"},"2426":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-38","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"A box that answers prints `ok:` lines instead of that warning - docker available with\nthe probe version, `node_exporter is running on :<port> (<image>)`, `DCGM exporter is\nrunning on :<port>, tag <image>`, and a `note:` with the counter count, the GPU count and\nthe models (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:612-644`). **Not verified from a live box**: no\nSpark was reachable from where these commands ran, so the answering-`box` table above is\nquoted from those source lines, not from an observed run. The unreachable form is what\nwas actually measured."},"2427":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-39","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"The only mutator. Set `SPARK_MONITOR_ENABLED=true` first or every box is skipped. Per box\nit reports each container as `created`, `started` (an existing one), or `already running`,\nand the counter list as `same`, `updated` or `absent`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:662-700`). Exit status is 0 when every box answered or is\nsimply not set up yet, and non-zero only when a mutator ran and a box failed - a Spark\nwith nothing installed is a `warning:`, not a failure, because that is the state a\nfreshly imaged box is in."},"2428":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-40","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"`--apply` was **not run for this page**: it creates containers on real hardware, and no\nSpark answered ssh from where the other commands ran. Read the plan output above as the\ncontract for what it does."},"2429":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-41","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"The `--` separator is not decoration. Measured:"},"2430":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-42","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"with exit 2 - Bazel claims the flag as its own build option and the script never starts.\nEvery flag this CLI takes goes after `--`."},"2431":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-43","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-rest","content":"`docker restart` on both containers per box (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:332`). This is what\nyou run after editing `observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv`, since a running exporter\nkeeps the field list it started with. A box with no such container is a `warning:` naming\n`--apply` (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:725`)."},"2432":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-44","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-rest","content":"Restricts any mode to one box number (`1`, `2`, `3`). A number outside the resolved fleet\nis refused with the fleet size and the resolved list. Measured, plan mode with `--box 1`:"},"2433":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-45","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-rest","content":"One JSON object on **stdout** and nothing else; every `ok:` / `warning:` / `summary:` line\nroutes to stderr while `--json` is set, so `| jq` sees a document. Verified: stdout was\none line, 1397 bytes, parseable, carrying `mode`, `host_source`, `monitor_enabled`, both\nimages, both ports, `ssh_port`, a `boxes` array with one object per box (`probed`,\n`docker`, `node_exporter`, `dcgm`, `dcgm_counter_count`, `gpu_count`, `gpu_names`,\n`probe_version`, `verdict`, `detail`) and a `summary` object."},"2434":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-46","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-rest","content":"The `file_sd` document, to stdout, writing nothing. With the flag off, verified output:"},"2435":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-47","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-rest","content":"With it on, four groups per box: the node_exporter on `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT`, the\nDCGM exporter on `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT`, and the two blackbox probe URLs on\n`SPARK_HTTP_PORT` (`/health` and `/v1/models`). Every group carries the three labels the\nrule files and the dashboards group by - `box`, `exporter`, and `hostname` - so the GPU\nrows, the host rows and the probe rows of one box all land on the same identity. `--help`\nprints the usage block from the script's own header. The modes are mutually exclusive:\n`--status cannot be combined with --apply; pick one mode`."},"2436":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-48","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"`--targets` prints the document; it never writes it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:340`). The\nfile lives at `observability/spark-targets/sparks.json` on the **stack host**, and that\npath is gitignored (`.gitignore:76`) precisely because it names your boxes - three\naddresses are not something this repo commits. `//scripts:spark-monitor` is what generates\nit on the host, from the three ports above."},"2437":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-49","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"Prometheus reads that one file with `file_sd_configs` at `refresh_interval: 60s` on all\nthree Spark jobs (`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:149`, `:171`, `:195`), so a\nchanged file is picked up within a minute without a restart. Because four kinds of target\nshare the file, each job keeps only its own with a relabel `action: keep` on the `exporter`\nlabel - without those filters the two probe URLs would be scraped as metric endpoints and\nevery box would carry two permanently failing targets. The two blackbox jobs take the\naddress from the same file and pass it as `__param_target`, so the target label carries the\nbox rather than the prober. The prober itself is scraped as a service of its own\n(`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:219`), which is what makes\n`up{job=\"blackbox\"}` exist for `BlackboxProbeGap` to fire on. Spark alerts stamp\n`tier=spark` and `spark=<hostname>`."},"2438":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-50","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"The consequence of the empty document above: **an unenrolled fleet reads as zero Sparks.**\nNo group in `sparks.json` means no target, which means no `up` series, which means an\nempty panel rather than a red one - the symptom looks like \"nothing is wrong\" from\nGrafana. Check it with `--targets` before you blame the dashboard."},"2439":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-51","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"Say these plainly, because each one costs someone an afternoon if they meet it\nunexpected:"},"2440":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-52","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"**The DCGM tag has never been pulled.** `nvcr.io` answers 404 to an anonymous token, so\nthe pin `nvcr.io/nvidia/k8s/dcgm-exporter:4.4.2-4.7.0-ubuntu22.04` was not verifiable\nupstream the way the other three were. The first `--apply` against a box is what proves\nit, and a wrong tag surfaces there as the registry's own error: `--apply` prints\ndocker's reason rather than swallowing it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:675`,\n`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:685`), so you read what the registry said. Correct the tag in\n`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE` and re-run - it is a key, not a code change."},"2441":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-53","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"**`--status` prints the tag a box is really running**, taken from the container\n(`scripts/lib/probes/spark-monitor.sh:111-114`), and says so when it is not the pin this\nCLI would install (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:644`). That `note:` line is how a stale pin\ngets caught: the box that was enrolled last spring keeps answering on the old tag and\nnobody looks."},"2442":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-54","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"**Profiling counters return nothing without `SYS_ADMIN`.** Every `DCGM_FI_PROF_*` field\nneeds the exporter started with `--cap-add SYS_ADMIN` (plus\n`NVreg_RestrictProfilingToAdminUsers=0` on the box), and this CLI does not pass that cap\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:239`). A box answers those fields with no rows at all rather\nthan an error - `DCGM_FI_PROF_*` being absent from a scrape is the expected result, not\na broken exporter (`observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv:98-99`)."},"2443":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-55","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"**A box without NVLink logs nothing for NVLink fields.** The per-link\n`DCGM_FI_DEV_NVLINK_*_L<n>` counters are commented out of the shipped field list for\nthat reason: on a GPU that exposes no NVLink each one is a per-field error in the\nexporter log on every scrape (`observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv:72-81`). The\naggregate `DCGM_FI_DEV_NVLINK_BANDWIDTH_TOTAL` ships and reads zero."},"2444":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-56","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#failure-modes","content":"**ssh will not reach a box.** In `--status` this is a `warning:` naming the ssh error, and\nthe exit status stays 0 (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:566-576`): a box that is off, or one\nthis shell has no credentials for, is a not-yet state. Under `--apply` or `--restart` the\nsame box is a `fail_item` and the run exits non-zero, because a fleet where two of three\nboxes were enrolled is precisely the drift this CLI exists to remove. Check\n`SPARK_SSH_USER` (default: your login name, `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:101`) and `--port`,\nand run it from the host shell - a workspace cannot resolve the LAN names. If the box\ndrops packets rather than refusing, you see `ssh was cut off after <n>s (timeout(1)\nguard)`; on a host without `timeout(1)` the CLI warns up front that the round trips run\nuncapped."},"2445":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-57","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#failure-modes","content":"**A port is already taken on a box.** `--apply` reports it per container as\n`<name> is not running (<state>): <what docker said>`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:675`, `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:685`) - the text after the\ncolon is docker's, so `port is already allocated` names the process holding 9100 or 9400.\nThe containers are idempotent by name, so this is a *different* process on the port, not\nthis CLI double-booking itself. Move the port with `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` /\n`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` - the target document and the scrape follow the key - and\nre-run `--apply`."},"2446":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-58","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#failure-modes","content":"**An exporter container exists and is not answering.** Two shapes, and they read\ndifferently. A container that `docker ps` does not list (exited, restarting) comes back\n`absent`, so the CLI says `node_exporter is absent -- Prometheus scrapes <host>:9100 and\ngets nothing` and points at `--apply --box <n>`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:616-624`). A container that *is* listed but does not serve\nstill counts as running for node_exporter, and shows up one line later on the DCGM side:\nthe probe counts counters from a live local scrape of `:<port>/metrics` and only falls\nback to the field list baked into the image when that scrape returns nothing\n(`scripts/lib/probes/spark-monitor.sh:196-206`), so a `note:` whose counter count matches\nthe image's default list rather than this repo's file means the exporter is up and not\nserving your field list. `--restart` is the first thing to try; `--apply` re-ferries the\nCSV if the file changed."},"2447":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-59","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#failure-modes","content":"When a Spark panel stays empty, work outward in this order: `--status` (is the container\nthere and serving), `--targets` (is the box in the document at all), then the scrape on\nthe host through `bazelisk run //scripts:observability-check`. Symptoms that outgrow those\nthree commands belong in troubleshooting.md."},"2448":{"id":"/troubleshooting","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"page","content":"Troubleshooting","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting"},"2449":{"id":"/troubleshooting-0","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Symptom-first list of what breaks on this box: the error string, the cause, the recipe, and what the fix costs."},"2450":{"id":"/troubleshooting-1","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Troubleshooting"},"2451":{"id":"/troubleshooting-2","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#stack-bring-up","content":"Stack bring-up"},"2452":{"id":"/troubleshooting-3","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"`not a directory: Are you trying to mount a directory onto a file` -> missing compose bind-mount source"},"2453":{"id":"/troubleshooting-4","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#fileaccessexception--permission-denied-on-homepageassetsiconsevo-t1svg---leftover-runfiles-bind-mount","content":"`FileAccessException` / `Permission denied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg` -> leftover runfiles bind-mount"},"2454":{"id":"/troubleshooting-5","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#error-env-not-found-from-bazelisk-run-scriptsdashboard-password---the-runfiles-copy-has-no-working-env","content":"`error: .../.env not found` from `bazelisk run //scripts:dashboard-password` -> the runfiles copy has no working `.env`"},"2455":{"id":"/troubleshooting-6","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"`Could not resolve host: github.com` -> Tailscale MagicDNS is the only nameserver"},"2456":{"id":"/troubleshooting-7","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#model-lane-spark-litellm-the-agent-backends","content":"Model lane: Spark, LiteLLM, the agent backends"},"2457":{"id":"/troubleshooting-8","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#env-line-188-qwen25-coder14b-command-not-found-from-bazelisk-run-scriptspull-models---the-model-list-was-being-parsed-as-shell","content":"`./.env: line 188: qwen2.5-coder:14b: command not found` from `bazelisk run //scripts:pull-models` -> the model list was being parsed as shell"},"2458":{"id":"/troubleshooting-9","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"How do I know the IPEX-LLM server came up? -> look for `runners=[ipex_llm]`"},"2459":{"id":"/troubleshooting-10","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Grok agent mode makes no tool calls (it narrates and stops) -> an Arc alias served it"},"2460":{"id":"/troubleshooting-11","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#the-tensorfold-log-is-full-of-warnings-or-resumed-at-0---read-the-line-shapes-first","content":"The TensorFold log is full of warnings, or `resumed at 0` -> read the line shapes first"},"2461":{"id":"/troubleshooting-12","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Sessions log `compact failed: exceeded wall-clock budget 300s (runaway generation)` -> a compaction stream that never sent a token, bought three times"},"2462":{"id":"/troubleshooting-13","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#a-summary-on-agent-xhigh-fast-or-agent-swarm-fast-still-takes-minutes---the-fast-compaction-hop-did-not-fire","content":"A summary on `agent-xhigh-fast` or `agent-swarm-fast` still takes minutes -> the fast-compaction hop did not fire"},"2463":{"id":"/troubleshooting-14","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-ch