2,"2855":1319,"2856":1854,"2857":1454,"2858":2449,"2859":1324,"2860":1553,"2861":2725,"2862":2539,"2863":2080,"2864":1325,"2865":2923,"2866":2228,"2867":2450,"2868":1296,"2869":2751,"2870":1402,"2871":1901,"2872":1323,"2873":1862,"2874":2372,"2875":1309,"2876":1561,"2877":2117,"2878":498,"2879":2312,"2880":2284,"2881":1338,"2882":2537,"2883":361,"2884":1206,"2885":2847,"2886":1093,"2887":614,"2888":2515,"2889":1328,"2890":499,"2891":2589,"2892":495,"2893":2793,"2894":2245,"2895":2707,"2896":2047,"2897":2394,"2898":1662,"2899":2636,"2900":1327,"2901":2856,"2902":2506,"2903":1287,"2904":41,"2905":126,"2906":9,"2907":1302,"2908":469,"2909":425,"2910":2124,"2911":1321,"2912":3035,"2913":2067,"2914":1418,"2915":1108,"2916":1844,"2917":1922,"2918":2604,"2919":1332,"2920":1354,"2921":1827,"2922":2583,"2923":1314,"2924":2401,"2925":1185,"2926":1335,"2927":3036,"2928":1692,"2929":1449,"2930":1695,"2931":1690,"2932":1304,"2933":1981,"2934":1292,"2935":2181,"2936":1147,"2937":1075,"2938":1260,"2939":2804,"2940":2799,"2941":2413,"2942":2800,"2943":2467,"2944":1658,"2945":1688,"2946":1493,"2947":1443,"2948":1747,"2949":1202,"2950":2189,"2951":1376,"2952":2402,"2953":1267,"2954":1946,"2955":2079,"2956":2070,"2957":239,"2958":2462,"2959":2429,"2960":1642,"2961":2403,"2962":2302,"2963":1947,"2964":2566,"2965":211,"2966":1428,"2967":1430,"2968":2115,"2969":2076,"2970":2752,"2971":1903,"2972":1190,"2973":3034,"2974":1674,"2975":1977,"2976":1268,"2977":2932,"2978":2380,"2979":2617,"2980":686,"2981":684,"2982":693,"2983":685,"2984":682,"2985":690,"2986":687,"2987":683,"2988":692,"2989":689,"2990":688,"2991":691,"2992":1239,"2993":240,"2994":179,"2995":473,"2996":85,"2997":180,"2998":181,"2999":1191,"3000":1809,"3001":2532,"3002":2123,"3003":2747,"3004":1905,"3005":1976,"3006":3019,"3007":2528,"3008":1978,"3009":1491,"3010":2976,"3011":1362,"3012":122,"3013":62,"3014":28,"3015":156,"3016":1068,"3017":2062,"3018":1906,"3019":1869,"3020":1942,"3021":2008,"3022":2415,"3023":1915,"3024":1001,"3025":999,"3026":1003,"3027":1000,"3028":1002,"3029":998,"3030":1961,"3031":1358,"3032":1098,"3033":2718,"3034":720,"3035":721,"3036":3025,"3037":2027,"3038":2862,"3039":905,"3040":904,"3041":906,"3042":907,"3043":2828,"3044":1836,"3045":1840,"3046":1841,"3047":1755,"3048":1902,"3049":1058,"3050":2616,"3051":2730,"3052":1439},"orderedDocs":[[1456,"**`--data DIR`** points the two volume checks at a directory instead of asking the\ndaemon where the volume is mounted, so the audit can be run against a copy of one."],[2441,"**`--status` prints the tag a box is really running**, taken from the container\n(`scripts/lib/probes/spark-monitor.sh:111-114`), and says so when it is not the pin this\nCLI would install (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:644`). That `note:` line is how a stale pin\ngets caught: the box that was enrolled last spring keeps answering on the old tag and\nnobody looks."],[1916,"**`--task`** calls `coder task create` once the repository exists. The Coder server here is v2.36.6\nand does ship `coder task`, but the Terraform provider is pinned to `~> 2.18`, so a task cannot be\ndeclared inside a template. The CLI therefore creates it at run time and, when it cannot reach the\nCoder API -- the usual case outside a logged-in host shell -- prints the exact command and continues\nwith exit 0. That follows `new-workspace.sh`, which treats \"cannot create right now\" as a not-yet\nrather than an error."],[1433,"**`--writable` matters more than it looks.** The official image sets\n`HERMES_WRITE_SAFE_ROOT=/opt/data`, so a file-tool write anywhere else is refused\noutright and no approval card even appears. To have the assistant edit files on the\nhost, name the directory: `--writable \"${STACK_DEPLOY_PATH:-/opt/stack-deploy}\"` or\nthe checkout path."],[1464,"**`agent-swarm`** is one of the nine Spark aliases, and one of four - with\n**`agent-explore`**, **`agent-review`** and **`agent-swarm-fast`** - that the LiteLLM\nsession pin does not cover: same model, same three boxes, same baked `xhigh`, but each\nrequest is placed on the least-busy healthy box. It is the lane for a parallel fan-out,\nnot for one long Hermes session - a single thread would give up the pin's prefix-cache\nlocality and could be prefilled on a different box each turn. Hermes keeps `agent` as its\ndefault.\nThe other three pinless groups serve a Grok Build subagent fleet rather than a chat:\n`agent-explore` bakes `low` for a wave of read-only mappers, `agent-review` bakes `xhigh`\nfor the skeptics that check what that fleet wrote, and `agent-swarm-fast` is `agent-swarm`\nagain for a wave of writers. The two `-fast` aliases, that one and its pinned twin\n**`agent-xhigh-fast`**, differ from their parents in one call only: the proxy, not the\nclient, re-serves that session's conversation summary from the Arc group\n`compaction-fast`, and every other turn still runs on a Spark. See\nlitellm-routing.md."],[1429,"**`check` is the first thing to run when an answer looks impossible.** It reports the\ndaemon, the compose CLI, the project, the container state, whether the service\nresolves, the image, whether the data volume is seeded, whether the model key is set\nor still a shipped placeholder, whether LiteLLM answers `/health/liveliness`, and the\nwork directory. It prints key names and never a value, and it launches nothing."],[165,"**`coder_env` is the only provably-earlier file write.** The agent's\n`writeSecretFiles` (`agent.go`) runs before any start script, `MkdirAll 0700` then\n`WriteFile 0600`. It takes a string value, so it can carry a payload, and it writes\ninto the agent's secret directory rather than anywhere you name."],[166,"**`CODER_SCRIPT_DATA_DIR` is the per-script scratch the agent already hands you.**\n`agent/agentscripts/agentscripts.go` appends `CODER_SCRIPT_DATA_DIR` and\n`CODER_SCRIPT_BIN_DIR` to every script's environment, which is a writable place to\nassemble something across scripts."],[1158,"**`Ctrl+V` on a secure origin is not a paste.** VS Code binds terminal paste to\n`Ctrl+Shift+V` on Linux and `Cmd+V` on macOS, so `Ctrl+V` reaches the pane as\nthe `\\x16` byte (readline's quoted-insert, vim's visual-block). The seed\nre-binds nothing there on purpose: a TUI in the pane owns that chord."],[2906,"**`litellm` alone is `down` with a 401**: the scrape sends the master key as a bearer\ntoken that it reads from a file, `/etc/prometheus-secrets/litellm_token`\n(`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml`), which Compose writes from\n`LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` when it creates the container. Measured on v3.16.0, the collector\nexpands environment variables under `global.external_labels` - they come back filled\nin at `/api/v1/status/config` - and nowhere a credential could hide: a scrape target\naddress and an `authorization` block send the written text back verbatim, so a value\nit could not substitute is rejected. That file is the one credential in the bundle a rotate can strand, and it\nbehaves the opposite way from the bind mounts above: it is a Compose `configs` entry,\nand Compose writes those when it *creates* the container. Measured with the compose\nthis repo ships, `up -d` after a rotate prints `Running`, keeps the same container and\nthe same `com.docker.compose.config-hash`, and the file inside still holds the old key;\na `restart` matches it. The recreate that works names the service -\n`docker compose -p evo-t1-prod --profile observability -f docker-compose.yml up -d\n--force-recreate prometheus`, the invocation `prod-stack` itself prints with the\nrecreate added, since `prod-stack up` passes no flags through. The response body names the fault: LiteLLM answers a\nmissing bearer with \"Malformed API Key passed in\", and a wrong one with\n`No connected db.` - which is an auth failure at v1.102.1, not a database complaint\n(this bundle runs LiteLLM with no DB at all). `bazelisk run //scripts:observability-check`\nprints that target's `lastError`, so it names the 401 without a `docker logs` read."],[1155,"**`Prefix m` is the wheel, not a copy route.** One press hands the pointer to\ntmux so the wheel walks pane history, the next hands it back. It is the escape\nhatch for a long scroll, because tmux paints in the alternate screen - it sends\n`ESC[?1049h` either way - so the client holds no scrollback of its own and a wheel\nover a pane has nothing to walk. Output that has scrolled away is copied by\nbringing it on screen first: `Prefix-[`, scroll it into view, select it with a\nclient drag, `q`. A drag while `Prefix m` has the pointer on selects nothing,\nsince the copy bindings stay unbound either way."],[2239,"**`pull` leaves `.env` alone and says so.** A failed export is an `error:` line naming\nthe file as untouched, and the verb exits 1. An export that comes back empty is a\n`warning:` and the same untouched file. A key the vault does not carry, or carries\nempty, is a `warning:` per key and the existing value in `.env` stays exactly as it was"],[2242,"**`rotate` stops at the first half.** If the vault refuses the write, `.env` is left\nuntouched and the verb exits 1 - the stack keeps running on the old value. The reverse\ncase prints the recovery in the error line: if the vault took the new value and the env\nwrite failed, re-run `pull` to converge."],[985,"**`run` can call a helper, and a helper that `exit`s is safe.** `run` forks, so\n`run --separate-stderr usage_error '…'` followed by `[ \"${status}\" -eq 2 ]` works even\nthough `usage_error` ends in `exit 2`. Pass a real command (`bash \"${SCRIPT}\" …`) when\nthe script is what is under test."],[984,"**`run` keeps stdout in `$output`.** With `run --separate-stderr` there is no `$stdout`:\n`$output` is stdout and `$stderr` is stderr. An assertion written against `$stdout`\ncompares against an empty string and fails, which is how a whole suite of real\nassertions can look like a broken harness."],[1163,"**`terminal.limited-color` during an agent turn.** A tool command launched by a\nrunning Grok session inherits `NO_COLOR=1`, `FORCE_COLOR=0`, `CLICOLOR=0` and\n`CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never` from that session, so a `grok doctor` run from inside\nan agent turn reads color as off. The `grok` process itself and the tmux server\ncarry none of those variables, so run it from a plain terminal for the real\ncapability: the line reads `color truecolor` and `themes all`."],[1165,"**`terminal.ssh-wrap`** is a recommendation about the computer you type `ssh`\non, since the wrapper has to sit in front of that client: run\n`grok doctor fix ssh-wrap` there, or `grok wrap ssh <host>` per connection. It\nforwards copies to the local clipboard and restores terminal modes when the\nlink drops. `/settings` -> **Show contextual hints** -> **SSH wrap** silences\nthe tip."],[1166,"**`unavailable` tmux and runtime probes.** `tmux.version`,\n`tmux.client-features` and the `runtime.*` lines come back `unavailable`\nwhenever Doctor has no interactive client of its own, which covers a piped run\nand a run from an agent tool call. Read them by hand (`tmux -V`,\n`tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'`) or from `/doctor` in a live\nsession."],[2238,"**`up` cannot be stopped by it.** Compose never contacts Infisical: it interpolates `.env`,\nwhich is a file on disk, and whether the three containers are part of the project is a\nprofile decision made from one key, not a network result. The runner's own pull runs before\nthat interpolation and a failed one costs a line, not the bring-up - `warning: the env file\nwas not refreshed from Infisical; <file> keeps the credentials it already has - retry with\nbazelisk run //scripts:infisical -- pull` - and compose recreates against what the file\nalready held. `//scripts:prod-stack` and `//scripts:update-stack` behave the same way; the\nlatter plans the fetch instead of running it under `--dry-run`."],[1164,"**`voice.no-input-device`.** Expected in a workspace. The container has no\ncapture device at all (`/dev/snd` and `/proc/asound` are absent) and no\nrecorder on PATH. The probe only looks for a recorder binary, so installing\n`alsa-utils` moves the line to `available` while capture still has nowhere to\nread from, and `features.voice_mode = false` leaves the finding in place.\nNeither would be a fix, so the stack ships neither and the finding stands as an\naccurate report of a headless container. Dictation belongs on a laptop-side\n`grok`. To give a workspace dictation instead: run PulseAudio on the workstation\nwith its TCP module enabled, export `PULSE_SERVER=tcp=<workstation>:4713` into\nthe workspace, and install `pulseaudio-utils` there for `parec`."],[2191,"**0** - the verb did its job. Always for `status` and `check`: a check that fails on a\nstack that never adopted Infisical is a check nobody runs. `run` exits 0 on the\nflag-off refusal too, having printed the `note:`; every other mutating verb exits 5."],[2192,"**1** - a real failure. The CLI errored, a read-back hash disagreed, a `rotate` target\nis not a managed key. A `rotate` or `run` that names no key and no command lands here as\nwell, on an `error:` line."],[2193,"**2** - an unknown subcommand, always raised before any work starts."],[2194,"**5** - refused, because `INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off or a prerequisite is empty. Printed\nas a `note:` that names the key to set."],[159,"**A `#!` line does not help.** The body still becomes\n`execve(env, [env, bash, -c, body])`; the interpreter line is content inside the\nargument."],[851,"**A Bats file is cheap; a script is not.** `tests-for tests/bats/common.bats` returns five targets.\n`tests-for scripts/lib/common.sh` returns 60 of the 67 test targets on this checkout, because every\nsuite declares `//scripts:doc_sources`, which is every `scripts/**.sh` (66 targets reach that\ngroup). That is the honest graph answer: editing a broadly sourced script genuinely re-runs nearly\nthe whole suite set, and knowing it before you start is what decides whether to run the derived\nlist or the aggregate."],[2443,"**A box without NVLink logs nothing for NVLink fields.** The per-link\n`DCGM_FI_DEV_NVLINK_*_L<n>` counters are commented out of the shipped field list for\nthat reason: on a GPU that exposes no NVLink each one is a per-field error in the\nexporter log on every scrape (`observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv:72-81`). The\naggregate `DCGM_FI_DEV_NVLINK_BANDWIDTH_TOTAL` ships and reads zero."],[40,"**A bug fix ships with its proof.** The test that fails before the change and passes after it,\nwith the reproduction's failing output quoted in the pull request. \"Cannot reproduce\" is a\nfinding to post on the issue, and the issue stays open."],[3014,"**A checked-in `config/flags.yaml`** once there are enough switches that a reviewer cannot\nhold them, or when one tree runs in several environments with different settings. Copy\n`config/flags.yaml.example` from this repository. Keep the schema - `default`, `owner`,\n`description`, `expires`, `environments`, optional `stacks` overrides - and keep the real\nprocess environment winning, so CI and an operator can still force a value."],[559,"**A defined-but-empty key crash-loops the server.** Any `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\ncounts as a configured provider even when its value is empty, and an empty key\nmakes `coder server` exit at boot with `external auth provider \"\" doesn't have a\nvalid id` - which the compose restart policy turns into a crash loop. Do not set\nonly `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_APP_INSTALL_URL` either: it suppresses the injected\nApp like any other key in the set. A custom GitHub App belongs in `coder.env`\nfully filled, including\n`APP_INSTALL_URL=https://github.com/apps/<slug>/installations/new`."],[1174,"**A fourth command rides that same body: `/ez-micro-plan`.** It converts nothing - it is\nthe parent's dispatch protocol for a fan-out of subagents: a repo-local plan at\n`.grok/plans/<slug>/plan.md`, the change split into micro tasks, layered context briefs\nbeside it, and a check that refuses a plan too narrow to fill the session's subagent\nslots. It shares the body because that body exists to stay under the one-argument\nceiling (coder-templates.md), not because\nthe four commands do the same job; three of the four carry a python helper next to their\n`SKILL.md` and `ez-trunk-based-repo` carries none. Its payloads are\n`templates/common/ez-micro-plan.SKILL.md` and\n`templates/common/ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py`, seeded into `~/.coder/skills/` and its\n`~/.agents` twin on the same absent-only contract, the helper under `scripts/` like the\ntrio's. What the plan carries and what the check prints is under\nThe fan-out seam."],[2171,"**A key the shell could not use as an identifier** - empty, starting with a digit, or holding a\ncharacter outside `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, which is also where blank and comment lines fall out - is\nskipped silently. Nothing is printed for it; the only line that says how many keys landed is the\nsuccess line, `ok: infisical: loaded N secret keys into <home>/.infisical/agent.env`, and it\ncounts what the publish loop actually wrote."],[1175,"**A leftover `/learn` seed retires itself.** This skill was seeded under the name\n`learn` until it moved, and a copy left standing keeps offering that old name beside\n`/ez-learn` for the life of the volume; there is no bundled `learn` for it to hide,\nbecause no published source names one. Both startup scripts delete\n`~/.coder/skills/learn/SKILL.md` and `~/.agents/skills/learn/SKILL.md` and prune the\ndirectory each leaves, but only while the file still carries the\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker; a marker-free `learn/SKILL.md` is user-owned\nand stays. `refresh-configs`, `storage-clean` and `seed-dev-env` retire the same two\npaths on reseed. That removal only finishes the rename: there is no upstream `/learn`\nwaiting on the name, because no published source names one."],[31,"**A local commit is free, a push is not.** Nothing leaves the machine until you push, so err\ntoward committing, including a `WIP:` checkpoint before a step that might break something green.\nHistory is local until then, so amend the message, fold the checkpoint into the commit it belongs\nto, and reorder there; that door closes at the push, after which a shared commit is never\nrewritten."],[2445,"**A port is already taken on a box.** `--apply` reports it per container as\n`<name> is not running (<state>): <what docker said>`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:675`, `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:685`) - the text after the\ncolon is docker's, so `port is already allocated` names the process holding 9100 or 9400.\nThe containers are idempotent by name, so this is a *different* process on the port, not\nthis CLI double-booking itself. Move the port with `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` /\n`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` - the target document and the scrape follow the key - and\nre-run `--apply`."],[2370,"**A published dev stack needs its own routers here.** The `dev-stack.env` behind\n`bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack` moves every published port by 10000 (the recipe is\nin the\nREADME),\nso that stack's `router` answers on `18080` (`STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` default 8080) and\nits names carry the environment label\n(`coder-dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`). A dev name is a different `Host` rule and a\ndifferent backend port: add a router per dev name to the file above aimed at\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:18080`, and the DNS records for those names have to exist\ntoo, or the browser fails before this box is ever asked."],[501,"**A token that authenticates still has to be granted.** A credential that was never\nGRANTED the repository fails clone and push, and reads prove nothing about write: a private\nrepository hidden from the credential answers 403 or 404 on both lanes, and a public one\nanswers a read probe with 200 for any authenticated caller, so a read-only credential\npasses a read test while every write to that repo is denied. `check` therefore asks the\nwrite question directly: one GET of `<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack`, the\nendpoint `git push` itself negotiates through."],[2170,"**A value whose opening single quote does not close on its own line** - a PEM or a multi-line\ncertificate is the shape - is dropped whole. Its continuation lines are swallowed until one\ncarries a quote, because publishing them would invent keys out of secret bytes. One note per\nboot, naming neither key nor value: `infisical: skipped a secret whose value spans more than\none line; only single-line secrets are published.` The body carries no other shape, so a\ncredential of that shape has to reach a workspace by some path other than this fetch."],[2258,"**A vault value is never sourced in the shape the CLI wrote it.** Measured against\n`infisical/infisical:v0.167.10` with CLI v0.45.8: `secrets export --format=dotenv` wraps\neach value in single quotes and leaves an embedded quote raw, so a secret carrying an\napostrophe closes its quote early and the rest of the line is shell - sourcing it either\naborts or runs the tail as a command. `--format=dotenv-export` uses the same writer and\nfails the same way. The boot body therefore takes every value apart and re-emits it under\nquoting that has no escape hatch, which is what makes `~/.bashrc`'s `set -a; . agent.env`\nsafe to keep. Anything else that reads that file inherits the guarantee; nothing should\nfeed the CLI's own dotenv output to a shell."],[2241,"**A workspace still starts.** The boot fetch is best-effort: with the flag off, with\n`INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START` off, with no CLI in the image (the golden image bakes the\npinned release, so that is an image older than the pin), with a refused Universal Auth\nlogin, with an empty export, or with a refused publish, it exits 0 and the workspace\ncontinues its startup. `agent.env` keeps whatever the last start wrote, since the body\nstages beside the destination and moves in one step rather than truncating first. A vault\noutage has never once stopped a workspace."],[785,"**Abbreviations.** A term listed in `docs/includes/abbreviations.md` as `*[TERM]: definition`\nis wrapped wherever it appears in prose. Hover or focus shows the definition; click or Enter\nopens it in a dialog. Text inside a link, a heading, inline code, or an existing term is left\nalone. Keep each definition tooltip-sized."],[2904,"**Absent from the list entirely**: the job is not in\n`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml`. Add it there and let the collector read it\nagain: that file arrives through a bind mount, which follows the *path*, so the edit is\nalready inside the container and only the running process is behind it. Restarting the\nservice picks it up, and `POST /-/reload` answers 200 on this one\n(`--web.enable-lifecycle`, `docker-compose.yml:854`) without dropping the scrape loop's\nin-flight state. A recreate is what you need when the mount line or another compose\nsetting changed, not for an edit to a file that is already mounted."],[635,"**Action cache.** An action's outputs are keyed on its inputs and command line, so an\nunchanged suite replays its cached PASS instead of re-running Bats."],[1840,"**Add an auto-reboot watchdog.** A box sitting in the initramfs with dropbear\nlistening is the recoverable state; rebooting on a timer destroys the\nrecovery window and can turn one bad boot into a loop. `-I` bounds the\nsession, and the listener stays."],[1052,"**After install:** the UI is on `https://<host>:4443`. Both ports serve TLS with the\nself-signed certificate Kasm's own installer generates, and plain `http://` reaches\nneither port."],[2116,"**Alertmanager's UI is published on loopback only** (`OBS_ALERTMANAGER_PORT`, 9093),\nfor the same reason Coder's `:2112` metrics port is: that UI can silence a firing\nalert, so an unauthenticated silencer on the LAN is a way to make a real fault\ninvisible. Read it from a shell on the stack host."],[1968,"**Alloy, not Loki, reads the disk.** Loki has no Docker credential and no host mount.\nThe only container that sees the host's container log directory is Alloy, and it sees\nit read-only. That directory is `${STACK_DOCKER_LOG_DIR:-/var/lib/docker/containers}`,\nmounted at the same path inside the collector, so a host whose daemon keeps its logs\nsomewhere else (rootless Docker does) needs that key set or the collector tails\nnothing."],[1979,"**An alert a person builds in Grafana goes to the same Alertmanager.** The provisioned\ncontact point is `type: alertmanager` at `http://alertmanager:9093`\n(`observability/grafana/provisioning/alerting/stack.yaml:28`), not a webhook, so it\nspeaks `/api/v2/alerts` and Alertmanager's grouping, inhibition and silences apply to\nGrafana-originated alerts exactly as they do to Prometheus'. One place holds the\nsilences, whichever engine fired."],[1156,"**An app inside the pane drives the machine clipboard over OSC 52; the browser\nbuild drops it.** `set -g set-clipboard on` with `set -g allow-passthrough on`\nstay in the seed for that one route: vim's `+` register, and anything else that\nemits the escape, reach the machine clipboard the way a native terminal hands them\nto it. Over ssh from Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm or iTerm2 that copy lands.\ncode-server's browser build drops the escape before any clipboard call: a\nhand-written `printf '\\033]52;c;<base64>\\a'` was just as silent with\n`set-clipboard off` as with it on, which puts the drop in the browser and not in\ntmux. Upstream\nmicrosoft/vscode#318254 is\nopen with two unmerged fixes, and the shipped 4.145.0 registers no OSC 52 setting\nat all, so there is nothing here to turn on. Nothing on the tmux side can spoil\nthat clipboard either: tmux has no copy left to send, so an accidental selection\nin the pane cannot overwrite what the client just put there."],[1143,"**An attached client is not the same thing as a viewer.** code-server's pty\nhost keeps the pty open after the browser side of a tab goes away, so the tmux\nclient stays attached, keeps its session's `session_attached` at 1, and\nreceives nothing at all. Measured on this workspace's live server: 7 of its 9\nclients received 0 bytes across a 4 s window of continuous pane output while\nthe 2 tabs actually on screen each received all 12858 of them, and the quiet\nones had reported no input for 30 minutes to 36 hours. That number is the\nwhole reuse decision in the attach helper, so one of these is enough to make a\nfree session look watched forever, and the folder pays a new session for every\nlater tab. Both helpers now sweep their own sessions for a client that has been\nquiet past a 6 h grace before they read the snapshot;\n`EVO_TMUX_STALE_AFTER` (seconds) moves the number. Seeing the state yourself:"],[2115,"**An empty `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL` is the shipped state, and it pages nobody.**\nThe rules still fire and stay readable on Prometheus's `/alerts` page and on Grafana's\nAlerting page; only the delivery step is silent. Set the key to a webhook URL when you\nwant a person told, and never put a URL that carries a credential in `.env`."],[852,"**An empty result means run everything.** Nothing changed against HEAD, or the change touches no\nfile the graph tracks, and `scope` says so and points at `//:test-fast`."],[2446,"**An exporter container exists and is not answering.** Two shapes, and they read\ndifferently. A container that `docker ps` does not list (exited, restarting) comes back\n`absent`, so the CLI says `node_exporter is absent -- Prometheus scrapes <host>:9100 and\ngets nothing` and points at `--apply --box <n>`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:616-624`). A container that *is* listed but does not serve\nstill counts as running for node_exporter, and shows up one line later on the DCGM side:\nthe probe counts counters from a live local scrape of `:<port>/metrics` and only falls\nback to the field list baked into the image when that scrape returns nothing\n(`scripts/lib/probes/spark-monitor.sh:196-206`), so a `note:` whose counter count matches\nthe image's default list rather than this repo's file means the exporter is up and not\nserving your field list. `--restart` is the first thing to try; `--apply` re-ferries the\nCSV if the file changed."],[1431,"**An interactive session needs a real tty** (the TUI draws on a pty compose allocates\nfrom your terminal), so `chat` and `shell` refuse from a pipe or a cron line - that is\nwhat `ask` is for."],[37,"**Announce a long fix.** For anything past an obvious edit, a short comment saying what is about\nto change lets a maintainer redirect the work early and keeps two people off the same file."],[1430,"**Approvals are the same gate, inline.** A dangerous command stops in your tty with\n`[o]nce | [s]ession | [a]lways | [d]eny`, deny by default. There is no browser card\nhere and nothing to approve elsewhere; the terminal door does not loosen\n`approvals.mode: manual`, and `--yolo` / `HERMES_YOLO_MODE` have no place in this\nstack. See Approvals."],[278,"**Autostop is disabled by default.**"],[1479,"**Both keyless switches stay off, and that is the part that keeps the promise.**\n`agent/web_search_registry.py` resolves a backend in five steps, and its last\none is an anonymous free-tier ring - `exa`, `parallel`, `firecrawl`,\n`keenable` - reached with no key at all. A seed that named no backend and left\n`web.keyless_fallback` and `web.keyless_rescue` at their shipped `true` would\nanswer a search from a vendor's public endpoint while looking local. With both\nfalse, a query either goes to your instance or fails on the box."],[28,"**Branch first, then edit.** The work branch is cut as the first action of the change, off the\ntip of `main`. `main` is not a place to work: an agent edits it only when the operator asked for\nthat in words. Cut late and the branch is cheap to add anyway - `git switch -c` carries the\nworking tree with it - which is the fix, where `stash`, `reset --hard`, or `checkout -- .` to\nclean the tree is how an unfinished change is destroyed."],[315,"**Build repo's dev container** (`use_devcontainer`, default `true`) is immutable:\nbuilding a dev container or not is a different topology. With Yes the editor lives in\nthe dev container; with No, in the plain container."],[357,"**Clones a non-empty Git repository field** into `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`\n(in `devcontainer`, onto the `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>` bind). The\nscript creates that folder first (`mkdir -p` plus a chown to the workspace user) so\nthe editor's wait loop can see it, then clones into it. This is idempotent, runs as\nthe workspace user so their git credentials are consulted, and refuses to touch an\nexisting non-empty non-git folder there. An empty pre-created target still clones."],[29,"**Commit each unit as it lands.** A unit is what a reviewer reads as one step: a script with its\nBats file, a template with its twin, a flag with the docs that name it. Commit when that unit's\nown check is green, then start the next one. `git log --oneline main..HEAD` then shows the shape\nof the change, and a wrong turn is one `git revert` away instead of a lost afternoon."],[3013,"**Compose `profiles:`** when what you are hiding is a whole service."],[561,"**Create `coder.env` only for a *custom* GitHub OAuth app**, and fill EVERY key -\n`ID` names the provider and `TYPE` says what kind it is. Restart coder to apply\nthe file."],[566,"**Device-flow login is not the App install.** Private repos need the Coder GitHub\nApp installed from\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new\n(the workspace \"Install GitHub App\" button), then a restart so `GH_TOKEN` remints.\nThe grant must include the repos you clone, or a connected credential authenticates\nand is still 403."],[787,"**Diagrams.** A ` ```mermaid ` fence renders through `Mermaid`, follows the light/dark switch,\nand shows the chart text beside a parse error rather than leaving a blank box."],[234,"**Docker-in-docker** (`dind`, Yes/No radio, immutable)."],[158,"**Each body is its own `execve`, so each gets its own ceiling.** Splitting a body into\ntwo `coder_script` resources relocates the wall; it does not raise it. On this server\n(v2.36) the start scripts fan out concurrently in an errgroup and `ready` is set only\nafter all of them return, so splitting does not reduce a peak either.\n`coder_script_order` needs Coder `>= v2.39`. This is the escape the stack now uses: the\nthree skill payloads ride `coder_script.stack_skills` rather than the startup argument,\nbecause the devcontainer twin had 491 bytes of headroom left and three more inlined\npayloads could not fit there. The same move later made room for the rulebook:\n`GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` is the largest payload the twins inline, and it left the startup\nargument for `coder_script.agents_seed` when that twin stood 196 bytes from the budget\nand one more instruction section could not land. `grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md` still\nrides the startup\nargument - it predates the split, it fits, and its own tests pin it there."],[1838,"**Edit `/etc/crypttab` or the LUKS headers.** The volumes are read to decide\nwhether the boot can open them. Key slots, passphrases and headers are never\nwritten, so this CLI cannot be the thing that destroys a key."],[241,"**Editor colour theme** (`vscode_color_theme`, free-form, mutable, default\n`Default Dark Modern`). Written as `workbench.colorTheme` in that workspace's\ncode-server User settings, where it wins over the shared editor-settings overlay."],[1839,"**Enroll a TPM.** `systemd-cryptenroll` is a different design with a\ndifferent failure mode - a firmware update that changes the PCR policy seals\nthe box with nobody in the loop - and it belongs in its own change."],[788,"**Fenced code.** Every fence needs a language, checked by the same contract test, because the\nhighlighter and the copy button both key off it."],[1051,"**First boot:** `https://<host>:3000` - run the install wizard once."],[1387,"**From the phone.** The same file runs inside the Hermes container, whose image has\nno bazelisk, straight off the identical-path checkout mount:"],[236,"**Git repositories (additional)** (`additional_repos`, textarea, mutable, default\nempty)."],[235,"**Git repository** (`repo_url`, input, mutable). The Terraform default is empty\n(`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`); after a push that forwards\n`.env` `REPO_URL` (this stack's own repo in the sample) the form prefills that URL.\nAnything you type is cloned into the workspace on the next start."],[314,"**Git repository** (`repo_url`) is mutable: switching repository is a re-clone plus a\ndevcontainer build, not a new topology the way `workspace`'s DinD toggle is."],[1978,"**Grafana lists the rules because the datasource says to.** The Prometheus datasource is\nregistered `alerting: true` (`observability/grafana/provisioning/datasources/stack.yaml:26`);\nwithout that flag the rules answer only on Prometheus' own `/alerts` page and never\nappear under Alerting -> Rules. Delivery still belongs to Alertmanager."],[1145,"**Grok Build** keeps its own session, keyed off the repository: the helper walks up\nfrom the terminal's folder with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or uses the\nterminal's `$PWD` outside a clone), names the session `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`\nafter that root, and runs `grok --cwd <repo root>` inside it. A terminal opened\nanywhere inside the clone rejoins the one session the repository shares, and starts\nGrok Build at the repository root. That profile is deliberately the old\nalways-reattach shape: watching the same agent run from several tabs is the\nfeature, and the mirrors pay what mirrors pay. What the seed gives them is\n`focus-events on`, so a TUI in the pane can idle its animations while its tab\nis blurred, and `escape-time 10`, which keeps typing responsive on each of\nthem. Two things it does borrow from the attach helper: the session is created\ndetached and attached in a second call, because the tab that brings the server\nup is otherwise the one that can take a running agent down with it (the same\n9-of-20 loss the helper documents), and the stale viewers above are detached on\nthe way in, which is where the 8-clients-on-one-pane count came from."],[239,"**Grok config extras** (`grok_config_extra`, textarea, mutable, default empty).\nWhatever you type is appended verbatim, under a comment header, to that workspace's\nrendered `~/.grok/config.toml`. That file is this workspace's own in either profile\nmode, so the extras reach it alone."],[238,"**Grok default model** (`grok_default_model`, dropdown, immutable, default `agent`)."],[237,"**Grok profile** (`grok_profile_mode`, dropdown, immutable, default `shared`).\n`shared` puts that workspace's notes, memory and sessions on one per-owner volume\neach; `private` mounts no per-owner volume, so all three live on that workspace's\nown home volume and die with it. `config.toml` is that workspace's own file in both\nmodes. Switching needs a rebuild."],[240,"**Grok theme** (`grok_theme`, free-form, mutable, default `grokbuild`). Rendered as\n`[ui] theme` in that workspace's `~/.grok/config.toml`; the literal `auto` renders the\nCLI's `groknight`/`grokday` pair instead of a fixed theme. Each workspace keeps its own."],[634,"**Hermetic sandbox.** `test --spawn_strategy=local,sandboxed` and `build\n--spawn_strategy=sandboxed`: an action sees its declared inputs and its runfiles tree, not\nthe whole checkout. That is why a suite declares its stubs and payload files as `data` and\nwhy a script resolves its repo root with `resolve_checkout` (`scripts/lib/common.sh`) instead of\ntrusting the cwd."],[1192,"**How a session learns its own ceiling.** `[subagents] max_concurrent` is the per-session\nnumber, and `sampling_limit` is left unset because it defaults to that same number - one\nvariable, so the two caps cannot drift apart. The parent reads it once per session and\ndispatches to it:"],[2996,"**How is anything deployed here?** Look, do not assume: `compose*.yml`, `docker-compose*.yml`,\n`kustomization.yaml`, `values.yaml`, `inventories/`, `*.tf`, `argocd/`, `manage.sh`,\n`Makefile`, `MODULE.bazel`, `.github/workflows/`, `templates/*/main.tf`."],[1206,"**How it is dispatched, and what the check prints.** `plan_check.py` is the gate, and it\nis the same file in both skill homes:"],[1219,"**How one is selected.** Four layers name an agent definition, and they are not the same\nknob:"],[567,"**How the token reaches a workspace.** It arrives as `GH_TOKEN` and `GITHUB_TOKEN`\nin the agent environment, and that pair is a snapshot: Coder mints it when the\nworkspace starts and never refreshes it, so it expires about an hour later while\nthe workspace keeps running. The template's `gh_token_refresh` coder_script\nre-mints on start and every 20 minutes (`0 */20 * * * *`) into the **gh auth store**,\nwhich is the copy that stays live. git asks its credential helper, and the\nstack's helper reads the store through a form that clears the stale pair first,\nso push follows the store and not the frozen environment. Check it with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- check <repo-url>` - pass the repository,\nbecause without it the doctor lists credentials and never asks the write\nquestion. `bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix <repo-url>` repairs a\nworkspace that cannot push.\ncoder-templates.md owns\nthe template-side detail."],[1199,"**How the work is cut.** `/ez-micro-plan` is the parent's protocol for splitting one\nmulti-file change across that fleet, and it writes its own document, separate from Grok\nBuild's `/plan`: `/plan` lives at `~/.grok/sessions/<cwd>/<session-id>/plan.md`, is the\nPlan Mode preview, and dies with the session, while this one is repo-local at\n`.grok/plans/<slug>/plan.md` and is what the parent and every child read from. It carries\n`## Goal` with numbered acceptance checks, `## Dispatch` (lane, `slots:`, where the slot\ncount came from, `parent keeps:` for the hotspots the parent merges itself), `## Lanes`,\n`## Tasks`, `## Ledger`, and `## Not doing`. Beside it sit the context briefs and the\nchild reports:"],[1157,"**Into the pane: `Ctrl+Shift+V` or right-click, on a secure context.** Both\narrive bracketed (`ESC[200~ ... ESC[201~`) in a bash terminal and in a tmux\npane alike, and a three-line paste arrives whole without a warning dialog. Both\nchords ask the browser's clipboard API, which the browser only exposes to a\nsecure context, so the URL the tab was opened on decides it. On the TLS public\nname they work; on `http://<LAN-address>` `navigator.clipboard` is absent,\nright-click stops with VS Code's own toast (\"This browser doesn't support the\nclipboard.readText API needed to trigger a paste, try Ctrl+V instead.\"), and\nthe browser's native paste event is what still delivers. A Clipboard site\npermission that was once denied breaks both API chords the same way, with\n`NotAllowedError: Read permission denied`. Neither is a tmux route: the prefix\npaste `]` and the buffer picker `=` are unbound, so the client's own clipboard is\nwhat arrives in the pane."],[1428,"**It enters the running container rather than starting a second one.** Hermes keeps\nits sessions and memory in a SQLite store under `/opt/data`, and two containers on one\ndata directory is the documented way to corrupt it - the WAL sidecars and the lock\nonly mean something inside one container. So while the gateway is up this is\n`docker compose exec`, and the terminal agent and the dashboard agent are literally\nthe same process on the same store. Only when nothing is running does it\n`docker compose run --rm --no-deps` a lone container. `--one-shot` forces the second\ncontainer anyway and says out loud what it risks."],[1454,"**It reports, it does not gate.** Exit 0 on a clean bill (warnings allowed), 1 when a\ncheck failed, 2 on a bad invocation, 5 when it cannot find a checkout.\n`hermes-shell -- check` stays the thing that decides whether to launch a session."],[1420,"**LAN:** `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` - plain HTTP, exactly like LiteLLM on `:4000`.\nThere is no self-signed cert for this port and no redirect to HTTPS. This URL stays\nwhen the public name is on. Tailscale reaches it the same way, on that port."],[560,"**Leave `coder.env` absent for the usual case.** Coder CE injects its own GitHub\nApp (provider id `github`, device flow) and both templates declare that id by\ndefault, so `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` works with no extra file.\nWorkspace owners connect GitHub under Settings -> Tokens / external auth."],[39,"**Let the merge close it.** `Closes #<n>` / `Fixes #<n>` claim the pull request fully satisfies\nthe report, and GitHub closes the issue when it lands on the default branch - a merge a human\nperforms. `Refs #<n>` is the keyword for a pull request that is one step of the work, or lands\nbefore the fix. Nobody closes an issue by hand to announce that a pull request exists."],[636,"**Local disk and repository cache.** `.bazelrc` points `--disk_cache` at\n`~/.cache/bazel-disk` and `--repository_cache` at `~/.cache/bazel-repo`. A contributor\ncontainer and a Coder workspace reuse those directories across runs. In CI the gate\ncontainer is `--rm` and runs with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)`, so a plain run keeps\nneither cache between jobs, and Bazel can name a uid the image passwd file does not\nlist; the cache that survives there is the image's buildx GHA cache. No remote cache is\nconfigured — the same two flags would take a `--remote_cache=<uri>` endpoint later."],[2117,"**Make silences in Alertmanager, not in Grafana.** Alertmanager owns grouping,\nsilences and the repeat interval, so a silence created anywhere else does not stop the\nroute - and the two views then disagree about why nobody was paged."],[1292,"**MCP servers: the default render declares three - `github`, `context7` and the\ngated `searxng` server, which is the search lane itself.** Each is a boolean gate in\nthe template, so one env flag parks exactly one block and a template re-push applies\nit fleet-wide: `GITHUB_MCP=false`, `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`, `SEARCH_MCP=false`.\nThe `searxng` block names the pre-installed `searxng-mcp-server` binary (pinned in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image), is gated on\n`search_mcp` (default true) and dials the service above through `SEARXNG_URL`.\nThat URL is one of six keys the render sets in `[mcp_servers.searxng.env]`; the\nothers pin the bridge's own tuning rather than taking its compiled-in defaults:\n`SEARXNG_TIMEOUT_MS` (20s, above the instance's per-engine budget and equal to the\nprobe's `--timeout`), `SEARXNG_CACHE_TTL_MS` (15 minutes, which switches on the\nbounded 128-entry response cache that ships disabled, so a repeated query stops\nre-dialling backends that already refused this instance), `SEARXNG_MAX_RESULTS`\n(a ceiling that keeps one search inside the MCP output cap - on the pinned 1.0.77\nCLI that cap rides `GROK_MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_BYTES`, because `grok inspect` still answers\n`[mcp] — unrecognized config key` and never applies it),\n`SEARXNG_DEFAULT_SAFESEARCH` (mirrors `search.safe_search` in\n`searxng/settings.yml`) and `SEARXNG_DEFAULT_LANGUAGE` (`all`, so an image bump\ncannot quietly start geo-filtering every agent search).\nThe five offline stdio servers (`filesystem`, `memory`, `sequential-thinking`,\n`git` and `time`) stay pre-installed in the image (`npm -g` for the first\nthree, `uv tool` for the Python two), but the rendered `config.toml` names\nnone of them: the session traces collected on this machine called none of\ntheir tools, and a declared-but-unused server still costs every session its\nhandshake time and tool schemas. Declare one by hand and check a stubborn\nserver with `grok mcp doctor <name>` inside a workspace."],[1967,"**Mimir is written to, not scraped from.** Prometheus `remote_write`s to\n`http://mimir:8080/api/v1/push`. A query against the `prometheus` datasource answers\nfrom the local TSDB; the `mimir` datasource answers through\n`http://mimir:8080/prometheus`. Those two disagreeing is the diagnostic, not a bug."],[163,"**Move the prose out.** Rationale belongs in this file, not in `startup.sh.tftpl`.\nComments in the template are bytes in everybody's argument; here they cost nothing."],[38,"**Name the issue in the branch, close it in one place.** A bug fix branches as `fix/<n>-<slug>`,\nand the closing keyword goes in the pull request body only. GitHub auto-links `#<n>` in a commit\nsubject too, so repeating `Fixes #<n>` down the branch multiplies one link and hides which\ncommit answers the report."],[2415,"**Neither of them exists until you run `--apply`.** A box that has never been enrolled\nruns no exporter, so Prometheus scrapes nothing and the Spark panels stay empty. This\nchange installs nothing on its own: no deploy, no `up`, no `refresh-configs` creates a\ncontainer on a Spark. `--apply` is the only flag that touches a box, and it says so in\nits own header line before the first one\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:327`)."],[568,"**Never a static PAT in `.env`.** `.env.sample` used to carry a `GITHUB_TOKEN=`\nline holding a long-lived fine-grained PAT, which `push-template.sh` forwarded to\nboth templates as a template variable, into every workspace's environment. That\npath is gone: there is no such template variable and no such key in the twin. A\nworkspace's GitHub credential comes from the External Auth provider alone, so an\nowner who connected nothing has no GitHub credential anywhere - and the only way\nto add one by hand is per workspace, after `check` has answered `REFUSED-GRANT`,\n`NOT VISIBLE` or `REJECTED` (a passing read is not that answer):"],[2065,"**No destination is configured, so nothing pages anyone as shipped.** The rules, the\nroutes and the receivers all exist; only the URL is missing. `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL`\nis empty in `.env.example`, so on a box nobody edited every alert is visible - on\nPrometheus' own alerts page and under Grafana's Alerting pages - and is delivered\nnowhere. Who gets woken is an operator decision, made in the env file."],[2260,"**No dynamic secrets, no cross-project sharing.** Both are paid features of Infisical\nand neither is used: no `LICENSE_KEY` is read, generated, or referenced. The model is\nstatic shared secrets exported as dotenv."],[2066,"**No healthcheck or dashboard card for the four new services.** None of\n`node_exporter`, `cadvisor`, `blackbox_exporter` or `alertmanager` declares a\n`healthcheck`, and the dashboard's **Observability** group still carries five cards\n(dashboard.md), so their liveness is the `status` row plus the container\nlog."],[2262,"**No multi-line value reaches a workspace.** The boot fetch publishes single-line values only\nand drops the rest with one note; see\nThe workspace boot fetch."],[2067,"**No object storage.** Loki, Mimir and Tempo keep blocks on named volumes on this\nbox's disk. The follow-up is a bucket and the storage config in each of those three\nfiles; nothing about the read path changes when it lands."],[1171,"**No published source names an upstream `/learn`, and the bundled skill set never\nlands here**: the `xai-org/grok-build` built-in command table, all 240 pages of\ndocs.x.ai, every first-party changelog under `x.ai/cli/changelogs/`, and the public\n`xai-org/plugin-marketplace` catalog carry zero occurrences of it. The bundled\nplatform skills are a separate set, enumerated by\n`FORMER_PLATFORM_SKILL_HASHES` in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/builtin.rs`\n(help, imagine, create-skill, check-work, check, xlsx, pptx, docx, code-review,\nbest-of-n, create-workflow), and neither `learn` nor `skillify` is among them. That\nset is downloaded at runtime through the CLI's proxy endpoints config\n(`cli-chat-proxy.grok.com`, gated by `GROK_SKILLS_BASE_URL`) and needs an x.ai\nsign-in: the upstream `product_skills*` fetch in\n`crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/slash_commands.rs` is gated on\n`xai_grok_login::GrokAuth`. This stack has no x.ai credential:\nLiteLLM is the sole model transport, with `env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"` and\n`base_url` `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`, so `~/.grok/bundled/` is never\ncreated and there is no bundled copy for `grok inspect` to label. The one surface\nnobody can read anonymously is the authenticated skills feed (`x.ai/skills/*` answers\n403), so the honest claim is that no published source names `/learn`, not that it\ncannot exist behind that feed. Nothing writes `~/.grok/skills/` either, but\nthat is a stack choice, not a CLI limit: the upstream user guide\n(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/docs/user-guide/08-skills.md`) documents both tiers -\n`~/.grok/skills/` is the User scope and `<repo_root>/.grok/skills/` the Repo one, and\n`grok inspect` labels each definition by its actual source (`user`, `project`,\n`bundled`) - and startup discovery skips project skills and commands in untrusted\nfolders, so a project-tier folder has to be trusted first or its skill is withheld.\nWhat separates the two consumers is that the **Coder Agents** chat agent reads\n`~/.coder/skills/` only, so reaching both means shipping both homes."],[2263,"**No scrape, no alert.** The three containers are log-only, like `ollama`, `kasm` and\nthe rest. `bazelisk run //scripts:observability-check` does not walk them; their state\nis `dev-stack -- status` and their logs are in Loki under their `container=` tag."],[2264,"**No second inventory.** Twelve keys, one file. A key added to compose without joining\n`infisical/managed-secrets.txt` stays outside the vault silently, which is why `check`\nasserts the count rather than trusting the file."],[2068,"**No trace client.** Tempo has an OTLP door and a datasource, and no caller. Pointing\nthe first service at `tempo:4317` is a change in that service, not in this bundle."],[2261,"**No vault dependency in the deploy path.** Compose reads `.env`, and the one call the\nrunners make before it is best-effort: warn, continue, bring the stack up on the values the\nfile already held. The follow-up that would change that is a compose-secrets rewrite of every\nservice block, and it would make every bring-up wait on a server nothing here waits on today."],[754,"**Not a credential store.** GitHub auth stays Coder External Auth; no static token and no\nPAT appears in any file this page describes."],[2608,"**Not a fast lane.** The hop is armed by the alias alone, so a session on `agent`,\n`agent-xhigh` or `agent-swarm` compacts on Spark by design. The groups allowed to hop are\n`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_LANES`, default `agent-xhigh-fast,agent-swarm-fast`."],[753,"**Not a place for contributor-only behavior in the image.** The image is shared with Coder\nworkspaces and CI, so anything that only a person at a keyboard wants (the Bazel volume\nseam, the warm-up, the git hooks, the socket ACL) lives in\n`.devcontainer/library-scripts/configure.sh`, which only this container runs. An `ENV` or\n`RUN` line added to `images/dev/Dockerfile` for the same purpose changes what a workspace\ngets, which is the mistake this split exists to prevent."],[752,"**Not a second toolchain.** Nothing in the contributor path installs pins onto a laptop\nor a runner — that is what the image is for."],[751,"**Not a template change.** `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/startup.sh.tftpl` are untouched by any of this; the contributor\nseed reads them as payloads, it does not rewrite the twins."],[750,"**Not the stack host.** The compose host (gmktec) is bootstrapped with\n`bash scripts/setup-host.sh`, and it cannot be this container: it owns the daemon that\nruns Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and Hermes. See\n../project-conventions.md for the ingress rule that keeps\nthat script the single documented `bash scripts/…`."],[2610,"**Not the summary call.** The CLI appends its instruction as the last turn, and the hook\nreads it there: one of the two asks the CLI sends, from a `user` or `system` role, in a\nturn no longer than the instruction itself. It does not read the system prompt, which\nrides every turn of the session, so a rulebook that quotes the sentence cannot move an\nordinary turn. A request that merely mentions one of the two asks is not a compaction\ncall and stays on its lane."],[1280,"**Nothing reads it back.** `~/.grok/sessions` stays the only session state the CLI,\nthe rulebook, and Coder's session management know about; no config key and no startup\nlogic points at the archive, and the hooks deny list\n(`templates/common/hooks.deny-patterns.txt`) refuses any agent command that names the\npath, the volume, or a globbed form of either. The point is a copy an operator can\ninspect later, from the host, after the workspace that made it is gone."],[3012,"**One env var, read at one seam**, documented in the dotenv sample with its off default. This\ncarries most needs: `FEATURE_AGENT_TUNNEL_V2=0`."],[1427,"**Options** (before the subcommand): `-p/--project-name` (`evo-t1-prod`; the dev\nsandbox is `evo-t1-dev`), `-f/--file`, `-e/--env-file`, `-m/--model <alias>` for one\nsession, `-w/--writable <dir>`, `--one-shot`, `--dry-run`, `-h/--help`."],[1152,"**Out of a pane: drag, and it is already on the clipboard.** tmux asks the outer\nterminal for mouse reporting only while `mouse` is on: measured on tmux 3.4, an\nattach with `mouse on` writes `ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the\nclient, and an attach with it off writes none of them. With the seed's\n`mouse off` tmux never sees a button, so a plain drag is the client's own\nhighlight and the seed's `terminal.integrated.copyOnSelection: true` puts that\nhighlight on the machine clipboard with no chord (the harness recorded\n`navigator.clipboard.writeText` firing on a client selection, before this switch)."],[983,"**Prepend; never replace PATH.** `use_stubs` and `with_path` put the stub directory *in\nfront of* the host PATH. `PATH=\"${BIN}\"` takes `bash`, `grep`, `cut`, `sort` and `wc` away\nfrom the script under test as well, and a script that pipes its answer through\n`grep | wc | tr` then fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the contract the\nsuite means to check."],[2905,"**Present and `down`**: the app is not answering on the name Prometheus dials. The\nscrapes are compose-DNS (`coder:2112`, `litellm:4000`), not the host mappings - a\n`curl 127.0.0.1:2112` that works from the host proves only that the port mapping is\nfine."],[2442,"**Profiling counters return nothing without `SYS_ADMIN`.** Every `DCGM_FI_PROF_*` field\nneeds the exporter started with `--cap-add SYS_ADMIN` (plus\n`NVreg_RestrictProfilingToAdminUsers=0` on the box), and this CLI does not pass that cap\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:239`). A box answers those fields with no rows at all rather\nthan an error - `DCGM_FI_PROF_*` being absent from a scrape is the expected result, not\na broken exporter (`observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv:98-99`)."],[1965,"**Prometheus is the only scraper.** Its scrape config targets the apps that publish\n`/metrics` - `coder:2112` and `litellm:4000` - plus the six components that publish\ntheir own (`prometheus:9090`, `mimir:8080`, `loki:3100`, `tempo:3200`,\n`grafana:3000`, `alloy:12345`), the stack host through `node_exporter:9101` and\n`cadvisor:8080`, Alertmanager itself at `alertmanager:9093`, and the Spark jobs that\nreach `blackbox_exporter:9115` as their prober. Fifteen jobs in all. The LiteLLM\nscrape carries a bearer token, so the metrics stay behind `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` the way\nevery other LiteLLM endpoint is. Measured on the pinned v3.16.0: `$VAR` and `${VAR}`\nunder `global.external_labels` come back filled in at `/api/v1/status/config`, where\n`$(VAR)` stays literal, and a scrape target address and `authorization.credentials`\nexpand nothing at all - `$(VAR)`, `${VAR}` and `$VAR` in the latter each reached a\nlistener that logs its request headers verbatim - so the key cannot travel that way,\nand it arrives as a file instead. Compose renders `configs.litellm_metrics_token` into\n`/etc/prometheus-secrets/litellm_token` in the container (`docker-compose.yml:839-841`,\n`:1452`), and the `litellm` job presents that file through\n`authorization.credentials_file` (`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:126-129`).\nThe config's `content:` interpolates the very same\n`${LITELLM_MASTER_KEY:-change-me-litellm}` the proxy boots with\n(`docker-compose.yml:1459`), so with no dotenv both ends land on the shipped\nplaceholder and the scrape works, and the fault an operator actually meets is the two\nends disagreeing: rotate the key without recreating `prometheus` and the file still\nholds the old one."],[1421,"**Public name, opt-in:** `hermes-<suffix>.<base>`, so\n`https://hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` under the shipped defaults, when\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` composes it or an explicit list names it. Bootstrap\nclassifies `hermes-*` and `hermes.*` and writes `STACK_HERMES_URL`. The path is the\nsame as the other public names: Cloudflare\n(orange cloud, WebSockets on by default), Traefik on the other LAN server (TLS\n`*.overeazy.io`, Authelia), the `router` on this box `:8080`, then\n`http://hermes:9119`, with the original Host preserved. Traefik's backend is\n`:8080`, not `:9119`. See public-access.md."],[32,"**Push once, when the pull request opens**, and pre-flight it: the log above reads as one commit\nper unit with imperative prefixed subjects, `git diff --stat main...HEAD` matches what was meant,\nand no `.env`, token, key, or workspace-local state is in any commit."],[1441,"**Push** is what makes a phone viable, because pull means opening a dashboard every\ntime. It rides upstream's ntfy adapter and is configured in the assistant's own\n`/opt/data/.env`, not in compose:"],[597,"**Read the guard beside the client threshold: that pairing picks the tier.** Both fast\naliases declare a `context_window` of 262144 and the shipped `[session]` block sets\n`auto_compact_threshold_percent` to 70, so a session on either one reaches its\nauto-compaction at about 183,500 tokens - over the guard, so the Arc was never going to read\nit and the call goes to the Spark tier instead. `agent-low` is what makes that work cheaper\nthan its parent: the 262144-token window stays and the baked `xhigh` thinking goes, which is\nthe cost the hop exists to stop paying. That is a routing choice with its own trade, not a\nfree win - the summary still runs on a Spark box, at a thinking level turned down. Neither\ntier has a timing of its own yet: the hop has not served a summary on a running stack, so\nthe only numbers attached to this feature are the 107 s to 1915 s and 18-of-36 figures for\nthe Spark lane before it existed, both swept out of the session logs in\ntroubleshooting.md. `bazelisk test //tests:bats_litellm_config_test`\npins the config and the compose passthrough, and `docs/troubleshooting.md` names the one log\nline that shows the hop firing."],[35,"**Read the report before you plan.** `gh issue view <n> --comments` is the requirements and the\nreproduction in one place, and it is the wording the change is measured against. An agent reads\nit through `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN gh ...` for the reason in\ntroubleshooting.md."],[1837,"**Reboot anything.** The reboot is the operator's decision, made after a\ngreen `check`."],[500,"**Refresh, and reconnect.** `coder external-auth access-token` is the live path when the\nstore goes stale: it re-mints on demand and answers with a live token, or with the\nre-authentication URL and exit 1; with no connected account the refresh script prints its\none note (pointing at **GitHub reauth**, and at **Install GitHub App** when the injected\nApp is in use) and exits 0, and mints nothing. After a reconnect, restart the workspace (or\nwait for the next `gh_token_refresh`) so the injected pair and the store match the new\ngrant. The refresh script unsets `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` before\n`gh auth login --with-token`, because gh refuses stdin while those names are set."],[1153,"**Right-click and middle-click paste that clipboard.** The seed sets\n`terminal.integrated.rightClickBehavior: \"copyPaste\"` - copy while there is a\nselection, paste when there is none - and pins\n`terminal.integrated.middleClickBehavior: \"paste\"`, because the config registry in\nthe shipped 4.145.0 gives that setting the default `\"default\"`, which pastes only\non the Linux build. tmux has no middle-click paste of its own to answer first:\n`MouseDown2Pane` is unbound in the root table."],[1457,"**Run it before a `--force` re-seed and after a merge.** The drift check names the\nfile that differs and prints the `hermes-bootstrap --data <mount> --force` line that\nwould overwrite it, which is the pair an operator wants side by side."],[36,"**Search before you file.** `gh issue list --state open --search '<keywords> in:title,body'`,\nthen the same with `--state closed`. A duplicate becomes a link to the original, something\n`main` already fixed becomes a comment naming the commit, and only a genuinely new report\nbecomes `gh issue create` - with the reproduction and the verbatim output in its body."],[2444,"**ssh will not reach a box.** In `--status` this is a `warning:` naming the ssh error, and\nthe exit status stays 0 (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:566-576`): a box that is off, or one\nthis shell has no credentials for, is a not-yet state. Under `--apply` or `--restart` the\nsame box is a `fail_item` and the run exits non-zero, because a fleet where two of three\nboxes were enrolled is precisely the drift this CLI exists to remove. Check\n`SPARK_SSH_USER` (default: your login name, `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:101`) and `--port`,\nand run it from the host shell - a workspace cannot resolve the LAN names. If the box\ndrops packets rather than refusing, you see `ssh was cut off after <n>s (timeout(1)\nguard)`; on a host without `timeout(1)` the CLI warns up front that the round trips run\nuncapped."],[30,"**Stage by path.** `git add <path> <path>`, and read the index back with\n`git diff --cached --name-only`. `git add -A`, `git add .`, and `git commit -a` are how a scratch\nfile, a build artifact, or someone else's edit walks into a commit - and the secret hygiene rule\nabove depends on the agent being able to say what is in each one."],[164,"**Stage the payload as a file, not an argument.** The obvious resource does not\nexist: the OSS provider's `ResourcesMap` (`provider/provider.go`) registers\n`coder_agent`, `coder_agent_instance`, `coder_ai_task`, `coder_app`,\n`coder_metadata`, `coder_script`, `coder_env`, `coder_devcontainer` and\n`coder_external_agent`, and nothing else. `coder_file` was requested in\n`coder/coder#3055` and closed `not_planned` on 2022-09-23; a maintainer was still\nasking for it in 2025. Do not plan around it."],[358,"**Starts the out-of-band session archive mirror** (boot stage 9). It chowns\n`/var/lib/grok-session-archive` to the home owner, folds the container hostname into a\nsafe leaf name under `workspaces/`, then leaves a backgrounded loop that `rsync`s\n`~/.grok/sessions/` into that leaf every 180 seconds, without `--delete`, for the life\nof the container. `flock -n` keeps it to one loop per leaf across repeated starts.\nBest-effort and invisible to the CLI: nothing in the workspace reads the volume back,\nand the hooks deny list refuses an agent command that names it. See\nthe out-of-band session archive."],[1426,"**Subcommands:** `chat` (the default), `ask TEXT` for one question and exit, `shell`\nfor a shell with the container's mounts and socket, `check` for the readiness report,\n`commands` for the assistant's own help. Every word after the subcommand is handed to\nthe assistant verbatim, so a flag this script has never heard of still works."],[786,"**Tables.** A content table gets its head pinned under the navbar while the table is on\nscreen, and a mirrored scroll bar at the bottom of the viewport when its columns are wider\nthan the page. Both come from `docs-site/components/table-chrome.tsx`; a table that already\ncarries a class is left as authored."],[1439,"**The API server** is the headless door: an iOS Shortcut or a Tasker profile can\nPOST a prompt and read the answer with no browser and no dashboard login."],[1966,"**The apps with no `/metrics` are observed by logs alone, on purpose.** `ollama`,\n`searxng`, `kasm`, `homepage`, the two nginx hops, `hermes`, and the three Infisical\ncontainers (`infisical`, `infisical-db`, `infisical-redis`) publish no Prometheus\nendpoint, so scraping them would leave a target answering 404 forever and a tally no\none trusts. Ollama is the one operators ask about: upstream has had the request for a\n`/metrics` endpoint open since 2024 (ollama/ollama#3144), and the IPEX-LLM image runs a\nstock `ollama serve`, so there is nothing to scrape. Its traffic is measured anyway,\nbecause every request reaches it through LiteLLM, which does publish. What every one of\nthose containers does contribute is its log stream, which is where a SearXNG rate limit\nor a Kasm start failure lands."],[1299,"**The binary is missing from the golden image.** A warning: the running\n`evo-t1-dev` predates the npm bake. Remedy: rebuild the image and recreate the\nworkspace, or run the `npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command the check prints\nto install the pinned package into the running box (pins in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`)."],[1298,"**The block is missing from the rendered config.** That is a failure, not a\nwarning: the default render declares the lane, so something took it away - a push\npassed its gate `false`, the workspace runs an older template, or the file lost\nits stack marker and nothing re-renders it. Remedy: clear the gate and re-push,\nthen re-materialize the file with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>` from\nthe stack host, or `bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --home <dir>` for a\nhost home."],[1300,"**The credential is missing.** Also a warning, and per lane it means something\ndifferent. `github` with `$GH_TOKEN` empty is a real gap: connect GitHub External\nAuth (the **GitHub reauth** app) and start a new session. An empty\n`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is not a gap - see the key's paragraph above, the lane answers\nkeyless at the public rate limit."],[2440,"**The DCGM tag has never been pulled.** `nvcr.io` answers 404 to an anonymous token, so\nthe pin `nvcr.io/nvidia/k8s/dcgm-exporter:4.4.2-4.7.0-ubuntu22.04` was not verifiable\nupstream the way the other three were. The first `--apply` against a box is what proves\nit, and a wrong tag surfaces there as the registry's own error: `--apply` prints\ndocker's reason rather than swallowing it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:675`,\n`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:685`), so you read what the registry said. Correct the tag in\n`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE` and re-run - it is a key, not a code change."],[533,"**The env files.** `docker-compose.yml` builds its variable references in two\nsteps: `${VAR}` inside `expose` expands during the shell phase, so compose\nnever sees those variables and a changed value needs a rebuild, and an unset\nvariable collapses to `127.0.0.1:57620:57620/tcp`, which fails to bind. So the\nhost ports and addresses live in a key=value env file - `.env` for the stable\nstack, `dev-stack.env` for the dev one - and each has a checked-in twin beside\nit that carries the keys and explains them in the comment lines above. A pull\nthat renames, adds or retires a key silently changes what the stack runs on."],[591,"**The fast-compaction hop has five keys of its own.** On `agent-xhigh-fast` and\n`agent-swarm-fast` only, the proxy callback `litellm/compaction_router.py`\nrecognises the CLI's conversation-summary call and re-serves it from somewhere\ncheaper than the Spark box it arrived on, in one of two tiers: the Arc group\n`compaction-fast` for a summary small enough for that window, or the Spark group\n`agent-low` for one too big for it. A summary needs no extra-high thinking, which is what\nthe Spark lane charges for it. The Grok Build CLI has no compaction-model\nsetting, so nothing in a client config points that call anywhere and the hop can\nonly live in the proxy - with the flag off, both lanes behave exactly like their\nnon-fast parents. All five are read in that one module and set in the compose\nlitellm service, the three that read a blank as unset as `${KEY:-default}` and the\ntwo tier targets as `${KEY-default}` so a blanked target stays blank, which is how\na tier is parked. A dotenv line overrides each one.\nlitellm-routing.md owns the mechanism."],[1453,"**The fourteen checks:** `approvals` (manual, with cron and unattended both deny),\n`terminal-cwd`, `write-root`, `web-lane`, `keyless-egress`, `mcp-servers`,\n`seed-drift`, `mount-identity`, `image-pin`, `model-key`, `dashboard`, `memories`,\n`anywhere-door` (the API server's enabled/keyed/bound state and whether the push\nchannel can be answered by strangers), and `agent-verbs` (whether the assistant can\nstill reach `scripts/coder-sessions.sh` and the socket those verbs read). The last\ntwo are the ones that keep the phone story honest: an open door nobody configured,\nand a closed door nobody can find, are the same class of surprise."],[1976,"**The gap alerts select on the job name.** `HostNodeExporterGap` and `HostCadvisorGap`\nfire when `job=\"node_exporter\"` or `job=\"cadvisor\"` carries no series, which is why the\njob name is the Compose service name (`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:142` and\n`:151`). Renaming either service without renaming its job makes two alerts permanently\nquiet, and no test will tell you."],[2609,"**The kill switch.** `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION` ships on, and `0`, `false`, `off` or `no`\nstops the hop on every lane at once. A value that is none of those and none of `1`,\n`true`, `on`, `yes` also leaves it off, with one\n`compaction router: EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION=... is neither on nor off` warning per\ndistinct value: a typo'd off is read as off. Compose gives each of the five keys its\ndefault in `docker-compose.yml`, and the value in force is the one interpolated from the\nenv file when the container was created, so a stack that was already up when someone set\nthe key is still running the old one. Clear the key (or set it back to `1`) and recreate:\na `restart` keeps the old environment."],[3015,"**The platform's parameters** (Terraform variables, Helm values, Ansible vars, Coder template\nvariables) when the flag selects infrastructure."],[2173,"**The residual, honestly.** That list names what a shell or a loader *executes*. It is not a list\nof every environment variable that changes what a program does, and it cannot be one. Anyone with\nwrite access to the Infisical project still chooses which ordinary variables a workspace boots\nwith, and because the fetch's line is appended to `~/.bashrc` and runs under `set -a`, one of\nthose names can overwrite whatever that file set earlier in it. Treat write access\nto the vault project as the trust boundary it is: it is shell access to every workspace that boots\nwith the fetch on. Read access is a much smaller grant than write, and the machine identity the\nworkspaces authenticate as should hold only the former."],[1196,"**The review pass is a panel.** The independent check that closes a fleet run is a wave of\nskeptics dispatched in one turn, each riding `agent-review` through the `grok_review_lane`\npin above, each owning no file, and each re-running the producers' own `done:` commands\nrather than reading their reports. The default panel is three angles: requirements, which\nanswers every acceptance check kept or broken; proof, which runs the `done:` commands and\nquotes what they printed; and adversary, which looks for the regression, the edge state and\nthe flag-off path. Each may fan out up to three read-only mappers of its own under the\ndepth above. `templates/common/ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py:227` accepts a `review:` key\nnaming the whole panel, comma or space separated, and holds each reviewer to owning nothing\nwhile needing every task that ships a file."],[2172,"**The security boundary**, stated so no operator has to guess at it: because `agent.env` is\nsourced by every shell a workspace starts, a vault key that the shell, the dynamic loader, an\ninterpreter or git would treat as code is not a secret, it is a command line chosen by whoever can\nwrite a secret in the project. The two defences are the deny list above, matched on the\nnormalised case, and the pre-publish pass that refuses to write the file at all and leaves the\nprevious `agent.env` in place."],[1977,"**The Spark tier is quiet until the boxes are enrolled.** Those two jobs read their\ntargets from `observability/spark-targets/sparks.json` through `file_sd_configs`, a file\nthe stack host generates and `.gitignore:76` keeps out of git. No file means no target\ngroups, which reads as zero Sparks rather than as a broken probe. The boxes' own\nnode_exporter and DCGM exporter are installed on the boxes by `scripts/spark-monitor.sh`,\nnot by this Compose file."],[1173,"**The stack ships its own three repository-conversion commands as `/ez-bazel-first`,\n`/ez-strong-types` and `/ez-trunk-based-repo`**, and these stand in for nothing upstream:\nthey convert whatever repository the workspace happens to hold, which is why they carry no\npath that only exists in this stack. They are seeded the same way, by\n`coder_script.stack_repo_skills`, from `templates/common/ez-bazel-first.SKILL.md`,\n`ez-strong-types.SKILL.md` and `ez-trunk-based-repo.SKILL.md`, plus the three python\nhelpers they shell out to. Same absent-only contract, same marker-based takeover rule,\nsame `[skills] disabled` escape hatch."],[1172,"**The stack ships its own three skill commands as `/ez-learn`, `/ez-skillify` and\n`/ez-create-skill`**, so they are stack payloads like the delegation skill, not something\nto install, and the `ez-` prefix keeps them clear of the upstream names they sit\nbeside: `/create-skill` is one of the CLI's bundled platform skills and\n`/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember`, `/memory` are its built-in memory commands, while no\npublished source names a `/learn` or a `/skillify` at all. A user-scope skill overrides\na same-named bundled one, so a seed named `create-skill` would have hidden the bundled\ncopy.\n`/ez-learn` writes memory files - it reproduces the upstream `/flush`, `/dream`,\n`/remember` and `/memory`; the other two write skill files.\nThe three payloads - `templates/common/ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md` - are seeded at workspace start by\n`coder_script.stack_skills`: each is written into `~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and\ntwin-copied to `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, each only while that file is absent.\nTo take a\ncopy over, edit it and delete the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker line: a\nmarker-free file is user-owned, and the seeder leaves it alone unless you pass\n`--force`. A deletion is undone - the boot writes the file again whenever it is\nabsent, and `refresh-configs` re-seeds an absent target, so the next boot restores\nthe stack's copy. To hide the slash command, set `disabled = [\"ez-learn\"]` under\n`[skills]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`. A seeded `SKILL.md` shows up in\n`grok inspect --json` as `userInvocable: true`, and a live session advertises it in\nboth `slash_commands` and `skills`, so it works on whichever CLI the image baked at\n`/usr/local/bin/grok` and this needs no image rebuild; the **Grok Build** terminal\nprofile gets it because that profile execs the same binary."],[1386,"**The state words** come from `state_word` in the script and are what the\n`coding-agents` Hermes skill teaches: `running` (output within 30 s), `quiet` (an\nagent loaded and silent for 30 s - finished, or parked on a question, which is what\n\"it's stuck\" usually is), `waiting` (a client attached at a plain shell - a person\nis already in that pane), `idle-shell`, `detached-shell`, and `unknown`. A state\nword is a pointer at the pane, never a verdict: `tail` before you say done."],[2612,"**The tier names a group nobody declared.** Neither target is validated by LiteLLM, so the\nhook checks both against the `model_name:` entries in `litellm/config.yaml` and refuses a\nname that is not among them, with one `compaction router: target ... is no model group in\nconfig.yaml` warning per call. A typo there therefore looks like a hop that never fires\nrather than a summary that hard-fails. `grep -E '^  - model_name: ' litellm/config.yaml` is\nthe list of legal answers for both keys."],[534,"**The workspace config.** `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.tmux.conf` and the `~/.coder`\nand `~/.agents` seeds are materialised inside each workspace when it first boots\nand are never revisited after that. `~/.grok/config.toml` and the code-server\n`~/.local/share/code-server/User/settings.json` are the two rendered files: each\nis that workspace's own, written from the template on every start, so a temporary\nhand-edit there survives until that workspace restarts and reaches no other\nworkspace, while a re-push plus a restart re-renders it from the latest template.\nThe shared overlay `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` is a rendered file too, but\nthe boot writes it only while absent, so the API keys an operator put there\nsurvive every restart. A re-pushed template only reaches workspaces\nthat have been **start**ed or **restart**ed since the push - **stop** and\n**start** is what re-runs the provisioning script and therefore re-runs\n`before_config`."],[1455,"**Three findings it exists to catch**, each one measured on a live box:\n`terminal.cwd` still carrying the `${STACK_HOST_PATH}` token (Hermes validates that\nvalue with `expanduser()` alone, so the token reads as a missing directory while\n`hermes config get` prints it looking healthy), `STACK_HOST_PATH` naming a directory\nwith no checkout (compose mounts an empty directory over the repo and every\ncontainer-level check still reads healthy), and a `web` block that stopped pinning\n`searxng` or flipped a keyless switch back on, which routes a search to a vendor's\nfree tier while the config still looks local."],[2374,"**Timeouts.** `proxy/router.conf` on this box already holds per-service read\ntimeouts - dashboard 60s, coder, hermes, grafana and infisical 300s, LiteLLM 600s,\nKasm 3600s - so Traefik must not be the shorter link. One transport for all seven at\n`3600s` is the simple choice because Kasm needs that floor anyway. To cap each tier\nseparately, declare one `loadBalancer` service per tier, each pointing at the same\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with `passHostHeader: true` and its own transport\n(`evo-t1-coder` / `evo-t1-hermes` / `evo-t1-grafana` / `evo-t1-infisical` at `300s`,\n`evo-t1-litellm` at\n`600s`, `evo-t1-kasm` at `3600s`), and point each router at its own service. If a\nsession dies at about a minute with the page still loaded, check the client-facing side\ntoo: an entryPoint's `transport.respondingTimeouts.readTimeout` (default 60s) and\n`idleTimeout` (default 180s) apply to the browser-to-Traefik hop."],[1154,"**tmux keeps no buffer, so there is nothing to paste out of one.** `Prefix-[`\nstill enters vi copy-mode and it is a viewer: the cursor keys, `PPage`/`NPage`,\n`Ctrl-u`/`Ctrl-d` and `k`/`j` walk history, `q` leaves, and `Escape` clears a\nselection. Every copy that used to end a selection is unbound - Enter, `C-j`, `D`,\n`A`, the word and line copies a double- or triple-click fires, the copy a drag\nrelease ran, and the `y` the earlier seed bound itself - along with the prefix\npaste `]` and the buffer picker `=`. Selecting inside copy-mode with `Space`, `V`\nor the cursor keys stays bound and is inert, with no copy command left for it to\nend on. Proof: sourcing the payload on a scratch server leaves `list-keys` with no\ncopy command in `copy-mode-vi`, no `paste-buffer` or `choose-buffer` in `prefix`,\nand no `MouseDown2Pane` in `root`. The pointer's other jobs keep their prefix\nkeys: `Prefix-o` or `Prefix-q` to select a pane, and `Prefix-Alt-arrow` to resize\n(plain `Prefix-arrow` selects a pane in the shipped 3.4's own default table)."],[1162,"**tmux options.** `templates/common/tmux.conf` seeds the four options Doctor's\nown fixes write, `set -g set-clipboard on`, `set -g allow-passthrough on`,\n`set -g extended-keys on` and the `*:RGB` `terminal-features` entry, plus the\n`xterm*:extkeys` entry Doctor leaves to the operator. Those last two decide\nwhat survives the multiplexer: tmux rewrites 24-bit color down to the palette\nan attached client advertises, which is what makes a theme look washed out in\nthe browser, and extkeys is what makes Shift+Enter and Ctrl+. arrive distinct\nfrom Enter. A client picks up `terminal-features` when it attaches, so after a\nreseed run `tmux source-file ~/.tmux.conf` and then detach and reattach\n(a `refresh-client` keeps the client on its old features). Proof:\n`tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'` lists `RGB` and `extkeys`.\n`tests/bats/tmux-fidelity.bats` boots a scratch server on the seed and reads\nall of it back."],[2611,"**Too big for the Arc, and the Spark tier was blanked.** The estimate covers the whole\nrequest, the tool schemas included, and charges a base64 payload and a non-ASCII reply at\ntheir own denser rates rather than the prose rate; anything above\n`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` (default 24000) goes to\n`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` rather than to the Arc: the\nArc weights answer inside a 32k window and Ollama would clamp such an input rather than\nfail it. Raising that key does not open the Arc tier: the fork takes the smaller of the\nkey and the `max_input_tokens` the target group declares in `litellm/config.yaml`, so\n`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT=100000` still forks at 32768 and logs one\n`compaction router: EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT=100000 is above the 32768 tokens\nconfig.yaml declares for 'compaction-fast'; forking at the declared window instead`.\nA target group that declares no `max_input_tokens` at all leaves the key whole, and says\nso once with `compaction router: 'compaction-fast' declares no max_input_tokens in\nconfig.yaml; forking at EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT alone` - that line is the\nsignal to go declare the window, because it is the one state where the fork can still\nhand an oversized summary to a model that reads it clamped.\nA shipped auto-compaction arrives near 183,500 tokens, so it is the Spark tier\nthat answers it. Blank that key, or name the lane the call arrived on, and an oversized\nsummary stays on its Spark lane at the lane's own thinking level - the pre-hop behaviour,\nand the one case where the 107 s to 1915 s above is the documented limit of the feature.\nA summary that comes back short of the history it should cover has one remaining path\nthat explains it: with all three Sparks cooled, the fallback chains end at `coder` -\n`agent-low: [\"coder\"]` for the oversized tier, and the same for the pair's own chains -\nand that Arc group answers from an input Ollama clamps down to its 32k window. That is every Spark\nlane's standing last resort rather than a defect of the hop, and\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs --tail 200 litellm` carries the health lines for\nthat window - the `fallbacks:` block of `litellm/config.yaml` is the map it followed.\nBlanking works only because compose passes that key as `${VAR-default}`: the colon form\nsubstitutes its default for an empty value too, and the parked tier comes back."],[1969,"**Traces arrive over OTLP only, and one client already speaks it.** Anything that wants\ntraces in this stack sends OTLP gRPC to `tempo:4317`. Grafana is the one shipped\ncaller: `GF_TRACING_ENABLED` points its own exporter at `tempo:4317`, so the trace\nviewer has the product's own spans before any workspace emits one. Tempo's\nmetrics_generator then computes span-metrics and service-graph series and\n`remote_write`s them back into Prometheus, which is why Prometheus carries\n`--web.enable-remote-write-receiver`: without that flag the write is refused with a 404\nand the traces dashboard has nothing to plot."],[326,"**Treat the repository as untrusted code.** Everything it builds runs on a privileged\ndaemon on this box, so a Dockerfile in that repo is root-equivalent on the stack host.\nThe create form's own description says so, and only repositories you trust belong in\nthe field."],[41,"**Triage belongs to a person.** Closing, reopening, reassigning, labelling and milestoning are\nthe maintainer's calls, and an agent makes them only when the operator asked for that in words."],[1385,"**Two details that make the answer correct.** Each workspace creates a\n`coder-<id>-dind-sidecar` carrying the same labels; it runs a Docker daemon and no\nshell, so it is dropped by name. And a tmux server belongs to one uid and listens on\n`/tmp/tmux-<uid>/default`, so the sockets are enumerated per uid - `docker exec`\nlands as the image's user, and asking *that* user for its default server reports\n\"no sessions\" for an agent the operator can see on screen."],[1194,"**Two numbers, one rule.** The same grep opens its four lines of context with\n`# fleet-width: 15` - the fleet's own ceiling, rendered into the same table from\n`var.grok_swarm_width` (`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:427`, fed at\n`templates/workspace/main.tf:558`) so an explicit width never erases it - and the pair is\nwhat the width gets read against. Equal numbers mean the session is sized for the fleet:\nevery slot it holds is a stream a box can actually serve. A width below its fleet-width\nmeans a persisted value - on the template, from a raw `coder templates push --var`, or on\nthe workspace, from `coder start --parameter` / `coder update --parameter` or the Variables\ntab (`--var` is a push-only flag; `coder start` has none) - unless an operator set it there\non purpose to sit inside one pinned box. `bazelisk run //scripts:subagent-check`\nreads both and names which of the two you have; the same comparison runs at every start, in\nstage 6 of `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl`, where a narrower width is one `note:`\nline on the startup log. Neither value can be cleared from inside the workspace, because\nboth sit in the Coder database on the host."],[1159,"**Two scroll-back stores, two different budgets.** `terminal.integrated.scrollback`\nin the settings seed is the xterm.js buffer, which is what a plain `bash`\nprofile scrolls through; tmux's `history-limit` is what `Prefix-[` walks\ninside a pane. They read 2000 and 100000 lines here, and the two numbers\ndiffer on purpose because the two stores are billed differently. What VS Code\npreallocates from the setting is the buffer's array of line slots - xterm.js\nbuilds `new Array(rows + scrollback)` for it up front\n(CircularList.ts,\nBuffer.ts)"],[836,"**Use `--json` when you want to act on the answer.** Prose (`== section ==`, `ok:`, `note:`,\n`summary:`) rides stderr and stdout carries only labels, one per line, which is the shape\n`bazelisk test` consumes:"],[2373,"**WebSockets need nothing here.** Traefik forwards `Upgrade` and `Connection`\nas-is; there is no `protocol: websocket` to set and no middleware to add. The Hermes\nChat tab's `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` open on that alone. They fail only when\nsomething in between drops the upgrade, and Cloudflare has WebSockets on by\ndefault, so the usual suspect is an Authelia middleware configured to buffer."],[2994,"**What does each non-trunk branch hold that the trunk does not?**"],[2997,"**What flags already exist?** `grep -rnE 'FEATURE_|_ENABLED|^\\s*profiles:'` over the compose\nfiles, dotenv samples, Terraform variables and scripts."],[2998,"**What is the gate you must not break?** `bazelisk test //:test-fast`, `npm test`,\n`make test`, `./manage.sh <check>`, `pytest`, `go test ./...`. Run it before you start so a\nlater red is attributable."],[1432,"**What it can see:** the checkout at `$STACK_HOST_PATH`, the deploy workspace at\n`${STACK_DEPLOY_PATH:-/opt/stack-deploy}`, `/opt/stack-skills`, the data volume, and\nthe docker socket. **What it cannot:** `/home` is not mounted, so no dotfiles, SSH\nkeys or shell history - by design. The image carries git and the Docker CLI but not\nbazelisk, so inside the container the same operations go through\n`docker compose -p evo-t1-prod -f \"$STACK_HOST_PATH/docker-compose.yml\"`."],[1452,"**What it reads:** the config the container actually loads, the environment it was\ncreated with, the mounts it was given, and the two seed files beside the checkout.\nThe created environment is the fact and the `.env` beside the compose file is the\nintention: compose interpolated the former at create time, so a key can be right in\nthe file and stale in the container until the next recreate. Every container-level\nfact comes from `docker inspect` or `docker compose ps`; nothing writes, restarts,\nrecreates, or execs."],[1195,"**What the lane choice buys.** `grok_subagents_lane` is the knob for the writers and it\ndecides placement, not size: the width comes from `grok_swarm_width` (default 15) on\nevery lane, and an explicit\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent` overrules it (its validation accepts null or 1..32). That\n15 is the fleet's arithmetic rather than taste. A parent and every child it spawns\ncarry the same `x-litellm-session-id`, so a pinned lane places the whole fan-out on one\nbox and the useful width is that box's `PARALLEL` - 5 by default, from\n`scripts/spark-configure.sh` - minus the parent's own stream; past that, extra slots are\nLiteLLM queue depth. `agent-swarm` is one of four Spark groups with no session pin -\n`agent-explore`, `agent-review` and `agent-swarm-fast` lift it the same way, and\nlitellm-routing.md\nis where what each lane buys is worked out - so each child lands on whichever reachable\nbox has the fewest requests in flight and the ceiling\nbecomes `PARALLEL` per box: 3 boxes x 5 = 15, which is every stream the fleet has and\ntherefore a one-workspace-at-a-time number. The pins no longer put every type on that one\nlane: `[subagents.models]` renders `general-purpose` and the seeded `implementer` and\n`tester` from `grok_subagents_lane`, `explore` and the seeded `explorer` from\n`grok_explore_lane` (default `agent-explore`), `plan` and the seeded `architect` from\n`grok_plan_lane` (default `agent`, still pinned, because one planner is a conversation that\nsteers every wave after it), and the seeded `reviewer` and `security-auditor` from\n`grok_review_lane` (default `agent-review`). What has not changed is why the block exists\nat all: every writer in a fan-out is a `general-purpose` child,\nand while only the two read-only types were pinned those writers inherited the parent's\nmodel, so a swarm-named wave still queued its edits behind one box's streams. The lane is\ntherefore a config decision: the guide documents `[subagents.models]` as the per-type\noverride that \"applies for any parent\", and documents the spawn call's own `model`\nargument as the agent's choice of one child's model, without saying which of the two wins\nwhen both name one. Set the pin when the lane matters."],[16,"**What this enables**"],[104,"**What this enables**"],[527,"**What this enables**"],[613,"**What this enables**"],[673,"**What this enables**"],[771,"**What this enables**"],[817,"**What this enables**"],[893,"**What this enables**"],[946,"**What this enables**"],[1014,"**What this enables**"],[1069,"**What this enables**"],[1134,"**What this enables**"],[1412,"**What this enables**"],[1534,"**What this enables**"],[1570,"**What this enables**"],[1621,"**What this enables**"],[1803,"**What this enables**"],[1862,"**What this enables**"],[1948,"**What this enables**"],[2079,"**What this enables**"],[2107,"**What this enables**"],[2149,"**What this enables**"],[2281,"**What this enables**"],[2324,"**What this enables**"],[2407,"**What this enables**"],[2539,"**What this enables**"],[2965,"**What this enables**"],[1443,"**What to ask from a phone** is the same list you would run at the desk, and the\n`coding-agents` skill is what teaches the assistant to answer it:"],[10,"**What's on this page**"],[97,"**What's on this page**"],[520,"**What's on this page**"],[607,"**What's on this page**"],[663,"**What's on this page**"],[765,"**What's on this page**"],[809,"**What's on this page**"],[886,"**What's on this page**"],[939,"**What's on this page**"],[1008,"**What's on this page**"],[1064,"**What's on this page**"],[1124,"**What's on this page**"],[1404,"**What's on this page**"],[1530,"**What's on this page**"],[1566,"**What's on this page**"],[1613,"**What's on this page**"],[1797,"**What's on this page**"],[1854,"**What's on this page**"],[1940,"**What's on this page**"],[2076,"**What's on this page**"],[2102,"**What's on this page**"],[2139,"**What's on this page**"],[2276,"**What's on this page**"],[2318,"**What's on this page**"],[2400,"**What's on this page**"],[2531,"**What's on this page**"],[2957,"**What's on this page**"],[2993,"**Which branches exist, how old are they, who owns them?**"],[1384,"**Why the daemon and not the Coder API.** A workspace that can host a session is a\n*running* one, and a running workspace is a labelled container: both templates stamp\n`coder.workspace_id` and `coder.workspace_name`, the same labels\n`scripts/github-auth.sh` enumerates. Reading the daemon needs no credential, which\nmatters because the only Coder token this stack mints is the root-equivalent one\n`scripts/push-template.sh` creates for the length of a push and deletes on the way\nout. A chat turn should not mint one to answer \"what is running\", so where a verb\nbelongs to Coder rather than to the daemon the CLI prints the hand-off command."],[499,"**Why the env pair goes stale.** Coder injects `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` into the agent\nenvironment when the workspace starts. That is a GitHub App user-to-server token, it\nexpires about an hour later, and the injected copy is never refreshed for the life of the\nworkspace: `gh_token_refresh` re-mints on start and every 20 minutes into the gh auth store\nand the gitconfig helper, never into the environment, and no process can rewrite another\nprocess's environment anyway. So for most of a workspace's life the helper echoes a dead\ntoken, github.com answers `remote: Invalid username or token. Password authentication is\nnot supported for Git operations.`, and the healthy store beside it is never asked. `gh`\nconsults the same pair before its own store, so inside that workspace `gh` needs\n`env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN gh <args>` for the whole of its life; `git` needs no\nprefix once the helper is the env-proof form above, which is what `fix` installs."],[233,"**Workspace image** (`workspace_image`, dropdown, immutable). `--var image=` seeds its\ndefault and is also injected as its own option, so a custom tag still reaches the\ncontainer while the form stays honest about what it is building."],[1449,"*is the box healthy* -> `//scripts:hermes-shell -- check`, and the `host-diagnostics` skill"],[1447,"*start one on repo X* -> `launch --dir /home/coder/<repo> <workspace>`"],[1446,"*tell it yes* -> `send <workspace> <session> \"yes\"`"],[1448,"*wake me when it finishes* -> `watch <workspace> <session> 1800`, then `hermes send`\nto the ntfy topic above"],[1444,"*what are my agents doing* -> `//scripts:coder-sessions -- list` / `show`"],[1445,"*what did it get stuck on* -> `tail <workspace> <session> 40`, then quote the pane"],[281,"`--activity-bump` goes to `0` with it: a bump only extends a deadline, and with no\ndeadline there is nothing to extend."],[477,"`--additional-repos` is the opt-in escape hatch for the service-and-the-library-it-uses\ncase. It takes a comma-separated list (`\"url1,url2\"`), one URL per line works too, and\nthe spaces around each entry are trimmed. It joins the list into one `additional_repos`\nparameter, so `\"a , b\"` and `\"a\\nb\"` both arrive in the template as `a,b`."],[1221,"`--agent-profile` overrides both and loads no file, so that session runs the default agent\nunless the client names one (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/15-agent-mode.md:69`)."],[2418,"`--apply` skips every box instead of installing a container nothing scrapes\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:541`),"],[2428,"`--apply` was **not run for this page**: it creates containers on real hardware, and no\nSpark answered ssh from where the other commands ran. Read the plan output above as the\ncontract for what it does."],[2304,"`--check` — the read-only mode `scripts/setup-host.sh` and `scripts/bootstrap.sh` both\ntake. `setup-host --check` names the tools that are missing, leftover runfiles that\nwill make `bazelisk run` fail with Permission denied, and whether `github.com`\nresolves, and installs nothing; `setup-host --repair` is the recovery for those\nleftovers and, on Linux, for MagicDNS-only host DNS (uses sudo when chmod or\n`/etc` is not enough). `bootstrap --check` prints the same\nprobes the full run makes (tools, compose plugin, placeholder secrets by name, hosts,\ncerts, GPU, images) and writes nothing. An unrecognised argument in either script is\nan error, never a silent fall-through to the mutating run."],[2558,"`--check` prints which nameservers are in play and whether `github.com` resolves.\nOther ways to keep a public resolver: add `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` as a global\nnameserver in the Tailscale admin DNS page."],[340,"`--devcontainer` forces the devcontainer template and `--template <name>` overrides the\ntemplate choice outright."],[483,"`--dry-run` prints the list under `also:` before it creates anything, so the decision\nis readable first."],[2189,"`--env-file PATH` points the verb at a dotenv file other than the `.env` compose itself\nreads. `--help` prints the usage block above."],[1312,"`--help` prints the same two commands with every flag and every `CODER_AGENTS_*`\noverride, which is the list to reach for from a shell."],[552,"`--home` takes a host directory shaped like a workspace `HOME`, which is also how\nyou try a change on a scratch tree before pointing a live stack at it. See\ntroubleshooting.md for the docker-vantage rules."],[1835,"`--iface ens3` with `--ip dhcp` produces the same pinned-device shape with\nDHCP kept. A name the kernel never assigned is a blocker, not a typo you\ndiscover after the reboot: `check` compares it against `/sys/class/net`."],[2652,"`--noproxy '*'` matters: with a proxy set in the environment curl hands the name to the\nproxy instead of resolving it, and `000` or a 5xx then describes the proxy."],[2303,"`--project-name` - every stack-runner subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`),\non both `dev-stack` and `prod-stack`. `dev-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-dev`, the dev sandbox;\n`prod-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-prod`, the stable stack it brings up, and it addresses a\nstable stack brought up by hand with `--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` (the checkout\nfolder's name). The env file follows the runner: the dev project takes `dev-stack.env`\nbeside the compose file when one exists, and any other project gets none, so a run aimed at\nthe stable stack never picks up the dev host ports; `prod-stack` names no env file unless\n`-e` does, and compose then reads the `.env` beside the compose file itself."],[623,"`--repair` is the recovery when `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` dies creating\nthe runfiles tree (`FileAccessException ... Permission denied` on\n`homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`). Bazel never reaches the stack script, so the\nrepair has to live in this shell entry point. On Linux it also installs public\nDNS so `github.com` still resolves when Tailscale MagicDNS is the only\nnameserver (`Could not resolve host: github.com` after a compose `up`). `--check`\nreports that lookup and writes nothing."],[2833,"`--source=chain` (the default) probes every credential that could answer for that\nrepository; `--source=env`, `--source=gh` or `--source=mint` answers one slot and says\nbeside the answer that git and gh consult the env pair first."],[2420,"`--status` still probes, because knowing what a box runs never needs the flag."],[2121,"`--targets` prints the `sparks.json` that Prometheus reads through `file_sd_configs`; the\nredirect that writes it on the host, the refresh interval, and what an absent file mean\nare in `observability/spark-targets/README.md`. The box-by-box walkthrough, the counter\nfile the DCGM container is handed, and the failure modes are\nspark-monitoring.md."],[2436,"`--targets` prints the document; it never writes it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:340`). The\nfile lives at `observability/spark-targets/sparks.json` on the **stack host**, and that\npath is gitignored (`.gitignore:76`) precisely because it names your boxes - three\naddresses are not something this repo commits. `//scripts:spark-monitor` is what generates\nit on the host, from the three ports above."],[2419,"`--targets` prints the empty document `[]` (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:302`),"],[1315,"`--wait` (or `--wait=SECONDS`) polls for that first owner instead of skip-0. The\nwindow is counted from the database's first \"no owner\" answer, so a slow buildinfo\nprobe or a slow first query cannot spend it. `SKIP_CODER_AGENTS=1` skips bootstrap\nand stack-up seeding. `CODER_AGENTS_WAIT_SECS`\nis what stack `up` forwards as `--wait=`."],[2457,"`./.env: line 188: qwen2.5-coder:14b: command not found` from `bazelisk run //scripts:pull-models` -> the model list was being parsed as shell"],[700,"`.bazelrc` points `--disk_cache` and `--repository_cache` at `~/.cache`, and Bazel's\noutput base lives under `~/.cache/bazel` as well. Inside a container `$HOME` is the\nimage's own filesystem, so all three die with the container, and a recreation - which\nis what every edit to this config or the Dockerfile causes - repays the whole cost:\nre-fetch every external archive, re-analyze the graph, re-run every action."],[746,"`.devcontainer/contributor-env` is a sentinel, not a config file. It is tracked in this\nrepository, and `probe_repo` (in `scripts/lib/workspace.sh`) treats a clone that tracks it\nas a **miss** — exit `3` — even when the same clone also tracks\n`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`."],[685,"`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` is what that reads:"],[714,"`.devcontainer/library-scripts/*.sh` is first-party shell: it carries\n`# @function` blocks, is linted by `//:lint` (shellcheck `-x --severity=warning`, shfmt\n`-s -i 2 -ci`), and its behaviour is pinned by\n`bazelisk test //tests:bats_contributor_devcontainer_test`. That suite reads\n`devcontainer.json` as data, so renaming a mount target, dropping the\n`EVO_T1_DEVCONTAINER` marker from one env layer, or pointing the build at something\nother than `images/dev` is a failing test rather than a surprise on someone's laptop."],[2417,"`.env.example:629`. It is read by **this CLI**, at `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:212` - not\nby Compose. Nothing in `docker-compose.yml` consults it, because the exporters do not run\nin the stack's project at all; they run on the boxes. With the key off:"],[558,"`.env.sample` deliberately carries no GitHub credential of any kind, so the env\npass has nothing to reconcile here: the twin declares no `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\nkey and `update-configs` will never add one. Those keys belong in `coder.env`\nbeside `docker-compose.yml` (gitignored), which is not a file this tool touches."],[58,"`.env` keys it reads"],[741,"`.github/workflows/ci.yml` builds `images/dev` with `docker buildx build` and the GitHub\nActions layer cache (`type=gha`), then `docker run`s that image and runs the checks inside\nit: `//:test-fast`, `//:lint`, `//:validate`, and the `docs-site/` Node checks."],[1242,"`.grok/workflows/fleet-verify.js` is the same three phases as a saved workflow rather than a\nprocedure an agent follows by hand: **Map** puts two read-only explorers on `agent-explore`,\n**Implement** puts one writer on `agent-swarm`, and **Verify** puts an independent skeptic on\n`agent-review` that re-runs the producer's own command and returns a structured verdict of\n`kept`, `broken` or `mixed` with the evidence behind each finding. Its three model ids are\naliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, so `grok inspect` is the check after an alias edit - a name\nthe catalog no longer carries fails the run rather than degrading it."],[2492,"`.terraform.lock.hcl is missing` -> a note, not a failure"],[2713,"`(D)elete it` is the right answer for an orphan. Do not pick `(R)ecover`: every start\nre-renders `config.toml` from `grok-config.toml.tftpl`, so recovery would restore bytes\nthe stack is going to replace regardless."],[1220,"`[agent] name` in `~/.grok/config.toml` picks the definition for an interactive `grok`\nrun; a missing name falls through to `GROK_AGENT`, which takes an absolute path or a bare\nagent name (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:124-132`, `:810`)."],[1182,"`[features]` carries `telemetry = false`, `web_fetch = true` and\n`active_agent_messages = true`, the last of them giving the parent\n`send_subagent_message`: a child already running can be handed a new instruction and\nkeep its context, which is cheaper than killing it and dispatching a replacement. All\nthree keys are static in `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:368-373` and none\nis rendered from a template variable."],[1178,"`[models] default` points at the `grok_default_model` alias - `agent` unless the\ntemplate variable or the create form's **Grok default model** dropdown names\nanother. `[models] default_reasoning_effort = \"xhigh\"`. The Spark lane answers\n`low` / `medium` / `xhigh`; `high` is never advertised."],[1180,"`[models]` also carries `extra_headers` with\n`x-litellm-session-id: ws-<workspace-id>` - the key LiteLLM uses to pin a\nworkspace's traffic to one healthy Spark box. Each workspace renders its own\nkey: `config.toml` is that workspace's own file."],[1186,"`[permission]` holds the guardrails the stack owns; the approval mode itself stays\na per-user call (`[ui] permission_mode`). `Bash(rm -rf *)` blocks the destructive\nform, and the dotenv family is split by what a file actually holds: the live\n`.env` and the populated suffixed shapes (`.env.local`, `.env.*.local`,\n`.env.production` / `.env.prod`, `.env.staging` / `.env.stage`,\n`.env.development` / `.env.dev`, `.env.test` / `.env.testing`, `.env.ci`,\n`.env.override`) are denied for `Read` and `Edit`, while the secret-free\ntemplates a repo ships (`.env.example`, `.env.sample`, `.env.template`,\n`.env.dist`, `.env.defaults`, plus the `.env.*.example` / `.env.*.sample`\nspellings) are named in `allow`, so an agent maintains the env key list without a\nprompt in any mode. Neither list is the old blanket `**/.env.*`: deny outranks\nallow, so the blanket form blocked the templates along with the live files and\nleft the key list -- the file that keeps a real `.env` honest -- to drift.\n`.gitignore` carries the matching half on the git side: the whole family is\nignored except the names `allow` lists."],[2823,"`[shadowed: the credential above outranks this slot, ...]` is the most misread line in\nthe report: the credential under it is healthy and git will not use it while the env\npair stands. `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN <command>` makes git or gh use that slot\nfor one command; `fix` makes it permanent for git. See\nthe Bad credentials entry."],[1223,"`[subagents.models]` pins the lane for that type, and it is what the workspace renders -\nsee The fan-out seam."],[1181,"`[subagents]` sets how wide a fan-out goes and `[subagents.models]` which lane each\ntype rides, both rendered from template variables: `grok_subagents_lane` (default\n`agent-swarm`) for the writers, three more for the reader, planner and skeptic roles named\nbelow, and `grok_subagent_max_concurrent` (default `null`, which means \"take\n`grok_swarm_width`, the fleet's width\"), and `grok_subagent_max_depth` (default 2,\nvalidated 1..3) sets how deep the tree of children may go. The two width keys are one\ncalculation, and `main.tf` is where it is made:\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent = var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent != null ? var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent : var.grok_swarm_width`\nSo the width is `grok_swarm_width` (default 15) whichever lane the writers ride;\n`--var grok_subagents_lane=agent` is the rollback that puts the writers back on\nthe parent's pinned box and leaves that width alone, and an explicit\n`--var grok_subagent_max_concurrent=N` always wins. The variable's\nown validation accepts null or a whole number 1..32, because past the fleet's ceiling\na bigger number is only a longer queue. A parent session and every subagent it spawns\ncarry the same `x-litellm-session-id`, so on a pinned lane the whole fan-out resolves\nto one deployment and competes for that box's `PARALLEL` streams - 5 by default, set in\n`scripts/spark-configure.sh` - so the width that is useful on one box is about 4: that\nbox's `PARALLEL` minus the parent's own stream, which is why an operator who pins\neverything to one box narrows the queue by setting `grok_subagent_max_concurrent`\nrather than by switching lanes. The `agent-swarm` default is where widening pays: that\ngroup carries no pin, so each subagent request is placed on\nwhichever reachable box has the fewest requests in flight and the ceiling becomes\n`PARALLEL` per box rather than per workspace - 3 boxes x 5 = 15, which asks the fleet\nfor every stream it has, so treat 15 as a one-workspace-at-a-time number.\n`sampling_limit` is deliberately left unset: it defaults to `max_concurrent`, so the\ntwo caps cannot drift apart. `limit_behavior = \"queue\"` below them holds the overflow\ninstead of failing the spawn, so overshoot costs a subagent latency rather than its\nlife. `max_depth` rides beside them, rendered from `grok_subagent_max_depth` and shipped\nat 2, and at 2 a child may spawn: `reviewer`, `security-auditor` and `tester` name\n`spawn_subagent` in their `tools` (`templates/common/reviewer.AGENT.md:5`), so a skeptic\nor a tester can fan read-only mappers out one level under itself. Each of those\ngrandchildren is its own session with its own `max_concurrent`, and what it costs the\nfleet is one model stream, so a tree is budgeted by summing the in-flight `1 + spawns`\nover every task and keeping that total at or below the fleet's stream count (`fleet:` in\nthe plan, 15 by default, `templates/common/ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py:42`). That total\nis a separate number from the session cap above, which counts one parent's own children.\n`max_depth = 1` is the rollback to no nesting, and it restores the behaviour the fleet\nshipped with before a child could spawn. `[subagents.models]` pins every subagent type a session can get to a lane of its\nown, and there are four variables behind those pins, not one: `grok_subagents_lane`\n(default `agent-swarm`) for the writers, `grok_explore_lane` (default `agent-explore`) for the\nmappers, `grok_plan_lane` (default `agent`) for the planner, and `grok_review_lane`\n(default `agent-review`) for the skeptics. Concretely the block renders\n`general-purpose`, `implementer` and `tester` from the writers' lane, `explore` and\n`explorer` from the reader's, `plan` and `architect` from the planner's, and `reviewer`\nand `security-auditor` from the skeptic's - the last six keys are the seeded role\ndefinitions named in the seeded fleet suite, and a key no\nsession selects is inert. Splitting the block that way is what lets one role go back on\nthe parent's lane without moving the others: a wave of mappers can spread over the whole\nfleet while the planner that steers the next wave stays pinned to one box. Pinning only the\ntwo read-only types was the defect this block fixes: a spawn that names no type gets\n`general-purpose`, and that is the type every writer in a fan-out runs as, so those\nchildren inherited the parent's model and queued behind the one box a pinned parent\nresolves to while the swarm-named readers spread over three. What those four variables\ndecide is placement only: the width comes from `grok_swarm_width` (default 15) on every\nlane, so `grok_subagents_lane` remains the knob a workspace turns on to fan out, and\nnarrowing the queue is the separate `grok_subagent_max_concurrent`."],[2579,"`[tensorfold] done chatcmpl-... prompt=58 ... effort=xhigh tokens=16 finish=length`\nfollowed by `warning: a reply reached max_tokens while still thinking, so its content\nis empty`: that is the LiteLLM proxy's health check, not a user. At the pinned tag the\nproxy sends the deployment's whole `litellm_params` through a real `acompletion`, so\nthe lane's baked `reasoning_effort` rides along, and it asks for the default\n`health_check_max_tokens` of 16. A thinking reply cannot finish its reasoning in 16\ntokens, so it returns empty content and the server warns about it. The reply is a\nhealthy 200 and the deployment stays in the pool."],[2581,"`[tensorfold] ended chatcmpl-... reason=client-left prompt=... after=Ns`: whoever asked\nhung up. A tiny prompt abandoned at around 81 s was a health check queued behind\nlonger work; a huge prompt abandoned at around 400 s is a client with its own timeout,\nand it re-prefills from zero if it retries."],[856,"`//:test-fast` runs in a sandbox: an action sees its declared inputs and its runfiles tree, not the\ncheckout. So \"what does this target read\" is a real question with a printed answer, and the answer has\ntwo depths worth keeping separate."],[902,"`//:validate` adds the Terraform half, and `//tests:bats_mypy_lint_test` and\n`//tests:bats_tf_types_test` are the asserting sides of the two newest gates."],[1029,"`//:validate` proves these files parse. `tests/homepage_config_check.py` reads them the\nway the app does and fails on the mistakes that parse happily:"],[792,"`//docs-site:nav_test` and the contract test together refuse a page that exists but is not\ndeclared, and a declaration whose file is gone, so an unlinked page cannot be merged."],[917,"`//lints:shell` runs ShellCheck at `--severity=warning` over every first-party\n`.sh`, and the tree is clean at that level today."],[2622,"`//scripts:coder-agents` reads that same health view before it registers anything, so\nonce the weights are back, re-running it registers the alias and takes the\n`compaction` / `title_generation` pins off it. A `warning:` line names the alias it\nrefused to register, and the closing `summary:` counts the warnings, so a run that\nquietly wired half the model list is visible."],[1808,"`//scripts:luks-unlock` writes that config and then proves the generated\ninitramfs really contains the unlock path. The proof is the point: every one of\nthe six settings involved fails by staying silent until the boot that needs it."],[1922,"`//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test` keeps `_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD[\"new-repo\"]` honest about what the CLI\nactually reads."],[1921,"`//tests:bats_new_repo_test` owns the renderer: token substitution in file contents and in file\nand directory names, the markdown `**NAME**` alias, the lane overlay and its `gitignore`\nfragments, the generator-owned paths stripped from every render, the argv of the scaffold clone,\nthe credential ladder that retries once with `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` cleared, the GitHub ladder\nstaying advisory (a refused step warns and never fails the run), and the Coder Task being a\nconvenience that cannot fail a render."],[971,"`//tests:py_coverage` discovers `tests/python/test_*.py` and\n`docs-site/test_gen_nav.py` under coverage.py 7.17, pinned in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image; without it the\ntarget fails with a pointer to `//scripts:test-in-image` rather than passing while\nmeasuring nothing. `//docs-site:unit_coverage` is `npm run unit:coverage`\n(vitest v8 provider, thresholds in `docs-site/vitest.config.ts`)."],[968,"`//tests:shell_coverage` is the one to understand before you write a shell test.\nIt builds a mirror of the checkout with `tests/coverage/instrument.py`: every\nproduction `.sh` is copied with one extra statement at the top of each function\nbody that appends `<path>\\t<function>` to `$EVO_COV_LEDGER`, and every other path\nstays a symlink into the real tree. `tests/bats_runner.sh` points `REPO_ROOT` at\nthat mirror when `EVO_COV_MIRROR` is set, so the suites execute the instrumented\ncopies through whichever access pattern they already use — run the CLI, source the\nlib, or `load_shell_function`. Then `tests/coverage/aggregate.py` names every\nfunction whose body never ran."],[1231,"`/bug-hunt <symptom-or-issue> [--bisect <good-ref>] [--no-fix]` reproduces before\ntouching anything, localises with explorers and a real bisect, tests competing hypotheses\nwith a fleet, and ships the test that fails before the fix and passes after it."],[1236,"`/debugger <symptom|failing-test|issue-number> [--no-fix]` is the gate against the\nguess-and-patch pass: no fix is written until a read-only `agent-explore` child has quoted the\nfailing command and its real output, and the skeptic re-runs both the red and the green run."],[1234,"`/documenter <commit-ish|path|PR-number> [--surface docs|readme|rulebook|comments]` documents\na change that already exists: read the diff, fan read-only mappers over every documentation\nsurface on `agent-explore` to find the text the change made wrong, write one file per writer,\nthen put the result past an independent skeptic on `agent-review` that runs every command the\nnew prose names."],[1238,"`/ez-code-index [repo-path]` writes the local map described below."],[405,"`/ez-learn` is seeded on the same absent-only contract because nothing here supplies the\ncommand: no published source ships an upstream `/learn` at all, and the built-in memory\nfamily it copies is gated behind a memory store that is off by default, so those names\nstay hidden. The bundle is a separate thing, and a real one: the CLI extracts\nplatform skills such as `create-skill` into `~/.grok/bundled/`, read back with\n`SkillScope::Bundled`, and that catalog is fetched per identity/team behind an x.ai\nsign-in this stack deliberately does not have - LiteLLM is the only transport - so the\ndirectory is never created and none of those skills ever lands. Writing the file is what\nmakes each command exist, and it is seeded under the `ez-` name, which exists to stay\nclear of the upstream ones. The same stage deletes a leftover `skills/learn/SKILL.md` from\nbefore the rename, but only while it still carries the stack marker; a marker-free one is\nuser-owned and stays. Deleting a seeded copy never keeps your own - the boot writes it\nback - so only dropping that marker does."],[1228,"`/feature-implementation <request-or-issue> [--slots N] [--dry-run]` takes one request\nfrom Plan Mode to a verified change: map with read-only explorers, hand the split to\n`/ez-micro-plan`, fan the writers out, close with an independent tester and reviewer."],[1495,"`/home` is **not** mounted in v1. Hermes cannot see your dotfiles, your SSH keys or\nyour shell history, and it should not need to."],[1237,"`/migrator <dep>@<version>|<api-change>` pins where a version actually lives by `path:line`,\nreads the upstream delta from a research lane rather than from memory, writes the plan and the\nrollback before touching anything, then fans the edits out and answers to a skeptic."],[1488,"`/opt/data` is a **named volume on purpose**. Hermes keeps a SQLite database there in\nWAL mode, and WAL on a virtiofs bind mount is not safe. Bind-mounting that path is how\nyou corrupt the assistant's own memory."],[1235,"`/performance-optimizer <path|command> [--metric <name>] [--no-change]` takes a baseline with\nan instrument that is already local (`time`, `rg --stats`, Bazel `--profile`), hunts the cost\nwith read-only mappers, ranks proposals by measured cost rather than how bad the code looks,\nlands at most one change, re-measures, and ends its skeptical pass on `agent-review`."],[1380,"`/reflect-and-evolve-repo` is its sibling against a different subject: where `/reflect-and-evolve` improves\nthe agent setup, this one reviews past work in *this repository* and turns what it finds into planned\nimprovements to code, project conventions, scripts, docs and configs. It reads the same ranked `signals.json`\ntable through the same bundled `skills/reflect-and-evolve/scripts/session_signals.py`, which stays the only\nsanctioned way into `~/.grok/sessions/`, and it adds what a repo run has and a session run does not: git\nhistory, and the repo's own tooling and gate. Repo-scoped like `/trunk-refactor` and not seeded into a\nworkspace home: canonical at `skills/reflect-and-evolve-repo/SKILL.md`, discovered through the\n`.grok/skills/reflect-and-evolve-repo/SKILL.md` symlink, pinned by `tests/bats/reflect-and-evolve-repo-skill.bats`."],[1379,"`/reflect-and-evolve` reviews past Grok Build sessions and turns what it finds into planned changes.\nIt reads the per-session `signals.json` under `~/.grok/sessions/<escaped-cwd>/`, ranked by the bundled\n`skills/reflect-and-evolve/scripts/session_signals.py`, plus the `/ez-learn` session logs and the notes file.\nIt changes the seeds in this repo, never the copies a workspace booted with. Repo-scoped like `/trunk-refactor`\nand not seeded into a workspace home: canonical at `skills/reflect-and-evolve/SKILL.md`, discovered through\nthe `.grok/skills/reflect-and-evolve/SKILL.md` symlink, pinned by `tests/bats/reflect-and-evolve-skill.bats`."],[1230,"`/security-audit <path-or-diff> [--scope secrets|injection|authz|egress] [--json]`\ninventories sources and sinks separately, traces each source to a sink with read-only\ntracers, and ranks findings by severity with one checkable line of proof each."],[1229,"`/skeptical-review <diff-spec|pr-number> [--focus <risk>] [--json]` takes the author's\nclaim as unverified, re-runs the exact commands the producer said proved it, attacks the\nchange from several independent angles, and reports each requirement kept or broken with\nthe line that decides it."],[2670,"`/tmp/coder-startup-script.log` holds exactly one `curl: (28)` line when the\nstart-time download was what failed."],[2728,"`# fleet-width:` is the fleet's own ceiling, `max_concurrent` this session's cap\n(`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:427-428`, devcontainer twin\n`templates/devcontainer/grok-config.toml.tftpl:428-429`). They stay two lines rather\nthan one number because an explicit `--var` owns `max_concurrent` and never moves the\nceiling. `15` over `4` is this entry; `15` over `15` is\nthe pinned-lane entry above."],[908,"`# type: ignore` needs a specific error code and a reason that names the real cause.\n`warn_unused_ignores` makes a stale one an error, so an ignore cannot quietly\noutlive the problem it papered over. Where the value is unstructured but an\n`isinstance` guard already decides what it is, a `cast` is the honest instrument:\nnarrowing there would change what happens to the malformed input."],[1504,"`<mount>` is the host directory of the Hermes data volume (what compose mounts at\n`/opt/data`). `--force` is how an existing volume picks up the seeded\n`dashboard.trusted_proxies`, and `--cwd /absolute/path/to/checkout` is how a host re-seed\nnames the working directory when the shell running it carries no `STACK_HOST_PATH`.\nSetting that key with `hermes config set` is the\nin-container alternative. `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is not in the volume;\nrecreate the container and the env applies."],[1335,"`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`"],[1348,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-bazel-first/scripts/glob_budget.py`"],[1346,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-bazel-first/scripts/owned_files_check.py`"],[1344,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-bazel-first/SKILL.md`"],[1342,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-create-skill/SKILL.md`"],[1338,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-learn/SKILL.md`"],[1358,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-micro-plan/scripts/plan_check.py`"],[1356,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-micro-plan/SKILL.md`"],[1340,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-skillify/SKILL.md`"],[1352,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-strong-types/scripts/type_baseline.py`"],[1350,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-strong-types/SKILL.md`"],[1354,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-trunk-based-repo/SKILL.md`"],[1336,"`~/.agents/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"],[702,"`~/.bazelrc` is the seam because Bazel reads it after the repo's `.bazelrc`, and\nbecause it reaches only this container: a Coder workspace booted from the same image\nkeeps using its own `~/.cache`. Everything outside the two marker lines belongs to\nthe developer and survives every run, and a re-run replaces the block rather than\nstacking a second one. `EVO_T1_BAZEL_CACHE_ROOT` is the one value both halves read,\nso the mount targets and the block move together, and\n`bazelisk test //tests:bats_contributor_devcontainer_test` fails if they drift."],[1333,"`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`"],[1347,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-bazel-first/scripts/glob_budget.py`"],[1345,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-bazel-first/scripts/owned_files_check.py`"],[1343,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-bazel-first/SKILL.md`"],[1341,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-create-skill/SKILL.md`"],[1337,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-learn/SKILL.md`"],[1357,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-micro-plan/scripts/plan_check.py`"],[1355,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-micro-plan/SKILL.md`"],[1339,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-skillify/SKILL.md`"],[1351,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-strong-types/scripts/type_baseline.py`"],[1349,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-strong-types/SKILL.md`"],[1353,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-trunk-based-repo/SKILL.md`"],[1334,"`~/.coder/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"],[2711,"`~/.grok/config.toml` is a real file on this workspace's own home volume, so a swap\nfile outlives a stop of that workspace and nothing else. The usual history is:\nsomeone opened the config in vim, the workspace was stopped with the editor still\nup, and the swap is now asking about a session whose container no longer exists."],[419,"`~/.grok/config.toml` is emitted from a quoted heredoc for the same reason: a\n`Bearer $${GH_TOKEN}` line the config template emits travels through as literal text\ninstead of being expanded there, and the CLI expands it from the agent environment. The\nsame holds for the `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` reference."],[374,"`~/.grok/config.toml` is re-rendered when it is absent, when\n`GROK_FRESH_INSTALL` is set, or while *either* marker is present: the `managed-by`\ncomment or the top-level `__evoT1Stack = true` key."],[1260,"`~/.grok/hooks/` is **marker-aware** on the same rule, with two spellings because its two\nkinds of file have two readers: the `\"__evoT1Stack\": true` key, which is the only marker\nspelling that leaves the manifest parseable JSON, and the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack`\ncomment in the four scripts and the deny list. A guard that drifted from the seed is a\nbehaviour change nobody sees, so this family gets the refresh rather than the first-write\nrule. See Hooks."],[380,"`~/.grok/notes-setup.sh` is the exception to the absent-only rule: it is stack-owned code\nand the copy in this workspace has to be the one this template shipped, so it\nis rewritten every start. It is written beside its target and `mv`'d in - and `chmod\n0644`'d first, because `mktemp` makes 0600 and `mv` keeps that mode - so the rename is the\nfirst moment the file exists for anyone to source, and a process that starts seconds\nlater never reads a half-written library."],[439,"`~/.infisical/agent.env` is what a successful fetch produces: mode `0600` inside a `0700`\n`~/.infisical` (`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:37-40`, `:92`, `:244-246`), written by nobody\nelse, staged as `~/.infisical/.agent.env.XXXXXX` in that same directory and `mv`'d in, so\na reader never sees a half-written file - the same write-then-rename discipline\n`~/.grok/notes-setup.sh` uses above. Its one reader is `~/.bashrc`, which sources it under\n`set -a` (`templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl:76-80`), and that login shell is what every\nterminal, tmux pane and command in the workspace inherits from. The file sits on the\nworkspace's own home volume (`templates/workspace/main.tf:1379-1382`), where every shell\nthat workspace runs can read it."],[556,"`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` is the one rendered target it does reseed, and\nit re-*renders* rather than copies: `${litellm_url}` and the `$${...}`\ndollar-brace unfoldings are rewritten, and that workspace's own `cline.apiKey`\nand `roo.openaiApiKey` are carried forward."],[372,"`~/.tmux.conf` and `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` are rewritten while the\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` line is present; editing the file away from the marker\nhands it to the user for good, and each prints one warning line when it is marker-free."],[361,"`~/.tmux.conf` is seeded *before* the two terminal helpers, which their own\n`coder_script` writes after this body returns. `tmux` reads that file once\nat server start and every terminal (plus an SSH client that reattaches) shares that one\nserver, so seeding after the first `tmux new-session -A` could run would leave that\nsession unconfigured for the whole boot. `mouse off` is what keeps every click with\nthe terminal client: tmux requests no mouse reporting, so a drag is the client's own\nhighlight, the settings seed's `copyOnSelection` puts it on the machine clipboard,\nand right-click and middle-click paste it back. Measured on the shipped tmux 3.4, an\nattach with `mouse on` writes `ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the\nclient and an attach with it off writes none of them. tmux keeps no clipboard of its\nown next to that: the copy-mode copies, the prefix paste `]`, the buffer picker `=`\nand the root-table middle-click paste are unbound, and `Prefix m` is only the switch\nthat hands the wheel to tmux for a long scroll and hands it back.\n`set-clipboard on` and `allow-passthrough on` stay for the one copy route that is\nnot tmux's own - an app inside a pane (vim's `+` register, anything that emits\nOSC 52) drives the machine clipboard through them, which is what a native terminal\nhands that app - and a client that blocks OSC 52 (iTerm2 by default) gets nothing at\nall, because there is no tmux buffer left to fall back to. The browser build of VS\nCode drops that escape as well\n(microsoft/vscode#318254), so a\npane app's OSC 52 copy is silent in the tab and lands over ssh from a real terminal\napp, while the operator's own copy in the tab is the plain drag."],[2653,"`000` on the first probe means nothing listened on 4000. A `200 application/json`\nmeans LiteLLM answered, so the model lane is healthy and the app lane failed\nsomewhere else."],[2654,"`000` on the second probe means the private lane is down as well, so the public\nfailure is not a cloud-side one. `421` means the Host header matched no\n`server_name` here, which is what a rewritten Host looks like. `STACK_ROUTER_CONF`\nin `.env` names the conf those `server_name` lines live in; the vhost side of it is\nin UI and logins."],[2883,"`421` means this box has no vhost for that Host. `STACK_ROUTER_CONF` in `.env` is\nwhat tells you which conf compose actually mounted, so read that file's\n`server_name` lines first, then recreate `router` so it picks up the one that\nlists `server_name hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`."],[2302,"`additional_repos` — the `--additional-repos` flag on the new-workspace CLI, and the\n`coder_parameter` of the same name that receives its value in both templates\n(`mutable = true`, default empty): extra git URLs for the workspace's clone folders,\ngiven as `url1,url2` or one URL per line. The flag exists on\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` only - the stack runners (`dev-stack`,\n`prod-stack`) have no `--additional-repos` (their flags are `-p`, `-f`, `-e`,\n`--tail`, `--follow`, `--json`, `-y`, `-h`)."],[589,"`AGENT_MODEL_URL` is the single `/v1/models` URL the \"Agent backend\" card uses\nfor both its browser link and its status probe. It defaults to box 2 - same\n`SPARK2_HOST`, same `SPARK_HTTP_PORT` - and all three boxes serve the same\nmodel id, so point it at any reachable one when you want the card to follow a\nparticular box; the per-box health keys are what tell you which one answered."],[1745,"`agent-swarm` degrades one step further: `agent-swarm: [\"agent\", \"coder\"]`. Health\nstate, cooldowns and in-flight counters are per group, so its own three deployments can\nall be cooled while the same boxes are still healthy under `agent`; `agent` is tried\nfirst, where `least-busy` may still have a box to pick, and Arc `coder` stays the last\nresort it is for every other Spark lane. The chain is acyclic: agent-swarm -> agent ->\ncoder, and `coder` declares no hop of its own."],[1463,"`agent` also thinks hard by default: it bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`.\n**`agent-low`**, **`agent-medium`** and **`agent-xhigh`** are the thinking variants of\nit - the same Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on the same three Spark boxes, with real\n`tool_calls` and that level baked into the alias instead. Use one when a run needs a\ndifferent level; Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and `xhigh` and nothing else."],[1666,"`agent` is not the unthinking default. It bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`, so a\nCoder Agents chat on the default alias thinks hard, and `agent-xhigh` asks the\nbox for the same level by name. A `reasoning_effort` the client sends in the\nrequest still wins over the baked one."],[124,"`agents-seed.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"agents_seed\"` body that writes\n`~/.grok/AGENTS.md` from `GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`. Marker-aware rather than absent-only: a\ncopy still carrying the stack marker is rewritten on every start, and one whose marker\nlines a person deleted stays theirs. `scripts/lib/config_seed.sh` names the same file and\ntarget for the host-side reseed."],[539,"`all` runs `env` and then `workspace`."],[1187,"`allow` also carries the verbs the branch-first, commit-per-unit policy needs:\n`Bash(git switch *)`, `Bash(git checkout -b *)` and `-B`, `Bash(git add *)`,\n`Bash(git commit *)`. A grant that cannot be had is how that policy fails quietly:\nan unattended run (`grok -p`, how the delegation skill launches its children)\nanswers \"headless run cannot ask for approval\" and drops the call, so a child\nwould finish a whole task with its work uncommitted. Everything on that list is\nlocal to the checkout and undone by another git command. `git push` is absent on\npurpose - the one step that leaves the machine keeps a human in it - and so is\nanything that can lose work (`git branch -D`, `git restore`,\n`git reset --hard`). The deny side closes the two ways a commit could land with\nno gate having seen it: `Bash(git commit --no-verify *)` / `-n`, past the\nhermetic pre-commit hook, and `Bash(git add -f *)` / `--force`, past\n`.gitignore`. Both are prefix globs, so they narrow that door rather than seal\nit, and the `Stage by path` rule in the seed text is the rest of the defence."],[1637,"`allowed_fails: 40` and `cooldown_time: 5` decide when a box stops being a candidate."],[911,"`allowJs` and `checkJs` are on, which is how the two operator CLIs\n`docs-site/scripts/serve_static.mjs` and `verify_site.mjs` are type-checked: each\nopens with `// @ts-check` and carries JSDoc `@param`/`@returns` on every function,\nwith `@typedef`s for the shapes it reads out of `lib/nav.json`. `zod` already\nvalidates the frontmatter at the content boundary in `docs-site/lib/content.ts`, so\nparsed pages arrive as typed `PageNode`s rather than as `unknown`."],[912,"`any`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, and `@ts-nocheck` are absent from every\ntracked file. The one exclusion is `docs-site/.source/`, the code fumadocs-mdx\ngenerates, which is gitignored. `docs-site/tests/unit/helpers/fumadocs-stub.ts` is\nwhere a dependency's shape gets stated once when a test needs one."],[2371,"`authelia@file` is a placeholder for the Authelia middleware that box already\ndeclares. Name it exactly as that box names it - a middleware created by labels is\n`authelia@docker`, and a name Traefik cannot resolve fails the router rather than\nserving it unauthenticated. The six interactive names (dashboard, Coder, Kasm, Hermes,\nGrafana, Infisical) carry it; LiteLLM must not."],[2355,"`auto` composes the seven names from the parts above;"],[1638,"`background_health_checks: true`, with `health_check_interval: 300` and\n`health_check_concurrency: 2`, re-probe a box on their own, so recovery does not\nwait for user traffic to rediscover it. Those two keys have to sit in\n`general_settings:`; under `router_settings:` the proxy raises `ValueError` at boot.\nThe interval is LiteLLM's own default and the concurrency is deliberately small:\na probe is a real generation that carries the lane's baked `reasoning_effort`, and\non a box whose prompt cache sits full it evicts a warm conversation to fit itself.\nSee the TensorFold log section of troubleshooting.md."],[2497,"`bash: -c: line 32: syntax error near unexpected token '('` after \"Running code-server in the background\" -> HCL `$$((` in the editor wait loop"],[1188,"`Bash(rm -rf *)` is a prefix glob, so it catches a benign per-path scratch cleanup\nalong with the destruction it is there to prevent. Agents run into that constantly:\nacross 271 workspace sessions, 86 recorded at least one denied tool call and 56\nattempted an `rm -rf`, and 55 sessions did both. The shapes that are never denied\nare per-path deletions - `find <dir> -type f -delete`, or `rm -f` naming each path -\nso clean scratch that way. An `allow` entry cannot rescue `rm -rf`, because deny\noutranks allow. Narrowing the pattern is an operator call and a deliberate one: it\nis the only thing between an agent and a recursive delete on a path it got wrong."],[979,"`bats_source_repo_root` — the checkout whose **bytes** are what the stack ships, which\nunder the coverage mirror is the one the mirror was built from (`EVO_COV_SOURCE_ROOT`)\nwhile `REPO_ROOT` is the mirror. A gate that counts the size of a shipped payload reads\nit there; the production functions it loads still come from `REPO_ROOT`, so measuring\nthe shipping tree costs no coverage mark."],[978,"`BATS_TEST_TMPDIR` — per-test scratch, removed after each test. `make_scratch` prints a\nfresh directory under it; `fake_root`-style helpers build a throwaway checkout there."],[834,"`bazel-map` is a thin shell over `bazelisk query`. It reads nothing and writes nothing; each verb\nis one query, run from the real checkout, with Bazel's stderr kept so a miss is reported instead of\nswallowed. Run it through Bazel like every other CLI:"],[2358,"`bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` derives:"],[681,"`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` refuses to build when a pin has no matching\n`ARG`, or when an `ARG` default drifted from the pin. Add both sides of a new pin in the\nsame change."],[506,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- -h` - full usage: the verdict kinds, both argument\norders, both modes and the fix contract."],[505,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- auth <container> [-]` - STACK HOST ONLY (it\ndrives `docker exec`). Installs a pasted PAT on stdin into that one workspace, then\nre-installs the env-proof helper that `gh auth setup-git` would otherwise overwrite.\nLegitimate only once `check` has already answered REFUSED-GRANT, NOT VISIBLE or REJECTED:\nthe repo rule is that a workspace's credential comes from the External Auth provider, so\nno verdict short of those justifies a pasted token. It REPLACES the per-start minted\ncredential, the next `gh_token_refresh` rewrites the store while an account is connected,\nand `gh auth logout` or a rebuild clears it - treat it as a stand-in and rotate or remove\nit in GitHub -> Settings -> Applications when the work ends. The PAT arrives on stdin only\n(the `-` form, or the hidden prompt): argv is readable through `ps` and keeps the token in\nshell history."],[503,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- check [container] [repo-url] [--source=<slot>]` -\nread-only. Inside a workspace or the contributor devcontainer it runs SELF mode with no\ndocker and prints `self:` lines for this environment's own chain; on the stack host it\nenumerates the labeled workspace containers, or probes a named container alone.\n`--source=chain|env|gh|mint` names one slot (chain is the default), and flags may go\nbefore or after the positionals. The last line is `push would WORK` or\n`push would FAIL`, keyed to the credential git would really send, and check exits\nnon-zero on FAIL. The verdicts are PUSH-READY (200 advertising refs), REFUSED-GRANT\n(403, it authenticates and every write is denied), NOT VISIBLE (404, hidden or absent),\nREJECTED (401, the credential itself is refused, as an expired mint is), or no useful\nanswer (offline)."],[504,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix [container] [repo-url]` - THE ONE COMMAND,\nand the answer to every `push would FAIL` line. Inside a workspace it repairs that\nworkspace; on the host a named container repairs that one workspace, and no container is\nrefused there because it names the blast radius. It mints a live token through Coder\nexternal-auth, writes the gh auth store, points git's `github.com` and `gist.github.com`\ncredential helper at that store through a form that clears the env pair first, then\nre-runs the write probe and prints the verdict again, so it proves itself. Idempotent.\nWhen nothing can be minted AND the store is empty its line begins `HANDOFF` and names the\nthree clicks the human owes it."],[2635,"`bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` covers both hops plus the config side in one\npass: the declared lanes, whether each `command=` binary is there, whether\n`~/.grok/config.toml` is still stack-managed, whether the lane's credential is\nset, whether the search lane returns results for a plain lookup rather than just\nan HTTP 200, and each server's handshake. A missing binary prints the exact\n`npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command that puts it back."],[327,"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <git-url> [name]` answers the two questions\nabove for you: which template, and whether to build the dev container."],[2788,"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` decides that for you, per repo:"],[2551,"`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` is the command that pair recovers with; the\n`make prod-stack-up` (and `make stack-up`) shims run `--repair` before calling that same\ntarget. Bare `bash scripts/setup-host.sh` repairs leftovers and then installs any missing\nhost tools. `bash scripts/setup-host.sh --check` lists leftover paths without writing."],[2763,"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` prints it because no lockfile is committed\nunder `templates/`, and Coder pushes the directory without one."],[139,"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` runs `scripts/push-template.sh`."],[2632,"`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check` (or `scripts/search-check.sh` from a shell that\ncan reach the host gateway) dials the service directly. It reads `$SEARXNG_URL`\nbefore its `http://127.0.0.1:8181` default, and every workspace's agent env carries\nthat key, so a workspace-side probe needs no `--url`. It separates engines that\nanswered, engines that were unresponsive, and an empty result page, and it names\neach engine the instance has parked together with the reason the backend gave\n(`parked by its backend: google: access denied`). A park expires after\n`search.suspended_times` in `searxng/settings.yml`, so re-run before writing an\nengine off. `--json` adds `engines_parked` for a dashboard to trend."],[579,"`bootstrap.sh` derives `STACK_DASHBOARD_URL` / `STACK_CODER_URL` /\n`STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL` / `STACK_GRAFANA_URL` /\n`STACK_INFISICAL_URL` from the prefix on each entry (`coder-` / `kasm-` / `litellm-` /\n`hermes-` / `grafana-` / `infisical-`, dotted form still\nrecognised) and upgrades `CODER_ACCESS_URL` to the coder name, so Coder builds\nworkspace links on a secure context with a real certificate. Compose sets\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the Chat tab's Host and\nOrigin)."],[2485,"`Cannot connect to the Docker daemon at tcp://dind:2375` -> the sidecar, or DinD off"],[300,"`cgroupns_mode = \"private\"` and `shm_size = 1024` (MB to the provider, so 1 GiB) are\nboth load-bearing; the sidecar is torn down with the workspace because it is gated on\n`start_count` as well as on the toggle."],[1467,"`chat` - `qwen2.5:7b` on the Arc, and it returns real `tool_calls`. The reliable\ndegrade path when every Spark box is down."],[421,"`clone_owner` is whichever user the workspace really belongs to: the owner of `$HOME`\nwhen the agent runs as root, otherwise whoever is running the script. It decides both the\nchown target and whose git credentials the clone consults."],[174,"`coder login` on the host must reach the API *before* it has a credential, and in\npublic mode the only URL it will use is `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, which Authelia gates."],[2771,"`coder server` logs it; the host `coder` CLI does not."],[2741,"`coder start` takes `--parameter name=value` and has no `--var`; `--var` belongs to\n`coder templates push`, which is the command above. The start itself re-renders\n`config.toml`, so that one line is enough to see the number move; it is also one-shot, so\nclear the stored value with `coder update <workspace> --parameter\ngrok_subagent_max_concurrent=15` or the Variables tab, or the next start narrows it again.\n`15` is `grok_swarm_width` under\nthe shipped recipe - 3 Spark boxes x `PARALLEL` 5 (`scripts/spark-configure.sh:92`) - so\na smaller fleet changes both numbers with `--var grok_swarm_width` on the push rather\nthan widening the session past what the boxes can hold."],[226,"`coder templates` is the canonical name; `template` is its alias."],[2769,"`CODER_ACCESS_URL` itself stays the public name: browsers need it, and only CLI\ntraffic should not use it."],[2628,"`CODER_ACCESS_URL` must be an `https://` URL: a LAN IP, or the public coder name in\npublic mode. Never `localhost` and never `127.0.0.1`."],[211,"`coder_agent_url` exists so agents keep dialing the local plain listener when\n`CODER_ACCESS_URL` is a public name."],[458,"`coder_app` resources are not gated on `start_count`. `coder/preview` warns\n`Unexpanded count argument on block coder_app.*` when they are: that value is\nnot expanded at workspace-create preview. Scripts, metadata and the container\nstay gated."],[216,"`CODER_CONTAINER_SERVICE` (default `coder`), `CODER_DB_SERVICE` (default `db`),\n`CODER_CONTAINER_API_URL` (default `http://127.0.0.1:3000`), `CODER_PUSH_USER`,\n`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, `DOCKER_BIN`, `PROBE_TIMEOUT` (20s) and `VERIFY_TIMEOUT`\n(60s) are the overrides."],[2231,"`CODER_DEFAULT_TOKEN_LIFETIME` - a duration, not a secret."],[1307,"`CODER_EXPERIMENTS` in `.env` (empty by default since 2026-10-08) has never gated\nanything about it: the old `agents` default was not a recognized experiment name,\nand the server logged `ignoring unknown experiment` and ignored it on every boot."],[562,"`CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_REGEX` is only for telling providers or hosts apart;\nomitted, it matches every `github.com` remote."],[452,"`coder_metadata.workspace_info` shows the image, the cloned repository (in `workspace`,\nor `none` when the Git repository field is empty), the LiteLLM URL, the default Grok\nmodel, whether DinD is `on (tcp://dind:2375)` or `off`, and - redacted - which key is\nwired in. A misconfigured workspace is therefore visible in the UI without shell\naccess. `devcontainer` adds a `devcontainer folder` tile and always reads\n`on (tcp://dind:2375)` for Docker-in-docker."],[323,"`coder_script.dind_bootstrap` is an ordering guard, not a starter: it waits up to 120s\n(180s cap) for the sidecar to answer `docker version`, because the agent runs its\n`run_on_start` scripts before it creates the dev containers."],[1468,"`coder` / `coder-fast` - the Qwen2.5-Coder weights on the Arc. Chat is good, but\nthose weights write the tool call into the message body as plain text, so agent\nturns silently lose their tools. See\nlitellm-routing.md."],[2683,"`command -v grok` answering `~/.grok/bin/grok` is the normal answer, not a leftover\ninstaller copy: the workspace `PATH` lists `~/.grok/bin` before `/usr/local/bin`, and\nthat entry is a symlink into `~/.grok/downloads/`, which holds one file per downloaded\nrelease and is where `grok update` installs - so moving it aside does not outlast the\nnext self-update. Compare `command -v grok` and both `--version` outputs\n(`~/.grok/bin/grok --version`, `/usr/local/bin/grok --version`) against `GROK_VERSION`\nin `images/dev/tool-versions.env`: the profile copy follows `grok update`, the image\ncopy follows the pin. Neither copy gates the three seeded commands, and the two\ndisagreeing is never on\nits own a reason to rebuild the image."],[2607,"`compaction router: moved a summary call from agent-xhigh-fast to compaction-fast` is the\nhop firing, once per summary, onto the tier it landed on: `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_TARGET`\nfor a summary within the guard (default `compaction-fast`, which degrades to `agent-low`\nthen `coder`) or `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` for one above it (default\n`agent-low`). No line at all is one of these:"],[1748,"`compaction-fast` degrades to a Spark rather than to another Arc alias -\n`- compaction-fast: [\"agent-low\", \"coder\"]`\n(`litellm/config.yaml:893`) - and that direction is the point. `coder`, `coder-fast` and\n`compaction-fast` all answer from the one Ollama container on the Arc, so a hop between\nthem is a hop to whatever is already down. `agent-low` is the quickest answer the fleet\nhas, which is what a summary was after on this lane in the first place. Acyclic:\ncompaction-fast -> agent-low -> coder. It is the one chain here that a user never picks:\nit belongs to the group the callback moves traffic to, not to an alias anyone types."],[1670,"`compaction-fast` is that hop's small-summary target, and a row no template offers a client: no\n`reasoning_effort` line, no tool-calling flags, and `max_input_tokens: 32768` rather\nthan the Spark rows' 262144, because a summary is prose and 32768 is the honest\nwindow of those weights."],[705,"`configure.sh` finishes by starting `library-scripts/warmup.sh` detached (`setsid`, so\nthe Dev Containers CLI's teardown of the postCreate process group does not take it\nwith it). It runs `bazelisk test //:test-fast` and then `bazelisk run\n//docs-site:install`, which is where a cold container actually spends its time: the\nBazelisk download, every `MODULE.bazel` fetch, and the site's `npm ci`. The editor is\nusable the whole time; the first real gate run then reads a warm cache. A warm-up that\ndid not finish is not skipped forever: `postStartCommand` re-runs it whenever the status\nfile still says `failed: <step>`, and only an `ok` one is left alone."],[350,"`connection_timeout = 300`."],[2235,"`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` - the optional Context7 MCP lane's upstream key, forwarded as\n`context7_api_key` by `scripts/push-template.sh:573-577` and seeded into\n`~/.grok/config.toml` by `//scripts:seed-dev-env`. Most operators never set it, and\n`pull` prints one `warning:` per inventory key the vault does not carry, so shipping\nit in the inventory would make the default state a warning on every fetch."],[1184,"`context7_mcp` (default `true`, parked the same way with `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`) adds\nthe cached `context7` stdio MCP server. Grok launches\n`/opt/npm-global/bin/mcp-cache-proxy` with\n`--config /home/coder/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/config.json`, and that proxy fronts the\nofficial `/opt/npm-global/bin/context7-mcp`; both are pre-installed in the golden\nimage, so nothing is fetched with `npx` at launch. The proxy config names\n`$CONTEXT7_API_KEY` and nothing else: the key reaches the workspace environment from\nthe sensitive template variable `context7_api_key`, filled by the push from\n`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` in the host `.env` - not from Coder External Auth, which is what\nfills `GH_TOKEN` above."],[2455,"`Could not resolve host: github.com` -> Tailscale MagicDNS is the only nameserver"],[1028,"`custom.css` lives in `/app/config` and is served by the allow-listed\n`/api/config/custom.css` route. It styles the sign-in page too, so keep internal\nhostnames out of it."],[698,"`customizations.vscode.settings` carries container-scoped settings only: the two\nlint-and-format tool pins (`shellcheck.executablePath` with\n`shellcheck.customArgs`, `shellformat.path` with `shellformat.flag`), the\n`bazel-*` and `node_modules` watcher excludes, and `git.branchProtection` for\n`main`. Each key belongs to an extension the row above installs, which is what\nmakes the pin do something. Editor taste (theme, minimap, word wrap) still\narrives from `seed-dev-env.sh` through `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`, so the\ntwo never fight."],[2909,"`DashboardScenePage.tsx:171 skipping rendering` is a leftover `console.log` in\nGrafana's own bundle (`public/app/features/dashboard-scene/pages/DashboardScenePage.tsx`,\nread out of the pinned image's source map). It prints when the page declines to render\nfor one pass because the route's dashboard uid changed under it, which is the normal\npath for any navigation between dashboards and for a trip to or from Home. It carries\nno diagnostic value, shares no cause with the `501`, and keeps printing after that one\nis fixed. Nothing in this stack can silence it, and nothing needs to."],[1245,"`debug-fix` (`{ symptom, issue, bisect }`) is the defect shape: evidence on `agent-explore`\nbefore any edit, because **Reproduce** quotes the failing command with the output it really\nproduced and no reproduction means no fix; two read-only explorers then localise on disjoint\nhypotheses (one bisecting when a known-good ref is given), one writer on `agent-swarm` lands\nthe fix with the test that is red before the change and green after, and a skeptic on\n`agent-review` re-runs both runs."],[778,"`description` is printed as the lead paragraph under the title and indexed for search. Quote\nit when it contains a colon, or YAML reads the line as a mapping."],[2529,"`dev-stack status` says `running`, never `healthy` -> those four images have no shell"],[2301,"`devcontainer.json` — a file the *application repository* carries, and the one thing the\n`devcontainer` template builds. `coder_devcontainer.repo` in\n`templates/devcontainer/main.tf` sets no `config_path`, so `@devcontainers/cli` on the\nDinD sidecar searches the clone for `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` (or a\n`devcontainer.json` anywhere inside it). This repo ships a `.devcontainer/` of its own —\nthe **contributor** one for a laptop and CI, built from `images/dev/Dockerfile` — and it\nis not that nested kind: `.devcontainer/contributor-env` alongside it makes\n`probe_repo` answer a miss, so a Coder workspace of this repo stays on the `workspace`\ntemplate instead of building its own container inside its own DinD sidecar. Other\nrepositories keep carrying their own nested dev-container files, and those are still a\nprobe hit. See contribute/devcontainer.md."],[2300,"`devcontainer` — the dev-container template (directory `templates/devcontainer`, Coder\ntemplate name `devcontainer`), always backed by its own DinD sidecar. Its sibling is\n`workspace`: directory `templates/workspace`, template name `workspace`, the plain\ndev container with the optional `dind` parameter."],[112,"`devcontainer` (`templates/devcontainer/`) is the DinD-backed one."],[1365,"`devcontainer` and `workspace` both seed it, so the seed is *not* fetched from\nGitHub any more: no `web_search`/`web_fetch` call is needed to inspect this repo,\nand the instruction text tells the agent so."],[242,"`devcontainer` asks two questions instead: **Git repository** and **Build repo's dev\ncontainer** (`use_devcontainer`, Yes/No, immutable), plus the same Grok and theme fields."],[114,"`devcontainer` reuses `workspace`'s variables. `repo_url` exists in both; `dind` does\nnot exist in `devcontainer`."],[137,"`devcontainer`: **Devcontainer default** (the template default) and **Plain clone**."],[2486,"`did not find docker image 'docker:29.8.1-dind'` -> plan-time failure, not runtime"],[305,"`dind` is not a variable here. A devcontainer workspace without a daemon is nothing,\nand the plain-clone fallback still wants a daemon its `docker` commands can reach."],[1050,"`disableIndexing: true` is the whole anti-crawler measure: the app then serves\n`/robots.txt` with `User-agent: *` / `Disallow: /`, and writes no robots meta tag into\nthe page. It matters only when `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` gives the dashboard a public name -\nthe page already sits behind the password, and its paths should not reach a crawler\neither."],[904,"`disallow_any_explicit` refuses `Any` typed into a signature by a person.\n`disallow_any_generics` refuses `list` and `dict` written without their element\ntype, which are the same hole reached by a different spelling. The binary is the\n`MYPY_VERSION` pin from `images/dev/tool-versions.env`, baked into the golden image\nby `images/dev/Dockerfile`, so the gate needs no network and no `pip install`."],[1147,"`display_apps.web_terminal` stays **off**: it would open a shell on the agent's\nworking dir (`/home/coder`). A custom command-type `coder_app` named **Web\nTerminal** (`order = 6`, after **VS Code Web** at `order = 5`, before **GitHub\nreauth** at `order = 7` and **Install GitHub App** at `order = 8`) takes that slot,\n`cd`s to the clone folder - an empty `repo_url` keeps it on `/home/coder` - and\nexecs a login shell. Like the built-in, it reads no `terminal.integrated.*`\nsettings."],[445,"`display_apps` gives: the **VS Code Desktop** helper (a locally installed VS Code /\nCursor / Windsurf plus the `coder.coder-remote` extension), port forwarding, and the\nSSH helper."],[291,"`docker build`, `docker run` and `docker compose` then work inside the workspace. The\n*AI workspace + DinD* preset is the usual way to get this shape, since the client\nbinaries ship only in the golden image."],[2431,"`docker restart` on both containers per box (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:332`). This is what\nyou run after editing `observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv`, since a running exporter\nkeeps the field list it started with. A box with no such container is a `warning:` naming\n`--apply` (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:725`)."],[301,"`DOCKER_CLI_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and the `docker:29.8.1-dind` tag\nin `templates/workspace/main.tf` and `templates/devcontainer/main.tf` are the same\nengine line (29.8.1). Bump all three together."],[2744,"`DOCKER_HOST` is injected per template, not per toggle. `templates/workspace` sets\nit only while the toggle is on; `templates/devcontainer` sets it always, because\nthat template always provisions the sidecar and the `devcontainer` CLI has to\nreach its daemon either way. So a set `DOCKER_HOST` tells you the template; the\n*docker-in-docker* metadata tile tells you the state (the devcontainer template\nalways reads `on (tcp://dind:2375)`, the workspace one reads `off` when unset)."],[290,"`DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375` is injected into the agent environment - absent when the\ntoggle is off."],[296,"`DOCKER_TLS_CERTDIR=` is deliberately **empty**, so the daemon serves plain\n`tcp://0.0.0.0:2375` with no TLS and no auth. (`DOCKER_TLS_CERTSIZE=0` does not do\nthis: the daemon would listen on 2376 with TLS and the client fails against 2375.)"],[2478,"`docker` -> `command not found` inside a workspace -> an older image, or the wrong preset"],[783,"`docs-site/lib/remark-page-brief.ts` recognises that four-node run and rewrites it into one\n`<PageBrief>` — the two-column card with the list and tick icons. The match is exact: the label\nhas to be the whole paragraph, bold, and spelled `What's on this page` and `What this enables`;\nthe lists have to be lists; and no heading may sit between the title and the pair. Get any of\nthat wrong and the page renders the lists as plain bullets, which is the only symptom."],[909,"`docs-site/tsconfig.json` starts at `strict: true` and adds the switches that find\nreal defects at no churn cost: `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `noImplicitOverride`,\n`noImplicitReturns`, `noFallthroughCasesInSwitch`, `noUnusedLocals`, and\n`noUnusedParameters`. Each carries a comment naming the class of bug it catches."],[793,"`docs/manage-docs.sh` is the same set as a single operator CLI (`serve`, `build`, `preview`,\n`verify`, `typecheck`, `test`, `nav`, `status`, `clean`) for a host that has Node but not Bazel.\nThe gates run through Bazel; see Testing with Bazel for how the npm-backed\ntargets are wired and why they stay out of `//:test-fast`."],[127,"`documenter.SKILL.md`, `performance-optimizer.SKILL.md`, `debugger.SKILL.md` and\n`migrator.SKILL.md` are the stack's four advisor commands (`/documenter`,\n`/performance-optimizer`, `/debugger`, `/migrator`), `ez-code-index.SKILL.md` is the fifth\nand ships its helper `ez-code-index.code_index.py` in a `scripts/` subdir of the same skill\ndirectory, and `documenter.AGENT.md` is the one role definition the `/documenter` skill\ndispatches. What each does and which lane each phase rides is\ngrok-build-and-agents.md."],[2481,"`E325: ATTENTION / Found a swap file by the name \"~/.grok/.config.toml.swp\"` -> a vim the last workspace stop killed"],[535,"`env` reconciles one env file with its twin. It keeps every value you have set,\nfills a key the twin adds with the twin's default (or a reusable value already\nset in a sibling live file - `.env` filling `dev-stack.env`), and moves a key\nthe twin no longer declares into a commented trailer at the end of the file -\nparked, with its value and its explanation still readable, rather than deleted.\nComment lines always come from the twin, even when every value stays as you\nset it, so a pull that only rewrote an explanation still lands in `.env`. The\nrewrite lands in a staging file and replaces the live file only at the end,\nwith mode `600` kept. On a TTY the pass then walks every key that is new this\npull or still a placeholder (`change-me…`, `NOTSET`, `YOUR_LAN_IP`). An empty\nvalue whose example default is also empty is the intended value (LAN-only\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`, a parked MCP flag) and is not asked again. Enter keeps a\nsibling's value or the example default. A missing live file is created from\nthe twin only after a `Y/n` confirm. `--non-interactive` (and `--dry-run`)\nlist those keys and write no answers. A `summary:` line closes the pass."],[2454,"`error: .../.env not found` from `bazelisk run //scripts:dashboard-password` -> the runfiles copy has no working `.env`"],[2490,"`Error: No configuration files` from `push-template` -> runfiles symlinks in the tar"],[595,"`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` (default `24000`) is the guard, and it\npicks a tier rather than ending the hop. The Arc weights are a 32768-token\nmodel and Ollama clamps an over-long input instead of refusing it, so a\nsummary above that estimate is never handed over to be quietly truncated.\nThe guard is a ceiling the module may lower and never raise: the Arc target\ngroup's own `max_input_tokens` in `litellm/config.yaml` caps it, and a value\nset above that window forks there anyway with one warning line naming both\nnumbers. A group that declares no window leaves the guard whole, and the\nmodule logs one line saying the fork runs on this key alone."],[593,"`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_LANES` (default `agent-xhigh-fast,agent-swarm-fast`)\nnames the model groups that may hop - name one of them to narrow the hop to\nthat lane alone."],[596,"`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` (default `agent-low`) is where a summary\nabove the guard goes instead: a Spark group, so it carries the 262144-token\nwindow a full-size summary needs, at thinking baked `low` rather than `xhigh`.\nBlank it, or name the lane the call arrived on, and an oversized summary stays\nwhere it is - the pre-hop behaviour."],[594,"`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_TARGET` (default `compaction-fast`) is the tier a\nsummary under the guard is moved to."],[592,"`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION` (default on) is the kill switch: `0`, `false`, `off` or\n`no` stop the hop outright, and a value that is none of those nor `1`, `true`,\n`on`, `yes` leaves it off too, with one warning line naming what was read."],[706,"`EVO_T1_WARMUP=off` is the only knob. It reaches the container through\n`containerEnv`'s `${localEnv:EVO_T1_WARMUP}`, so a laptop can opt out permanently from\nits own shell profile without editing the config, and an unset value keeps the default.\nA skipped or failed warm-up costs a slow first command and nothing else: every gate\nstill runs, and `//:lint` does not depend on it."],[2022,"`evo-sparks-overview` and `evo-spark-host` each declare a `host` templating variable, both\nof them a `query` variable whose query is `label_values(spark:box_up, hostname)`, so the\ndropdown lists the boxes Prometheus actually sees and nothing else. On `evo-spark-host` the\nvariable is labelled `Spark` and every panel on the page filters to whatever it holds."],[125,"`ez-bazel-first.SKILL.md`, `ez-strong-types.SKILL.md` and `ez-trunk-based-repo.SKILL.md`\nare the stack's three repository-conversion commands, and they stand in for nothing\nupstream: `/ez-bazel-first` puts every tracked file behind a target and budgets the\n`glob()`s, `/ez-strong-types` pins a strict checker profile per language and ratchets the\nbroad-type census, and `/ez-trunk-based-repo` turns environment branches into\nenvironments and unfinished work into flags. The family's fourth member,\n`ez-micro-plan.SKILL.md`, converts nothing: it is the parent's dispatch protocol, the one\nthat cuts a change into micro tasks a fleet of subagents can each finish alone. It writes\nthe dispatch plan to `.grok/plans/<slug>/plan.md` (Goal, Dispatch, Lanes, Tasks, Ledger,\nNot doing) with `ctx/` briefs and `results/` reports beside it, in the same shape the\nrulebook at `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` tells the parent to expect, and the plan tree is\ngit-ignored by `templates/common/notes-setup.sh` beside the two scratch note names.\nUnlike the authoring trio these are not single files: three of the four shell out to a\npython helper, so `ez-bazel-first` carries\n`ez-bazel-first.owned_files_check.py` and `ez-bazel-first.glob_budget.py`,\n`ez-strong-types` carries `ez-strong-types.type_baseline.py`, and `ez-micro-plan` carries\n`ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py`, each written to a `scripts/`\nsubdirectory of the same skill directory. The bodies name the `~/.agents` spelling of\nthose helpers, which is why the twin copy is not optional."],[122,"`ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and `ez-create-skill.SKILL.md` are the\nstack's three skill commands: `/ez-learn` writes memory files, reproducing the upstream\nmemory family `/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`; `/ez-skillify` captures the\nsession that just worked into a skill; `/ez-create-skill` scaffolds one from what the\noperator describes. Skill authoring is those last two - `/ez-learn` stores facts, not\nprocedures. They ride `coder_script.stack_skills` rather than the startup argument (see\n\"The one-argument ceiling\"), which writes each absent skill into\n`~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and twin-copies it to `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`,\neach only while absent. Upstream there is no `/learn` and no `/skillify` to shadow: no\npublished source names either one. `/create-skill` is real, but only as a *bundled*\nplatform skill whose catalog is fetched per identity/team behind an x.ai sign-in this\nstack does not have; and the memory family `/ez-learn` copies is no skill at all but the\nCLI's own `/flush`, `/dream` and `/memory` registry entries plus the pager's `/remember`,\nwith the registry three gated on a memory store, which is off by default and never\nconfigured here. So these payloads are what make the three commands exist, and the `ez-`\nprefix is what keeps them clear of those upstream names."],[1636,"`fallbacks:` carries the degrade path `agent: [\"coder\"]`, repeated for each thinking\nalias, because those groups go down with exactly the same boxes. The four unpinned\nlanes and the two fast-compaction lanes hop one step further, through the lane that\nbakes their own level, so a lane that asked for `low` is not served an `xhigh` answer\nby its fallback."],[2453,"`FileAccessException` / `Permission denied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg` -> leftover runfiles bind-mount"],[24,"`fix/…` means a defect that is already on `main`. **A hotfix branches from `main` and lands\non `main` first** — every environment deploys *from* `main` (or from a tag cut on `main`),\nso there is no `staging` or `production` branch to patch and then remember to merge back."],[2908,"`GET /apis/quotas.grafana.app/v0alpha1/namespaces/default/usage?group=dashboard.grafana.app&resource=dashboards`\nanswering `501 (Not Implemented)` is upstream grafana/grafana#136271: with\n`unified_storage.overrides_path` unset the resource server's quota service is nil and\nrefuses the call, while the browse-dashboards view fires it on every page load. This\nbundle closes it by pointing Grafana at `observability/grafana/unified-storage/overrides.yml`\nthrough `GF_UNIFIED_STORAGE_OVERRIDES_PATH`, so the call answers `200`. The file sets\nno limit; its presence is the whole fix, and what the endpoint then reports is\nGrafana's own default - `{\"usage\":0,\"limit\":1000}` for both dashboards and folders on\n`grafana/grafana:13.3.1`. No cap is known to take effect there: four plausible shapes\nwritten under `overrides:` left that 1000 untouched, and three other shapes stop the\ncontainer at boot, so the file's own header says to prove a limit on the pinned image\nbefore committing one. If the `501` is back, the bind or the variable went missing -\nand the knob cuts both ways: pointed at a path that is not there, Grafana does not\nfall back to the 501, it exits 1 with `Error: overrides file does not exist`\n(measured on 13.3.1), and `restart: unless-stopped` loops it. A missing bind source\nmakes that shape on its own, since Docker mounts a directory where the file should\nbe, so a checkout that never got `observability/grafana/unified-storage/overrides.yml`\ncosts the whole dashboard rather than one console line."],[132,"`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl` is the shared GitHub-token refresh `coder_script`,\nrendered with `templatefile()` so `${github_auth_id}` and\n`${coder_github_app_install_url}` interpolate."],[2508,"`gh: command not found` inside a workspace -> the helper needs gh"],[2507,"`gh` answers `Bad credentials` -> the stale env pair, not the store"],[2995,"`git cherry` marks a commit `-` when an equivalent patch is already upstream. A branch that\nis `ahead=0` behind trunk is done, and its row reads \"delete\" without further argument."],[495,"`GIT_ASKPASS`, when no helper answers. Where the staged binary is alive it mints on\ndemand; where it answers `unrecognized subcommand` git gets no answer at all and falls\nthrough to prompting, which is where `could not read Username` comes from."],[2847,"`git` needs no prefix once `fix` has run, because the helper it installs clears the pair\nitself:"],[1183,"`github_mcp` (default `true`, so the block renders unless you park it with\n`GITHUB_MCP=false` and re-push) adds the `github` HTTP MCP server\n(`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`), whose `Authorization` header references\n`${GH_TOKEN}` **by name**, so no token value is ever part of a template variable.\nThe agent environment fills it from the Coder External Auth provider (id `github` by default); a\nworkspace whose owner connected no GitHub account renders the block anyway and pays\none failed handshake per session."],[206,"`GITHUB_MCP`, `CONTEXT7_MCP` and `SEARCH_MCP` share one polarity: all three MCP blocks\nship from the template default (`true`), so the only value worth setting is `false`,\nand `true`/unset/empty pushes nothing at all."],[1161,"`grok doctor` checks terminal, clipboard and microphone support without starting a\nsession, and `/doctor` inside a session adds the checks that need a live one. Each\nitem in the **Findings** list carries its own fix, and `--json` prints the same\nreport for a script. What the report should read here:"],[1094,"`grok doctor` findings in a workspace"],[2475,"`grok doctor` flags `voice.no-input-device` or `terminal.limited-color` -> a headless container, and where you ran the command"],[2634,"`grok mcp doctor searxng` inside the workspace probes the MCP hop. A healthy\nsearch-check plus a failed handshake points at the server, the URL or the port."],[117,"`GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` is the orchestration-protocol seed text that becomes every\nworkspace's `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` (read with `file()`, chomped). It rides\n`coder_script.agents_seed` rather than the startup script, for the reason that section\ngives: it is the largest payload the twins inline."],[1185,"`grok_config_extra` appends whatever the create form's **Grok config extras** field\nholds, verbatim under its own comment header."],[420,"`grok_config` arrives already rendered from `grok-config.toml.tftpl`. Its boolean keys\n`github_mcp`, `search_mcp` and `context7_mcp` only decide whether their\n`[mcp_servers.*]` block appears, and no credential byte is part of that decision;\n`search_url` supplies the URL the searxng block dials, and the workspace affinity key is\na per-workspace routing hint (a workspace UUID, not a secret)."],[370,"`GROK_FRESH_INSTALL` records that an install *completed* this boot - the second\n`command -v` probe inside the install branch is what sets it, so a failed download leaves\nthe flag at `0`. The installer writes its own `~/.grok/config.toml` (a\n`[cli] installer = ...` stub) that is indistinguishable from a user's config, so the\nconfig stage may treat that stub as stack-owned only on a completed install: a download\nthat failed must never wipe a user-owned config in its place. Without that flag a\nbrand-new workspace would be left with an unconfigured CLI, because the stub carries\nneither stack marker and the file already exists."],[570,"`GROK_PROFILE_MODE` in the twin is the default for the template variable\n`grok_profile_mode`, which the create form also shows per workspace as the **Grok\nprofile** dropdown. Read once per push, so changing the default here needs\n`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`. Overriding it for one workspace happens on\nthat dropdown and needs a rebuild, like `dind`."],[1198,"`GROK_SUBAGENTS_LANE` names the writers' lane and `GROK_SUBAGENT_MAX_CONCURRENT` the width,\nwith `GROK_SWARM_WIDTH` (default 15) behind it, the same fallback `main.tf` makes to\n`grok_swarm_width` (that number also fills the `# fleet-width:` line the seed writes\ndirectly above the width, `scripts/seed-dev-env.sh:286`, so a contributor home carries the\nsame pair a workspace renders); `GROK_EXPLORE_LANE` (default `agent-explore`),\n`GROK_PLAN_LANE` (default `agent`) and `GROK_REVIEW_LANE` (default `agent-review`) name the\nthree role lanes the same way. A value the seeder cannot use is refused in place and\nthe default goes in, so a typo is a line in the output rather than a broken key in\n`~/.grok/config.toml`:"],[2721,"`GROK_SUBAGENTS_MAX_DEPTH` in the agent environment outranks what `config.toml` says, so\na workspace that nests differently from its rendered config is carrying that key;\n`env | grep GROK_SUBAGENTS` names it."],[121,"`grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md` and `coder-agents.md` are the Coder Agents\nskill and instruction seeds (chomped `file()`)."],[472,"`grok-build-terminal` keeps its own session, `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`, keyed off\nthe repository root found with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or the terminal's own\n`$PWD` outside a clone) and runs `grok --cwd <that root>` inside it. It is the\nalways-reattach case on purpose: every tab of the clone watches the one agent run.\nThe session is created detached and attached in a second call, so the tab that\nbrings the server up never takes a running agent down with it, and it makes the same\nstale-client sweep its sibling does. With no `tmux` on PATH, and on any create or\nattach it cannot complete, it hands the tab a plain login shell."],[2473,"`grok` -> `command not found` inside a workspace -> the CLI was never installed"],[1493,"`group_add: ${DOCKER_GID}` plus the socket is the same credential the `coder` service\nalready holds: root-equivalent on the host. Nothing new is exposed here that the Coder\ncontainer does not already have - which is precisely why approvals, below, are the\ncontrol rather than the mount list. A public Hermes name has that same standing as\npublic Coder."],[2817,"`HANDOFF` means there is no credential anywhere in reach: click **GitHub reauth**,\nfinish device-code login, install the Coder GitHub App if the reauth page says to, and\nrestart. Those clicks are in\nthe reauth entry,\nand the script's own note on a workspace with no connected account points at the same\ntwo buttons."],[1473,"`hermes --provider arc-chat` does not need the model named beside it - it sends\n`chat` on its own, and `-m <alias>` is there only to send something else through\nthat row. A run that names no flag is untouched by any of this, so the default stays\n`agent` and a healthy Spark box behaves exactly as before. The rows go through\nLiteLLM rather than `http://ollama:11434` because the proxy is the stack's sole model\ntransport, it is where the aliases and the Arc's context window are defined, and\n11434 is deliberately unpublished."],[2892,"`hermes config set` on `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is the in-container\nalternative. `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` does not need that reseed."],[1511,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` does not remove that requirement. Leave\n`approvals.mode` on `manual`."],[1459,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` is a deprecated no-op. Setting it changes nothing and buys\nnothing, and it is not how the public name is opened."],[2878,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` is a no-op. Logs:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs hermes`."],[2890,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` has to be the public origin. Bootstrap writes\n`STACK_HERMES_URL` from a `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry, and compose copies\nthat into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`. The variable is environment: recreate\nthe container after bootstrap."],[1494,"`HERMES_UID` / `HERMES_GID` are set to the host operator, so a file Hermes writes into\n`$STACK_HOST_PATH` is yours to commit and not owned by uid 10000."],[1451,"`hermes-shell -- check` answers \"can I open the assistant\". It cannot answer \"is this\nstill the assistant this repo describes\", and the two come apart on a long-lived box:\nan operator edits the config inside the volume, a recreate reads a stale `.env`, a\nmerge changes the seed and a seed-only-when-absent copy never takes it. `hermes-doctor`\nanswers the second question. It is read-only and prints key names with no value."],[1491,"`hermes/skills/` is tracked in git. Add a skill by editing the repo, never by editing\nthe volume; the volume copy is not the record."],[2793,"`history-limit` reads 100000 but a pane still forgets old output: tmux applies\nthe option when the pane is created, so that pane keeps the limit it was born\nwith. Open a new pane, or restart the server with `tmux kill-server`."],[1027,"`homepage/config/*.yaml` plus `custom.css` are bind-mounted read-only, so `git diff`\nstays the record of dashboard changes, and the directory itself has to stay writable\nbecause the app creates `logs/` inside it. A new config file (`kubernetes.yaml`, ...)\nneeds its own mount line in `docker-compose.yml` before the app sees it."],[129,"`hooks.evo-t1-hooks.json` is the hook manifest Grok Build reads at\n`~/.grok/hooks/evo-t1-hooks.json`, and five more files land under `hooks/bin/` beside it:\nthe four handlers it names (`hooks.secret-guard.sh`, `hooks.dangerous-command-guard.sh`,\n`hooks.post-edit-format.sh`, `hooks.selective-test-runner.sh`) plus the\n`hooks.deny-patterns.txt` the command guard reads one ERE per line. The manifest has to stay\nparseable JSON, so its stack marker is the `\"__evoT1Stack\": true` key rather than a comment\nline."],[348,"`host.docker.internal` mapped to `host-gateway`, so workspaces reach the Coder server\nand LiteLLM."],[1024,"`https://<host>/` serves the dashboard behind a self-signed certificate that\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` issues, so expect a one-time browser warning. Port 80 redirects\nto HTTPS."],[2382,"`https://gmktecbeast.overeazy.io/`, `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`,\n`kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`, `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`,\n`grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` and `infisical-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` 302 to\n`https://authelia.overeazy.io/`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm, Hermes, Grafana and Infisical\nare interactive sessions, and the Hermes policy is already in place. Covering a newly named host\nis an Authelia-side config choice; nothing here adds or requires a policy, so the\nGrafana and Infisical policies are the two this box cannot assume exist. Hermes\nis not an API bypass: it stays behind that interactive policy, and after Authelia\nit asks for its own HTTP basic auth. Both prompts on the public URL are expected.\nGrafana and Infisical are the same shape: Authelia first, then each one's own login."],[679,"`images/dev/tool-versions.env` is the single source of truth for every tool version:\nBazelisk, buildifier, shfmt, shellcheck (apt), bats, Terraform/tflint/terragrunt, the\nCoder CLI, the Docker *client* trio, Node, Python, Go, protoc and the pinned Grok CLI."],[1244,"`implement-reviewed` (`{ goal, question, verify }`) is `fleet-verify` with the repair round\nadded: map on `agent-explore`, fan the change out on `agent-swarm`, close on `agent-review`,\nthen one bounded fix round for the findings the skeptic marked `broken` and one more skeptic\npass. Without `verify` the writer names the proof and the skeptic challenges that choice."],[2233,"`INFISICAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD` - the login `bootstrap` uses to create the project and the\nmachine identity (`scripts/lib/infisical.sh:1216`), minted by\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh:119`. It is spent before the project exists, so there is no\nvault to read it from yet."],[2251,"`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD` has the same first-init behaviour and no reconciler beside it - the\ncompose block says so at its own comment - so rotating it needs the `infisical-db-data`\nvolume reset, not a recreate."],[2230,"`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD`, `INFISICAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, `INFISICAL_AUTH_SECRET` - the\nvault's own bootstrap credentials. By definition they cannot come from the vault: it\nneeds them to start. They live in `.env`, and you set them."],[2234,"`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET` - the machine identity's Universal Auth\nsecret, which is the credential every fetch authenticates with\n(`scripts/lib/infisical.sh:803`). Storing it in the project it unlocks would make the\nvault a precondition of reading the vault."],[133,"`infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"infisical_secrets\"` body that fetches\nthat workspace's own copy of the stack's credentials from the self-hosted vault on start\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:771-786`). Its map is a single key, **`boot_log_lib`**\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:783-785`): it declares no Terraform variable of its own, so\nthe rendered text is identical for every workspace and carries no secret, because Coder\nstores a rendered `coder_script.script` as text on the server, and the one value it does\ntake is the same library text everywhere. Everything else it needs arrives as an agent-env\nvariable. The vault itself - its three services, the operator CLI, the managed\nkey inventory and rotation - is operate/secrets.md; what the\ntemplates have to keep true about it is\nThe Infisical boot fetch."],[2512,"`infisical: pull-on-start is off (INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START)` -> this boot never dials the vault, which is the shipped state"],[2513,"`infisical: refused to publish <home>/.infisical/agent.env; the staged file names a shell-control variable` -> the belt fired, not the filter"],[2514,"`infisical: skipped a secret whose value spans more than one line` -> the vault holds a multi-line secret"],[2511,"`infisical: vault not configured for this stack` -> the boot fetch ran with an empty name on the agent environment"],[2580,"`info: prompt cache: 80693 tokens, resumed at 79747, kept 1; 12 states, 6289 MiB and\n0 MiB spare of 6465 MiB (hits ..., evicted ...)`: the box is out of prompt-cache room.\n`resumed at N` is how much of the prompt it did not have to recompute, so a healthy\nconversation logs a number just under `tokens`. `resumed at 0` means it recomputed the\nwhole prompt: something evicted that conversation's state. Watch `evicted` and the\nstate count rather than any one line."],[1811,"`install` installs the two packages, writes the config, regenerates the\ninitramfs, and then runs the same check over what it produced. Add `--dry-run`\nfirst and every write and command is printed as `would: ...` instead of\nexecuted. Nothing reboots."],[2494,"`installed terraform version newer than expected` -> harmless"],[1053,"`KASM_UI_PORT` has to match on **both** sides of the port mapping. The daemon-side\nlistener listens on that number, so the mapping is `4443:4443`."],[1687,"`least-busy` picks the healthy deployment carrying the fewest in-flight requests."],[1729,"`litellm_settings.callbacks` must name the *instance*,\n`compaction_router.proxy_handler_instance`, never the class. `ProxyLogging` dispatches\n`async_pre_call_hook` only to a callback that IS a `CustomLogger` and overrides that hook\nin its own class dict. At the pinned `v1.102.1` a class entry fails loudly:\n`litellm/proxy/common_utils/callback_utils.py` maps it to a `ValueError` that names\n`proxy_handler_instance` and the config does not load. That check is recent, so the older\nwording you may find in a comment - a class boots clean, the lanes keep working, the hop\nnever fires - describes what it used to do."],[2488,"`lookup registry.terraform.io on 127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving` from `push-template` -> coder cannot reach the Terraform registry"],[2682,"`ls ~/.grok/bundled/skills` printing nothing is the expected answer, not a symptom:\nwithout the sign-in there is no bundled cache at all, on any version. Because a\nuser-scope skill overrides a same-named bundled one, and the built-in memory names are\nthe CLI's, this stack's seeds deliberately carry `ez-` names; the boot and the reseed\nretire a marker-carrying `skills/learn/SKILL.md` that predates the rename rather\nthan relying on you to delete it, since deleting a seeded copy would not retire the\nold command name anyway - the boot and the reseed write it again. What works: keep a\nmarker-free local copy only if you also stop the stack from seeding it, or disable\nthe lane with `disabled = [\"<name>\"]` under `[skills]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`."],[312,"`main.tf` therefore spells the editor's `folder` per hosting environment\n(`local.editor_folder`): `/workspaces/<repo-name>` inside the dev container,\n`/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>` on the plain container, and\n`/home/coder` when no repository was named."],[21,"`main` is the only long-lived branch. It stays green and deployable: `main` is what a\nmachine is built from, so a merge to `main` is by definition deployable to the stack host."],[632,"`make test`/`bats`/`lint`/`fix`/`validate` and the `stack-*`/`prod-stack-*` shims are the\nsame commands;\n`make help` lists them. `//:test-fast` does not start Compose, pull models, or SSH to a Spark\nbox: the suites exercise those scripts against `tests/bats/tool_stubs`. `bazelisk run\n//scripts:<stem> -- <args>` is how a human runs one of them for real."],[2291,"`make` only delegates. An operator CLI runs as `bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`\n(`<stem>` = the file name under `scripts/`); `make help` lists the shims."],[2718,"`max_concurrent` is how many a session may have in flight. `[subagents.models]` is\nwhere each type's traffic goes, and a writer pinned to `agent` carries the workspace's\nown `x-litellm-session-id`, so the whole fan-out lands on the one Spark box that key\nresolves to. That box admits `PARALLEL` (5 by default, `scripts/spark-configure.sh`)\nfull-length streams and the parent session holds one of them, which is why the useful\nwidth there is about 4 no matter what the number above it says. The rest queue:\n`limit_behavior = \"queue\"` holds the overflow rather than failing the spawn."],[131,"`mcp-cache-proxy.json` is the context7 MCP cache-proxy blob the startup script\nwrites, into `~/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/config.json` and only while that file is absent\n(that path is per workspace, so each workspace warms its own SQLite cache). It\nnames no secret: the empty `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` value is what tells `mcp-cache-proxy` to\ninherit `process.env`."],[1640,"`model_group_retry_policy:` gives the nine agent lanes their own `RetryPolicy`. The\nreason it exists is narrow enough to be worth its own section below."],[1634,"`model_list:` is the alias list; `router_settings:` is the routing."],[1672,"`model:` is sent to the backend verbatim, so `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` has to match the\nserved id byte-for-byte, case and hyphens included."],[903,"`mypy.ini` at the repo root is the whole policy. It is `--strict` written out option\nby option, plus the two that make this a rule rather than a preference:"],[2824,"`NO GitHub credential in the <slot> slot (git will prompt and be refused)` means there\nis nothing to send, so the account comes first:\nthe reauth entry."],[2832,"`no useful answer`: github.com gave nothing useful (offline? probe again). A 200 that\ndoes not advertise refs is this verdict too: a push is not proven."],[2452,"`not a directory: Are you trying to mount a directory onto a file` -> missing compose bind-mount source"],[2830,"`NOT VISIBLE` (404): a private repo hidden from it answers 404, and so does an absent\none; clone and push would both fail."],[363,"`notes_materialize` runs *before* the recursive chown of a fresh clone: the notes file\nand the `.git/info/exclude` write are the stack's to make, and the chown afterwards\nsettles their ownership."],[119,"`notes-setup.sh` is the library behind each clone's private `notes.md`. The\nstartup script inlines it with `file()` the same way, writes it into\n`~/.grok/`, sources it, and calls `notes_materialize` per clone. That call also\nseeds the ignore entries that keep the file uncommitted; see \"What is shared\" in\n`docs/grok-build-and-agents.md` for what it writes into git config.\nOnce every clone has been through it the same script calls `run_notes_watch`, which\nreaches `notes_sync_start` on the home owner's uid and leaves the container with one\nbackground watcher (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL` seconds, 30 by default; one instance per\ncontainer behind a `flock` on `$HOME/.notes-sync.lock`) copying each clone's notes back\nto the owner's notes volume between boots. The workspace twin watches\n`$HOME/workspace`; the devcontainer twin passes its `$dc_dir`, because those clones sit\non the `/srv/coder-devcontainers` bind."],[1271,"`notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/notes.md` is keyed per git remote, so every clone a\nworkspace holds — the primary and each additional repository — has its own file. What you\nedit is the regular file at the clone root: that is what the editor, the terminal and a\nnested dev container all see, since the inner container mounts the clone bind and has\nits own `~/.grok`. The copy runs both ways, and not only at boot. Startup copies the live\nfile here and restores it when a recreate leaves the clone with nothing or with only the\nseeded stub line, and then leaves **one background watcher per container** that repeats\nthe back-copy about every 30 seconds (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL`, in seconds) over every clone\nunder `$HOME/workspace`; the devcontainer template watches its `/srv/coder-devcontainers`\nbind root instead, since those clones sit there rather than under the workspace home. The\nwatcher is single-instance behind a non-blocking `flock` on `$HOME/.notes-sync.lock`, in\nthe container's own home rather than the shared volume, because each of the owner's\nworkspaces needs its own."],[1639,"`num_retries: 2` covers a transient blip; moving off a busy box is routing's job, not\nthe retry chain's. `request_timeout: 1200` is now the tightest deadline on the chain -\nthe CLI's streaming idle allowance (`inference_idle_timeout_secs`) sits at 1800 s - so\nthe proxy is the end that gives up first, at a full twenty minutes per attempt."],[1779,"`OLLAMA_NUM_GPU=999` offloads every layer to the 140T, and the iGPU pulls its weights\nstraight out of the 96 GB DDR5."],[2369,"`passHostHeader: true` is the line that makes this work at all. Without it Traefik\nsends the upstream URL's host, no `server_name` matches, and this box answers `421`."],[2479,"`Permission denied` / `jq: error: Could not open file` on `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` -> empty named volume is root-owned"],[1249,"`PostToolUse`, matcher `Edit|Write|search_replace|write` -> `bin/post-edit-format.sh`: the\nformatters the golden image already ships, at the same invocations `//:fix` uses for shell and\nBazel files - `shfmt -w -s -i 2 -ci` for `.sh`, `buildifier -mode=fix` for `BUILD`,\n`BUILD.bazel`, `MODULE.bazel` and `.bzl` - plus `ruff format --quiet` for `.py`. It refuses\nanything outside `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`, addressed directly or reached through a link (the\ncontainment test runs again on the resolved path),\nanything under `.git/`, `bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `bazel-testlogs/`, `node_modules/` or\n`__pycache__/`, `.env` and `.env.*`,\nand any path containing `/.grok/`, `/.agents/` or `/.coder/`. It only ever answers, never\nblocks: a rewrite is reported as `additionalContext`, and silence means nothing changed or\nthe formatter is not on PATH."],[1250,"`PostToolUse`, matcher `Edit|Write|search_replace|write` -> `bin/selective-test-runner.sh`:\n**off unless `EVO_T1_HOOK_TESTS=1`**. When it is on, the hook derives one Bazel label -\n`//tests:bats_<stem>_test` straight from a `tests/bats/<stem>.bats` path, otherwise the first\nlabel `bazelisk run //scripts:bazel-map -- tests-for <path>` prints - and starts\n`bazelisk test <label>` detached with `nohup`, logging to\n`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/evo-t1-hook-tests/<sanitized-label>.log`. It returns one context line naming\nthe label and that log; it never runs a test inline, so the edit turn does not wait on the\nsuite."],[2489,"`pq: value too long for type character varying(256)` from `push-template` -> editor icon data URL"],[1248,"`PreToolUse`, matcher `Bash|run_terminal_command` -> `bin/dangerous-command-guard.sh`: tests\n`toolInput.command` against the list beside itself and denies, naming the file, the line\nnumber and the pattern that matched."],[1247,"`PreToolUse`, matcher `Edit|Write|search_replace|write` -> `bin/secret-guard.sh`: denies a\nwrite whose text carries an AWS access-key id, a GitHub token, a Slack token, a Google API\nkey, a `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` block, a URL that carries both a user and a\npassword, or a credential-keyword assignment (`api_key`, `secret`, `token`, `password`, and\ntheir upper-case SDK spellings) whose value carries a digit. It sweeps the fields a write\nputs text into - the flat `new_string`, `content`, `contents`, `command` and `text`, and\n`new_string`, `new_text`, `content`, `contents` and `text` inside each entry of a batched\n`edits` array - one level deep and no deeper, so an unknown nesting is not swept and the\ntruncation check is what covers it. It leaves the read-side `old_string`/`old_text`\nalone, so a secret an older commit wrote cannot convict every later edit beside it. A value\nthat names the environment instead of carrying a secret is the correct pattern, not a hit, so\n`os.environ/X`, `os.environ[\"X\"]`, `process.env.X` and `getenv(\"X\")` are folded to one short\ntoken before the scan. The deny reason names the pattern\nclass and never the matched text. It fails open on an event it cannot parse, and denies when\nthe runner reports `toolInputTruncated`, because that is text the scan never saw\n(`templates/common/hooks.secret-guard.sh`)."],[385,"`private` mounts no per-owner volume at all: the three state directories then live on\nthis workspace's own home volume too, so they are its own and die with it."],[572,"`private`: no per-owner volume at all. Notes, memory and sessions then live on that\nworkspace's own home volume, differ per workspace, and die with the workspace;\n`config.toml` is per workspace either way."],[1266,"`private`: no per-owner volume at all. The notes, the memory and the sessions live\non that workspace's own home volume, differ from its siblings, and die with the\nworkspace. `config.toml` is per workspace either way."],[2083,"`prod-stack` addresses the stable project by name from any directory; `dev-stack` does the\nsame for the dev one. `update-configs` is what a `git pull` cannot do for you, because the\nfiles that go stale are not tracked — see\nconfig-updates.md."],[735,"`push would FAIL` from the check above means one command:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix https://github.com/toxicoder/evo-t1-coder-stack.git`.\nIt mints a live token, writes the store, installs that helper, and re-proves the verdict.\nFor `gh` itself, prefix `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN`: no process can rewrite another\nprocess's environment."],[2826,"`push would WORK (the credential git consults writes <repo>)` and `push would FAIL` are\nthe lines to gate on, keyed to the credential git would really send; `check` exits\nnon-zero on FAIL so a script or an agent can refuse to push on it."],[2828,"`PUSH-READY` (200 advertising refs): a push would negotiate. If the push still fails,\nthe credential is not the failing thing."],[282,"`push-template.sh` re-sends both after every push, and `new-workspace.sh` names no\n`--stop-after`, so a script-made workspace gets no autostop deadline either."],[563,"`push-template.sh` reads `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` from `coder.env` and\nforwards it as `--var github_auth_id=` so the templates declare the same id.\nChanging the id orphans existing GitHub account links."],[1782,"`qwen2.5-coder:32b` is treated here as a Mixture-of-Experts model: 32B-class quality\nwith far fewer active parameters per token."],[1679,"`qwen2.5:7b` on the same runtime does return a real `tool_calls` array."],[987,"`real_bin <tool>` resolves a tool with the stub directory removed from PATH. That is the\nintegration-tier gate: `if [ -z \"$(real_bin terraform)\" ]; then skip \"terraform not\ninstalled\"; fi` — a test that could talk to a real daemon skips when the tool is absent\ninstead of failing."],[2829,"`REFUSED-GRANT` (403): that credential authenticates and every write to the repo is\ndenied, which is the push 403."],[2516,"`Refusing to bind dashboard to 0.0.0.0` -> empty Hermes basic-auth env"],[2831,"`REJECTED` (401): github.com refused the credential itself, as it refuses an expired or\nrevoked token - the fate of the injected pair about an hour after start. `fix` re-mints."],[2491,"`REPO_ROOT: unbound variable` from `push-template` -> the size guard read an ambient root"],[977,"`REPO_ROOT` — where the checkout is *for this run*. `tests/bats_runner.sh` exports it;\noutside Bazel it is whatever `bats_canonical_repo_root` resolves. Derive it, never\nhardcode a machine path, and treat \"cannot resolve\" as a `skip`, not a failure."],[209,"`repo_url` prefills the Git repository field on both create forms. The Terraform\ndefault is still no clone (`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`).\n`.env.sample` sets `REPO_URL` to this stack's GitHub URL, so a normal push prefills\n`https://github.com/toxicoder/evo-t1-coder-stack.git` and **VS Code Web** opens\n`~/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`. A blank `REPO_URL=` line stays unpassed."],[2249,"`restart_coder_if_pg_auth_failed`, sourced by both stack runners, bounces `coder` when\nits logs show `password authentication failed` - which is the failure mode a stale\npassword produces when the two halves land in the wrong order."],[1642,"`RetryPolicy` in that tag has no `APIConnectionErrorRetries` field. `DefaultRetries` is\nthe only field that can reach the error, which is why each agent lane declares one:"],[1635,"`routing_strategy: least-busy` is the picker;\n`optional_pre_call_checks: [\"session_affinity\"]` is the pinning layer above it."],[639,"`rules_shell` only symlinks an `sh_test` or `sh_binary` source when that file is executable.\n`//tools:exec_shell.bzl` writes a generated launcher and runs the real `.sh` from runfiles\nwith bash, so a checkout whose scripts are mode `100644` still builds."],[365,"`run_notes` and `run_notes_watch` follow the clone's identity rule rather than the\nagent's uid, because that stage writes into the workspace home and runs\n`git config --global`: run as root it would rename a root-owned `~/.gitconfig` over the\nworkspace user's and leave `notes.md` unreadable on an already-cloned folder."],[2564,"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in its logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."],[1781,"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in the ollama logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."],[848,"`scope` derives the test list from what this checkout changes, which is the loop that replaces\nre-running the whole fast gate after every edit:"],[638,"`scripts/BUILD.bazel` declares one `exec_shell` launcher plus one `sh_binary` wrapper per\n`scripts/*.sh`, generated from the directory, so a new CLI is a Bazel target without a second\nedit there. `//:validate` is an alias for `//scripts:validate`; `//:fix` keeps the same\nlauncher pattern at the repo root."],[1683,"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (the default, with `chat` standing in when the Spark lane is dark),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, `agent-swarm`, and `chat` as Coder Agents chat\nmodel configs, for the same reason: an agent turn without callable tools cannot browse or\nedit, so registering `coder` or `coder-fast` would only add a visibly broken model to\nthe pickers. Nine Spark aliases drive tools, so nine could be registered; only the\nfive above are - the thinking aliases are there to make the level selectable from that\npicker, while `agent-explore` and `agent-review` answer a parent's dispatch rather than\na person at the dropdown, so registering them would add two names nobody types. The two\nfast-compaction lanes are absent for a mechanical reason of the same kind: the hop keys\non the summary instruction the Grok CLI appends, and the aibridge behind this dropdown\nnever sends one, so a chat on `agent-xhigh-fast` is a chat on `agent-xhigh` wearing a\nlonger name."],[1770,"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (the default, with `chat` standing in when the Spark lane is dark),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, `agent-swarm`, and `chat` as the Coder Agents\nchat model configs, through `CODER_AGENTS_PROVIDER_URL`\n(default `http://litellm:4000/v1`). `agent-swarm` is registerable but is never picked\nfor an auxiliary lane pin: the control-plane chat agent is one conversation, and one\nconversation wants the pin."],[2347,"`scripts/lib/domain.sh` owns the grammar and every part is normalized before it is\njoined, so a value copied out of a browser bar cannot leak into an origin or a\n`server_name`:"],[2248,"`scripts/lib/pg-apply-password.sh` reconciles the password at the `db` entrypoint, so\nthe stored credential is brought in line with `CODER_PG_PASSWORD` on every start of that\ncontainer."],[914,"`scripts/lib/tf_types.awk` is run by `//:validate` over both template twins and\nreports three findings: `missing-type`, `broad-any`, and `bare-container` (a `list`,\n`set`, or `map` with no element type, which is `any` wearing a different hat). Only\nthe `type` attribute of a top-level `variable` block is read, so a `type` key inside\na `default` body is never mistaken for the constraint, and `any` is matched as a word\nso `company_mcp` is not a finding."],[715,"`scripts/seed-dev-env.sh` materializes, into a laptop or CI home, the same stack-owned\nfiles a Coder workspace writes at startup — from the same template payloads, with no\nTerraform and no docker. The contributor container runs it for you: `postCreateCommand`\ncalls `configure.sh create`, which calls this script with `--devcontainer --context7-mcp`\nand nothing else, so a rebuilt container re-materializes every stack-marked file from the\ncurrent seed and leaves anything you own alone. Everything below is what that one call\ndoes, and what to re-run by hand after a seed change:"],[622,"`scripts/setup-host.sh` is the only script run straight from a shell, and it is the\n**stack host's** bootstrap: the box that runs Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and\nHermes, where Compose and Bazel have to live on the host itself. It installs what Bazel\nneeds (Bazelisk, shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier, Terraform), so it necessarily runs before\nBazel exists — that is its whole exemption, not a convenience:"],[1287,"`searxng` (SearXNG metasearch, host port 8181) is the search backend the stack runs\nfor itself: keyless engines only, no key, no account, no request logging. A\nworkspace reaches it through the host gateway, so the workspace networks stay\nuntouched."],[1383,"`send`, `launch` and `stop` change things; the rest read. `watch` blocks: it returns\n0 as soon as a session stops working and 4 when its budget runs out with the session\nstill `running`, which is what makes \"tell me when it's done\" a single call an\nassistant can follow with a push. Global options\n(`--json`, `--container`, `--timeout`, `--dry-run`) go before the subcommand, and\n`--json` makes `list`, `show` and `watch` printable by a phone client."],[2598,"`ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` is 0, not 2, on each of the nine Spark lanes\n(`agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`, `agent-swarm`, `agent-explore`,\n`agent-review`, `agent-xhigh-fast`, `agent-swarm-fast`): one attempt for a dead stream,\nso a hung request is bounded at 1200 s instead of up to 3465 s.\n`DefaultRetries: 2` is untouched on purpose - TensorFold's `NoRoom` refusal arrives as\n`APIConnectionError`, which is absent from `_RETRIES_BY_EXCEPTION_TYPE`, so that walk\nfalls through to `DefaultRetries` and keeps its two attempts. The proxy reads the file\nat boot:"],[2603,"`ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` is the only one of the two that can be zeroed; zeroing\n`DefaultRetries` breaks the `NoRoom` contract above."],[1692,"`session_affinity` sits above the picker, in `router_settings.optional_pre_call_checks`,\nand it arms the router's `DeploymentAffinityCheck`. Checked on LiteLLM 1.102.1."],[920,"`set -euo pipefail` is the floor, so an unset parameter is a failure rather than\nan empty string."],[461,"`settings.json.tftpl` is copied once, the first time `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`\nis missing, onto the per-owner volume `vscode-settings-<owner-id>`, where it stays as\nthe overlay below. Startup chowns that volume (and the three `~/.grok` state volumes)\nto the home owner before the write: an empty Docker named volume is `root:root` 755, and the agent runs\nas `coder`. A failed write logs a warning instead of running `jq`/`python`\nagainst a file that was never created."],[1265,"`shared` (the default here): the three state directories ride their owner's\nvolumes, so the notes, the memory and the sessions are the same files in every\nworkspace that account owns, and they survive a rebuild. `config.toml` is not part\nof that share - each workspace renders its own."],[571,"`shared` (the default): every workspace of a user mounts three of that user's\nvolumes nested inside `/home/coder` - `grok-notes-<owner-id>` at `~/.grok/notes`,\n`grok-memory-<owner-id>` at `~/.grok/memory`, `grok-sessions-<owner-id>` at\n`~/.grok/sessions` - so the notes, the memory and the sessions are one set of state\nthat account's workspaces share, and they survive a rebuild. `config.toml` is not\npart of the share: each workspace renders its own on every start, so this\nworkspace's model, theme and extras are its own and no sibling's boot overwrites\nthem."],[218,"`SKIP_TEMPLATE_PUSH=1 bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` skips the step."],[1508,"`SOUL.md` is the identity - voice and stance, a short persona. It is not a fork of\nAGENTS.md: the repo's shell conventions stay in that file, where the\nwriters and the linters read them."],[585,"`SPARK_HTTP_PORT` (default 8888) is the port every box of the recipe answers\non - TensorFold's `/v1` and `/health`, for the `agent` lane and the cards."],[587,"`SPARK_n_API_KEY` is the bearer token for each endpoint. TensorFold is\nvLLM-lineage, so treat it like vLLM/SGLang: any non-empty string is accepted\nunless the server was started with `--api-key`, in which case set the REAL key\nper box. Empty is not a working value - LiteLLM v1.102.1 fails every request at\nclient construction (`AuthenticationError`, before reaching the box) and `agent`\nfalls back to `coder`. The placeholder `local-spark` is not a secret and is\nignored by a keyless backend."],[588,"`SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` (kept commented in the twin) feed the dashboard's per-box\nSpark cards - one widget per box, so no card assumes one \"usual\" box. Compose\nbuilds each from `SPARKn_HOST` plus `SPARK_HTTP_PORT`; nothing but the\ndashboard reads them."],[584,"`SPARKn_HOST` is where each box is named - once. docker-compose.yml composes\nevery Spark URL from it plus the port below, so a box that gets a new\nDHCP name or a static IP is edited in this one place and the LiteLLM\ndeployments, the dashboard's per-box cards, and the host list\n`//scripts:spark-verify` / `//scripts:spark-configure` probe all move\ntogether. The shipped placeholders do not resolve on purpose: leave a box you\ndo not run empty and its alias and its card both report down."],[586,"`SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` is the OpenAI-compatible `/v1` endpoint. It is an\noverride, kept commented in the twin: set it when that box answers somewhere\nthe host plus port cannot express (https behind a reverse proxy, two boxes on\none host). A value set there wins outright, and that box's dashboard card does\nnot follow it - name the same host in `SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` too. A workspace\npins its session to one healthy `/v1` box; fresh session-id-less traffic fans\nout by `least-busy`, shortest in-flight queue first."],[2349,"`STACK_CODER_NAME` / `STACK_KASM_NAME` / `STACK_LITELLM_NAME` / `STACK_HERMES_NAME` /\n`STACK_GRAFANA_NAME` / `STACK_INFISICAL_NAME` (defaults `coder` / `kasm` / `litellm` /\n`hermes` / `grafana` / `infisical`) are the service prefixes. `STACK_GRAFANA_NAME`\nnames the observability UI only: the metrics backends it fronts get no key of their\nown, and stay unexposed.\n`STACK_DASHBOARD_NAME` is empty by default, which puts the dashboard on the zone apex."],[2360,"`STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL` /\n`STACK_GRAFANA_URL`, the card `href`s. A `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is what fills\n`STACK_HERMES_URL`. Compose copies that value into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`, the\nHost and Origin the Chat tab checks. The same prefix rule fills `STACK_GRAFANA_URL`\nfrom a `grafana-*` or `grafana.*` entry, and compose copies that into\n`HOMEPAGE_VAR_GRAFANA_URL`, the observability card's `href`;"],[2359,"`STACK_DASHBOARD_URL`, which compose feeds to `HOMEPAGE_EXTERNAL_URL` so NextAuth\nbuilds its redirects and Secure cookie from the public origin;"],[1492,"`STACK_DEPLOY_PATH` (default `/opt/stack-deploy`) is the **deploy workspace**: the one\ndirectory meant for clones, builds, and the compose project of a third-party project\nthe assistant brings up. It is mounted at its own absolute path for the same reason\n`STACK_HOST_PATH` is - a bind source inside a project file the assistant writes has to\nresolve on the host daemon - and bootstrap fills it in without overwriting a path you\nset yourself. It is deliberately outside `/home`, so giving the assistant somewhere to\nwork did not mean giving it your dotfiles. An existing container gains the mount on\nrecreate, since `restart` keeps the old mounts."],[2348,"`STACK_DOMAIN_BASE` (default `overeazy.io`) is the zone the edge terminates TLS for;\n`STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX` (default `gmktecbeast`) is this box's label, shared by every\nname."],[2351,"`STACK_ENV_LABEL` is empty for the stable stack and sits **after** the service prefix.\nTwo reasons: the one-label rule below rules the dotted form out anyway, and\n`classify_public_entry` in `scripts/lib/bootstrap.sh` routes an entry by its service\nprefix, so `coder-dev-box.example.com` is Coder while `dev-coder-box.example.com`\nfalls through to the dashboard."],[1489,"`STACK_HOST_PATH` is the checkout's `pwd -P`, or, from a subdirectory or a linked\nworktree, its git top-level. Compose bind-mounts that host path at\nthe same path inside the container, so `docker compose -f $STACK_HOST_PATH/docker-compose.yml`\ntalking through the socket names bind sources the daemon can find. Empty (CI\n`compose config`) falls back to `.:/opt/stack`. `bootstrap` refreshes the key only\nafter checking the path holds this `docker-compose.yml`: compose mounts whatever the\nkey names, so a stale value leaves the assistant working an empty directory while\nevery container-level check still reads healthy."],[2350,"`STACK_INFISICAL_NAME` (default `infisical`) is the seventh prefix and one of the two\nbehind a compose profile - `STACK_GRAFANA_NAME` is the other, whose `grafana` service\nsits under `profiles: [\"observability\"]`. The vault's three containers start only under\n`profiles: [\"secrets\"]`, which `INFISICAL_ENABLED` (off as shipped) selects, so a\nstack with the flag off has no vault behind that name and answers `502`. `domain.sh`\nreads no flag for either of them, so both names still compose and still reach\n`HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS`, which is the safer half of that trade. The router sends it\nto `http://infisical:8080` - the container port, not the published one - and the\nmapping is `${INFISICAL_PORT:-8182}:8080`, published plainly rather than\nloopback-bound for the same host-gateway reason the search lane gives for `:8181`."],[2361,"`STACK_INFISICAL_URL`, from a `infisical-*` or `infisical.*` entry, the vault card's\n`href`. Its badge reads down while `INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off, which is the flag\nsaying so rather than a broken link;"],[2365,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE=auto`, which records that the names are derived. Under `auto`\nthe parts are the source of truth and `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is output only, so a list\nedited by hand there is ignored and bootstrap prints a `note:` naming the key to\nclear. It clears itself on the LAN-only and explicit-list paths;"],[2887,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` does not create the vhost. It is what bootstrap writes\ninto `STACK_HERMES_URL` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` for the Chat tab.\n`bootstrap` now also warns when a name in that list is missing from the\nconf it mounts."],[2353,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` has three states, and only the middle one composes:"],[575,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is the one switch, and it has three states. Empty keeps everything\nbelow LAN-only and every derived `STACK_*_URL` stays empty. `auto` composes the seven\nnames from the naming parts - `STACK_DOMAIN_BASE`, `STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX`,\n`STACK_ENV_LABEL` and the per-service `STACK_*_NAME` prefixes, all documented in\n`.env.sample`. A comma-separated list is the hand-pin: those names are used verbatim."],[128,"`stack-advisors.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"stack_advisors\"` body that seeds those seven\npayloads: each skill into `~/.coder/skills/<name>/` and its `cp` twin into\n`~/.agents/skills/<name>/`, with `documenter.md` written under `~/.grok/agents/` beside the\nskill that needs it so a start has the command and its role or has neither. Absent-only and\nsilent on a healthy workspace, the way the two skill bodies above are."],[130,"`stack-hooks.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"stack_hooks\"` body that seeds that family, from\nsix `chomp(file())` entries, on its own body for the usual ceiling reason. Unlike the three\nskill bodies above it is **marker-aware** rather than absent-only: a copy still carrying the\nmarker is rewritten on every start, because a guard that drifted from the seed silently\nchanges what a session may run and what it may write. The four `.sh` files are written\nexecutable, with the payload's `#!` line kept on row 1, because the runner starts a relative\ncommand out of the manifest directly; the manifest and the deny list are data and carry no\nexecutable bit. What the four guards decide, and how an operator extends the deny list, is\ngrok-build-and-agents.md."],[126,"`stack-repo-skills.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"stack_repo_skills\"` body that seeds\nthat family, from eight `chomp(file())` entries: one `SKILL.md` per command plus the four\npython helpers. It is a second body rather than eight\nmore heredocs in the first one for the reason this whole file keeps repeating: each\n`coder_script.script` is its own `execve` argument with its own ceiling, and a family\nthat ships helpers grows faster than one that ships a `SKILL.md` each."],[123,"`stack-skills.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"stack_skills\"` body that seeds that trio,\nrendered with `templatefile()` from the three `chomp(file())` entries `main.tf` passes it.\nIt is absent-only and silent on a healthy workspace, because its log is what an operator\nreads when a skill is missing."],[349,"`startup_script_behavior = \"blocking\"`, so a workspace only reports *ready* once the\ntoolchain is genuinely staged - no opening an editor mid-install."],[2657,"`startup_script` is blocking by design, so a failure there surfaces as a **failed**\nbuild with the reason in the workspace startup log. The container is not marked\nhealthy until the toolchain is staged."],[2709,"`startup_script` is blocking, so it finished before the agent reported ready and\nbefore code-server started. Check the workspace agent logs first."],[160,"`startup_size_guard` in `scripts/push-template.sh` enforces this before the CLI runs,\nbeside the icon and preset-description guards. It sums each body's composed bytes and\nrefuses the push past `STARTUP_SIZE_BOUND` (122880, which leaves 8 KiB, about two\ndocumentation-sized commits, under the kernel's own limit). Its reader is\n`scripts/lib/startup_size.awk`; `//tests:bats_startup_script_size_test` re-derives the\nsame totals with an independent parser and pins the two together, so a guard that starts\nmeasuring something else goes red in review."],[317,"`startup.sh.tftpl` clones the repository onto that bind in **both** toggle modes."],[2095,"`storage-clean` is the janitor for the persistent layer and asks before it deletes; the\ndev-stack `reset` and a Kasm reset both destroy state that has no backup. The cost of each\nis spelled out on the entry in troubleshooting.md — read that line\nfirst."],[981,"`stub_bin <tool…>` copies each stub out of `STUB_BIN_DIR` into `${BATS_TEST_TMPDIR}/bin`\nand `chmod 755`s it; `use_stubs` = `stub_bin` + a fresh `STUB_LOG` + the PATH shadow.\nThe copy-plus-chmod is load-bearing: the Bazel sandbox and some tar extractions strip the\nexec bit, and a per-test copy keeps one test's argv log out of the next test."],[986,"`stub_log <tool>`, `stub_log_last`, `stub_log_count` read back what the stub recorded from\n`$STUB_LOG/<tool>.log`; assert on the argv, not on prose."],[467,"`subdomain = false` everywhere: there is no `CODER_WILDCARD_ACCESS_URL` on this box, so\nthe editor is proxied on the path-based access URL."],[779,"`tags` are indexed, so a reader who searches for the word you filed the page under finds it.\n`pageSchema` strips unknown keys, which is why `tags` is declared explicitly in\n`docs-site/lib/content.ts` — a new field needs the same treatment or it silently never\nreaches the index."],[151,"`templates push` carries no description (`push` has no `--description` flag), so\n`templates edit --description` is the write. That text is the routing hint the Coder\nAgents chat reads when it picks a template to provision."],[116,"`templates/common/` exists once instead of twice, and both templates consume it.\n`template_push_one` stages every file in this list beside the staged `main.tf`; a\nmissing one fails the push before the CLI runs."],[1227,"`templates/common/<name>.SKILL.md` for `feature-implementation`, `skeptical-review`,\n`security-audit` and `bug-hunt`, each seeded twice - `~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for\nthe Coder Agents chat and `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for the Grok Build CLI, the\nsecond always a `cp` of the first:"],[2162,"`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl` is the body both twins render into\n`coder_script \"infisical_secrets\"` (`run_on_start`, 120s timeout). One fact about its output\ndecides everything else in this section: `~/.infisical/agent.env` is sourced by every shell the\nworkspace starts, under `set -a`, from the line the startup script appends to `~/.bashrc`. It is\nnot a config file one process reads; it is an `export` line per key in the environment of every\nterminal, tmux pane and command from then on, and it outlives the start that wrote it."],[2298,"`templates/workspace/` (the plain dev container) and `templates/devcontainer/` (the\nDinD-backed one) keep independent `.tftpl` payload copies (`startup.sh.tftpl`,\n`settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). The twins must change together — today\nonly `settings.json.tftpl` is byte-identical across the two directories, so edit both\ncopies of that payload. `templates/common/` exists once above the twins instead and\nholds the single-copy payloads both templates consume: `GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`,\n`vscode-alt.svg`, `notes-setup.sh`, `tmux.conf`, `grok-build-terminal.sh`,\n`vscode-terminal-tmux.sh`, `grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md`, `ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md`,\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md`, `stack-skills.sh.tftpl`,\n`ez-bazel-first.SKILL.md`, `ez-bazel-first.owned_files_check.py`,\n`ez-bazel-first.glob_budget.py`, `ez-strong-types.SKILL.md`,\n`ez-strong-types.type_baseline.py`, `ez-trunk-based-repo.SKILL.md`,\n`ez-micro-plan.SKILL.md`, `ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py`,\n`stack-repo-skills.sh.tftpl`,\n`coder-agents.md`, `mcp-cache-proxy.json`, and `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`. Markdown\nand JSON are read with `file()` (chomp the markdown); shell helpers that Terraform must\nnot re-interpolate are also `file()` (so `$folder` stays `$folder`); `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`\nis `templatefile()` because it interpolates `${github_auth_id}`. `coder_script`\nbodies are sibling `.tftpl` files next to each `main.tf` (`code-server.sh.tftpl`,\nand on `devcontainer` also `dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`) rendered with `templatefile()`;\n`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`, `stack-skills.sh.tftpl` (the body that seeds the\n`ez-learn` / `ez-skillify` / `ez-create-skill` trio) and `stack-repo-skills.sh.tftpl` (the\nbody that seeds the four-command repository family `ez-bazel-first` / `ez-strong-types` /\n`ez-trunk-based-repo` / `ez-micro-plan`, each python helper into a `scripts/` subdir\nbeside its `SKILL.md`)\nare the three that live in\n`templates/common/` instead, so both twins share one copy of them.\nThe SVG stays small enough that the resulting data URL fits Coder v2.36's 256-character\n`workspace_apps.icon` column. The push\n(`scripts/push-template.sh` through `template_push_one` in `scripts/lib/template-push.sh`)\nstages every common payload beside each template's staged `main.tf` copy, which is the only place the\nserver-side Terraform can read them from, so editing the common copy changes both\ntemplates; a staged copy that lacks any one of them fails the push early by design."],[840,"`templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/` keep independent payload copies that must move\ntogether, and `templates/common/` holds the files both consume as one shared copy. The verb matches\nbasenames over the union of the three payload groups, so the twin set is the graph's answer rather\nthan a convention you have to remember:"],[1498,"`terminal.backend: local` keeps the dangerous-command checker active. The `docker`\nbackend skips that checker, because it treats the container as the safety boundary -\nand here the container is mounted onto the host, so that boundary is not real."],[469,"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `\"tmux\"`. The automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build/tasks commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."],[1140,"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `tmux`; the automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build and task commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."],[2688,"`terminal.limited-color`: a tool command spawned by a running Grok session\ninherits `NO_COLOR=1`. Rerun `grok doctor` from the terminal itself and the\nreport reads `color truecolor` with `themes all`."],[2690,"`terminal.ssh-wrap` asks to be run on the workstation you type `ssh` on:\n`grok doctor fix ssh-wrap` there. Nothing a workspace installs satisfies it."],[150,"`terraform init` on the server still talks to `registry.terraform.io` (and\n`registry.coder.com` for `devcontainer`'s `devcontainers_cli` module), even\nwhen a previous provider version is cached. The `coder` compose service\nforwards those lookups to `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` so a host whose only\nnameserver is Tailscale MagicDNS does not SERVFAIL the push. Recreate\n`coder` after pulling that compose change (`prod-stack up`) before pushing."],[955,"`tests/bats_runner.sh` — what Bazel executes: it locates bats-core and this checkout in\nrunfiles, exports `REPO_ROOT`, and execs bats."],[951,"`tests/bats/*.bats` — one suite per module. `tests/bats.bzl` turns each file into one\n`bats_<stem>_test` sh_test (dashes become underscores: `bats_spark_configure_test` for\n`spark-configure.bats`) and adds it to the `bats` suite that `//:test-fast` runs."],[2028,"`tests/bats/observability-dashboards.bats` asserts seventeen properties over every file in\nit: the folder is non-empty and each file parses with the repo's own parser; each is pure\nASCII, LF-only, one trailing newline; the pinned top-level keys and house values are\npresent; UIDs and titles are unique across the folder; panel ids are unique integers;\nevery panel carries a full `gridPos` and no two visible panels overlap; every panel stays\ninside the house key set; every target opens with `refId` and carries its own datasource;\nevery datasource is one of the four provisioned UIDs; every querying panel carries a\n`description`; every stat states its own empty reading rather than leaving it to Grafana;\nevery referenced variable is declared; every templating variable is well\nformed; every link names a dashboard that exists in the folder; every UID besides\n`evo-home` is named by a link panel or a `links:` entry there; and no file carries an\nimport block, a host name, or a dead metric shape."],[952,"`tests/bats/test_helper.bash` — the shared helpers, pulled in with `load 'test_helper'`."],[954,"`tests/bats/tool_stubs/` — the fake external tools (`docker`, `ssh`, `curl`, `git`,\n`coder`, `psql`, `timeout`, …); `tests/bats/fixtures/` holds a fixture that has to answer\nlike the real client, such as the `psql-token` copies that echo `returning id`."],[646,"`tests/bazel_deps.py` re-derives all of them by scanning the shell and Bats sources and\nreports the drift both ways: a script that starts reading a payload its target does not\ndeclare, and a target that still ships a payload nothing opens. `//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test`\nruns it, so the BUILD files stay something an automated reader can trust, and a\n`bazelisk query` of any target names the files a change to which should be reviewed with it."],[2287,"`tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails the build when a header or a block is missing."],[956,"`tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a function under `scripts/` is not named under\n`tests/`; `tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails when a `# @function` block (or the file header) is\nmissing or incomplete. So every new function needs, in the same change as the function:"],[953,"`tests/test_helpers.sh` — the assertions, shared with the bash gates."],[2510,"`the env file was not refreshed from Infisical` -> the vault did not answer the bring-up refresh"],[2476,"`The terminal process \"/bin/bash '-lc', 'exec \"$HOME/.grok/bin/grok-build-terminal\"'\" terminated with exit code: 1` -> something closed the tmux session under the tab"],[2477,"`The terminal process \"/bin/bash '-lc', 'exec \"$HOME/.grok/bin/vscode-terminal-tmux\"'\" terminated with exit code: 127` -> the launcher was never written"],[1225,"`thorough-researcher`, `senior-implementer` and `skeptical-reviewer` ship as\n`templates/common/<name>.PERSONA.toml` and land at `~/.grok/personas/<name>.toml`, where the\nfile stem is the persona name (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:93`). Discovery runs\nthe project's `.grok/personas/`, then this user directory, then the bundled catalog, and an\ninline `[subagents.personas]` table outranks all three (`:89-93`). A persona is an overlay\nonly: its `instructions` reach the child as a\n`<system-reminder>` and move tone, output format and task focus, never the agent type, the\nmodel, or the tools (`:77`) - which is why none of the three names either, and why the header\ncomment in each file says so. `thorough-researcher` and `skeptical-reviewer` also declare\n`[[inputs]]` and `[[outputs]]` contracts, so a parent knows what to hand each role and what\nartifact to expect back; `senior-implementer` declares none."],[2602,"`TimeoutErrorRetries: 0` would not help: the hang never becomes a `litellm.Timeout`."],[777,"`title` is the sidebar label, the search result title, and the string your `# H1` has to\nmatch character for character."],[2372,"`tls: {}` means \"serve whatever certificate matches the SNI\", which is the zone's\n`*.overeazy.io` pair. Add `tls: { certResolver: <name> }` if that box issues through\nan ACME resolver instead. SNI has to be the public name itself - the wildcard covers\nexactly one label, which is why the hyphenated one-label form is the only one that\nhandshakes."],[120,"`tmux.conf` is the fidelity config the startup script writes. `grok-build-terminal.sh`\nand `vscode-terminal-tmux.sh` are the two terminal launchers, and they ride\n`coder_script \"terminal_launchers\"` with the shared body\n`templates/common/terminal-launchers.sh.tftpl` rather than the startup argument, which\nis the one execve that runs out of room: nothing at boot runs either script, the\ncode-server terminal profile is their only consumer. Helpers use `$folder` (not\n`$${folder}`) because `file()` contents are not re-interpolated."],[2496,"`Unexpanded count argument on block \"coder_app.github_app_install\"` -> apps were gated on start_count"],[2493,"`unexpected non-JSON response \"text/html; charset=utf-8\"` -> Authelia answered the CLI"],[2687,"`voice.no-input-device`: the container has no microphone and no recorder on\nPATH. The probe checks for a recorder binary, so installing `alsa-utils` makes\nDoctor answer `available` with nothing to record from; the image ships no\nrecorder and the finding stays. The PulseAudio route that would make capture\nreal: the voice bullet in grok-build-and-agents.md."],[118,"`vscode-alt.svg` is the editor icon (read with `filebase64()`). It stays small\nenough that the resulting `data:image/svg+xml;base64,...` URL fits Coder\nv2.36's `workspace_apps.icon` column (`varchar(256)`)."],[471,"`vscode-terminal-tmux` keeps `vscode-<folder>-<hash>` as its base session name. It\nre-joins one of that folder's sessions while no client is attached to it, oldest\nfirst (the closed-tab rejoin: an unattached session is reused even while sibling\nterminals are live); it takes the next numbered sibling, `vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`,\n`-3`, ... only when every session of the folder already has a client. With no\n`tmux` on PATH it falls back to a plain login shell.\nBefore it reads who is attached it detaches any client on those sessions that has\nreported no activity for over 6 hours (`EVO_TMUX_STALE_AFTER`, in seconds): a tab\nwhose browser side went away can leave its client attached and draining nothing,\nand one such client is enough to keep a free session looking busy forever.\nBecause the profile runs inside a session rather than in the tab's own process,\nclosing a tab detaches the client and leaves whatever was running alive."],[1480,"`web_extract` is deliberately unconfigured. SearXNG aggregates engine results\nand does not fetch pages, so the seed leaves `web.backend` and\n`web.extract_backend` empty and page extraction fails with \"no extract provider\nconfigured\". Set `web.extract_backend` yourself when you decide a fetch is\nworth a third-party extractor; nothing reaches one by default."],[1478,"`web_search` goes to the stack's SearXNG container: the seeded config sets\n`web.search_backend: searxng`, and compose gives the container\n`SEARXNG_URL=${STACK_HERMES_SEARCH_URL:-http://searxng:8181}`. The variable's\npresence is what registers the provider (`plugins/web/searxng` reads\n`SEARXNG_URL` as its key env), and it is the only bundled web provider that\nneeds no API key. The name resolves on the compose network, so this adds no\npublished port."],[982,"`with_path <dir…>` prepends directories to PATH when a test needs a hand-written fake\ninstead of a shared stub."],[1308,"`workspace_external_agent`, the entitlement the docs name, gates *external* agents\n(Claude Code, Codex, ...), not this one, and nothing here needs it."],[111,"`workspace` (`templates/workspace/`) is the plain dev container."],[551,"`workspace` addresses docker, so run it from a **host** shell. A terminal inside a\nworkspace is not a host shell: the `docker` CLI there is only installed so you can\ndrive that workspace's own DinD sidecar, so `docker ps` answers empty and an empty\n`docker ps` means \"wrong vantage\", not \"stack down\". The pass now prints which\ndaemon it addressed and tells you to use a host shell, or to name the target:"],[113,"`workspace` goes first, `devcontainer` last: a dependent template pushes last so a\npartial failure leaves the base template usable."],[480,"`workspace` keeps the clone tree under `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`;\n`devcontainer` keeps it under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>`."],[538,"`workspace` re-materialises the stack-owned config inside the targets you name.\nThe reseed set is the home-relative targets the templates seed, listed by\n`seed_seed_targets` in `scripts/lib/config_seed.sh` -\n`~/.tmux.conf`, `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.coder/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`, the two `grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md` copies, the six\n`ez-learn`, `ez-skillify` and `ez-create-skill` `SKILL.md` copies (two homes each), the\nsixteen `ez-bazel-first`, `ez-strong-types`, `ez-trunk-based-repo` and `ez-micro-plan`\ncopies (four `SKILL.md` and four python helpers, two homes each), and\n`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` - and the same rule\napplies as in `refresh-configs`: a file is replaced only while it still carries its\nstack marker - the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment, the\n`\"__evoT1Stack\": true` JSON key (settings files) or the `__evoT1Stack = true` TOML\nkey (`~/.grok/config.toml`) - so a config you hand-edit in a terminal is your\nfile and this tool will not move it. `--force` overrides that, per run, and says\nso. The six skill copies are the stack's three skill commands, each seeded at\n`~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and twin-copied to its `~/.agents` twin:\n`/ez-learn`, which turns a session into durable memory (this stack's copy of the\nupstream `/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory` - the CLI's own commands, not\nskills: flush the conversation into a session log, dream those logs into organized\nmemory, save one note, browse or manage the store. Three of the four are gated on a\nmemory store that is off by default, so they stay hidden until one is configured);\n`/ez-skillify`, which captures the session that just worked; and\n`/ez-create-skill`, which scaffolds one from a description. The sixteen other skill copies\nare four more commands, seeded the same way into the same two homes - `/ez-bazel-first`,\n`/ez-strong-types` and `/ez-trunk-based-repo`, which convert whatever repository the\nworkspace is sitting in, and `/ez-micro-plan`, which cuts one change into micro tasks for a\nfleet of subagents and checks the cut before wave 1 - and eight of those sixteen are the\npython helpers three of them run, under a `scripts/` subdirectory of the same skill\ndirectory;\na reseed recreates that directory when a workspace lost it. To take a seeded copy\nover, edit it and\ndelete the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker line: a marker-free file is\nuser-owned, and the seeder leaves it alone unless you pass `--force`. A deletion is\nundone - the workspace boot writes the file again whenever it is absent, and\n`refresh-configs` re-seeds an absent target, so the next boot restores the stack's\ncopy. To hide one of the slash commands, set `disabled = [\"ez-learn\"]` (or\n`ez-skillify`, `ez-create-skill`, `ez-bazel-first`, `ez-strong-types`,\n`ez-trunk-based-repo`, or `ez-micro-plan`) under `[skills]` in\n`~/.grok/config.toml`. An edited copy which keeps its marker is still rewritten\nback to its `templates/common/ez-<name>.SKILL.md` by this tool. The pass also retires the two\n`skills/learn/SKILL.md` paths this skill occupied before the rename, and only while\neach still carries the stack marker, so a leftover copy cannot keep offering a `/learn` the\nstack no longer maintains, and a file someone took over stays."],[136,"`workspace`: **AI workspace** (golden image, the selected default), **Minimal shell**\n(bare `ubuntu:24.04`, for when the golden image is mid-rebuild), **AI workspace + DinD**\n(golden image plus a privileged Docker daemon)."],[2648,"`X-Coder-Request-Id` on the response, beside `X-Coder-Build-Version`, means Coder's\nown Go proxy answered, so Traefik and Cloudflare let the request through and the\nfault sits behind them. Nothing on this box sets `Via` or `CF-RAY`."],[2649,"`X-Evo-Proxy` names the `server_name` that matched here: `coder`, `litellm`, `kasm`,\n`homepage` or `hermes`. Its absence means the request never reached this box's nginx."],[1542,"<Card title=\"Contribute\" href=\"/contribute/devcontainer/\" description=\"The golden image, the contributor container, and the Bazel gates a change runs through.\" />"],[1539,"<Card title=\"Getting started\" href=\"/getting-started/\" description=\"First boot, the order the services come up in, and what to check once Coder answers.\" />"],[1540,"<Card title=\"Learn the stack\" href=\"/learn/\" description=\"Templates, model routing, the workspace agent, Hermes, conventions, branching.\" />"],[1541,"<Card title=\"Operate the box\" href=\"/operate/\" description=\"Dashboard and desktops, config refresh, public access, troubleshooting.\" />"],[1573,"> **Not the same as the `/ez-learn` command.** This tab is the concepts index for people\n> reading the docs. `/ez-learn` typed at the Grok Build prompt turns the session you are in\n> into durable memory - flush it into a session log, dream those logs into organized memory,\n> remember one note, or browse and clear the store; it is this stack's copy of the upstream\n> `/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`. Skill authoring is its siblings:\n> `/ez-skillify` captures the session that just worked and `/ez-create-skill` scaffolds one\n> from a description. `grok inspect` lists the three once their seed has landed. A missing\n> one is the skill-seed entry in troubleshooting.md."],[2984,"| #   | Criterion                                     | How you prove it                                             |\n| --- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 5   | Every environment has one home for its values | the file or overlay exists and no branch is needed to deploy |"],[2987,"| #   | Criterion                                | How you prove it                                                                            |\n| --- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 8   | The flag inventory is complete both ways | `grep -rn \"<FLAG>\" .` finds a reader for every declaration and a declaration for every read |"],[2981,"| #   | Criterion                               | How you prove it                                           |\n| --- | --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 2   | No branch holds commits the trunk lacks | `git rev-list --count origin/main..origin/<branch>` is `0` |"],[2983,"| #   | Criterion                           | How you prove it                              |\n| --- | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| 4   | Branch names and ages obey the rule | `git for-each-ref` over `refs/remotes/origin` |"],[2980,"| #   | Criterion                          | How you prove it               |\n| --- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| 1   | One trunk, no environment branches | `git ls-remote --heads origin` |"],[2986,"| #   | Criterion                         | How you prove it                                                     |\n| --- | --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 7   | Unfinished work is off by default | the flag's default reads as false in the sample dotenv or flags file |"],[2990,"| #   | Criterion                      | How you prove it                       |\n| --- | ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------- |\n| 11  | The repo's own gates are green | the gate command with its summary line |"],[2989,"| #   | Criterion                    | How you prove it                                                |\n| --- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 10  | The docs agree with the tree | branching doc, contributing, and agent rules say the same thing |"],[2985,"| #   | Criterion                  | How you prove it                                |\n| --- | -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| 6   | One artifact, many targets | the promotion command block, pasted and working |"],[2991,"| #   | Criterion             | How you prove it                                                                         |\n| --- | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 12  | Nothing was destroyed | no force-push, no unconfirmed remote delete, every proposed deletion carries its tip SHA |"],[2988,"| #   | Criterion            | How you prove it                              |\n| --- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| 9   | No committed secrets | `git ls-files` shows only sample dotenv names |"],[2982,"| #   | Criterion      | How you prove it                                                                     |\n| --- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 3   | Trunk is gated | the CI file names a job th