) |"],[628,"| Target   | What it runs                    |\n| -------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `//:fix` | buildifier and shfmt write mode |"],[268,"| Variable                        | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| ------------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical_machine_identity_id` | `\"\"`    | → `INFISICAL_MACHINE_IDENTITY_ID`. The machine identity the boot authenticates as - the id, never the token. It has no slot in any CLI argv, since the client id/secret are the credential, so it gates the fetch and records which identity the stack means (`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:10-13`). |"],[253,"| Variable                       | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_subagent_max_concurrent` | `null`  | `[subagents] max_concurrent`: how many subagents one session may have in flight. `null` takes `grok_swarm_width` above, so the cap is 15 on every lane: `main.tf` renders it as exactly `grok_subagent_max_concurrent = var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent != null ? var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent : var.grok_swarm_width` (`templates/workspace/main.tf:553`), with no lane test anywhere in that expression. An explicit value wins, and a validation keeps the override between 1 and 32 (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:64-72`); past the fleet's 15 streams a number deepens LiteLLM's queue rather than buying capacity. An explicit value persists in two places - on the template, from a raw `coder templates push --var`, and on the workspace, from `coder start --parameter` / `coder update --parameter` or the Variables tab (`--var` is a `coder templates push` flag alone; on Coder v2.36.6 `coder start --help` has no `--var`) - so a workspace's own value outlives every template version, while a value carried as a template version's default is replaced by the next push - `//scripts:push-template` sends no fan-out width of its own, so a re-push takes that parameter back to the `coder.tf` default. Which of the two holds the number is not visible from inside the container: both live in the Coder database, on the host, and neither answers the agent token. The check that reads the pair asks to be run again after the restart, because the number moving is the only proof either remedy had an effect. The render keeps the pair legible from in there - `# fleet-width: 15` sits directly above `max_concurrent` in `[subagents]`, and `bazelisk run //scripts:subagent-check` compares them and prints what it found - and stage 6 of `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl` prints the same mismatch on the startup log as it re-renders the file (Marker ownership of the seeded files). This is also the knob a pinned lane narrows: `agent` only ever reaches the one box its session key resolves to, whose `PARALLEL` (5 by default, `scripts/spark-configure.sh`) is the useful width, so setting it near 4 there keeps the session inside that box's streams instead of queueing behind them - an operator's choice, never the shipped default. Overflow is held, not failed (`limit_behavior = \"queue\"`), so overshoot costs a subagent latency rather than its life, and `sampling_limit` is left unset because it defaults to `max_concurrent`. |"],[270,"| Variable                  | Default           | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| ------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical_client_secret` | `\"\"`, `sensitive` | → `INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET`, and only when non-empty: it keeps its own conditional so an empty secret omits the key rather than handing the boot script a credential it would try to authenticate with (`templates/workspace/main.tf:654-657`). Never printed, and never interpolated into the body, which only names the variable (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:244-252`). |"],[254,"| Variable                  | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `grok_subagent_max_depth` | `2`     | `[subagents] max_depth`: how deep the subagent tree may go. `1` keeps nesting off - only the top-level session spawns - and that is the rollback. `2` is the shipped default: a child may spawn read-only mappers of its own, so a skeptic scopes out its own evidence and the parent skips a round trip. `3` is the operator's experiment ceiling. Nesting is bounded by the role definitions: only `reviewer`, `security-auditor` and `tester` name `spawn_subagent` in `tools`, so a depth-2 wave is read-only work and a mapper has no spawner to recurse with. Every in-flight child holds one of the fleet's 15 streams (3 Spark boxes x `PARALLEL` 5, `scripts/spark-configure.sh`), so a parent sums `1 + spawns` over its in-flight tasks and keeps that total at or below the fleet's stream count - the `fleet:` figure a micro-plan states, which is a separate figure from the session's `slots`. An over-admitted stream is refused by the box. `--var grok_subagent_max_depth=N` sets it for one workspace; same bounded-knob shape as the width above, and the same rendering pass (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:74-82`). |"],[272,"| Variable                  | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ------------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical_pull_on_start` | `false` | The second gate, under `infisical_enabled`, and it decides only whether a start *waits* on the vault. Forwarded only when both `.env` keys are truthy (`scripts/push-template.sh:636`, `:668-671`). Off leaves the script present but idle: one log line naming the gate and exit 0 (`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:27-30`). Handed over as the string `\"true\"`/`\"false\"`, because a `coder_agent` env value has to be a string and the body compares it against a literal (`templates/workspace/main.tf:649-652`). |"],[271,"| Variable                | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ----------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical_environment` | `prod`  | → `INFISICAL_ENVIRONMENT`. Which Infisical environment the export reads; the CLI takes `--env` on every read, so a fork that keeps another environment slug has no default that fits it (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:254-261`). |"],[267,"| Variable               | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ---------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical_project_id` | `\"\"`    | → `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID`. The project the export reads. Not a credential, but no read works without it, so an empty value disables the fetch the way an empty URL does (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:221-227`). |"],[248,"| Variable              | Default       | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| --------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_subagents_lane` | `agent-swarm` | The Grok catalog key the **writers** run on, rendered into `[subagents.models]` for `general-purpose` (the type a spawn gets when it names none, so every writer in a fan-out) and for the seeded `implementer` and `tester` definitions. It used to carry all three built-in types, which made the swarm lane a lie: the writers inherited the parent's pinned model and queued behind one box anyway. Each other role has a knob of its own now, so this one no longer moves the whole fleet by itself. A `--var` knob only, and the default ships the writers on the unpinned fleet lane, where the least-busy picker places a fan-out across all three boxes; `--var grok_subagents_lane=agent` is the rollback that puts them back on the same pinned lane as the parent session. It chooses placement only; the fan-out width below comes from `grok_swarm_width`, and no lane knob moves it. |"],[269,"| Variable              | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| --------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical_client_id` | `\"\"`    | → `INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_ID`. The Universal Auth client id; **not** sensitive, because it identifies the identity but cannot authenticate alone. `.env` spells this key the way the Infisical CLI reads it, which is the one name pair here that differs (`scripts/push-template.sh:709-714`). |"],[247,"| Variable             | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| -------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `grok_default_model` | `agent` | `agent` is the tool-capable Spark lane (thinking `xhigh` by default, picker `low` / `medium` / `xhigh`); `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` bake the level into the alias. |"],[249,"| Variable            | Default         | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------------------- | --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_explore_lane` | `agent-explore` | The Grok catalog key the read-only mappers run on: `[subagents.models]` for the built-in `explore` type and for the seeded `explorer` definition. `agent-explore` is the second Spark group with no LiteLLM session pin, thinking baked to `low`, so a wave of one-shot mappers is placed across all three boxes instead of queueing behind one box's streams - and the pin was never protecting anything here, because a mapper gets one turn and never re-reads its own prompt, which is what a warm prefix would have paid for. `--var grok_explore_lane=agent-low` keeps `low` with the pin back on; `=agent` sends mapping work at the parent's full depth. A validation block keeps the value one bare catalog key (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:28-31`). |"],[260,"| Variable            | Default  | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ------------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_profile_mode` | `shared` | `shared` mounts the owner's three Grok state volumes (`grok-notes-<owner-id>`, `grok-memory-<owner-id>`, `grok-sessions-<owner-id>`) at `~/.grok/notes`, `~/.grok/memory` and `~/.grok/sessions`, inside that workspace's own home volume; `private` mounts none of them and the workspace starts with an empty `~/.grok`. Also the default behind the per-workspace **Grok profile** dropdown; switching a live workspace between the two modes needs a rebuild. |"],[265,"| Variable            | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical_enabled` | `false` | The vault's switch, and the first of two gates. Off means the workspace cannot tell Infisical exists: no `INFISICAL_*` key in the agent env, no `coder_script.infisical_secrets` at all (`templates/workspace/main.tf:720-721`), no boot log line. Forwarded only as `--var infisical_enabled=true` for a truthy `.env` `INFISICAL_ENABLED` (`scripts/push-template.sh:636-638`), which is the deliberate inverse of `OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED` - that bundle ships on, so here an absent key is off (`scripts/push-template.sh:623-630`). All eight `infisical_*` variables exist in both twins (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:207-274`, `templates/devcontainer/coder.tf:194-261`). |"],[264,"| Variable            | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_config_extra` | `\"\"`    | The **Grok config extras** textarea: free-form TOML appended verbatim, under a comment header, to that workspace's rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`. |"],[263,"| Variable           | Default           | Notes                                                                                |\n| ------------------ | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `context7_api_key` | `\"\"`, `sensitive` | Handed to the workspace as `CONTEXT7_API_KEY`. The rendered file names the variable. |"],[251,"| Variable           | Default        | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------------------ | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_review_lane` | `agent-review` | The Grok catalog key the skeptics run on: `[subagents.models]` for `reviewer` and `security-auditor`, the two seeded definitions the mandatory independent-review phase of a multi-file change runs as. `agent-review` bakes thinking to `xhigh` and lifts the session pin the way `agent-swarm` does, so a wave of reviewers spreads over the fleet and each one thinks as hard as the parent session it reports to. It is also the single seam for the cost of that phase: `--var grok_review_lane=agent-medium`, with the alias in `litellm/config.yaml` moved with it, makes every review cheaper without touching the writers, the mappers or the planner. Same bare-key validation (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:48-51`). |"],[252,"| Variable           | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_swarm_width` | `15`    | The fleet's own fan-out width, and the number `[subagents] max_concurrent` takes whenever `grok_subagent_max_concurrent` is left `null` - which is how the template ships, so the cap is 15 on every lane, pinned or unpinned. The width is fleet arithmetic, not taste: 3 Spark boxes x `PARALLEL` 5 full-length streams (`scripts/spark-configure.sh`) is everything the fleet can hold at once. It is a plain default rather than a lane-conditional number because `agent-swarm` is always selectable - the `[model.\"agent-swarm\"]` block ships ungated in `grok-config.toml.tftpl`, so a session whose default lane is pinned can still name it on any subagent - and a config sized at one box's `PARALLEL` would cap a swarm the operator had only to ask for. Same 1..32 validation, and above the fleet's 15 streams a number deepens LiteLLM's queue rather than buying capacity (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:54-62`). It also travels into the rendered profile on its own, as the `# fleet-width: 15` line directly above `max_concurrent` inside `[subagents]` (`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:427`, fed by `grok_subagent_fleet_width = var.grok_swarm_width` at `templates/workspace/main.tf:558`), so a profile that a `--var` narrowed still shows the count the fleet was sized at. |"],[259,"| Variable           | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                 |\n| ------------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `additional_repos` | `\"\"`    | The **Git repositories (additional)** textarea under the repository field. Extra URLs cloned into their own folder beside `repo_url`. |"],[256,"| Variable          | Default                            | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `coder_agent_url` | `http://host.docker.internal:3002` | The URL the agent dials. Kept on the local plain listener so a public `CODER_ACCESS_URL` does not hairpin agent traffic through the reverse proxy. Empty keeps the provider-rendered access URL. |"],[250,"| Variable         | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| ---------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_plan_lane` | `agent` | The Grok catalog key the planner runs on: `[subagents.models]` for the built-in `plan` type and for the seeded `architect` definition. The one role lane that ships **pinned**, deliberately - a planner is a single conversation that re-reads what it just read and whose answer steers every wave after it, so stickiness and depth both pay where spreading would only re-prefill the same history. `--var grok_plan_lane=agent-medium` trades depth for turnaround on a busy fleet; `agent-xhigh` is the other way. Same bare-key validation (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:38-41`). |"],[266,"| Variable        | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| --------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `infisical_url` | `\"\"`    | Handed to the boot fetch as `INFISICAL_DOMAIN`: the base URL of the self-hosted instance, e.g. `http://host.docker.internal:8182`. The CLI dials Infisical Cloud when handed no domain, so an empty value disables the fetch rather than guessing one, and a self-hosted vault nobody names is a wrong-server bug (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:215-219`). `//scripts:push-template` composes the value as `http://host.docker.internal:<INFISICAL_PORT>` rather than forwarding `.env`'s `INFISICAL_URL`, because that key is the address the stack host dials and a workspace has to reach the host gateway instead (`scripts/push-template.sh:652-661`). |"],[255,"| Variable        | Default | Notes                                                         |\n| --------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `docker_socket` | `\"\"`    | Optional Docker socket URI; empty means the provider default. |"],[262,"| Variable       | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| -------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `context7_mcp` | `true`  | Wires the cached `context7` MCP stdio server into Grok's rendered `config.toml`. The key is optional, so it ships without one. Set `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` and re-push to drop the block. |"],[245,"| Variable      | Default                               | Notes                                    |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `litellm_url` | `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1` | Reached through the Docker host gateway. |"],[246,"| Variable      | Default           | Notes                                        |\n| ------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `litellm_key` | `\"\"`, `sensitive` | Handed to the workspace as `LITELLM_API_KEY` |"],[261,"| Variable     | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `github_mcp` | `true`  | Wires the `github` MCP HTTP server into Grok's rendered `config.toml`. Its `Authorization` header names `$GH_TOKEN` and nothing else, so no token value is ever part of a template variable. Set `GITHUB_MCP=false` and re-push to drop the block. |"],[258,"| Variable   | Default                                                                                                                                                 | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `repo_url` | `\"\"` in `workspace`, `https://github.com/coder/coder` in `devcontainer` (Terraform); `.env.sample` `REPO_URL` overrides both to this stack's GitHub URL | Prefills the **Git repository** field. Both templates clone that URL on the first start; a later start leaves a folder that already holds a `.git` alone. `REPO_URL` in `.env` overrides the default. The sample value is this stack's own repo so **VS Code Web** opens `~/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`. |"],[244,"| Variable | Default             | Notes                                                                                                                       |\n| -------- | ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `image`  | `evo-t1-dev:latest` | Seeds the **Workspace image** dropdown default. In `devcontainer` it decides nothing about the toolchain inside the editor. |"],[257,"| Variable | Default | Notes                                                                                           |\n| -------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dind`   | `false` | Turns on the per-workspace privileged daemon described below. Does not exist in `devcontainer`. |"],[2183,"| Verb          | What it does                                                                                                    | Writes     |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| `install-cli` | Fetch the pinned release (`INFISICAL_CLI_VERSION`) and install the binary. Never pipes a download into a shell. | the binary |"],[2187,"| Verb         | What it does                                                          | Writes                 |\n| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| `rotate KEY` | Mint a fresh value for one managed key, vault first, env file second. | the vault, then `.env` |"],[2188,"| Verb         | What it does                                                  | Writes  |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `run -- CMD` | Exec a command with the project's secrets in its environment. | nothing |"],[2184,"| Verb        | What it does                                                                                                                          | Writes                    |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `bootstrap` | Create the super admin, the organization and the machine identity on a fresh instance, then record the identifiers into the env file. | the instance, then `.env` |"],[2181,"| Verb     | What it does                                                                                                                                                           | Writes  |\n| -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `status` | What this box is configured to do: the flag, the profile argv, the port, the vault URL, the inventory size, and which required keys are still empty. Contacts nothing. | nothing |"],[2182,"| Verb    | What it does                                                                                                                                                              | Writes  |\n| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `check` | `status` plus whether the CLI binary is installed, whether the required keys are all set, and whether the machine identity can actually exchange a token. Run this first. | nothing |"],[2185,"| Verb   | What it does                                                                                                            | Writes    |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| `push` | Seed or migrate the managed keys from the env file into the vault, then read the vault back and compare a hash per key. | the vault |"],[2186,"| Verb   | What it does                                                 | Writes |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------ |\n| `pull` | Reconcile the managed keys from the vault into the env file. | `.env` |"],[3029,"| Verdict         | Test                        | What you do                                     |\n| --------------- | --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| release-bearing | carries an untagged version | tag the tip, push the tag, then list the branch |"],[3025,"| Verdict    | Test                                     | What you do                                  |\n| ---------- | ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| superseded | its files exist on trunk in another form | list for deletion, citing what superseded it |"],[3027,"| Verdict  | Test                    | What you do                          |\n| -------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| preserve | valuable and incomplete | port behind a flag that defaults off |"],[3024,"| Verdict | Test                                                            | What you do       |\n| ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| merged  | `rev-list --count` is 0, or `git cherry` marks every commit `-` | list for deletion |"],[3028,"| Verdict | Test                              | What you do                       |\n| ------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| dead    | no value, or explicitly abandoned | list for deletion with the reason |"],[3026,"| Verdict | Test                          | What you do                                                 |\n| ------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| port    | small, complete, still wanted | re-adapt onto a new short-lived branch, open a pull request |"],[2048,"| What                | Where                                                                                                                                                                               | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| The three exporters | `node_exporter` on `127.0.0.1:9101` (`OBS_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT`), `cadvisor` on `127.0.0.1:9290` (`OBS_CADVISOR_PORT`), `blackbox_exporter` on `127.0.0.1:9115` (`OBS_BLACKBOX_PORT`) | Prometheus reaches all three by service name on the compose network; the loopback mapping exists so you can read a metric off the box itself. None reaches the LAN, which matters most for blackbox: it is a general-purpose outbound HTTP prober. |"],[2047,"| What         | Where                                             | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Alertmanager | `http://127.0.0.1:9093` (`OBS_ALERTMANAGER_PORT`) | Loopback on purpose. Its UI silences alerts and the service ships no auth, so an Alertmanager reachable by the whole LAN is not something to hand a network. Read silences here from a shell on the stack host, or through Grafana's Alerting pages. |"],[2042,"| What       | Where                                       | Notes                                                                        |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Prometheus | `http://127.0.0.1:9090` (`PROMETHEUS_PORT`) | `:2112` metrics port. Reading a graph there needs a shell on the stack host. |"],[2041,"| What    | Where                                                                                                  | Notes                                                                                                                                                          |\n| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Grafana | `http://<lan>:3005` (`GRAFANA_PORT`), and `grafana-<suffix>.<base>` when `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` names it | The only published name in the bundle. Login is `GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER` / `GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD` from `.env`; anonymous access and analytics reporting are off. |"],[2045,"| What  | Where                                                     | Notes                             |\n| ----- | --------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Tempo | nothing published - `tempo:3200` query, `tempo:4317` OTLP | Reach it through Grafana Explore. |"],[2043,"| What  | Where                                                   | Notes                                          |\n| ----- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| Mimir | nothing published - `mimir:8080` on the compose network | Reach it through Grafana's `mimir` datasource. |"],[2046,"| What  | Where                             | Notes                                                                              |\n| ----- | --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Alloy | nothing published - `alloy:12345` | The component graph UI stays inside the network; read the container's log instead. |"],[2044,"| What | Where                           | Notes                             |\n| ---- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Loki | nothing published - `loki:3100` | Reach it through Grafana Explore. |"],[1359,"1 and 2 are the Coder agent's *default* discovery paths."],[1361,"1-4 are written by `startup.sh.tftpl`. 5-10 are the stack's three skill commands and are\nwritten by `coder_script.stack_skills`, whose own `execve` carries the three payloads;\nthey ride their own script body rather than the startup argument because that argument\nhas a single-argument ceiling of its own\n(coder-templates.md). Each `.agents`\ncopy is a `cp` of the managed `.coder` copy, never a second inline body, so the pair\ncannot drift."],[1362,"11-26 are the four commands of that second family - the three repository conversions\nplus the `/ez-micro-plan` dispatcher - and are written by\n`coder_script.stack_repo_skills`, a second body with its own ceiling, for the same\nreason and because this family also ships the python helpers 13-14, 16, 19-20 and 25-26\nthat three of its four bodies invoke by `~/.agents` path. Same absent-only contract and\nthe same `cp` twin rule; the helper directories are made by the body, so a workspace that\nalready has the `SKILL.md` but lost a helper still gets the helper back."],[2696,"127 is the shell saying the file it was told to exec is not there. Both editor terminal\nprofiles exec a launcher under `~/.grok/bin`, and `coder_script \"terminal_launchers\"`\n(`templates/common/terminal-launchers.sh.tftpl`) is what writes those two files. The\nprofile, `tmux` and the tab are all fine; the file is missing. The same message naming\n`grok-build-terminal` is this same fault in the other profile, and the one naming\nneither is the exit code 1 case above,\nwhere the file exists and tmux refuses."],[1360,"3 and 4 are the same text under the Grok Build CLI's convention, and the CLI is\nwhat `execute`-lane delegation drives. The chat agent dedupes skills by name,\nfirst match wins, home scope before a working-dir path, so one agent never loads a\nskill twice: the twins exist for the CLI, not as a second source for chat."],[1363,"5 and 6 are `/ez-learn`: this stack's copy of the upstream memory commands `/flush`,\n`/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`. It turns the current session into durable facts -\nflush the conversation into a session log under the workspace's `~/.grok/memory/`\ndirectory, dream those logs into an organized `MEMORY.md`, save one note, or browse and\nclear what was stored. Reaching past those logs into the raw transcripts under\n`~/.grok/sessions/` is opt-in depth, done only when the operator asks for it. Its\ndefault mode is report-only, and it writes memory files, never a skill. 7 and 8 are\n`/ez-skillify`, which turns the session that just worked into a skill from its own\ntranscript, and 9 and 10 are `/ez-create-skill`, which scaffolds one from what the\noperator describes. The three payloads are\n`templates/common/ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md`, on the same absent-only contract. `/ez-create-skill` stands\nin for the bundled platform skill `create-skill`, which arrives only in the skills\nbundle behind an x.ai sign-in this stack does not have; `/ez-skillify` has no published\nupstream counterpart at all; and the memory family `/ez-learn` copies is upstream\n*built-ins*: `/flush`, `/dream` and `/memory` are gated behind cross-session memory,\nwhich is off by default, while `/remember` is a pager command with no such gate. Either\nway the seed is what makes the three commands exist. Each ships under its own `ez-`\nname to stay clear of those upstream names, since a user-scope skill overrides a\nsame-named bundled one and a seed named `create-skill` would hide the bundled copy on a\nworkspace that ever does sign in. The boot and the reseed delete a leftover\nmarker-carrying `skills/learn/SKILL.md` from before the rename. See the CLI section\nabove."],[448,"A **GitHub reauth** external app (`order = 7`, `/icon/github.svg`) opens Coder's\n`/external-auth/<id>` page in a slim window on the user's Coder session: connect, or\n**Reauthenticate** when the account is already linked. It stays on the main agent so\na failed clone (or a `devcontainer` subagent that never starts) still leaves the\nbutton on the workspace page. Device-flow login is not the App install: with the\ninjected App (id `github`) that page prints \"You must install the GitHub App to\nclone private repositories\" as unlinked plaintext, because Coder omits\n`AppInstallURL` on the default provider."],[2640,"A `~/.grok/config.toml` carrying neither the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` line\nnor the `__evoT1Stack = true` key is a file the stack stopped managing, so neither\nthe startup script nor the seed writes it again and its default lanes never arrive.\nThe usual cause is not a hand edit: `grok mcp add` persists its own settings by\nrewriting that file and deletes every comment line, which used to revoke stack\nownership by itself. The TOML key is what survives that write, so a config rendered\nby the current templates keeps its marker through `grok mcp add`. Take the file\nover deliberately by removing both lines. To give it back: delete the file and\nrestart the workspace, or re-materialize it with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --force`."],[2655,"A `404` is what this stack answers for an unknown app slug or an agent that is not\nConnected; the agent is the text after the first `.` in `<ws>.<agent>`."],[219,"A `DEV_IMAGE` that is not in the local image store is a note, not a stop: build it\nfirst with `bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`."],[45,"A `fix/…` branch that also has to rename a template directory, retarget a `.bats` suite, and\nupdate `docs/` is one change: the rename is not green until the last reference follows.\nWhen a rename cannot be proven complete, the reference stays (a rename is only \"done\" when\nthe old name is gone outside historical docs) and the CI file says so in a comment."],[276,"A `github_mcp` of `true` with no GitHub account connected gives that workspace a server\nthat fails its handshake once per session, because `$GH_TOKEN` is empty. A\n`context7_mcp` of `true` with an empty `context7_api_key` is *not* the same shape:\nthe key is optional, the block renders either way, and the server handshakes and\nanswers without one - no `Authorization` header goes out, so the lane runs at the\npublic rate limit and private libraries are the only thing out of reach. That keyless\nblock is the pushed default and what a fresh deploy gets, since nothing in git\ncarries a key, and `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` only warns about it. Park a\nlane deliberately with `GITHUB_MCP=false` / `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed\nby `push-template.sh`) or `--var github_mcp=false` / `--var context7_mcp=false` on\nthe push; a block missing from a render that got neither of those is a defect, not a\npark. Fill the context7 key with `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` in `.env` plus a re-push when a\nsession needs private libraries or a higher rate limit."],[2680,"A `learn` directory still listed by the last `ls` is a copy someone took over: the boot\nand the reseed delete that path only while it still carries the stack marker, and a\nmarker-free file is user-owned, so it stays. A marker-carrying leftover is retired on\nthe next boot or reseed, and that removal only finishes the rename: no published source\nnames an upstream `/learn` for the name to free."],[1040,"a `search` widget with `provider: custom` carries a url ending in `=`, because v2.4.0\nappends the encoded query to whatever `url` says and has no `{{query}}` token"],[1033,"a `siteMonitor` or widget `url` names a compose service or a token, never a loopback\nor LAN-address literal, which would bake the LAN into git"],[913,"A `variable` block is the only place a Coder template can declare what an input is,\nand an undeclared one takes whatever HCL guessed. `search_mcp = \"flase\"` then reaches\na `== \"true\"` comparison and reads as false, so the operator gets a workspace that\ncame up wrong and a plan that said nothing. A declared constraint turns that into a\nplan-time error."],[2619,"A `warning:` that a stored model config is disabled, is not the default, or compacts\nagainst the wrong window names a row this script found already in place. It reads\nbefore it writes and this surface has no PATCH, so the correction is the one click in\nAdmin -> Experiments -> Agent Chat the line names."],[2724,"A `ws-...` value means Terraform rendered it; `contributor` means\n`bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env` did, and that path takes the lane from the\n`GROK_SUBAGENTS_LANE` environment variable (`scripts/seed-dev-env.sh`), defaulting to\n`agent-swarm`, and the ceiling from `GROK_SWARM_WIDTH` (`scripts/seed-dev-env.sh:185`),\nso a contributor home carries the same pair."],[280,"A 0 default TTL leaves the checkbox unchecked and leaves `coder create` with nothing to\ninherit, so a workspace runs until its owner stops it."],[1507,"A bind-mounted skill needs no recreate when the agent reloads skills from disk; start a\n**new session** - a fresh Chat tab, or a new `hermes-shell` run - and the new skill is\noffered. Recreate only if it still does not show up, since `restart` keeps the old\nmounts."],[408,"A body takes the library as one bare line under its `set -u`. Terraform does not\nre-interpolate a map value, so the library's own dollar-brace shell text lands verbatim -\nno here-doc, no temp file, no second copy to drift. That is also why the escape rule below\nis absolute rather than a style preference: Terraform reads an interpolation token wherever\nit sits, a `.tftpl` comment included, so no comment in one of these files may spell one."],[1038,"a bookmark's value is a list of single-key mappings carrying both `abbr` and `href`;\nwritten as a plain mapping the entry loads with a name and nothing else, because the\napp drops the keys it did not ask for"],[2426,"A box that answers prints `ok:` lines instead of that warning - docker available with\nthe probe version, `node_exporter is running on :<port> (<image>)`, `DCGM exporter is\nrunning on :<port>, tag <image>`, and a `note:` with the counter count, the GPU count and\nthe models (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:612-644`). **Not verified from a live box**: no\nSpark was reachable from where these commands ran, so the answering-`box` table above is\nquoted from those source lines, not from an observed run. The unreachable form is what\nwas actually measured."],[1834,"A box with no DHCP gets the raw initramfs form, which is passed through\nuntouched because `initramfs-tools` parses it, not this CLI:"],[1303,"A built dev container sees neither the volume nor that overlay, so it is the one\nplace the seed is **baked** rather than layered: the inlined\n`coder_script.code_server` fork bakes a base64 copy of it and writes it over the\ndev container's private `User/settings.json` (or merges over a copy that survived a\nrebuild, when `jq` is on PATH; without `jq` the existing file is kept). Edits\ninside a built dev container therefore survive neither a rebuild nor a look at the\nshared file. Everywhere else no settings map is passed at all, because a merge\nwould replace the symlink."],[464,"A built dev container sees neither the volume nor that overlay, so it is the one place\nthe seed is baked rather than layered."],[1696,"A busy pinned box sheds its own overflow in its own scheduler instead of queueing\ninside the proxy."],[1032,"a card `href` is a token or a public URL, never a compose service name: the browser\ncannot resolve `http://coder:3000`, only the dashboard server can"],[1018,"A card per service, with status pulled from each service's own API over the compose\nnetwork - `http://coder:3000`, `http://litellm:4000`, `http://ollama:11434`,\n`http://hermes:9119/api/status` - so nothing here needs a daemon credential. The\nheader carries boxed tiles for host CPU, memory, CPU temperature, disk and `eth0`\nthroughput, beside the clock (date and 24-hour time), local weather (open-meteo,\nkeyless) and a search box."],[2620,"A chat that starts but narrates instead of calling tools is the no-Spark case above."],[1317,"A chat turn needs an enabled chat model config - the chat's own pick, else the\ndeployment default, else it errors before the first tool call - and that wiring is\nwhat the script writes. It mints a short-lived admin API token from the stack's own\nPostgres (`token_name 'stack-coder-agents'`, deleted by the trap, never printed) and\ndrives the server's experimental chat API:"],[2557,"A checkout that does not yet contain that `--repair` (the usual case: `git pull`\nis already failing) needs the same commands by hand, then pull:"],[1222,"A child's type names its definition: `spawn_subagent` offers project and user agents in\nits `subagent_type` enum, which is how a parent asks for `reviewer` by name\n(`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:69-71`). A depth-1 child that spawns resolves\nits own children through these same layers, so a grandchild reaches the reader lane\nthrough the one `grok_explore_lane` pin and needs nothing set on the spawner."],[438,"A CLI error is scrubbed before it is printed. The CLI can echo the bearer token back in an\nerror line, and that line lands in the workspace's script log, so both the token and the\nclient secret are replaced with `<token>` / `<secret>` on their way to stderr\n(`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:68-77`)."],[2364,"a Coder certificate whose DNS SANs now carry the public names alongside the existing\nones. The LAN cert keeps working; in public mode the browser never sees it, since\nTraefik presents real ones;"],[2356,"a comma-separated list is used verbatim, which is how you pin a name by hand (leave\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE` empty, or that key keeps re-deriving)."],[1197,"A contributor home gets the same seam from the environment of the seed, so a laptop\nmatches what a template `--var` renders:"],[1695,"A conversation that goes quiet for longer than the TTL may move."],[1691,"A cooled box therefore stays visibly out of `healthy_deployments` instead of blending\ninto the group's aggregate stats."],[2501,"A copy from an app inside a tmux pane never reaches the laptop clipboard in VS Code Web -> the browser build drops OSC 52"],[2714,"A current workspace clears these at boot by itself. `grok_sweep_config_swaps` in the\n`agents-seed.sh.tftpl` body moves a swap of that name into `~/.grok/stale-swaps/` once\nnothing in the container holds it open and it is over an hour old. The boot log prints\nthe archived name, and `vim -r` on that path gets the bytes back for a month; the files\nin there are named `sweep-...orphan...` and only those names are ever pruned, so a file\nyou put in that directory yourself stays. Two reasons the sweep may stay silent, both\nworth checking before you blame it: the config lost its stack marker, since the sweep\ndeliberately stands down on an unmanaged config (`grep -cE\n'managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack|__evoT1Stack = true' ~/.grok/config.toml` answers it, and\neither spelling is enough; `grok mcp add` rewriting the file is the usual cause, and\n`bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --force` is the fix), or the workspace predates\nthe body, which a `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` and one restart settles."],[447,"A custom **Web Terminal** command-app at `order = 6` takes the terminal slot. Custom\napps are ordered among themselves; static helper buttons have no `order` or `icon` knob\nin this Coder release and are not reorderable."],[1934,"A dashboard is a committed file"],[2642,"A declared context7 server: an empty `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is the shipped default and\ndoes *not* fail the handshake. With no value the server sends no `Authorization`\nheader, so it handshakes and answers at the public rate limit, and only private\nlibraries are out of reach. A key in `.env` plus a re-push fills the env. When a\ncontext7 handshake really fails the key is not the cause: run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` and it says which of the block, the binary, the\ncache config or the handshake is not OK."],[2641,"A declared github server that fails handshake: the owner has not connected\nGitHub External Auth, so `$GH_TOKEN` is empty. Use the **GitHub reauth** app,\nthen start a new Grok session."],[2639,"A declared server whose `command=` the box cannot find is image drift: the\ntemplates declare a binary the running image never got, because it predates the\nnpm bake in `images/dev/Dockerfile`. Rebuild `evo-t1-dev` and recreate the\nworkspace, or install the pinned package into the running box (`npm i -g --prefix\n/opt/npm-global searxng-mcp-server@<pin>`, the pins live in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`)."],[3049,"A default-branch rename is its own confirmed step, done after the content has converged,\nbecause GitHub rewrites links, protections and open pull requests when it happens."],[208,"A dev stack that moved the search port needs its own template push: `searxng_url`\nis derived from `STACK_SEARCH_PORT` as read from `.env`, so a push made while\n`dev-stack.env` carries `18181` still names `8181` unless the key is set in `.env`\ntoo. A workspace dials the host gateway, where the compose network does not help,\nso the port in the rendered `SEARXNG_URL` has to be the one actually published."],[2548,"A failed bind from an older runfiles compose file can also leave a **directory**\n(or a root-owned file) at `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg` inside\n`bazel-out/.../prod-stack.runfiles`. The next `bazelisk run` then dies before\nthe stack script starts; that spelling is the next heading."],[425,"A failed clone is loud and non-fatal. The workspace still boots, an empty editor folder\nis fixable from a terminal, git removes its own partial clone on failure, and nothing in\nthe script deletes a folder that may hold someone's work."],[153,"A failed description edit and a failed autostop pin only add a note; neither blocks the\ntemplate."],[727,"A file carrying the stack marker - the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment, the\n`\"__evoT1Stack\": true` JSON key, or the `__evoT1Stack = true` TOML key - is\nstack-owned and is reseeded when the seed in this repo drifted. Taking\n`~/.grok/config.toml` over means deleting both its marker lines, since the Grok CLI\ndeletes comments when it rewrites that file:\n../grok-build-and-agents.md."],[2255,"A file that holds one is mode 600 and is removed on the failure path too, by the `EXIT`\ntrap the seam installs - export, push payload, verify read-back and rotate value all go\nthrough it. bash keeps one `EXIT` handler, so the seam captures whatever the caller had\ninstalled and runs it after its own temps are gone; what an entry script must never do is\ntake that slot back after sourcing the seam, which would strand a file of secrets on the\nfailure path."],[842,"A file the graph does not know is a warning naming that path, and the usual cause is a file you just\ncreated that no filegroup claims yet. `owners` answers \"is this visible to the harness at all\":"],[1273,"a file written in the last two seconds is left for the next tick, because copying it\nwhole could archive a half-saved file. Going the other way, two guards stop a recreate from\nerasing notes: a live file that is empty or still holds only the seeded stub never replaces\na canonical copy carrying real notes, and a real canonical copy restores over such a stub\nin a clone. Before any substantive canonical copy is replaced it is archived as\n`notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/backups/notes-YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS.md`, keeping the newest\n`NOTES_BACKUP_KEEP` (10 by default), and that directory is where an operator recovers notes\nafter an accident. `grok_profile_mode=private` mounts no shared volume, so those notes die\nwith that workspace; `shared` is the default. Like the memory tree, the last workspace\nstarted for a given user and repository wins."],[2681,"A file you deleted or edited on purpose is not a fault. To keep your own version,\nedit it and delete the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker line: a marker-free\nfile is user-owned, and the seeder leaves it alone unless you pass `--force`. A\ndeletion is undone - the workspace boot writes the file again whenever it is absent,\nand `refresh-configs` re-seeds an absent target, so the next boot restores the\nstack's copy. Copy the payload back from `templates/common/<name>.SKILL.md` to give\nthe file back to the stack."],[3016,"A flag service, an SDK, or a database-backed flag table is a decision an operator makes\nexplicitly. It is not the default for a home lab or a single-node host, and it is never what a\nfirst pass should add."],[424,"A folder that already holds a `.git` is left alone: re-fetching is the user's call,\nbecause a pull there could conflict mid-edit or fail on a dirty tree, and either way the\nworkspace would start on code nobody chose."],[1279,"A fresh named volume arrives `root:root` while the agent runs as `coder`, so the\nstage chowns the mount point to the home owner first, exactly as the `~/.grok` and\n`~/.shared` volumes above do."],[1073,"A GMKtec EVO-T1 (or any box with the Arc 140T and enough RAM for the model in\n`OLLAMA_MODELS`) with Docker and Compose v2 installed."],[2684,"A golden image that predates a pin bump reports an older `/usr/local/bin/grok` than\n`GROK_VERSION` (1.0.77 at the time of writing), and rebuilding `images/dev`\n(`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`) is the CLI upgrade path - but the seeded skill commands\nwork on either CLI, so this symptom alone never justifies an image rebuild."],[1327,"A Grok Build session logging `compact failed: exceeded wall-clock budget 300s\n(runaway generation)` is the session-model summary hanging before its first token on\nthe lane that also serves the whole agent loop. Its only live bounds are\n`GROK_COMPACTION_WALL_CLOCK_SECS`, rendered into the agent `env` because no TOML table\ntakes it, and the proxy's `request_timeout`; troubleshooting.md\nowns the measurement and the retry split that stops one hang being bought three times."],[2240,"a half-seeded project must not lock every service out of its own credential."],[2937,"A hand-typed `docker compose ... up -d` selects no profile at all, flag or not -\nprofiles are per command, which is exactly what the runners add. A service the profile\ndid create also keeps running after the flag goes off until you stop it."],[2647,"A hit in the first means Coder tried the upstream and gave up, so the fault sits on\nthis box: check the devcontainer or workspace container first, then `DOCKER_HOST`."],[2257,"A key the vault does not carry is a warning, never a blanked line."],[1297,"A lane an agent needs fails in exactly three shapes, and that check tells them\napart, so run it before editing anything:"],[498,"A live `coder external-auth access-token <id>` mint, on demand. Not a helper: it is\nwhat `github-auth fix` calls, and what a workspace shell can call, whenever a token has\nto be certainly fresh."],[1039,"a local `icon:` path is under `/icons/`, the one path the auth middleware exempts from\nthe sign-in redirect"],[2663,"A log that stops mid-boot is a spent budget, not lost scrollback. One agent's whole stream -\n`startup_script` plus every `coder_script` on it - is 1 MiB, a Postgres check on\n`workspace_agents.logs_length`\n(dump.sql, v2.36.6).\nWhen it fills the agent's uploader drops the NEWEST lines\n(`codersdk/agentsdk/logs.go`,\n`maxBytesQueued = 1048576`, the `log queue full; truncating new logs` path), and once the\nserver answers `LogLimitExceeded: true` nothing further is uploaded. So the head of the boot\nsurvives and the tail is what you lost: read from the top, and treat a body whose `summary:`\nis missing at the bottom of the log as unproven rather than as a failure - it may have\nfinished after the budget ran out."],[1262,"A managed path that is a **symlink** is left alone in both families. The marker test\nreads the file a path points at, so writing through a link would replace a file the\nstack does not own - a link aimed at `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` would have the seed overwrite\nthe rulebook. A link a person planted stays theirs, and one symlinked root stops the\nseed before its first write."],[728,"A marker-free file is user-owned and stays untouched unless `--force` says otherwise.\nThe terminal helpers and `notes-setup.sh` are the exception: startup rewrites those on\nevery boot, so this script does too."],[723,"A missing `[mcp_servers.context7]` block in that file means the seed ran keyless and\nwithout `--context7-mcp`: re-run the seeder with the flag, adding `--force` when the\nexisting file carries no stack marker and the seeder left it alone. On a Coder\nworkspace the same repair runs from the stack host:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>`."],[2638,"A missing `[mcp_servers.github]` or `[mcp_servers.context7]` in\n`~/.grok/config.toml` means the last push passed `--var ...=false`, or the\nworkspace started from an older template, or the file carries no stack marker at all\n(next bullet). No sibling can blank it: `~/.grok/config.toml` is this workspace's own\nfile, so an absent block is this workspace's own render, not another workspace's.\nClear the gate and re-push, then re-materialize the file and restart the workspace so\nthe managed `config.toml` re-renders. Two routes re-materialize it:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>` from the\nstack host, for a running workspace container, and\n`bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --home <dir>` for a host home (a laptop, CI,\nor the contributor container)."],[217,"A missing `docker`, `timeout`, `tar` or `openssl`, a stack that is not running, or no\nactive Coder account is a *skip*: a one-line reason and exit 0. `bootstrap.sh` calls\nthis script, and a fresh clone has none of those prerequisites yet."],[299,"A missing image fails the workspace build at **plan** time, before anything is\ncreated, with `did not find docker image 'docker:29.8.1-dind'` - see\ntroubleshooting.md."],[965,"A native `bazelisk test` on macOS is convenience only: the BSD userland differs from the\nGNU one in the image (`sed`, `date`, `find`, `sort`), so a red run there is not proof of a\ndefect and a green one is not the run that unlocks the merge button. CI and branch\nprotection gate on the image result either way."],[845,"A new `scripts/*.sh` is a Bazel target automatically (the package is generated from the directory),\nbut it is not wired automatically: it needs a `_SCRIPT_LIBS` key (the `scripts/lib` closure it\nsources) and a `_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD` key (the payload groups it opens), both in `scripts/BUILD.bazel`.\nUntil those keys land the graph does not know the file at all, which is worth saying out loud:\n`rdeps` reads the graph, not the filesystem."],[1773,"A new alias is a client catalog entry as well as a proxy entry. Both `templates/*/main.tf`\ntwins carry one `{value, name, description}` object per alias in `local.model_choices`,\nwhich is what the workspace create form offers for `grok_default_model`, and both\n`templates/*/grok-config.toml.tftpl` twins carry its `[model.\"<alias>\"]` block. An alias\nthe config has and the twins do not is an alias no workspace ever lists."],[1731,"A new group inherits neither the retry policy nor the affinity lift. Both fast lanes\ndeclare their own six-key `model_group_retry_policy` block, and only `agent-swarm-fast`\njoins `model_group_affinity_config` with `[]`; `agent-xhigh-fast` is absent from that map\non purpose, so it keeps falling back to the global `[\"session_affinity\"]` and stays\npinned like its parent. That one key is the whole difference between the pinned twin and\nthe spread one."],[844,"A new top-level payload directory belongs in its own package with its own `filegroup` targets, joins\n`//:stack_data` by label, and is named in the map of every target that reads it, as\nbuilding-with-bazel.md spells out. `unlisted` is how you find out you have\nnot done that, rather than finding out that the gate never noticed."],[2886,"A part carrying an extra dot is the other failure this table cannot catch: the\ncomposed name sits more than one label under the zone, the wildcard does not cover\nit, and the browser aborts with `ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` before Traefik\nis ever reached, so no `421` is ever printed."],[2357,"A part that cannot be a DNS label composes nothing: that service is left out of the list\nrather than sent as an empty entry, and a missing zone keeps the whole stack LAN-only."],[841,"A per-tree file whose sibling tree has no counterpart is a `warning:` naming the group that claims\nit: that asymmetry is the drift worth knowing about before you edit, not after."],[347,"A persistent per-workspace home volume (`coder-<wsid>-home`)."],[1044,"A plain `restart` keeps the generated page, so day-to-day YAML edits need nothing.\nNew asset files do need a recreate (`up -d`), since `restart` keeps the old mounts."],[3032,"A port that also reformats is two pull requests. A port of a whole branch at once is a review\nnobody does properly."],[482,"A private extra repository still has to be GRANTED to the connected GitHub account, or\nits clone is refused, just like the primary one."],[2297,"A probe-style CLI takes its target as a flag (`--url`) rather than a hard-coded host, and reports\n\"nothing answered\" as a result rather than a crash, so one run works from the host and from inside\na workspace and still answers the question the operator asked. Its suite stubs the transport."],[1983,"A profile decides which services a command *selects*; it does not delete what is already\nrunning. The ten containers stay up until you stop them, so after flipping the key off\neither take the project down (`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- down`) or delete the\nten by name through compose. Their named volumes survive either way, and `reset` is the\none that destroys them."],[744,"A pull request that does not touch `images/dev/**` replays the cached layers instead of\nrebuilding them, which is what keeps the gate's wall time flat."],[1517,"A push channel is an inbound door even when you only use it to listen. Every\npublisher to an ntfy topic is one identity to the gateway, so the push-only\nposture - `NTFY_TOPIC` and `NTFY_PUBLISH_TOPIC` set, `NTFY_ALLOWED_USERS` not -\nis the one this doc recommends, and the topic name is the secret."],[184,"A push is also the only thing that *registers* a template: Coder stores the source as a\nFilestore row, not as files it scans on disk."],[927,"A Python file that lands outside the runner's list is unchecked code that looks\ngated, which is the failure `//tests:bats_mypy_lint_test` exists to catch: it\nenumerates the tree and fails on any `.py` the target list does not cover, with\n`tests/bats/fixtures/` the one sanctioned exclusion because that tree is scaffold\npayload typed in the repository it is copied into."],[871,"A reachability proof, for when you must know that a gate really does sit on top of a file:"],[1321,"a read-only drift check on the stored configs: a config that is disabled, is not\nthe deployment default, or carries a `context_limit` that does not match the alias's\nserving window is reported with a `warning:` and the screen to fix it on. The\nexperimental chat API has no PATCH for model configs, so these are named rather than\nwritten."],[2915,"A refused or failing `remote_write` (target `http://mimir:8080/api/v1/push`) shows up\nhere, and an ingester that refuses a block shows up in\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs --tail 200 mimir`."],[2060,"A refused or failing `remote_write` shows up in the Prometheus log. The clean version of\nthe same question is asked in the UI: the `prometheus` datasource has the series and the\n`mimir` one does not, which is a write fault, not a query fault."],[1633,"A rename or deletion is the edit that can reach workspaces: both\n`templates/*/grok-config.toml.tftpl` twins render the alias list into\n`~/.grok/config.toml`, a running workspace keeps the copy it rendered at start, and\nthe next start re-renders it."],[170,"A repeated name is one push, not two, and the push order stays `TEMPLATES_DEFAULT`\norder whatever the argument order is. Naming `devcontainer` first cannot orphan it."],[2787,"A repo that ships none wants the **Plain clone** preset, or the dev-container toggle\nset to **No** at create time. Either one runs code-server on the plain workspace\ncontainer and opens the clone there."],[316,"A repository that ships no `devcontainer.json` gets no dev container and hence no\neditor with the toggle on. The toggle - or\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <git-url>`, which reads the repository and\npicks for you - is how that case is meant to be handled."],[1676,"A request carrying `tools` for `qwen2.5-coder:32b` or `:14b` comes back as plain text,\n`finish_reason: \"stop\"`, and no `tool_calls` field at all."],[2720,"A review panel that arrives one at a time is the same queueing. The panel is three\nskeptics dispatched in one turn - requirements, proof, adversary - each on\n`agent-review`, each owning no file, each allowed up to 3 read-only mappers of its own,\nand all of them want the fleet's streams at once. While writers are still in flight the\npanel waits behind them, so dispatch it once the writers have landed."],[2377,"A router per name pointing at `http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080`, including\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` and `grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`. That backend\nis `:8080` with the original Host, the same as the other names - not `:9119` and not\n`:3005`. A router aimed at a container port skips this\nbox's vhost. The file in Traefik configuration is that\nconfig; diff what the box runs against it."],[1775,"A routing hook adds two artifacts beyond the alias itself. `litellm/compaction_router.py`\nis a payload file beside the config, bind-mounted single-file as\n`./litellm/compaction_router.py:/app/compaction_router.py:ro` - never a directory mount\nover `/app`, which would hide `config.yaml` - and it is live only while\n`litellm_settings.callbacks` names `compaction_router.proxy_handler_instance`, the\ninstance rather than the class."],[847,"A script missing its key outright fails `//scripts` analysis before any test runs, and a script\nwhose declared map disagrees with its sources is what that checker reports, naming the pairing and\nthe direction. Once the keys are in place the same verb resolves the file, and\n`tests-for scripts/bazel-map.sh` lists `//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test` among its consumers. Every new function additionally owes one `# @function` block and one asserting test in exactly\none suite, which test-with-bazel.md covers; `//tests:shell_inventory` and\n`//tests:doc_coverage` are the two gates, and both are test targets the graph can point you at\nbefore the gate does."],[990,"A script must work when the cwd is **not** the checkout. Under `bazel test` the working\ndirectory is an exec root or a runfiles tree that holds *copies* of whatever the target\ndeclares as `data`; under `bazel run` it is wherever the operator typed `bazelisk`, and\n`bazel run` alone sets `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY`. Neither guarantees a working Docker\ndaemon, an `ssh`, or a reachable Spark box — a sandboxed action usually has none of them on\nPATH."],[2295,"A script never runs another repo script by relative path, and never assumes the cwd is the\ncheckout: it resolves the repo root with `resolve_checkout` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` and then\n`cd`s there (see the harness doc), because a Bazel sandbox may only hold a copy. That helper tries\n`${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` / `${PWD}` /\n`${SELF_DIR}/..` in order, and a candidate counts only when it holds the marker file\n(`docker-compose.yml` unless the caller names another) and its `pwd -P` is not under a\n`bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or `*.runfiles` tree — an explicit `REPO_ROOT` is\nthe one candidate trusted as given, which is how the Bats runner names the copy under test.\nNothing proving out is one\n`error: unresolvable repo root: ...` line on stderr and exit `5`, never a guessed cwd."],[648,"A script that needs the checkout reaches it the same way: source its libs by absolute path from\n`SELF_DIR`, resolve the root with `resolve_checkout` (`scripts/lib/common.sh`), then `cd` into\nwhat it prints. Never `cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\"` - under `bazelisk run` the launcher execs the\nrunfiles copy of the script, so that is the runfiles tree, which holds only `//:stack_data` and is\nrecreated on the next build."],[790,"A section holds an ordered mix of leaves and collapsible folders. A leaf is `(label, path)` and a\nfolder is `(label, [child, ...])`. Folders stay ordinary directories: a section whose first entry\nis an index page uses that page as its clickable label."],[1170,"A self-installed CLI under `~/.grok/bin` stays first on the login-shell PATH, so it\nwins over the baked copy."],[446,"A separate in-browser **code-server** editor is Coder's VS Code fork (no local VS\nCode), labelled **VS Code Web** with `order = 5` so it sorts before GitHub reauth (7)\nand Install GitHub App (8) and reads apart from the **VS Code Desktop** helper. It is\nthe only app in the bar the agent still proxies."],[1763,"A single conversation pinned to one box now keeps its streams on that box while it\nstays healthy - that is the behaviour the burst predates."],[1505,"A skill is one directory: `hermes/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, with\nagentskills.io frontmatter for the name and description.\nCommit it, and the next `up` has it."],[1041,"a Spark URL in `docker-compose.yml` takes its host from an env var. The boxes are\noff-stack, so compose DNS cannot reach them and a hostname written here names a\nmachine nobody owns: `SPARKn_HOST` in `.env` names each box once and compose\ncomposes the card's probe URL from it (see\nconfig-updates.md)"],[2474,"A stack skill command missing from Grok Build on code-server web -> the stack's own seed is what provides it"],[961,"A suite is one cached action. Its `.bats` file, `test_helper.bash`, `test_helpers.sh`,\n`bats_runner.sh` and the `scripts/**.sh` plus stub/fixture files it declares as `data` are\nits inputs, so editing one script re-runs only the suites that read it, and an unchanged\nsuite replays its cached PASS. Beyond that common set each suite names only the payload\ngroups it opens, in `_SUITE_PAYLOAD` in `tests/BUILD.bazel`; a suite whose target does not\ndeclare a payload it reads finds that payload missing under its own target, and\n`//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test` is the gate that keeps the map honest."],[2462,"A summary on `agent-xhigh-fast` or `agent-swarm-fast` still takes minutes -> the fast-compaction hop did not fire"],[2613,"A summary that moved and still came back slowly is the fallback rather than the hop: each\nfast lane degrades to the lane it is the twin of (`agent-xhigh-fast` to `agent-xhigh`,\n`agent-swarm-fast` to `agent-swarm`), and a twin is not in the lane list, so a session that\nlost its own group to a cooldown takes its summary back to a Spark with it. That is the\nalias-health case further down this page."],[393,"A swap some process in this container still holds open stays, read straight off\n`/proc/*/fd` and compared by exact path. Comparing it as a glob would let one bracket in\n`$HOME` hide a live holder."],[392,"A swap written within the last hour stays. The hour is a courtesy toward a session that\nmay still be open in a sibling container, which is exactly what the archive is for."],[825,"A sweep of this repo's own agent session logs measured the habits the graph is meant to\nreplace. Corpus: the 199 parent sessions recorded for this checkout under\n`~/.grok/sessions/` before 2026-11-01, counted with `jq` and `grep -c` over each session's\n`chat_history.jsonl`. Every count is a floor, because a truncated tool result hides a call."],[463,"A symlink you made yourself at that path is left alone and named on stdout: the stack\ncreates no symlink, and it never replaces one."],[548,"A target named without a flag is a container, the same reading `refresh-configs`\ngives a bare word, so these two lines address the same container:"],[1205,"A task is micro when all of this holds: one deliverable, three or fewer files in `owns`\nfrom one directory family, a `calls:` budget of 15 or fewer counting the reads a child\nneeds to get oriented, one `done:` command a reviewer can run, no other ready task\nsharing a path, and `needs:` naming only finished tasks. Below that bar the plan costs\nmore than it saves, which is why the skill opens by asking whether the change touches\nabout five files at all."],[2498,"A template push stops in \"Detecting persistent resources\" with `vars map does not contain key` -> a body used the one-dollar brace form for a shell variable"],[154,"A template whose push or verification failed is counted in the\n`N of M template(s) did not reach an active version` line and makes the run exit\nnon-zero, while every other template still gets its push attempt."],[1322,"a three-line system-prompt addendum, appended after Coder's default prompt text\n(`include_default_system_prompt: true`), that keeps the delegation and\nsecret-hygiene rules which must hold even when a chat has no workspace attached."],[2689,"A tmux finding that survives a reseed of `~/.tmux.conf` is a reload gap: a\nclient reads `terminal-features` when it attaches. Run `tmux source-file\n~/.tmux.conf`, detach, reattach, then `tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'`\nand expect `RGB` and `extkeys` in the list."],[334,"A tracked `.devcontainer/contributor-env` outranks every other signal: the probe is a\n**miss** even when the same clone also tracks `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`. That is\nhow this repo keeps its own Coder workspaces on `workspace` — the `.devcontainer/` it\nships is the contributor one for a laptop and CI, not a nested toolchain to rebuild\ninside the workspace's own DinD sidecar (see\ncontribute/devcontainer.md). Application repositories do\nnot carry the sentinel, so their dev containers stay a hit."],[402,"A twin is copied only while absent, and only from the managed `~/.coder` copy, so a\nhand-placed file in `~/.agents` is never overwritten and the dedupe keeps exactly one\nlive source. The CLI-side spelling of the instruction file keeps the `INSTRUCTIONS.md`\nname because that is the name the `~/.agents` convention was seeded under."],[2253,"A value does not travel in argv except where upstream offers no other channel. The seam's\nheader names the exceptions: `--client-secret=` on the Universal Auth login and `--password=`\non `bootstrap`, because that CLI takes neither on stdin; and the `KEY=@<file>` form for a\nsingle write, which names a file rather than the value, as `--file=<tmp600>` does for a bulk\nset. Everything else that carries a managed value crosses the seam by redirect into a\nmode-600 file, never through `$( )`, a pipeline, or a variable the CLI fills."],[1732,"A value has to look like an id to count: alphanumerics, hyphens or underscores, 8\ncharacters or more. The sources, in order, are `x-litellm-trace-id`, then\n`x-litellm-session-id`, then any `x-<vendor>-session-id`, then\n`session-id`/`session_id`/`thread-id`/`conversation_id` (Codex style), then W3C\ntraceparent and baggage, plus `metadata.session_id` in the body."],[2254,"A value never appears in a log line, including a failure line. Every ok, warning and\nerror names the **key**, never the value, and counts rather than listing. The one function\nallowed to put a credential on a stream is `infisical_login`, whose whole job is handing the\naccess token to its caller on stdout while its commentary goes to stderr; the callers that do\nnot need it send that stream to `/dev/null`. The boot body scrubs the token and the client\nsecret out of the CLI's own error text before it prints what the export said."],[2936,"A value of `false`, `0`, `no` or `off` turns the bundle off; anything else turns it on.\nOne helper decides, `compose_observability_args` in `scripts/lib/common.sh`, so the\nrunners (`dev-stack`, `prod-stack`, `update-stack`) cannot disagree with each other."],[2742,"A width under the ceiling is not always a fault: it is the queue an operator sets on\npurpose for a session whose writers ride a pinned lane, where one box's `PARALLEL` is the\nuseful width. The check prints the same warning either way, so the question is what\n`general-purpose` names under `[subagents.models]`, not how big the number is."],[1070,"A working stack on a fresh box without reading the whole README first"],[2334,"A workspace **app** link (the **VS Code Web** button is the only app this stack still\nproxies) rides the coder row of that table. `coder_app.code_server` sets\n`subdomain = false`, so its URL keeps the `/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/<slug>` path\nform and goes to `coder:3000` on its way to the editor on `localhost:13337` inside the\nworkspace. A 502 on that link is an app-lane failure and `litellm-gmktecbeast.` took\nno part in it."],[2468,"A workspace app link answers `Bad Gateway` -> find who answered"],[573,"A workspace moving `shared` -> `private` starts on whatever those three directories\nalready hold on its own home volume: nothing is copied and the owner's volumes are\nleft alone."],[341,"A workspace name can follow the URL. Coder names start with a letter (40 characters\nmax); the derived default is the repository leaf."],[283,"A workspace owner can still tick the box for one workspace: `allow_user_autostop`\nstays on, because CE cannot turn it off -- `--allow-user-autostop=false` is marked\n\"can only be disabled in enterprise\"."],[2256,"A write is verified by hashing the value read back before anything claims `ok:`."],[1758,"A wrong model id does not crash the proxy: 1.102.1 builds the Router with\n`ignore_invalid_deployments` forced to True, so a bad id drops that deployment at boot\nwith one log line and later surfaces as \"No deployments available\"."],[1392,"Access"],[896,"Adding a `.py`, `.ts`, or `.tf` file without guessing which list it belongs on"],[885,"Adding a file"],[892,"Adding a new language, or a new file to an existing one"],[772,"Adding a page that shows up in the menu, the search index and the prev/next footer"],[615,"Adding a script without editing a BUILD file"],[947,"Adding a test that passes in the Bazel sandbox and on a laptop"],[2207,"Adopt what is already in `.env`:"],[2132,"Adopting the vault"],[2616,"After a `stack up`, the seeder that runs it in the background keeps its output in\n`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/coder-agents-seeder.log` (the `note:` line names the path). A chat\nthat never got wired usually means that file holds a `skipped:` line: no coder\ncontainer yet, no db container, or nobody had registered when the stack came up.\nRe-run the command above once the stack is up and somebody has signed in."],[1004,"After a recreate: revalidate once"],[1373,"After creating or editing anything, check discovery and provenance with `grok inspect`,\nand `grok inspect --json` for each skill's `source` path and `userInvocable`. `/skills`\nopens the same set as a menu, where a toggle takes a skill out of the slash menu\nwithout deleting the file. Discovery reloads from disk, so an edit lands next turn\nwith no restart."],[2168,"After the loop the staged file is read once more against one alternation built from that same\nvariable, and a match makes the body **refuse to publish**: it removes the staged file, prints\n`infisical: refused to publish <home>/.infisical/agent.env; the staged file names a\nshell-control variable. Nothing was written and no secret value is shown.` and exits 0. The\n`agent.env` a previous boot wrote then stays exactly as it was, which is the point of refusing\nrather than correcting. The obvious alternative - appending `unset PATH HOME IFS` lines to the\nfile - is the wrong shape: those names would be gone from every shell that sourced the file and\nfrom everything those shells then launch. A refusal costs one refresh; that would cost the\nworkspace."],[1681,"Agent mode therefore needs either the Spark `agent` lane or the small `chat` model."],[2630,"Agent traffic ignores that URL and dials `host.docker.internal:3002`; see the\n`coder_agent_url` template variable."],[2099,"Alerts"],[1928,"Alerts and rules"],[1232,"All four name only the local aliases - `agent-explore` for their mapping lanes,\n`agent-swarm` for their writers, `agent-review` for their skeptic - and none of them reaches\nfor a cloud model id, which is the rule in\nlitellm-routing.md rather than a preference."],[2797,"All of that applies to a client actually being read. Measured on this\nworkspace, 7 of 9 attached clients received 0 bytes over a 4 s window in which\nthe pane produced 12858, because code-server's pty host keeps the pty open\nafter the browser side of the tab goes away and nothing drains it any more;\ntmux stops feeding a client it cannot write to, which is why 15 such clients\nagainst the same flooding pane took the server only from 450 ms to 730 ms. What\na ghost really costs is the reuse decision. It holds `session_attached` at 1,\nand that is the number the attach helper reads, so the folder pays a whole new\nsession - another pane, another xterm.js, another history - for every later\ntab. Count the clients against the panes, and count what each one is taking in:"],[1475,"All of that lives in the seed, and the seed reaches the volume once:\n`scripts/hermes-bootstrap.sh` copies `hermes/config.yaml` into the data volume only\nwhen the file is absent. A box that ran an earlier seed therefore has no `litellm`\nrow at all - `--provider litellm` names a row its config does not have, the eleven\ncatalogue aliases are undeclared there, and with no row carrying\n`discover_models: false` the picker adopts `compaction-fast` off the proxy. Check it\nwith `bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-doctor -- diff`, which names the file that\ndiffers and prints the `hermes-bootstrap --data <mount> --force` line that re-seeds\nit; a hand-edited volume copy is worth reading before you overwrite it."],[2178,"All three carry `restart: unless-stopped`, the `secrets` profile, and the same three-line\n`json-file` logging block the rest of the stack uses, with `tag:` equal to the service\nname so Alloy ships each container to Loki under `container=<name>`. None of the three is\na Prometheus target - Infisical publishes no `/metrics` endpoint the stack can rely on and\nneither alpine image ships one - so they are recorded as log-only in\nobservability.md."],[2526,"Alloy ships no logs and Loki stays empty -> the host's log root is not `STACK_DOCKER_LOG_DIR`"],[449,"An **Install GitHub App** external app (`order = 8`, same icon, opens in a tab)\nis the clickable install path for that default App:\n`https://github.com/apps/coder/installations/new`. It is declared only while\n`github_auth_id` is `github`. After installing, grant the private repos the\nworkspace clones, then restart so `GH_TOKEN` remints."],[1318,"an `openai-compat` AI provider named **`litellm`** (not `litellm-auto`), base URL\n`http://litellm:4000/v1` - LiteLLM over the compose network, *server-side*,\nauthenticated with `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`. The agent dials the in-process AI Gateway\n(aibridge), which forwards to the provider's `base_url` + `/chat/completions`, so\n`/v1` has to be part of the base URL and the key has to match the live proxy: with\n`.env` leaving the key empty, compose still boots the proxy on its\n`change-me-litellm` interpolation default, and that default is what gets sent.\nCreated when absent, drift-patched when an existing provider carries a drifted\n`base_url` or key."],[485,"An `ssh://` or `git@` URL reaches nothing here - this stack provisions no SSH keys."],[2633,"An agent whose searches come back empty while the service answers 200 is nearly\nalways parked engines rather than a broken deployment: the general-purpose engines\nare the ones the public backends bot-wall first. The same query without an\n`engines` pin usually still returns results, which is why the guidance in\n`~/.grok/AGENTS.md` tells an agent to leave the engine choice to the instance."],[1631,"An alias is a `model_name:` entry in `litellm/config.yaml`, not a model."],[304,"An always-on privileged `docker:29.8.1-dind` sidecar on the per-workspace bridge\nnetwork, with `DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375` injected **unconditionally** and\n`DOCKER_TLS_CERTDIR=` empty for the reasons above. `internal` stays false for the same\none reason."],[2925,"An empty `data` with the flag present and no Tempo error means nothing is producing spans\nyet. Grafana is the one shipped client (`GF_TRACING_ENABLED`), so drive a few requests\nthrough the Grafana API and check again; a `traces_spanmetrics_calls_total` series with a\n`service_name` is the proof the whole path works."],[2836,"An empty github.com/settings/apps page is the usual\nsurprise here: it means no App is installed under your account, so there is no\n**Configure** link to click and nothing to grant, while the provider itself stays\nconfigured and keeps minting tokens that authenticate. Install Coder's App from the\nworkspace **Install GitHub App** button, or at\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new,\ngrant the repo, then restart. A private repository is never visible to a credential\nthat was never granted it."],[843,"An empty list is the finding: no filegroup carries the file, so `test-fast` and `lint` never open it.\nThe pair verb, `unlisted`, asks it per directory by diffing `git ls-files` against the graph's own\nsource list. It skips Bazel's own declaration files (`BUILD.bazel`, `*.bzl`, `MODULE.bazel`), which\nBazel reads implicitly and which only appear in the graph when a package deliberately exports them,\nso a directory holding nothing but a BUILD file reads as clean."],[1290,"An empty result page and \"no answer\" are different answers, which is why the probe\nreports which one it got. It also names every engine the instance has parked and\nthe reason its backend gave (`parked by its backend: google: access denied`),\nread out of the reply body whether or not that reply found results - an instance\nrunning on half its engines is visible there before it starts answering empty. A\npark is temporary: SearXNG holds an engine out for `search.suspended_times`\nseconds after it refuses, so re-run before writing one off. `--json` adds\n`engines_parked`. It checks the service hop; `grok mcp doctor searxng`\ninside the workspace checks the MCP hop. The binary it launches\n(`/opt/npm-global/bin/searxng-mcp-server`) is baked into the golden image, so a\nprobe that answers with results plus a failed handshake is a server, URL or port\nproblem, not a missing package."],[2676,"An entry for `<name>` with `userInvocable: true` in the `grok inspect --json` output is\nthe pass condition: the slash command exists on that CLI, and the **Grok Build**\nterminal profile shows it because that profile execs the same binary."],[2972,"An environment is a set of values, a set of secrets, and a place to run. It is a GitHub\nEnvironment, a compose project, a namespace, an inventory group. It is not a git branch."],[2999,"An environment may differ from its neighbour in values, secrets, and where it runs. If a branch\ndiffers in *code*, that code belongs on the trunk behind a flag."],[2363,"an extended `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` - homepage validates the incoming Host against\nthat exact-match list, so an unlisted name fails its host check;"],[481,"An extra folder never becomes the folder the tools open on: the editor, the Web\nTerminal button and the tmux helpers all stay on the primary clone."],[2522,"An Observability card reads DOWN while the container is up -> the badge probes an endpoint, not a process"],[1807,"An Ubuntu server with a LUKS root stops in the initramfs on every reboot and\nwaits for a passphrase. With no keyboard attached, that wait is the outage: the\nmachine is up, the disks are unreadable, and the only fix is a person at the\nconsole. The distro answer is to move an SSH server and cryptsetup's unlock\nprompt into the initramfs, so the passphrase is typed over SSH from anywhere."],[2362,"an upgraded `CODER_ACCESS_URL` (the public coder name);"],[1295,"and `search_tool` lists the live tool names rather than a hardcoded list. Prove it\n  with `grok mcp doctor context7` inside a workspace."],[874,"And set algebra, for \"is this payload covered by the suite I trust with it\":"],[1160,"and the cell data lands one `BufferLine` at a time as output arrives, at\n  3 `Uint32` words per column per line\n  (BufferLine.ts),\n  against a VS Code default of 1000\n  (terminalConfiguration.ts).\n  So the setting costs a pointer array per terminal in the tab either way, and\n  real memory only for the lines a pane actually emits - which is why a busy\n  build log is where a large value is felt, and an idle prompt is not. tmux\n  holds its history once per pane, in the server, and every client of that pane\n  shares the one buffer. Hence the split: the deep store is tmux's and the\n  browser keeps a short viewport. Inside a tmux pane the client's wheel has nothing\n  to walk - tmux paints in the alternate screen, so that buffer holds no pane\n  history - and the 100000 lines live behind `Prefix-[`, paid for in the server and\n  not in the tab. Measured on tmux 3.4 with one pane pushed to its limit, 100000\n  lines of 120-column output holds the tmux server at 381 MB RSS; the same 100000\n  lines of plain 80-column output costs 60 MB. The same sweep repeated at 120\n  columns came out close to linear at about 3 KB of server per history line -\n  10 MB at 2000, 35 MB at 10000, 160 MB at 50000, 308 MB at 100000 - so this is\n  the one terminal number that turns a chatty pane into real container memory,\n  and it is a deliberate trade: the deep store is what lets `Prefix-[` still\n  reach the start of a build. tmux reads `history-limit` when a pane is created,\n  so an existing pane keeps the number it was born with."],[436,"and the seven keys the\n  `env = merge({...})` block hands it are its whole interface\n  (`templates/workspace/main.tf:692-706`, `:778-780`)."],[2064,"And the shipped payload checks, which cover the dashboard group and its card URLs:"],[2949,"Ansible"],[795,"Answer what-depends-on-this with one bazelisk query instead of a grep sweep: the bazel-map CLI, the raw query predicates behind it, and the empty-result trap."],[2379,"Any authentication as the Traefik router's middleware. Authelia already fronts\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` (the name 302s), the same interactive policy as\nthe homepage and Coder names, and `grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` belongs behind\nthat same policy: Grafana is an interactive login, not an API endpoint, and the name\nfronts the metrics bundle. The sidecar forwards what it receives and\nauthenticates nothing, so a name whose router skips the auth middleware reaches\nits container the same way the LAN does. The app path is behind that policy\nlike the rest of its name, so an expired session surfaces there too."],[2236,"Anything compose interpolates that looks like a credential and appears nowhere on this\npage is either a bug in the inventory or a bug in the compose file, and `check` is the\ncommand that catches the first."],[2884,"Anything else means the router matched (Hermes basic auth is `401` until you\nsend credentials). A `421` only on the public URL, with this probe succeeding,\nis a rewritten Host or a Traefik backend that is not `:8080`."],[549,"Anything that carries a prefix other than `c:` (container) or `h:` (host\ndirectory) is reported as unrecognized and counted in the closing summary's\nskipped tally. A target never leaves the pass unheard."],[1311,"Apply it (or re-run after a skip):"],[1411,"Approval prompts, bootstrap seeding, and the skills directory"],[1400,"Approvals"],[2057,"Are the scrapes up:"],[2061,"Are the three unpublished backends alive: their health endpoints are\n`http://loki:3100/ready`, `http://tempo:3200/ready` and `http://mimir:8080/ready`, all\ncompose-DNS only. Those exact URLs are the `siteMonitor` of the **Observability** group's\ncards on the dashboard, so the card badge is the probe - and because those four\ncontainers have no healthcheck, it is the only automatic one. For the logs behind a red\nbadge, use the container log rather than an exec, since a distroless image has no shell\nto exec into:"],[55,"Argument handling"],[169,"Arguments are template *names*, and they select which names to push. A name that is not\none of the known names is an error that lists the known names, so a typo cannot quietly\ndo nothing."],[1415,"Asking from a terminal you already have - the right Linux command, a triage, a\nGitHub project deployed into the deploy workspace - with the same skills, memory\nand approval gate behind the answer"],[989,"Assertions come from `tests/test_helpers.sh`: `fail`, `assert_eq`, `assert_match`,\n`assert_not_match`, `assert_file_exists`, `assert_file_not_exists`, `load_shell_function`.\n`load_shell_function` is the one harness place that expands a function body (it is how a\nsingle entry-CLI function gets unit-tested); production scripts still never use `eval`."],[1700,"At the pinned `v1.102.1` tag that key is a real `Router.__init__` argument and is not in\n`ROUTER_SETTINGS_MANAGED_OUTSIDE_CONFIG`, so `config.yaml` reaches it, and its valid flags\nare exactly `deployment_affinity`, `responses_api_deployment_check`, `session_affinity`\nand `encrypted_content_affinity`. An entry REPLACES the global flag list for the group it\nnames rather than adding to it, so the empty list means no affinity check of any kind runs\nfor `agent-swarm`. Every group absent from the map keeps falling back to the global\n`[\"session_affinity\"]`, which is why the five lanes a conversation rides still pin exactly\nas they did. Setting the key also self-arms the affinity callback, so those groups keep\npinning."],[1177,"At top level the render sets `disable_web_search = true`, which parks Grok's native\n`web_search` tool: no transport this box ships serves that tool type, so the\nself-hosted `searxng` MCP server is the search lane. The search lane section below\ncarries the measurement and how to flip the key back."],[1395,"Audit and drift"],[1396,"Auth"],[2316,"Authelia"],[2591,"Auto-compaction always fired at about 75 per cent of the 262144-token window, the\nsetting in force when this was measured: all 124 `auto_compact_started` events carried\n`tokens_used` between 196,636 and 214,585. Both template twins now ship\n`[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` at 70, so a future sweep measures the\nevents firing at a lower point than this."],[277,"Autostart, autostop and TTLs are template *metadata*, so they stay out of the Terraform\n(where they would fight the UI) and travel on the CLI after pushing."],[64,"Autostop and TTLs"],[228,"Autostop is metadata, not Terraform. Re-pin it with `templates edit` after a push made\nfrom the checkout root, or on the dev stack swap `-p evo-t1-coder-stack` for\n`-p evo-t1-dev --env-file dev-stack.env`."],[101,"Autostop, TTLs, Docker-in-Docker, and the DinD sidecar failure modes"],[921,"awk is POSIX-subset and single-purpose: a program in `scripts/lib/*.awk` takes its\ninput in `ARGV`, its configuration in `ENVIRON`, and prints tab-separated fields the\ncaller reads line by line. The types are that line shape, and they are documented in\nthe program's header block — see `scripts/lib/tf_types.awk` for the shape to copy.\nBats suites are bash and follow the same rules as their shell."],[1331,"Backgrounded and polled because `execute` waits only a short window per tool call\n(about a minute), so a synchronous foreground `grok -p` would die mid-run on any big\ntask. Coder Agents then reviews the delegated diff, runs the project's tests, and\ncommits. Subagents spawned by Coder Agents cannot create workspaces: provisioning\ndecisions stop at the top-level chat."],[1167,"Baked in at `/usr/local/bin/grok`, with an `agent` link beside it, **outside** the\n`/home/coder` home volume, so a rebuilt volume cannot lose it."],[916,"Bash has no type system, and no annotation comment buys one. What the repo does\ninstead is put the checking where the language can actually be checked:"],[884,"Bash, awk, and Bats"],[891,"Bash, awk, and Bats: what an untyped language owes instead of annotations"],[599,"Bazelisk as the ingress for tests, lint and the operator CLIs, and what the runfiles layout means for a script you just added."],[617,"Bazelisk is how this repo runs tests, lint, and the operator CLIs. The Makefile targets call\nthe same commands. test-with-bazel.md covers the Bats harness;\n../branching.md the branch rules that CI guards;\ndevcontainer.md the container that carries the toolchain."],[2289,"Bazelisk is the only ingress, with one exception: `scripts/setup-host.sh`. That script is\nthe **stack host's** bootstrap — it installs Bazelisk, the shell/Starlark lint toolchain\n(shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier) and Terraform — so it has to run before Bazel exists, and\nstays the single documented `bash scripts/setup-host.sh`. On a laptop you do not run it:\nthe contributor container or `bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image` brings the same pinned\ntoolchain in from `images/dev/` instead\n(contribute/devcontainer.md). `--repair` is the other half of\nthat exemption: leftover Docker bind-mounts in Bazel's runfiles tree make\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` die creating `prod-stack.runfiles` (`Permission\ndenied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`) before any stack script can run.\nOn Linux it also writes a systemd-resolved drop-in (`1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8`) and\nruns `tailscale set --accept-dns=false` so host `git pull` still resolves\n`github.com` when MagicDNS is the only nameserver."],[1913,"Because git and `gh` consult `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` before the stored login, an expired token\npair from an old refresh can shadow a working credential. Every call tries the environment first\nand, on refusal, retries once with the pair cleared and says so in a `warning:`. Re-mint with\n`coder external-auth access-token github`."],[812,"Before a large edit: the twin copies, the filegroup, the map key, the test a new function owes"],[799,"Before a large edit: what moves with this file?"],[2992,"Before any edit, answer five questions and keep the answers in a scratch file:"],[2075,"Before you reach for a hammer"],[1060,"Before you start"],[26,"Below the subject leave one empty line, then the bullet points, and leave an empty line\nbetween every two bullets, so each bullet is its own paragraph. The same rule is in\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, which is the text every workspace seeds into\n`~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, so a commit written by an agent and one written by hand read the\nsame way in `git log`."],[1275,"Beside the three state mounts there is one more volume, and it shares nothing with\nthem: `grok-session-archive`, mounted read-write at `/var/lib/grok-session-archive`.\nIt is deliberately outside `~/.grok`, and it is host-wide rather than per-owner - every\nworkspace on that daemon mounts the same volume, each writing under its own\n`workspaces/<hostname>/` leaf."],[1401,"Bootstrap and skills"],[1522,"Bootstrap options, including `--data` and `--force`:"],[2204,"Bootstrap the instance:"],[1076,"Bootstrap the toolchain — the only step that runs before Bazel exists, because it is what\ninstalls Bazelisk:"],[2679,"Both `ls` paths present but no `<name>` entry: the loader dropped the file. Frontmatter\nmust start on line 1, so anything above the opening `---` silently kills the skill."],[1366,"Both `main.tf` files deliberately set **no** `CODER_AGENT_EXP_*` env keys on\n`coder_agent.main`: those keys *replace* the agent's discovery defaults, and the\ndefaults already cover exactly the seeded paths - the `~/.coder` roots plus a\nworking-dir-relative `.agents/skills`, which resolves inside the agent's working\ndir (`/home/coder` in `workspace`; in `devcontainer` with the toggle on, the\ncloned folder under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/...`, where a dev container built on\nthe sidecar carries its own home and no seeded path is visible from inside it\nanyway)."],[2599,"Both `templates/*/main.tf` twins carry `GROK_COMPACTION_WALL_CLOCK_SECS = \"1200\"` in\nthe agent `env` merge, next to `GROK_MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_BYTES`. The CLI reads that budget\nfrom the environment only (resolver\n`crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/util/config/resolve/compaction.rs`, remote-settings\nkey `compaction_wall_clock_budget_secs`); no TOML table accepts it, so a line pasted\ninto `grok-config.toml` is silently ignored, the same trap `[mcp] max_output_bytes`\nalready documents. It is honoured: setting it to `1` makes `/compact` fail with\n`exceeded wall-clock budget 1s`. Push, then stop and start the workspace so the new\nagent env lands:"],[2416,"Both containers are idempotent by name (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:79-80`), so a second\n`--apply` starts what is already there rather than creating a duplicate on a port the\nfirst one holds. The DCGM exporter is not run on the image's own field list: the CLI\nferries `observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv` to `/opt/evo-t1/dcgm/custom-counters.csv`\non the box and bind-mounts it read-only\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:97-98`, `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:239`), so the `DCGM_FI_*`\nseries Prometheus sees are the ones this repo reviews. Editing that CSV and re-running\n`--apply` reports `updated`, and the CLI then tells you to `--restart` so the running\nexporter rereads it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:696-697`)."],[1629,"Both doors authenticate with the same `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` value."],[1984,"Both flag paths are gated: `bazelisk run //scripts:validate` runs\n`docker compose config -q` twice, once plain and once with `--profile observability`, so\na compose file that only parses with the bundle on (or only with it off) fails the build\nrather than the deploy."],[1738,"Both float across the healthy boxes by load."],[213,"Both gates are read with this script's own `env_get`, not with `infisical_enabled` from\n`scripts/lib/infisical.sh`: no push variable there reads the process environment, so a\nstray exported `INFISICAL_ENABLED` cannot change what gets pushed\n(`scripts/push-template.sh:632-635`)."],[2161,"Both gates reach the templates through `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`: the CLI forwards\n`--var infisical_enabled=true` and `--var infisical_pull_on_start=true` only for a truthy value in\n`.env`, so the shipped state pushes neither word and each template's own `default = false` stands.\nWith the flag off the `coder_script` resource is absent outright - no script, no boot log line,\nno `INFISICAL_*` key on the agent environment - and the `.bashrc` hook is not written either,\nbecause that line is gated on `infisical_hook`, which both twins set from `var.infisical_enabled`."],[621,"Both give you the GNU userland the gate runs on, which a macOS laptop cannot promise\nnatively. Neither replaces the bootstrap below — that one is for the stack host."],[2668,"Both of these answer nothing in that case; the first is the one to type:"],[1776,"Both of those live in `docker-compose.yml` and `litellm/config.yaml` respectively, so\nthey restart differently: `restart litellm` re-reads the config file, while a new mount\nand the five `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION*` keys need the container recreated with the same\ncompose invocation and `up -d litellm`."],[2938,"Both paths parse or the build fails: `bazelisk run //scripts:validate` runs\n`docker compose config -q` once plain and once with `--profile observability`."],[2250,"Both run as part of `up`, so the operator's job is one `up` after the rotate. Read the\n`db` and `coder` logs if Coder takes longer than a recreate to come back:"],[152,"Both the preset descriptions and the two routing hints fit the same 128-byte ceiling.\n`coder_parameter` descriptions are **not** capped (some run past 450 characters) and are\nnot checked."],[915,"Both twins conform today: every `variable` carries a `type` and a `description`, and\nneither uses `any`, `list(any)`, or a bare container. `//tests:bats_tf_types_test`\nholds that, with fixtures that prove the gate still reports each finding rather than\nhaving quietly stopped looking."],[454,"Both twins pin the editor release in one `code_server_version` local in their own\n`main.tf`, and the script hands that value to the upstream installer as\n`--version`. Without the flag `https://code-server.dev/install.sh` follows\n`latest`, so a release nobody reviewed would reach every new workspace with no\ncommit in this repo - the same reason the Registry module was forked out. The two\nlocals carry the same value and `//tests:bats_coder_apps_test` fails a drift. To\nbump: read `https://github.com/coder/code-server/releases`, edit both locals,\n`bazelisk test //tests:bats_coder_apps_test`, `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`,\nthen restart the workspaces. The pin is a template value and deliberately stays out\nof `images/dev/tool-versions.env`, where every key has to be an `images/dev/Dockerfile`\n`ARG` and this tool ships in no image at all."],[457,"Both twins' `coder_app` opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`. The\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL, in the Web Terminal's `command`, and as a positional\nargument to the code-server binary with `--ignore-last-opened` (path-based apps\ndrop the query string, and without that flag the last restored workspace wins)."],[1734,"Both workspace templates render `x-litellm-session-id = \"ws-<workspace-uuid>\"` under\n`[models]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`, so one key covers every conversation of a\nworkspace, subagent calls included."],[2953,"Branch cleanup cookbook"],[2976,"Branch cleanup is part of the work, not a chore someone does later."],[43,"Branch protection on `main` should require a pull request before pushing, and the two\nBazel checks before merging — `//:test-fast` and `//:lint`, run inside the golden image\nthe workflow builds, so a green check means the suite passed on the Linux toolchain the\nstack actually ships rather than on whatever the runner image carried that day."],[1,"Branching"],[3,"Branching"],[2574,"Bring the TensorFold server up on the box. `<host>` is what `.env` names, and `--apply`\nwrites the per-host override file that server runs:"],[528,"Bringing an old checkout back to a runnable state with one command"],[1066,"Bringing the stable stack up and pulling the models"],[2500,"Browser editor (code-server) stays unhealthy -> check these three, in order"],[683,"Build it locally when you want the tag in the local store (the Coder docker provider\nresolves `evo-t1-dev:latest` from there and never pulls):"],[598,"Building with Bazel"],[600,"Building with Bazel"],[824,"building-with-bazel.md explains the maps, runfiles, and the daily\ncommands; test-with-bazel.md explains the suites those queries name and the\ntwo inventory gates; ../project-conventions.md the CLI vocabulary this\ntool prints in."],[2772,"Builds still succeed, so verify with a workspace build before acting on it. Nothing\nin this repo's compose file sets the provisioner's Terraform."],[2025,"but it is invisible to git and to the gate below, which is the same drift the `:ro` mounts\nabove exist to prevent."],[1021,"Card `href`s come from `STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` /\n`STACK_HERMES_URL`, which default to LAN URLs built from `STACK_LAN_HOST` and the\nport variables. Change a port or re-detect the LAN IP and the links follow; set `auto`\nin `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` and the cards follow the names bootstrap composes from the\nnaming parts (public-access.md). The Hermes card\nfollows `STACK_HERMES_URL` when bootstrap sets it, and stays on\n`http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` when that key is empty. See hermes.md.\nThe search card and the header box share `STACK_SEARCH_URL`. The router composes public\nnames for the dashboard, coder, kasm, litellm and hermes only, so search has no public\nname to fall to: leave the key empty and both stay on the LAN, where the card's status\nstill reports for anyone who can reach the dashboard."],[2409,"Catching a box left on an old exporter tag before someone asks why its panels differ\nfrom its neighbours'"],[1805,"Catching a config that looks right and boots dead, before the reboot"],[820,"Catching a payload directory no filegroup claims before the gates stay green by accident"],[895,"Catching a wrong-shaped input at plan time or type-check time rather than in a container"],[2903,"Cause: \"no target\" and \"one target, `down`\" are different faults. Check which one you\nhave from the stack host:"],[2555,"Cause: `/etc/resolv.conf` names only Tailscale MagicDNS (`100.100.100.100`).\nDocker's iptables, and Kasm's privileged nested dockerd, can make that stub\nunreachable. When it is, **every** lookup dies, including `github.com`, so\n`git pull` over HTTPS fails. This is host DNS, not GitHub auth."],[2758,"Cause: `bazelisk run` executes from the runfiles tree, where `templates/**` are\nsymlinks into the execroot. A tar that stores those links unpacks in the\nprovisioner as dangling paths, so Terraform sees no `.tf` files. The two\n`templates/common/` payloads still land (copied with `cp`, which follows\nlinks), which is why the archive is small rather than empty."],[2773,"Cause: `coder/preview` warns when a `coder_app` `count` is not expanded at\nworkspace-create preview. `start_count` is that value. The default is then\nassumed to be 1. The only `coder_app` carrying a `count` today is\n`coder_app.github_app_install`, and it counts on `github_auth_id`, which the\npreview does expand."],[2545,"Cause: `docker compose` resolved `./proxy/homepage.conf` (or another file bind\nmount) next to a compose file that did not have that file. Under `bazelisk run`\nthat file used to be the runfiles copy, which only holds `//:stack_data`. Docker\nthen created a directory at the source path and failed mounting it onto nginx's\n`/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf`, which is a file in the image."],[2934,"Cause: `OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED` is read from the environment the compose command runs\nwith, and each project reads a different file. `dev-stack` passes `--env-file\ndev-stack.env` when that file sits beside the compose file, so a dev run never sees\n`.env`; `prod-stack` names no env file unless `-e` does, and compose then reads `.env`\nbeside the compose file. Setting the key in the wrong one changes nothing."],[2761,"Cause: `startup_size_guard` looks up a payload the twin does not keep beside its\nown `main.tf` under `templates/common/`, and it did that by reading an ambient\n`REPO_ROOT`. Nothing in `scripts/` ever sets that variable: the checkout is the\n`root` the script resolved and `cd`'d into at startup. Under the script's own\n`set -u` the read aborts the run. It only fires for a template that inlines a\ncanonical payload, which is why a twin carrying its own copy never showed it."],[2777,"Cause: `templatefile()` reads a single-dollar brace reference anywhere in a\n`coder_script` body as an interpolation - inside a shell comment or a `for` loop\nas readily as in a command - and the plan then asks the caller's map for a key\n`main.tf` never declares. Both twins fail at once, because both render the same\nbody from `templates/common/`:"],[2665,"Cause: a pinned download returned an error. The layer prints `curl: (22)` and stops\nrather than shipping a partial image."],[2868,"Cause: after the publish loop the body reads the staged file once more against one\nalternation built from its own deny list, and a match deletes the staged file, prints\nthat, and exits `0` - it refuses rather than correcting, and the `agent.env` an earlier\nboot wrote stays exactly as it was. That is why the workspace keeps working, and why the\nline names neither a key nor a value."],[2789,"Cause: an app inside the pane - vim's `+` register, anything that emits OSC 52 -\npushes its copy out through the pane, `set-clipboard on` with\n`allow-passthrough on` forwards it, and code-server's terminal hands the escape to\nnobody. There is no setting for it: the shipped 4.145.0 registers no OSC 52 key,\nand upstream\nmicrosoft/vscode#318254 is\nopen with two unmerged fixes. Over ssh from Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm or iTerm2 the\nsame copy lands, which puts the drop in the browser and not in tmux."],[2549,"Cause: an older `bazelisk run` used the runfiles copy of `docker-compose.yml`, so\ncompose mounted `./homepage/assets/icons` from `bazel-out`. Docker then created a\ndirectory where `evo-t1.svg` belongs, or the homepage container chowned those\nfiles to root. Bazel cannot recreate `prod-stack.runfiles` over that leftover,\nand the stack script never starts, so a repair inside `prod-stack` cannot help."],[2552,"Cause: an older target ran the runfiles copy of the script, so `$(dirname \"$0\")/..`\nwas the runfiles tree, and that tree ships only `//:stack_data`. The working `.env`\nis untracked, so it exists only in the checkout, and the rotation reported a missing\nfile on a host that had one. The path in the message tells the two apart: a\n`bazel-out/.../dashboard-password.runfiles/...` path is this entry. The same bug had\n`pull-models` pulling its default list instead of the checkout's `OLLAMA_MODELS`,\n`coder-agents` provisioning the compose placeholder `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` instead of the\ncheckout's, and `spark-configure` / `spark-verify` writing `sparks/` output into a tree\nBazel recreates."],[2770,"Cause: an upstream pin. The Coder image ships its own provisioner binary at\n`/usr/local/bin/terraform` (1.15.5 in `coder:v2.36.6`) and Coder caps the version it\nwas tested against."],[2686,"Cause: both are properties of the environment. Neither points at a workspace that\nneeds repair, and neither is fixable from inside the container."],[2907,"Cause: both show up in the browser console on every dashboard page, and only one of\nthem means anything."],[2755,"Cause: both templates inline `templates/common/vscode-alt.svg` as a\n`data:image/svg+xml;base64` URL on `coder_app.code_server`. Coder v2.36 stores\n`workspace_apps.icon` as `varchar(256)`. A multi-kilobyte SVG produces a data\nURL thousands of characters long, and the insert fails. The provisioner\nreports it at `insert agent app: upsert app`."],[2932,"Cause: by design. Only `prometheus` and `grafana` declare a healthcheck, because\n`grafana/mimir`, `grafana/loki`, `grafana/tempo` and `grafana/alloy` are distroless -\nno shell, no `wget`, no `curl` - so any command a healthcheck ran would fail in the image\nrather than on the service. `running` is the best state those four can report, and adding\na healthcheck to one of them is not the fix for an anxious operator."],[2844,"Cause: Coder injects `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` into the agent environment when the\nworkspace starts. That GitHub App user-to-server token expires about an hour later and\nthe injected copy is never refreshed for the life of the workspace. `gh` consults that\npair before it reads its own auth store, so it sends the dead token while the store\nbeside it, re-minted by the `gh_token_refresh` coder_script, is healthy and unreachable."],[2775,"Cause: Coder runs every `coder_script` as `bash -c`. HCL heredocs in `main.tf` only\nescape `$${name}` to `${name}`; `$$((` is two literal dollars. The editor wait loop\nthen becomes `waited=<pid>((waited + 1))`, which is the syntax error. The echo\nbefore the loop still prints. `already cloned, skipping` on the next line is the\nconcurrent clone stage, not the failure."],[2707,"Cause: Docker creates an empty named volume as `root:root` 755. The\n`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume is empty on first use (the image has no\nfiles there to copy in), so `coder` cannot write `settings.json`. `jq` and\n`python` then fail because the file was never created. `already cloned, skipping`\non the next line is informational: the clone already exists."],[2796,"Cause: each client attached to a tmux session is a full xterm.js in its own\nChrome tab re-parsing the same stream, each holding a line-slot array sized\nfrom `terminal.integrated.scrollback`, and each taking its own copy of the\nrendered stream out of the server - 16 mirrors of one pane pulled 78.7 MB\nthrough an 8 s window in which a single mirror pulled 4.9 MB. The server\nrenders the pane once, so a mirror is paid for in the tab rather than in server\nCPU: those same 16 moved the server only from 420 ms to 630 ms. A client\nactivating or resizing repaints the window's other clients as well."],[2875,"Cause: Hermes v0.22.5 refuses a non-loopback bind when no\n`DashboardAuthProvider` is registered. The bundled basic-auth plugin skips\nregistration when the password is empty. Compose interpolation\n`${HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD:-}` (empty default) did that after a pull that\nadded the Hermes keys: `.env` never gained them, so `prod-stack up` without\n`bootstrap` / `update-configs` crash-looped the gateway. The logs repeat\n\"no auth providers are registered\" and do not mention `plugins.disabled`."],[2675,"Cause: no published source names an upstream `/learn`, so there is none to miss. The\n`xai-org/grok-build` built-in command table, all 240 pages of docs.x.ai, every\nfirst-party changelog under `x.ai/cli/changelogs/`, and the public\n`xai-org/plugin-marketplace` catalog all carry zero occurrences of it, so there is no\npublished copy to vendor in. The memory family\n`/ez-learn` copies (`/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember`, `/memory`) is a different case:\nthose are upstream commands the CLI ships itself - `/flush`, `/dream` and `/memory` are\n*built-in* registry entries gated behind cross-session memory, which is off by default\nhere, and `/remember` is a pager command with no such gate. The CLI does download its\nbundled platform skills at runtime (`GROK_SKILLS_BASE_URL`, `cli-chat-proxy.grok.com`)\nand that route needs an x.ai sign-in: from a workspace `api.x.ai` answers 404 and\n`cli-chat-proxy.grok.com`\nanswers 401 anonymously, and a session started with `--sandbox off` still leaves\n`~/.grok/bundled/` uncreated. LiteLLM is the sole model transport here, there is no\nx.ai credential, and the fetch never runs. So each slash command this stack ships is its\nown seed, written at workspace start by `coder_script.stack_skills` into\n`~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` with a twin copy at\n`~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, and a missing command means that seed is\nmissing, not that the image is stale. Each carries its own `ez-` name to stay clear of\nthe upstream names: a user-scope skill overrides a same-named bundled one, so a seed\nnamed `create-skill` would hide the CLI's bundled platform skill of that name on any\nCLI that does sign in."],[2568,"Cause: no Spark box was reachable, so the Arc answered, and the Arc path returns tool\ncalls as plain text."],[2780,"Cause: path-based Coder apps drop the `?folder=` query before the browser sees\nit, and code-server restores the last opened workspace (often `/home/coder`).\nThe clone itself is fine (`already cloned, skipping` is not a failure)."],[2751,"Cause: provisionerd runs `terraform init` inside the `coder` container.\nDocker's embedded DNS (`127.0.0.11`) forwards names it does not know to\nthe host resolver. When that resolver is Tailscale MagicDNS only, or\nDocker/Kasm iptables has made `100.100.100.100` unreachable from a\ncontainer, every public lookup SERVFAILs. The host CLI is fine; only the\nprovisioner job dies. The lockfile reuse lines (`Reusing previous version\nfrom the dependency lock file`) do not skip the registry: Terraform still\nfetches the discovery document."],[2923,"Cause: Tempo's metrics_generator does not expose span-metrics for Prometheus to scrape.\nIt computes them and `remote_write`s them to `http://prometheus:9090/api/v1/write`, and\nPrometheus answers that with `404 remote write receiver needs to be enabled with\n--web.enable-remote-write-receiver` until the flag is on. The service is healthy either\nway, so the only visible symptom is a tracing dashboard with no series and a Tempo log\nthat repeats `msg=\"non-recoverable error\" ... failedSampleCount` once a flush interval."],[2614,"Cause: that chat has no model config on this deployment, so there is nothing to route to."],[2667,"Cause: that container started before the CLI was baked into the image, or its\nstart-time download failed."],[2705,"Cause: that container was built from an image predating the Docker client toolchain,\nor from the bare-Ubuntu *Minimal shell* preset."],[2747,"Cause: that image is not in the host's local image store, and Terraform never pulls\nit. The `docker_image` data source only inspects the store."],[2855,"Cause: that refresh is best-effort on purpose. With the flag on, `up` pulls the managed\nkeys into the very file compose is about to interpolate\n(`scripts/dev-stack.sh:677-683`), and a vault that cannot be reached must not hold the\nstack down - so a failed pull costs one warning and the bring-up continues on the\ncredentials the file already held. The reason sits directly above it\n(`scripts/lib/infisical.sh:984-992`): `error: the Infisical export failed; <file> is\nuntouched` when the CLI could not reach the vault, `warning: the Infisical export\nproduced an empty file; <file> is untouched` when it answered with nothing, or\n`note: the pull was refused before it ran; <file> keeps the values it has` when a\nrequired key is still empty."],[2560,"Cause: that target used to read `.env` with `. ./.env`, so the shell parsed the\noperator's file as code. An unquoted value that holds a space is a variable\nprefix followed by a COMMAND, and `set -e` ended the run on that line before a\nsingle pull:"],[2911,"Cause: the `mimir` datasource is `http://mimir:8080/prometheus`, and that suffix is\nMimir's Prometheus-compatible query namespace - not its root and not its write endpoint.\n`/api/v1` on its own is the write-side namespace and answers 404 to a `query_range`, so a\ndatasource pointed there still reads green in the UI and returns nothing in a panel. That\nURL is the single most common cause of this symptom."],[2586,"Cause: the auto-compaction summary hung before its first token and nothing abandoned it.\nThe CLI's 300 s wall-clock budget does not abort a call in flight - it is evaluated only\nafter the response returns - so at 300 s its entire effect was to reject a summary that\nhad already streamed in and make the session generate it again. Meanwhile the proxy\nre-dispatched every hung attempt to the same session-pinned Spark box, so one hang cost\nup to three `request_timeout` periods. Both halves are fixed here."],[2872,"Cause: the body publishes single-line values only\n(`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:190-209`). A value that opens a single\nquote without closing it on the same line is a multi-line secret - a PEM, say - and its\ncontinuation lines would be republished as keys of their own, which corrupts the value\nand puts secret bytes into key names. So the whole entry goes, including every line up\nto the one that closes the quote (`:146-153`). The vault stores such a value happily;\nonly the workspace publish drops it, and the host-side pull of the twelve managed keys\nis a different path that never passes through this loop\n(`scripts/lib/infisical.sh:958-1017`)."],[2859,"Cause: the body's first gate, `templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:14-20` - any\none of those six empty on the agent environment prints this and exits `0`, so the\nworkspace boots with no vault secrets and nothing else complains. The resource exists at\nall only while `infisical_enabled` is true (`templates/workspace/main.tf:721`), so this\nis not the flag being off; it is one value that never arrived. The push forwards each of\nthe five read-side values only when `.env` carries something real - `env_get_forwardable` drops an\nempty value and one still spelling `change-me*` (`scripts/push-template.sh:198-205`, the five\nforwards at `:696-730`) - and the template variable then keeps its own empty default\n(`templates/workspace/coder.tf:223-227`), which hands the boot body a key that is\npresent and empty."],[2864,"Cause: the body's second gate, `templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:27-30`. Each\ntwin declares `infisical_pull_on_start` with `default = false`\n(`templates/workspace/coder.tf:270-274`, `templates/devcontainer/coder.tf:257-261`), and\nthe push forwards\n`--var infisical_pull_on_start=true` only when `.env` carries a truthy\n`INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START` *under* a truthy `INFISICAL_ENABLED`\n(`scripts/push-template.sh:636`, `:668-671`), so an adopted vault still ships a boot that never\nwaits on the network. That off is the safe side of a real trade: a fetch that reaches the\nvault is a fetch bounded by the script's own `timeout = 120`\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:730`), and the exit is `0` either way because no secret\nmanager gets to fail a workspace start."],[2794,"Cause: the browser exposes `navigator.clipboard` to a secure context only, and\nCoder's LAN door is plain HTTP by default (`CODER_ACCESS_URL` is the LAN\naddress). Opened that way the editor has no clipboard API to call, so\nright-click paste stops with VS Code's own toast - \"This browser doesn't support\nthe clipboard.readText API needed to trigger a paste, try Ctrl+V instead.\" - and\n`Ctrl+Shift+V` only works at all through the browser's native paste event. A\nClipboard site permission that was once denied does the same damage on an https\norigin, and there VS Code answers `NotAllowedError: Read permission denied`."],[2900,"Cause: the card's `siteMonitor` is a specific path (`/api/health`, `/-/healthy`,\n`/ready`), and \"the container exists\" says nothing about that path answering. Four of the\nsix containers are distroless, so nothing inside them can be asked; the log is the only\ninterrogation available."],[2889,"Cause: the Chat tab is a WebSocket on `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`. Hermes sends a\nkeepalive every 20 seconds. Close code `4403` is the Host / Origin / peer guard."],[2766,"Cause: the CLI is talking to a public subdomain, and Authelia answers its API calls\nwith the sign-in page."],[2605,"Cause: the hop is a proxy behaviour, not a client one, and it needs four things true in the\nsame moment. With any one of them false the summary rides a Spark box exactly as it did\nbefore these two aliases existed - the 107 s to 1915 s measured above - and that is the slow\npath, not a fault. The Grok Build CLI has no compaction-model key, so there is nothing\nclient-side to point elsewhere: the alias the session started on is the whole decision, and\n`[models] session_summary` is still not the compaction lane (see the entry above). The\ndecision lives in `litellm/compaction_router.py`, and the five `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION*`\nknobs named below are read there and nowhere else."],[2621,"Cause: the LiteLLM alias behind that model has no deployment the router will use. The\nArc aliases (`chat`, `coder`, `coder-fast`) go unhealthy the moment their weights are\nnot in the IPEX-LLM container, which answers `model \"...\" not found, try pulling it\nfirst`, and the Spark `agent` aliases go unhealthy when no box answers."],[2919,"Cause: the only host path in the bundle is\n`${STACK_DOCKER_LOG_DIR:-/var/lib/docker/containers}`, mounted read-only into Alloy. If\nthe daemon writes its json-file logs somewhere else, Alloy tails an empty directory and\nLoki's badge stays green, because `/ready` reports the store, not the supply."],[2691,"Cause: the profile asks tmux questions before it hands the tab to anything, and tmux\nexits 1 whenever it refuses. When a running agent meets it, the usual cause is the\nagent's own cleanup sweep - `tmux kill-server`, `tmux kill-session`, `pkill -f tmux` -\nclosing the session the agent is typing in. The tab's process is that session's\nclient, so the command takes the agent, its tool call and the in-flight reply down\ntogether."],[2813,"Cause: the refresh script inherits `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` from the agent, and\n`gh auth login --with-token` refuses stdin while those names are set (`The value of the\nGH_TOKEN environment variable is being used for authentication`). Current templates\nunset the pair, log out, then log in with `--git-protocol https`, and print gh's\nstderr (token redacted) if that still fails - which is the line above."],[2926,"Cause: the same `421` the Hermes entry above describes - this box's `router` answers it\nwhen the Host matches no `server_name` - with one extra way to reach it that the other\nfive names do not have. `scripts/lib/domain.sh` composes the Grafana name from the naming\nparts and reads no flag, so `bootstrap` writes the vhost and `STACK_GRAFANA_URL` even when\n`OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED=false`. The router then has a correct `server_name` with nothing\nbehind it, and the browser gets a `502` from Traefik or a `421` if the Host was rewritten\nbefore it arrived."],[2743,"Cause: the sidecar is unhealthy, its image was never pulled, or the workspace was\ncreated with DinD off."],[2697,"Cause: the three per-owner Grok volumes mount *under* `~/.grok` - `grok-notes-<owner-id>`\nat `~/.grok/notes`, with the memory and sessions pair beside it - and the Docker daemon\ncreates a bind mount's target path as `root:root` 755. Whenever those mount points are\nnew, `~/.grok` arrives owned by root and the workspace user cannot create anything in\nit. This is the same daemon behaviour the\n`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` entry\ncovers one directory over."],[2881,"Cause: this box's `router` answers `421` when the Host matches no `server_name`.\nThe hermes vhost is not typed by hand: `STACK_ROUTER_CONF` in `.env` names the conf\ncompose mounts (`proxy/router.gen.conf`, which bootstrap renders from\n`proxy/router.conf.template`, or the checked-in `proxy/router.conf` while that key is\nempty), and every `server_name` in it is composed from the naming parts. Under\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` a missing or mistyped part is what produces a name nothing\nanswers. Traefik pointed at `:9119` never hits that file. A rewritten Host also\nmisses every block."],[1111,"Caveats, honestly"],[2040,"Change a cap in the env file and recreate. The Prometheus size cap is the one that\nactually bounds the box: it is the only figure that does not depend on how fast the\nseries arrive. Tempo's cap is the odd one, because Tempo 3.x moved it out of the config\nfile - it reaches the container as `-backend-scheduler.provider.work.compaction.block-retention`\non the command line in docker-compose.yml, so editing tempo.yml for it changes nothing.\n`bazelisk run //scripts:storage-clean` is the interactive janitor for the persistent\nlayer if a volume outgrows what you meant it to hold."],[1135,"Changing a model alias in one place and knowing which workspaces see it"],[107,"Changing a template variable without guessing which file owns it"],[1268,"Changing the mode needs a **rebuild** - the three nested mounts either exist or they\ndo not. A workspace that goes from `shared` to `private` starts on whatever those\nthree directories already hold on its own home volume: nothing is copied, and the\nowner's volumes are left alone."],[1319,"chat model configs `agent` (context limit 262144, set as the default; LiteLLM bakes\n`reasoning_effort` `xhigh`), `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` (same\nlane with a baked thinking level, not default), `agent-swarm` (same lane and the same\nbaked level with the session pin lifted for that group, not default), and `chat`\n(32768), for models not already registered. `coder` and `coder-fast` stay out on\npurpose: on this stack's IPEX-LLM runtime those weights return tool calls as plain\ntext with no `tool_calls`, and an agent turn without callable tools cannot browse or\nedit, so registering them would only add a silently broken model to the pickers.\n`context_limit` is the agent's own compaction budget (a 70 per cent default\ncompaction threshold applies), which is why it is pinned per alias. The\ntwo fast-compaction lanes are absent too, for a mechanical reason rather than a measured\none: the hop keys on the summary instruction the Grok CLI appends, the aibridge behind\nthese pickers never sends one, and `compaction-fast` is no client alias at all, so an\nAgents chat on `agent-xhigh-fast` would be a chat on `agent-xhigh` under a longer name.\nBefore that sweep the script asks the proxy `GET /v1/model/info` and `GET /health`\nand joins them on `model_id`, so it knows which aliases still have a healthy\ndeployment. An alias with none is not registered: `chat` goes unhealthy the moment\n`qwen2.5:7b` is not pulled on the Arc, and a dead entry in the model picker is a\nchat that errors on the first send. `/health` is read without\n`?check_deployments=true` because that form fires a live completion per deployment\nand evicts the Spark prompt caches the health-check notes in `litellm/config.yaml`\nwarn about. The gate fails open: a proxy that will not answer leaves every alias\nregisterable, and `CODER_AGENTS_LIVE_ALIASES=\"agent chat\"` overrides it outright."],[1330,"Chat on the Agents page -> if the ask needs more tooling than chat has, Coder Agents\nprovisions or uses a workspace through `execute` -> big multi-file tasks are\n*delegated inside* that workspace: a headless Grok Build CLI run, started backgrounded\nand polled:"],[1743,"Chat stays usable while agent mode quietly loses its tools. That is the point of the\nhop, not an accident."],[2518,"Chat tab never connects, or the socket closes `4403` -> public URL, trusted proxies, or a dropped Upgrade"],[2782,"Check 1: the code-server install script's log is green in the workspace's Scripts/Log\ntab."],[2783,"Check 2: this answers from inside the workspace:"],[2784,"Check 3: the browser is on `https://` with a trusted cert. Plain `http://` is an\ninsecure context, which is what breaks `crypto.randomUUID()`; see \"Public subdomains\n(optional)\" in README.md."],[231,"Check it in the Coder UI afterwards: **Default autostop** must read *Disabled* (0 h)."],[285,"Check it in the Coder UI: the template editor's **Default autostop** reads *Disabled*\n(0 h), and a workspace's **Schedule** tab shows **Enable Autostop** unchecked."],[1766,"Check that `SPARK_1_API_KEY`, `SPARK_2_API_KEY` and `SPARK_3_API_KEY` are still\nnon-empty. An empty key fails every request inside the proxy, before it ever reaches\nthe box."],[2920,"Check the source path on the stack host, where the daemon actually is (inside a workspace\n`docker` addresses that workspace's own sidecar, so the answer you get is about the wrong\ndaemon):"],[2315,"Checking the other machine"],[2323,"Checking the other machine (including the Hermes WebSocket), Authelia, and the two follow-ups left by hand"],[764,"Checks"],[3031,"Cherry-picking is for a coherent run of commits you want verbatim, and `-x` keeps the source SHA\nin the message so the provenance survives:"],[105,"Choosing between the plain container and the devcontainer before you click create"],[762,"Chrome that arrives on its own"],[8,"CI and protection"],[3011,"Climb as far as you need and stop."],[1736,"Cline and Roo editor traffic carries no session id: the extension settings have no\ncustom-header field."],[1302,"Cline and Roo/Kilo are pre-wired to the LiteLLM proxy (endpoint from `litellm_url`,\ndefault `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`; model alias `agent`, with\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`/`agent-xhigh-fast`/`agent-swarm`/`agent-swarm-fast`/`coder`/`coder-fast`/`chat`\nlisted in `__evoT1StackModels`). The seed is\n`settings.json.tftpl`, copied onto the per-owner volume\n`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` (mounted at `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`) only\nwhile that file is absent, where it stays as the shared overlay. What code-server\nactually reads, `~/.local/share/code-server/User/settings.json`, is a real file on\nthis workspace's own home volume, which `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl`\nrewrites on every start in three passes: the per-workspace render as the base, then\nevery key of that overlay on top so an API key entered once still wins, then\n`workbench.colorTheme` re-asserted from the render last, so a theme put in the shared\noverlay loses to this workspace's own theme (the **Editor colour theme** field,\n`vscode_color_theme`, default `Default Dark Modern`). Startup chowns that volume to\nthe home owner before the first write (empty named volumes are `root:root`\n755). Edits to the overlay stick, and every workspace\nthat owner has gets them layered over its own render; delete it to copy the template\nin again. From the\nhost it is the volume's `settings.json`:"],[70,"Clone and build"],[84,"Cloning as the workspace user"],[92,"Cloning more than one repository"],[2330,"Cloudflare proxies the name (orange cloud) in front of Traefik. WebSockets are on\nthere by default, which is what the Hermes Chat tab needs."],[462,"code-server's User settings file, `~/.local/share/code-server/User/settings.json`, is\na real file on this workspace's own home volume. Each start writes it in three passes:\nthe per-workspace render (which carries `workbench.colorTheme` from the **Editor colour\ntheme** field), then every key from that shared overlay on top so an API key entered\nonce stays, then the theme re-asserted last so the overlay can never veto this\nworkspace's own theme. The write is atomic (`<path>.tmp` then `mv`)."],[1113,"Coder Agents (control-plane)"],[2463,"Coder Agents chat refuses every prompt, or its model dropdown is empty -> no model config"],[1737,"Coder Agents chats carry none either. Coder's aibridge forwards model calls with the\nprovider key only and adds no per-conversation session id."],[1381,"Coder Agents is control-plane-native. This stack does not wrap Grok Build: the wiring\nabove *teaches* (skill + instructions) and *equips* (the workspace-side CLI with its\nLiteLLM alias) the native Coder agent to drive Grok Build when work is too big to\nhand-drive. Delegation is a taught habit, not the product."],[2464,"Coder Agents offers a model that errors, or a chat never gets a title -> an alias with no healthy deployment"],[1133,"Coder Agents on the control plane and the model config it needs"],[2951,"Coder and template parameters"],[2520,"Coder login problems -> the admin account, and `CODER_ACCESS_URL`"],[279,"Coder reads the template's **Default autostop** (its default TTL) when it seeds a\nworkspace. That field also decides whether the create form's **Enable Autostop**\narrives ticked, and it ships at 24 h - which is how a workspace would otherwise start\nwith a shutdown deadline nobody chose."],[1852,"Coder side"],[1305,"Coder v2.36.6 ships *Coder Agents*: a coding agent that lives in the Coder **server** -\na chat UI and an API beside your workspaces, not inside one. A chat runs its tools\n(`read_file`, `edit_files`, `execute`, `spawn_agent`) against a workspace over that\nworkspace's agent connection, and consults two pieces of per-workspace context first:\nan **instructions** file and any **skills** it discovers. The agent itself is upstream\nCoder, not a home-grown agent."],[46,"Coder workspace templates"],[48,"Coder workspace templates"],[1085,"Coder workspace templates — what the two templates build and which\nvariable does what."],[1575,"Coder workspace templates owns the templates, the create form and\nthe variables; Grok Build, Coder Agents, and the workspace terminal\nowns what runs inside the container once it is up."],[1326,"Coder's chat dies at its context budget when `compaction` and `title_generation` both\nsit on a dead `chat` alias. `bazelisk run //scripts:coder-agents -- --apply` moves the\nstale pin, 