e an `unset PATH HOME IFS` line had the body appended one instead\n(`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:225-229`). Clear whatever wrote to the\nstaged file, prove the vault answers from the stack host, and restart the workspace to\nrun the fetch again:"],[974,"coverage.py resolves the symlink and records the **checkout** path, while the\nprocess cwd is the runfiles tree. A relative `--include=scripts/lib/*.py` is\nmatched against the cwd, matches nothing, prints `No data to report`, and exits\n`0`. The gate therefore decides from `tests/coverage/py_report.py`, which lists\nthe `.py` files the tree ships and requires each one to appear in the machine-\nreadable report above the floor. A module nobody imported is a named failure."],[2674,"Covers all six stack-shipped skill commands: `/ez-learn`, `/ez-skillify`,\n`/ez-create-skill`, `/ez-bazel-first`, `/ez-strong-types` and `/ez-trunk-based-repo`. The\ndiagnosis and the commands below are identical for each one; only the name changes, so read\n`<name>` as whichever of the six you are chasing. The last three are the\nrepository-conversion family and ride `coder_script.stack_repo_skills` rather than\n`coder_script.stack_skills`, so a missing one of those points at that body's log."],[1045,"CPU, memory, CPU temperature and disk are host-wide through `/proc`. A network widget\nonly sees the container's own netns (`lo`/`eth0`) - `/sys/class/net` is namespaced, so\nthe host Wi-Fi NIC stays invisible even with `/sys` mounted. That tile reads `eth0`."],[1083,"Create the first workspace. One repository per workspace is the default, and the template\npicks itself from the URL:"],[1075,"Create the one file that holds real secrets, and lock it down before anything else reads\nit:"],[330,"Create the workspace it picks:"],[62,"Create-form fields"],[1068,"Creating the first workspace from a git URL"],[57,"Credentials"],[734,"Credentials still arrive by the one supported path: Coder External Auth mints them, and\nthe gh auth store is what git actually reads. That store is the target because\n`GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` are the copy injected when the container started and expire\nabout an hour later, and the plain `!gh auth git-credential` helper echoes that pair, so\ngit would keep sending a dead token. The helper this stack installs clears the pair first,\nwhich is why `gh auth setup-git` alone is not enough. There is no static token and no PAT\nin this repo's tooling."],[23,"Cut `feat/…`, `fix/…`, `refactor/…` (and `docs/…`, `test/…`, `chore/…`, `ci/…`) from the\ncurrent tip of `main`, one branch per change, and delete it the moment it merges. A branch\nthat outlives its change is a branch nobody rebases any more.\n`bazelisk run //scripts:branch-hygiene` is the check for that sentence and for the two\nbelow: it lists every branch the trunk already contains, every unmerged branch past the\nbranch lifetime, and every branch that carries an environment in its name, each printed\nwith its tip SHA because that is the record a deletion has to leave behind. It reports\nand never deletes - `--plan` prints the commands, and deleting stays the operator's call.\nPass `--fetch` before acting on what it names, so the remote rows are the remote's\ncurrent answer rather than the last one this checkout happened to see. That flag is\nthe run's only write: it is `git remote update --prune`, which refreshes and prunes\nthis checkout's own remote-tracking refs and deletes nothing on the remote."],[603,"Daily commands"],[996,"Dashboard and desktops"],[998,"Dashboard and desktops"],[1419,"Dashboard on host port **9119**, from `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PORT`. The dev sandbox shifts\nit to **19119** the same way it shifts every other published port."],[2914,"Data in `prometheus` and none in `mimir`: read what the write did."],[2071,"Day-to-day operation — the dashboard and desktops, what to refresh after a git pull, the public shape, and the symptom-first fix list."],[1951,"Deciding what to add next without guessing what is already wired"],[2152,"Deciding whether adopting the vault is worth it, from what is actually wired"],[108,"Deep reference for `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/`: what they build,\nhow they get pushed, what each template variable means, and how the create form and the\nin-editor terminal and editor behave."],[47,"Deep reference for `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/`: what they build, how they get pushed, and what every variable means."],[1624,"Deep reference for the model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request\npicks a backend, what a pinned conversation does, and what happens when a Spark box goes\ndown."],[1138,"Deep reference for what runs *inside* a workspace and in the Coder control plane: the\nterminal profiles, the per-user `~/.grok` profile, where the CLI and the chat agent get\ntheir models, and what the stack seeds for the agent."],[2542,"Deep, symptom-first list. The operational `dev-stack`/`prod-stack`, `search-check` and\n`spark-verify` targets are described in README.md and\nproject-conventions.md."],[849,"Default scope reads `git diff HEAD`; add `--staged` for the index, `--untracked` to count files git\ndoes not track yet (a file you just created is exactly the case worth covering), or `--ref <ref>` for\nwhat this branch adds over a base. `--all` also lists the aggregate suites that reach everything\n(`//:test`, `//:test-fast`, `//:test-all` and `//tests:bats`), which is useful when you want the full\nset named explicitly rather than the leaf suites you can invoke. The default already includes the\nlint targets: every test rule here, `//lints:shell` and `//lints:shfmt` among them, is an `sh_test`."],[2966,"Deleting the `development` branch without losing a commit that was only ever on it"],[1912,"Dependabot **secrets** are a different permission that this App does not currently hold, which is\nwhy the CLI does not attempt them. When a step is refused the CLI prints the endpoint, GitHub's\nmessage, and the permission to ask for, then re-mints nothing and continues."],[2967,"Deploying dev, staging and production from one commit instead of three builds"],[1144,"Detaching one needs its tty: this tmux 3.4 answers `detach-client -t` for a\n`/dev/pts/…` path, refuses a pid with `can't find client`, and has no\n`kill-client` command at all."],[292,"Details that matter:"],[1523,"Dev sandbox, which puts the dashboard on 19119:"],[86,"Dev-container preflight"],[1088,"Develop and\nBranching — the gates a change passes on its way back to `main`."],[1623,"Diagnosing a wrong-backend answer from the routing rather than from the client"],[2056,"Did Grafana come up and can it read its own database:"],[2644,"Discover live tool names with `search_tool`, then call `use_tool` with that\nschema. Do not hardcode MCP tool names from memory."],[3052,"Discovery reloads from disk, so the copy above needs a new turn before the slash command\nappears. The run ends with the report the skill specifies: summary, criteria table, branch\ndecisions, environment mapping, flags introduced, promotion commands, deletions pending\nconfirmation, and next manual steps."],[2380,"DNS records for all seven names, each one label deep and Cloudflare-proxied (orange\ncloud). WebSockets are on there by default. A missing record shows up as a DNS\nerror, a rewritten Host as `421`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm and LiteLLM resolve\nthat way and complete the TLS handshake, checked from this box; the Hermes\nname is the same zone and the same proxy. The Grafana and Infisical records are new\nwith their names and have not been measured from this box, so check them there."],[749,"Do not copy the sentinel into another repository. An application repo's\n`.devcontainer/` is exactly what `new-workspace` is supposed to find, and the sentinel in\nfront of it silently downgrades that workspace to the plain template."],[2839,"Do not hand-roll a credential helper. Measured across 222 past sessions in this\nworkspace: 29 hit a GitHub auth error (`Bad credentials` 19, `Password authentication is\nnot supported` 11, `could not read Username` 5, `unrecognized subcommand` 4), and agents\nhand-rolled their own askpass scripts in 5 sessions and hand-edited `credential.helper`\nin 4. Both were wrong the same way: they fought the credential-helper precedence that\n`fix` repairs, and they put a live token where `ps` could read it, while the line that\nnames the real cause was one `check` away. `fix` is idempotent, keeps every token off\nargv and out of any file it creates, and repairs the helper plus the store it reads."],[2947,"Docker Compose"],[703,"Docker creates a named volume once and reuses it, so the caches survive recreations\nuntil you deliberately drop them. The volumes belong to the host's daemon, so a host\nshell is the reliable place to run this: from inside the container the same commands\nwork only while the config's socket mount is in place."],[310,"Docker creates the missing host directory itself (root-owned) and `startup.sh.tftpl`\nchowns it."],[65,"Docker-in-docker"],[2484,"Docker-in-docker"],[2535,"Docker-in-Docker, Terraform, and the host `coder` CLI"],[2929,"Does the router carry the vhost? `STACK_ROUTER_CONF` in `.env` decides which conf\ncompose mounts, so a rendered `proxy/router.gen.conf` that predates the Grafana naming\nhas no `grafana` block in it. Re-run `bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` and compare."],[2299,"Dollar escaping differs across those two layers. A `templatefile()` `.tftpl` treats `$$`\nas an escaped `$` everywhere (`$name` or `$${name}`), and leaves `$((` arithmetic\nalone. An HCL heredoc in `main.tf` (`<<-EOT`) only escapes `$${name}` to `${name}`;\na lone `$` is already literal, so shell arithmetic is `$((...))` with one dollar.\n`$$((` is two dollars, and Coder runs `coder_script` bodies as `bash -c`, which\nexpands `$$` to the PID. Do not wrap `templatefile()` in `chomp()`."],[704,"Drop them when a Bazel upgrade or a corrupt action cache is the suspect, not as\nroutine cleanup - deleting them costs one cold gate run each."],[329,"Dry run (decision only, creates nothing):"],[680,"Each `KEY` there is also an `ARG` in `images/dev/Dockerfile`, and the `ARG` default\nmirrors the pin, so a bare `docker build` matches a scripted one."],[2857,"Each answer is one line: `note: <KEY> is empty; set it in <file> before this verb can\nreach the vault` per key that is not filled (`scripts/infisical.sh:215`),\n`note: no Infisical CLI installed; run: bazelisk run //scripts:infisical -- install-cli`\nwhen the binary is what is missing (`scripts/infisical.sh:346`), and `warning: the\nmachine identity could not exchange a token; check the client id and secret, and that\nthe vault answers` when the vault is there but will not authenticate the identity\n(`scripts/infisical.sh:361`). Then retry the pull and bring the stack up again, because\ncompose reads the file only when it recreates a container\n(`scripts/infisical.sh:527`):"],[411,"Each body opens with its own `== <body>: start ==` and, on every path that got that far, its\nfinal act is `boot_summary`, so the eleven bodies of one agent share a single log and stay\ntellable apart by the name in those two lines (`boot_begin`'s first argument, echoed again in\nthe `summary:`). `note:` is the only verb nothing counts, which is how a gated-off body\nreads: notes, then `summary: 0 ok, 0 warnings, 0 failed`."],[640,"Each CLI target also declares the repo files that script reads by path: the libs it\nsources (`_SCRIPT_LIBS`) and the payload groups whose files it opens (`_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD`),\nboth keyed by script stem in `scripts/BUILD.bazel`. Those payload groups live one per\npayload directory (`//litellm:config`, `//searxng:settings`, `//homepage:config`,\n`//homepage:icons`, `//hermes:seed`, `//hermes:skills`, `//proxy:confs`,\n`//images/dev:dockerfile`, `//images/dev:tool_versions`, and a `:payload` in each of\n`templates/common`, `templates/workspace`, `templates/devcontainer`), with the root\nBUILD.bazel holding the groups that have no directory of their own (`//:compose_file`,\n`//:env_sample`, `//:env_example`, `//:license`, `//:devcontainer`, `//:githooks`, and the\n`//:ci_*` groups). `//:stack_data` is the explicit aggregate of all of them, and\n`//scripts:doc_sources` (every `scripts/**.sh`, so a sourced `scripts/lib/*.sh` resolves\ntoo) rides along on every CLI. `proxy/certs/` is `.bazelignored` (bootstrap issues those on the host), so a\n`bazelisk run` of a stack runner, of `push-template`, or of the six CLIs that read a file the\ncheckout alone holds (`dashboard-password`, `pull-models`, `coder-agents`, `spark-verify`,\n`spark-configure`, `build-dev-image`) prefers `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout)\nover the runfiles copy as the compose project directory, the live `.env`, and the template tree (runfiles\n`templates/**` are symlinks that Coder cannot follow after unpack). Without the committed payloads a\nsandboxed run would read a partial copy of the repo and fail for the wrong reason. A new\ntop-level payload directory gets its own package with its own `filegroup` targets, joins\n`//:stack_data` by label, and is named in the map of every target that reads it: `test-fast`\nand `lint` only see repo files that travel in runfiles, so an unlisted directory is invisible\nto the harness rather than checked loosely."],[1747,"Each fast lane hops to the lane it is the twin of:\n`- agent-xhigh-fast: [\"agent-xhigh\", \"coder\"]` and\n`- agent-swarm-fast: [\"agent-swarm\", \"coder\"]`\n(`litellm/config.yaml:887-888`). Same boxes, same baked level, same pin behaviour, so a\nsession that lost its own group keeps everything except that group's routing state. Both\nchains are acyclic and end at `coder` like every other here: agent-xhigh-fast ->\nagent-xhigh -> coder, agent-swarm-fast -> agent-swarm -> coder."],[2543,"Each fence below is one copy-paste action. A heading quotes the error string where there\nis one, so start with `grep -n` on the text you saw."],[401,"Each file also gets a twin in the `~/.agents` tree, because the `execute`-lane delegation\nthis stack teaches starts `grok -p` inside the workspace and that CLI reads the\n`~/.agents` convention. The chat agent would only ever take the `~/.coder` copies - skill\ndedupe is first-match-wins, home scope before working-dir paths - so one agent never\nloads a skill twice; the twins exist for the CLI, not as a second source for chat."],[1211,"Each is a markdown file with YAML frontmatter whose body is the child's system prompt; the\nseeds are `templates/common/<name>.AGENT.md` and they land at\n`~/.grok/agents/<name>.md`. A definition is what fixes a role's model, tools and prompt\nmode, which a persona deliberately does not (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:77`)."],[1885,"Each lane lives at `<scaffold>/.template-lanes/<lane>/`. A file there is copied over the rendered\ntree at the same path, so a lane may add files or replace a core one. `LANE.md` and\n`gitignore.fragment` are lane metadata: the renderer appends the fragment to `.gitignore` and drops\nboth from the output. Ask for a lane the fetched tree does not have and the run dies naming the\nlanes it does offer."],[2590,"Each link in the chain, measured:"],[780,"Each page writes its own `# H1`, once, as the first heading of the body, spelled exactly like\nthe frontmatter `title`. That heading is the one the table of contents and every `#anchor`\npoint at, so `DocsH1` prints it and then the description beneath it as the lead paragraph. A\npage whose first heading is not its title keeps a plain heading and gets no lead paragraph,\nwhich is the visible sign that the two strings drifted apart."],[1710,"Each reader lane is the default for its own role and is set by its own template\nvariable, so either rolls back alone without moving the other or the writers'\n`agent-swarm` default:"],[1367,"Each seeded file carries the stack marker - the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack`\ncomment, or the `\"__evoT1Stack\": true` / `__evoT1Stack = true` key in a JSON or TOML\nfile - in `SKILL.md` directly under the `---` frontmatter, since the agent's skill\nparser requires frontmatter on line 1 and silently drops anything else. Delete a file\nto take it over; edit it and the stack leaves it alone. `~/.grok/config.toml` is the\none seed where deleting a file is not the way: it needs both marker lines gone, see\nThe rendered `~/.grok` config. Either way the marker\nnever reaches the model: the agent strips HTML comments from injected instruction\ntext."],[577,"Each service name is **one label** under the zone and carries its prefix with a\n**HYPHEN**: a wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so the dotted\ntwo-label form has no certificate at the public edge."],[343,"Each template creates one Docker container per workspace. Extra named volumes\n(`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` at `~/.shared/vscode`, in shared mode the three state\nvolumes `grok-notes-<owner-id>` at `~/.grok/notes`, `grok-memory-<owner-id>` at\n`~/.grok/memory` and `grok-sessions-<owner-id>` at `~/.grok/sessions`, and the host-wide\n`grok-session-archive` at `/var/lib/grok-session-archive`) are chowned to the home owner\nby `startup.sh.tftpl` - the first four at its start, the archive by its own stage -\nbecause Docker creates an empty named volume as `root:root`\n755 and the agent runs as `coder`. The archive volume sits outside `~/.grok` on purpose\nand is read by nothing in the workspace: see\nthe out-of-band session archive."],[115,"Each template dir keeps its own independent copy of the `.tftpl` payloads\n(`startup.sh.tftpl`, `settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). Change one twin,\nchange the other."],[1169,"Each template's startup script probes `command -v grok` on every start and\nre-downloads from `https://x.ai/cli/install.sh` - bounded, with retries - only\nwhile `grok` is missing from PATH. That installer follows the `stable` pointer, not\nthis pin. It deliberately does **not** fetch on every boot: the bake-in under\n`/usr/local/bin` is the upgrade path, so shipping a new CLI means bumping the pin,\nrebuilding the image (`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`) and re-pushing the templates."],[1744,"Each thinking alias carries the same hop of its own - `agent-low: [\"coder\"]`, and the\nsame for `agent-medium` and `agent-xhigh`. Groups are independent, so none of them\ninherits `agent`'s entry, and the five pinned lanes go down with the same boxes."],[489,"Each workspace's agent injects a `GIT_ASKPASS` helper pointing at the `coder` binary it\nstages, alongside the `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` pair in the agent environment. Some\nbuilds of that binary answer every prompt with `unrecognized subcommand`, so the stack\ndepends on the helper below rather than on askpass, and `check` prints a note line when\nthat askpass answers nothing."],[1764,"Edit `litellm/config.yaml`."],[1607,"Editing an alias"],[134,"Editing the common copy changes both templates; the staged copy overwrites any\nsame-named file the template dir still carries."],[90,"Editor settings seed"],[2495,"Editor, credentials, the clone"],[2536,"Editor, credentials, the clone, UI logins, and the public names (`421`, Chat `4403`, Traefik)"],[88,"Editor, terminal, and the metadata tiles"],[1026,"Either form rewrites `.env`, recreates the container and verifies the new value landed.\nThe script's job is keeping the secret out of shell history, `ps` and git, and leaving\n`.env` at mode 600."],[321,"Either way `folder` is the clone, so code-server and its terminal profiles land on the\nrepository, and the **Web Terminal** button `cd`s there too."],[2576,"Either way keep `SPARK_n_API_KEY` non-empty: `SPARK_1_API_KEY`, `SPARK_2_API_KEY`,\n`SPARK_3_API_KEY`. An empty key fails every request inside the proxy before it ever\nreaches the box."],[1263,"Either way the fix is the seed under `templates/common/` and a re-materialize, never a patch\nto the copy that is running - the same rule the two skill families above live by\n(the three stack-seeded skill commands)."],[2354,"empty is LAN-only;"],[2410,"Enrolling or un-enrolling the fleet with one key, without editing a compose file"],[2109,"Enrolling the Sparks without hand-editing a Prometheus config"],[2388,"Enrolling the three DGX Sparks into the Prometheus stack: the two exporter containers a box needs, the one opt-in key, the plan/prove/install commands, how the boxes reach Prometheus through file_sd, and what this change leaves unproven."],[352,"Ensures the **Grok Build CLI** is on PATH. The golden image ships it, so the\n`https://x.ai/cli/install.sh` download runs only where `grok` is missing from PATH -\nthe `ubuntu:24.04` image option, or a home volume that never received the CLI."],[576,"Entries are DNS names, not URLs: no scheme, no port, no path. The entry with no\nprefix is the dashboard."],[805,"Enumerate targets instead of guessing them"],[814,"Enumerating real target names instead of guessing them"],[3009,"Environment is a workspace or an `envs/<env>/` directory that instantiates one shared module;\n`terraform plan` against `envs/staging` and `envs/production` is the same module revision twice.\nArgoCD gets one `Application` per environment, all pointing at the same Kustomize or chart path,\nand promotion is a commit that bumps the image tag (by hand, or by the Image Updater). A\nper-environment ArgoCD branch is a sync-drift generator and a rollback trap."],[44,"Environments are where future protection rules and secrets go, per environment:\n`development`, `staging`, `production`. `production` stays restricted to `main` — or to\ntags — with required reviewers. See the header comment in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`.\nThose three words are environments and never branch names, and\n`bazelisk run //scripts:branch-hygiene` is what keeps that honest: a branch or a\nremote-tracking ref named `dev`, `staging`, `prod`, `uat` or the like - bare, or as the\nfirst segment of a path - is an error and exits 1. It is an operator CLI rather than a\nCI step on purpose: the branches that break the rule live on the remote, where a job\nchecked out at `main` cannot see them."],[2946,"Environments without environment branches"],[426,"Every `[mcp_servers.*]` block the render writes names its binary by the absolute path the\ngolden image bakes (`/opt/npm-global/bin/...`), so the config and the image are two\nartifacts that have to agree - and they do not always. A workspace running an image built\nbefore that binary was added boots with a lane that can never start, and the CLI only\nsays so in its own MCP log."],[1030,"every `{{HOMEPAGE_VAR_*}}` token has a key in `docker-compose.yml`, and every\n`HOMEPAGE_VAR_*` key is used by some config file"],[441,"Every `INFISICAL_` name is filtered out of that file, and that filter is the load-bearing\nline (`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:180`): the vault's own address and\nUniversal Auth pair are plausible keys in the very project being exported, and they must\nnot be republished as workspace environment through a file, when the agent environment is\nthe place Coder renders them per start (`:86-87`). The log line that follows reports a key\n*count*, never a value, and counts what the publish loop actually wrote (`:249-251`)."],[2335,"Every `location` adds `X-Evo-Proxy` with the upstream name, and `always` is what\nmakes it survive a 502. Read it beside `X-Coder-Request-Id`, which Coder's own proxy\nsets on an answered request: neither header means the request stopped at Traefik or\nCloudflare, and `421` means the Host header matched no server block here."],[2285,"Every `scripts/**/*.sh` file starts with a `# ## ` header that names the file and says whether it is an entry point or a sourced library."],[1632,"Every alias is a stable name. A workspace setting never has to change when a backend\nmoves behind one."],[2659,"Every body speaks the same shapes (`templates/common/boot-log.lib.sh`, handed to each body\nas the `boot_log_lib` key of its `templatefile()` map):"],[34,"Every change answers something, and the tracker is where the answer gets recorded. This is the\nsame etiquette `templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` teaches every workspace, so a bug an agent\nfixes and a bug a human fixes leave the tracker in the same state."],[2110,"Every command here is a Bazel target run from a **host** shell. Inside a Coder workspace\nthe `docker` CLI only reaches that workspace's own DinD sidecar, so a check aimed at the\nstack's own containers has to be run where the stack lives."],[100,"Every create-form field and template variable, with its default"],[435,"Every credential rides the **agent environment** rather than the script body, for the\nreason every credential in `main.tf` rides it: Coder stores a rendered\n`coder_script.script` as text on the server, so a value interpolated into one would sit in\nCoder's database in plain sight, while an agent-env value is rendered per start and never\nwritten to disk (`templates/workspace/main.tf:687-691`). The body accordingly declares no\nTerraform variable of its own - its `templatefile()` map is the single `boot_log_lib` key"],[406,"Every exec'd body narrates itself, so a workspace start reads as a staged run rather than a\nwall of file copies. The verbs are `scripts/lib/common.sh`'s own words - `== section ==`,\n`-> phase`, `ok:`, `warning:`, `note:`, `error:`, and a closing `summary:` - so one\nvocabulary covers the host CLIs and the workspace boot."],[547,"Every flag goes **after** the subcommand, so `update-configs --dry-run env` is a\nusage error and prints the usage rather than guessing."],[2286,"Every function carries this block immediately above the definition:"],[919,"Every function in `scripts/` carries a `# @function` block with `Globals:`,\n`Arguments:`, `Outputs:`, and `Returns:`, and each `Arguments:` line states what\nthe parameter is. `//tests:doc_coverage` enforces the block and\n`//tests:shell_inventory` enforces that a test names the function."],[2288,"Every function under `scripts/` is named by an asserting test under `tests/`: one suite per\nmodule, plus one test per entry-level function that drives the whole script (`tests/bats/\nentry-fns.bats` is where those live). `tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a name is\nmissing, and `tests/doc_coverage.sh` when a `# @function` block is missing or incomplete.\nTests are hermetic: docker, ssh, curl, git and the other external tools resolve through\n`tests/bats/tool_stubs`."],[2166,"Every key beginning `INFISICAL_` is dropped, so the vault's address and its Universal Auth pair -\nboth plausible keys in the very project being exported - are never republished as workspace\nenvironment. Those names reach the workspace only on the agent environment, which Coder renders\nper start and never writes to disk."],[1257,"Every multi-file change ends with an independent review panel that wrote none of it, on\n`agent-review`, before anything is reported done: three angles sent in one turn\n(requirements, proof, adversary), each owning no file, where one skeptic over all three\nangles is enough for a single-file fix. The rulebook states it as a requirement,\nnot a suggestion (`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, \"Mandatory independent review\"),\nand its reasoning is one sentence: a delegated result is a claim, and a review that trusts\nthe claim rather than the artifact is not a review. Each panelist re-runs the producers'\n`done:` commands rather than reading their reports of them, which is why `tester`,\n`reviewer` and `security-auditor` exist as separate definitions whose spawn tool reaches\nread-only mappers, rather than as instructions tacked onto the writer."],[2167,"Every name on the body's deny list is dropped, compared on the key upper-cased so `BASH_ENV`,\n`bash_env` and `Bash_Env` all fall in one arm. The list is what a shell, the dynamic loader, an\ninterpreter or git treats as code or as loader configuration rather than as data: `BASH_ENV` and\n`ENV` (executed by every non-interactive bash), `PROMPT_COMMAND` (by every interactive one),\n`PS4` and `IFS` (how the sourcing shell parses itself), `PATH` and `HOME` (which binary and\nwhich config the next command reads), the `LD_*` group (`LD_PRELOAD`, `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`,\n`LD_AUDIT`, `LD_DEBUG`), the interpreter-option group (`NODE_OPTIONS`, `PYTHONPATH`,\n`PYTHONSTARTUP`, `PERL5OPT`, `PERL5LIB`, `RUBYOPT`), the `GIT_*` group (`GIT_SSH_COMMAND`,\n`GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`, `GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF`, `GIT_DIR`), and the program-choice group (`PAGER`,\n`LESS`, `EDITOR`, `VISUAL`, `SSH_ASKPASS`, `SUDO_ASKPASS`)."],[502,"Every one prints slot names, credential kinds and verdicts, and none of them prints a\ntoken. Repo convention: `bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- <subcommand> ...`."],[776,"Every page declares three fields, and `docs-site/tests/unit/docs-content.test.ts` refuses a\npage that is missing any of them:"],[394,"Every path that cannot decide leaves the file where it is and says nothing: a `find` or\n`basename` that is missing or errors, a `readlink` that cannot answer (the body runs it\nand checks the result instead of just looking for the binary, since an empty answer\nreads exactly as \"no descriptor points here\"), a `/proc` with nothing to scan, an\narchive directory that cannot be made, an archive name already taken, and a `mv` that is\nrefused. The E325 prompt then waits for a person, which is a visible nuisance with no\ndata behind it, while a wrong deletion is quiet and has data behind it."],[1823,"Every row below is a `check` line you can get instead of an `ok`, and each one\nhas booted dead in the field."],[414,"Every script the template renders is a `templatefile()` body: the agent's\n`startup_script` (`startup.sh.tftpl`) and one body per `coder_script` -\n`agents-seed.sh.tftpl`, `code-server.sh.tftpl`, `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`,\n`infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl`, `stack-fleet.sh.tftpl`, `stack-advisors.sh.tftpl`,\n`stack-hooks.sh.tftpl`, the two skill\nfamilies, and `dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl` in the devcontainer twin. Every braced shell\nexpansion in one has to be written `$${name}`; the\none-dollar braced form is swallowed as an interpolation, in a comment or a `for` loop as\nreadily as in a command. The unbraced form (`$name`) needs no escape.\n`infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl` is the sparsest body: its map is the single `boot_log_lib`\nkey (`templates/workspace/main.tf:783-785`), so apart from the one line that takes it every\nbraced expansion in it is shell text, none of it interpolates, and the rendered bytes are\nthe same for every workspace."],[1031,"every service and bookmark group has a `layout` entry, and every `layout` key names a\nreal group; a `style: row` group's `columns` equals the number of cards it holds"],[367,"Every stage from the Coder Agents seeds onward is best-effort: an offline first boot has\nto leave a working terminal, and a repository that will not clone has to leave a\nworkspace someone can fix, so nothing there may abort the script. In `devcontainer` a\nmissing or half-cloned folder is not silent either: `coder_devcontainer` fails on its own\nwith a visible error naming the folder it looked in, and the clone stage warns first when\na clone arrives with no devcontainer configuration in it."],[2740,"Every start re-renders `config.toml` from the template, so the push only clears a\ntemplate-level value. When the number sits on the workspace, put the fleet's own width\nback there instead:"],[950,"Every suite under `tests/` is hermetic Bats: it runs against stubs and a throwaway tree,\nnever against a daemon, a network, or the operator's real `.env`."],[2058,"Every target should read `\"health\":\"up\"`. A target missing entirely is the scrape config;\na target present and `down` is the app not serving `/metrics`, or - for `litellm` alone -\nthe bearer file not holding the key the proxy answers with. That failure describes itself\ntwice over: a file that is not there leaves one red target whose `lastError` names\n`/etc/prometheus-secrets/litellm_token` while Prometheus loads the rest of the config\nnormally, and a file holding the wrong key brings LiteLLM's 401 body `No connected db.`,\nwhich at v1.102.1 is the wrong-token branch rather than a database complaint - its auth\npath compares the bearer against `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` and returns PROXY_ADMIN before it\nlooks for a DB. Either way the fix is the file and a recreate of `prometheus` so Compose\nrenders it again; the metrics stay authenticated. What that 401 offers instead is turning\nthe endpoint's auth off (`litellm_settings.require_auth_for_metrics_endpoint`), and this\nstack refuses it: `:4000` is published to the LAN, and these metrics name model aliases\nand traffic volumes."],[898,"Every value has a declared shape at the boundary where it enters a module, and a\nbroad type is a design smell rather than a shortcut. Where a language has a type\nsystem, the strictest setting that compiles is the setting the repo uses. Where it\nhas none, the convention is the gate."],[2169,"Every value is rewritten rather than copied, and that is a security step, not a stylistic one -\nsee the last bullet of The credential boundary for the upstream\nquoting bug that makes it necessary. Two shapes still do not land, and the log says a different\nthing for each:"],[861,"Every verb is one of these, so a question outside the verb set needs one predicate, not a grep plan.\nAll of these run warm in about 50 to 70 ms."],[1264,"Every workspace gets its own `/home/coder` volume, and `~/.grok` sits on it as an\nordinary directory, `config.toml` included. What is shared is **state**: three nested\nmounts inside that home volume, one per concern, each a Docker volume named for the\nowner (`grok-notes-<owner-id>`, `grok-memory-<owner-id>`, `grok-sessions-<owner-id>`),\nreferenced by name rather than declared as a Terraform resource, which every workspace\nof that owner mounts read-write at its own path **while that workspace is in `shared`\nmode**."],[1722,"Everything a caller can tune is one of five environment variables, read in\n`litellm/compaction_router.py` and nowhere else, which makes the litellm service's\n`environment:` block in `docker-compose.yml` the whole policy:"],[2180,"Everything an operator does goes through one CLI, from any cwd:"],[172,"Everything Coder-facing runs **inside the coder container**, against\n`http://127.0.0.1:3000`."],[624,"Everything else is a Bazel target, including every `scripts/*.sh`. CI repeats the gate from\na clean runner inside the same golden image: `.github/workflows/ci.yml` builds\n`images/dev` with buildx and the Actions layer cache and docker-runs the two suites below in\nit, so the bazel job does not install the pins onto the runner at all — the image already\ncarries them. The persistent cache on that path is the buildx GHA layer cache, so a pull\nrequest that leaves `images/dev/**` alone replays the image layers."],[2290,"Everything else is a Bazel target:"],[2082,"Everything else is a target those two feed:"],[383,"Everything else under `~/.grok` - `config.toml` above all - is a regular file on this\nworkspace's own home volume. Each start re-renders `config.toml` for this workspace\nalone, so its default model (or, in `devcontainer`, its model alias), its theme and\nits config extras are its own, and a hand edit there lasts until that one workspace\nrestarts."],[2031,"Everything lands on named volumes under Docker's volume root on the host disk. Nothing\nhere reaches an object store, so the caps are what protect that disk:"],[1525,"evo-t1-coder-stack"],[1527,"evo-t1-coder-stack"],[2810,"Existing workspaces pick up the buttons after `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`\nand a workspace update to the new template version."],[995,"Exit `5` with one `unresolvable repo root` line beats guessing a cwd and half-running a\nfleet check against a copy of the repo."],[2190,"Exit codes, which are how a script tells \"the vault said no\" from \"the vault is switched\noff\":"],[1136,"Explaining why one workspace's `~/.grok` disagrees with another's"],[2325,"Exposing the stack without breaking the LAN routes anyone at home uses"],[2296,"External tools are probed with `command -v` and reported as \"not on PATH\", not assumed.\n`require_tool` skips locally and fails under `CI=true`."],[2399,"Failure modes"],[1602,"Fallback: the Spark lanes to `coder`"],[1466,"Fallbacks, in the order worth trying them:"],[2269,"File header"],[487,"Fill every `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*` key or omit them all. A defined-but-empty key\ncrash-loops the server, which is why these keys live in `coder.env` and not in `.env`."],[1061,"First boot"],[1058,"First boot on a GMKtec EVO-T1, top to bottom — bootstrap the host, bring the stable stack up, register Coder, push the templates, open a workspace."],[2615,"First run this to see the plan, then run it again without the flag to apply it:"],[2723,"First work out which producer wrote the file, because there are two and they take the\nlane from different places:"],[1233,"Five more commands ride a `coder_script` of their own, `stack_advisors`\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:784`, `templates/devcontainer/main.tf:835`), whose body is\n`templates/common/stack-advisors.sh.tftpl`. Four are advisors that each gate a different kind\nof work, and the fifth writes the local map described at the end of this section. Same contract\nand same two homes as the four above: `~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for the chat and the\n`cp` twin at `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for the CLI, each written only while absent,\nand `//scripts:seed-dev-env` seeds the same copies into a contributor home through the twelve\nadvisor rows of `seed_seed_targets` (`scripts/lib/config_seed.sh:148-159`)."],[2860,"Fix it on the stack host: fill the five read-side keys (`INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID`,\n`INFISICAL_MACHINE_IDENTITY_ID`, `INFISICAL_ENVIRONMENT`,\n`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_ID`, `INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET`) in\n`.env`. The sixth name, `INFISICAL_DOMAIN`, is the one the push composes, so it is\nempty only when the flag itself is off; `INFISICAL_URL` is the host CLI's own origin\nand reaches no workspace. Then read back what still counts as empty and push the\ntemplate again:"],[2749,"Fix with the `docker pull` above, or turn DinD off for that workspace."],[2573,"Fix, one of these:"],[2774,"Fix: `coder_app` resources are not gated on `start_count` (scripts, metadata\nand the container still are). Push the templates; new creates stop showing the\nwarning. Existing workspaces pick it up on the next update to the new template\nversion."],[2752,"Fix: `docker-compose.yml` sets `dns: [1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8]` on the `coder`\nservice (embedded DNS still answers `db` and other compose names). Both\ntemplates pin the same resolvers on the workspace and DinD containers so\na later `git clone` of `github.com` does not hit the same SERVFAIL. Recreate\n`coder` so the new upstreams apply, then push:"],[2546,"Fix: `homepage/**` and `proxy/*.conf` belong in `//:stack_data`, and the stack\nrunners prefer `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout) so relative\nmounts and bootstrap-issued `proxy/certs/` resolve there. Re-run from the stack\nhost:"],[2759,"Fix: `push-template` now prefers `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the live\ncheckout) and stages with `tar -h` so the archive holds regular files. Pull\nthis commit onto the stack host and re-run:"],[2666,"Fix: check that version in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` against the upstream\nrelease, then rebuild the image."],[2876,"Fix: compose interpolation defaults now match `.env.sample`\n(`change-me-hermes` / `change-me-hermes-secret`). Recreate the service:"],[2795,"Fix: open the editor on the TLS public name (the `coder-<host>.<domain>` door\nTraefik holds a real certificate for) rather than `http://<LAN-address>`, and\ngive the site Clipboard permission - Chrome: the lock icon in the address bar,\nSite settings, Clipboard, Allow. The templates are not the variable here: both\ndoors serve the same payload, so a workspace that pastes on one URL and does not\non the other is an origin problem. What still works on plain HTTP: copy with a\ndrag - the selection is the client's own, since tmux asks for no mouse reporting,\nand `copyOnSelection`'s `document.execCommand('copy')` fallback really lands - and\npaste with `Ctrl+V`, the browser's own paste event - which is exactly what VS Code's\ntoast tells you to do."],[2768,"Fix: point the CLI at the container's loopback listener, `http://127.0.0.1:3000`,\nwhich nothing can gate. See \"Coder workspace templates\" in README.md."],[2703,"Fix: push the templates from the stack host and restart the workspace - the bodies are\ntemplate text, so a workspace on an older template version keeps the race."],[2550,"Fix: recover from a shell, then re-run. `--repair` uses sudo when chmod is not\nenough:"],[2762,"Fix: the guard measures against the resolved `root`, and a parser it cannot open\nis now a hard stop rather than a silent \"everything fits\". Pull this commit onto\nthe stack host and re-run:"],[2776,"Fix: the increment is `waited=$((waited + 1))` (one dollar). Push the templates and\nrestart so the editor script relaunches."],[2781,"Fix: the inlined fork starts code-server with `--ignore-last-opened` and the\nclone folder as a positional argument (`/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` on the\n`workspace` template). Push the templates and restart so the editor process\nrelaunches."],[2799,"Fix: the seed keeps one pane per visible terminal, and both launchers sweep their own\nsessions for a client that has reported no activity past a 6 h grace before they read\n`session_attached` - `EVO_TMUX_STALE_AFTER` (seconds) moves the\nnumber. tmux 3.4 only lets you detach by the client's tty path\n(`detach-client -t /dev/pts/N`): it refuses a pid with `can't find client` and\nhas no `kill-client`. `vscode-terminal-tmux`\nreattaches a folder's session only while nothing is attached to it, and opens a\nnumbered sibling (`vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`, `-3`, ...) beside live ones, so an\nextra tab is a new pane rather than a mirror. Every session target in that\nhelper carries tmux's `=` exact-match prefix, because a bare name is also\nanswered by any session starting with it - once `foo` is closed, `foo-2` would\notherwise answer for it and put a second client on a live pane. And when tmux\n3.4 does take a session down under a tab that is reopening it, the helper raises\na fresh one rather than letting the tab exit with it. The settings seed drops\n`terminal.integrated.scrollback` to 2000 - the browser keeps a short viewport\nand tmux's `history-limit 100000` behind `Prefix-[` holds the deep history - and\nturns `cursorBlinking` off, and `templates/common/tmux.conf` sets\n`set -s escape-time 10` and `set -g focus-events on`. The first is the freeze:\n3.4 holds back any Escape it reads for 500 ms to decide whether a sequence\nfollows, so Alt and the arrow keys feel like dropped input and a focus-in can\nwait that long - twice, with `set-timing` - before tmux repaints. Upstream cut\nthe default to 10 ms in 3.5, and 0 - the value vim guides suggest - is the one\nto avoid: measured on this build, a throttled tab that delivers Esc and the next\ncharacter more than 10 ms apart loses its Alt chords, and 0 makes that far\nlikelier. `focus-events` is what gives tmux a focused client to work with at\nall. `aggressive-resize` stays at its default on purpose: its own entry in\n`options-table.c` scopes it to the `smallest` and `manual` `window-size` modes,\nthe shipped default is `latest`, and tmux(1) calls it \"poor for interactive\nprograms such as shells\". Both files reseed on the next start only while they\nstill carry the stack marker, so delete either one you edited by hand. Before\ndebugging a setting that will not take: tmux reads `~/.tmux.conf` and then\n`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux/tmux.conf` and `~/.config/tmux/tmux.conf`, last file read\nwins, so either of those quietly outranks everything the seed writes.\n`focus-events` is read when a client attaches, so the tabs need to be gone for\nit to land: close them all after the restart and reopen one per pane."],[2756,"Fix: the SVG is a compact ribbon whose data URL fits the column, and\n`push-template` rejects a too-large copy locally before talking to the server.\nPull this commit onto the stack host and re-run:"],[2562,"Fix: the target now reads the one key it needs through `env_get`\n(`scripts/lib/common.sh`), which takes the line as text. Quoted and unquoted\nvalues behave the same, and nothing else in `.env` reaches the pull. A value\nthat is not a model tag - a stray `;`, a leftover quote, a trailing comment - is\nrefused by name before the daemon is asked and before any of the ~35 GB\ndownload starts:"],[2708,"Fix: the template chowns that volume (and the three per-owner state volumes `shared`\nmode mounts under `~/.grok`) to the home owner before the seed, and skips\n`jq`/`python` when the write fails. Push the templates and restart the workspace;\nthere is nothing to delete."],[2790,"Fix: there is nothing to turn on in tmux. Copy the text the way the rest of the tab\ndoes - a plain drag is the client's own highlight, because tmux asks for no mouse\nreporting (measured on the shipped tmux 3.4: an attach with `mouse on` writes\n`ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the client, one with it off writes\nnone), and the settings seed's `terminal.integrated.copyOnSelection: true` puts\nthat highlight on the machine clipboard with no chord (the headless harness on\n4.145.0 recorded `navigator.clipboard.writeText` firing on a client selection,\nbefore this switch). Right-click pastes\nit back (`terminal.integrated.rightClickBehavior: \"copyPaste\"` pastes when nothing\nis selected) and middle-click pastes it too (`middleClickBehavior` pinned to\n`\"paste\"`). tmux keeps no buffer of its own, so `Prefix-]` is unbound and there is\nno `tmux show-buffer` to read: the machine clipboard is the only clipboard here.\n`Prefix m` is the wheel switch, not a copy route: one press hands the wheel to tmux\nfor a long scroll, the next hands it back. Paste is `Ctrl+Shift+V` or right-click in\na bash terminal and a tmux pane alike, and `Ctrl+V` is not a paste on a secure\norigin (the pane gets the `\\x16` byte). Both directions, and what a pane app may\nstill do over ssh, are in\nCopy and paste between a tmux pane and the machine you type on."],[2553,"Fix: those six CLIs (`dashboard-password`, `pull-models`, `coder-agents`,\n`spark-verify`, `spark-configure`, `build-dev-image`) resolve the checkout with\n`resolve_checkout` (`scripts/lib/common.sh`) before they read or write anything, so\nthey honour `REPO_ROOT` and the `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` that `bazel run` names.\nWhen nothing proves out - no candidate holds `docker-compose.yml` outside a\n`bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or `*.runfiles` tree - the run exits `5` with one\n`error: unresolvable repo root: ...` line instead of half-running against a copy. Re-run\nfrom the stack host:"],[2779,"Fix: write the shell variable through the double-dollar escape, which renders\nback to the literal `${name}` in the emitted script. The unbraced form (`$name`)\nneeds no escape. The gate that refuses the one-dollar form is\n`//tests:bats_tftpl_interpolation_test`; name the offending body and line with:"],[1016,"Fixing a card that reports down when the service itself is up"],[774,"Fixing a docs failure from the assertion that named it"],[513,"Flags"],[2567,"Flags are accepted before or after `logs`; `--project-name` and `--tail` move either\nway. Only `logs` takes trailing service names, and here that trailing word is the\nservice to read."],[835,"Flags, accepted anywhere in argv: `--staged`, `--untracked`, `--ref <ref>`, `--all`,\n`--label-kind`, `--json`, `-h|--help`. Exit codes: 0 when every named thing resolved, 1 on a query\nfailure, 2 on a usage error (an unknown verb or flag, or a verb invoked without the argument it\nneeds)."],[331,"Force the `workspace` template:"],[335,"Found means `devcontainer`; not found means `workspace`. A probe that fails **entirely**\nis also a `workspace` answer, with a warning."],[2021,"Four of those carry a `How to read this page` text panel - `evo-alerts`,\n`evo-inference-models`, `evo-litellm` and `evo-stack-traces` - and it is worth reading\nbefore you chase a flat line on one of them."],[2229,"Four secret-shaped keys compose interpolates are among them, and each one is\noutside for a reason worth knowing before you add it:"],[1975,"Four things about that wiring are worth knowing before you edit it:"],[1964,"Four things are worth naming precisely, because each is a place a panel goes empty while\nevery container stays up:"],[72,"From a git URL: `bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace`"],[737,"From a machine that is **not** inside the container, ask the image to run them instead of\ninstalling the toolchain onto that machine:"],[1394,"From a phone, or anywhere"],[1393,"From the host terminal"],[1500,"From the terminal door the same gate is inline in your tty: a dangerous command stops\nwith `[o]nce | [s]ession | [a]lways | [d]eny` and does not proceed until you answer,\nwith deny the default. An approval is scoped to the session you answer in, so a yes\ngiven over there does not cover a fresh session opened at this door.\n`--yolo` and `HERMES_YOLO_MODE` bypass the gate entirely; they have no place here."],[759,"Frontmatter"],[2842,"Full usage, verdict kinds and both argument orders:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- -h`."],[2270,"Functions"],[1535,"Getting oriented in one screen before opening a terminal"],[1057,"Getting started"],[1059,"Getting started"],[2312,"Getting the naming right"],[616,"Getting the same Bazel, Bats, shellcheck and Terraform that CI uses without installing\nany of them"],[1864,"Getting the same scaffold on a laptop, in CI, and from a workspace"],[2080,"Getting to the right runbook in one click instead of searching the README"],[2505,"gh_token_refresh says gh would not store the minted token"],[492,"git never reads `GH_TOKEN` itself: it asks its credential helpers in order and sends the\n**first** answer. That order is what decides what github.com sees, and the first slot that\nanswers wins outright - everything under it exists but a real push never reaches it, which\nis the whole difference between a credential that works and a push that works."],[423,"git never runs as root, so the credentials it consults - a helper, `~/.git-credentials`,\nssh keys - are the workspace user's. `HOME` is passed explicitly through `env` because\n`runuser` keeps the invoker's environment; a root agent would otherwise hand git `/root`\nand miss those files entirely. An empty prefix means \"run git directly\", which is correct\nfor a non-root agent and for a root agent with no derivable workspace user."],[658,"GitHub auth in the container"],[669,"GitHub auth inside the container, and why `github-auth check` runs SELF there"],[2305,"GitHub auth is Coder External Auth, id `github` by default (Coder's injected\nGitHub App when coder.env is absent). A custom OAuth app in coder.env keeps\nits `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` (commonly `primary-github`); push-template.sh\nforwards that id. There is no GitHub-token template variable and no PAT in\nany agent env map."],[484,"GitHub credentials arrive by one path only: opt-in, and HTTPS-only. This is the canonical\npage for that path: where the token comes from, the order git consults it in, and the four\ncommands that read or repair it. The troubleshooting entries link here rather than\nrepeating it."],[93,"GitHub credentials for a workspace"],[516,"GitHub external auth and coder.env"],[2504,"GitHub token expired, or the workspace has no GitHub account -> GitHub reauth"],[2198,"Give the vault its own bootstrap credentials and its admin address in `.env`.\n`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD`, `INFISICAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY` and `INFISICAL_AUTH_SECRET` ship\nas `change-me-*` placeholders in `.env.example`, and `INFISICAL_ADMIN_EMAIL` ships\nempty while `INFISICAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD` ships as a placeholder - `bootstrap` refuses\neither if it is still empty or still starts with `change-me`. These four cannot come\nfrom the vault, because the vault needs them to start."],[2524,"Grafana's dashboard page logs a quotas `501` and `skipping rendering` -> two separate upstream lines, one of them closed here"],[2525,"Grafana's datasource tests green but the panel is empty -> the Mimir query path, then the tenancy header"],[1970,"Grafana's four datasources carry fixed UIDs - `prometheus` (the default), `mimir`,\n`loki`, `tempo` - and every shipped dashboard references those UIDs, so renaming one in\nthe UI breaks the dashboards that were provisioned against it."],[2459,"Grok agent mode makes no tool calls (it narrates and stops) -> an Arc alias served it"],[1107,"Grok Build state: shared state, per-workspace config"],[1682,"Grok Build, Cline, Roo and Kilo all default to `agent`."],[1089,"Grok Build, Coder Agents, and the workspace terminal"],[1091,"Grok Build, Coder Agents, and the workspace terminal"],[2480,"Grok profile missing in a fresh workspace -> startup already finished, so read the log"],[517,"Grok profile mode"],[1690,"Health checks, cooldowns and those counters all attribute per deployment through\n`model_info.id`, which is why each box carries a unique id: `agent-spark-1`,\n`agent-spark-2`, `agent-spark-3`. Each thinking group, the swarm group, the two\nfan-out groups and the two fast groups carry their own triple -\n`agent-low-spark-1/2/3`,\n`agent-medium-spark-1/2/3`,\n`agent-xhigh-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-swarm-spark-1/2/3`,\n`agent-explore-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-review-spark-1/2/3`,\n`agent-xhigh-fast-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-swarm-fast-spark-1/2/3` - so the same physical\nbox is a separate deployment, with its own health state and its own in-flight counter,\ninside every group it belongs to. `compaction-fast` needs no triple: the Arc is one box\nand its single deployment is `compaction-fast-arc`."],[1389,"Hermes host assistant"],[1391,"Hermes host assistant"],[1577,"Hermes is the interactive assistant with the checkout, the compose project and\nthe docker socket in reach, gated by approval prompts. Its public name is opt-in with\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto`, which composes it from the naming parts; with that key empty\nthe dashboard stays on the LAN.\npublic-access.md is the page that decides what the box exposes."],[1416,"Hermes is the Nous Research Hermes Agent running as the interactive assistant **on the\nstack host itself**: not inside a workspace, not in a streamed desktop, but with the\ncheckout, the compose project and the docker socket in reach. It adds an official Web\nDashboard with a Chat tab that carries approval prompts, so the assistant can plan a\nchange, show the plan, and wait for you to approve it before it touches anything."],[2346,"Hermes is the same additive hop as the other four. Traefik forwards\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with the original Host, and the router sends that\nHost to `http://hermes:9119`. The LAN URL `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` stays.\nHTTP basic auth on the dashboard stays too; Authelia is the extra public gate.\nSee hermes.md."],[1458,"Hermes since the June 2026 hardening refuses a non-loopback bind without a\n`DashboardAuthProvider`. Binding `0.0.0.0` with no provider is a boot error, not a\nwarning."],[1462,"Hermes talks to the LiteLLM proxy the stack already runs: base URL\n`http://litellm:4000/v1`, model alias **`agent`**. That is the tool-capable Spark lane,\nwhich is what an agent loop needs, and it stays the default."],[929,"Hermetic Bats: how a suite is discovered, what the stub PATH gives you, and the two inventory gates a new function has to satisfy."],[2150,"Holding the stack's credentials in one place instead of in twelve lines of a dotenv file"],[999,"Homepage dashboard"],[1104,"Hooks"],[2753,"Host `git pull` still needs a public resolver beside MagicDNS when the host\nitself is MagicDNS-only (heading above; `setup-host --repair` on Linux).\nExisting workspaces pick up the container DNS on the next start after this\ntemplate version is active."],[1005,"Host stats, and the docker socket"],[99,"How `push-template.sh` ships the source, and the same push by hand"],[52,"How `push-template` works"],[1106,"How a seeded file becomes yours"],[1129,"How a subagent fan-out is sized, laned, and dispatched from a micro-plan"],[769,"How abbreviations, tables and diagrams pick up their chrome without a component call"],[2960,"How an environment is expressed without a branch, per stack"],[14,"How an issue is read, referenced by the branch, and closed by the merge that fixes it"],[2458,"How do I know the IPEX-LLM server came up? -> look for `runners=[ipex_llm]`"],[2471,"How do I read the workspace start log? -> the body header, the last stage line, and the truncated tail"],[1845,"How scripts/new-repo.sh fetches the ezrepo scaffold from GitHub, renders it into a new repository, and what the Coder side adds."],[2397,"How the boxes get into Prometheus"],[2404,"How the boxes get into Prometheus, and why an unenrolled fleet reads as zero Sparks"],[2811,"How the credential is assembled, and what every slot in that report is, is one page:\nGitHub credentials for a workspace."],[1927,"How the data moves"],[1562,"How the pieces fit"],[1533,"How the sidebar tabs divide the subjects"],[1933,"How the Sparks pages reach one machine"],[1569,"How this tab differs from the `/ez-learn` slash command in Grok Build"],[12,"How to cut, name and delete a short-lived work branch, and when the branch and its commits happen"],[1405,"How to reach the dashboard, on the LAN and on the public name"],[1510,"HTTP basic auth stays, including on the public URL. Authelia is the gate in front of\nthat name only. LAN `:9119` and a matching Host on `:8080` bypass Authelia, so the\nbasic-auth provider is load-bearing. It is a provider, not a toggle."],[2944,"Ideal-state checklist"],[1750,"If a concurrency cap is ever reintroduced, its pairing is `fallbacks` plus\n`context_window_fallbacks`. Both, not one."],[2800,"If a tab keeps dying on you and you want to know whether the pane's shell\nexited or tmux took the session down under the tab, run `tmux set -w\nremain-on-exit on` in it first: the pane then stays on screen with its exit\nstatus in `#{pane_dead_status}` instead of vanishing, which is what the\ntmux FAQ recommends for exactly this\nquestion. Turn it back off afterwards - a dead pane is not yours to keep."],[2808,"If that page says \"You must install the GitHub App to clone private repositories\"\nand lists no accounts, the device-flow login already succeeded. Click **Install\nGitHub App** on the workspace (or open\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new),\ngrant the private repos, then restart. Coder's injected provider omits\n`AppInstallURL`, so the warning on `/external-auth/github` is unlinked plaintext."],[2898,"If the UI is unreachable, check that `CODER_ACCESS_URL` matches the address you are\nbrowsing from: the LAN IP by default, or the public coder name in public mode."],[1309,"If you know a real experiment you want (`chat-advisor`, `chat-virtual-desktop`,\n...), list it in `.env` and restart the coder service. Empty means no experiments\non."],[344,"Image from `images/dev/Dockerfile` (overridable with the `image` variable) carrying:\nNode 22 + 24, Python 3.12 + 3.11, Go 1.24, Bazelisk/buildifier,\nTerraform/tflint/terragrunt, protoc and its Go plugins, kubectl/helm/kubeconform,\nshfmt/shellcheck/bats/ruff/mypy/prettier, the Coder CLI, and the pinned Grok Build CLI\nat `/usr/local/bin/grok`."],[2472,"Image missing tools, or the build aborts mid-layer -> `curl: (22)`"],[782,"Immediately after the title, two bold labels each followed by a bullet list:"],[386,"In `devcontainer` the mounts are on the workspace container only. A dev container the repo\nbuilds on the sidecar keeps its own home and its own `~/.grok`, which is why the live\n`notes-setup.sh` there is a regular file reached through the clone bind rather than a\nlink into `~/.grok`, and why a built dev container sees neither the settings volume nor\nthe shared editor overlay."],[403,"In `devcontainer`, working-dir resolution has a second case: with the dev-container\ntoggle off the agent's working dir is `/home/coder`, exactly as in `workspace`; with it\non, the dev container's own working dir is the cloned folder under\n`/srv/coder-devcontainers/...`, and that folder ships no skill tree, so the `~/.coder`\ncopies remain the live source either way."],[382,"In `shared` mode (the default `grok_profile_mode`) the container mounts three of the\nowner's Docker volumes read-write, nested inside that workspace's own `/home/coder`\nvolume: `grok-notes-<owner-id>` at `~/.grok/notes`, `grok-memory-<owner-id>` at\n`~/.grok/memory` and `grok-sessions-<owner-id>` at `~/.grok/sessions`. Those three\ntrees are then the same files every other workspace this owner has reads and writes,\nand they survive a rebuild."],[1269,"In `shared` mode, three directories: `notes/` (the canonical copy of each\nrepository's private scratch `notes.md`), `memory/`, and `sessions/` - one volume\neach, mounted at `~/.grok/notes`, `~/.grok/memory` and `~/.grok/sessions`. Nothing\nelse under `~/.grok` is shared: `config.toml`, `skills/`, the MCP warm caches and a CLI\nyou installed yourself under `~/.grok/bin` are regular files on this workspace's own\nhome volume, and those die with the workspace. The CLI's own skill directories,\n`skills/` and `bundled/`, are never created here without an x.ai sign-in; the stack's\nskill seeds live under `~/.coder/skills` and `~/.agents/skills`, outside this tree."],[1735,"In `shared` profile mode that is one file per workspace, re-rendered on that workspace's own\nstart, so two workspaces of one owner can carry different renders of that key."],[404,"In a `SKILL.md` the marker sits *after* the `---` frontmatter: the skill parser needs\n`---` on line 1 and silently drops a skill whose first line is anything else. In an\ninjected instruction file the marker is pure provenance, because injected content has its\nHTML comments stripped anyway. The stage is best-effort: a read-only or missing `$HOME`\nchanges nothing observable."],[2593,"In every one of the 79 wall-clock failures no first token ever arrived. Successful\ncompactions do stream: their `elapsed_ms` spans 107 s to 1915 s, and 18 of the 36 ran\npast 300 s, which is the summary the budget was discarding."],[2974,"Incomplete or experimental work lands on the trunk behind a flag that defaults off."],[2272,"Ingress"],[2278,"Ingress rules: what runs from a shell and what is a Bazel target"],[311,"Inside a built dev container the same folder appears under `/workspaces/<repo-name>`,\nthe devcontainer CLI's default mount target and where the dev-container editor opens."],[557,"Inside a running container the same targets are `refresh-configs`'s job too: same reseed set, same marker rule, `--json` for a machine-readable\nanswer, and `--litellm-url <url>` for a stack environment that publishes\nLiteLLM on a port other than 4000 (the reseeded `settings.json` then still\nnames that stack's own model transport). A third tool covers the same set on a\nhost home rather than a container:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --home <dir>` materialises it into a\nlaptop or CI home, and it takes the fan-out knobs from the environment\nwhile it renders the config - `GROK_SUBAGENTS_LANE` names the lane the\nwriters ride, `GROK_SUBAGENT_MAX_CONCURRENT` the width,\n`GROK_SUBAGENT_MAX_DEPTH` the nesting depth (the `grok_subagent_max_depth` template\nvariable), and\n`GROK_EXPLORE_LANE`, `GROK_PLAN_LANE` and `GROK_REVIEW_LANE` the aliases the\nreader, the planner and the skeptic ride, and `GROK_THEME` (default `grokbuild`)\nthe `[ui]` theme name. The width default is the fleet's\nown `GROK_SWARM_WIDTH` (15: three Spark boxes at `PARALLEL` 5), which is the same\n`grok_swarm_width` the templates take, on every lane rather than one number per lane;\na pinned lane only ever keeps one box's streams busy, so an operator who pins\neverything sets `GROK_SUBAGENT_MAX_CONCURRENT` by hand. The depth default is 2, which\nlets a child spawn read-only mappers of its own, and the seeder holds the value to 1..3,\nwith 1 putting nesting back off (`scripts/seed-dev-env.sh:187-197`). A value the seeder\ncannot use - a lane that is not a bare model id, a width that is not a count or sits past\n32, a depth that is not a count or sits past 3 - is refused with\na `note:` line naming the key, and the derived default goes in, so a typo lands as output\nyou read rather than as an unusable key in the rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`.\ngrok-build-and-agents.md covers the profile\nvolume and what seeds on first boot."],[2805,"Inside a workspace it repairs that workspace; on the stack host `fix <container>\n[repo-url]` names the one workspace it repairs, and a host run with no container is\nrefused for exactly that reason. It mints a live token through Coder external-auth (the\ninjected pair is never trusted, because it is the frozen copy that expired), writes the\ngh auth store, points git's `github.com` and `gist.github.com` credential helper at that\nstore through a form that clears the env pair first, then re-runs the write probe and\nprints the verdict again, so it proves itself. It is idempotent and it never prints a\ntoken."],[1630,"Inside a workspace the key travels as the `LITELLM_API_KEY` environment variable, which\n`~/.grok/config.toml` references by `env_key`, so no key byte lands in a file."],[963,"Inside the contributor dev container (devcontainer.md) the commands\nabove are exactly what CI runs."],[74,"Inside the workspace container"],[2395,"Install (`--apply`)"],[2851,"Install gh, then run `fix` again. The golden image ships a pinned copy (`gh 2.101.0`,\nfrom the release tarball, at `/usr/local/bin/gh`); apt's `gh` is 2.45 and predates\nsubcommands the agent experience uses, so the durable fix is the image, not the distro\npackage. Rebuild the image, then rebuild the workspace: the image is fixed at create\ntime."],[2200,"Install the CLI binary on the host."],[1304,"Installing Cline, Roo, the Red Hat YAML extension and Material Icon Theme is a\nmanual step in the Extensions panel (Open VSX by default). If an extension build\nnames its settings differently, set the OpenAI-compatible endpoint and key in its\nUI."],[351,"Installs `tmux` if it is missing, then seeds `~/.tmux.conf` (only while that file is\nabsent or still carries the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker)."],[2945,"Inventory first"],[1610,"IPEX-LLM over vanilla Ollama"],[2931,"Is Grafana behind a login you have not supplied? `GF_AUTH_ANONYMOUS_ENABLED` is `false`\nin this stack, so an unauthenticated probe is redirected to `/login` and a monitoring\ncheck that treats a redirect as a failure reports the UI as down."],[2054,"Is the bundle selected at all:"],[2928,"Is the container there at all? A name with no container is the flag, not the router."],[2062,"Is the whole thing still well-formed on both flag paths:"],[2059,"Is the write to Mimir landing:"],[2930,"Is Traefik aimed at the router and not at `:3005`? The public path is\nTraefik -> router `:8080` -> `grafana:3000`; a backend that points straight at the\ncontainer never reads the vhost file and Grafana answers with its own 404."],[7,"Issues, and how they close"],[1209,"It also refuses a missing task key, a task with no `done:` check, a `calls` budget over\n25 (over 15 is a warning - 15 is the bar for a micro task, 25 is where the plan is wrong),\na dependency cycle or an unknown dependency id, a lane or context file that does not\nexist on disk, a task without a `## Ledger` row, and a first wave narrower than `slots`\n(compared against `min(slots, task count)`, so a small plan is not scolded for being\nsmall, but a coarse one is). `--slots N` overrides the plan's number, `--json` prints the\nsame findings as an object with `plan`, `slots`, `tasks`, `lanes`, `ready`, `errors` and\n`warnings` for a script to read, `--no-files` skips the on-disk existence check for the\nbriefs, and `--strict` makes warnings fail. Exit is 0 clean, 1 with violations, 2 when the\nplan path is missing or unreadable. The plan is a work artifact rather than the record, so\nits tree stays out of git: `notes_ensure_excludes` in `templates/common/notes-setup.sh`\nadds `.grok/plans/` to the stack's global excludes file\n(`~/.grok/notes/exclude`, or the `core.excludesFile` you already point at) next to\n`notes.md` and `.scratch-notes.md`, and `results/` is deleted once the change is\ncommitted. The seam is pinned by `bazelisk test //tests:bats_grok_subagent_slots_test`\n(both twins' `[subagents]` block, the `coder.tf` validation, the `main.tf` derivation, and\nthe rulebook's slot-filling rule) and `bazelisk test //tests:bats_ez_micro_plan_test`\n(the checker driven over a scratch plan tree)."],[1677,"It behaves that way on both `/api/chat` and `/v1/chat/completions`."],[2730,"It can sit in two places, and they clear differently:"],[325,"It carries its own copies of `startup.sh.tftpl`, `settings.json.tftpl` and\n`grok-config.toml.tftpl`, so the tmux-default terminal and LiteLLM wiring above apply\nto it too."],[146,"It confirms an **active** version."],[1689,"It decides only for unpinned traffic: the affinity pin below resolves first, and\n`least-busy` runs when nothing is pinned, when the pinned deployment is unhealthy or in\ncooldown, and for any request with no session id. Every `agent-swarm`,\n`agent-explore`, `agent-review` and `agent-swarm-fast` request is unpinned traffic,\nwhich is the whole of those four lanes."],[2820,"It does not ask \"is there a credential\" and it does not trust the environment: it runs\ngit's own configured helper the way git runs it, takes the password that would go on the\nwire, and probes THAT credential against the endpoint `git push` negotiates through -\none GET of `<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack`. A read-only credential\nprints REFUSED-GRANT, never a pass; a healthy-but-shadowed one prints `push would FAIL`,\nnever `ok`. Inside a workspace (or the contributor devcontainer) it runs SELF mode with\nno docker and prints one `self:` line per credential; on the stack host it enumerates\nthe labeled workspace containers, or probes a named container alone. Flags may go before\nor after the positionals, and a path below `owner/repo` is refused rather than probed."],[2601,"It does not stop the upstream from hanging. It stops the hang being bought three times,\nand stops a finished summary being thrown away."],[555,"It does not touch `~/.grok/config.toml`. That file is rendered *inside* the\nworkspace, and it is a per-workspace file on that workspace's own home volume:\nthe model list, the session-affinity key and the `[ui]` theme belong to one\nworkspace, so a copy pushed from the host would stamp this checkout's defaults\nover what that workspace's own create-form answers rendered. A\n**start/restart** re-renders it from this checkout, which is the only way a\nper-workspace value gets in, and the pass says so after it reseeds."],[339,"It either runs the command (`--no-wait`; watch with `coder ls` / `coder logs <name>`)\nor, with no usable `coder` login, prints the same command to run by hand and exits 0."],[1703,"It has no pin, and that is what makes it the wrong home for a long single-thread\nconversation. Pinning buys prefix-cache locality; without it the next turn may be\nprefilled on a different box and pay prefill again. A chat, an orchestrator, or\nanything whose turns share a warm prefix belongs on `agent`. The lane is for\nindependent parallel work - subagents, batch edits, one-shot jobs, which is also why\nthe two reader lanes below lift the pin the same way."],[2841,"It installs that credential into that one workspace's gh auth store and then\nre-installs the env-proof helper that `gh auth setup-git` would otherwise leave behind,\nsince that helper goes straight back to echoing a stale env pair over the token just\npasted. A PAT is never typed as an argument: argv is readable through `ps` and the\ntoken lands in shell history. Pipe it in (`echo \"$TOKEN\" | bazelisk run\n//scripts:github-auth -- auth <container> -`) or run `auth <container>` alone and\nanswer the hidden prompt. In the line above, `-` is the only argument after the\ncontainer and `< PAT` is a shell redirect, not an argument. It REPLACES the per-start\nminted credential for that workspace, and while the account stays connected the next\n`gh_token_refresh` run rewrites the store, so treat a pasted PAT as a stand-in and\nrotate or remove it in GitHub -> Applications when the work ends."],[1288,"It is **not** a model transport, and the native tool is parked: both twins render\n`disable_web_search = true` at top level, so a session starts with no `web_search`\ntool at all. Measured 2026-10-25: no model transport this box ships serves that\ntool type. The `agent` group is LiteLLM in front of the Spark boxes\n(`openai/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next`), and it refuses the request before anything is\nfetched - `tools of type 'web_search' are not supported: this server runs function\ntools only`, HTTP 400. `supports_backend_search = false` does not route around it,\nbecause the client-side fallback still hands the model alias a `web_search`-typed\ntool, which is the exact shape that endpoint rejects. The fallback lane fails too:\nit is the `coder` alias, Ollama `qwen2.5-coder:32b`, and that model is not pulled on\nthe host. So `web_search = \"agent\"` and `supports_backend_search = false` stay as\nthey are - the role stays filled so re-enabling is a one-key flip - but while the\nkey reads `true` nothing dials it."],[428,"It is advisory only: one warning line per missing path, never a failed start, so the call\nswallows whatever status the function returns."],[171,"It is also the manual path after you register an account, and the retry path for one\ntemplate."],[1114,"It is always on"],[369,"It is bounded and retried on purpose: one unbounded attempt burned a whole start on a\nsingle stalled connection to x.ai (`curl: (28) SSL connection timeout`) and still booted\nthe workspace without a CLI."],[333,"It looks for a dev container in `.devcontainer/`, `.github/devcontainers/`, or a\n`devcontainer.json`/`devcontainer.yml` anywhere in the tree."],[338,"It names no `--stop-after`, so a script-made workspace gets no autostop deadline."],[554,"It never drops a key your env file still sets; retired keys are commented out\nin place."],[342,"It never mints, reads, or stores a token - unlike `push-template.sh`, which mints its\nown because a template push has no other credential path. With `CODER_SESSION_TOKEN`\nset but `CODER_URL` unset it refuses to guess, and an empty token is a *not yet* state\nrather than an error."],[553,"It never prints an env value that looks like a credential."],[729,"It never writes a secret, never writes a GitHub token or a PAT, and never installs the\nGrok CLI — the golden image already carries `/usr/local/bin/grok`. Exit `5` means no\nproven checkout was found (it wants `docker-compose.yml` where it is looking), which is the\nsame repo-root probe every other script in the repo makes."],[391,"It only runs when `config.toml` still carries a stack marker, either spelling of it, by\nthe same contract as\nMarker ownership of the seeded files. A config\nthat lost its marker is the operator's file, swap and all. The usual way one loses the\nmarker is `grok mcp add` rewriting the file, and the remedy there is\n`bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --force`."],[1207,"It prints one `error:` or `warning:` line per finding and then a tally, so a parent reads\nsix lines instead of a plan:"],[148,"It re-pins **Autostop** to *Disabled*."],[147,"It re-sends the template's own description."],[143,"It rejects a `coder_workspace_preset` description over Coder's 128-**byte** ceiling."],[144,"It rejects a `vscode-alt.svg` whose inlined data URL would exceed Coder's\n256-character `workspace_apps.icon` column."],[140,"It resolves the repo root from `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout\n`bazelisk run` names) ahead of the runfiles copy, so `.env` and the template\nfiles are regular files rather than execroot symlinks."],[141,"It resolves the running stack on the Docker **daemon** by container label, never\nthrough a cwd-resolved compose project."],[429,"It runs after the clone because it is about what the clone holds, and it also runs on a\nre-start that cloned nothing, because the devcontainer build re-runs then too."],[177,"It runs the image's own `coder` binary, so client and server are the same build."],[332,"It shallow-clones (`--depth 1 --no-checkout --single-branch`) into a temporary\ndirectory, from the host, and lists the tracked files."],[1440,"It ships off and the published mapping binds **loopback**, because this server\n\"adds full access to the toolset, including terminal commands\" per upstream, on a\ncontainer that holds the docker socket. Turning it on is two deliberate edits: the\nflag, and `HERMES_API_BIND` if something off this host should reach it.\n`API_SERVER_KEY` is generated by bootstrap with the other secrets and is required\nwhenever the server is on; upstream fails closed rather than serving unauthenticated.\n`API_SERVER_MODEL_NAME` (default `hermes`) is what a model-listing frontend shows.\n`API_SERVER_CORS_ORIGINS` stays unset: a browser calls the dashboard, not this port,\nand an origin allowlist is only for a client you named on purpose."],[2617,"It skips while the coder or db container is down, or nobody has registered yet, and\nre-running it repairs a provider that drifted and moves a model pin whose alias has\nsince gone unhealthy."],[1761,"It spread 12/10/8 across the three deployments and returned 30x 200."],[145,"It streams the source as a tar on stdin, with symlinks dereferenced into regular\nfiles. Coder's provisioner unpacks that archive into an empty workdir and does\nnot follow host paths, so a stored runfiles symlink plans as \"No configuration\nfiles\"."],[337,"It then assembles `coder create` with the template's parameters: `repo_url=<url>`\nalways, `use_devcontainer=false` whenever `devcontainer` was chosen but no dev\ncontainer was found (forced with `--devcontainer` onto a repo that has none, or a probe\nthat could not check the repo - the cases where leaving the toggle at its default would\nbuild nothing anyway), `dind=true` on `--dind`, and `additional_repos=<list>` whenever\n`--additional-repos` named at least one extra URL."],[992,"It tries `${REPO_ROOT:-}`, then `${BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY:-}`, then `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}`,\nthen `${PWD}`, then `${SELF_DIR}/..`, and prints the first candidate that proves out."],[1749,"It used to be a chain, `agent -> agent-fast -> coder`. `agent-fast` was a second alias\nfor the same model on a second box with a lower output ceiling; one group of three equal\ndeployments already covers every reachable box, so the chain collapsed to one acyclic\nhop."],[2051,"It walks the bundle outward from the dotenv - the operator keys, the host's log\ndirectory, the profile render, each container's state, each endpoint, whether data is\nactually moving - and prints the stack's usual `summary: N ok, W warnings, F failed`. It\nis the check that catches the two failures a `running` state hides: an Alloy pointed at\nan empty log root, and a Prometheus that scraped everything for a day and never wrote a\nsample to Mimir. `--no-docker` skips the four unpublished backends when you are not on a\nshell that can address the daemon."],[1241,"It writes `code-index.json` for a program and `code-index.md` for a person under\n`~/.grok/notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/` - the same notes directory `ez-learn` owns, outside the\nclone, carried by the owner's `grok-notes-<owner-id>` volume, so nothing new runs and\nnothing is committed.\n`--home DIR` points it at another profile home instead of `$HOME`, and `--json` prints\nthe whole record instead of the one `ok: N files, M symbols -> ...` line. The helper is\n`templates/common/ez-code-index.code_index.py`, seeded into a `scripts/` subdir of the same\nskill directory the way the other helper-carrying skills ship theirs. The `code-index.md`\nheader records the `sha` it indexed, so compare that line against `git rev-parse HEAD` before\ntrusting a `path:line` the map hands you; a stale map is a fresh `rg`."],[1688,"Its counters are per deployment and expire after a hardcoded 3600 s; there is no\n`routing_strategy_args` block to set, and `ttl` there would be a paste from newer docs."],[175,"Its first unauthenticated call (the first-user check) gets the HTML sign-in page back,\nand the CLI reports that as `unexpected non-JSON response \"text/html; charset=utf-8\"`."],[2572,"Its host list comes from `SPARK_HOSTS` in the process environment, then the\n`SPARKn_HOST` keys in `.env`, then the host part of `SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` - in that\norder, which is why the default run needs no arguments. With none of them set it\nfalls back to the placeholders `.env.sample` ships, `spark-1.lan spark-2.lan\nspark-3.lan`, which do not resolve. Export `SPARK_HOSTS=\"host1 host2 ...\"` only to\ncheck a fleet that `.env` does not name. A `SPARKn_OLLAMA_URL` left over in `.env`\nfrom the era when a Spark box served Ollama is no longer read, so the box it names is\nsimply absent from the fleet; the run reports that itself with a `warning:` line that\nnames the key and the host, and the fix is to name the box with `SPARKn_HOST` or\n`SPARKn_OPENAI_URL`, or drop the key. A host that does not answer is a skip, not a\nfailure."],[1753,"Its per-box widgets go to error state while `agent` still answers, because `agent`\nanswers from the Arc while every Spark is down."],[182,"Its scope has to be `coder:all` with `allow_list` `{*:*}`: a narrowed token fails the\nCLI's opening organization lookups, so least privilege is not available on v2.36."],[2052,"Its service list is still the original six (`OBS_SERVICES`, `scripts/observability-check.sh:165`).\nThe four exporters and Alertmanager declare no `healthcheck` and publish no readiness path\nof their own, so their state comes from the `status` rows and the container logs below,\nnot from this check."],[2734,"Its verdict for this fault (`scripts/subagent-check.sh`):"],[2948,"K3s / Kubernetes"],[1055,"Kasm is a privileged Docker-in-Docker container, and privileged whether or not the\nobservability bundle is on. The other privileged containers are `cadvisor`, which comes\nup only with the `observability` profile\n(observability.md), and the opt-in workspace DinD sidecar. Keep Kasm\nLAN-only unless you have decided who may reach the public name\n(see SECURITY.md)."],[2332,"Kasm is the only hop that speaks TLS to its origin. `proxy_ssl_verify` is off there\nbecause the certificate is the one Kasm's installer generates and reissues. Hermes,\nlike Homepage, Coder and LiteLLM, is plain HTTP at the origin (`http://hermes:9119`)."],[2386,"Kasm stores its connection endpoints in its own setup database (first-boot wizard or\nadmin UI), so the endpoint has to change there too or streams keep dialing the LAN\naddress."],[2519,"Kasm wizard gone after install -> expected"],[1007,"Kasm Workspaces"],[1013,"Kasm's wizard-and-UI port pair, and what resetting it destroys"],[476,"Keep it that way unless a workspace genuinely needs two trees open at once."],[3047,"Keep the existing gates green and extend what exists. No new CI system, no Kubernetes, no\nflag platform, no new dependency."],[284,"Keep the other licensed flags out of the CE stack too. `--failure-ttl` (Failure\ncleanup), `--dormancy-threshold` and `--dormancy-auto-deletion` are all marked \"This\nlicensed feature's default is 0h (off)\" in `templates edit --help` on v2.36.6, and\n`--allow-user-autostart` works the same way. Older notes also list `--inactivityTTL`\namong the rejected names; v2.36.6 has no flag of that name, and `--default-ttl` and\n`--activity-bump` are the closest."],[1649,"Keep what one workspace puts on one box at or under that box's `PARALLEL` (5). A\npinned lane sends the whole fan-out to the box its session key resolves to, so the\nwidth worth asking for there is 4: one box's streams, minus the parent's own request.\nThe unpinned `agent-swarm` lane is what the writers ride by default, and the one that\ncan afford a wider wave - 15 subagents spread by `least-busy` over three boxes fill each\nbox's `PARALLEL` exactly, which is the\ndifference between buying wall-clock and buying queue depth. That default asks every box\nfor all of its streams, and it reserves nothing for the parent's own request the way\nthe one-box width of 4 does. That is a deliberate trade rather than an\noversight: the parent's conversation, the reader waves and the review pass all arrive\non the same three boxes and share those `PARALLEL` streams, so at 15 a single busy\nworkspace can have the fleet fully subscribed and the last arrivals wait. What makes\nthat survivable is `limit_behavior = \"queue\"` holding the overflow instead of failing\nthe spawn, so the cost of overshoot is a subagent's latency. That 15 is\n`grok_swarm_width` (3 boxes x `PARALLEL` 5), the default on every lane whatever the\noperator picks there; drop it when several workspaces share the fleet, or set\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent` to hold a width outright. The\ntwo reader lanes\nspread the same way, and each group counts its own in-flight requests, but the box's\n`PARALLEL` streams are one pool that every alias arriving on it shares.\nPast 15 across the fleet the boxes have no\nstreams left to give, whatever the lane.\nThe same 15 is the budget of a whole tree, because `max_depth` (rendered from\n`grok_subagent_max_depth`, default 2, validated 1..3) lets a child spawn. `reviewer`,\n`security-auditor` and `tester` name `spawn_subagent` in their `tools`, and every mapper\nsuch a child raises holds a stream the same way its parent does, so a parent sums the\nin-flight `1 + spawns` over every task and keeps that total at or below the fleet's\nstream count (`fleet:` in the plan, 15 by default,\n`templates/common/ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py:42`). That checker refuses a wave whose\ncost passes the fleet, and the sum is a separate figure from the session cap\n`[subagents] max_concurrent` sets, because a grandchild opens its own session. The box\nrefuses an over-admitted stream the same way it refuses any other, with `NoRoom`."],[508,"Keeping a checkout's config current"],[510,"Keeping a checkout's config current"],[656,"Keeping it maintained"],[672,"Keeping the container maintained as the project moves"],[1283,"Keyed by **owner id**, never host-wide and never cross-user: another user's\nworkspace mounts a differently named volume, and a stop or start of a *different\nuser's* workspace never sees the volume at all."],[2134,"Keys the vault does not hold"],[59,"Knobs and skips"],[2541,"Knowing the cost of a fix before running it, including what it destroys"],[19,"Knowing which green check actually gates a merge into `main`"],[1572,"Knowing which page owns a concept when two of them seem to"],[1071,"Knowing which step to re-run when a later one says a prerequisite is missing"],[1137,"Knowing why the native `web_search` tool is parked and the SearXNG lane answers"],[1880,"Lane ids are not declared here. `scripts/new-repo.sh` discovers them from the fetched tree's\n`.template-lanes/` directory at run time, so a lane the scaffold repository adds needs no change\nin this repo. The four below are what it ships today:"],[1849,"Lanes"],[931,"Layout"],[1559,"Learn the stack"],[1561,"Learn the stack"],[1497,"Leave the mode on `manual`. `off` is `--yolo`: every mutating command executes on the\nhost with no card."],[1276,"Like the per-owner volumes it is a plain string name in `main.tf`, not a\n`docker_volume` resource, so Docker creates it on the first container start that\nnames it and reuses it afterwards."],[1579,"LiteLLM model routing"],[1581,"LiteLLM model routing"],[1086,"LiteLLM model routing — why the answer you got came from the box it\ncame from."],[1001,"Login"],[1006,"Look"],[2569,"Look at the dashboard's **Agent backend** card first: its per-box widgets go to error\nstate while `agent` still answers."],[176,"Loopback inside the container cannot be gated, so the push cannot fail that way."],[1056,"Losing the wizard after install is expected: use `:4443`. Resetting Kasm means\nremoving the `kasm-data` volume, which destroys all Kasm config."],[4,"main"],[948,"Making a new function satisfy `//tests:shell_inventory` without guessing"],[894,"Making an annotation that a reviewer cannot argue about, because a tool decides it"],[2106,"Making the three DGX Sparks Prometheus targets with `//scripts:spark-monitor`"],[1904,"Markdown carries a token in a second spelling. Prettier reads a bare `__PROJECT_NAME__` as strong\nemphasis and rewrites it to `**PROJECT_NAME**`, so a markdown formatter run over the template turns\nevery prose placeholder into that form; the renderer substitutes both spellings. Which of the seven\nthe scaffold actually uses, in either spelling, is the scaffold's own invariant and its own\n`.self-check/` suite checks it."],[78,"Marker ownership of the seeded files"],[2283,"Matching the house style instead of inventing a local one"],[85,"MCP command reachability advisory"],[1675,"Measured against this box's IPEX-LLM Ollama, whose bundled `ollama` is 0.9.3."],[935,"Measured coverage"],[1759,"Measured on a harness boot of this config against the live Spark boxes, before\n`session_affinity` existed."],[450,"Memory (90 percent) and disk (85 percent, `/home/coder`) usage gauges, six resource\nmetadata tiles sampled every 30s - four `coder stat` calls (`cpu --host`,\n`mem --host`, `disk --path /home/coder`, `cpu --host`) and two awk one-liners for swap\nand load average."],[2456,"Model lane: Spark, LiteLLM, the agent backends"],[1115,"Model wiring"],[1397,"Models"],[1611,"MoE over dense"],[2096,"Monitoring and alerts"],[2098,"Monitoring and alerts"],[2798,"More clients than you have visible terminals means the tabs are mirrors, and a\nclient whose `#{client_written}` stands still while its pane is busy is a tab\nwhose browser side is already gone."],[371,"Most seeded files are written only while absent, so a hand-edited copy is user-owned and\nthe stage re-runs harmlessly. Three of them are re-written on every start, and ownership is\ndecided by a marker - never by mtime and never by first boot:"],[1399,"Mounts and \"be the host\""],[1622,"Moving a workload between the Arc iGPU and a Spark box without touching a workspace"],[959,"Name it in exactly one suite. A chain test that happens to mention the name also satisfies\nthe inventory, but the owning suite is the one that asserts its behaviour."],[25,"Name the branch after the change, not the author, and keep the commit subject imperative\nwith one of `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `chore:`, `ci:`, `refactor:`."],[3000,"Name the deployment, not the branch:"],[309,"Named volumes cannot substitute: a volume is a daemon-local object and does not cross\nto the sidecar's daemon."],[2275,"Names and flags"],[2280,"Names, flags, and the style guide shell follows"],[17,"Naming a branch and a commit so CI and `git log` read the way the rest of the repo does"],[818,"Naming every suite that must move with a payload file in one query, in about 60 ms"],[740,"Native `bazelisk test` on macOS is a convenience, not a merge path. The suites run there,\nbut the BSD userland differs from the GNU one the image ships (`sed`, `date`, `find`,\n`sort`), so a green Darwin run is not the run that gates the merge and a red one is not\nproof of a defect. The container, or `test-in-image`, is the answer\ntest-with-bazel.md and ../branching.md gate on."],[794,"Navigating the repo with the Bazel graph"],[796,"Navigating the repo with the Bazel graph"],[763,"Navigation"],[400,"Neither `main.tf` sets `CODER_AGENT_EXP_SKILLS_DIRS` or the\n`CODER_AGENT_EXP_INSTRUCTIONS_*` keys: those *replace* the default lists rather than\nappending, and the seeded copies already sit where the unmodified defaults point. Chat\nitself is on by default in 2.36.6 - no experiment name and no entitlement gates it."],[2650,"Neither header, and a `Server: cloudflare` with a `cf-ray` instead: Traefik or\nCloudflare answered, and there is nothing on this box to read."],[1768,"Neither stack runner has a `restart` subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`\nare the whole set), so the proxy restart stays a direct compose call. Stable stack:"],[1148,"Neither template installs code-server through the Registry. Each `main.tf` carries\nan inlined fork (`coder_script.code_server` + `coder_app.code_server`), because the\nupstream module hardcodes `/icon/code.svg` and has no icon input. Both twins pin the\nrelease in one `code_server_version` local and pass it to the upstream installer as\n`--version`: without that flag `https://code-server.dev/install.sh` follows `latest`,\nand the editor would move under every workspace with no commit in this repo.\n`workspace` installs code-server only while that pinned release is missing from\n`/home/coder/.local/share/code-server` (the guard names\n`lib/code-server-<version>/bin/code-server`, the directory the installer unpacks, not\nthe `bin/code-server` symlink, which points at the last release installed and would\nhold a warm volume on its first one) and starts it with `--auth none --port 13337\n--app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`, passing no settings payload.\n`devcontainer` has no persistent prefix to reuse, so it installs the pinned release\nfresh under `/tmp/code-server` on every start."],[453,"Neither template installs code-server through the Registry. Each `main.tf` carries an\ninlined fork (`coder_script.code_server` + `coder_app.code_server`) because that\nupstream module hardcodes `/icon/code.svg` and has no icon input. The script body\nlives in a sibling `code-server.sh.tftpl` and is rendered with `templatefile()`\n(workspace: `{ editor_folder, code_server_version }`; devcontainer: that plus\n`settings_b64`). Do not wrap that `templatefile()` in `chomp()`."],[1844,"New projects from the template"],[1846,"New projects from the template"],[149,"No `.terraform.lock.hcl` is committed under `templates/`. Each `main.tf` pins\n`coder/coder` `~> 2.18` and `kreuzwerker/docker` `~> 4.6` in `required_providers`; Coder\npushes with a warning when no lockfile sits beside them. `coder_devcontainer` needs a\nserver `>= 2.21`, which this stack runs (v2.36.6)."],[2921,"No `*-json.log` files there means the daemon has a different `--data-root`. Rootless\nDocker is the usual case - it keeps them under the user's own state directory\n(`~/.local/share/docker/containers`) - and a daemon started with an explicit\n`--data-root` is the other. Name the real one in the env file that belongs to that stack\nand recreate the service:"],[2677,"No `<name>` entry and both `ls` paths missing: the seed never landed, which means the\nworkspace booted from templates pushed before the seed existed. Re-push the templates\nand restart the workspace, or reseed into the running container from the stack host:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>`."],[1037,"no `block` customapi widget declares more than four mappings - the shipped bundle\nslices the list, so a fifth row silently never renders"],[322,"No `config_path` is set, so the CLI discovers the file the way VS Code does, and a repo\nusing the other legal locations behaves the same."],[2292,"No `eval`. Prefer `\"${var}\"`, `[[ ]]`, and `$(...)`."],[2643,"No `web_fetch` either, and that is not a misconfiguration: `features.web_fetch` is\non, but on a non-xAI model endpoint that tool runs only through\n`toolset.web_fetch.proxy_endpoint` and this stack sets none. Route a URL already in\nhand to `searxng`'s `fetch_content`, anything else on the web to the `searxng`\n`search` tool, and a library, framework, CLI or setup question to `context7`\n(`resolve-library-id` first, then `query-docs` on that id)."],[742,"No Actions `container:` key is involved. The job runs on a plain runner and reaches the\ntoolchain through `docker run --user \"$(id -u):$(id -g)\"` with `HOME=/tmp` and\n`USER=$(id -un)`, so what gates the merge is an image tag, the bind-mounted checkout\nstays writable, permission tests see a non-root identity, and Bazel can name a uid the\nimage passwd file does not list."],[168,"No arguments pushes every template in `TEMPLATES_DEFAULT` order."],[2423,"No flag, or `--dry-run`, is a plan: it opens no ssh session, starts no container, and\nprints what is configured plus the exact `docker run` lines `--apply` would ferry. This\nis the run to make before pointing Prometheus at three boxes you have not looked at.\nCaptured output, with the CLI's placeholder host names replaced by `<host>`:"],[3044,"No force-push, no rewritten shared history, no merging your own pull request."],[1514,"No GitHub PAT in Hermes. GitHub auth in this stack is Coder External Auth only, and a\ntoken here would sit on a container that can both write to the checkout and drive the\nhost daemon. See SECURITY.md."],[178,"No host `coder` CLI and no `coder login` are involved, and a stale host-side session\ncannot aim the push at some other deployment."],[2846,"No process can rewrite another process's environment, so the stale pair stays in the\nenvironment until the workspace restarts; the prefix is the permanent form, not a\nworkaround you graduate from."],[3045,"No remote branch deleted without explicit confirmation, and never a trunk ref."],[3046,"No secret in a committed file, including the flags file and per-environment value files.\nSamples hold key names and inert defaults; secrets go to the GitHub Environment or the\ncluster's secret store."],[2853,"None of this appears on a stack that never adopted the vault: every line below needs\n`INFISICAL_ENABLED` truthy in `.env`, and a verb that needs the vault answers `note:\n<verb> refused: INFISICAL_ENABLED is off. Set INFISICAL_ENABLED=true in .env to adopt the\nvault, then re-run.` (`scripts/infisical.sh:230`). The flag, the twelve managed keys, the\nthree containers and the operator CLI are\noperate/secrets.md; what is here is the text you actually see when\nsomething in that path does not answer."],[273,"Notes on the two dropdowns that take a custom value:"],[2916,"Nothing anywhere: the scrape is the fault, so take the target entry above."],[784,"Nothing below needs a component call in the page."],[187,"Nothing else about GitHub travels in a `--var`."],[2196,"Nothing else in the repo shells out to this CLI. The three runners that address the compose\nproject - `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack`, `//scripts:prod-stack` and `//scripts:update-stack` -\nsource `scripts/lib/infisical.sh` and call `infisical_pull` themselves, immediately before compose\ninterpolates the env file, so a key rotated in the vault lands on the bring-up in front of you\ninstead of waiting for a human to run the verb. Each prints one `-> vault: refreshing the env file\nfrom Infisical` phase line when the flag is on, and each treats a failed pull as one `warning:`\nnaming the retry - `the env file was not refreshed from Infisical; <file> keeps the credentials it\nalready has - retry with bazelisk run //scripts:infisical -- pull` - and then continues. The\nbranch never runs while the flag is off, so a stack that never adopted the vault reads and prints\nexactly what it did before. `bazelisk run //scripts:update-stack -- --dry-run` plans that fetch\n(`note: dry run: would refresh <file> from Infisical`) and writes nothing; the two stack runners\nhave no dry-run mode, so their refresh happens only on a real `up`."],[431,"Nothing here changes what the build does. With the toggle on, a clone carrying no\ndevcontainer configuration fails the build either way, and the editor hangs off the dev\ncontainer, so no dev container also means no editor and no in-container terminal; the\nwarning is only early notice in the same log the clone warnings use. With the toggle off\nthe same miss is expected and the editor opens the clone directly."],[2333,"Nothing in this path replaces the LAN routes: `https://<lan-ip>/` still serves the\ndashboard on the self-signed cert, `https://<lan-ip>:3001` still serves Coder's\nown TLS listener, and `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` still serves Hermes."],[2368,"Nothing in this repo configures Traefik. This is the file the other box needs: drop it\nin the directory that box's `providers.file.directory` watches and Traefik picks it up\nwithout a restart. The shape below is the one Traefik v2 and v3 both read; the\nentryPoint name, the certificate and the middleware name have to match what that box\nalready defines, so treat those three as the values to align, not as new requirements."],[2729,"Nothing in this repo renders a `4`. Both twins derive the width from the fleet -\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent = var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent != null ? var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent : var.grok_swarm_width`\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:553`, `templates/devcontainer/main.tf:581`) - and take the\nceiling beside it from that same variable (`templates/workspace/main.tf:558`). Measured:\n`terraform apply` with the shipped defaults prints `15`, and the same run with\n`-var grok_subagent_max_concurrent=4` prints `4`. So a rendered `4` is a number somebody\nset, and a `4` still there is one that outlived what set it."],[2030,"Nothing in this section has been rendered in a live Grafana from this checkout: the pages\nare proven by that structure and by metric names a source in this repo backs, not by\npixels."],[699,"Nothing in this table belongs in the image. `images/dev/Dockerfile` is shared with\nthe Coder workspaces and the CI gate, so it carries the toolchain and stays\nunaware that a contributor container exists; everything above is container shape,\nread from the checkout on every start. That is what makes the whole thing editable\nwithout a rebuild."],[2856,"Nothing is broken yet and nothing repairs itself by waiting: the stack is up, on values\nthe vault may no longer hold. Ask the vault and the inventory first - `check` is a\ndiagnostic and always exits `0`:"],[22,"Nothing is committed straight to `main`. Integration into `main` is by pull request, and a\nPR is only mergeable once CI is green:\n`bazelisk test //:test-fast`, `bazelisk test //:lint --test_tag_filters=manual` and the\nfull gate `bazelisk test //:test-all`, all\nexecuted inside the golden image (`images/dev/Dockerfile`) that CI builds with buildx and\ndocker-runs — the toolchain comes from that image, not from anything installed onto the\nrunner. Locally, run them the same way: inside the contributor container\n(contribute/devcontainer.md) or with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image --`. `bash scripts/setup-host.sh` is the bootstrap\nfor the stack host, where the suites have to run on the host itself.\nTwo things run those checks for you so the gate is not a thing to remember:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:pre-commit` on demand, and, once\n`bazelisk run //scripts:install-hooks` has pointed `core.hooksPath` at `.githooks`,\n`.githooks/pre-commit` on every commit and `.githooks/pre-push` before anything is\npushed. `bazelisk run //:validate` runs the fast pair plus\n`docker compose config -q` and the `.env.sample`/`.env.example` comparison; the\npre-PR gate is `//:test-all`, which `//:validate` and the pre-push hook both reach."],[440,"Nothing is copied out of the export line by line: each value is taken apart and re-emitted\nas `export KEY='value'` under quoting with no escape hatch, because the CLI's own dotenv\nwriter leaves an embedded single quote raw, which makes its output either unsources or\nrunnable (`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:99-107`, `:217`). That is what makes the\n`.bashrc` source above safe, and it is why the reader and the publish step are one pair -\nthe startup comment says to read the publish step before changing either side\n(`templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl:71-72`)."],[297,"Nothing is published to the host, and 2375 is reachable only from that workspace's own\nnetwork - which is also why the network is not `internal`. An internal network gets no\ngateway route or NAT, so `host.docker.internal` would resolve and never answer, and the\nagent would strand. See SECURITY.md."],[1980,"Nothing leaves the box until the operator says who to tell. Alertmanager's own config\nparser cannot expand an environment variable inside a webhook URL, so\n`observability/alertmanager/alertmanager.yml` carries no URL at all: all four webhook\nreceivers read `url_file: /alertmanager/webhook_url`. That file is written at container\nstart by the image's entrypoint, `observability/alertmanager/boot-webhook-url.sh`, from\nthe `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL` key (`.env.example:635`, empty as shipped), and the script\nthen `exec`s the binary. An empty key is a working configuration: the container starts,\nthe rules fire, the receivers are no-ops, and the entrypoint regenerates the file every\nstart so a removed key cannot survive as a stale destination on disk. The Alertmanager UI\nis loopback-bound for the same reason it has no auth - it can silence alerts - so read it\nfrom a shell on the stack host, or use Grafana's Alerting pages, which need no extra port."],[490,"Nothing long-lived lives in any file, env or template: the rendered `config.toml`\nnames `GH_TOKEN` and nothing else, and the pair stops at the workspace container's edge\n(a dev container built on the sidecar is not handed either key)."],[2594,"Nothing on the client aborts the hang. `signals.json` reports `inferenceIdleTimeouts`\nat 0 or 1 across the failing sessions, and no hung call was ever cut off at the idle\ndeadline, so `inference_idle_timeout_secs` (then 1200 s) is not on this path. The key\nnow ships at 1800 s in both template twins, which does not put it on this path either:\nthe CLI still does not apply it to compaction. Reproduced directly: with an upstream\nheld open and no byte sent, the CLI was still waiting at 3000 s."],[413,"Nothing on the template side raises that budget, so do not re-add an argument for it.\n`coder_agent` in the provider this stack pins (`~> 2.18`, locked 2.18.0 in\n`templates/workspace/.terraform.lock.hcl`) has no log-size argument: `terraform plan`\nanswers `An argument named \"log_buffer_size\" is not expected here.` The agent-side constant\nis not read from the environment either - its own comment records the number as \"taken from\ndump.sql `max_logs_length` constraint\". Where to read the log and what a truncated one\nmeans is\ntroubleshooting.md."],[2873,"Nothing prints which key, on purpose, so the shape to look for is the secret you added\nmost recently that is not one line - a private key or a certificate. Either store it as\none line (base64 of the PEM is the usual form) or keep it host-side, then restart the\nworkspace to fetch again."],[2664,"Nothing raises that budget from here, and the loud fix is the wrong one: `coder_agent` in the\nprovider this stack pins (`~> 2.18`, locked 2.18.0 in\n`templates/workspace/.terraform.lock.hcl`) takes no log-size argument, `terraform plan`\nanswering `An argument named \"log_buffer_size\" is not expected here.`, and the agent-side\nconstant is not read from the environment. The bound and the about-40-lines-per-body writing\nrule it forces are in\ncoder-templates.md."],[1476,"Nous Portal is an optional upstream, not a default, and this page does not document it.\nNothing here needs an account."],[2521,"Observability"],[1923,"Observability bundle"],[1925,"Observability bundle"],[2122,"Observability bundle - the ten profile-gated services, the path\na metric takes, the retention, and what the bundle costs on disk"],[3019,"Off by default on the trunk. An on-by-default flag is an unfinished feature that became\nload-bearing on the day it shipped."],[1557,"Ollama is reachable only from inside the compose network: LiteLLM is the gateway, and it\nis the thing that carries the master key. The four observability backends publish nothing\nfor the same reason - Grafana reaches them over the compose network - and all six sit\nbehind one Compose profile, `OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED`."],[2785,"On `devcontainer`:"],[964,"On a host that is not that container, `//scripts:test-in-image` docker-runs\n`evo-t1-dev:latest` as this process's uid:gid, with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER` set so\nBazel can name that uid, and runs them there. `EVO_T1_BAZEL_CACHE=<dir>` keeps the\narchives Bazel downloads between runs; both CI jobs set it and wrap it in\n`actions/cache` (building-with-bazel.md)."],[1783,"On a memory-rich but bandwidth- and latency-constrained iGPU that is the sweet spot,\nbecause an equivalent dense model would move every parameter per token and run much\nslower at the same footprint."],[550,"On a TTY the env pass asks by default (`-i`/`--interactive` is then a no-op;\n`--non-interactive` turns it off). For every key that is new this pull or still\n`change-me…`/`NOTSET`/`YOUR_LAN_IP` it prints the explanation the twin carries\nabove that key and prompts for a value. Enter keeps a reusable value already\nset in a sibling env file, or the example default. A key whose\nname carries `KEY`, `SECRET`, `TOKEN` or `PASSWORD` is echoed nowhere - the\nreport says `set` or `empty` and the prompt does not echo what you type - so a\nscreenful of this output is safe to paste into an issue. With no terminal to\nprompt through the pass warns and leaves those keys alone, which is why\n`--dry-run` / `--non-interactive` is the shape that belongs in a script or CI."],[2556,"On Linux, `bash scripts/setup-host.sh --repair` writes\n`/etc/systemd/resolved.conf.d/evo-t1-public-dns.conf` (`DNS=1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8`),\nruns `sudo tailscale set --accept-dns=false`, and keeps MagicDNS for `ts.net`\nvia `resolvectl` split-DNS. `--check` still writes nothing."],[2725,"On the Terraform path, no dotenv key, no CLI flag, and no `//scripts:push-template`\npassthrough names that variable, so it was set by a raw\n`coder templates push --var grok_subagents_lane=...`. Undo it the same way from the\nstack host, or by re-pushing once the default in `templates/*/coder.tf` is\n`agent-swarm`:"],[2807,"On the workspace page, click **GitHub reauth**. Coder opens `/external-auth/<id>`\n(device flow for the injected App, OAuth for a custom app in `coder.env`). If the\npage says you are already authenticated, click **Reauthenticate**."],[1062,"Once Coder answers"],[957,"one asserting test in the **one** suite for its module — a `scripts/lib/<mod>.sh` helper\nbelongs to `tests/bats/<mod>.bats`, and a helper that only an entry CLI defines belongs to\n`tests/bats/entry-fns.bats`, which loads single functions with `load_shell_function`;"],[1151,"One clipboard is in play: the one on the computer you are typing on. The seed runs\n`set -g mouse off` and unbinds every key and every click that could move text into\nor out of tmux's own buffer, so the terminal client keeps each button and does the\ncopying itself, the way it does in a native terminal on that machine. OSC 52 is the\none route that still depends on what sits at the other end of the terminal, and it\nbelongs to an app inside the pane rather than to the operator. Where each claim\nbelow comes from: the tmux ones were read off the shipped tmux 3.4 on this box (what\nan attach writes to the client, and what `list-keys` still holds once the payload is\nsourced), and the setting names, values and defaults were read out of the shipped\ncode-server 4.145.0's own config registry (VS Code base 1.145.0). The two\nClipboard-API observations -- that a client selection fires\n`navigator.clipboard.writeText`, and that `document.execCommand('copy')` is the\nfallback that still lands on an insecure origin -- come from the earlier\nheadless-Chromium pass over that build with the rendered `settings.json` twin and the\ntmux payload loaded, when `mouse` was still on and the copy out was a Shift+drag.\nThat path is the client's own selection, which is what a plain drag drives now; the\nplain drag is due the same pass before it is restated as measured under `mouse off`."],[2733,"One command reads both numbers and names the case, from inside the workspace:"],[1526,"One compose stack that turns a GMKtec EVO-T1 into a self-hosted dev-workspace server — Coder, LiteLLM, IPEX-LLM Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and Hermes."],[491,"One connection covers every workspace, and `gh auth status` inside a workspace is how\nyou check it landed. Ask it with the pair cleared - `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN gh\nauth status` - because gh reports the env pair in preference to the store."],[1282,"One consequence of the volume being host-wide rather than per-owner: the leaf is named\nfor the workspace, and Coder workspace names are unique per organization. Two owners\nwho both call a workspace `dev` mirror into the same `workspaces/dev`, and every\nworkspace user is uid 1000, so nothing on the filesystem separates them inside the\nvolume. That is acceptable for the archive's only purpose - an operator reading their\nown box's history - and is why no agent is ever given a route to it. Give the leaf an\nowner component if a box ever holds more than one trusted operator's sessions."],[1693,"One conversation from one client sticks to the box that answered its first turn, for as\nlong as that box stays healthy."],[1686,"One endpoint per box, one box per endpoint. Never point two deployments, or two\naliases, at the same physical machine: the proxy counts deployments, not machines."],[1582,"One front door"],[1593,"One group, three deployments"],[306,"One host bind, `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>`, mounted at that **identical**\npath in both containers."],[313,"One host-side path for all three would open the editor on a folder that exists nowhere\nthe editor runs, and VS Code answers that with\n`Workspace does not exist. Please select another workspace to open.`"],[678,"One image — `images/dev/Dockerfile`, built as `evo-t1-dev:latest` — is the contributor and\ntest runtime on macOS, on Linux, and on GitHub. The same Dockerfile serves the Coder\nworkspaces the stack provisions, the contributor dev container on a laptop, and the\ncontainer CI docker-runs its checks inside. Every version in it comes from\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`; ../coder-templates.md covers the\nworkspace side, and CONTRIBUTING.md\nis the checklist the PR follows."],[2433,"One JSON object on **stdout** and nothing else; every `ok:` / `warning:` / `summary:` line\nroutes to stderr while `--json` is set, so `| jq` sees a document. Verified: stdout was\none line, 1397 bytes, parseable, carrying `mode`, `host_source`, `monitor_enabled`, both\nimages, both ports, `ssh_port`, a `boxes` array with one object per box (`probed`,\n`docker`, `node_exporter`, `dcgm`, `dcgm_counter_count`, `gpu_count`, `gpu_names`,\n`probe_version`, `verdict`, `detail`) and a `summary` object."],[2156,"One key decides whether the bundle exists: `INFISICAL_ENABLED`, default `false`, in\n`.env.example` beside its own comment block. Owner: `platform`. Expiry: `2026-12-31`."],[1981,"One key decides whether the bundle exists: `OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED`, default `true`, in\n`.env.example` and `.env.sample` beside it. All ten services carry\n`profiles: [\"observability\"]`, and `compose_observability_args()` in\n`scripts/lib/common.sh` is the single place that reads the key - it prints\n`--profile observability` when the value is truthy and nothing for `false`, `0`, `no` or\n`off`. Every stack runner goes through it: `dev-stack.sh` and `prod-stack.sh` in their\n`compose_prefix()` and `run_compose()`, and `update-stack.sh` in `stack_compose()`."],[1699,"One key lifts the pin; apart from that key and the group's own deployment, retry-policy\nand fallback entries, nothing in the config distinguishes it from the five pinned lanes:"],[2624,"One lane reaches that port: a process inside the workspace. The two reads that matter\nare the probe from inside the workspace and the liveliness probe from the stack host,\nand those share nothing but the destination."],[2293,"One language per file where reasonable. A bash, python, or awk program that lives\ninside another file is its own tested file, imported with `source`, `cat` + `sh -c`\n/ `ssh`, `python3 file.py`, `awk -f`, or Terraform `file()` / `templatefile()`. Keep\n1–3 line awk, usage heredocs, Dockerfiles, YAML data, docs fences, and\n`python3 -m webbrowser`. Remote probes are POSIX and self-contained under\n`scripts/lib/probes/`; the host `cat`s them and tests run the file with `sh`."],[2279,"One language per file, the shared log contract, and the payload copies the templates share"],[1375,"One line each: `/ez-bazel-first` puts every tracked file behind a build target, budgets the\n`glob()`s and drives the gate to full file coverage; `/ez-strong-types` pins a strict\nchecker profile per language and turns the broad-type census into a ratchet that fails on\nany widening; `/ez-trunk-based-repo` retires environment branches, lands unfinished work\nbehind a flag that defaults off, and presents the leftover branches for deletion with their\ntip SHAs. All three take a repository path and work on that checkout, so they are useful in\na workspace cloned against any of the fleet's repositories."],[1369,"One line each: `/ez-learn` turns a session into durable memory - flush it into a session\nlog, dream the logs into organized memory, remember one note, or browse and clear the\nstore; `/ez-skillify` captures the session that just worked, working from its own\ntranscript; `/ez-create-skill` builds a skill from what the operator describes. The last\ntwo write skill files; `/ez-learn` writes memory files."],[2871,"One line per boot, whatever the number of such secrets, and it names neither the key nor\nthe value (`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:200-204`):"],[3048,"One logical change per branch and per pull request, with docs and tests in the same change."],[2971,"One long-lived branch. It is normally `main`, it is always deployable, and it is what every\nenvironment is built from."],[876,"One mistyped character, an empty stdout, and a shell that tests only `-z \"$out\"` reports a clean\nresult. So before you believe an empty list: check the exit status (Bazel exits non-zero here), read\nstderr, and confirm the operand resolved at all with `bazelisk query '\"litellm/config.yaml\"'`, which\nprints its label back. A miss you can see is a finding; a miss you cannot see is a green gate over a\nfile nobody tests."],[3004,"One namespace per environment, one overlay per environment, one image reference:"],[3018,"One naming convention. `FEATURE_<THING>` for behaviour, a plain value for a setting. The name\nin the inventory is exactly the name the process reads, so `grep` is a complete audit."],[1239,"One new role definition ships with this family rather than with the fleet: `documenter`\n(`templates/common/documenter.AGENT.md`, landing at `~/.grok/agents/documenter.md`) is the\nlight read-and-write role `/documenter` dispatches, and it rides the same body as the skill so\na start has both or has neither."],[2678,"One of the six missing from both homes while the others landed points at the one body\nthat seeds it: `coder_script.stack_skills` carries the authoring trio and\n`coder_script.stack_repo_skills` carries the repository-conversion family. That script's\nown log is where its seed body ran or failed, and the body is silent on a healthy\nworkspace, so an empty\nlog with a missing file means the body did not run at all (restart the workspace and\nread that script's log). The trio moved out of `startup.sh.tftpl` into that one body, so\na startup-script log that says nothing no longer covers it. A `SKILL.md` that is present\nwhile its `scripts/*.py` helper is missing is the same story on the helper's own absent\nguard: the family seeds six payloads, each guarded on its own path."],[1576,"One OpenAI-compatible door, several backends behind it: the Arc 140T through IPEX-LLM, and\noptional Spark boxes. Which one answers is a routing decision made in\nlitellm-routing.md, not in the client — and that page also states the\none thing the local model cannot do."],[536,"One other writer touches the env files, and it is not this tool. With\n`INFISICAL_ENABLED` on, `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack -- up`,\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` and `bazelisk run //scripts:update-stack` each\npull the env file from the vault immediately before compose interpolates it, through\n`infisical_pull` in `scripts/lib/infisical.sh`. That pass **does** overwrite values you\nset, and only the twelve it manages: the keys listed in `infisical/managed-secrets.txt`,\nwhich are the stack's service credentials. Every other key in the file - the ports, the\nhosts, the naming parts, the URLs, the feature flags - keeps what you set. A managed key\nthe vault does not hold, or holds empty, is left alone too, with one `warning:` naming\nthe key instead of a blanked line, and a failed export leaves the file untouched so the\nbring-up runs on the credentials it already has. With the flag off - the shipped default"],[1142,"One pane per visible terminal is what that rule buys. Clients attached to one\ntmux session share that session's current window, so a design that always\nreattaches makes every extra browser tab a mirror of the same pane: another\nfull xterm.js in the Chrome tab re-parsing the same stream (each holding a\nline-slot array sized from `terminal.integrated.scrollback`, with the cell\ndata allocated per line as output arrives), and another full copy of the\nrendered stream through the pty. Measured on one flooding pane, 16 mirrors\ntook 78.7 MB out of the server over an 8 s window where a single mirror took\n4.9 MB, while the server's own CPU only moved from 420 ms to 630 ms - it\nrenders the pane once, so a mirror is paid for in the tab. A client\nactivating or resizing repaints the window's other clients too. Measured on\nthis workspace: 14 attached clients against 2 real panes. Two terminals\nopening in the same instant can still land on one session - that same\nmirror, and rarer than a keystroke."],[288,"One privileged `docker:29.8.1-dind` sidecar on a bridge network private to that\nworkspace (`coder-<wsid>-dind`, network alias `dind`)."],[474,"One repository per workspace is the default, and that is what `repo_url` and the\n**Git repository** field mean: one URL, cloned into one folder."],[2113,"One Spark alert closes the same shape on the other side. `SparkDown` needs the probe to\nfail as well as the scrape, so a box that is genuinely up and simply not reporting host\nmetrics pages nothing, and `SparkGpuSeriesSilent` asks only the DCGM family.\n`SparkHostMetricsSilent` is the one that reads a box whose health probe succeeds while\n`spark:box_up` has never been 1 - the usual cause being a hand-edited file where the\nstamped `exporter` label lost its exact spelling, which drops that box's group out of\nevery job's keep filter."],[3023,"One verdict per branch, with the evidence beside it."],[303,"One workspace container from the golden image: launcher and clone target only. It still\nhas to be the golden image, because that is what ships the Docker client the\ndevcontainer flow talks through. The editor's real environment is whatever the repo\nbuilds, so `image` decides nothing about the toolchain inside it."],[1204,"One writer per layer is the rule that keeps a child from inheriting the parent's session:\nchildren write only their own `results/T<nn>.md`, never a brief, and the parent is the\nonly thing that moves a fact sideways from one child to the next (a child reports\n`found:`, the parent writes it into the lane brief before the next wave). `ctx/L0.md` is\nfrozen before wave 1, because children already running read the copy that was there when\nthey started - editing it mid-run is a re-dispatch, not an edit."],[2671,"One-off fix, from the stack host:"],[2482,"Only about 4 subagents run at once though `[subagents] max_concurrent` says 15 -> the writers are on a pinned lane"],[294,"Only the literal string `\"true\"` enables it; anything else reads as off."],[415,"Only the variables `main.tf` passes interpolate. The `workspace` startup script gets\neleven: `boot_log_lib`, `grok_config`, `notes_setup`, `tmux_conf`,\n`grok_build_delegation_skill`, `coder_agents`,\n`mcp_cache_proxy`, `repo_url`, `additional_repos`, `vscode_settings`, `infisical_hook`.\nThe `devcontainer` twin adds `dc_dir` and `build_devcontainer`. The payloads of the other\nfamilies are not among them: `ez_learn_skill`, `ez_skillify_skill` and\n`ez_create_skill_skill` belong to the `coder_script.stack_skills` map,\n`grok_build_agents` to `coder_script.agents_seed`, the fleet's thirteen to\n`coder_script.stack_fleet`, the advisors' seven (`documenter_skill`, `documenter_agent`,\n`performance_optimizer_skill`, `debugger_skill`, `migrator_skill`, `ez_code_index_skill`,\n`ez_code_index_py`) to `coder_script.stack_advisors`, and the guards' six\n(`evo_t1_hooks_json`, `secret_guard_sh`, `dangerous_command_guard_sh`,\n`post_edit_format_sh`, `selective_test_runner_sh`, `deny_patterns_txt`) to\n`coder_script.stack_hooks`. Each of those `chomp(file())` calls sits inside its own block\nrather than behind a `local.`, because a size reader credits a payload by the\n`chomp(file(\"${path.module}/name\"))` literal it can see in the body's own block, and\nfollows a `local.` exactly one hop (`scripts/lib/startup_size.awk`, invariants)."],[2917,"Only then look at tenancy. Mimir is multi-tenant by design and keeps tenancy on until\na config says otherwise; once it is on, every read and every write must carry an\n`X-Scope-OrgID` header or be refused. This stack turns it off with\n`multitenancy_enabled: false` in `observability/mimir/mimir.yml`, so the provisioned\ndatasource sends no tenancy header and the compose network is the trust boundary. A\nquery that starts insisting on a header means that file changed, not that the data went\nmissing - and re-enabling tenancy is a change to the `remote_write` credentials as\nwell, not just to Grafana."],[381,"Only three directories under `~/.grok` are shared, and that decides several of the\nrules above."],[395,"Only vim's own `.sw?` suffix on that one name is a candidate, and a symlink at that name\nis left alone, since archiving a link would move the name and strand the bytes behind it.\nSo `.notes.md.swp` and a `.config.toml.newswp` are never touched. Each move is announced\non the body's log with the archived path in it, because the name carries a suffix nobody\ncan guess."],[653,"Open it on a laptop"],[1519,"Open the assistant in your own terminal (subcommands `chat`, `ask`, `shell`, `check`,\n`commands`; `--help` for the flags):"],[619,"Open the checkout in the contributor dev container (`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`\nbuilds it) and run the commands below unchanged."],[665,"Opening the repo as a contributor container in VS Code or a compatible editor"],[1406,"Opening the same assistant in your own terminal with `hermes-shell`"],[2070,"Operate the box"],[2072,"Operate the box"],[1843,"operate/index.md for the rest of the day-to-day runbooks."],[1938,"Operator checks"],[2294,"Operator CLIs source `scripts/lib/common.sh` and do not redefine `log` / `die` /\n`skip`. Grouping: `section` prints `== name ==`, `phase` prints `-> text`, `ok`\nprints `ok: text`, `summary` prints `summary: N ok, W warnings, F failed` plus\n`  failed: …` and optional `next: cmd`. `JSON_MODE` set sends prose to stderr.\nCLIs without `--json` unset `JSON_MODE` after source. Keep prefixes `warning:` /\n`note:` / `error:`."],[286,"Opt-in in `workspace` (the **Docker-in-docker** Yes/No question, preset *AI workspace +\nDinD*, template variable `dind`). With the toggle off, `docker` in that workspace fails\nthe normal way rather than against a daemon that does not exist."],[2571,"Or check the whole fleet at once, from the checkout. `spark-verify.sh` takes `--json`\nand `--no-tests`; `--no-tests` skips the recipe's own probes (a 195k-token needle plus\na 1/2/4/5-client sweep, minutes per box) and leaves the quick read-only check:"],[2575,"Or repoint `SPARK1_OPENAI_URL`, `SPARK2_OPENAI_URL` and `SPARK3_OPENAI_URL` in `.env`\nat a tool-capable OpenAI-compatible endpoint, then restart the proxy so it re-reads\nthem. The stack runners have no `restart` subcommand, so this one stays a direct\ncompose call:"],[620,"Or, from a machine that is not in that container, `bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image --`\ndocker-runs `evo-t1-dev:latest` and runs the gate inside it."],[1767,"Order matters when an alias is new rather than changed: the proxy reads\n`litellm/config.yaml` at boot and a workspace renders its catalog from that file when it\nstarts, so restart the proxy first and boot the workspace after it. A workspace older than\nthe proxy asks for an alias the config does not have, and the router answers \"No\ndeployments available\" rather than falling back. The rollback is one variable per lane, and\nit does not need the proxy at all: `--var grok_subagents_lane=agent` re-pins the writers to\none box, and `--var grok_explore_lane=agent-low` and `--var grok_review_lane=agent` put each\nreader role back on a pinned alias that every config version already carries."],[2692,"Other refusals answer with the same exit code: `sessions should be nested with care`\n(a `TMUX` the tab inherited), `open terminal failed: not a terminal`, and `-c <dir>`\nnaming a directory that is gone."],[2791,"Output already scrolled off the screen: `Prefix-[` is a viewer, not a copier -\nwalk up with `k`, `PPage` or `Ctrl-u` until the line is painted on the screen,\nselect it with a client drag, then `q`. A selection made *inside* copy-mode is\ninert: every copy binding in `copy-mode-vi` is unbound, so nothing ends it."],[3020,"Owner and expiry on every flag, and one `chore:` pull request that deletes the flag, its\noff-path, and any branch still carrying it."],[733,"Pass only the repository URL. A named container argument is refused with a pointer to the\nhost shell, because that container lives on a daemon this environment cannot enumerate."],[2206,"Paste the machine identity's Universal Auth pair into `.env`. `bootstrap` prints the `note:`\nthat says so once it has written the identifiers, and nothing downstream guesses:\n`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_ID` and `INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET` are two of\nthe six keys `check` reports as empty, and a `push` or `pull` with either blank is refused\nbefore it authenticates. Create the credentials for that identity in the Infisical UI and put\nboth values in the env file; neither belongs in a commit."],[142,"Per template it checks `main.tf` exists (otherwise \"run this from the repo root\") and\nnotes a missing `.terraform.lock.hcl`."],[2954,"Per-repository notes"],[2963,"Per-repository notes for the four reference repos"],[2673,"Permanent fix: rebuild the image and the workspace, which then starts from the\nimage's own copy. See the next two entries for the command."],[2685,"Pin notes and the seeding contract: grok-build-and-agents.md."],[2764,"Pin provider versions locally only if you want to, per template directory. The push\ngoes ahead either way, and `workspace` and `devcontainer` are the two names:"],[1818,"Pin the identity while the box is still up, so a stranger's key cannot be\nmistaken for it later:"],[1168,"Pinned as `GROK_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` (1.0.77 at the time of\nwriting, mirrored as the Dockerfile `ARG` default). The image build unpacks that\npinned `.zst` and runs `grok --version` in two PATH contexts as a build-time smoke\ncheck."],[1865,"Pinning a scaffold revision, or rendering a local tree with no network at all"],[61,"Pinning autostop by hand"],[1320,"pins for the four auxiliary lanes - `general` and `explore` (the two subagent\ntypes) prefer `agent`, `compaction` and `title_generation` (the helper calls)\nprefer `chat` - chosen only from the aliases the health gate left standing, and\nwritten when a lane carries no pin or is pinned to one of these configs whose alias\nhas since gone unhealthy. A pin aimed at a config the script did not create is the\nadmin's and is left alone. Both helper lanes landing on a dead `chat` is what made\nconversations lose their titles and die at the compaction threshold, which is why a\nstale pin now moves instead of being kept forever. Neither preference list names\n`agent-swarm`: that config is registered for the model picker only, because these lanes\nserve the control-plane chat agent, which is one conversation, and one conversation\nwants the pin."],[1903,"Placeholders are substituted in file contents *and* in file and directory names:\n`__PROJECT_NAME__`, `__PROJECT_SLUG__`, `__PROJECT_MODULE__`, `__PROJECT_DESCRIPTION__`,\n`__PROJECT_OWNER__`, `__PROJECT_LICENSE_HOLDER__`, `__PROJECT_YEAR__`. Those seven are named once,\nin `NEW_REPO_TOKENS`, and they are the whole contract between this script and the scaffold -- a\nceiling rather than an equality, so the template may use a subset of them. A token that survives is\na hard failure, not a warning -- a repository that ships with its placeholders showing is worse\nthan no template at all."],[2544,"Placeholders: `<wsid>` is a workspace id and `<ws>` a workspace name. The workspace\ncontainer is `coder-<owner>-<ws>` (so `coder-alx-self-coder`); the `<wsid>` form names\nthe DinD pieces around it - the sidecar `coder-<wsid>-dind-sidecar`, on the network\n`coder-<wsid>-dind`, with its own `coder-<wsid>-dind-lib` volume. What runs \"from the\nstack host\" needs a shell there: inside a workspace `docker` addresses that workspace's\nown private daemon through `DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375`, so a `docker inspect` aimed at\nthe stack's `coder` or `router` container and run from inside a workspace queries the\nwrong daemon and answers `no such container`. `bazelisk run //scripts:...` also runs\nfrom the checkout."],[2393,"Plan (the default, and the one that is safe anywhere)"],[2120,"Plan is the default and opens no ssh session and no docker call, so the first two are\nsafe to run before anything is decided. `SPARK_MONITOR_ENABLED` (false as shipped) is the\nopt-in that decides whether `--targets` prints the three boxes or an empty array, and the\nDCGM image comes from `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE`. That one tag -\n`nvcr.io/nvidia/k8s/dcgm-exporter:4.4.2-4.7.0-ubuntu22.04` - is the pin this stack could\nnot verify upstream, so `--apply` is what proves it: it surfaces whatever the registry\nanswers when the pull fails, and `--status` prints the tag a box actually runs."],[229,"Point the CLI at the in-container plain listener, with a token by name:"],[3030,"Porting is re-adaptation. Take the files, then fix them against today's trunk:"],[773,"Predicting what a reader sees from what you wrote, without opening a browser to find out"],[1513,"Prefer structured commands - the existing Bazel scripts and `docker compose` - over\nad-hoc shell. A script the repo owns is reviewable and test-covered; a one-liner the\nmodel invented is neither. See AGENTS.md."],[1078,"Prepare the box: rotate the secrets, set `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, issue the self-signed\ncertificates, build `evo-t1-dev:latest`, pull the DinD image. With `INFISICAL_ENABLED` on\nin `.env`, a minted secret goes to the vault first and into `.env` only once Infisical has\naccepted it; if the vault refuses, that key keeps its `change-me` placeholder, the run\nwarns, and a later re-run picks it up. operate/secrets.md covers the\nvault itself. It is idempotent, so re-run it after changing `docker-compose.yml`,\n`litellm/config.yaml`, `searxng/settings.yml`, `infisical/` or `proxy/*.conf`."],[138,"Preset keys are parameter *names*; `display_name` is UI-only and would be ignored."],[51,"Presets"],[135,"Presets collapse the create form to one click."],[2506,"Private-repo clone or push denied while a credential is present -> the grant, not the format"],[2583,"Probe cadence. `health_check_interval` and `health_check_concurrency` in\n`litellm/config.yaml` decide how often every Spark deployment gets a real generation.\nThey sit at LiteLLM's own default of 300 s and a concurrency of 2 on purpose: at 30 s\nacross five deployments a round was putting more probes on a box than user requests,\nand each one took a prompt-cache state from a conversation. Auto-recovery does not\nneed 30 s -- a box that is actually down is pulled by `allowed_fails` and\n`cooldown_time` under live traffic, and the re-probe is what brings it back. If the\nwarnings come back every few seconds, that is the first key to look at."],[2570,"Probe that endpoint. The card reads the same URL: `AGENT_MODEL_URL` from `.env` when\nit is set, and otherwise box 2 composed from `SPARK2_HOST` plus `SPARK_HTTP_PORT`,\nwhich is what `spark-verify` prints as `spark2_openai` in its `--json` facts:"],[2266,"Project conventions"],[2268,"Project conventions"],[1578,"project-conventions.md is what a script has to look like to be\nmergeable, and branching.md is how it gets to `main`. The Bazel harness they\nboth assume is in the Contribute tab."],[2437,"Prometheus reads that one file with `file_sd_configs` at `refresh_interval: 60s` on all\nthree Spark jobs (`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:149`, `:171`, `:195`), so a\nchanged file is picked up within a minute without a restart. Because four kinds of target\nshare the file, each job keeps only its own with a relabel `action: keep` on the `exporter`\nlabel - without those filters the two probe URLs would be scraped as metric endpoints and\nevery box would carry two permanently failing targets. The two blackbox jobs take the\naddress from the same file and pass it as `__param_target`, so the target label carries the\nbox rather than the prober. The prober itself is scraped as a service of its own\n(`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:219`), which is what makes\n`up{job=\"blackbox\"}` exist for `BlackboxProbeGap` to fire on. Spark alerts stamp\n`tier=spark` and `spark=<hostname>`."],[2523,"Prometheus shows no target for a service -> the scrape list, the `/metrics` path, or the bearer"],[3005,"Promotion edits the tag, not a branch:"],[2975,"Promotion is the same commit or tag applied to a different environment. Nothing is rebuilt\nper environment, because a per-environment build is a per-environment bug surface."],[3008,"Promotion is the same playbook revision and role versions against a different inventory. A\nbranch that carries playbook changes is a pull request; a branch that carries only variable\nchanges is a file under `group_vars/`."],[2394,"Prove (`--status`)"],[1296,"Prove all three at once with `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check`: it reads the\nrendered `~/.grok/config.toml`, reports whether each lane is declared, whether its\n`command=` binary is present, whether the lane's credential and cache config are\nthere, and what each server's handshake says. A binary the box cannot find is image\ndrift (the running `evo-t1-dev` predates the npm bake), and the check prints the\npinned `npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command that restores it."],[2933,"Prove liveness the way the stack does: the **Observability** cards on the dashboard\n(`http://loki:3100/ready`, `http://tempo:3200/ready`, `http://mimir:8080/ready` over\ncompose DNS), or a datasource's *Test* button in Grafana. Those three publish no host\nport, so there is no host-side curl to fall back to, and `docker compose exec` cannot\nhelp either - there is nothing to exec into. The container log is the last word:"],[2587,"Prove recurrence before you touch anything. The events are in each session's\n`updates.jsonl`, so one pass over the store counts attempts against outcomes. The\nstore rides the owner's `grok-sessions-<owner-id>` volume (`shared` mode), so run\nit where the sessions are (inside a workspace), and leave the `~/.grok/sessions`\npath %-escaped as the CLI wrote it:"],[2209,"Prove the round trip:"],[2528,"Public `grafana-gmktecbeast` answers `421` -> the name composed, the container did not"],[2517,"Public `hermes-gmktecbeast` answers `421` -> no vhost, a rewritten Host, or Traefik aimed past the router"],[518,"Public hosts and the dashboard"],[2049,"Public mode follows the same path as the other names: `STACK_GRAFANA_NAME` (default label\n`grafana`) is composed by `scripts/lib/domain.sh` with the rest, `bootstrap` writes\n`STACK_GRAFANA_URL`, and the router sends that Host to `grafana:3000`. Only Grafana gets\na name - the other nine have no reason to answer the LAN, let alone the WAN, and\nAlertmanager least of all. Full\ndetail on the naming parts and the Traefik side is in\npublic-access.md."],[2307,"Public subdomains"],[2309,"Public subdomains"],[1842,"public-access.md for what the box exposes on the network\nafter it boots - the initramfs listener is gone by then."],[1081,"Push both templates (`workspace` first, `devcontainer` last). The source travels as a tar\non stdin and the credential is minted, used and deleted in the same run, so there is no\nhost `coder` CLI and no login here:"],[2383,"Put LiteLLM behind it and every client breaks: LiteLLM authenticates from the\n`Authorization` header, and header-carried API traffic cannot pass an interactive\nsign-in. Either leave `litellm-gmktecbeast` outside any policy or add an explicit\nbypass - and then `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` is the only credential in front of an\nendpoint that went from LAN-only to reachable WAN-wide (see\nSECURITY.md)."],[353,"Puts `~/.grok/bin` on PATH (login shells and that process), and appends the one-line\nreader that sources `~/.infisical/agent.env` under `set -a` into `~/.bashrc` - the reader\nis what makes the vault fetch worth doing at all\n(see The Infisical boot fetch)."],[2327,"Putting the seven routers on the Traefik box without guessing its timeouts"],[881,"Python"],[888,"Python: the mypy profile, the aliases that replace `Any`, and how to add a file"],[1481,"Query the lane the same way the agent does:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check` from a host shell counts results per\nengine, which is how an empty answer stays distinguishable from no answer.\nInside the container,\n`curl -s -H 'Accept: application/json' \"$SEARXNG_URL/search?q=hermes&format=json\"`\nis the exact request the provider makes. The instance only answers JSON\nbecause `searxng/settings.yml` adds `json` to `search.formats`; without it the\nrequest is refused with 403 before the query is parsed."],[807,"Raw query recipes when a verb does not fit"],[1482,"Re-point the lane with `STACK_HERMES_SEARCH_URL` in `.env` (a sibling box, say)\nand recreate. An operator who wants a keyed provider sets\n`web.search_backend` plus that vendor's key env in the container themselves -\nthe seed carries neither, and adding one is a decision with a privacy cost."],[378,"Re-rendering carries over the tables the CLI owns instead of wiping them: `[cli]` holds\n`cli.installer`, which picks the CLI's update path; `[ui]` holds `permission_mode`, so\nre-adopting a file must never flip an operator back to prompting; and `[marketplace]` is\nCLI settings the stack never owns."],[2695,"Reaching a shell once the sessions are gone: the profile does it for you now, and the\n**bash** profile in the terminal dropdown remains the route that no helper mediates.\nThen reseed from a host shell (`bazelisk run //scripts:update-configs -- workspace`)\nand restart the workspace, which is what re-renders its `~/.grok/config.toml`."],[1414,"Reaching the assistant on the LAN, and on the public name `auto` composes from the parts"],[1536,"Reaching the page that owns a subject from one card, instead of reading four that touch it"],[1937,"Reaching the UIs"],[2588,"Read `auto_compact_failed` against `auto_compact_started`. Over the 11-day window this\nwas investigated, the store held 82 failures across 15 sessions in 3 workspaces,\nescalating from a 39 per cent failure rate to 85 per cent (45 attempts in one day, 19\nof them in the worst single session), and 3 of the 15 sessions ended on a dangling\n`auto_compact_started` and were abandoned."],[2716,"Read both halves of the rendered config, not just the width:"],[1281,"Read it with `bazelisk run //scripts:session-archive -- list`. Every verb mounts the\nvolume read-only, so the reader cannot write it either, and there is no pruning verb:\nthe volume grows until an operator removes it. The raw `docker run -v <volume>:ro` and\n`docker volume rm <volume>` forms are host-shell commands, and the same deny list that\nprotects the archive from a workspace agent refuses them there - run them in a host\nshell, not from an agent prompt."],[3021,"Read once, branch there. A flag checked in nine modules is nine edits when it retires."],[2712,"Read the prompt's own lines before choosing. `modified: no` means it holds no\nunsaved work. The `host name` names the container whose vim wrote it, and each\nworkspace renders its own `config.toml` now, so a name that is not this\nworkspace's means the swap predates that split - it is left over from when\n`~/.grok` was one whole-profile per-owner volume, which a current boot mounts\nread-only at `~/.grok-legacy` for the one-time state copy and reads nowhere else.\nA name that is this workspace's own is the ordinary case: the home volume outlives\nevery stop and recreate."],[2822,"Read the report by its last line, then by these. `git would send this credential\n(source: <slots>)` is the credential a real push uses, in git's consult order; the same\ntoken held by several slots is named once, as one credential with several names. A plain\n`credential present (source: <slots>)` is a credential that exists and that git never\nreaches."],[2699,"Read the start log before touching the profile. The same boot takes the rest of the\n`~/.grok` family with it, because those bodies raced the same chown: `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.grok/agents/`, `~/.grok/personas/` and `~/.grok/hooks/`. An affected workspace is\nmissing all of them at once, which is how to tell this from one payload that drifted."],[1643,"Read these three facts before changing the block:"],[2719,"Read those same four lines for the depth, because the block now prints `max_depth`\nbeside `max_concurrent` (`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:428-429`), rendered\nfrom the `grok_subagent_max_depth` variable whose default is 2. At 2 a child spawns\nread-only mappers of its own, so a wave can be wider than the parent's own `slots`, and\neach in-flight child holds one of the fleet's 15 streams: a parent sums `1 + spawns` per\ntask and keeps that total at or below the fleet's stream count, because a stream past 15\nis refused by the box. At 1 nothing nests, which is the rollback."],[1520,"Read-only audit of what the assistant was created with, and how far it has drifted from\nthis checkout (`report`, `posture`, `web`, `diff`; `--check` narrows to one):"],[106,"Reading a failed push or a stuck workspace against what the template actually does"],[2408,"Reading GPU utilisation, temperature, power and framebuffer pressure per Spark in\nGrafana instead of ssh-ing into a box and running `nvidia-smi`"],[1015,"Reading the box's health from one page without a shell"],[1949,"Reading the box's history from Grafana instead of from `docker compose logs`"],[1806,"Reading the exact ssh command and host key fingerprint to expect"],[1603,"Reading the routing back"],[1620,"Reading the routing back, and editing an alias safely"],[1588,"Reading the table"],[2834,"Reads prove nothing about push, so nothing here asks a read question: a public repo\nanswers a read for any authenticated caller, so a credential that READS a repo can still\nbe refused every WRITE to it. Go to the GitHub side only after the report says\nREFUSED-GRANT or NOT VISIBLE."],[1819,"Reboot the box."],[1804,"Rebooting a server nobody is standing next to"],[2706,"Rebuild the image, then rebuild the workspace; the image is fixed at create time."],[3037,"Record the tip SHA of every branch in that table before anything goes. A recorded SHA is\nrecoverable until garbage collection; an unrecorded one is not."],[18,"Recovering a half-finished change with one revert, because each landed step was committed as it landed"],[2211,"Recreate the consumers so they pick up what the vault holds:"],[676,"Recreating the container without paying for a cold Bazel cache again"],[2081,"Refreshing a stale checkout before diagnosing what it looks like"],[791,"Regenerate after you change either the tree or the file layout:"],[1080,"Register the first account — that account is the site admin. Browsers only expose\n`crypto.randomUUID()` in a secure context, so this points at the TLS listener:"],[1067,"Registering the first Coder account, pushing both templates, wiring the chat agent"],[1139,"Related: coder-templates.md (the template files themselves),\nlitellm-routing.md (what the aliases route to), and\ntroubleshooting.md."],[1626,"Related: coder-templates.md (the templates that render\n`~/.grok/config.toml`), grok-build-and-agents.md (the CLI and\nthe control-plane chat), project-conventions.md."],[1418,"Related: dashboard.md (the card that links here),\nlitellm-routing.md (the aliases Hermes calls),\npublic-access.md (the public name, which `auto` composes),\nSECURITY.md."],[2265,"Related: observability.md (the `GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD` login and\nthe log stream these containers ship), public-access.md (the\n`infisical-*` public name), troubleshooting.md."],[109,"Related: project-conventions.md (shell and payload conventions),\nSECURITY.md, and\ndocs/grok-build-and-agents.md\n(the long prose on the tmux profiles and the Grok Build session)."],[2739,"Remedy, from the stack host, in this order:"],[1176,"Rendered per workspace from `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl` (and\n`templates/devcontainer/`'s copy of the same payload), and re-rendered idempotently\nwhile the managed block is intact."],[1850,"Rendering a repository"],[1858,"Rendering a repository with `new-repo`, its flags, and its lanes"],[356,"Renders `~/.grok/config.toml` (the three gated MCP servers ship by default; the five\noffline stdio ones are not declared)."],[949,"Reproducing a CI failure locally by running the suites in the image CI runs them in"],[674,"Reproducing CI's failure on your own machine with the same image CI runs"],[2896,"Reset Kasm by removing the `kasm-data` volume, which destroys all Kasm config. Stop\nthe stack first, and use the name your stack owns: `evo-t1-coder-stack_kasm-data` for\nthe stable stack, `evo-t1-dev_kasm-data` for the dev one."],[2801,"Residual: a tab you hide rather than close keeps its xterm instance alive and\nstill buffers whatever the pane prints, and nothing in the stack caps that.\nNeither does tmux: 3.4 has no per-client output limit, and an ordinary\nattached client that stops draining its pty is never dropped (measured here:\none stayed attached past 15 minutes while the server kept serving commands).\nThe only \"too far behind\" disconnect is in control mode, behind a hardcoded\n300 s age in `control.c`, which a VS Code tab never uses.\nmicrosoft/vscode#327437\nis open (a heap snapshot showed ~38 live terminals holding ~80 MB of a 484 MB\nrenderer heap) and\ncoder/code-server#6413\nreports browser-tab RAM reaching several GB with a continuous-output terminal\nleft running after the tab is closed; neither has a maintainer mitigation.\nClosing the tab is the mitigation, and the numbered sessions are what make the\nlive set match the tabs you can see."],[1765,"Restart the proxy on the stack you changed, so it re-reads that file."],[2809,"Restart the workspace afterwards so `GH_TOKEN` and the gh auth store match the new\ngrant. `fix` makes git push work without the restart; the injected pair itself only\nchanges when the workspace restarts or `gh_token_refresh` next runs."],[2726,"Restarting is not optional: the value is baked into the agent's startup script at push\ntime, and `update-configs` deliberately never re-copies `config.toml`.\ndocs/litellm-routing.md and docs/grok-build-and-agents.md#the-fan-out-seam."],[2432,"Restricts any mode to one box number (`1`, `2`, `3`). A number outside the resolved fleet\nis refused with the fleet size and the resolved list. Measured, plan mode with `--box 1`:"],[1936,"Retention and disk"],[1946,"Retention caps, the named volumes the data lands on, and how to reach each UI"],[2124,"Rotate a credential - what to re-check after a key the bundle reads moves"],[2151,"Rotating a key without opening an editor, and knowing when the running service picks it up"],[2136,"Rotating CODER_PG_PASSWORD"],[1470,"Route and model are two separate switches. A `providers:` row selects the route -\nbase URL, api key, api mode - and the model for a turn is otherwise the value in\nthe `model:` block. Measured on the shipped image, `--provider <row>` moves the\nroute and takes that row's `default_model` with it: `--provider arc-chat` sends\n`\"model\":\"chat\"`, `--provider arc-coder` sends `\"model\":\"coder\"`,\n`--provider arc-coder-fast` sends `\"model\":\"coder-fast\"`, and `--provider litellm`\nsends `\"model\":\"agent\"` - that row's `default_model` is `agent`, which is also what\nthe `model:` block holds. `-m/--model <alias>` overrides it: `--model agent-swarm`\nsends `\"model\":\"agent-swarm\"`, and `--provider litellm --model chat` sends\n`\"model\":\"chat\"`. A run with no flags sends the `model:` block's value (`agent`)\nwhatever rows exist, and `hermes config set model.provider <row>` changes the route\nand leaves that value alone - the `default_model` pickup is gated on the CLI flag,\nnot on the config key."],[1592,"Routing inside the `agent` group"],[1618,"Routing inside the `agent` group, the four unpinned lanes (`agent-swarm`,\n`agent-explore`, `agent-review`, `agent-swarm-fast`), and the `agent` to `coder`\nfallback"],[1971,"Rules are evaluated by Prometheus and delivered by Alertmanager: the `alerting:` block at\n`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:52` names `alertmanager:9093`, so grouping,\ninhibition, silences and the repeat interval all belong to that one service."],[3017,"Rules that keep flags alive long enough to be removed:"],[2411,"Run every command here in a **shell on the stack host**. The probe reaches each box over\nssh, and a Coder workspace container is not a shell that resolves the LAN names the\n`SPARKn_HOST` keys hold - the workspace-side run in the examples below shows exactly what\nthat costs. The whole bundle this depends on sits behind the Compose profile\n`profiles: [\"observability\"]`, and observability.md is the page for\nthe six services that do the scraping."],[532,"Run it after you pull, not before: a pull is what changes the templates these\ntools copy from. `bazelisk run` feeds the real checkout (`BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY`),\nnot the runfiles copy, so the env pass rewrites the live `.env` beside\n`docker-compose.yml`."],[1810,"Run it before every reboot of an encrypted box, and after any kernel or\n`initramfs-tools` update. It exits `1` when something blocks the unlock, so it\nfits in front of a maintenance script as a gate."],[905,"Run it directly when you want the error text without Bazel in the way:"],[2913,"Run the same query in Explore against the `prometheus` datasource and then the `mimir`\none, over the same range. Data in one and not the other is a write-path fault, not a\ndashboard fault."],[933,"Running a suite"],[614,"Running any operator CLI from a foreign working directory with pinned inputs"],[2956,"Running it"],[942,"Running one suite, from any directory"],[1413,"Running stack operations by conversation with a plan and an approval first"],[675,"Running the Bazel gate on macOS without maintaining a second, BSD-flavoured toolchain"],[659,"Running the gate here"],[2097,"Running the observability bundle day to day: where the 30 alerts live, what Alertmanager pages on and off, why its UI stays on loopback, where a silence belongs, and how the three DGX Sparks become Prometheus targets."],[2969,"Running the same procedure in any of this fleet's repositories with `/trunk-refactor`"],[819,"Running the suites that cover the files you touched instead of the whole fast gate"],[2306,"Runtime stays `docker compose`: Bazel runs the CLIs that drive Compose and never talks to a\ndaemon, a Spark box, or a model registry by itself. No Kubernetes layer."],[2955,"Safety lines"],[2863,"Same script, one line, and it is a note rather than a fault:"],[180,"Same trust model as `coder reset-password`, which also bypasses the API and talks to\nthe database directly."],[945,"Sandbox-safe repo-root probing"],[938,"Sandbox-safe: probe for the repo root, then say so"],[2439,"Say these plainly, because each one costs someone an afternoon if they meet it\nunexpected:"],[9,"Scope"],[605,"Script targets and runfiles"],[612,"Script targets, runfiles, and why a new `scripts/*.sh` is a target automatically"],[1310,"Scripted, idempotent, re-runnable, and already started from `prod-stack` /\n`dev-stack` `up` (`--apply --wait`, default 1800 s). Registering the first account\nis the only operator step: the seeder continues once an owner exists, and the Agents\npage is usable with no extra Coder UI setup. Print the plan:"],[2273,"Scripts"],[1112,"Search lane and MCP servers"],[2466,"Search never answers (or answers empty) -> prove the search lane before blaming the MCP hop"],[2375,"SearXNG is deliberately absent. It stays LAN-only on `:8181`, which is where the\nworkspaces reach it through `host.docker.internal`; putting it behind