er tokens create`."],[1267,"Set in two places: the template variable `grok_profile_mode`\n(`GROK_PROFILE_MODE=shared|private` in `.env`, forwarded by `push-template.sh` as\n`--var grok_profile_mode`), and the create form's per-workspace **Grok profile**\nfield, which defaults to the variable. One workspace can therefore sit outside the\nshared state while its siblings stay inside it."],[2367,"Set the naming parts in `.env` and put `auto` in `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` (`.env.sample`\ndocuments every part and shows the explicit-list shape beside it), and in\n`dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs beside the stable one. Then re-run `bootstrap`\nand recreate the stack you switched. The commands are in the README."],[582,"Set the parts in `.env`, and in `dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs beside\nthe stable one, then re-run `bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` and recreate the\nstack you switched. That one command is the whole rename: it re-renders the router's\nvhost table and rewrites the derived URLs, so a hostname is no longer an edit split\nacross `proxy/router.conf`, `scripts/bootstrap.sh` and `docker-compose.yml`. A\n`hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is part of that list:\nbootstrap writes `STACK_HERMES_URL`, and the LAN `:9119` listener stays either\nway. Leave the hermes name out and the Homepage card keeps the LAN URL.\npublic-access.md owns the naming rule, the measured\n`ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` on the dotted form, the request path and the\nchecks to run on the Traefik box."],[854,"Seven logged sessions guessed a target name and got `ERROR: no such target`. A target is never\nguessed, it is listed:"],[839,"Seven suites, printed in about 60 ms. Change that file and those are the suites that go red; edit one\nof them and the same verb in the other direction tells you what it covers."],[67,"Shape"],[2284,"Shell follows the Google Shell Style Guide.\nBranch rules live in branching.md; the Bazel harness lives in\ncontribute/building-with-bazel.md and\ncontribute/test-with-bazel.md."],[2968,"Shipping work that is not finished, on the trunk, without exposing it"],[1625,"Short version lives in README.md (\"LiteLLM model aliases\"); this file is\nthe long version behind it. Symptom-first checks live in\ntroubleshooting.md."],[2877,"Sign-in is `HERMES_DASHBOARD_USER` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD` from `.env`\n(default `admin` / `change-me-hermes` until bootstrap rotates the\nplaceholders). To fill missing keys and replace those placeholders:"],[1422,"Sign-in is HTTP basic auth from `.env`: `HERMES_DASHBOARD_USER` (default `admin`) and\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD`. Bootstrap generates the password; it is **not** the\nHomepage password, and `bazelisk run //scripts:dashboard-password` does not rotate it.\nThat basic auth stays on every door. Hermes v0.22.5 refuses to bind `0.0.0.0` with no\nprovider. On the public URL Authelia challenges first and basic auth challenges\nsecond; two logins are expected. LAN `:9119`, and LAN `:8080` with this name's Host\nheader, never see Authelia, so basic auth is the credential on those paths."],[1407,"Sign-in: HTTP basic auth, with Authelia in front of the public name"],[2656,"Signed into Authelia with the link still failing puts the fault behind Authelia: the\napp path is gated by the same policy as the rest of the coder name."],[1246,"Six payloads in `templates/common/` become one manifest and five files under `~/.grok/hooks/`:\n`evo-t1-hooks.json` plus, under `hooks/bin/`, the four guards and the deny list the command\nguard reads.\nGrok Build reads `~/.grok/hooks/*.json`, and a handler `command` that is a relative path\nresolves against the manifest's own directory and is started directly, so each script is seeded\n`0755` with the payload's `#!` line still on row 1. Every one reads one line of event JSON on\nstdin. The two PreToolUse guards always print a parseable decision there - `{\"decision\":\"allow\"}`\nor `{\"decision\":\"deny\",\"reason\":...}` - and a deny additionally exits 2; a PostToolUse handler\ncannot block at all, so it stays silent on stdout unless it has something to tell the model. No\nguard exits non-zero on its own account, so a broken one cannot wedge a session."],[33,"Small commits cost nothing at the gate: `.githooks/pre-commit` runs the hermetic half on every\ncommit and `.githooks/pre-push` runs the full set before anything leaves the machine, so a stream of\nfocused commits is held to the same bar as one large one. See `main` for installing them."],[3002,"so `docker compose up` stays clean and `--profile experimental` opts in."],[2910,"So a console that shows only `skipping rendering` is a healthy Grafana, and a panel that\nis empty is not this line's fault - take the datasource path below."],[1777,"So an alias edited or deleted in `litellm/config.yaml` moves in the Agents model\ndropdowns exactly as it does in the CLI."],[2243,"So neither side can fail to *start* over a vault that is down. The workspace is the one that\nnever can: every path in the boot body, refusals included, exits 0 and the agent goes on with\nthe environment it already had. The stack is the one with state to corrupt, and the way it\nbreaks is the opposite of an outage - the refresh succeeds by writing, so a vault that answers\nbut holds a value nothing authenticates with is what brings `coder`, `litellm`, `homepage`,\n`hermes`, `grafana` or `searxng` up unable to log in. That is a converge-and-recreate problem,\nnot a start problem: fix the value in the vault, run `up` again, and read the `db` and `coder`\nlogs if Coder takes longer than a recreate to come back."],[991,"So resolve the root from the environment first, then prove it, and fail loudly when the\nproof fails. `resolve_checkout` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` is that resolver and every CLI that\nreads a file the checkout alone holds calls it, so do not write a second one. It takes the calling\nscript's own directory as its last candidate and the marker to probe for (`docker-compose.yml` by\ndefault); the call site in every one of those CLIs is this:"],[1023,"so the card that opens Grafana Explore for Loki and Tempo opens the same Grafana the\ndatasources point at. The Grafana card also carries a `customapi` widget on\n`/api/health` - the one endpoint that needs no session or API key - so the tile shows the\nstorage state and the running version, which is what you compare against the image pin in\n`docker-compose.yml` after a pull. Only Grafana has a URL a browser can resolve; the\nthree backends are reached from inside it. See observability.md."],[308,"So the clone must exist at the same path inside the sidecar too, or it dies with\n`bind source path does not exist`."],[1680,"So the limitation tracks the model, not the runtime."],[1325,"So the two compactions fail for unrelated reasons, and the fix for one is not the fix\nfor the other:"],[2026,"So the way to change a panel is to edit its JSON and let the interval apply it. The folder\nis bind-mounted read-only onto the path the provider scans (`docker-compose.yml:1049`), so\nan edit to a file that is already there is visible to the running container and shows up\nwithin a minute - no restart, and no recreate. A new file needs no registration either, for\nthe same reason. Recreate only when the mount line or the provisioning tree itself changed\n(`docker-compose.yml:1047-1048`), and then with the `up -d` rule from the section above:"],[2748,"So the workspace build fails at **plan** time, before anything is created, and it\nprints in the workspace's build log rather than anywhere inside the workspace."],[1785,"So you can flip `coder` from the local Arc to a Spark box, or add a new alias, and no\nworkspace setting has to change."],[519,"Spark agent model group"],[2387,"Spark monitoring"],[2389,"Spark monitoring"],[2123,"Spark monitoring - enrolling the three DGX Sparks: the two\nexporter containers, the `SPARK_MONITOR_ENABLED` opt-in, and what `--apply` does to a\nbox."],[1685,"Spreading across every reachable box is the point of running three of them, five full\n262144-token streams each. Pinning only narrows the candidate list first."],[1521,"Stable stack - `hermes` comes up with the rest:"],[2565,"Stable stack (`prod-stack` addresses the `evo-t1-prod` project by default; name\n`--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` to read a stack brought up by hand under the\ncheckout folder's own project name):"],[1316,"Stack `up` starts that seeder in the background and keeps its output in\n`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/coder-agents-seeder.log` (`CODER_AGENTS_SEED_LOG` moves it, and the\n`note:` line stack `up` prints names the path it chose). Read that file first when\nthe Agents chat is dead after a boot: the seeder's most common outcome is a `skipped:`\nline that exits 0 - no coder container yet, no db container, nobody registered - and\nan unwritable log path degrades to the old discard with a `warning:` instead of\nfailing the boot."],[2451,"Stack bring-up"],[2532,"Stack bring-up: bootstrap, ports, containers that refuse to start"],[1190,"Stack ownership of the file rests on either marker spelling: the line-1\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment or the top-level `__evoT1Stack = true` key.\nBoth twins emit both, because the comment alone does not last - `grok mcp add`\npersists its own settings by rewriting the file and deletes every comment line, so a\ncomment-only marker handed the config to the user with no human in the loop, and the\ndefault lanes silently stopped arriving. A hand-edited config is left alone only\nwhile it carries one of them, so takeover means deleting **both** lines; delete the\nfile and restart to hand it back. When the guard does re-render, the CLI-owned tables\n`[cli]`, `[ui]` and `[marketplace]` are carried over rather than wiped, because the\nstack owns no approval mode - `[ui] permission_mode` / yolo stays a per-user call."],[602,"Stack-host bootstrap"],[608,"Stack-host bootstrap: the one script run straight from a shell"],[80,"Stale editor swap files"],[1528,"Start here"],[1079,"Start the stable stack, and pull the models `OLLAMA_MODELS` names (a large download):"],[2202,"Start the three services. `up` selects the profile now that the flag is on:"],[2050,"Start with the one command that asks all of these at once, then drop to the individual\nprobes when it names a section:"],[2606,"Start with the proxy's own line for the call:"],[1863,"Starting a new repository with the quality gate already in place, in one command"],[2540,"Starting from the error text you actually saw and landing on one command"],[83,"Startup script templatefile rules"],[1793,"Static networks"],[822,"Stopping the late-gate discovery of a coupling rule the graph already recorded"],[877,"Strong types"],[879,"Strong types"],[574,"Switching either way is a **rebuild**, not a stop/start, because the nested mount\nis decided when the workspace is built.\ngrok-build-and-agents.md covers what the state volumes\nhold and what seeds on first boot; the `workspace` pass above never\nreseeds `~/.grok/config.toml` for exactly that reason."],[2449,"Symptom-first list of what breaks on this box: the error string, the cause, the recipe, and what the fix costs."],[2760,"Symptom: `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` prints `== Preflight ==` and\n`== Variables ==`, then aborts inside `== Templates ==` with\n`push-template.sh: line 740: REPO_ROOT: unbound variable` and never prints a\n`summary:` line. Nothing was pushed."],[2750,"Symptom: `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` queues, then dies during\n\"Initializing Terraform Directory\". `workspace` cannot list versions for\n`coder/coder` and `kreuzwerker/docker` on `registry.terraform.io`;\n`devcontainer` cannot fetch `module \"devcontainers_cli\"` from\n`registry.coder.com`. Both lookups fail with\n`dial tcp: lookup … on 127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`. Coder itself\nstays up; `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs coder` repeats the same Terraform\nlines."],[2754,"Symptom: `coder templates push` plans, then dies during \"Cleaning Up\" with\n`internal provisionerserver error: execute transaction: insert resource: insert\nagent app: upsert app: pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`.\nCoder logs `coder_app's id was unexpectedly empty` and then that Postgres error\nat `inserting template import job resource`."],[2757,"Symptom: both templates fail during \"Detecting persistent resources\" with\n`Terraform initialized in an empty directory!` / `Error: No configuration files`.\nCoder logs `unpacking source archive  size_bytes=30720` (tens of KB, not the\n~120 KB of real `.tf` / `.tftpl` contents)."],[897,"Telling a contributor why `Any` came back from CI"],[2108,"Telling a firing alert from a missing exporter before you restart anything"],[529,"Telling a ported env key apart from a value you set yourself and must keep"],[1866,"Telling a refused GitHub permission apart from a broken render"],[2326,"Telling a router problem apart from an Authelia problem when a name fails"],[821,"Telling a rule class from a target name without opening a BUILD file"],[1950,"Telling a scrape fault from a write fault from a read fault in three commands"],[677,"Telling the contributor container, the Coder workspace and the stack host apart in one look"],[183,"Template source travels as a tar on stdin (`coder templates push --directory -`), so\nnothing is copied into the container or its volumes."],[63,"Template variables"],[243,"Template variables are pushed defaults; the create form can still override them per\nworkspace. The four `grok_*_lane` knobs below are `--var` only, and each moves one role, so\na single role can be put back on the parent's pinned lane without moving the others - the\nrollback an operator needs when a workspace is older than the LiteLLM config that registers\nits alias (`templates/workspace/coder.tf:19-22`)."],[468,"Template-side facts only; the long prose lives in\ndocs/grok-build-and-agents.md."],[2274,"Templates"],[2055,"Ten rows when `OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED` is on, none when it is off. Expect `running`, not\n`healthy`, on the eight that declare no `healthcheck`: `mimir`, `loki`, `tempo` and\n`alloy` (those four images are distroless, so they carry no shell and no `wget`/`curl`\nfor a healthcheck to run), plus `node_exporter`, `cadvisor`, `blackbox_exporter` and\n`alertmanager`. Only `prometheus` and `grafana` declare one. A missing row means the\nprofile was not selected - see the\ntroubleshooting entry on which env file the dev project actually reads."],[2935,"Ten rows when the profile is selected, none when it is not. With the flag off the five\n**Observability** cards on the dashboard read `down` together, which is the honest signal\nrather than a card bug: the badge reports what each endpoint answers, and the flag is the\nreason none of them answer. Then:"],[1952,"Ten services sit behind one Compose profile. Six of them store and read - `prometheus`,\n`mimir`, `loki`, `tempo`, `grafana` and `alloy` - and four watch the box and speak up -\n`node_exporter`, `cadvisor`, `blackbox_exporter` and `alertmanager`. They answer one\nquestion the rest of the stack cannot - what this box was doing ten minutes ago - and they\nare the only part of the stack that keeps data after a container is gone. Everything else on this page is about the seams between them,\nbecause that is where a monitoring stack silently stops working: a service can be\n`running`, a dashboard can be green, and a panel can still be empty."],[1641,"TensorFold on the Spark boxes refuses an over-admitted stream with the Zig error value\n`NoRoom`, delivered as an HTTP 500 body. The OpenAI SDK hands that to LiteLLM with no\nint `status_code`, which at the pinned `v1.102.1` puts it on the status-less branch of\n`exception_mapping_utils`, so the router sees `APIConnectionError`."],[91,"Terminal and editor defaults"],[1092,"Terminal and editor defaults"],[102,"Terminal and editor defaults inside the container, and workspace GitHub credentials"],[1125,"Terminal and editor defaults, and the profile the templates seed"],[883,"Terraform"],[2950,"Terraform / OpenTofu with ArgoCD"],[2487,"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI"],[890,"Terraform: why a `variable` without a `type` is a runtime bug"],[928,"Testing with Bazel (the Bats harness)"],[930,"Testing with Bazel (the Bats harness)"],[2271,"Tests"],[725,"That `notes.md` is `notes_materialize` from the same library: it restores the canonical copy\nunder `~/.grok/notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/` over a live file that is empty or still the\nseeded stub, and copies a real live file back there, so a stub never walks over written\nnotes. Here that runs once, at seed time - the background watcher that repeats the back-copy\nabout every 30 seconds is a workspace-startup thing (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL`; see\n../grok-build-and-agents.md) - so re-run the\nseeder to push a fresh edit to the canonical copy in this container."],[1760,"That 30-request burst was multi-session, fan-out-style traffic."],[1019,"That box asks the stack's own SearXNG rather than a public engine, so a search from the\ndashboard goes to the same instance the workspace agents and Hermes dial. The **Host**\ngroup carries the matching card: its badge probes `http://searxng:8181/healthz`, which\nanswers from the instance's own state without querying an upstream engine, and its three\nfields come from `GET /config` on the same port - the release tag, and the two switches\nthat decide whether the box is a public instance (`limiter`, `public_instance`). The\ndashboard never asks the instance for result rows: a polled `/search` would park engines\non the rate-limit and CAPTCHA walls, which is why that probe is\n`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check`'s job, run on demand. See\ntroubleshooting.md."],[537,"that branch never runs and the file keeps every value it has, and\n`bazelisk run //scripts:update-stack -- --dry-run` plans the fetch and writes nothing.\noperate/secrets.md owns the vault, its inventory and its verbs."],[1707,"That check is a panel of three skeptics dispatched in one turn - the requirements angle,\nthe proof angle that re-runs the producers' own `done:` commands, and the adversary angle -\neach riding `agent-review`, each owning no file, and each allowed up to three read-only\nmappers of its own under `max_depth` 2. Three spawners with three mappers each is twelve of\nthe fifteen streams the fleet owns, which fits beside a parent still holding its own\nconversation, and the panel is cheap precisely because the writers have all landed by the\ntime it runs: the fleet is then idle, and both the skeptics and the mappers they spawn are\nunpinned traffic that `least-busy` can place on all three boxes at once."],[748,"That container is the *laptop and CI* environment: it mounts the host docker socket and\nassumes a workstation daemon, neither of which belongs inside a workspace's nested dev\ncontainer."],[2203,"That creates `infisical`, `infisical-db` and `infisical-redis`, on the image tags the\n`image:` lines in `docker-compose.yml` pin (`infisical/infisical:v0.167.10` as shipped). The\nvault answers on `http://127.0.0.1:8182` (`INFISICAL_PORT`) on the stack host, and on the\npublished LAN face. Expect the two alpine containers to read `running` rather than `healthy`:\nneither declares a healthcheck, because the only consumer waits on `service_started`. With\nthe flag already on, this first `up` also tries the env-file refresh and gets nothing: there\nis no `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID` yet, so the seam prints `note: the pull was refused before it\nran; <file> keeps the values it has` and the runner follows with its `warning:` line. That\npair is noise until step 5 writes the identifiers - it is not a failed bring-up."],[523,"That env comments always refresh from the twin, even when values stay"],[319,"That exposes the dev container as a sub-agent with its own terminal and apps, and the\nin-browser editor attaches to *that* sub-agent, so the editor runs inside the dev\ncontainer where the cloned repo's toolchain lives."],[2717,"That first block prints two numbers now: the fleet's own ceiling as `# fleet-width:`,\nabove this session's cap as `max_concurrent`\n(`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:427-428`, devcontainer twin\n`templates/devcontainer/grok-config.toml.tftpl:428-429`). `15` over `15` is this entry -\nthe number is right and the placement is not. `15` over `4` is a different fault, where\nthe width itself was set, and\nthe rendered-width entry\nis the one that answers it."],[475,"That folder is what code-server, the Web Terminal button and the Grok Build session\nopen on. Inside a dev container, code-server sees that same folder under its mount\ntarget, `/workspaces/<repo-name>`."],[221,"That form addresses the stack through `docker compose` instead, so it names the\nproject with `-p`. From the checkout root the folder name would have said the same thing."],[2063,"That gate is what makes the monitoring payloads ordinary source: it parses the three rule\nfiles, `alertmanager.yml`, `blackbox.yml` and the Grafana alerting policy, and parses every\ndashboard JSON through `scripts/lib/parse_json.py` (`scripts/validate.sh:104`). A panel\nthat is not well-formed JSON, a dashboard whose `title` is blank, and the generated Spark\nfile-SD document are gate failures now rather than an empty tile someone notices in the\nbrowser."],[1809,"That is `check`, the default verb, and it writes nothing. It answers one\nquestion - if this box rebooted right now, could you decrypt it from a laptop -\nand closes with either `ok: this box can be unlocked from a laptop after a\nreboot` or `error: do not reboot yet - N blocker(s)`."],[1329,"That is still the shape on the two fast-compaction lanes, and it is why the remedy is\nserver-side rather than a client setting: a session on `agent-xhigh-fast` or\n`agent-swarm-fast` sends the same in-session summary call on its session model, and\n`litellm/compaction_router.py` moves that one request onto the Arc group\n`compaction-fast` before the proxy picks a deployment. Nothing in `~/.grok/config.toml`\nasks for it, every other call of that session still rides the Spark lane, and a session on\nany other alias compacts as it always has. The limit is the Arc's own 32768-token window:\nabove `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` (24000 estimated tokens) the summary goes to\n`agent-low` instead, which keeps the 262144-token window and drops the baked `xhigh`,\nbecause a slow summary beats one Ollama silently clamped. The key cannot raise that limit:\nthe target group's declared `max_input_tokens` caps it and logs one line when it does."],[302,"That is the *AI workspace + DinD* shape, always on, plus a repository that brings its own\ntoolchain."],[1705,"That is the arithmetic the lane exists for: 3 boxes x `PARALLEL` 5 full-length streams is\n15 concurrent streams, and a pinned workspace only ever reaches the 5 of the one box its\nkey resolves to."],[738,"That is the host ingress: it `docker run`s `evo-t1-dev:latest` as this process's uid:gid\n(`$(id -u):$(id -g)`), with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)` so Bazel has a writable home\nand a name for a uid the image passwd file does not list, and runs the same suites CI\nruns. A macOS laptop gets the Linux result without a Linux box. Build the tag first with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` if the daemon does not have it."],[173,"That is the server's own plain listener (`CODER_HTTP_ADDRESS` binds `0.0.0.0:3000`\ninside the container), reached without crossing the reverse proxy, Authelia, or the WAN."],[1388,"That is the whole integration - see hermes.md for the doors Hermes\nopens, and the `coding-agents` skill for the order the assistant works them in."],[1417,"That is the whole point of the service: stack ops by conversation. \"Recreate the dev\nstack and tell me which container restarts\", \"why is the coder agent offline\", \"run the\ngate and fix what it reports\" - each one runs with a plan first and an approval card\nbefore the mutating command."],[1272,"That is what makes \"survives workspace recreate\" true rather than a hope: an edit is on the\nvolume within roughly one interval, so a recreate no longer waits on someone restarting the\nworkspace to pick up what was typed since the last boot. What is not promised is the tail"],[417,"That is why the `.git` suffix is stripped with `sed` rather than the shorter\n`${leaf%.git}`: a braced expansion would add an interpolation `main.tf` does not pass."],[2154,"That last sentence is the design, so it is worth stating exactly. Compose interpolates\n`${VAR:-default}` against the `.env` beside `docker-compose.yml`, and nothing in the\ncompose layer ever opens a connection to a secrets service. An unreachable vault must not\nbe able to stop `up`, so the integration does not replace the dotenv chain - it feeds it.\n`pull` writes the managed keys into `.env` and compose reads that file exactly as it\nalways has. The vault is an editor for `.env`, not a dependency of the project. Read that\nas the whole delivery path: a credential change reaches a service through the env file and\na recreate, never through a live call to the vault. The one place the deploy path dials the\nvault at all is the runners' own refresh before compose interpolates, and that call is\nbest-effort - one `warning:` and the bring-up continues on whatever the file already held."],[569,"That pipes a PAT straight into that container's `gh` credential store - no `.env`\nedit, no template push - and `gh auth logout` inside the container, or a rebuild,\nremoves it. The repositories a token can reach are whatever that GitHub app\ninstallation was granted."],[1918,"That preset/CLI pair is the whole hand-off. It is where this repo hands a new project over, and the\nonly coupling between the two repositories: this repo names the scaffold it clones, the scaffold\nnever names this one."],[2746,"That probe only asks whether the daemon answers `docker version`. It says nothing\nabout the workspace that used to reach it."],[375,"That render is also what puts the fleet's own ceiling inside the file, as\n`# fleet-width: 15` directly above `max_concurrent` in `[subagents]`\n(`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:427`, fed from `var.grok_swarm_width` at\n`templates/workspace/main.tf:558`), so an explicit `grok_subagent_max_concurrent` never\nerases the number the fleet was sized at. Stage 6 reads the pair the moment the render\nlands: right after `boot_ok \"config.toml re-materialized with the stack markers\"`\n(`templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl:258`) it compares them and, when the width is the\nnarrower of the two, prints one `note:` line naming the gap through `boot_note`\n(`templates/common/boot-log.lib.sh:51`, the verb that carries no counter), pointing at\nthe rendered width is below fleet-width.\n`bazelisk run //scripts:subagent-check` is the same comparison for a shell, and the same\ntwo numbers answer it: a profile whose marker the operator removed gets neither the line\nnor the note, because both are products of this render."],[1739,"That request routes elsewhere, to whichever box `least-busy` picks."],[2424,"That run was made from a Coder workspace, which is why the fleet fell back to the\nplaceholder trio. From the host shell, with the `SPARKn_HOST` keys in the checkout's\n`.env`, the same command prints three real boxes and no host warning, and adds the\n`docker run` lines the plan promises (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:412-425`)."],[859,"That target resolves to 198 source files through 25 declarations: the gap is the `filegroup` and\n`scripts/lib` expansion under each label. `labels(\"data\", ...)` is the narrower question (\"what does\nthe BUILD file say\"), `files` is the wider one (\"what is in the sandbox\"), and the two disagreeing is\nusually the bug."],[526,"That the vault rewrites the managed keys before a bring-up interpolates them, and\nnothing else in the file"],[846,"That warning is the honest version of \"nothing depends on this\", and it is the reason to keep\nstderr with a query. Go to the gate that owns the map instead of guessing its label:"],[2866,"That writes the stack's env file, not the workspace's. For the workspace's own copy, set\n`INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START=true` in `.env`, re-push, and restart - every later start then\nfetches, and the note stops printing:"],[1915,"The **`New project (scaffold ready)` preset** in both templates is a form shortcut. It pins the\ngolden image, turns on the DinD sidecar in the workspace template, and passes this stack as\n`additional_repos`, which is what puts `scripts/new-repo.sh` inside a fresh workspace beside the\nproject being started. The scaffold does not ride along: the CLI fetches it from GitHub when it\nruns. It is a preset, so it only sets parameter values."],[2626,"The **agent bar** keeps `:4000` off its own path: the workspace page's `litellm` tile\nis `coder_metadata` text (`value = var.litellm_url`), a read-out rather than a\n`coder_app`, so `litellm-gmktecbeast.` is never consulted here. Health-check the\nproxy with `curl http://host.docker.internal:4000/health/liveliness` on the stack\nhost. The app lane under `A workspace app link answers Bad Gateway` below is the\n**VS Code Web** editor link and stays on 13337 inside the workspace."],[1423,"The **Chat tab** is the primary UX. It embeds the Hermes TUI - a PTY over WebSocket\non `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` - so the terminal agent and the browser are the same\nprocess, and approval prompts render as cards in the chat rather than vanishing into\na detached tty. Hermes sends a keepalive every 20 seconds. Traefik and Authelia have\nto pass the `Upgrade` hop. A close code **4403** is the Host / Origin / peer guard.\nCompose sets `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the seed file\ndoes not carry `public_url`). The seeded `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is\n`[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]`, which is what makes Hermes honour `X-Forwarded-Proto` from the\nrouter (the Secure cookie and `wss`). `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is environment\nand applies when the container is recreated. An existing `hermes-data` volume does\nnot pick up `trusted_proxies` until a reseed or `hermes config set` - the volume\nseed is copy-once. The reseed command is below."],[1020,"The **Host** group carries one more card, for the secrets vault: its badge probes\n`http://infisical:8080/` - the container port, not the published `INFISICAL_PORT`,\nbecause this dashboard reaches every service over compose DNS - and `/` is the path the\npinned image's own healthcheck calls live. Its `href` rides\n`{{HOMEPAGE_VAR_INFISICAL_URL}}`, which bootstrap fills from `STACK_INFISICAL_URL` when\nthe public names are set. The bundle is one flag, `INFISICAL_ENABLED`, off as shipped:\nwith it off compose starts none of the three containers and the badge reads down with\nthem, so run `bazelisk run //scripts:infisical -- status` before chasing the card."],[2625,"The **model lane**: a process inside the workspace dials\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1` directly. `host.docker.internal` is the docker\nbridge gateway (`172.17.0.1`), compose publishes the proxy's container port 4000 on\nhost port 4000, and nothing between the two authenticates. `LITELLM_BASE_URL` and the\ncoding CLIs use this lane."],[1022,"The **Observability** group is the one group that reports on services publishing no port.\nIts five cards - Grafana, Prometheus, Loki, Tempo, Mimir - carry a `siteMonitor` on\ncompose DNS (`http://grafana:3000/api/health`, `http://prometheus:9090/-/healthy`,\n`http://loki:3100/ready`, `http://tempo:3200/ready`, `http://mimir:8080/ready`), which is\nwhat proves the bundle is wired up: four of those six containers ship no healthcheck at\nall (distroless image, no shell), so the badge is the only automatic signal they have. The\nbundle's four other services - `node_exporter`, `cadvisor`, `blackbox_exporter` and\n`alertmanager` - carry no card and publish nothing beyond the stack host's loopback, so\ntheir liveness is read there, not here.\nThe hrefs go through `HOMEPAGE_VAR_GRAFANA_URL` and `HOMEPAGE_VAR_PROMETHEUS_URL`, which\ndefault to the LAN URLs built from `STACK_LAN_HOST`, `GRAFANA_PORT` and\n`PROMETHEUS_PORT`, and follow `STACK_GRAFANA_URL` once bootstrap composes the public name"],[1034,"the **Observability** group, card by card: five cards, one per bundle service, each\nprobing its own health path over compose DNS - `http://grafana:3000/api/health`,\n`http://prometheus:9090/-/healthy`, `http://loki:3100/ready`,\n`http://tempo:3200/ready`, `http://mimir:8080/ready`. Those four distroless containers\nship no in-container healthcheck, so this probe is the only liveness read the stack has\nfor them, and a badge pointed anywhere else is caught here"],[2731,"the **template**, from a raw `coder templates push --var grok_subagent_max_concurrent=4`.\n`//scripts:push-template` passes no fan-out `--var` of its own - its only `grok_*` one\nis `grok_profile_mode` (`scripts/push-template.sh:528`) - so a template-level value is\nsomebody's raw push, the same shape as the lane rollback above;"],[2645,"The **VS Code Web** button is the one app this stack still proxies: its URL is\n`/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/code-server` on the coder hostname, because\n`coder_app.code_server` sets `subdomain = false`. So a failing app link is an app-lane\nfailure, and a 200 from `litellm-gmktecbeast.` is not evidence about it."],[2732,"the **workspace**, from `coder start <workspace> --parameter ...`, from\n`coder update <workspace> --parameter ...`, or from the Variables tab in the\ndashboard. (`coder start` takes `--parameter name=value`; `--var` is a\n`coder templates push` flag and that pair has no `--var` to give it - on\nCoder v2.36.6 `coder start --help` lists `--parameter`, `--parameter-default`,\n`--rich-parameter-file`, `--use-parameter-defaults` and `--no-wait`, and no\n`--var`.) A workspace value survives every template push and is re-applied on every\nstart, which is how a number typed in for one experiment months ago still caps a\nfan-out today."],[328,"The `--` ends Bazel's own flags, so a URL that begins with a dash still reaches the\nscript. Every fence below is one complete command."],[2429,"The `--` separator is not decoration. Measured:"],[442,"The `[ -f ... ]` test for that file lives **inside** the appended `.bashrc` line rather\nthan around it, because the startup script and the fetch are two `coder_script`s with no\npromised order (`coder_script_order` needs Coder >= v2.39, see\nThe one-argument ceiling): a first boot whose startup script\nwins the race still ends up with a working hook, and the leading `grep -qs` keeps a later\nstart from appending the block twice (`templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl:64-80`)."],[873,"The `//` half is the honest measure of what the gates can see, and it moves as files are added, so\ntreat any absolute total in a document as a snapshot rather than a fact to assert."],[958,"the `# @function` block above the definition, with Globals/Arguments/Outputs/Returns."],[993,"The `${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` default is not decoration: these scripts run\nunder `set -euo pipefail`, and a bare `${REPO_ROOT?}` aborts with `REPO_ROOT: parameter not\nset` the moment a sandbox or CI runner leaves the variable unset."],[2894,"The `0.0.0.0` bind refusal above is a different failure: empty basic-auth env,\nnot this public path. Full notes: hermes.md."],[2577,"The `agent` -> `coder` fallback keeps chat answering while a Spark is down, which is\nexactly when tools disappear."],[1673,"The `agent` lane deliberately carries the `openai/` provider prefix. `ollama_chat/`\nwould route those boxes through the Ollama-specific code path, and `api_base` has to\ninclude `/v1`."],[583,"The `agent` model group is three deployments behind one LiteLLM alias, one per\nSpark box, and these are the keys that fill it:"],[1664,"The `agent` row is ONE model group with three deployments, one per Spark box,\nall equal."],[811,"The `bazel-map` CLI, its verbs, and the `--json` split that feeds `bazelisk test`"],[42,"The `ci` workflow (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs for `push` to `main` and for a\n`pull_request` whose **base** is `main` or one of the sanctioned work-branch prefixes\n(`feat/**`, `fix/**`, `refactor/**`, `docs/**`, `test/**`, `chore/**`, `ci/**`).\n`pull_request.branches` filters on the base rather than the head, so naming only\n`main` left a stacked pull request - a dependent branch that names its parent as its\nbase, the shape the agent guidance in `templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` tells\nyou to open - with no gate at all: it collected\njust the docs deploy its branch push earned from `deploy-docs`. Gate a stack the same\nway as any other pull request. The other shipped workflow, `deploy-docs`, also runs\non a `push` to any branch (`branches: [\"**\"]`) whose diff touches its `paths:` list -\n`docs/**`, `docs-site/**`, the two `scripts/ci/` helpers it publishes through\n(`publish-pages-tree.sh`, `docs-alias.sh`), and its own workflow file - and by\n`workflow_dispatch`."],[2148,"The `CODER_PG_PASSWORD` rotation path, the volume-init trap under it, and the probe defect that blocks the verb today"],[1324,"The `compaction` pin above belongs to Coder's chat agent and to nothing else. It is one\nof the four auxiliary lanes the seeder writes into Coder's own model-config store, so it\ndecides which alias serves *that* agent's summariser and title call. It never reaches a\nGrok Build CLI: the CLI reads no Coder table, and its compaction summary is an\n**in-session call on the session model** - the `[models] default` alias, `agent` here -\nwhich is why the summary inherits that lane's output ceiling (measured on the wire at\n`max_tokens: 32768`) instead of a cheap helper lane's. `[models] session_summary` is not\nthat call either; the key serves titles and the session recap."],[1294,"The `context7` server (library and API docs) also renders by default, and is the\nonly cached one. `context7_mcp` (default true) is its gate, and the block it\nrenders runs `mcp-cache-proxy` in front of the official `context7-mcp` - two packages\npinned in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image, so parking\nor restoring it is a flag plus a template push, while changing the packages\nthemselves takes an image rebuild. The proxy keeps its SQLite cache under\n`~/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/`, which sits on this workspace's own\nhome volume, so each workspace warms its own cache, and the cache TTL is 12 h: the first lookup of a\nlibrary reaches the Context7 API and needs network, while an identical lookup inside\nthe TTL is answered locally. `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is optional and the lane does not\ndepend on it: an empty value sends no `Authorization` header at all, so the server\nhandshakes and answers at the public rate limit, with private libraries the only\nthing out of reach. A key in `.env` plus a re-push fills the env. What *parks* the\nlane on purpose is `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed by `push-template.sh`) or\n`--var context7_mcp=false` on a template push; a `[mcp_servers.context7]` block\nmissing from a render that got neither of those is a defect, not a park. The lookup\norder is fixed - `resolve-library-id` first, then `query-docs` on the id it returns"],[459,"The `devcontainer` editor cannot set `agent_id` with\n`local.use_dc ? coder_devcontainer.repo[0].subagent_id : coder_agent.main.id`.\n`coder_devcontainer.repo` is also gated on `start_count`, so the stop /\ntemplate-import ephemeral plan has an empty tuple even with the toggle on\n(the default), and that ternary then fails with `Invalid index` / `empty\ntuple`. `local.code_server_agent_id` is\n`concat(coder_devcontainer.repo[*].subagent_id, [coder_agent.main.id])[0]`:\nthe sub-agent when that resource exists, the parent agent on the stopped\npage. The start-gated `coder_script.code_server` uses the same local."],[456,"The `devcontainer` fork has no persistent prefix to reuse - both toggle modes rebuild\nthe container that hosts the editor - so it installs the pinned release fresh under\n`/tmp/code-server` on every start, and bakes the settings seed as base64 into `settings_b64` (empty when the\ntoggle is off, so the shell skips the merge). The DinD wait lives in\n`templates/devcontainer/dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`."],[66,"The `devcontainer` template"],[988,"The `docker` stub passes `exec`/`compose exec` through by default (`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=1`),\nso a binary the script execs inside a container lands on its own stub; set\n`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=0` with `STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RC`/`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_OUT` for the\n\"the daemon answered, and it said No\" case."],[2434,"The `file_sd` document, to stdout, writing nothing. With the flag off, verified output:"],[2554,"The `first boot? run bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap to create it` hint alongside it\nmeans the checkout has no `.env` at all: that is the bootstrap path. `DASHBOARD_ENV_FILE`\npoints the rotation at another file and exists for test harnesses; there is deliberately\nno flag for it, because a flag that names a file this CLI overwrites is one mistype away."],[496,"The `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` pair - reached through the helper, because\n`gh auth git-credential` echoes either name whenever one is set. A helper that does not\nclear the pair therefore answers with the injected copy and never asks the store: that\nis the shadow, and it is why a hand-edited helper or a hand-rolled askpass script is the\nwrong instrument (see\nthe push-denied entry)."],[1293,"The `github` server (`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`) renders by default even\nthough it needs a token and outbound network: with GitHub tools on, the agent can\ndrive the repository it is working in, and the cheaper failure is the block that\nrenders with no credential and pays one failed handshake per session. `github_mcp`\n(default true) is the gate; `$GH_TOKEN` comes from Coder External Auth, never from a\nPAT."],[2001,"The `grafana` service carries two settings that live in its `environment:` block\n(`docker-compose.yml:1034-1035`) rather than in any file above, each one there to quiet a\nGrafana 13.3.1 defect this box pays for as log noise.\n`GF_LOG_FILTERS: search-manifest-watcher:error` drops that one logger to `error` and\nleaves every other logger at its default. The watcher warns twice each poll, and the\npolls in the log this branch was cut from were 41 to 59 s apart -\n`source listed zero manifests`, then `zero manifests, keeping previous set` - whenever no\napp publishes a search manifest, which is every OSS install with nothing provisioned into\nthe app platform; upstream calls that a logging defect (grafana/grafana#137058) and\nconfirms search keeps working, because retrying an empty source is intended and only the\nlevel is wrong. The filter is a workaround for that defect, not a preference: it comes\nout when the pin moves to a release carrying the fix, and the pin today is\n`grafana/grafana:13.3.1` (`docker-compose.yml:976`).\n`GF_UNIFIED_STORAGE_OVERRIDES_PATH: /etc/grafana/stack-overrides.yml`\npoints the unified-storage resource server at the file the table names: without it the\nquota lookup the browse view fires on every dashboard page answers 501, since that\nserver's quota service is nil with nothing pointed at it (grafana/grafana#136271), and the\nbox prints one error line per page load while the browser console fills with 501s; with it\nthe same call answers 200. Both halves were measured on `grafana/grafana:13.3.1` as two\ncontainers differing only in that variable: the one without the file answered 501 for both\n`dashboard.grafana.app/dashboards` and `folder.grafana.app/folders` and the one with it\nanswered 200 for both, with `/api/health` 200 either way. On the filter side, over one\n300-second window the unfiltered container wrote 16 `search-manifest-watcher` lines, all\n`level=warn`, and the filtered one wrote none from that logger - the only line in it that\nstill names it is Grafana's own `level=info` record that the setting came from the\nenvironment. The overrides file itself says `overrides: {}`, which is\nGrafana's own defaults - it is the file's presence that clears the 501, not any limit set\nin it. What the quota call then reports is that default, `{\"usage\":0,\"limit\":1000}` for\ndashboards and for folders on 13.3.1; four plausible cap shapes left the number where it\nwas, and a scalar or an unparseable document under `overrides:` stops the container at\nboot, so the file's header asks for a limit to be proven on the pinned image before it is\nwritten down."],[274,"The `grok_default_model` aliases are `agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`,\n`agent-xhigh`, `agent-xhigh-fast`, `agent-swarm`, `agent-swarm-fast`, `coder`,\n`coder-fast` and `chat`, plus a **Custom** entry when the variable names another one. The\ntwo `-fast` entries are the Spark lane each is named after, unchanged, with one call\nmoved: the proxy re-serves that session's conversation summary from the Arc group\n`compaction-fast`, which is the whole difference, because the CLI has no compaction-model\nkey of its own."],[2179,"The `infisical` container takes `ENCRYPTION_KEY`, `AUTH_SECRET`, `SITE_URL`,\n`DB_CONNECTION_URI`, `REDIS_URL`, `HOST=0.0.0.0` and `PORT=8080` from the environment.\n`DB_CONNECTION_URI` and the db service's `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` both interpolate\n`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD`, which is the only thing keeping the role and its pointer in step\nacross a rotation. The public name, when you turn one on, is `STACK_INFISICAL_NAME`\n(default label `infisical`) composed by `scripts/lib/domain.sh` like every other name;\nthe router sends that Host to `http://infisical:8080`. See\npublic-access.md."],[869,"The `kind` filter is what makes an `rdeps` usable. A bare `rdeps(//..., \"litellm/config.yaml\")`\nincludes `//:test-fast`, which reaches every file and therefore tells you nothing about this one:"],[1860,"The `New project` workspace preset and the optional Coder Task"],[2563,"The `ollama` service is IPEX-LLM on the Arc 140T, not Ollama from a registry."],[1289,"The `searxng` MCP stdio server is what answers instead, and it is the search lane\nrather than a fallback: both templates render that block gated on `search_mcp`,\nwhich defaults to true. It dials `$SEARXNG_URL` (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:8181`), the same key `main.tf` puts in the agent\nenvironment. It carries nine tools - `search`, `image_search`, `news_search`,\n`video_search`, `music_search`, `paper_search`, `fetch_content`, `autocomplete`,\n`list_engines` - and an agent's two workhorses are the first and the seventh:\n`search` for anything that is not a library question, `fetch_content` for a URL\nalready in hand. Measure the self-hosted lane with:"],[50,"The `templates/common/` payloads"],[1141,"The `tmux` profile gives each terminal of a folder its own pane. The base session\nname is unchanged: `vscode-<folder>-<hash>`. A new terminal walks into one of\nthat folder's sessions only while no client is attached to it, oldest first -\nthat is the closed-tab rejoin, so a tab you closed or reloaded leaves its\nsession unattached and the next terminal finds it with its history intact, and\n`ssh` + `tmux attach -t <session>` lands there too. A terminal opened when\nevery session of the folder already has a client takes the next numbered\nsibling instead: `vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`, `-3`, ... Sessions persist\nunattached until the container restarts, so a reattached terminal still\nreaches the output a closed tab produced. `tmux ls` lists the names and\n`tmux list-clients` says who is looking at each. Every target the helper\ngives tmux carries the `=` exact-match prefix (a bare name is answered by any\nsession starting with it, so a closed `foo` could be answered by a live\n`foo-2` and re-create the mirror), and a reopen whose session vanished\nmid-attach raises a fresh session rather than exiting along with the tab.\nTwo tmux behaviors shape that path: a session raised by a tab's own\nforeground `new-session` has its first pane closed when that client exits\n(superuser#575909),\nwhich is why the helper creates detached and attaches separately; and with\nthe default `exit-empty` the server itself leaves when its last session does\n(tmux#2081), which is why a\nreopen probes the server before raising anything - a tab whose server is gone\nhas ended normally, and re-raising there would boot a server for a terminal\nnobody is looking at (tmux has no server probe: `has-session` answers the same\nfor a missing session and a missing server, which is why upstream declined to\nadd `check-server`, tmux#2523).\nNo upstream issue accounts for the session loss: `destroy-unattached` is off,\nso a detached session is meant to survive, and tmux gives a session no grace\nperiod after it loses its last client\n(tmux#5821 is an open request for\none)."],[455,"The `workspace` fork installs code-server **only when the pinned release is\nmissing** from `/home/coder/.local/share/code-server`, and starts it with\n`--auth none --port 13337 --app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`,\npassing no settings payload. The guard tests\n`/home/coder/.local/share/code-server/lib/code-server-<version>/bin/code-server`,\nthe directory the installer unpacks, because `bin/code-server` beside it is a\nsymlink to whatever release was installed last: a guard on that symlink would keep\na warm volume running its first release after a bump. A bump therefore re-downloads\non the next start and leaves the previous release under `lib/`; removing that old\ndirectory by hand is what reclaims the space. Before that launch it `mkdir -p`s the\nfolder and, when the folder is not `/home/coder`, waits up to 60 s for\n`<folder>/.git` so a first-start\nclone and the editor no longer race. That wait loop increments with\n`waited=$((waited + 1))` (one dollar) inside the tftpl: `templatefile()` interpolates\n`${…}` and leaves `$((` alone. `$$((` would expand `$$` to the PID. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the agent would\nchdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail the agent. The\neditor's settings are a per-start merge: the boot\n(`templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl`) writes `~/.local/share/code-server/User/settings.json`\nas a real file from this workspace's render, then layers the per-owner\n`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` seed (`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`, where an\noperator's API keys live) over it, then re-asserts `workbench.colorTheme` from the\nrender last so the seed cannot veto this workspace's own theme."],[1755,"The `x-litellm-model-id` response header names the deployment that answered."],[2584,"The 195k needle. `//scripts:spark-verify` runs the recipe's `needle.py --length 195000`\nas the window proof, and a 195k prompt the server has never seen is the largest single\neviction on the box. It now skips itself while `/health` reports `requests_running`\nabove zero, so it does not land on a box that is serving:"],[2144,"The adoption sequence, one command per step, with the container each step touches"],[1604,"The Agent backend card"],[155,"The agent runs `coder_agent.startup_script`, and every `coder_script.script`, as\n`bash -c <body>`. The whole body therefore travels as **one** argument to `execve(2)`,\nand Linux caps a single argument at `MAX_ARG_STRLEN`, 32 pages = 131072 bytes. Measured\non this host: an argument of 131071 bytes execs and one of 131072 fails with `E2BIG`.\nPast the wall the agent logs `fork/exec /bin/bash: argument list too long` with\n`exit_code=255` a few milliseconds in, the home directory never seeds, and the workspace\nbanner reads \"A startup script has failed\". Coder puts no limit there and the plan\nreports no error, so the wall only announces itself on a live recreate."],[2103,"The alerts the bundle ships, and the two pages that show them"],[1587,"The alias table"],[1615,"The alias table, and which backend each alias resolves to"],[2845,"The answer for every `gh` call, for as long as that workspace lives:"],[1516,"The API server is the widest door here and ships closed. It carries the same\ntoolset as the dashboard, terminal included, and its only gate is the bearer key,\nso it is off in compose and its host mapping binds loopback until an operator\nnames both. Treat `HERMES_API_SERVER_KEY` as a root credential: rotate it by\nediting `.env` and recreating the container, and give a phone a Shortcut that\nholds it rather than a URL that carries it."],[1472,"The Arc's own weights also ship as named rows beside that catalogue - `arc-chat`,\n`arc-coder` and `arc-coder-fast` - each pointing at the same LiteLLM door. The\npicker does not show them beside it: Hermes folds rows that share an endpoint,\ncredential and wire protocol into one group, so a single `litellm` entry lists\nevery alias those rows declare, and the Arc rows stay reachable as `--provider`\nroutes and as a typed `/model <alias>`. Each row's `default_model` is its weight's\none declaration, and that declaration is what makes `/model chat` routable: two\nrows declaring the same id makes `switch_model` refuse the name outright, so\n`chat`, `coder` and `coder-fast` are declared only by their Arc rows. These are the\nroute verbs, and `arc-chat` is the row to route through when a session wants the\nArc's tool-capable model:"],[2880,"The assistant also opens in your own terminal with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-shell`. When you cannot tell whether the agent,\nits volume, or the model lane is the broken part, start with the readiness\nreport — it launches nothing and names each blocker (daemon, container state,\nseeded volume, model key set or still a placeholder, LiteLLM\n`/health/liveliness`):"],[1477,"The assistant can look things up, and the query leaves through your own SearXNG."],[1564,"The assistant on the host"],[667,"The background warm-up, and the one knob that turns it off"],[1708,"The baked level is the only thing that separates them. An explore child is a lookup, so it\ntakes `low` and answers fast; a review child is the check that a landed claim is actually\ntrue, so it takes the `xhigh` `agent` itself carries. Each names itself with an empty flag\nlist beside the swarm lane's (`litellm/config.yaml:721-729`) and declares its own six-key\n`model_group_retry_policy` block, because a group inherits nothing from its siblings."],[1208,"The bare ids after the summary are the wave to spawn - `--ready` prints the tasks whose\n`needs:` are all satisfied, capped at `slots`, and the parent takes that set rather than\nthe plan's order and spawns the whole wave in one turn. The refusals are the collisions a\nfan-out otherwise discovers at wave 3; a plan with two ready tasks on the same file and a\nread-only role handed that file prints:"],[743,"The Bazel job no longer installs the pins onto the runner: the image already has them at\nthe pinned versions."],[494,"The blank first value is git's reset marker: it drops any helper inherited from a\nlower-precedence config file, and git takes the **last** value it finds for that host.\nThe startup seed rewrites both keys every start (idempotent), and `github-auth fix`\ninstalls the same form, leaving a helper that already ignores the pair alone. This\nhelper is the slot that wins, and the clearing is the subject of the paragraph below."],[397,"The blind spot the design accepts is a holder this process cannot see in `/proc` at all: a\nsibling container's vim, which the PID namespace hides, or a process whose `/proc/<pid>/fd`\nis unreadable from here. Its buffer and the archived bytes both survive, and the move is\nreversible for a month; what goes missing is the E325 prompt that would have warned the\nnext person to open the file. That window is narrow, it sits on a file every start\noverwrites anyway, and it is why the sweep stops at one name here."],[2164,"The body gates itself twice before it dials anything. First the **six configured names**:\n`INFISICAL_DOMAIN`, `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID`, `INFISICAL_MACHINE_IDENTITY_ID`,\n`INFISICAL_ENVIRONMENT`, `INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_ID` and\n`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET`. Any one of them empty prints which names are missing and\nwhere they come from, and exits 0. Then `INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START`, whose off path prints that the\nboot \"keeps the credentials it already has\" and also exits 0. Every other failure path - no\n`infisical` binary in the image, no `HOME`, a refused Universal Auth login, an empty export, a\nrefused publish - exits 0 as well. The golden image bakes the pinned CLI (`images/dev/Dockerfile`,\n`INFISICAL_CLI_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env`), so the missing-binary path is what an\nimage older than that pin hits. A vault outage has never once stopped a workspace."],[437,"The body runs in this order, and every step that cannot proceed prints what it needs and\nexits 0: no `infisical` binary in the image (`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:8` - the golden image bakes the pinned release, so that is an image older than `INFISICAL_CLI_VERSION`),\nthe six-name not-configured guard (`:14-20`), the `INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START` gate\n(`:27-30`), no `HOME` to write into (`:32-36`), the Universal Auth login as the machine\nidentity (`:61-65`), the dotenv export into a `mktemp` dump (`:45`, `:67`), and the publish\n(`:245`). Both temp files die in the `EXIT` trap whatever happens between the `mktemp` and\nthe `mv` (`:45-52`)."],[389,"The boot clears them, from `grok_sweep_config_swaps` in\n`templates/common/agents-seed.sh.tftpl`. It runs there rather than in `startup.sh.tftpl`\nbecause the startup argument sits under a byte gate that holds the composed body to 110592\nbytes (12288 of room under the 122880 budget this repo sets for itself, which is itself\n8 KiB under the kernel's measured 131072 one-argument ceiling; see\nThe one-argument ceiling), and the `devcontainer` twin already\ncomposes 110242 of those, leaving 350 bytes for everything else. It also runs there because\nthat body is already where a stack-owned file under `~/.grok` is refreshed against its\nmarker. It does not lean on running after\nthe `startup_script`: a `coder_script` runs in no promised order with it\n(`templates/workspace/coder.tf:272`), so the argument is the marker itself. A\n`config.toml` that still carries the marker is one every start replaces from\n`grok-config.toml.tftpl`, whichever of the two runs first, so what an orphaned swap holds\nis doomed either way."],[1822,"The boot continues on its own once the mapping appears - no second reboot. If\nthe SSH connection is refused, the box is still in the initramfs or before it:\nthe boot does not proceed past an unmapped root, so waiting is safe and the\nconsole (out-of-band console, IPMI, or the provider's serial console) still\nshows where it stopped."],[82,"The boot log each body prints"],[412,"The bound every body is written against: one agent's whole log stream - `startup_script`\nand every `coder_script` on it together - is 1 MiB. It is a Postgres check,\n`CONSTRAINT max_logs_length CHECK ((logs_length <= 1048576))` on `workspace_agents`\n(dump.sql, v2.36.6),\nand `workspace_agents` is the only table in that schema that carries agent logs at all, so\na script's lines draw on the same budget as the startup script's. The agent's uploader\nholds the same number as a constant\n(`codersdk/agentsdk/logs.go`,\n`maxBytesQueued = 1048576`) and counts it across every log source at once, returning before\nit appends when the queue is full: the lines you lose are the NEWEST ones, and once the\nserver answers `LogLimitExceeded: true` the uploader drops everything still queued. Hence\nthe about-40-lines-per-body rule, and hence a command with a stream of its own is aimed at\na file rather than at the log - the editor launch redirects to `/tmp/code-server.log`\n(`templates/workspace/code-server.sh.tftpl:67`) while the narration says the same thing in\none `boot_phase` line."],[1538,"The box deliberately runs **two isolated copies** of the stack: a stable one you work in\nand a dev one you break. Two stacks on one box\nin the README is the short version; docs/config-updates.md covers\nwhat a `git pull` leaves behind."],[1648,"The box-side reclaim switch. The recipe measured the same refusal going from\n\"2 of 5 streams refused\" to \"5 of 5 admitted\" once patch 0029 is live with\n`TF_FLASHNEXT_ADMIT_RECLAIM=1`. `//scripts:spark-configure --apply` owns the per-host\noverride file those settings belong in, and `//scripts:spark-verify` already fails a\nbox whose `streams.max` drifts from the fleet's `PARALLEL`. Asserting the\nadmit-reclaim setting itself is still open work: neither script names that key today."],[27,"The branch and the commits are in place while the work happens, and the push waits. This is the\norder the agent seed teaches and the order a human contributor gets the most out of:"],[2962,"The branch-cleanup cookbook: classify, port, preserve, list for deletion"],[2014,"The bundle itself:"],[1985,"The bundle's payloads are one directory of their own, bind-mounted read-only, so\n`git diff` is the record of every monitoring change and nothing is edited in the running\ncontainer:"],[654,"The cache seam"],[666,"The cache seam: the mounted volumes, the `~/.bazelrc` block, and when to drop them"],[565,"The callback URL on the GitHub side is\n`<CODER_ACCESS_URL>/external-auth/<id>/callback` - `github` for the injected\nApp, `primary-github` for the sample app above."],[1754,"The card and its per-box widgets read `AGENT_MODEL_URL` and `SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` from\n`.env`, and compose builds both from the box's `SPARKn_HOST` plus `SPARK_HTTP_PORT`,\nso a card follows the hostname you set once and they answer without a key."],[1226,"The caveat worth knowing before you wire one in: Grok Build applies personas through subagent\nresolution and roles, not through a `spawn_subagent` parameter - the parent does not pass a\npersona name when it spawns a child (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:97`). A persona\nis therefore configured, not handed over: it attaches through a role or through\n`[subagents.personas]`, and `/personas` is where the catalog is read."],[399,"The chat agent discovers its context through its *default* paths (verified against the\nv2.36.6 agent binary and its `agentcontextconfig` source): the instruction file\n`AGENTS.md` under `~/.coder`, skills under `~/.coder/skills`, and a working-dir-relative\n`.agents/skills`, which here resolves to `/home/coder/.agents/skills` - the same folder\nthe Grok Build CLI looks in."],[1306,"The chat agent is compiled into coderd unconditionally."],[2879,"The Chat tab is a PTY over WebSocket on the same port. Approvals render as\ncards there; a mutating command that seems to hang is waiting for the card.\nSeeded `approvals.mode` is `manual` — never `off`. `hermes-init` must exit 0\nbefore `hermes` starts. If the gateway loops on a missing `config.yaml`, the\nnamed volume was not seeded: run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap -- --help` and recreate. Full\naccess / mounts / socket-risk notes: hermes.md."],[1425,"The Chat tab is the door for someone at a browser. The terminal door is for someone\nalready on the box, and it opens the **same** assistant - same `SOUL.md`, same seeded\n`config.yaml`, same git-tracked skills, same LiteLLM lane, same memory, same manual\napproval gate:"],[2627,"The checks that apply:"],[364,"The chown of `~/.grok` and `~/.shared` runs a second time at the end of the script,\nbecause the CLI installer and the seeds above may have created files as root when the\nagent ran as root."],[1917,"The CLI never creates the workspace itself. It prints\n`next: bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <url>` and stops, because `new-workspace.sh` owns the\ndev-container probe and the template choice, and a second copy of that decision would eventually\ndisagree with the first."],[798,"The CLI: bazel-map"],[362,"The clone folder is created before git runs, and before the `coder_script.code_server`\nscript's own `mkdir`: the editor starts in parallel with the startup script, and an\nempty tree is a folder VS Code can open while the clone is still writing. An empty\ndirectory is also what `git clone` accepts, so pre-creating it does not change what gets\ncloned - the emptiness guard still refuses a folder holding anything."],[336,"The clone is a plain `git clone` from the host, so it consults the host's own git\ncredentials. A private repository therefore needs a grant covering it **on the machine\nyou run this from**."],[1314,"The closing summary counts how many of the four auxiliary lanes carry a pin, and a lane\nleft without one prints a `warning:` that names the lane."],[2618,"The closing summary counts the auxiliary lanes that carry a pin. A `warning:` naming a\nlane means that lane falls back to Coder's own pick, and `title_generation` and\n`compaction` are the two an operator notices first: conversations stop getting titles,\nthen a long one stops at the compaction threshold. Re-run `--apply` once the alias\nbehind that lane answers."],[1240,"The code index is a file, not a service:"],[465,"The code-server module is deliberately given no settings map in plain mode: its merge\nruns on every start and would `mv` over the file the boot just layered, dropping the\noverlay's keys. (`devcontainer` passes the seed to the module only in the mode where\nthe merge is wanted, and bakes it as `settings_b64` for the built container.)"],[81,"The Coder Agents seeds and their agents-tree twins"],[387,"The Coder Agents seeds under `~/.coder` and `~/.agents` are not on those mounts: they live\non the workspace's own home volume, and that stage neither needs nor disturbs it."],[1254,"The command guard matches the whole command string, case-sensitively and byte-wise, so a\ncommand that *quotes* a denied form is refused the same way a real one is: `awk '/dd if=/'\nnotes.txt` denies, and so does a commit message that names `rm -rf` followed by a space.\nStripping quoted regions before matching would clear those and would catch a quoted dot\n(`find \".\" -type f -delete`) for free, and it would equally blind the guard to `sh -c \"...\"`\nand `bash -c \"...\"`, the indirection a command actually travels through most - so the string\nis read as written, and the few rules that have to see inside a quote spell the quote\nthemselves. The remedy for the rest is to rewrite the quoted sentence; the stack's own\n`Bash(rm -rf *)` permission deny behaves the same way for the same reason."],[1789,"The command that matters"],[2392,"The commands"],[1072,"The commands are the README's spin-up section\nwith the reasoning attached. Run them on the stack host, not inside a workspace: a\nworkspace's `docker` addresses its own DinD sidecar, and the whole-stack targets reach the\ndaemon that runs Coder."],[2311,"The composed names"],[1560,"The concepts behind the box — what the templates build, how a model request is routed, what the agents file seeds, and the rules a change follows."],[377,"The config file usually already exists, because an earlier start of this workspace\nwrote it, so the marker rather than first boot is what decides."],[701,"The config mounts three named volumes under `/var/cache/evo-t1-bazel`, and\n`.devcontainer/library-scripts/configure.sh` writes this block into `~/.bazelrc`:"],[970,"The consequence for a test author: a function counts when something calls it. A\nname in a comment, a stub, a fixture or a quoted grep pattern satisfies\n`//tests:shell_inventory` and satisfies nothing here."],[2438,"The consequence of the empty document above: **an unenrolled fleet reads as zero Sparks.**\nNo group in `sparks.json` means no target, which means no `up` series, which means an\nempty panel rather than a red one - the symptom looks like \"nothing is wrong\" from\nGrafana. Check it with `--targets` before you blame the dashboard."],[707,"The container is code in this repository, and it rots the same way a lockfile does.\nThese are the moments that oblige a contributor to touch it, and each has a gate:"],[757,"The contract every page in docs/ has to satisfy before the site will render it: frontmatter, the authored H1, the opening scan pair, and the navigation file that decides whether a page is reachable at all."],[730,"The contributor container is its own credential chain, so the doctor runs in SELF mode\nthere — exactly as it does inside a Coder workspace:"],[601,"The contributor path"],[609,"The contributor path — the dev container, or `test-in-image` — and where it fits"],[1628,"The control-plane chat agent dials the same proxy at `http://litellm:4000/v1`, over the\ncompose network, from inside the `coder` container. That is the server-side door."],[1741,"The conversation goes back to the box once it is healthy again."],[1277,"The copy is made by a background `rsync` that stage 9 of `startup.sh.tftpl` starts\nand leaves running for the life of the container: every 180 seconds it mirrors\n`~/.grok/sessions/` into `workspaces/<leaf>/`, skipping `*.tmp` and `*.lock`. It\nruns without `--delete`, so a session pruned from the live tree keeps its archived\ncopy. `flock -n` on a per-leaf lock file in the volume root is what keeps the stage\nidempotent: Coder re-runs the startup body on every start, and a second loop over the\nsame leaf would only double the write rate. The stage is best-effort: it warns and\ncarries on when the volume root or the leaf is not writable, or when `flock` is\nmissing, and re-tests for the sessions directory each tick because the CLI creates\nthat directory on its first run, which can be long after boot."],[832,"The cost of not querying shows up late. In the sessions that hit a coupling rule, the first\n`FAIL: //` line appeared only after a hundred or more messages of editing: the rule was\ndiscoverable in the graph on message 1, and was instead discovered by running the gate. One agent\naudited `//:stack_data` by listing the repo's top-level directories rather than diffing the graph\nagainst `git ls-files`, which is the same question asked in a way that cannot answer it."],[1869,"The coupling runs one way: this repo may name the scaffold repository; the scaffold repository\nnever names this one. And the scaffold's quality gate is its own -- the `.self-check/` suite and\nthe CI that runs it in that repository -- so a scaffold change is tested where it is made, and a\nlane or a file the scaffold adds needs no change here."],[2137,"The credential boundary"],[493,"The credential helper configured for `https://github.com`. Both template twins seed\n`~/.gitconfig` at startup, for `github.com` and `gist.github.com` alike:"],[1906,"The credential here is the Coder External Auth GitHub App **user-to-server token**; this stack\nnever introduces a static token or PAT. An App can be granted one endpoint and refused another, so\nthe CLI runs the publish side as independent steps, each reporting `ok:` or `warning:` on its own.\nNo step is fatal, because aborting on the first refusal would abandon the steps that were allowed."],[95,"The credential ladder, in git's consult order"],[460,"The custom icon `templates/common/vscode-alt.svg` travels as a\n`data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from `templates/common/`. Coder serves icons\nonly from its bundled `/icon/*` set or from a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is\nnot bundled. `workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a\ncompact ribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`. `push-template` rejects\nthat locally, before the CLI runs."],[298,"The daemon image is resolved with `data \"docker_image\"`, which inspects the host store\nand **never pulls**, so `docker:29.8.1-dind` must already be there. `bootstrap.sh`\npre-arms it; to do it manually:"],[295,"The daemon's `/var/lib/docker` lives on its own volume (`coder-<wsid>-dind-lib`), so a\nstop/start does not re-pull every image the builds used. It is not gated on\n`start_count`, so it survives a stop and is reused by the next start."],[610,"The daily commands, and the Makefile shim over them"],[1752,"The dashboard's **Agent backend** card exists to surface exactly that failure."],[1931,"The dashboards"],[2328,"The default shape is LAN-only. `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` additionally serves the same\nseven services on public names over real TLS, each name composed from the parts you set\nonce. It is purely additive: every LAN route keeps working. Grafana and Infisical are the\ntwo new services on this path: the observability backends behind Grafana (Prometheus,\nLoki, Tempo, Mimir) stay unexposed, because\nSECURITY.md keeps\nmetrics and logs off the LAN, let alone the WAN."],[1117,"The delegation sketch"],[1255,"The deny list is a floor, not a fence. It catches the forms this repo has actually measured,\nand a novel destructive command walks straight through it. Nothing in a hook reads the state\nthe command will act on, which is why \"confirm before an action that is hard to undo\" stays a\nrulebook rule that no pattern can replace."],[1251,"The deny list is the extension point. `~/.grok/hooks/bin/deny-patterns.txt` holds one extended\nregex per line, `#` starts a comment and blank lines are ignored, so an operator forbids one\nmore command by appending one line - and because there is no allow list, deleting a line is the\nonly way to permit something again. That copy is stack-owned: the boot rewrites it from\n`templates/common/hooks.deny-patterns.txt` while its `managed-by:` marker stands, so a line\nappended in a workspace is gone at the next start. Land the rule in the seed (with the Bats case\nin `tests/bats/stack-hooks.bats` that denies it), or delete the marker from that one file to keep\na copy of your own, which the boot then leaves alone. A line that does not compile as an ERE is\nskipped with a\nsingle note on stderr rather than blocking every shell call, which is why the file's own header\ncarries the one-line compile check to run before you commit a rule:\n`LC_ALL=C grep -E -q -- '<pattern>' </dev/null`."],[606,"The dependency maps and their gate"],[1518,"The deploy workspace is writable by the assistant's file tools only when you say so\nwith `--writable`, and the file tools refuse anything outside the image's\n`HERMES_WRITE_SAFE_ROOT` (the official default is `/opt/data`) with no prompt at all.\nName the narrowest directory that finishes the job."],[1313,"The deployment default follows the health gate. `agent` is registered as the default,\nand when LiteLLM reports no healthy deployment behind the Spark alias the Arc `chat`\nconfig takes the default in its place. That matters because a chat opened without an\nexplicit model pick has only the default to resolve to, so a stack that comes up while\nthe Spark lane is dark still gets an Agents chat that answers. An existing config is\nleft as it stands, since this API surface has no PATCH: the script names the Admin\nscreen to correct it when a stored row drifted."],[2786,"The dev container must build first, and that needs `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`\nin the cloned repository."],[2345,"The dev row is what `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack -- up` composes for the\n`evo-t1-dev` project: that command applies `STACK_ENV_LABEL=dev` when neither the shell\nnor the env file already sets a label, and only for that project, so\n`--project-name evo-t1-review` gets none. A label goes on every name, so that stack's\ndashboard is `dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` too, and all seven need their own Traefik\nrouters (see below). `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` never invents a label."],[1769,"The dev sandbox takes the same restart with its own project name and env file:"],[307,"The devcontainer CLI always bind-mounts the workspace folder into the dev container it\ncreates, and a bind source is resolved by the daemon that *runs* that container - here\nthe sidecar, not the workspace's."],[2007,"The DGX Sparks - the off-stack boxes Prometheus probes through blackbox:"],[857,"The direct inputs are the `data` the dependency maps write, which is what you edit when a target\nneeds one more payload group:"],[345,"The Docker **client** toolchain only (`docker`, `docker buildx`, `docker compose` as\nCLI plugins under `/usr/local/lib/docker/cli-plugins`). No daemon: the build asserts\n`dockerd`, `containerd`, `runc` and `docker-proxy` are absent."],[227,"The docker provider resolves `evo-t1-dev:latest` from the local image store and never\nattempts a registry pull, which is what keeps workspace creation working with no\ninternet."],[1048,"The docker socket stays unmounted: service status comes from each service's API, which\navoids handing the dashboard a root-equivalent host credential. The Hermes card in the\n**Host** group is the same pattern (`siteMonitor` of `http://hermes:9119/api/status`);\nthe Hermes container itself *does* mount the socket — that credential lives there, not\nhere. See hermes.md."],[2874,"The drop is a stated boundary rather than a bug: see the **No multi-line value reaches a\nworkspace** bullet in operate/secrets.md."],[1149,"The editor opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`: the\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL and as a positional argument to the code-server binary\nwith `--ignore-last-opened`. `workspace` uses `/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` (and\n`/home/coder` with no repository); `devcontainer` uses `/workspaces/<repo>` inside a\nbuilt dev container and `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo>` on the\nplain container. On first start the inlined `coder_script.code_server` creates that\nfolder and waits up to 60 s for `.git` (skipped when the folder is `/home/coder`) so\nthe clone can land before code-server launches. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the\nagent would chdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail\nthe agent. With the sample `REPO_URL` that folder is\n`/home/coder/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`."],[1774,"The editor roster is the third copy of the name. `__evoT1StackModels` in both\n`templates/*/settings.json.tftpl` twins is the alias list Cline and Roo/Kilo are offered,\nand that seed is copied onto the per-owner `vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume only while\nthe file is absent - a roster that gains an id never reaches an owner whose\n`settings.json` already exists, so re-seeding means deleting that volume's file."],[1665,"The eight `agent-*` rows are that same lane with a thinking level baked into\n`litellm_params.reasoning_effort`. Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and\n`xhigh` and nothing else - there is no `high` - so the level is a name to pick\nrather than a knob to turn, and `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` are\nhow the level reaches Coder Agents: its model dropdown chooses an alias, not a\nrequest parameter. The `agent-explore` and `agent-review` rows are the same idea aimed\nat a fleet role rather than at a person in that dropdown - see\nthe fan-out lanes."],[1714,"The eighth and ninth Spark groups, and the only two that ask the proxy to answer one of\ntheir calls somewhere else. Everything else about each is its parent: `agent-xhigh-fast` is\n`agent-xhigh` - three equal `openai/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` deployments, `xhigh` baked, the\nsession pin kept - and `agent-swarm-fast` is `agent-swarm`, the same three boxes with the\npin lifted for it alone among the new lanes. The nine Spark lane groups are now\n`agent agent-low agent-medium agent-xhigh agent-swarm agent-explore agent-review\nagent-xhigh-fast agent-swarm-fast`, plus the Arc group `compaction-fast`."],[808,"The empty result is not an answer"],[745,"The equivalent run on your own machine is the host ingress above, so a CI-only failure is\nreproducible without a Linux box or a workflow rerun."],[479,"The extras are cloned **beside** the primary repository, into sibling folders, by the\nsame clone-once rule (a folder that already holds a `.git` is left alone on later\nstarts, never pulled on)."],[2978,"The failure that motivates all of this is the merge that goes the wrong way. A `development`\nbranch that is 134 commits ahead of `main` (a real number from a real repository in this fleet)\nis not a staging area, it is a second product whose differences nobody is reconciling, and the\nday someone finally merges it, the review is unreviewable."],[1258,"The family splits by home, and the split is deliberate:"],[1597,"The fan-out lanes: `agent-explore` and `agent-review`"],[1097,"The fan-out seam"],[1598,"The fast-compaction lanes: `agent-xhigh-fast` and `agent-swarm-fast`"],[443,"The fetch is best-effort and cannot fail a workspace start. `timeout = 120`\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:730`) bounds the realistic slow case, a vault container\nstill coming up, and every failure path - a refused login, an export that came back empty,\nan `mv` that did not land - prints one line, exits 0, and leaves the boot running on\nwhatever `agent.env` the previous start wrote\n(`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:62-65`, `:68-77`, `:252-254`).\noperate/secrets.md states the same bound from the other side."],[1698,"The fifth Spark group, and the first of the four that carry no pin. Same\n`openai/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` on the same three boxes, same baked `xhigh`, same tool-calling\nflags and 262144 window - the\ndifference is routing state only, so no pin resolves and every request goes to the global\n`least-busy` picker, landing on the healthy box with the fewest requests in flight."],[2277,"The file header and the function docstring block both gates require"],[2174,"The file itself is written mode 600 inside a mode 700 `~/.infisical`, staged beside its\ndestination and moved into place in one step rather than appended, so a reader never sees a\nhalf-written file. `docs/coder-templates.md` carries the template side of all of this: the eight\n`infisical_*` variables, why the credential pair rides the agent environment rather than the\nrendered script body, and the order the boot body runs in."],[994,"The file probe (`-f \"${candidate}/${marker}\"`) says \"this is a repo root\"; refusing a derived\ncandidate whose `pwd -P` lands under `bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or a\n`*.runfiles` tree says \"and it is the real checkout, not a copy\", because runfiles carries its\nown copy of the marker. Two probes, because either one alone passes in the wrong case: writing a\nrotated `.env` or a `sparks/<host>.env` through the runfiles copy reports success while the\ncheckout keeps the old bytes. An explicit `${REPO_ROOT}` is exempt from the second probe — it is\na statement about where to work, and this harness points it at the copy of the repo under test."],[802,"The filegroup that must claim a new payload file"],[858,"The files it can actually open are the transitive closure of those groups, which is what the verb\nprints:"],[1501,"The first `compose up` runs `hermes-init`, which is the in-container equivalent of\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap`: it runs\n`scripts/hermes-bootstrap.sh --in-container` and copies `hermes/config.yaml` and\n`hermes/SOUL.md` into `/opt/data` **only when those files are missing**. Editing the\ncopy in the volume is how an operator ends up with a config no one can reproduce."],[2227,"The first eight are the list `scripts/bootstrap.sh` used to carry twice; that duplication\nis what this inventory replaced."],[2897,"The first registered account is the site admin."],[1102,"The five advisor commands"],[346,"The five offline MCP servers ship in the image, but the rendered config declares none\nof them by default, so a workspace needs no network to start a session."],[212,"The five values the boot reads the vault with are forwarded **whether or not the flag is\non** (`scripts/push-template.sh:696-730`), so adopting the vault later is a re-push of one\nword rather than a re-config of five, and a stack that never adopted Infisical pushes none\nof them. None of the five is echoed either: a project id and a machine identity id both end\nup transcribed into whoever's CI log this output lands in, so the log line names only the\nkey a value came from (`scripts/push-template.sh:696-700`). The sixth value, the instance\nURL, is the one a workspace cannot do without and the one this script composes rather than\nforwards, inside the flag gate (`scripts/push-template.sh:652-661`): `.env`'s\n`INFISICAL_URL` is the address the *host* dials, and a container handed that spelling reaches\nits own loopback, where nothing listens."],[1929,"The flag"],[2128,"The flag"],[2391,"The flag"],[2924,"The flag is on the Prometheus command in `docker-compose.yml`. Confirm it survived, then\nconfirm the series arrived:"],[2952,"The flag ladder"],[2961,"The flag ladder, from one env var up to a checked-in `config/flags.yaml`"],[2141,"The flag that decides whether the bundle exists, and the second gate on the workspace side"],[2422,"The fleet itself comes from `SPARK_HOSTS` or the `SPARKn_HOST` keys and is resolved by\n`derive_spark_hosts` (`scripts/lib/spark.sh:67`); this CLI derives no hostname, and every\nbox line it prints names the host, because three Sparks of which one is quiet is otherwise\nindistinguishable from three Sparks of which one is down."],[1259,"The fleet's six `~/.grok/agents/*.md` definitions and its three\n`~/.grok/personas/*.toml` overlays are **marker-aware**, the way\n`~/.grok/AGENTS.md` is: a copy that still carries `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` is\nstack-owned and gets the current seed text on every start, so a stale role definition\ncannot silently change how every child in every workspace behaves. Delete the marker line\nand the file is the person's - the boot leaves a marker-free copy alone."],[2027,"The folder is source, so it is tested as source:"],[96,"The four commands"],[2039,"The four exporters keep nothing between runs: what they hold is a scrape interval deep, so\nrecreating one costs no history and needs no volume of its own."],[1224,"The four layers can name different lanes: the guide ranks the per-type pin as applying \"for\nany parent\" without ranking it against a definition's own `model:`\n(`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:287`). All six bake the same alias their\npin defaults to, `implementer` and `tester` among them, so a workspace that wants its\nwriters somewhere specific should set the variable rather than rely on the file.\n`/config-agents` (alias `/agents`) is where a person sees and toggles the set."],[1101,"The four skills"],[1378,"The fourth payload `coder_script.stack_repo_skills` seeds is not a converter.\n`/ez-micro-plan` is the parent's protocol for splitting one change across a fleet of\nsubagents and proving the split dispatches; how its slots, lane and check fit together is\nThe fan-out seam."],[2941,"The full procedure for moving a repository onto trunk-based development: environments become configuration instead of git branches, unfinished work ships behind a flag that defaults off, and every other branch is inventoried, ported, or listed for deletion. Worked examples for Compose, K3s, Ansible, Terraform/ArgoCD, Bazel and manage.sh stacks."],[1935,"The gate"],[3022,"The gate runs with it off; at least one test or documented smoke command runs with it on."],[497,"The gh auth store (`~/.config/gh`, 0600): the one copy on disk, maintained by\n`gh_token_refresh`. Reachable only past a helper that clears the pair."],[2835,"The grant behind the credential - the GitHub App installation that the External Auth\nprovider (id `github` by default) minted its token from - has to include the repository\nthe workspace clones or pushes: GitHub -> Settings -> Applications -> `<app>` ->\n**Configure** -> Repository access."],[1095,"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image"],[1127,"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image, and how it is pinned"],[77,"The Grok Build CLI probe, the download, and the installer stub"],[1733,"The Grok CLI forwards `extra_headers` verbatim."],[1036,"the group's place on the page: `Observability` has its own `layout` entry, renders as\none `style: row` band whose `columns` equals the cards it holds, and sits after the\n**Host** group"],[161,"The guard also says the number while it is still a warning: a body within\n`STARTUP_SIZE_WARN` (4096) of the bound prints its composed total and the bytes left,\nbecause the distance to the budget is what a reviewer needs and a hard failure only\never reports the crossing. Measured on 2026-11-06 with the byte algorithm in\n`tests/bats/startup-script-size.bats`: the widest body is now the devcontainer startup\nscript at 115967 bytes, 6913 clear of the bound, with the workspace twin at 113767 and\n9113 clear. Before the trio moved out that same devcontainer body measured 122389 with\nonly 491 clear, and `ez-learn.SKILL.md` plus its heredoc accounted for roughly 7 KB of\nit. The three payloads now travel in their own body, `coder_script.stack_skills`, which\ncomposes to 36847 bytes. Measured again on 2026-11-09 after the repository-conversion\nfamily landed: `coder_script.stack_repo_skills`, carrying three `SKILL.md` payloads and\nthree python helpers, composes to 60336 bytes, 62544 clear of the bound, and neither\nstartup twin moved. Measured again on 2026-11-14 after the family's fourth member\nlanded: that same body now carries four `SKILL.md` payloads and four python helpers and\ncomposes to 91203 bytes, 31677 clear of the bound, and neither startup twin moved -\nwhich is the point of giving a payload family its own `coder_script` instead of feeding\nit to `startup_script`. Measured again on 2026-11-16 with the same reader\n(`awk -f scripts/lib/startup_size.awk templates/workspace/main.tf`, bytes summed per body)\nafter the advisor and guard families landed: `coder_script.stack_advisors`, carrying four\nadvisor `SKILL.md` payloads, the `documenter` role, the code-index skill and its helper,\ncomposes to 71032 bytes, and `coder_script.stack_hooks`, carrying the manifest, four guards\nand the deny list, to 39399 - 51848 and 83481 clear of the bound respectively, with neither\nstartup twin touched."],[373,"The guard family under `~/.grok/hooks/` follows the same rule for a sharper reason. The\nmanifest and the five files under `hooks/bin/` are rewritten on every start while they carry\nthe marker - the `\"__evoT1Stack\": true` key in the JSON, the only spelling that leaves it\nparseable, and the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment in the four scripts and the deny\nlist. A hook decides what a session may run and what it may write, so a copy that drifted\nfrom the seed is a silent behaviour change, while a stale `SKILL.md` misleads one run; that\ndifference is why the skills stay absent-only (the advisors' twelve copies and their\n`~/.grok/agents/documenter.md` included) and this family does not. Each write ends by\nappending the marker line if the payload lost its own, so a rewritten file never becomes\nunrecognisable as stack-owned on the next boot."],[1010,"The header tiles: CPU, memory, temperature, disk, interface throughput, clock, search"],[1424,"The Homepage card in the **Host** group links to `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the public\norigin when set, otherwise the LAN URL). Its `siteMonitor` is\n`http://hermes:9119/api/status`, which resolves over compose DNS, so the card stays\ncorrect whatever the browser link is. See dashboard.md."],[997,"The Homepage dashboard and Kasm Workspaces: what each card reads, how the password is issued, and how a desktop gets streamed."],[1150,"The icon travels as a `data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from\n`templates/common/vscode-alt.svg`: Coder serves icons only from its bundled\n`/icon/*` set or a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is not bundled.\n`workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a compact\nribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`."],[2959,"The ideal-state checklist a refactor has to satisfy before it is called done"],[1054,"The image ships default users (`admin@kasm.local` / `user@kasm.local`); change them\nduring the wizard."],[682,"The image ships the Docker client only. The build ends with `test ! -x\n/usr/local/bin/dockerd` (plus `containerd`, `runc`, `docker-proxy`), so the merge-gate\ncontainer drives a daemon it is given rather than owning one."],[2011,"The inference backends - the software that serves the models, not the machines it runs\non, which are two rows above:"],[87,"The Infisical boot fetch"],[103,"The Infisical boot fetch: its two gates, its eight agent-env keys, and `~/.infisical/agent.env`"],[89,"The inlined code-server fork"],[368,"The install check is `command -v grok`, not `[ -x ~/.grok/bin/grok ]`: the golden image\nbakes the CLI into `/usr/local/bin`, so on a golden-image boot the probe answers yes and\nnothing downloads. The download exists for the **Minimal shell** (`ubuntu:24.04`) preset\nand for a home volume that never received a CLI."],[2214,"The inventory is data, not code: `infisical/managed-secrets.txt`, read through\n`infisical_managed_keys()`. Never re-hardcode the list. The count is asserted - `check`\nwarns when the inventory is not exactly twelve, because a silently-short list would leave\na credential outside the vault while still reporting a clean sync. Every key must also\nexist in `.env.example`, since compose keeps interpolating that file."],[960,"The inventory strips comment lines before it looks for a name, so a name that only ever\nappears in a `# @function` block or in prose is still untested: it has to sit in a code line\nsomewhere under `tests/`. The usual way to get one there is a unit case that calls the\nhelper directly:"],[1778,"The IPEX-LLM runtime is Intel's XPU path (oneAPI / Level Zero) for the Arc stack."],[1465,"The key is `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`, substituted from the environment into the seeded\n`config.yaml`. Do not copy the key into a Hermes file - one secret, one owner."],[376,"The key is what makes the rule hold. The CLI's own settings writer (`grok mcp add` and\nfriends) deletes every comment line, so a comment-only marker can vanish with nobody\ntouching the file."],[1720,"The known limit on that tier is its fallback, and it is the stack's standing one: every\nSpark group's last resort is the Arc, so `agent-low: [\"coder\"]`\n(`litellm/config.yaml:862`, and the same shape under `agent-xhigh-fast: [\"agent-xhigh\",\n\"coder\"]` and `agent-swarm-fast: [\"agent-swarm\", \"coder\"]`) means that when all three Sparks\nare cooled an oversized summary lands on `coder` - a 32768-token group - and is answered\nfrom a truncated reading, which is the outcome the fork exists to prevent. Nothing here\nmakes that worse: the pair's own chains end at `coder` too, and so does\n`compaction-fast: [\"agent-low\", \"coder\"]`. The fork changes who tries first. What an operator\nholds is the choice of who tries second: blank `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` to leave\nthe summary on the lane it arrived on, point it at another Spark group, or edit the fallback\nlist beside it in the same routing file. When the Sparks are unreachable the answer is the\none every agent lane already has - the working degraded mode is `chat`."],[1074,"The LAN address you will browse to. `CODER_ACCESS_URL` has to be an `https://` URL that\nis not `localhost`, or a workspace cannot reach LiteLLM."],[1833,"The last one is worth dwelling on: `check` can read every config file and still\nbe looking at an image GRUB cannot load, so an encrypted `/boot` is reported as\na warning to verify by hand rather than cleared."],[1049,"The layout follows the upstream README example - logo plus greeting top-left, boxed\nresource tiles, clock, weather and search top-right, cards on a wallpaper. The\nwallpaper (`background.webp`) and the logo (`evo-t1.svg`) live in\n`homepage/assets/icons/`, mounted at `/app/public/icons` and served as `/icons/...`.\nHomepage's auth middleware exempts only that path from the sign-in redirect, which is\nwhat lets the same wallpaper render on the login page, and is why `favicon` points at\n`/icons/evo-t1.svg` too: a tab icon from anywhere else 307s to `/auth/signin`."],[940,"The layout: suites, helpers, stubs and fixtures"],[1278,"The leaf is the container hostname with anything outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` folded to\n`-`, so a name carrying a slash cannot write outside `workspaces/` and an empty one\ncannot collapse every workspace into a single mirror. The sanctioned reader accepts\nexactly that character set, so the write side and the read side agree."],[2604,"The lever, now pulled: `[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` ships at 70 in both\ntemplate twins, so auto-compaction fires near 183,500 tokens rather than the 197k-214k\nthese events measured, and the ~200k-token summary input shrinks with it. The store\nshows no size threshold separating failure from success - the successes reach the same\n197k-214k range - so this is a load reduction, not a proven cure. Re-run the first\ncommand above to measure whether the ratio moved."],[2858,"The line is in the workspace start log, in the script Coder labels **Fetch stack secrets\nfrom Infisical** (`templates/workspace/main.tf:723`, same body in the devcontainer twin\nat `templates/devcontainer/main.tf:774`):"],[2867,"The line is the workspace's, from the same script, and the path is that home directory\n(`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:238-243`):"],[507,"The lines `check` prints are documented with the failures they explain, in\ntroubleshooting.md:\n`git would send this credential (source: ...)`, `NO GitHub credential ...`,\n`[shadowed: ...]`, `push would WORK` / `push would FAIL`, and the dead-`GIT_ASKPASS` note.\n`gh` refusing a healthy store is its own entry there, at\n`gh` answers `Bad credentials`,\nand a workspace with no `gh` binary at\n`gh: command not found`."],[416,"The list above is checked, not trusted: `//tests:bats_tftpl_interpolation_test` pulls the\nkeys of the `workspace` `startup_script` map out of the checker's own reading of\n`main.tf` and diffs them against the names this page lists. The same suite reads every\n`templatefile()` call in both twins, takes the keys each map declares and the names each\nbody interpolates, and fails on either direction of drift. Run the checker directly to\nsee the offending body and line:\n`python3 scripts/lib/tftpl_interpolation.py templates --strict`."],[525,"The long form of the `.env.sample` comments: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, the Spark `agent` group"],[803,"The map key a new `scripts/*.sh` needs, and the test a new function needs"],[1606,"The measured burst"],[1284,"The memory and index files are SQLite, opened live by every workspace holding the\nmount, so two workspaces writing memory at once can race. *Roughly in sync* is the\nbar, not transactional sharing."],[670,"The merge gate (Linux image) versus the convenience path (native Darwin)"],[962,"The merge gate is the Linux golden image — the same `images/dev/Dockerfile` the Coder\nworkspaces boot and CI builds:"],[736,"The merge gate is the Linux image, so run it in the contributor container:"],[2778,"The message names the body and the position, which is the whole diagnosis: line\n32 there is `if [ -L \"${evo_t1_root}\" ]; then`, a shell loop variable."],[975,"The mirror pads every `.sh` it instruments, so a suite that measures a payload's\n**byte size** through `REPO_ROOT` prices the harness instead of the product. This is\nhow `//tests:shell_coverage` failed an execve budget the checkout had already passed:\n`templates/common/notes-setup.sh` weighed 1182 bytes more in the mirror than it\nships. A size gate resolves its tree through `bats_source_repo_root`, and\n`startup-script-size.bats` builds a real instrumented mirror to prove it still\nmeasures what ships."],[2958,"The model in six statements, and what each one rules out"],[1563,"The model lane"],[2533,"The model lane: Spark boxes, LiteLLM, and the agent backends"],[1580,"The model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request picks a backend, and what happens when a Spark box goes down."],[1678,"The model writes the JSON call into the message body, which no agent driver can consume."],[2943,"The model, in six statements"],[1469,"The models come from a `providers:` block in `hermes/config.yaml`. Its `litellm` row\ndeclares the shared catalogue - the aliases that are not one of the Arc's own three\nweights - and each Arc row declares its weight with its `default_model`. Between\nthem those declarations are every client-selectable alias in\n`litellm/config.yaml`, and a declaration is what makes an alias routable: Hermes\nmatches a typed `/model <alias>` against the ids each row declares and routes it to\nthe row that declares it, while a name no row declares is left on the current\nprovider as an unknown hidden model. `litellm/config.yaml` owns the set, so an alias\nis added there and a row follows; the gate fails when the two drift apart, or when\none alias ends up on two rows."],[1408,"The models it calls through LiteLLM"],[1730,"The module is mounted as a single file,\n`./litellm/compaction_router.py:/app/compaction_router.py:ro`. A directory mount over\n`/app` hides `config.yaml` beside it, and it has to sit in the same directory as the\nconfig file because that is the directory the proxy resolves a callback's dotted name\nagainst - the bare module name in\n`callbacks` resolves no other way."],[324,"The mount covers the workspace container only; a dev container built on the sidecar\nkeeps its own home either way."],[214,"The name shift on the Universal Auth pair is deliberate: the templates name the variables\nafter what the values *are* (`infisical_client_id`, `infisical_client_secret`), `.env`\nnames its keys after how the Infisical CLI reads them\n(`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_ID`, `..._SECRET`), and `bazelisk run //scripts:infisical\n-- check` is the reader that keeps the `.env` spelling honest\n(`scripts/push-template.sh:686-689`, `:709-714`, `:725-730`)."],[580,"The names also go into `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` (compose appends this raw value)\nand into the Coder certificate's subjectAltName."],[2320,"The naming rule, and what `auto` composes from the parts you set"],[1644,"The nine Spark groups are independent and none inherits `agent`, so each needs its own\nsix keys. The zeros pin the exceptions `DefaultRetries` would otherwise reach; at this\ntag `NotFoundError` has no field of its own, so without a policy a typo'd model id is\nretried on `DefaultRetries`."],[2825,"The note line, `note: GIT_ASKPASS is set but answers no credential, which is what turns\na refused token into \"Password authentication is not supported for Git operations.\"; a\nworking credential helper makes it moot`, is the second half of the story. The agent env\nexports `GIT_ASKPASS` pointing at the `coder` binary the agent staged under\n`/tmp/coder.<rand>/`, and in a measured workspace that binary answered every prompt, and\n`coder gitaskpass` itself, with `unrecognized subcommand` and no output, so git got no\nanswer at all and fell through to prompting. Clearing `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` helps\nonly while the helper answers; with a silent helper it changes nothing."],[2735,"The note offers both remedies because the check cannot tell them apart from\ninside the container: a workspace value and a template version's default live in\nthe same database on the host, and neither is readable over the agent token. The\nsecond run of the check is what settles it, which is why the note asks for it."],[966,"The npm-backed gates (`//docs-site:typecheck`, `:unit`, `:unit_coverage`, `:verify`) are\n`manual` for the same reason, and they all run out of `docs-site/node_modules` in the\ncheckout: `run_npm.sh` resolves its own runfiles copy back into it, so a cold checkout\ninstalls once for the whole family, under `docs-site/.npm-ci.lock`. Without that lock\nevery test action ran its own `npm ci`, and a gate that started while another was still\nwriting the tree died on a SIGBUS reading a short esbuild binary. A box with no `flock`\n(a macOS laptop) installs unlocked, since nothing else is running there."],[2538,"The observability bundle: a card that reads down, a scrape with no targets, a collector with nothing to ship"],[1390,"The on-host assistant with two doors - the Web Dashboard on :9119 and the host terminal via hermes-shell: how to reach it, what it may touch, and how approvals gate a mutating command from either."],[2662,"The one case where a body prints nothing at all and nothing is wrong: the vault body leaves\nbefore it opens its run when the image carries no `infisical` binary\n(`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:15`), which is silent by design because the\nvault is optional."],[2814,"The one command does the same three steps and then proves them:"],[1799,"The one command that answers \"can I unlock this from a laptop\""],[2819,"The one command, run where the push was attempted, naming the repository:"],[2804,"The one command, whichever of the two it was:"],[1943,"The one flag that decides whether the bundle exists, and how to turn it off"],[1614,"The one front door every client dials, and why nothing else is published"],[652,"The one image, the one pin file"],[2402,"The one key that decides whether the fleet is monitored at all, and what reads it"],[650,"The one Linux golden image behind laptop work, the Coder workspaces and CI: what the contributor dev container builds, how it caches, how it seeds itself, and which path is the merge gate."],[410,"The one precondition a body cannot inherit from another body is a writable directory\nunder `$HOME`. The three per-owner Grok volumes mount *under* `~/.grok`, and the Docker\ndaemon creates a bind mount's target path as `root:root`, so `~/.grok` arrives\nroot-owned on any boot where those mount points are new. The startup script chowns it\nback to the home owner, and nothing orders that before a sibling body: the start\nscripts fan out in one errgroup, startup script included, which is the same\n`script_count=10` burst The one-argument ceiling counts.\nSo a body that writes under `~/.grok` calls `boot_ensure_dir <dir>`\n(`templates/common/boot-log.lib.sh:65-140`) rather than a bare `mkdir -p`. It creates\nthe directory and takes back the ownership of each component of it that is not this\nboot's to write, walking from `$HOME` down, stopping below `$HOME` itself, and never\nrecursing into the mounted volumes that sit beside the target. It returns 1 and prints\n`error:` when the directory stays unwritable, which is what lets a body refuse its own\n`ok:`. `templates/common/terminal-launchers.sh.tftpl` is the case that earned it: a\nlauncher that never landed is a code-server tab that dies with `exit code: 127`, and\nthe body closed at `summary: 2 ok, 0 warnings, 0 failed` while writing nothing. The\nbodies that seed the two skill homes under `~/.coder` and `~/.agents` still `mkdir -p`\ndirectly - neither is a bind-mount parent, so nothing arrives there owned by root."],[2029,"The one to read before you write an `expr`: every metric name in every `expr` has to sit on\na pinned allow-list, and that list is assembled at test time from the recording rules this\nrepo ships under `observability/prometheus/rules/` as well as the pinned names\n(`tests/bats/observability-dashboards.bats:688`). A new recording rule therefore needs no\ntest edit, and a metric nothing here records fails the build instead of rendering an empty\ntile. `bazelisk run //scripts:validate` covers the coarser layer: it parses every dashboard\nJSON through `scripts/lib/parse_json.py` (`scripts/validate.sh:104`), the gate that already\ncovers the three rule files."],[54,"The one-argument ceiling"],[1612,"The one-front-door rule"],[1065,"The one-time host bootstrap, and why it is the only step run straight from a shell"],[2595,"The only live bound was the proxy's `request_timeout: 1200`, multiplied by every\nattempt. Observed failure durations were 1366 s, about 2400 s and 3465 s - the deadline\nhonoured once, twice and three times."],[2427,"The only mutator. Set `SPARK_MONITOR_ENABLED=true` first or every box is skipped. Per box\nit reports each container as `created`, `started` (an existing one), or `already running`,\nand the counter list as `same`, `updated` or `absent`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:662-700`). Exit status is 0 when every box answered or is\nsimply not set up yet, and non-zero only when a mutator ran and a box failed - a Spark\nwith nothing installed is a `warning:`, not a failure, because that is the state a\nfreshly imaged box is in."],[761,"The opening scan pair"],[2131,"The operator CLI"],[2126,"The opt-in secrets vault: the twelve managed keys, the operator CLI verbs and their exit codes, the adoption sequence, what the workspace boot publishes and what it refuses, what happens when the vault is unreachable, and how a rotated credential reaches its service."],[360,"The order above is not incidental; each line is a constraint the script has to keep."],[1817,"The order is deliberate: the hook packs the authorized key at generation time,\nso a key written after `update-initramfs` is not in the image. That is why\n`install` ends by reading the image back."],[1568,"The order that makes the pieces click, if you are reading them all"],[2378,"The original Host header preserved - a rewritten one comes back as `421`."],[1868,"The other *template* is a **project scaffold**: a tree of files that `scripts/new-repo.sh` copies,\nsubstitutes, and hands to `git init` as the first commit of a brand new repository. It is not\nvendored here. It lives in its own repository -- by default `https://github.com/toxicoder/ezrepo`\n-- and this repo's whole job is to fetch that tree and render it. The scaffold is a product with\nits own CI and its own release rhythm, and a vendored copy drifts from the day it is committed, so\nnothing here is kept in step with it."],[2902,"The other cause is that the service genuinely is not ready: Mimir, Loki and Tempo report\n`/ready` only once their ingester is, so a badge that goes down under load is a real\nsignal, not a stale card."],[2421,"The other keys, all in `.env.example`: `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (line 607),\n`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` (line 612), `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE` (line 621, the pin\nabove, and the knob you correct if the registry refuses that tag). The host-side exporters\nthis bundle adds live beside them - `OBS_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (583), `OBS_CADVISOR_PORT`\n(590), `OBS_BLACKBOX_PORT` (596), `OBS_ALERTMANAGER_PORT` (601) - and\n`ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL` (635) is empty by default; nothing requires it. Those four\nhost exporters publish on the **host's loopback only**, so a probe of them runs in a host\nshell, the same as Prometheus' own UI on `PROMETHEUS_PORT`."],[1110,"The out-of-band session archive"],[2237,"The outage is bounded, because the only read the deploy path makes is a best-effort refresh\nwhose failure is one `warning:` line."],[2023,"The overview does not ask you to choose first. Seven stat panels - `Spark $host`,\n`GPU util`, `Framebuffer fill`, `Temp headroom`, `Power`, `CPU`, `Memory` - each carry\n`repeat` on that same variable with `repeatDirection: h`, so the strip renders one column\nper enrolled box. The link panel `Open this Spark` repeats alongside them and points at\n`/d/evo-spark-host/one-dgx-spark?var-host=${var:queryparam}`: the tile under the box you\nare reading opens that box's page with `host` already set. That is the path from a red tile\non the overview to the GPU rows for that machine, and it is the only link between the two\nboards. `evo-sparks-fleet` declares no variable at all, because it aggregates across every\nbox by design."],[760,"The page title"],[422,"The parent folder (`/home/coder/workspace`, or the `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<wsid>` bind\nin `devcontainer`) is chowned before git runs, and *non-recursively*: other clones live\nalongside this one and are not ours to take over. The clone folder itself is chowned too,\nor a root agent leaves it root-owned and the clone dies writing `.git` inside it."],[1025,"The password gate is homepage v2's built-in one, and it is worth keeping: the page\nlists every service on the box. `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` holds the plaintext, because\nhomepage hashes that env var itself but still needs the value."],[1011,"The password gate, the proxy in front of it, and how to rotate the password"],[2888,"The paste-ready Traefik file (one backend at `:8080`, `passHostHeader: true`,\nAuthelia on the four interactive names, LiteLLM without Authelia) is in\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."],[1942,"The path a metric, a log line and a span take before Grafana renders them"],[2310,"The path a public request takes"],[2319,"The path a public request takes, from the browser to the container"],[486,"The path is Coder's external-auth broker. With no `coder.env`, Coder CE injects\na GitHub App whose id is `github`, and both templates declare that id by\ndefault. A custom OAuth app lives in `coder.env` (beside `docker-compose.yml`);\n`push-template.sh` forwards `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` so the templates declare\nthe same id. Link that provider to **both** templates."],[906,"The pattern the profile pushes you toward is a named alias for anything unstructured.\n`scripts/lib/mcp_lane_report.py` reads TOML and JSON, so rather than widen a\nsignature it declares the two document shapes and narrows with `isinstance`:"],[1012,"The payload gate: which dashboard mistakes the build catches before a deploy does"],[79,"The per-owner grok state volumes"],[1131,"The per-user state volumes, the per-workspace `config.toml`, and what each boot rewrites"],[1594,"The picker: least-busy"],[1694,"The pin idles out after 1 h (3600 s) without a request, and every request refreshes it.\nIt bounds idle time between requests, not conversation length."],[1740,"The pin itself is kept."],[1595,"The pin: session_affinity"],[3007,"The playbook is the trunk; the environment is inventory:"],[1434,"The point of the assistant is that the operator leaves the desk and the box keeps\nworking for them. Four doors reach the same agent, the same seeded config, the same\nskills and the same memory store; which one is open is a decision in `.env`, not a\nrebuild."],[1645,"The policy is scoped to the Spark lanes. `coder` (Arc), `chat` and `compaction-fast`\n(the Arc group a summary hops to) keep the plain `num_retries` path."],[2727,"The profile carries two numbers and the gap between them is the diagnosis:"],[3001,"The project name (`-p`) is what isolates networks, volumes and container names, which is how two\nenvironments share a host. Every published port has to be a value for that to work:\n`\"${APP_PORT:-3000}:3000\"`. Optional services go behind a profile:"],[2024,"The provider owns the `EVO-T1` folder, so nothing you do in the browser to a shipped panel\nsurvives: `allowUiUpdates: false` refuses the save, `disableDeletion: true` refuses the\ndelete, and the 60-second poll quietly undoes whatever the UI let you stage. A board you\nbuild from scratch is not thrown away - it lands in Grafana's own database on `grafana-data`"],[275,"The provider requires a parameter default to be one of its options, which is why a\ncustom `--var` value is injected as its own option instead of being dropped."],[1757,"The proxy's own `/health` lists every deployment, so trust it over the absence of\nboot errors."],[396,"The prune matches the `sweep-`-prefixed names this function gives itself, at one level of\n`~/.grok/stale-swaps/` and no deeper, so a person who reads this page and parks a file in\nthat directory keeps it."],[2882,"The public backend is the same hop as the other names. Replay it from a\nworkspace, which reaches the published router and skips Cloudflare, Traefik and\nAuthelia:"],[1509,"The public name is opt-in (`hermes-*`, which `auto` composes from the naming parts or\nan explicit list pins). It does not change what the container can do: local terminal,\nhost mounts and the docker socket still mean this agent can affect the real host.\nTreat its approvals the way you treat a root shell. That is the same standing as\npublic Coder."],[2862,"The push prints only the key each value came from, never the value\n(`scripts/push-template.sh:696-700`), so a CI log that shows\n`infisical_project_id passed from INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID in .env (value not shown)` proves\nthe key was read, not that it was right. `check` is the read that answers. The instance\nURL is the one vault value the log does print, because the push composes it rather than\nforwarding it: a workspace is handed `http://host.docker.internal:<INFISICAL_PORT>`\n(`scripts/push-template.sh:652-661`), never the host's own `INFISICAL_URL`."],[1674,"The Qwen2.5-Coder weights do not emit structured tool calls on this runtime."],[816,"The raw `bazelisk query` predicates for when a verb does not fit"],[781,"The reason the authored heading is the only title: the page header used to print the frontmatter\ntitle and the body printed its own, so every page opened with the same string twice."],[2715,"The reason the prompt keeps coming back is that the file is being hand-edited at all.\nThis workspace re-renders its own copy on every start, so an edit made there survives\nuntil that workspace restarts and touches no other workspace - no sibling's boot\noverwrites it any more. The keys live in the template instead:\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent`, `grok_subagents_lane`, and `grok_config_extra`.\ndocs/coder-templates.md#stale-editor-swap-files."],[1128,"The rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`: model aliases and role fallbacks"],[1096,"The rendered `~/.grok` config"],[2483,"The rendered width is below fleet-width"],[2821,"The repair is also one command, and every `push would FAIL` line names it:"],[1121,"The repo-scoped runbook"],[637,"The repository cache is the one worth persisting in CI, and `EVO_T1_BAZEL_CACHE=<host dir>`\nis the seam: `//scripts:test-in-image` bind-mounts that directory into the gate container and\npoints `--repository_cache` at it, so each external archive downloads once per key. Each CI\ngate step exports it as `${RUNNER_TEMP}/bazel-repo-cache` and is wrapped in an `actions/cache`\npair keyed on `MODULE.bazel`, `MODULE.bazel.lock` and `.bazelrc`, with the save under\n`if: always()` — the archives fetched\nbefore a red gate are exactly what the next run would otherwise pay for again. That endpoint\nmatters: `github.com/.../releases/download/...` answers 504 for a slice of requests, one such\n504 aborts Bazel's analysis, and a refetch-on-every-job job decides its colour by luck. The\nin-container path is passed as an explicit option because the `~` in `.bazelrc` expands from\nthe Bazel server's own `user.home`, which this container's uid does not appear in: a mount\nplaced from that `~` could sit where Bazel never writes and look like a working cache for\nexactly one run. With the variable unset the script mounts nothing, which is what a\ncontributor laptop gets."],[359,"The repository leaf is derived the same way in `main.tf` and in `startup.sh.tftpl`:\nsplit on `/`, drop empty segments, take the last, drop a trailing `.git`, map every\ncharacter outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` to `-`, and fall back to `repo` when that leaves no\nleading alphanumeric. Both derivations must agree, or the editor and the terminal\nhelpers point at a folder that was never cloned."],[418,"The repository URLs arrive as rendered literals and are read through a *quoted* heredoc,\nso no shell quoting has to survive and the value is only ever expanded as data. An empty\nGit repository field means a home-directory workspace, so the whole clone stage is\nskipped rather than attempted with an empty URL."],[2396,"The rest"],[1461,"The result fails closed: no credentials, no dashboard. There is no unauthenticated\nread-only mode to fall back to."],[2366,"the router's vhost table, whose token set is exactly the seven vhosts it answers\n(`__EVO_HOST_INFISICAL__` among them: a name that will not compose refuses the render\nrather than writing half a table): `proxy/router.conf.template` is rendered into\n`proxy/router.gen.conf` (gitignored) and `STACK_ROUTER_CONF=router.gen.conf` is\nwritten, because compose mounts `./proxy/${STACK_ROUTER_CONF:-router.conf}`. The\n`warn_missing_router_hosts` check runs against that rendered file, so a name the\ntable does not answer is reported before the recreate rather than as a `421` after\nit."],[2331,"The router's whole job is picking an upstream from the Host header, and an unmatched\nHost answers `421` rather than a guess, so a mistyped or rewritten Host fails loudly\ninstead of landing in the wrong service. A rewritten Host matches no server block\nhere, so it also arrives as `421`."],[880,"The rule"],[855,"The rule class is the part that saves the round trip. `//docs-site:app` looks like the site's test\ntarget and is a `filegroup`, so `bazelisk test` on it fails to start; the real test targets are the\n`sh_test` rows. The same listing filtered by a name fragment is how you find a Bats suite's target\nwithout knowing the stem convention:"],[887,"The rule in one sentence, and the two gates that hold it"],[1879,"The rule that ties it together: **a check that silently skips is a bug**. Two existing\nrepositories print `tool not found ... Skipping` and then exit zero, and a pre-commit config in\nanother wraps every hook in `command -v ... || exit 0`. Nothing in the scaffold may do that, and\nthe scaffold's own `.self-check/` suite is what holds the line."],[1105,"The rule the suite exists to enforce"],[1193,"The rulebook (`~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, from\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`) states the ceiling as that key rather than as a\nnumber of its own: the prompt used to hardcode a smaller fleet and to tell the parent to\nrun two or three children per wave, and both are gone, because a cap the prompt invents is\nwrong on both lanes - it leaves slots empty on a swarm session and overbooks a pinned\none. Overflow is held, not failed (`limit_behavior = \"queue\"`), so an under-wide wave is\nsilent and counting it stays the parent's job. The same grep prints the `max_depth` line\nbeside `max_concurrent`, and that key is now rendered from `grok_subagent_max_depth`\n(default 2, validated 1..3) by `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:429`. At the\nshipped 2 a child may spawn: `reviewer`, `security-auditor` and `tester` name\n`spawn_subagent` in their `tools`, so a skeptic can put a wave of read-only mappers under\nitself and get its evidence without a round trip through the parent. Each grandchild is its\nown session, so it is not charged to the slot count above; what it does cost is a model\nstream, which is why a tree is counted by summing the in-flight `1 + spawns` over every\ntask and keeping that total at or below the fleet's stream count (`fleet:` in the plan, 15\nby default). `max_depth = 1` is the rollback: no child spawns, and one parent's slot count\nagain describes the whole tree."],[1565,"The rules a change follows"],[2252,"The rules that shape every write in this subsystem, inherited from\n`scripts/dashboard-password.sh`:"],[1902,"The run exits 0 when the render succeeded -- a refused GitHub step is a `warning:`, not a failure --\nand 1 on a render failure, a bad argument, or a scaffold that cannot be fetched."],[2159,"The runner appends the profile argv, so the three services come up with the rest of the\nproject. A profile selects services; it does not delete what is already running, so\nflipping the key back off needs a `down` (or deleting the three by name) before the\ncontainers actually go away. Their named volumes survive that, and `reset` is the verb\nthat destroys them."],[2964,"The safety lines that never move"],[976,"The same \"empty looks like green\" rule applies to the vitest gate: it must measure\nfiles no test imported. Vitest 4 made that the default and removed the `all` key\nfrom `CoverageOptions`. To prove the default still holds after a vitest bump, drop\na `lib/__cov_probe.ts` exporting one uncalled function and confirm the run reports\nit at 0% and fails; then delete the file."],[2566,"The same command aimed at the dev stack, so with `dev-stack` and its default\nproject name (`evo-t1-dev`):"],[2547,"The same command with `dev-stack` brings the dev project up. A leftover\n`homepage-proxy` container from the failed create is recreated on that `up`."],[907,"The same file writes its two fixed vocabularies as `Literal`s, which is what makes a\ntypo in a check name a gate failure rather than a line an operator never sees. A\nrecord that travels between functions gets a `NamedTuple`, a `@dataclass`, or a\n`TypedDict` — `tests/bazel_deps.py` names its Starlark map `LabelMap` and its\n`drift()` result `Drift`, and `tests/coverage/py_report.py` types the slice of\n`coverage report --json` it reads with three `total=False` TypedDicts."],[2738,"The same finding arrives by itself at every start: the profile stage of the boot compares\nthe two numbers right after re-materializing the config\n(`boot_stage 6 \"the Grok Build profile\"`, `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl:224`,\nthe comparison at `:259-271`, same lines in the devcontainer twin) and prints through\n`boot_note` (`templates/common/boot-log.lib.sh:51`), so the workspace start log carries\n`note: subagents width 4 is below the fleet's 15 - a value that outlived its template,\nunless you set it on purpose`."],[1035,"the same group's links: every href rides a `{{HOMEPAGE_VAR_*}}` token and none carries\na URL literal (Loki, Tempo and Mimir publish no port, so their two hrefs open\n`{{HOMEPAGE_VAR_GRAFANA_URL}}/explore` rather than a store nobody can reach), and no\nprobe bakes a loopback or LAN address"],[2559,"The same MagicDNS-only resolver is what makes Terraform inside the `coder`\ncontainer SERVFAIL `registry.terraform.io` (`127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`).\nThat spelling lives under \"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI\" below. Compose\npins public resolvers on `coder` so a template push does not wait on this host\nfix."],[2669,"The same probe in the other spelling: `test -x ~/.grok/bin/grok`."],[60,"The same push, by hand"],[1103,"The saved workflows"],[1886,"The scaffold is fetched before anything is rendered: a shallow (`--depth 1`), single-branch clone\nof `--template-ref` into a scratch directory an EXIT trap removes. `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` and an\nempty credential helper keep a scaffold that turned private from hanging on a prompt nobody can\nanswer inside a workspace. A fetched tree with no `template.yml` at its root is a hard failure --\nthat manifest belongs to the scaffold repository, so pick a ref that has it. The new repository's\nfirst commit records what it was built from: `- Scaffold: <url>@<sha>.`, or\n`- Scaffold: local tree <dir>.` for a `--template-dir` render."],[1920,"The scaffold's `.self-check/` suite, in that repository and run by its own CI, owns the template's\ninternal invariants: every token the template uses is one this script declares, every lane ships\nits `LANE.md` plus `gitignore.fragment` pair, every tool is pinned rather than `@latest`, the\nworkflows declare `permissions`, the docs links resolve, and the pins cover the lanes present."],[647,"The scan treats a comment, a here-doc body, a log message, and a scratch file a suite writes\nas prose rather than a dependency, so the maps record reads and not mentions. When the scan\ngenuinely cannot see a pairing (a lib that builds a path and returns it as a string, a\n`case` glob over `git diff` output, help text), the entry goes in the `_ALLOW` map with the\nreason as a comment above the key; the Bats gate fails an `_ALLOW` key that has no reason."],[179,"The script mints a short-lived token by inserting a row into the stack's own Postgres."],[1210,"The seam above is what a fan-out costs; this is who is in it. The family has its own seeding\n`coder_script`, `stack_fleet` (`templates/workspace/main.tf:753`,\n`templates/devcontainer/main.tf:804`), which puts thirteen payloads in place on every start\nfrom `templates/common/`, and `//scripts:seed-dev-env` seeds the same thirteen into a\ncontributor home through the `~/.grok/agents`, `~/.grok/personas` and fleet-skill rows of\n`seed_seed_targets` (`scripts/lib/config_seed.sh:130-147`, seventeen of them; the\n`documenter.md` row at 136 sitting in the middle belongs to the advisor family below). The\nbody that writes them is\n`templates/common/stack-fleet.sh.tftpl`, its own body for the same one-argument reason the\ntwo skill families have theirs\n(coder-templates.md). Thirteen is six agent\ndefinitions, three persona overlays, and four parent-side skills."],[1132,"The search lane and the MCP servers behind it"],[2922,"The second cause is the logging driver: a container not using `json-file` has no such\nfile to tail, so its logs are missing from Loki while every other service's arrive. The\ncollector's own log names which files it opened:"],[1253,"The secret guard's generic assignment scan requires a digit on the matched line, which is\nwhat keeps a variable name from reading as a credential. The cost runs in both directions:\n`api_key = XyZaBcDeFgHiJk` (a name the list carries, no digit) walks through, and so does\n`github_token_string = Abcd1234EFGH5678` (a digit, a name the list does not carry). Only the\nfive known-format scans (AWS, GitHub, Slack, Google, private-key block) plus the\ncredential-bearing URL are exact, and they run on the text before the environment-reference\nfold, so a vendor key pasted inside `getenv(\"...\")` still denies. What the fold does excuse\nis entropy sitting in the name position of a reference - `os.environ/KEY_XyZaBc12345678` -\nbecause that position is where the correct pattern lives and the guard's whole job is to\nkeep it writable. A missed shape is a line in `hooks.secret-guard.sh`, not a reason to trust\nthe ones it does catch."],[2537,"The secrets vault: the bring-up refresh, the workspace boot fetch's notes, and what it refuses to publish"],[473,"The seed also pins `terminal.integrated.cwd` and `terminal.integrated.splitCwd` to the\nopened folder, adds a `terminal.integrated.env.linux` block, and carries a\n`commandsToSkipShell` list - all described in the sibling doc."],[466,"The seed in git carries no API key. Cline and Roo read their key out of `settings.json`\nand have no `env_key` field, so when `LITELLM_API_KEY` is set the startup script injects\nit into the seed once (`jq`, falling back to `python3`) and nowhere else."],[1502,"The seed is a template for one key: `${STACK_HOST_PATH}` in `terminal.cwd` is rendered\nto a literal path on the way into the volume, from `--cwd`, then `STACK_HOST_PATH`,\nthen `/opt/stack` (announced when it falls back). `${LITELLM_MASTER_KEY}` stays a token\non purpose - Hermes expands it at boot, and a byte of the key in a volume copy is what\nMounts and \"be the host\" argues against."],[2891,"The seed sets `dashboard.trusted_proxies` to `[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]` so Hermes\nhonours `X-Forwarded-Proto` (Secure cookie and `wss`). A `hermes-data` volume\nfrom before that seed keeps the old file until you reseed. `<mount>` is the\nhost directory of that volume:"],[1496,"The seeded config sets `approvals.mode: manual`. `cron_mode` and `unattended_mode` are\nboth `deny`, so a scheduled or unattended run cannot approve anything either."],[1098,"The seeded fleet suite"],[1130,"The seeded fleet suite: six definitions, three personas, four skills, the five advisor\ncommands, the seeded guard hooks, and the skeptic that closes every multi-file change"],[1368,"The seeds live per workspace, on that workspace's own home volume, and seeding\ndoes not care whether the three shared state mounts exist."],[2631,"The self-hosted SearXNG MCP stdio server is the search lane, declared by default\n(`search_mcp` defaults to true). Grok's native `web_search` is parked: the render sets\n`disable_web_search = true`, because no model transport this box ships serves that\ntool type - LiteLLM's `agent` group answers `tools of type 'web_search' are not\nsupported: this server runs function tools only` (HTTP 400) before anything is\nfetched, and `supports_backend_search = false` does not route around it. An agent that\nstill spends a turn on a native search call and comes back with that message behaved\nas configured; there is no native lane to fall back to. Two probes split a SearXNG\nfault in half:"],[1189,"The self-teardown family is denied alongside it, because an agent that closes its own\nterminal cannot finish the task that closed it. `tmux kill-server` / `kill-session` /\n`kill-window` / `kill-pane` / `restart-server`, `pkill` and `killall` naming `tmux`,\n`node`, `code-server`, `grok` or `bash`, those killers aimed at a whole user or parent\n(`pkill -u`, `-U`, `-P`), and the shell forms that signal a whole process group\n(`kill 0`, `kill -- -<pgid>`, `kill -9 -1`) each match a `deny` rule. The reason is the\nshape of the workspace: the Bash tool runs inside the tmux session the agent was\nlaunched in, so a sweep that tidies up \"stray\" sessions or processes takes the agent,\nits tool shell and the in-flight reply down together, and the editor tab dies with\n`terminated with exit code: 1` over work left half-done. An agent stops one process it\nstarted with the `kill_command_or_subagent` tool; bringing down a whole tmux server or\neditor host stays an operator call. These are globs over the parsed command text, so\nan abbreviated tmux verb (`tmux kill-sess`) or a bracketed pattern (`pkill -f [t]mux`)\nwalks past them - the seed rule that names the prohibition is the other half, and the\nplain `bash` profile is how a session reaches a shell once they are all gone."],[747,"The sentinel outranks every other signal, so `new-workspace` answers `workspace` for this\nrepo and a Coder workspace of it never builds the contributor container on its DinD\nsidecar."],[2197,"The sequence, in this order. Each step names what it changes."],[2578,"The server on each Spark box logs four shapes, and three of them are this stack's own\ntraffic rather than anything wrong with the box. Read the shape before you reach for\nthe box."],[222,"The service appears twice by necessity: the first `coder` selects the compose service, the\nsecond is the CLI binary inside it."],[1382,"The sessions above are tmux panes, which means they outlive the browser tab that\nopened them and are visible to nothing else. `//scripts:coder-sessions` is the door\nfor the operator who is not at the desk:"],[2352,"The shape is load-bearing: each name is **one label under the zone**, and each service\nprefix joins it with a **hyphen**. A wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so\nthe dotted two-label form (`coder.gmktecbeast.`) has no certificate at the public edge:\nthe browser aborts with `ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` and Traefik never sees the\nrequest. Measured on this box - `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` completes the handshake\nwith the zone's `*.overeazy.io` cert, while the dotted form fails with a TLS alert 40\nand presents no certificate at all."],[409,"The shapes, verbatim from `templates/common/boot-log.lib.sh:22-63`:"],[2267,"The shell, function, script, template and naming rules this repo holds itself to, and the gates that enforce them."],[2918,"The shipped datasources carry fixed UIDs (`prometheus`, `mimir`, `loki`, `tempo`) that\nevery shipped dashboard references, so a datasource you rebuilt by hand in the UI is a\ndatasource no panel will ever query. Diff anything suspicious against\n`observability/grafana/provisioning/datasources/stack.yaml`: that file is the record of\nwhat the working config is."],[2722,"The shipped default puts the writers on the unpinned `agent-swarm` lane, where the\nleast-busy picker spreads them over every box and the 15 is the fleet's real width\n(3 boxes x `PARALLEL` 5). Four keys come from that one variable: `general-purpose`,\n`implementer`, `documenter`, `tester`. If those read `agent`, the template was pushed\nwith `--var grok_subagents_lane=agent`, which is the documented rollback to one box."],[789,"The sidebar is not read from the filesystem: most pages sit flat in `docs/` while belonging to\ndifferent sections, and the reading order a prev/next footer needs is an editorial decision. The\ntree is declared once in `NAV` inside `docs-site/scripts/gen_nav.py` and generated into\n`docs-site/lib/nav.json`, which the page-tree builder imports and a Zod schema validates."],[1558,"The sidebar tabs divide the rest: **Learn** covers how the pieces work, **Operate** covers\nrunning the box day to day, and **Contribute** covers the gates a change runs through.\nEach subject has exactly one page, and the README links to that page rather than\nrepeating it."],[664,"The single golden image, and the file that pins it"],[775,"The site is a Fumadocs app that reads `docs/` straight out of the repository, so the markdown\nhere is the source of record: nothing is copied into `docs-site/`, and the README's links keep\npointing at these files. Most of the chrome is derived from the prose rather than authored in\nit, which is what keeps 20-odd pages consistent."],[1099,"The six agent definitions"],[2530,"The six never come up after setting the flag -> which env file that project reads"],[430,"The six paths it probes are the ones `@devcontainers/cli` looks in: `devcontainer.json`\nand `devcontainer.yml` at the root, under `.devcontainer/`, and under\n`.github/devcontainers/`."],[1706,"The sixth and seventh Spark groups, `agent-explore` and `agent-review`, lift the same pin\nthe swarm lane lifts, and for the same reason taken one step further: both serve a child\nthat answers once. The `explore` type and the seeded `explorer` agent dig for evidence and\nreturn a digest; `reviewer` and `security-auditor` read what landed and return findings.\nNeither carries a long warm prefix into a next turn, so the prefix-cache warmth the pin\nprotects is worth nothing here, while its cost is paid in full - the parent loop and every\nchild it spawns carry that workspace's one `x-litellm-session-id`, so a pinned wave of six\nresolves to a single deployment and waits on that box's `PARALLEL` admission slots while\nthe other two boxes sit reachable and under-offered."],[156,"The size that matters is not `startup.sh.tftpl`. `main.tf` composes the body from that\ntemplate plus every `templates/common/` payload it inlines, so any edit to the rulebook,\nthe skills, the tmux config, the notes seeder, or the Grok config moves the number. That\nis how six commits crept from 104 KB to 130 KB and one documentation commit crossed the\nwall at 135 KB."],[1261,"The skill copies are **absent-only**, like the `ez-` families: a `SKILL.md` that\nalready exists belongs to the user and is never rewritten - the fleet's eight, and the\nadvisor family's twelve, which are five `SKILL.md`s plus `code_index.py` in each of the two\nhomes. The advisor family's `~/.grok/agents/documenter.md` rides that absent-only contract\nwith the skill it serves rather than the refresh the fleet's six keep, on the body's own\nreasoning that a stale piece of advice misleads one run while a stale guard decides what a\nsession may do."],[3050,"The skill is `skills/trunk-refactor/SKILL.md`. A session in this repository reaches it through\n`AGENTS.md`; any other repository gets it by installing it into the two skill homes the agents\nread:"],[2979,"The skill's twelve binary criteria, in prose. Each needs a command's output as proof."],[1671,"The Spark boxes serve TensorFold's OpenAI-compatible `/v1` and nothing else: Ollama\nruns on the Arc, so a Spark box never sees the Ollama protocol."],[2100,"The Spark exporters"],[1919,"The split follows the ownership: the scaffold's own invariants are checked in the scaffold\nrepository, and this repo checks only the renderer."],[1963,"The split is upstream's own: Prometheus is a fast local scratch store, Mimir is the same\nPromQL over longer retention, and Alloy is the single agent that touches the host\nfilesystem. This box needs all three because a container that dies takes its own log\nfile out of `docker compose logs` reach only if you are too late - but a workspace that\nwas slow at 03:00 is unrecoverable without retention."],[1704,"The spread is bought by removing a pin, not by adding a proxy queue or a cap. There is\nstill no `rpm`, `tpm` or `max_parallel_requests` anywhere in the config, and\n`queue_class`, `queue_class_config` and `enable_queue` do not exist at this tag. A\nsaturated box keeps shedding load in its own scheduler; the proxy parks nothing behind\nan in-memory semaphore."],[2004,"The stack host - the box the bundle itself runs on:"],[1524,"The stack runners take `up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`; `status` and `logs` are\nwhere a Hermes that will not bind its port is diagnosed first. Status across the whole\nbox, including this card, is in dashboard.md; the LAN and public\nrouting rules are in public-access.md, and the service inventory is\nin README.md."],[427,"The stage reads the *rendered* file, pulls every `command = \"...\"` value out of it, and\nnames the ones that are not executable files, in the same startup log that wrote the\nconfig. `sed` keeps only a double-quoted value and a TOML path carries no spaces, so an\nempty entry there is an odd line rather than a missing binary."],[937,"The stub-PATH recipe"],[944,"The stub-PATH recipe, and the two paths a suite may take"],[1042,"The suite also breaks each invariant in a scratch copy, so a check that has stopped\nfailing fails the build."],[800,"The suites that read a payload"],[2592,"The summary call rides the **session model**, the `agent*` thinking family, and\ninherits its output ceiling: captured on the wire as `max_tokens: 32768`, alongside 25\ntool schemas and the workspace's `x-litellm-session-id` affinity header.\n`[models] session_summary` is **not** that lane - it serves titles and the session\nrecap."],[220,"The supported push is `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`: no arguments pushes every\ntemplate, a template name retries just that one, and the image tag comes from `DEV_IMAGE`\nin `.env`. The raw equivalent below is what that script does internally — it streams the\nsame tar and makes the same CLI call the script makes for each template, execing into the\ndaemon's `coder` container by name."],[388,"The swap file's own persistence turns a killed editor into a permanent nuisance. Open\n`vim ~/.grok/config.toml`, stop the workspace with the editor still up, and the agent's\nprocess tree dies while the swap file stays on the home volume. The next `vim` on that name stops with `E325: ATTENTION` and a question about a\nsession whose container has been gone for days, and because \"(E)dit anyway\" leaves the\nswap in place and mints `.swo`, `.swn`, `.swm` beside it, an operator who keeps answering\nthe prompt ends up with four of them and a prompt that never goes away."],[1596,"The swarm lane: `agent-swarm`"],[185,"The tar is built from a throwaway staging copy, not from the template dir itself.\n`.terraform/` is excluded (pushing it would put the plan's provider binaries into the\nversion's Filestore row), symlinks are dumped as their targets (`tar -h`), and the\n`templates/common/` payloads are copied in beside `main.tf`. A staged copy that still\nholds a symlink is refused before the CLI runs."],[1924,"The ten profile-gated monitoring services: what each one does, how metrics, logs and traces reach Grafana, how an alert reaches Alertmanager, where each config file lives, what the data costs on disk, and what is deliberately left out."],[2899,"The ten profile-gated services (`prometheus`, `mimir`, `loki`, `tempo`, `grafana`,\n`alloy`, `node_exporter`, `cadvisor`, `blackbox_exporter`, `alertmanager`) fail quietly,\nbecause a monitoring stack that stopped collecting still answers\n`/ready`. The shape of every entry below is the same: the read path (Grafana) and the\nwrite path (Prometheus -> Mimir, Alloy -> Loki) are separate, so decide which side is\nempty before you touch either. What each piece is for and where its config lives is in\nobservability.md."],[1926,"The ten services"],[2460,"The TensorFold log is full of warnings, or `resumed at 0` -> read the line shapes first"],[1515,"The terminal door adds a way in for someone who already has a shell on this host, and\nopens no new listener, no new port and no new credential. `hermes-shell` authenticates\nnothing of its own: `docker` on that host is the credential, and it was always\nroot-equivalent here. Its one guard is that the assistant's approvals stay `manual` on\nboth doors."],[1364,"The text of 1-4 is the orchestration protocol (fan-out / fan-in, the edit-conflict gate,\nprobe-one-worker-first, verify-what-a-worker-actually-produced, the git cadence that\ncuts the branch before the first edit and commits each unit as it lands, holding the push\nfor the pull request, and the issue etiquette that reads the report before planning, puts a\nsingle closing keyword in the pull request body, and leaves the actual close to the merge).\nIt travels as\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, staged beside the staged `main.tf` and\nread back with `file()` / `filebase64()`; `templates/common/` keeps one copy for\nboth templates, and the push stages it into each template's pushed copy."],[2002,"The thirteen dashboards are provisioned, so they appear on a fresh Grafana in the\n`EVO-T1` folder with no clicking - the section below names each one and the question it\nanswers. The LiteLLM board is the one that depends on a credential: its metrics sit behind\n`LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` like every other LiteLLM route, and the bearer Prometheus presents is\nthe file Compose rendered from that key, so anything that puts the two ends out of step -\na key rotated in the env file with `prometheus` never recreated, a token file that is\nmissing or wrong - leaves every panel on that board reading empty while the others stay\nfull."],[2143,"The three `secrets`-profile containers, and the CLI verb that reads the flag"],[2403,"The three commands - plan, prove, install - plus one box, JSON, and the target document"],[2119,"The three DGX Sparks are not scrape targets until you make them one. Each box needs two\ncontainers - node_exporter on `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (9100) and the NVIDIA DCGM\nexporter on `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` (9400) - and neither exists on a box until\n`--apply` creates it:"],[2118,"The three exporters feeding those rules sit beside the bundle on the same\n`observability` profile, all published to `127.0.0.1` only: node_exporter\n(`quay.io/prometheus/node-exporter:v1.12.1`) on `OBS_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (9101), cAdvisor\n(`ghcr.io/google/cadvisor:v0.61.0`) on `OBS_CADVISOR_PORT` (9290), and blackbox_exporter\n(`prom/blackbox-exporter:v0.29.1`) on `OBS_BLACKBOX_PORT` (9115). The data path behind\nthem, and what each config file costs to change, is\nobservability.md."],[2406,"The three failures worth recognising from the CLI's own words"],[2637,"The three MCP servers (`github`, `context7`, `searxng`) ship by default, so a lane\nmissing from a session has one of three shapes: the block absent from the rendered\nconfig, the `command=` binary absent from the golden image, or the credential absent.\nRun `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` first - it reports the declared lanes, their\nbinaries, their credentials and their handshake in one pass, and it names which shape\nyou are in (an undeclared lane is a failure, a missing binary a warning).\n`grok mcp doctor` inside the workspace lists the declared servers and their handshake.\nA *park* is deliberate and looks like this: `GITHUB_MCP=false` / `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` /\n`SEARCH_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed by `push-template.sh`) or\n`--var github_mcp=false` / `--var context7_mcp=false` / `--var search_mcp=false` on a\ntemplate push. With none of those set, a missing block is a defect, not a park."],[936,"The three paths a suite may use"],[1100,"The three persona overlays"],[1120,"The three repository-conversion commands"],[1668,"The three rows after `agent-xhigh` differ from the four above them in routing state\nonly: same model, same three boxes, the thinking level each row names, and no session\npin. See\nthe swarm lane and\nthe fan-out lanes."],[2146,"The three runners that refresh the env file from the vault before compose renders"],[1901,"The three scaffold flags exist so the source can be pointed elsewhere without editing anything:\nthe env pair covers a mirror or a fork for a whole deployment, and `--template-dir` renders a tree\nyou already hold, which is the offline route and the one the hermetic tests use."],[2130,"The three services"],[1119,"The three stack-seeded skill commands"],[1328,"The thresholds are separate as well. `context_limit` above is Coder's own compaction\nbudget with its 70 per cent default, while the CLI fires at\n`[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` (70) of its 262144-token window."],[293,"The toggle is `mutable = false`: turning it on or off means a workspace **rebuild**,\nbecause a network, a volume and a second container appear or vanish. The preset makes\nthat one click."],[181,"The token is scoped to minutes (`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, default 10), deleted on the\nway out including on a failed or interrupted run, never printed, and minted **once** for\nthe whole run however many templates it covers."],[618,"The toolchain Bazel drives comes from one Linux golden image, `images/dev/Dockerfile`, and\nthe contributor path is that image rather than a set of tools installed on your machine:"],[2527,"The traces dashboard is empty and Tempo logs a 404 once a minute -> Prometheus has no write receiver"],[1697,"The trade is deliberate: stickiness buys prefix-cache locality and one predictable box\nfor the whole conversation, so one workspace's agent loop can sit on a busy box."],[2322,"The Traefik file-provider config the other box needs, pasted as-is"],[581,"The Traefik on the other box owns TLS for those names and must forward plain\nHTTP to this box's router container **with the original Host header intact** -\n`STACK_ROUTER_CONF` names the conf that container mounts (`proxy/router.gen.conf`,\nrendered by bootstrap from `proxy/router.conf.template`, or the checked-in\n`proxy/router.conf` while the key is empty), and it picks the service from\n`$http_host` on `STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` (default 8080). A rewritten Host comes back\nas `421`.\nThat box needs one file, and the paste-ready version of it - one `loadBalancer`\nat `:8080`, a router per name, Authelia on the six interactive names, the\nlong-lived timeouts - is\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."],[1179,"The twelve aliases (`agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`, `agent-swarm`,\n`agent-explore`, `agent-review`, `agent-xhigh-fast`, `agent-swarm-fast`, `coder`,\n`coder-fast`, `chat`) are defined with `base_url` set to `litellm_url` and\n`env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"`. The key is injected as an environment variable, so\nit never appears in a file. `agent` carries three\n`[[model.\"agent\".reasoning_efforts]]` tables (`xhigh` is the default). The\n`agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` / `agent-swarm` / `agent-explore` /\n`agent-review` / `agent-xhigh-fast` / `agent-swarm-fast` aliases set\n`supports_reasoning_effort = false` because LiteLLM bakes the level for callers\nthat have no reasoning-effort field. The `agent-explore` / `agent-review` pair is the\nreader and the skeptic: the `explore` type and the seeded `explorer` ride\n`agent-explore` (`low`), and the seeded\n`reviewer` and `security-auditor` ride `agent-review` (`xhigh`). What each lifts, what\nit costs, and the `--var` that rolls it back are in\nlitellm-routing.md.\nThe `agent-xhigh-fast` / `agent-swarm-fast` pair is their parent lane with one call of the\nconversation answered elsewhere: on those two aliases only, the proxy callback\n`litellm/compaction_router.py` re-serves the CLI's conversation-summary call from the Arc\ngroup `compaction-fast` and leaves every other call of the session on its Spark box.\nNothing in either `[model.*]` block names that target, because no client key could - the\nhop is server-side, and it forks on `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` (24000 tokens\nestimated): within it the summary goes to the Arc group, above it to the Spark group\n`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` (default `agent-low`), because the Arc weights\nanswer inside a 32768-token window and a bigger input would be truncated into a useless\nsummary of the history the call exists to preserve. Both are selectable as\n`grok_default_model` and neither is a Coder Agents chat model. The mechanism, the five\n`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION*` knobs, and the 107 s to 1915 s Spark-side compactions that\nmotivated it are in\nlitellm-routing.md."],[2133,"The twelve managed keys"],[2145,"The twelve managed keys, who consumes each one, and what breaks when it is wrong"],[801,"The twin template copies"],[2073,"The two commands"],[2078,"The two commands an operator runs most"],[2401,"The two containers that make a Spark a Prometheus target, and the fact that neither\nexists until you run `--apply`"],[1771,"The two fan-out lanes are absent from that list on purpose. `agent-explore` and\n`agent-review` exist to be pinned by `[subagents.models]`, not chosen from a picker, so\nediting them moves a fleet role's traffic and leaves the Agents dropdowns alone."],[1746,"The two fan-out lanes degrade the way the swarm lane does, one step further than the\npinned lanes, and each hops through the lane that bakes its own level:\n`agent-explore: [\"agent-low\", \"coder\"]` and `agent-review: [\"agent-xhigh\", \"coder\"]`\n(`litellm/config.yaml:882-883`). The extra step is worth taking for the same reason -\nthese groups' own three deployments can all be cooling while the same boxes are healthy\nunder the pinned alias, since health state and cooldowns are kept per group - and the\nhop is chosen so a parent that dispatched an explore child at `low` gets a `low` answer\nfrom the fallback rather than an `xhigh` it did not ask for. Both chains are acyclic and\nend at `coder`: agent-explore -> agent-low -> coder, agent-review -> agent-xhigh ->\ncoder, and neither fallback lane names a lane that hops back here."],[1772,"The two fast-compaction lanes are absent for a mechanical reason of the same kind: the\nhop keys on the summary instruction the Grok CLI appends, and the aibridge behind those\ndropdowns never sends one, so an Agents chat on `agent-xhigh-fast` is a chat on\n`agent-xhigh` under a longer name. They are a CLI and a workspace-form choice instead."],[1619,"The two fast-compaction lanes, and the proxy callback that moves one summary call of\na session onto a cheaper tier"],[941,"The two inventory gates that tie a production function to a test"],[1647,"The two levers that actually move load off a shedding box:"],[641,"The two maps in `scripts/BUILD.bazel` and the one in `tests/BUILD.bazel` are the\nmachine-readable record of which repo file each target reads:"],[1108,"The two modes"],[768,"The two opening lists that become the scan card, and the order they have to sit in"],[512,"The two passes"],[522,"The two passes of `update-configs`, and the flags that select one"],[69,"The two questions"],[207,"The two research lanes split by question type: a library, framework, CLI or setup\nquestion goes to `context7` (resolve the library id first, then query docs on that\nid), anything else on the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool, and a URL already\nin hand goes to `searxng`'s `fetch_content`. Grok's native `web_search` is parked\nhere, and native `web_fetch` is unavailable because that tool needs an egress proxy\nendpoint this stack does not run, so `SEARCH_MCP=false` leaves a session with library\ndocs and no way to reach the web."],[722,"The two research lanes that config can render are split by question type: a library,\nframework, CLI or setup question goes to `context7` (resolve the library id first, then\nquery docs on that id), anything else on the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool,\nand a URL already in hand goes to `searxng`'s `fetch_content` - Grok's native\n`web_search` is parked here and native `web_fetch` needs an egress proxy endpoint this\nstack does not run, so neither is a lane. Prove what this home declares with:"],[1669,"The two rows after those are not new lanes. `agent-xhigh-fast` is `agent-xhigh` and\n`agent-swarm-fast` is `agent-swarm`, deployment for deployment, and what differs is\nwhere one call of the conversation is answered - the CLI's summary request, which a\nproxy callback re-serves from `compaction-fast` on the Arc when the summary fits that\nwindow, and from a Spark group at thinking `low` when it does not. See\nthe fast-compaction lanes."],[354,"The two terminal-profile tmux helpers are written by their own\n`coder_script \"terminal_launchers\"` body, which runs after this one returns (see\nbelow). Nothing at boot executes either script -- the code-server terminal profile is\nthe only consumer -- so landing them a step later costs an operator nothing, and each\nbody names itself in the start log. The default one gives each visible terminal its\nown pane: it re-joins a folder's session only while nothing is attached to it, and\notherwise opens a numbered sibling."],[878,"The typing rule for every language in this repo: Python under mypy with no Any, TypeScript under the strictest flags it compiles, Terraform variables with a declared constraint, and what bash and awk owe instead."],[853,"The uncached pass is not replaced. `bazelisk test` replays a cached PASS for an action whose inputs\ndid not change, so the derived list proves your change against whatever actually rebuilds, and one\n`bazelisk test //:test-fast --nocache_test_results` remains the pass you owe before the PR, per\nbuilding-with-bazel.md. Use the derived list for the loop and keep the full\nuncached pass for the end, instead of paying for sixty suites to learn one thing per edit."],[1801,"The unlock itself, step by step, from the operator's own machine"],[1836,"The unlock key authorizes a root shell on a machine whose disks are still\nsealed, and the initramfs image holding it sits on an unencrypted `/boot`.\nAnyone who can read `/boot` can read that public key, so treat it as a\nseparate credential with its own purpose: a dedicated key whose private half\nlives on the laptop you bring to a reboot, not the key you push to every\nrepository. `UMASK=0077` keeps the image itself out of unprivileged hands, and\nthe initramfs host keys stay separate from `/etc/ssh` on purpose - they are\ngenerated per boot image, and sharing them would leak which key the sealed\nmachine answers to."],[379,"The unmanaged case is announced rather than silent, because the render is the only place\na workspace's model catalog and MCP lanes are ever written."],[2765,"The usual wrapper is `Failed to check server \"https://...\" for first user, is the URL\ncorrect and is coder accessible from your browser?`"],[1713,"The variables are declared in `templates/workspace/coder.tf:24-52` and its devcontainer\ntwin, and they are what fills `[subagents.models]` in\n`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl`. The fan-out width rides a different variable\nand does not follow any of these three: `grok_swarm_width` (default 15) is the width on\nevery lane, and `main.tf` renders it as exactly\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent = var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent != null ? var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent : var.grok_swarm_width`.\nThe lane decides placement, not width - what a pinned lane changes is where those 15 land,\nand one box only ever admits `PARALLEL` (5) of them. The workspace side of that seam is\ngrok-build-and-agents.md. A contributor home\nnames the same three lanes with `GROK_EXPLORE_LANE`, `GROK_PLAN_LANE` and\n`GROK_REVIEW_LANE` for `//scripts:seed-dev-env`. Neither lane is registered as a Coder\nAgents chat model, so a pin is the only way onto either one."],[215,"The vault itself is not this page's subject: the three compose services, the operator CLI,\nthe managed key inventory, the outage bounds and rotation are\noperate/secrets.md. What the templates do with what these keys\nforward is The Infisical boot fetch."],[2244,"The verb mints a fresh value, writes the **vault first**, and only touches `.env` once the\nvault accepted it - so there is no moment where `.env` holds a password the vault does not\nknow about. `rotate` names one managed key; naming anything outside the inventory is an\n`error:` and exit 1."],[833,"The verbs below are the shapes of those questions."],[407,"The verbs live in one payload, `templates/common/boot-log.lib.sh`, and both twins hand it\nto every exec'd body as the `boot_log_lib` key of that body's `templatefile()` map: the\nstartup body plus ten `coder_script`s in `workspace`\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:509-510`), and eleven in the `devcontainer` twin, which adds\none body, `dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`. The two bodies nothing execs, `settings.json.tftpl` and\n`grok-config.toml.tftpl`, take no such key."],[1471,"The verbs that do change the model: `/model <alias>` inside a session,\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-shell -- -m <alias>` from the host, which sets\n`HERMES_MODEL` in the container, and `hermes config set model.default <alias>` to\npersist it in the live config under `/opt/data`. `model.model` is the legacy\nspelling of that same value: the loader copies it to `default` only while the block\ncarries none, and drops it after that - which is the shape a persisted `/model`\nwrites. So after one in-session switch, `hermes config set model.model <alias>`\nprints a success line and changes nothing, and `hermes config get model.model`\nanswers \"Config key not set\" for the same reason even when a model is set; read it\nback with `hermes config get model.default`."],[2827,"The verdicts, and what each one costs you:"],[655,"The warm-up"],[366,"The watcher left at the end is what makes notes survive a recreate that lands *between*\nedits. The boot-time sync runs once, so anything written later lived solely inside that\ncontainer, which is how a whole workspace's notes went missing on a recreate."],[2737,"The whole surface, for a script or a CI step that wants the answer rather than the prose\n(`--help` prints the same):"],[872,"The whole-graph enumeration `unlisted` is built from, which is also the answer to \"how much of the\nrepo do the gates actually see\":"],[384,"The whole-profile volume this template used to mount over all of `~/.grok`,\n`grok-profile-<owner-id>`, is still mounted in `shared` mode, but **read-only** and at\n`~/.grok-legacy`, only so the first start after the split can hoist its `notes/`,\n`memory/` and `sessions/` across (`notes_hoist_legacy` in\n`templates/common/notes-setup.sh`). Nothing reads it afterwards, and a later `chore:`\nremoves that mount."],[1270,"The whole-profile volume this template used to mount over all of `~/.grok`,\n`grok-profile-<owner-id>`, is still mounted in `shared` mode, but **read-only** and at\n`~/.grok-legacy`, purely so the first start after that split can copy its `notes/`,\n`memory/` and `sessions/` across once: `notes_hoist_legacy` in\n`templates/common/notes-setup.sh` takes a concern only when the destination is missing\nor empty, and nothing reads that mount afterwards. A later `chore:` removes it."],[2585,"The window is still read passively on every run without the needle, from the server's\nown startup lines (`context_pairs` and `startup_estimate` in the report), so a box\nquietly rebuilt with a shorter window is still visible; the needle is the active\nconfirmation of it. `--no-tests` skips all four recipe probes for a quick read-only\npass, and that is what `//scripts:bootstrap` runs."],[2895,"The wizard only listens on `:3000` at first boot; after install, use `:4443`."],[2561,"The word the shell says it cannot find is the second tag. The same read would\nalso have executed anything written on a line of its own in that file, and it\nexported every other key there (`LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` included) into the\nenvironment of the `docker exec` calls."],[1490,"The working directory the agent runs in is `terminal.cwd`, and it has to be a literal\npath. Hermes validates it in `agent/runtime_cwd.py` with `expanduser()` alone, so a\n`${VAR}` left in the file is reported as `does not exist` and the agent works somewhere\nelse, while `hermes config get terminal.cwd` still prints the token because the config\n*loader* does expand `${VAR}` - the two paths disagree, and only the runtime one runs\ncommands. `hermes-bootstrap` renders the placeholder in the seeded `config.yaml` at\nseed time (`--cwd DIR` overrides what it renders), which is the part that fixes a\nlong-lived box. compose also sets `TERMINAL_CWD` from the same key, as the backstop for\na config that names no `terminal.cwd`: `apply_terminal_config_to_env` lets an explicit\n`terminal.cwd` in the file own that variable, so the file decides wherever it carries\none. Re-seed a volume that predates the render with\n`hermes-bootstrap --data <mount> --force`."],[2129,"The workspace boot fetch"],[289,"The workspace container attaches to **both** `bridge` and that private network; once a\n`networks_advanced` block appears the provider stops letting `network_mode` do the\nattaching, so `bridge` has to be listed explicitly."],[2629,"The workspace container must resolve `host.docker.internal`; the model lane reaches\nLiteLLM through that host gateway, and so does `LITELLM_BASE_URL`."],[2838,"Then check **how** the credential is sent, because the two GitHub endpoints disagree\nabout the scheme. `api.github.com` and `gh` accept `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. The\ngit data endpoint refuses it: one token answered `/user` with `200` and\n`<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack` with `401` plus\n`remote: invalid credentials` while the token rode a bearer header. git has to send it\nas **Basic** auth with the token as the password. The username text is not checked\n(measured on Coder v2.36.6: `toxicoder`, `x-access-token`, `somebody-else` and `octocat`\neach returned `200`), but an empty username or an empty password is refused, and the\nempty password is what produces the misleading `Invalid username or token. Password\nauthentication is not supported for Git operations.`"],[2837,"Then check the URL scheme, which no probe can fix: that credential covers\n`https://github.com/...` remotes only, so a `git@github.com:` or `ssh://` URL gets\nneither helper nor askpass token and needs an SSH key you placed yourself, which this\nstack provisions nowhere."],[564,"Then connect your GitHub account in Coder (Settings -> Tokens / external auth)\nand link the same provider to **both** workspace templates (Templates -> edit\n-> External Auth)."],[488,"Then connect your GitHub account. On a running workspace, click **GitHub reauth** on\nthe agent bar (Coder v2.36's `/external-auth/<id>` page; use **Reauthenticate** if the\npage says you are already authenticated). First-time connect also works from the\nworkspace create/update form and from Settings -> Tokens / external auth. Device-flow\nlogin is not enough for private repos: install Coder's GitHub App with **Install\nGitHub App** on the same bar (or open\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new),\ngrant those repositories, then restart. An empty\ngithub.com/settings/apps page is not a misconfiguration:\nwith `auth_type=client_secret` Coder has no App to install, and Coder's built-in GitHub\nApp only earns a page there once it is installed on the account. Until something is\ninstalled and granted, that page simply has nothing to **Configure**, and the credential\nthe provider mints still authenticates - it just cannot write to a private repository."],[1084,"Then finish the two browser-only steps: run the Kasm wizard once at `https://<host>:3000`\nand use `https://<host>:4443` after it, and open the dashboard at `https://<host>/` with\nthe `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` that `bootstrap` printed."],[3033,"Then hand the deletions to a human:"],[2702,"Then look at the `== terminal launchers: start ==` run in the workspace start log and\nread its `summary:` line: a green `2 ok, 0 warnings, 0 failed` with no files is the old\ntemplate, and `3 failed` with two `error:` lines is a boot that still cannot write."],[1077,"Then look before you change anything. `--check` is read-only: missing tools, placeholder\nsecrets, certificates, images."],[2672,"Then open a new terminal; `PATH` there already includes `~/.grok/bin`."],[224,"Then push `workspace` (the repo is not mounted into the container, so the directory has to\ntravel as a tar):"],[230,"Then re-pin both values:"],[2815,"Then read the store with the pair cleared, because `gh auth status` on its own reports\nthe env pair rather than what is stored:"],[2861,"Then restart the workspace. The six are agent environment values Coder renders per start\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:549`, `:639-653`) and the body is `run_on_start` (`:720`),\nso a running workspace keeps the empty set until it starts again."],[2710,"Then run `bash -lc 'command -v grok'`. A workspace on a current image answers\n`/usr/local/bin/grok`, so an empty answer means the CLI is missing, not the profile."],[2246,"Then the note the CLI prints is the real edge, and it is worth repeating here: compose\nreads `.env` only when a container is recreated, so the rotated password reaches Coder on\nthe next `up`. Nothing hot-reloads it."],[2213,"Then, any time after, `check` is the single command that asks every precondition at once:"],[2053,"Then, from the checkout on the stack host, in this order. Each one splits one symptom from\nthe next."],[3051,"Then, in a new session in the repository you want to convert:"],[186,"Then, per template, it confirms an **active** template version. A push can exit 0 and\nstill leave the template inactive if its Terraform run failed, which shows up as the\ntemplate missing from the create dropdown."],[1751,"There are no rpm, tpm or `max_parallel_requests` caps anywhere, on purpose. A cap parks\nrequests behind an in-memory semaphore inside the proxy; without one, a fully offered\nbox sheds load in its own scheduler."],[2160,"There is a **second gate** under the first: `INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START`, default `false`.\nIt decides only whether a workspace's boot fetch dials the vault at all. It is off by\ndefault so a workspace never waits on the vault, and it does nothing while\n`INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off. Both gates are read inside the boot body itself, so the rendered\nscript is one text for every workspace and the flag pair arrives on the agent environment."],[2195,"There is deliberately no `--json` mode: a JSON answer about a credential is an answer that\nleaks it in a different font."],[210,"There is no GitHub-token template variable, so no push ever carries a personal access\ntoken. See GitHub credentials for a workspace."],[1286,"There is no longer one `config.toml` to set once for a whole account: each\nworkspace renders its own from the template on start. A hand edit there lasts until\n**that** workspace restarts and touches no other workspace, and the next start after\na template re-push brings the file back from the new template."],[2259,"There is no reason to open `.env` by hand while this subsystem is adopted, and no command\nhere prints a credential. If you are cat-ing the env file to debug a sync, you are reading\nthe wrong file: `status` and `check` answer the same question."],[2636,"There is no second lane behind this one, so run these probes before touching anything\nelse. A service that answers but a server that will not handshake is the MCP hop; a\nserver that cannot launch at all usually means no\n`/opt/npm-global/bin/searxng-mcp-server`, and that binary is baked into the golden\nimage - an image that predates it needs a rebuild and a workspace recreate, not a\nconfig edit. Park the lane with `search_mcp = false` on the next template push\n(`SEARCH_MCP=false` in `.env` applies it to every new workspace) when a session should\nhave no search tool at all. To restore the native tool, flip `disable_web_search` to\nfalse in both template twins and re-push - only for an endpoint that really serves the\ntool, or the 400 comes straight back."],[2336,"These are what the shipped defaults compose. The operator sets the parts, not these\nstrings: the grammar is `<service>-<environment>-<suffix>.<base>`, always ONE label\nunder the zone."],[1870,"These files belong to the scaffold repository. This repo does not ship them, lint them, or keep a\ncopy: it fetches them at run time and renders them. The scaffold is language-neutral at the core,\nwith optional per-language lanes. Everything below was chosen from what is measurably missing\nacross the account, not from taste: across a sample of eleven ordinary projects, `SECURITY.md`,\n`CONTRIBUTING.md`, issue templates, a pull-request template, `dependabot.yml`, `CODEOWNERS` and\n`.editorconfig` were each present in none of them, and no repository in the surveyed set had a\nsingle release or tag."],[531,"These four sections are also the long form of the `.env.sample` comments that\npoint here: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, and the\nSpark `agent` model group. Where a topic page already owns a subject, this page\npoints at it instead of restating it."],[1646,"These retries are instant and stay on the same box. A status-less exception means\n`_deployment_ids_to_skip_on_retry` excludes nothing and `_time_to_sleep_before_retry`\nreturns 0 while any deployment is healthy, and on the five pinned groups the\nworkspace-wide `x-litellm-session-id` pin sends every subagent of a workspace to one\nbox anyway. The block makes a refusal less likely to surface. Relocation comes from the\nbox and the fan-out. The four unpinned lanes - `agent-swarm`, `agent-explore`,\n`agent-review` and `agent-swarm-fast` - are the exception: with no pin to name a box,\neach of their retries is re-placed by `least-busy` and can land on another one."],[1376,"They differ from the authoring trio in one structural way: two of them shell out to a\npython helper, so their seed ships `scripts/owned_files_check.py`, `scripts/glob_budget.py`\nand `scripts/type_baseline.py` beside the `SKILL.md`. Those helpers are stack payload like\neverything else here, which means the copy in a workspace is stack-owned while it carries the\nmarker, and the way to fix a helper is to fix the seed under `templates/common/` and\nre-materialize, never to patch the copy that is running."],[1617,"Thinking levels: what `agent` bakes, and what the baked aliases change"],[2003,"Thirteen JSON files in `observability/grafana/dashboards/` are the only dashboards this\nstack ships. `observability/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/stack.yaml:11` loads them all\ninto one `EVO-T1` folder (`:14`) with `disableDeletion: true` (`:16`),\n`updateIntervalSeconds: 60` (`:17`) and `allowUiUpdates: false` (`:18`). Read those three\nvalues before you try to fix a panel. Then find your question in the tables below: every\npage name and every panel name quoted here is copied out of the JSON, so you can search\nthe UI for it. Panel counts include the panels a collapsed row holds."],[2205,"This creates the super admin, the organization and the machine identity **inside the\n`infisical` container**, and writes the returned identifiers into `.env`. It is\nidempotent in the safe direction: if `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID` or\n`INFISICAL_MACHINE_IDENTITY_ID` is already set it prints a `note:` and refuses to run\nagain. It needs `python3` on PATH to parse the response, and the CLI from step 3. The\nmachine identity's own token is checked for presence and deliberately not stored: it stays\ninside Infisical and every later run exchanges the pair below for a fresh one."],[3003,"This fleet's own dev-versus-stable split is the reference implementation: separate project\nnames, separate dotenv files, and a subdomain composed from parts rather than from a branch\nname, driven by `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack -- up`,\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up`, and `bazelisk run //scripts:update-stack` for the\npull-and-deploy pair. In that repository the environment branches were still sitting on the\nremote (`dev`, `development`, `staging`, `uat`, `prod`) while every one of them reported\n`ahead=0` against `main`: five branches, zero unique commits, waiting for someone to confirm the\ndelete."],[2767,"This is not a broken credential. It happens with a valid token too, because Authelia\ngates every route."],[398,"This is not an invitation to hand-edit the file. `config.toml` is re-rendered from\n`grok-config.toml.tftpl` on every start of this workspace, so an edit a person makes\nthere is overwritten by the next boot and touches no other workspace. The keys\na person actually wants are template variables: `grok_subagent_max_concurrent`,\n`grok_subagent_max_depth` and `grok_subagents_lane` for the fan-out\n(the fan-out seam), and `grok_config_extra`\nfor anything the managed block does not set. Change the seed or push the variable, then\nrestart; `bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --force` re-materializes the file from\nthis checkout when the intent really is to rewrite it."],[2155,"This is the **free self-hosted tier**. No dynamic secrets, no cross-project sharing, and\nno `LICENSE_KEY` anywhere in the checkout: both of those features are paid, and the\nbundle ships neither. What is here is static shared secrets in one project, exported as\ndotenv."],[2869,"This is the belt, not the filter. The loop already drops every deny-listed name before a\nline is staged (`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:181-189`), compared\nupper-cased so `BASH_ENV`, `bash_env` and `Bash_Env` fall in one arm, and the body's own\ncomment reserves the second pass for a filter that regressed or a staged file edited by\nhand (`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:219-229`). A vault key that is simply\non that list is therefore *not* this entry: it disappears from `agent.env` with no line\nat all, which is the quiet path."],[2970,"This is the human-readable twin of the `trunk-refactor` skill\n(`skills/trunk-refactor/SKILL.md`). The skill is what an agent session follows; this page is\nwhat a person reads, with the examples the skill leaves out to stay short. The branch rules\nthemselves live in Branching - this page is about getting a repository *onto*\nthose rules."],[2425,"This is the only mode that looks at a box. It ferries one read-only probe\n(`scripts/lib/probes/spark-monitor.sh`) base64-encoded through `probe_transport`\n(`scripts/lib/spark.sh:173`), which prints twelve `KEY=value` lines per box: docker\navailability, both exporters' state, port and image, the DCGM counter count, and the GPU\ncount and models. The probe never pulls, never writes, never installs, and exits 0 even\nwhen the box has nothing on it - the CLI decides what is a warning. Captured output from\nthis workspace, host names elided:"],[838,"This is the question that costs the most sessions, because the answer is spread over four different\nmechanisms and each one is normally discovered by a failing gate."],[875,"This is the trap that makes a hand-check unreliable, and it is the reason the CLI keeps Bazel's\nstderr instead of discarding it. A query can print `ERROR: no such target` and leave stdout empty,\nwhich looks exactly like \"nothing depends on this\":"],[860,"This is why an unlisted payload directory stays invisible rather than being checked loosely: a file\nthat travels in no runfiles tree is a file no sandboxed action can open, so `test-fast` and `lint`\nnever read it and never fail. Nothing prints a warning about that. `owners <path>` and\n`unlisted <dir>` are the two ways to ask, and they are the difference between a payload the harness\nguards and a payload nobody has ever executed."],[1374,"This is workflow curation, separate from the memory system, which stores durable facts\n(upstream `/remember`, `/memory`, `/dream`, shipped here as `/ez-learn`), and from\n`AGENTS.md`, which is the always-loaded rules file. A skill loads when its `description`\nmatches, and holds a procedure."],[1701,"This key is the opt-out; a client header is not. An empty or short `x-litellm-session-id`\ndoes not skip the pin at this tag - that header is read verbatim at any length, and only\nthe generic `x-<vendor>-session-id` family is shape-checked - so a caller that tried to\nspread by sending an empty value would collapse every swarm request onto one shared pin."],[2376,"This repo cannot verify any of it; check it on the Traefik box."],[823,"This repo keeps its coupling in BUILD files: `scripts/BUILD.bazel` names the libs and payload\ngroups each CLI reads, `tests/BUILD.bazel` names what each Bats suite opens, and the root\n`BUILD.bazel` aggregates every payload group into `//:stack_data`. `//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test`\nfails when those maps drift from the sources, so the graph is a record an automated reader can\ntrust, and \"which suites cover this file\" has a ground truth sitting in it. Answering that from\n`grep` re-derives what one `bazelisk query` prints."],[1377,"This repo keeps its own project-scoped `/trunk-refactor` under\n`skills/trunk-refactor/SKILL.md`, which is the rule of record for *this* repository and\ncites its gates by label. `/ez-trunk-based-repo` is the portable version of the same model:\nit discovers a target repo's own gates at inventory time rather than assuming Bazel, so it\nworks in a Compose, K3s, Ansible or Terraform repository too."],[1867,"This repository already uses the word *template* for a **Coder template**: the Terraform tree\nunder `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/` that `push-template.sh` registers with\nthe Coder server."],[1537,"This repository brings up one Compose project on a GMKtec EVO-T1 (Intel Core Ultra 9\n285H, Arc 140T iGPU, 96 GB DDR5) and keeps it runnable by conversation, by dashboard, or\nby `bazelisk`. Everything is a Bazel target addressed as\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`. On the stack host, `scripts/setup-host.sh` is\nthe one script meant to run straight from a shell. On a laptop, reopen the repo in the\ncontributor environment or run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image`."],[2208,"This seeds the twelve managed keys into the vault, skipping any key the env file does\nnot hold or holds empty - with a `warning:`, never an empty write. It then exports the\nproject again and compares a hash per key, so a set the server accepted and dropped is\nreported as a failure rather than a clean seed."],[2201,"This writes only the binary, at the path `infisical_cli_install_path` prints, and nothing\nelse changes. The release it fetches is the pin: `INFISICAL_CLI_VERSION` in `.env.example`\n(0.45.8 as shipped), from the goreleaser asset name the seam builds. Nothing here pulls a\nversion out of the network."],[1667,"Those eight groups reuse the same `SPARK1/2/3_OPENAI_URL` / `SPARK_n_API_KEY`\npairs as `agent`. They are separate model groups on the same hardware, not eight\nmore boxes: routing state is per group, so least-busy's counters and the session\npin are computed inside a group and two chats on different aliases can land on the\nsame box at once. What does not change is the box - 5 full 262144-token streams in\ntotal, whichever aliases arrive on it. This is not the \"two deployments on one\nbox\" mistake below, which is about a single group counting two URLs on one machine\nas two machines."],[451,"Those four are the whole bar. The proxy address reaches the operator as the `litellm`\nitem on the `workspace_info` tile below (`value = var.litellm_url`), a read-out\nrather than a proxied app, so health-checking the proxy is\n`curl http://host.docker.internal:4000/health/liveliness` on the stack host."],[1046,"Those numbers are also series now: `node_exporter` reads the same `/proc` and `/sys`\nread-only and `cadvisor` covers the containers, so the tile answers \"what is the box doing\nnow, as the dashboard sees it\" and the Grafana board answers \"and what was it at 03:00\".\nThe tile survives the bundle being off; the board does not."],[633,"Those operator runs are bounded: a script's non-interactive `docker exec` closes stdin\n(`</dev/null`), and the docker wrappers go through `timeout(1)` with `--kill-after`, so a\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem>` cannot sit forever the way `docker exec -i` did under\n`bazelisk run`, waiting for an EOF that never came. `logs --follow` is the intentional\nlong-running exception, and you stop it with Ctrl-C."],[2596,"Those re-dispatches came from this repo's own config. At the pinned tag\n`ghcr.io/berriai/litellm:v1.102.1` a stream that dies before its first chunk raises\n`MidStreamFallbackError`, which subclasses `ServiceUnavailableError`\n(`litellm/exceptions.py:1114` at that tag), and `get_num_retries_from_retry_policy` walks the\nexception MRO most-specific first, so it lands on `ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` and\nnever reaches `DefaultRetries`. A hung request never cools the box either: cooldown\nhard-excludes any exception whose string contains `APIConnectionError`, and\n`_deployment_ids_to_skip_on_retry` only skips deployments that answered a non-retryable\nint status code. Under the workspace-wide `x-litellm-session-id` pin every attempt\ntherefore returned to the same saturated box."],[1684,"Three deployments, one per Spark box, and no `order:` tier. A tier would make the\nlower boxes unreachable while any tier-1 box stayed healthy."],[1252,"Three limits are worth knowing before you trust a clean scan, and each is a choice rather than\nan oversight."],[2232,"Three more are credentials that never appear in a compose interpolation, so they\ntravel to their consumer through a CLI rather than through `${VAR}`:"],[2112,"Three of the host alerts exist because the obvious design has a blind spot. The two\nsingle-exporter gap alerts each wait for the *other* exporter to vouch for the box, so\nboth exporters dying at once - a powered-off host, a restarted Docker - was silent;\n`HostExportersSilent` asks no second opinion and fires on the double absence. A dead\nblackbox prober produces no probe series at all, so nothing it probes can report it;\n`BlackboxProbeGap` reads `up{job=\"blackbox\"}`, which is why Prometheus scrapes the prober\nas a service of its own. And the same argument one layer up: every rule pages *through*\nAlertmanager, and an Alertmanager that is gone loses no series that any other rule reads,\nso the bundle keeps evaluating and firing into nothing. `AlertmanagerGap` is critical\nwhere those two are warning, and asks both shapes of the fault - `absent()` for the\nservice being gone and `up == 0` for a container that runs without answering."],[2111,"Three rule files arrive with Prometheus and need no second registration:\n`observability/prometheus/rules/stack.rules.yml` carries the twelve host alerts,\n`spark.rules.yml` the eight Spark alerts, and `inference.rules.yml` the ten inference\nalerts - 30 in all. Prometheus evaluates them. The bundle now also runs Alertmanager\n(`prom/alertmanager:v0.35.0`) to group them, hold them, and decide who hears about them."],[972,"Three runfiles traps bit this gate, and all fail *silently*, so they are worth\nnaming:"],[2745,"Three separate reads, from the stack host. The first prints `unhealthy` rather than\nan error when the daemon fails its own probe, and `no such container` when the\nworkspace has no sidecar at all:"],[1191,"Three stack-owned numbers decide whether a fan-out of subagents is parallel here at all:\nhow many slots one session gets, which lane those children's traffic rides, and whether\nthe work was cut finely enough to fill the slots. The first two are the rendered\n`[subagents]` keys just above; the third is a seeded skill and one command the parent runs\nbefore wave 1."],[967,"Three targets answer \"is this code actually executed by a test\" instead of\nassuming it. They are `manual` like the npm gates, so `//:test-fast` stays fast, and\nthey are members of `//:test-all`, which pre-push and CI run:"],[1370,"Three things about skills matter in practice here:"],[1728,"Three ways to land this feature and get nothing. The first is caught for you at the pinned\nLiteLLM and the other two are silent, which is why the silent two are what the gates check:"],[2623,"Titles that never appear and a long chat that dies at its context budget are the\nhelper lanes pointing at a dead alias. Re-running the script moves them."],[2329,"TLS terminates at Traefik. The `router` sidecar holds no certificate."],[2158,"To adopt it, set the key in the env file that belongs to the project you run - `.env` for\nthe stable stack, `dev-stack.env` for the dev one - and bring the stack up as usual:"],[2704,"To bring one workspace back while the restart waits, re-materialize the two files from a\nshell in it: `bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --home \"$HOME\"`. The **bash**\nprofile in the terminal dropdown stays the route no helper mediates."],[444,"To change the body, edit `templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl` and re-materialise\nit with `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`. It is not one of the twins:\n`scripts/lib/template-push.sh:188` stages the common copy beside each `main.tf` and\noverwrites any same-named file a template dir still carries, and what a running workspace\nholds is Coder's Filestore row for its template version, which the next push replaces too.\nNothing here is fixed from inside a workspace."],[1291,"To put native search back on a transport that really serves the tool: flip the key\nto `false` in the seed - both `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/`'s copy - and re-push, which applies it fleet-wide. For one\nworkspace without a push, the create form's **Grok config extras** field appends\nfree-form TOML verbatim to that workspace's file. Either way start a new session,\nand expect the same 400 back if the endpoint still answers function tools only."],[1503,"To reseed deliberately:"],[2651,"To take the same reading from inside the workspace, replay the path on `:8080`, the\npublished `router` port. That reaches `coder:3000` through the sidecar while\nbypassing Traefik, Authelia and the WAN, so a reply there that differs from the\nbrowser's `https://` URL names the cloud hops."],[1982,"To turn the bundle off, set the key in the env file that belongs to that project -\n`.env` for the stable stack, `dev-stack.env` for the dev one - and bring the stack up:"],[1589,"Tool calls: the measured limitation"],[1616,"Tool calls: the measured limitation and the path that supports them"],[2893,"Traefik and Authelia have to pass `Upgrade`. Cloudflare's orange-cloud proxy\nhas WebSockets on by default. A page that loads and a Chat tab that dies is\nthis hop, not the basic-auth provider."],[2314,"Traefik configuration"],[2448,"Troubleshooting"],[2450,"Troubleshooting"],[1087,"Troubleshooting — start here when a step above says no."],[1841,"troubleshooting.md for the symptom-first list once the\nbox is back up."],[2940,"Trunk-based + flags refactor"],[2942,"Trunk-based + flags refactor"],[2,"Trunk-based branch rules: main is the only long-lived branch, work branches are short-lived, and a pull request is the only way back in."],[20,"Trunk-based, GitHub-Flow shaped: one long-lived branch, short-lived work branches, and a pull\nrequest as the only way back into `main`."],[2199,"Turn the flag on: `INFISICAL_ENABLED=true` in the project's env file."],[2939,"Turning the flag on needs the payload tree, not just the flag: the six bind mounts read\n`observability/**`, so a checkout that has `docker-compose.yml` ahead of the\n`observability/` directory (a partial pull) fails mounting, the same failure shape as\nthe missing bind-mount source at the top of this page."],[1332,"Twenty-six files seed into every new workspace, on first boot and only while the file is\nabsent (the fleet family adds seventeen more paths under `~/.grok/agents`,\n`~/.grok/personas` and the two skill homes, on the contracts in\nThe seeded fleet suite):"],[2000,"Two consequences of the `:ro` mounts, both the same rule the other payload directories\nfollow. A bind follows the *path*, so an edit made in the checkout is already inside the\nrunning container, and the only question left is when the process reads it again:\nand these are directory binds, so measured against the pinned image an in-place rewrite\nof a mounted file and a rename of a new one over it both reached the container with no\nrestart and no recreate, which is why the dashboards section below can promise a minute\nrather than a redeploy. The one\ncredential that behaves differently is the LiteLLM bearer token, and the reason is that\nit is not a bind - Compose writes a `configs` entry when it creates the container, so\nrotating `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` takes `up -d --force-recreate prometheus`, where a plain\n`up -d` leaves the old bytes in place, and so does a `restart`. And treat the provisioned\ndashboards and datasources as read-only in the UI: `allowUiUpdates: false` and\n`disableDeletion: true` stop a save from overwriting the JSON, so anything you build by\nhand lands in Grafana's own database in `grafana-data` instead - it works, and it drifts\nfrom git invisibly."],[2412,"Two containers per box, both created by this CLI and by nothing else:"],[1855,"Two different things called \"template\", and which one this page is about"],[1583,"Two doors, one key"],[870,"Two exclusions earn their place. `except kind(\"test_suite\", //...)` drops the aggregates, which is\nwhat the CLI filters on so `//:test-fast` cannot answer a per-file question, and\n`except kind(\"sh_test\", //lints:*)` drops the two manual lint targets, `//lints:shell` and\n`//lints:shfmt`, that ride on every script:"],[1702,"Two facts not to get wrong:"],[2317,"Two follow-ups, both by hand"],[899,"Two gates carry most of it, and both are members of `//lints:all`, so `//:lint`,\n`//:test-all`, and the `bazel` job of `.github/workflows/ci.yml` run them:"],[433,"Two gates, both `default = false`: `infisical_enabled` decides whether the vault is part\nof the workspace at all, `infisical_pull_on_start` only whether a start waits on it\n(`templates/workspace/coder.tf:207-274`). Neither is inferred from absence the way\n`OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED` is: that bundle ships on and an explicit `false` drops it, while\nhere an absent key is off, and `push-template.sh` therefore passes no `--var` at all\nrather than passing `false` (`scripts/push-template.sh:623-630`)."],[932,"Two gates, one rule"],[470,"Two helpers are seeded into `~/.grok/bin` on every start, both `chmod +x`:\n`vscode-terminal-tmux` (the default profile) and `grok-build-terminal`. The two\nprofiles in `settings.json` exec exactly those paths, so the seed body reads both\nfiles back and prints `error:` naming the `exit code: 127` a tab gets when one did not\nland, rather than closing green. The missing-file cause and its remedy are in\ntroubleshooting.md."],[2646,"Two hops can answer 502 on that path: this box's nginx, when `coder:3000` will not\nresolve or connect, and Coder's own Go proxy, whose `ReverseProxy` ErrorHandler\nfires when the app upstream answers nothing. Two reads tell them apart, both from\nthe stack host:"],[1794,"Two keys, two purposes"],[530,"Two kinds of file go stale in this repo, and because neither of them is\nchecked in, `git pull` cannot fix either one for you. `update-configs` is the\none command that refreshes both, and it is written to be safe to run when you\nare not looking closely at the diff."],[1905,"Two kinds of path are removed on the way out: a lane's reserved metadata (`LANE.md`,\n`gitignore.fragment`, whose contents are consumed rather than copied) and the generator-owned paths\nin `NEW_REPO_TEMPLATE_META`, stripped from every render: `.template-lanes/` (the overlay source this\nscript reads), `.self-check/` (the scaffold's conformance suite, which goes stale the moment a real\nproject diverges from the template it was checked against), and `.git` (which would hand the new\nrepository the template's history and origin remote)."],[1847,"Two kinds of template"],[2163,"Two kinds of volume meet in that path and they are not the same thing. What rides per owner is\nstate only: `~/.grok/notes`, `~/.grok/memory` and `~/.grok/sessions` on `grok-notes-<owner-id>`,\n`grok-memory-<owner-id>` and `grok-sessions-<owner-id>`, plus the `vscode-settings-<owner-id>`\noverlay at `~/.shared/vscode`. Everything else under `~/.grok` is a real file on that workspace's\nown home volume, `config.toml` above all - it carries this workspace's own model lanes, its\nsession-affinity key and its Grok theme. `~/.infisical` rides no per-owner mount at all, so\n`agent.env` sits on this workspace's own home and only this workspace reads it. That asymmetry is\nwhy the body filters rather than trusting a share boundary: a file a workspace can read is a file\nanything running inside it can read."],[1650,"Two knobs that look right and do nothing here: `retry_after` is only a `min_timeout` and\nis never consulted while the group has healthy deployments, and `fallbacks_by_model`\ndoes not exist in this tag (the proxy warns and ignores an unknown `router_settings`\nkey, so a config that looks correct silently does nothing, which is why\n`//tests:bats_litellm_config_test` rejects it by name)."],[2228,"Two lists sit outside the inventory, and they are outside for different reasons."],[1243,"Two more sit beside it in the same directory, invocable by name under the same rule that every\nid in them is a local alias:"],[2736,"Two near-misses read differently on purpose. A profile whose `[subagents]` table\nis simply absent answers `warning: no 'max_concurrent' line - this profile has no\n[subagents] table; restart the workspace to re-materialize`, and a restart is the\nright order there. A table that lost only its width line answers `warning: the\n[subagents] table carries no 'max_concurrent' line - a hand edit, which no restart\nundoes`, because the render writes that line and a file the render still owns\nnever loses it on its own. A profile that answers `warning: no '# fleet-width:'\nline above max_concurrent - this profile predates it; restart the workspace to\nre-materialize` has no ceiling to compare against yet, so that one is a restart\ntoo."],[1474,"Two parts of that list are deliberate. `compaction-fast` is served by the proxy and\nkept out of the picker on purpose: it is the Arc summariser the two `-fast` lanes hop\nto for one conversation summary, it carries no tool support, and a session must not\ndrive itself with it. `discover_models: false` on the `litellm` row is what keeps it\nout of the list - left on, Hermes probes the proxy's `/v1/models` and adopts whatever\nit advertises. Read that as a hide rather than a wall: no row declares the id, and a\nname no row declares is *accepted* as an unknown hidden model on the current\nprovider, so a typed `/model compaction-fast` still puts a turn on it. Nothing\nrefuses it for you."],[3043,"Two patterns worth reading across those rows. A branch family that is `ahead=0` (this repo,\n`inkorporated`) is a cleanup with no risk once a human confirms it. A branch family that is\nhundreds of commits ahead (`nvidia-dgx-spark-lab`, `ez-comfy-stack`) is a porting programme:\nslice by area, land each slice green, and let the flags hold what is not finished. Both begin\nwith the same inventory, and both end with a table a person signs off on."],[1442,"Two reasons it lives on the volume: the gateway registers the adapter from the\n*presence* of `NTFY_TOPIC`, so an empty `NTFY_TOPIC=` in compose would hand it a\nhalf-configured platform, and a token in a container's environment is readable by\nanyone who can `docker inspect` it. Leave `NTFY_ALLOWED_USERS` unset and the channel\nis push-only - your phone gets the notices, and nobody can talk back to the agent\nthrough the topic. Set it (to the topic name) only for inbound, and only on a topic\nno one else can guess: upstream's own warning is that every publisher to a topic is\ntreated as the same user. `NTFY_SERVER_URL` defaults to `https://ntfy.sh`; a\nself-hosted ntfy keeps the notices on your hardware."],[726,"Two rules decide every write, and they are the same rules the workspace startup applies:"],[1017,"Two services that read the box rather than serve code: the Homepage dashboard (the\nlanding page) and Kasm Workspaces (streamed desktops)."],[110,"Two templates live under `templates/`, and a push covers both."],[1574,"Two templates turn a git URL into a running container: the plain `workspace` template, and\n`devcontainer`, which builds the clone's own `.devcontainer` on a DinD sidecar. Both are\npushed from this checkout, and both seed the agent config the workspace starts with."],[1274,"Two things about that start are worth knowing before you go looking for a vanished\nsetting. Boot **writes `git config --global core.excludesFile`** — but only when you\nhave not set it yourself. Left unset it points at `~/.grok/notes/exclude`, which lists\n`notes.md` and `.scratch-notes.md`, so from then on *every* git repository in that\nworkspace ignores both names at any depth; if you already set the option, those two\nlines are appended to the file it names and nothing else about it is touched. Boot also\ndrops the same two lines into each clone's own `.git/info/exclude`, which is the layer\nthat works even with no global config at all. And when a repository tracks a `notes.md`\nof its own, the scratch file is written as `.scratch-notes.md` beside it instead — your\nclone is never asked to delete somebody else's committed file."],[157,"Two things follow from the mechanism:"],[2582,"Two things on this stack cause the evictions, and both are tuned here rather than on the\nbox:"],[850,"Two things scope tells you that a habit of `bazelisk test //:test-fast` hides:"],[1256,"Two things seed them, and both are wanted. `coder_script \"stack_hooks\"`\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:810`, `templates/devcontainer/main.tf:861`) runs\n`templates/common/stack-hooks.sh.tftpl` on every workspace start (`run_on_start`, gated on\n`data.coder_workspace.me.start_count`), and `scripts/lib/config_seed.sh:160-165` carries the\nsame six targets for a host tree or a workspace that missed a boot. This family is\n**marker-aware** rather than absent-only like the skills: a copy that still carries the stack\nmarker - the `\"__evoT1Stack\": true` key in the manifest, which has to stay parseable JSON, and\nthe `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment in the shell files - is rewritten on every start.\nThat is deliberate, because a guard that drifted from the seed is a silent behaviour change:\nthese scripts decide what a session may run and what it may write. Delete the marker and the\ncopy is yours, and the boot leaves it alone."],[1914,"Two things, and neither replaces the CLI."],[910,"Two well-known switches are deliberately off. `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` collides\nwith React's optional DOM props and with fumadocs' `StaticOptions`;\n`noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature` collides with how the unit tests read the\nfumadocs source config. Both would be paid for in casts, which is the wrong\ndirection."],[1285,"Two workspaces of one owner that both hold a clone of the *same* repository each run a\nnotes watcher over the same canonical path, so there too the last write wins, tick by\ntick. The stub and empty guards and the `backups/` archive are what bound the damage a\nlosing tick can do; they do not merge two diverging files."],[1821,"Type the passphrase:"],[739,"Typed *inside* the contributor container that same target touches no docker:\n`EVO_T1_DEVCONTAINER=1` tells it this environment already is the golden image, so it\nexecs `bazelisk` in place. The command is the same on both sides of the seam, and a\ncontainer never nests inside itself."],[882,"TypeScript"],[889,"TypeScript: the compiler switches, and how the `.mjs` scripts get checked"],[2515,"UI and logins"],[2885,"Under `auto` both sides come from the same parts, so a `421` on a name nobody\nrecognises is a part problem, not a router problem: an empty `STACK_DOMAIN_BASE`\ncomposes nothing (the stack stays LAN-only) and a mistyped `STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX`\nor `STACK_ENV_LABEL` composes a name no vhost, no DNS and no certificate serves.\nRead the composed list back from `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` in `.env`, fix the part, and\nre-run `bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap`, then recreate. Editing the rendered\n`proxy/router.gen.conf` instead only survives until the next bootstrap, which\nre-renders it from the parts."],[578,"Under `auto` the parts are the source of truth and `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is output\nonly: bootstrap writes the resolved list back and records the intent in\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE=auto`, so a later run re-composes and a list edited by hand\nin `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is ignored. Bootstrap prints a `note:` naming the key to\nclear when that happens."],[973,"Under `bazelisk test` every source arrives as a **symlink**. A `find -type f`\nover the tree then sees no regular file and reports an empty suite, which reads\nas \"nothing to test\". `py_cov.sh` uses `find -L`."],[2247,"Under that sits one trap. The Postgres image honours `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` **only at first\nvolume init**, so changing it in `.env` and recreating `db` would leave the stored\npassword unchanged and lock Coder out of its own database. Two things cover it:"],[1571,"Understanding a behaviour before trying to change it"],[2157,"Unlike `OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED`, which ships on, **an unset key means off**. The\nacceptance test is `truthy()` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` - `on`, `1`, `true`, `yes` - and\nanything else, including the key being absent entirely, is off. Off means the three\ncontainers are never selected (`profiles: [\"secrets\"]`), the runner CLIs pass no profile\nargv (`compose_infisical_args` in `scripts/lib/infisical.sh` prints nothing, mirroring\n`compose_observability_args`), and every mutating verb of the CLI refuses with a `note:`\nthat names the key to set. That is deliberate: a subsystem nobody opted into must not be\nable to hold the stack's credentials hostage."],[1786,"Unlock an encrypted host remotely"],[1788,"Unlock an encrypted host remotely"],[1791,"Unlocking from a laptop"],[2901,"Use the name that failed, and `dev-stack` for the dev project. A service that restarts\ninto the same failure has usually been handed a config file it rejects -\n`observability/loki/loki.yml` and its three siblings are bind-mounted read-only, so the\nfile in the checkout is the file that failed, and `git diff` names what changed."],[225,"Use this shape for reference, or for pushing a tag `.env` does not carry."],[684,"Use VS Code, Cursor, or any editor with the Dev Containers extension, and reopen this\nrepository **in a container**."],[1043,"v2.4.0 serves a prerendered page with the settings baked in, so a freshly created\ncontainer shows an empty default layout (`initialSettings {}`) until the config is\nre-read. Click the refresh button in the header, or:"],[1780,"Vanilla Ollama on an Intel iGPU is effectively CPU-bound, and several times slower on a\n32B coder model."],[918,"Variables are declared before they are used and carry their shape in the\ndeclaration: `local -a` for an indexed array, `local -A` for an associative one,\n`local -r` or `declare -r` for a constant. Naming the kind is what makes an array\nthat was meant to be empty distinguishable from one that was never set."],[3038,"Verified against the remotes on 2026-11-08. Repositories move: re-run the Phase 0 inventory\nrather than trusting this table."],[2499,"VS Code Web opens `/home/coder` instead of the cloned repository -> last workspace won"],[2503,"VS Code Web tab in Chrome creeps to gigabytes, or freezes now and then -> every tab was another mirror of one pane"],[1820,"Wait a minute, then connect on the initramfs port. The check prints the\ncommand; it keeps its own `known_hosts` on purpose:"],[1123,"Watching those sessions from anywhere"],[1398,"Web research"],[1409,"Web research through the stack's own SearXNG, with the third-party fallback off"],[2381,"WebSocket `Upgrade` forwarded by Traefik and Authelia on the Hermes router. The\nChat tab uses `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`, and Hermes sends a keepalive every 20\nseconds. A proxy that drops `Upgrade` loads the page and then fails the tab."],[2321,"What `bootstrap.sh` changes when the names are set"],[2313,"What `bootstrap` changes when the names are set"],[1126,"What `grok doctor` reports inside a workspace, and which findings are expected"],[1800,"What `install` writes, and which file each line belongs to"],[11,"What `main` is allowed to be, and what may not be committed to it"],[668,"What `seed-dev-env` materializes into that home, and what it refuses to touch"],[232,"What a create form shows, in `workspace` order."],[766,"What a page's frontmatter has to carry, and what each field is used for"],[815,"What a target actually reads, and why an unlisted payload directory stays invisible"],[806,"What a target reads, and why an unlisted directory is invisible"],[2147,"What a vault outage does and does not do to `up`, to `pull`, and to a workspace boot"],[1591,"What agent mode needs"],[1584,"What an alias is"],[2104,"What an empty `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL` means for who gets paged"],[604,"What Bazel brings"],[611,"What Bazel brings over running the scripts directly"],[1599,"What carries a session id"],[660,"What CI does"],[671,"What CI does, and why this repo's own Coder workspaces stay on `workspace`"],[15,"What CI runs, what branch protection should require, and where secrets go"],[2816,"What each answer means: a store that now holds a token means git is repaired and the\nrefresh script keeps it fresh (start, then every 20 minutes); `gh refused the token, so the\nstore is unchanged` means the credential is revoked or the account disconnected, so run\n`check` to see what github.com says about it, then take the clicks from\nthe reauth entry;\n`cannot install the credential helper - gh is not installed` is\nthe gh-not-installed entry."],[1009,"What each dashboard card shows and which API it reads, the search lane included"],[1851,"What each GitHub step needs"],[1941,"What each of the ten services does, and why this stack ships it at all"],[1600,"What floats freely"],[2165,"What gets published is whatever the workspace's own machine identity can read in the project,\nwhich is what the vault scopes - the twelve-key inventory is a host-side list\n(`infisical/managed-secrets.txt`) and is deliberately not repeated in the body. So the workspace is\nnot limited to those twelve names, and two filters run on the key name before a line is written:"],[511,"What goes stale"],[509,"What goes stale after a `git pull`, what `update-configs` refreshes, and what it deliberately will not touch."],[1721,"What has been measured is the old path, not the new one. The 107 s to 1915 s spread and the\n18 abandoned successes above are both from the Spark lane before the hop existed, swept out\nof the session logs in troubleshooting.md. Neither tier has a timing\nof its own yet: the hop has not served a summary on a running stack, so no speed-up figure\nis claimed for either one. `tests/python/test_compaction_router.py` proves which group each\nrequest shape lands on; the operator's own log line is what will prove what it costs."],[1790,"What install writes"],[167,"What is deliberately not done: bind-mounting `templates/` into the workspace so the\nscript can `cat` a payload off the host, and fetching one over HTTP at boot. The first\ncouples boot to a host path that a template push rewrites, and the second puts a\nnetwork dependency on the path that creates the home directory."],[1939,"What is deliberately not here"],[2138,"What is deliberately not here"],[1947,"What is not here - no pager destination until you set one, no object storage, no trace client"],[1109,"What is shared"],[73,"What it decides"],[2600,"What it deliberately does not do:"],[53,"What it does and does not do"],[1716,"What it is not: a client setting. The Grok Build CLI has no compaction-model key, so there\nis nothing in `~/.grok/config.toml` that points that call anywhere, and\n`[models] session_summary` is not the compaction lane either - it serves titles and the\nsession recap. The whole remedy therefore lives server-side, and the measurement behind it\nis the `compact failed: exceeded wall-clock budget 300s` section of\ntroubleshooting.md."],[71,"What it reuses, and the trust warning"],[1000,"What it shows"],[515,"What it will not do"],[724,"What it writes: `~/.tmux.conf`, the shared `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` (with the\ncode-server and desktop VS Code User settings symlinked to it), `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`, the rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`, the two terminal helpers in\n`~/.grok/bin`, `~/.grok/notes-setup.sh` plus this clone's `notes.md`, and the\n`~/.grok/bin` line in `~/.bashrc`."],[2803,"What its answers mean here: `REJECTED` beside `env token (GITHUB_TOKEN/GH_TOKEN)` is an\nexpired mint (401 refuses the credential itself, which is what an hour-old injected\ncopy is); `NO GitHub credential in the chain slot` means nothing is connected and\nnothing can be minted; `PUSH-READY` means the credential is fine and you want\nthe push-denied entry\ninstead."],[2390,"What lands on a Spark"],[390,"What makes the step safe is that it destroys nothing it cannot prove. Neither of its two\nchecks establishes that a writer is gone. vim writes the swap once about four seconds after\na change and then stays quiet while you read, so a live session idling over the file looks\nlike a crashed one; and each workspace runs in its own PID namespace, so the `/proc` scan\nsees this container's descriptors only, which leaves a sibling workspace holding the same\nfile open on the shared volume invisible from here. So an orphan is moved into\n`~/.grok/stale-swaps/` under a name the sweep gives itself, and stays recoverable with\n`vim -r` for a month. The archived copy is re-dated to the moment it was set down: `mv`\ncarries the swap's own mtime, and a workspace stopped for six weeks leaves a swap older\nthan the retention, which that same boot would otherwise archive and prune in one breath.\nA wrong call is one `ls` from undone."],[1529,"What runs here"],[1090,"What runs inside a workspace and in the Coder control plane: terminal profiles, the shared `~/.grok` profile, and where models come from."],[1531,"What runs on the box and which door each service answers on"],[1118,"What seeds the agent's context"],[1506,"What ships, and which question each one answers: `evo-stack-ops` (pull, status,\nrestart, logs for this stack), `coder-workspace` (workspaces and their GitHub auth),\n`coding-agents` (what the coding agents in those workspaces are doing right now,\nand how to answer, start, or stop one — the `//scripts:coder-sessions` verbs),\n`model-routing` (which LiteLLM alias, and how to switch it), `host-linux-ops` (the\nright Linux command, what each flag does, and the blast radius before it runs),\n`host-diagnostics` (read-only triage for a slow, hot, full or failing box),\n`web-research` (what is worth looking up, how to read an empty answer from the\nlane above, and what that lane refuses to do), and\n`deploy-from-github` (a named repository to running, with the read-first, pin-the-ref,\nisolate, preflight, health-check, keep-a-rollback order). The last four are what make\nthe terminal door worth opening: they carry the mentoring stance - inspect, explain,\npropose, then act on the yes."],[2114,"What that means day to day:"],[1301,"What the agent is told about all this lives in `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, which\n`coder_script.agents_seed` renders from `templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` into a\nbody of its own (see \"The one-argument ceiling\" in\ncoder-templates.md). Two sections cover\nthe lanes. **Research\nlanes** makes a lookup part of the normal loop rather than a last resort, with the\nrouting fixed by question type: a library, framework, CLI or setup question goes to\n`context7` (resolve the library id first, then query docs on that id); anything else\non the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool; a URL already in hand goes to\n`searxng`'s `fetch_content`, because Grok's native `web_fetch` is unavailable here\ntoo - `features.web_fetch` is on, but on a non-xAI endpoint that tool only runs\nthrough `toolset.web_fetch.proxy_endpoint`, and this stack sets none. It also states\nthat native `web_search` is parked so nobody spends a turn rediscovering the 400.\n**Tool output stays out of the parent context** makes that\ntool use delegated work: the child returns a digest - numbered findings, each one\nclaim, one source URL and at most two lines of support, no raw result dumps,\nconfidence stated - and keeps the raw pages to itself."],[2865,"What the boot keeps is whatever `~/.infisical/agent.env` an earlier start wrote, which on\na workspace that has never pulled is nothing at all. To load the credentials now, on the\nstack host:"],[1792,"What the check catches"],[1802,"What the check catches, and what it deliberately leaves alone"],[1410,"What the container mounts, and what \"be the host\" costs"],[2597,"What the fix changed, and how to land it:"],[1003,"What the gate checks"],[1715,"What the hop is: on those two aliases only, the proxy recognises the CLI's\nconversation-summary call and re-serves it from somewhere cheaper than the Spark box it\narrived on - `compaction-fast`, the small Ollama-served coding weight on the Arc, when the\nsummary fits that window, and a Spark group thinking at `low` when it does not. That is\nwhere the speed comes from - the Spark lane answers at xhigh thinking and a summary does\nnot need it. The CLI appends its summary instruction as the last turn of the body - the\nauto-compaction ask, or the `/compact` one wrapped in a `<summary_request>` tag - and the\ncallback matches a stable clause inside whichever one it sees,\n`produce a faithful, concise summary of the conversation so far` and\n`summarize the conversation so far`, in a turn short enough to be the instruction itself.\nIt reads that ask nowhere else in the body: the system prompt rides every turn of the\nsession, so matching there would move a whole conversation to the summary target the moment\nan operator pasted one of those sentences into a workspace rulebook. It reads it from\nwhichever key the protocol carries the turns under - `messages` for Chat Completions and\nAnthropic Messages, `input` for a model whose `api_backend` is `responses` - so the hop does\nnot quietly stop working on a protocol choice. A recognized ask is\nrewritten to the target model, with `tools`, `tool_choice` and `reasoning_effort` dropped\nfrom it: a summary is prose, the schemas are dead weight to it, and a small model handed a\ntool schema may answer with a call instead of a summary. Every other call of the same\nconversation stays on its Spark box, and a session on any other alias compacts exactly as\nit always has. The hop is a property of the alias, not of the proxy: picking one of these\ntwo aliases is the opt-in, and `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION=0` is the kill switch beside it. One\nresidue is worth naming: a user turn whose entire content is a quoted ask looks like an ask,\nand gets the summary target's answer."],[2140,"What the integration is, and the one property it deliberately does not change"],[1709,"What the lift costs is what it costs the swarm lane, said plainly: a second turn of the\nsame conversation is not sticky, so it may be prefilled on a different box and pay prefill\nagain. For a child that answers once there is no second turn to pay for; point a\nconversation at these aliases and there is. A long single-thread chat, an orchestrator, or\nanything whose turns share a warm prefix belongs on `agent`."],[2977,"What the model rules out: long-lived `development`/`staging`/`production` branches; \"merge to\ndevelopment first\"; a release branch that has to be kept forever; cherry-picking a fix from one\nenvironment branch to another; and a flag platform nobody asked for."],[1856,"What the scaffold repository ships, and the account-wide gaps it closes"],[1848,"What the scaffold ships"],[2700,"What the seed does about it now: each body that writes under `~/.grok` asks for its own\ndirectory through `boot_ensure_dir` (`templates/common/boot-log.lib.sh`), which creates\nit and takes back the ownership of each component that is not this boot's to write, and\nthe launcher body reads its two files back and prints `error:` naming the 127 when one\ndid not land. A boot that cannot write now says so in its own summary line."],[2693,"What the seed does about it, in the payload itself: the profile creates its session\ndetached and attaches in a second call, so the tab that brings the server up is not\nthe one whose close can take a running agent with it; every control call is bounded\nby `timeout`; and a create or attach that still fails lands the tab in a plain login\nshell rather than in VS Code's terminated-process message. So the message above names\ntmux itself failing, not the profile's logic. Confirm with:"],[2694,"What the stack does about it: both config twins deny the self-teardown family, so an\nagent's `kill-server`, `kill-session` or `pkill*tmux` is refused before it runs. The\n`[permission]` bullets in grok-build-and-agents.md carry\nthe whole list and what an agent uses instead."],[75,"What the startup script does, in order"],[49,"What the two templates make"],[98,"What the two templates make, and when the `devcontainer` toggle matters"],[432,"What the vault is, how to adopt it, and how to rotate a key in it is\noperate/secrets.md. This is only what the two templates do about it."],[2142,"What the workspace boot publishes into `~/.infisical/agent.env`, what it refuses, and the residual it cannot refuse"],[2398,"What this change does not prove"],[2405,"What this change does not prove: the DCGM tag, the profiling counters, NVLink"],[1795,"What this deliberately does not do"],[662,"What this is not"],[1122,"What this is not"],[1063,"What to read next"],[1590,"What was measured"],[2849,"What you see is `gh: command not found`, or `fix` refusing with `cannot install the\ncredential helper - gh is not installed in this environment; install gh (apt install gh,\nor the pinned copy the golden image ships) and run this again`. `check` reports the same\nhole honestly: with no gh there is no `gh stored login` slot to read, and a chain with\nnothing else in it prints `NO GitHub credential`."],[2818,"What you see is one of: `remote: Invalid username or token. Password authentication is\nnot supported for Git operations.`, `remote: Repository not found.`, `fatal:\nAuthentication failed for 'https://github.com/...'`, or `fatal: could not read Username\nfor 'https://github.com'`. None of them names the token or its age, and the first is\nwhat a refused token plus a silent askpass looks like, not anything to do with\npasswords."],[2854,"What you see is one warning between `-> vault: refreshing the env file from Infisical`\nand `-> compose up`, printed by `scripts/dev-stack.sh:681` and its prod twin\n`scripts/prod-stack.sh:657` (`${vault_env}` there is the env file that project reads, so\nthe checkout's `.env` on a default run):"],[2812,"What you see, in that `coder_script`'s log, is its own two lines:"],[2843,"What you see, inside a workspace: `gh api /user`, `gh pr create` or `gh repo view`\nanswers `Bad credentials` (HTTP 401) while the same command on the stack host works, and\n`gh auth status` still insists you are logged in. This is the most frequent GitHub\nfailure here: 19 of 222 past sessions in this workspace hit `Bad credentials`."],[2802,"What you see: `git push` answers `remote: Invalid username or token. Password\nauthentication is not supported for Git operations.`, or `gh auth status` calls the\ntoken invalid. An expired token and a never-connected account read the same from that\nmessage, so ask which one you have before you touch either."],[162,"When a body really does need to grow past the budget, the options are:"],[1586,"When a box answers `NoRoom`"],[1601,"When a pinned box cools or goes unhealthy"],[2447,"When a Spark panel stays empty, work outward in this order: `--status` (is the container\nthere and serving), `--targets` (is the box in the document at all), then the scrape on\nthe host through `bazelisk run //scripts:observability-check`. Symptoms that outgrow those\nthree commands belong in troubleshooting.md."],[2069,"When a symptom shows up as an empty panel, start at\ntroubleshooting.md, Observability section: the failures an operator\nactually hits are all on one side of a seam - the scrape, the write, the tenant path, or\nthe log root Alloy was given."],[1742,"When every Spark box is down, `router_settings.fallbacks` degrades `agent` straight to\n`coder`, which is text-only."],[2135,"When Infisical is unreachable"],[2806,"When nothing can be minted AND the store is empty its line begins `HANDOFF` and names\nthe three clicks below. That line is the boundary: no command run in that workspace can\ndo them."],[755,"When the container itself is the suspect, `bazelisk run //:validate` from inside it is the\nfastest answer, and ../troubleshooting.md covers the stack-side\nfailures it rules in or out."],[13,"Where a hotfix branches from and where it lands first"],[1861,"Where all of this is enforced"],[1608,"Where an alias edit lands"],[1944,"Where each component's config lives in the checkout, and what it costs to change"],[1930,"Where each config lives"],[2658,"Where it is: open the workspace in the Coder UI and read its start log, and from a host\nshell the same text is `coder logs <workspace>`. The agent's `startup_script` output is the\nbody of that log, and each `coder_script` prints under the name Coder labels it with -\n**Seed the Grok Build rulebook**, **Seed the fleet agents, personas and skills**, **Fetch\nstack secrets from Infisical**, and the rest, which are the `display_name` values at\n`templates/workspace/main.tf:731-1015`."],[56,"Where it runs, and why"],[524,"Where the command may run, and where it refuses to"],[1719,"Where the guard sits against the client: the shipped `[session]` block sets\n`auto_compact_threshold_percent = 70` and both fast aliases declare a `context_window` of\n262144 (`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:387`, and `:284` and `:301` for the two\naliases), so a session on either one reaches its auto-compaction at about 183,500 tokens -\nover seven times the guard. A summary that size cannot be read faithfully by a 32768-token\nmodel, so the Arc tier was never going to serve it and the guard is what stops it trying: on\nthat estimate the call goes to `agent-low`, which keeps the window and drops the baked\n`xhigh` thinking. Pointing `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_TARGET` at `agent-low` is the lever that\ncovers a full-size summary even with the limit left alone, and it is a routing choice with\nits own trade, not a free win: the summary is still a Spark call, holding a stream on a box\nthe writers want, at a thinking level turned down from the one the lane exists to bake."],[1857,"Where the scaffold is fetched from, and how to pin or replace it"],[770,"Where the sidebar comes from, and which check refuses a page it cannot reach"],[3010,"Where the switch selects infrastructure rather than behaviour, use the platform's own parameter\nsurface: a `variable` in the workspace template plus the values each workspace supplies, which is\nhow this stack already separates a development workspace from a stable one. Bump the template\nrevision; do not fork a template per environment."],[94,"Where the token comes from"],[1853,"Where this is enforced"],[2074,"Where to look"],[1796,"Where to look next"],[2101,"Where to look next"],[514,"Where to run it"],[1532,"Where to start depending on what you came to do"],[2245,"Whether a name counts as managed is decided by `infisical_is_managed_key` in\n`scripts/lib/infisical.sh`, which reads the inventory and compares the names inside the\nshell. The first version piped the reader into `grep -qx` instead, and under the CLI's\n`set -euo pipefail` that lost: `grep -q` exits on its first match, the reader then died of\nSIGPIPE, the pipeline reported 141, and the verb called a genuinely managed key unmanaged\non 195 of 200 attempts. Two suites now hold the line - one drives all twelve names through\nthe predicate inside a `pipefail` shell, the other runs the verb against each of them - so\nthe shape cannot come back unnoticed. A name outside the inventory is still an `error:` and\nexit 1, and the inventory itself stays the only list."],[2852,"Whether you are looking at a missing tool or a missing credential, `check` is what tells\nthem apart: a missing binary shows up as a slot that cannot answer, a missing grant\nshows up as `REFUSED-GRANT`."],[1047,"Which board, exactly, is catalogued in observability.md under\n`## The dashboards` - four families of them: the stack host (`evo-host-taskmanager`,\n`evo-containers`), the DGX Sparks (`evo-sparks-overview`, `evo-spark-host`,\n`evo-sparks-fleet`), the inference backends (`evo-inference-models`,\n`evo-inference-backends`), and the bundle itself (`evo-home`, `evo-stack-overview`,\n`evo-alerts`, `evo-stack-logs`, `evo-stack-traces`, `evo-litellm`). The question-to-page\ntable lives there; this page stays about the dashboard and the desktops."],[2660,"Which body failed: the `== <body>: start ==` header names it, and that name is\n`boot_begin`'s first argument rather than the file's. The eleven in the shipped `workspace`\ntwin are `workspace startup` (`templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl:19`), `code-server`,\n`agents seed`, `stack skills`, `repo skills`, `stack fleet`, `stack advisors`,\n`stack hooks`, `terminal launchers`, `github token refresh`, and `infisical secrets`;\n`devcontainer` adds `dind bootstrap`."],[1605,"Which box answered"],[521,"Which files go stale and why `git pull` cannot fix them"],[1859,"Which GitHub steps need which App permission, and what the CLI does when one is refused"],[1945,"Which of the thirteen provisioned dashboards answers which question, and how a panel actually gets changed"],[2077,"Which page answers which operational question"],[1932,"Which page answers which question"],[1567,"Which page explains which part of the stack"],[943,"Which runtime actually gates a merge, and which one is a convenience"],[934,"Which runtime gates"],[2589,"Which sessions, worst first, with the same `head` cap so a busy store stays readable:"],[2661,"Which stage it died in: the last `== <body>: stage i/N: <title> ==` line above the failure.\nOn every path that got past its header a body's final act is `boot_summary`, so a body that\nprinted a header and no closing `summary:` never reached the end of its own script - it died\nin the stage that line names, or Coder stopped it at the script's `timeout`. The startup\nbody's eight stage titles (`workspace startup`\nstages 1-8, `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl:37-326`) are the map for anything about\ntmux, the CLI, the shell profile, the skills and the notes library, the editor settings,\nthe Grok profile, ownership and the git credentials, or the repositories."],[1718,"Which tier answers: the guard is `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` (default 24000), and\nit picks a tier rather than ending the hop. Below it the summary goes to `compaction-fast`,\nwhich is the right place for it, because those weights answer inside a 32768-token window\nand Ollama clamps an over-long input instead of refusing it - a summary pushed in over that\ncomes back quietly truncated, and a truncated summary is worse than the slower one it would\nreplace. That window is also the ceiling the guard cannot raise past: the fork happens at the\nsmaller of `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` and the `max_input_tokens` `config.yaml`\ndeclares for the Arc target group, so `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT=100000` forks at\n32768 and logs one `compaction router: EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT=100000 is above\nthe 32768 tokens config.yaml declares for 'compaction-fast'; forking at the declared window\ninstead` line. The declared window is a fact about the weights and the pinned LiteLLM has no\ncontext-window check between the hook and Ollama, so a knob cannot reach past it; a target\ngroup that declares no window leaves the guard whole, and prints one\n`compaction router: 'compaction-fast' declares no max_input_tokens in config.yaml; forking at\nEVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT alone` line rather than failing quietly.\nAbove it the summary goes to `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` (default\n`agent-low`): the same three Spark boxes, the 262144-token window a real summary needs, and\nthe thinking baked to `low` instead of `xhigh`. Blank that knob, or name the lane the call\narrived on, and an oversized summary stays where it is, which is the pre-hop behaviour. The\nestimate is counted over everything the backend prefills - each turn's text, the payloads\nthe text flattener cannot see, and every request-level schema key - and it charges three\nclasses of character separately: ASCII prose at four characters to a token, a base64 payload\nat two, and anything outside ASCII at one. Reading those denser classes at the prose rate was\nhow a 90,000-character screenshot read as 22,500 tokens and went to a 32768-token model.\nEvery one of them errs toward the larger number, and the larger number means the Spark tier."],[813,"While you iterate: the derived test list, and when you still owe one uncached pass"],[804,"While you iterate: which tests, and when you still owe a full pass"],[1116,"Whose compaction is whose"],[969,"Why a ledger and not `bash -x`: xtrace traces bats-core itself, wrote 3.4 GB of log\nfor one suite, and leaked trace lines into the stderr bats captures per test, which\nturned output-asserting suites red. The ledger measures the narrower thing the gate\nasks and breaks nothing."],[797,"Why a query, not a grep"],[1798,"Why a reboot is the dangerous moment on an encrypted headless box"],[810,"Why a structural question is a query here, not a grep sweep"],[2105,"Why Alertmanager's UI is loopback-only, and where a silence belongs"],[767,"Why every page writes its own `# H1` and what the layout prints beside it"],[2850,"Why it blocks the repair rather than just the CLI: the helper `fix` installs is\n`gh auth git-credential` wrapped in `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN ...`, and the\ncredential it points git at is the gh auth store. With no `gh` binary there is no\nenv-proof helper to install and no store to write, so `fix` refuses and says so instead\nof leaving a half-repaired `~/.gitconfig` behind."],[1717,"Why the extra config is worth it: compactions that did succeed on the Spark lane took 107 s\nto 1915 s, and 18 of the 36 sampled successes ran past the CLI's 300 s wall-clock budget -\nthey are the summaries that budget was discarding. What the hop changes without a stopwatch\nis who answers: a 14B Arc weight with no thinking level and no schemas in the prompt, rather\nthan a Spark box thinking at xhigh over the same request with its tool schemas attached. No\ntiming has been measured on the hop yet, so no speed-up figure is quoted here for either\ntier; `docs/troubleshooting.md` names the one log line that shows it firing, and that line\nis where a measurement starts."],[2698,"Why the repair missed: the startup script does chown `~/.grok` to the home owner, and\nnothing orders that before this body. On Coder v2.36 the startup script and every\n`run_on_start` body fan out in one concurrent errgroup - ten bodies launched inside the\nsame millisecond in the start log - so the seeds run first and meet a directory they\ncannot write. They then said nothing about it: `mkdir -p ... 2>/dev/null` swallowed the\nrefusal, `cat >` failed, and the run printed `ok:` for each launcher and closed at\n`summary: 2 ok, 0 warnings, 0 failed`."],[68,"Why the shared bind path is load-bearing"],[76,"Why the stages sit where they do"],[661,"Why this repo's own workspaces stay on `workspace`"],[1609,"Why this shape"],[1082,"Wire the control-plane chat agent — print the intended end state, then apply it:"],[1756,"With a pin, it names the pinned box; without one, whichever box `least-busy` picked."],[2430,"with exit 2 - Bazel claims the flag as its own build option and the script never starts.\nEvery flag this CLI takes goes after `--`."],[3006,"With Helm the same shape is `values/dev.yaml`, `values/staging.yaml`, and\n`helm upgrade --install app ./chart -n lab-staging -f values/staging.yaml --set image.tag=...`.\nSecrets come from the cluster's own store or an external secret, never from the overlay."],[2435,"With it on, four groups per box: the node_exporter on `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT`, the\nDCGM exporter on `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT`, and the two blackbox probe URLs on\n`SPARK_HTTP_PORT` (`/health` and `/v1/models`). Every group carries the three labels the\nrule files and the dashboards group by - `box`, `exporter`, and `hostname` - so the GPU\nrows, the host rows and the probe rows of one box all land on the same identity. `--help`\nprints the usage block from the script's own header. The modes are mutually exclusive:\n`--status cannot be combined with --apply; pick one mode`."],[287,"With it on, per workspace:"],[590,"With no `SPARKn_HOST` and no `SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` set, compose takes the host from\n`STACK_LAN_HOST`, which is at least a machine this stack owns: `agent` stays\ndefined but unusable and LiteLLM falls back to `coder`, the Arc alias. That\nfallback is what the \"Agent backend\" card exists to catch - Grok Build, Cline and\nRoo need structured tool calls, which the Arc IPEX-LLM Ollama returns as plain text\nfor the Qwen2.5-Coder weights. litellm-routing.md owns the alias\ntable, the picker, the pin, and the measured tool-call limitation (including which\naliases do emit real calls)."],[1762,"With no concurrency cap configured, the proxy neither queued nor shed inside itself,\nand a fully offered box absorbed the overflow in its own scheduler."],[1146,"With no tmux (offline first boot) the helper execs a plain login shell, so the\ndefault terminal still opens."],[3036,"with the commands they will run:"],[434,"With the first gate off the `coder_script` is not merely idle - its `count` is `0`, so the\ntemplate renders no script, no boot log line, and no `INFISICAL_*` agent-env key, and a\nworkspace whose stack never adopted Infisical cannot tell the difference\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:708-721`). With the first on and the second off the resource\nexists and does nothing but name its gate (`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl:27-30`)."],[320,"With the toggle off - or on a repo that carries no `devcontainer.json`, where the build\nhas nothing to build - the editor attaches to the plain workspace agent instead, opened\non the same clone path."],[318,"With the toggle on, `coder_devcontainer.repo` then builds the repo's own\n`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` on the sidecar through the Coder agent\n(`@devcontainers/cli` is installed into the parent workspace container by a\n`run_on_start` module, and the golden image does not ship it)."],[2210,"With the vault and `.env` agreeing this writes nothing new. It is the read path, so it\nis the one to run before you trust `push`."],[1218,"Within these six, only `implementer` names `search_replace`, and only `implementer` and\n`tester` name `write` at all; the other four name no edit tool and each says in its own body\nthat it edits nothing. The seventh definition `templates/common/` holds, `documenter`, is the\nadvisor family's rather than this one's and names both `search_replace` and `write`\n(`templates/common/documenter.AGENT.md:5`), because the deliverable of that role is an edit to\na page - see The five advisor commands. `explorer` is the one\ndefinition here that inherits the MCP catalog, because it is the one whose tools name\n`search_tool` and `use_tool`; the other five set `mcpInheritance: none`,\nthe frontmatter key the guide documents for that choice\n(`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:228`), on the seeds' own reasoning that an\ninherited catalog with no MCP tool in `tools:` would be empty anyway. Three of the seven\nname `spawn_subagent`: `reviewer`, `security-auditor` and `tester`\n(`templates/common/reviewer.AGENT.md:5`, `templates/common/security-auditor.AGENT.md:5`,\n`templates/common/tester.AGENT.md:5`). `explorer`, `architect`, `implementer` and\n`documenter` name none of it, so a mapper cannot recurse and the wave a spawner raises one\nlevel down is made of read-only mappers. The `tools` list is what enforces that ceiling; the\nbody prose of each seed only explains it."],[837,"Without `--json` the same labels are interleaved with the report lines, so piping them straight into\nanother command feeds it `ok: tests-for` as an argument. Paths are repo-relative, and a batch costs\none query: when a batch fails, the verb re-runs it per path and names the path the graph does not\nknow instead of hiding every other answer."],[478,"Without the flag the script passes exactly the parameters it used to pass, and th